export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","name":"ModelCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"modelcraft","title":"0.7 లో కొత్తవి ఏమిటి","summary":"మాడిఫైయర్లతో మెష్‌లు, శిల్పం, UVలు మరియు కర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు, గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై పాత్ ట్రేసింగ్ మరియు రెండర్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్, స్కాటర్ మరియు పనోరమాలతో రియల్-టైమ్ లైవ్ వ్యూ, నోడ్ మెటీరియల్‌లు, బోన్‌లు మరియు ఫిజిక్స్‌తో యానిమేట్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్, USD, FBX, STEP మరియు .3dm ఫైళ్లు, మరియు ప్రారంభకుడు శైలితో ప్రింట్ టూల్స్.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["కొత్తవి","విడుదల గమనికలు","మార్పుల లాగ్","మార్పులు","0.7","వెర్షన్","కొత్త ఫీచర్లు","మెష్","మెష్‌లు","మెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్","మాడిఫైయర్లు","సబ్‌డివిజన్","క్రీజ్","uv","అన్‌రాప్","సీమ్‌లు","కర్వ్‌లు","కర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు","nurbs","బెజియర్","శిల్పం","రీమెష్","రీటోపాలజీ","విభజించు","లెక్క","పాత్ ట్రేసింగ్","webgpu","రెండర్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్","రెండర్ క్యూ","mp4","exr","డీనాయిజ్","భౌతిక ఆకాశం","hdri","ies","డెప్త్ ఆఫ్ ఫీల్డ్","లైట్ మిక్సింగ్","పొగమంచు","కాస్టిక్స్","లైవ్ వ్యూ","రియల్ టైమ్","నడక","స్కాటర్","పనోరమా","టూర్","వ్యూయర్","షేడింగ్","నోడ్స్","నోడ్ మెటీరియల్‌లు","మెటీరియల్ లైబ్రరీ","materialx","డికాల్స్","టెక్చర్ పెయింటింగ్","బేకింగ్","యానిమేట్","గ్రాఫ్ ఎడిటర్","క్లిప్‌లు","షేప్ కీలు","డ్రైవర్లు","ఆర్మేచర్","బోన్‌లు","స్కిన్నింగ్","ik","కన్‌స్ట్రెయింట్లు","ఫిజిక్స్","usd","usdz","gltf","fbx","step","3dm","alembic","ply","acis","3d సాలిడ్‌లు","సపోర్ట్‌లు","గోడ మందం","బోలు","మరమ్మతు","ప్రారంభకుడు","సహాయకులు","మీ యంత్రం","యూనిట్లు","పోర్చుగీస్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"కొత్తవి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"మెష్‌లు","level":3},{"id":"section-3","text":"డ్రాయింగ్","level":3},{"id":"section-4","text":"రెండరింగ్","level":3},{"id":"section-5","text":"లైవ్ వ్యూ మరియు ప్రెజెంటేషన్‌లు","level":3},{"id":"section-6","text":"మెటీరియల్‌లు","level":3},{"id":"section-7","text":"యానిమేషన్","level":3},{"id":"section-8","text":"ఫైళ్లు","level":3},{"id":"3d","text":"3D ప్రింటింగ్","level":3},{"id":"ai","text":"AI సహాయకులు మరియు మీ యంత్రం","level":3},{"id":"section-9","text":"పరిష్కరించినవి","level":2},{"id":"section-10","text":"అన్ని యాప్‌లలో","level":2},{"id":"section-11","text":"ఈ వెర్షన్‌లో ఇంకా లేనివి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft 0.6 తో పోలిస్తే 0.7 లో ఏమి మారిందో ఇది. ModelCraft ఇప్పుడు నెట్టు/లాగు జ్యామితితో పాటు మెష్‌లను కూడా మోడల్ చేస్తుంది, గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై రెండర్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ప్రతి రకమైన పనికి ఒక వర్క్‌స్పేస్ కలిగి ఉంది: టూల్‌బార్‌కు ఎడమవైపు ఉన్న \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E జాబితా నుండి దాన్ని ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eకొత్తవి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eమెష్‌లు\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమెష్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక గ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్‌ను ఎంచుకుని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంటిటీ సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెష్‌గా మార్చు\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి: అది ఏ సంఖ్యలోనైనా మూలలున్న ముఖాలతో మెష్ అవుతుంది, అవి టెక్చర్ కోఆర్డినేట్లు, నార్మల్‌లు, రంగులు మరియు వెయిట్‌లను కలిగి ఉండగలవు. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eజ్యామితిగా మార్చు\u003C/span\u003E దాన్ని తిరిగి మారుస్తుంది. మెష్‌పై అన్‌డూ ప్రతి దశ మార్చినదాన్ని మాత్రమే ఉంచుతుంది. మెష్ ఉన్న ఫైల్‌ను తెరవడానికి ModelCraft 0.7 అవసరం. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Eమెష్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్.\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక మెష్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E జాబితాలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెష్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, దాని శీర్షాలు, అంచులు మరియు ముఖాలను సవరించడానికి: లూప్‌లు మరియు రింగ్‌లు, అనుపాత సవరణ, ఎక్స్‌ట్రూడ్, ఇన్‌సెట్, బెవెల్, లూప్ కట్, నైఫ్, బైసెక్ట్, స్పిన్, మెర్జ్, డిజాల్వ్, ఫిల్, గ్రిడ్ ఫిల్, బ్రిడ్జ్ మరియు స్లైడ్; చివరి ఆపరేషన్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు తర్వాత ప్యానెల్‌లో మార్చబడతాయి. ప్లేన్, క్యూబ్, గోళం, టోరస్ మరియు ఇతరాల నుండి ప్రారంభించండి. నెట్టు/లాగు కీలు మరియు మోడెలర్ కీలు రెండూ పనిచేస్తాయి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003Eమెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో మెష్‌ను సవరించడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమాడిఫైయర్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E మిర్రర్, అర్రే, సబ్‌డివిజన్ సర్ఫేస్, బెవెల్, సాలిడిఫై, బూలియన్, డిస్‌ప్లేస్, వెల్డ్, డెసిమేట్, స్మూత్, లాటిస్, కర్వ్ డిఫార్మ్ మరియు ష్రింక్‌ర్యాప్ ఒక మెష్ ఏమి చూపుతుందో మారుస్తాయి, దాని జ్యామితిని మార్చకుండా. వాటిని పేర్చండి, ప్రతిదాన్ని వీక్షణ కోసం మరియు రెండర్‌ల కోసం ఆన్ లేదా ఆఫ్ చేయండి, మరియు శాశ్వతంగా కావాలనుకున్నప్పుడు ఒకదాన్ని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్తింపజేయి\u003C/span\u003E చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003Eమెష్‌పై మాడిఫైయర్లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eక్రీజ్‌లతో సబ్‌డివిజన్.\u003C/strong\u003E మెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లోని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eక్రీజ్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకున్న అంచులను సబ్‌డివిజన్ ఉపరితలం కింద పదునుగా ఉంచుతుంది. రెండర్‌లు వీక్షణ కంటే ఎక్కువ లెవల్‌లను ఉపయోగించవచ్చు, మరియు పెద్ద మెష్‌పై వీక్షణ తక్కువ లెవల్ చూపుతుంది మరియు అలా చెబుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers#subdivision-surface\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\" data-hash=\"subdivision-surface\"\u003Eసబ్‌డివిజన్ సర్ఫేస్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eకర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E బెజియర్, NURBS మరియు సరళరేఖ కర్వ్‌లు ట్యూబ్, బ్యాండ్, టేపర్ లేదా నింపిన ప్లేట్‌ను, మరియు కర్వ్‌గా టెక్స్ట్‌ను తయారు చేస్తాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవక్రరేఖ\u003C/span\u003E వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో వాటి పాయింట్లను సవరించండి; ఆకారం సరైనప్పుడు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెష్‌కు\u003C/span\u003E. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/curves\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/curves\"\u003Eకర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUV వర్క్‌స్పేస్.\u003C/strong\u003E సీమ్‌లను గుర్తించండి (లేదా పదునైన అంచుల వద్ద ModelCraft గుర్తించనివ్వండి), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅన్‌రాప్\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యాక్\u003C/span\u003E చేయండి, లేదా బాక్స్, సిలిండర్, గోళం లేదా వీక్షణ ద్వారా ప్రొజెక్ట్ చేయండి. 3D వీక్షణ పక్కన ఉన్న UV ఎడిటర్ ముక్కలను కదిలిస్తుంది, తిప్పుతుంది మరియు స్కేల్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ఒక చెక్కర్ మరియు స్ట్రెచ్ రంగులు ఫలితాన్ని చూపుతాయి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/uv\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/uv\"\u003Eమెష్ UVలను అమర్చడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eశిల్పం.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eశిల్పం\u003C/span\u003E వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో డ్రా, క్లే, స్మూత్, గ్రాబ్, ఇన్‌ఫ్లేట్, క్రీజ్, పించ్, ఫ్లాటెన్ మరియు మాస్క్ బ్రష్‌లు ఉన్నాయి, X అంతటా మిర్రరింగ్, మరియు మీరు శిల్పం చేసే చోట త్రిభుజాలను జోడించే \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవివరం\u003C/span\u003E. పెన్ ఒత్తిడి బ్రష్ పరిమాణం మరియు బలాన్ని సెట్ చేస్తుంది. ప్రతి స్ట్రోక్ ఒక అన్‌డూ దశ. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sculpt\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sculpt\"\u003Eమెష్‌ను శిల్పం చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరీమెష్ మరియు రీటోపాలజీ.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరీమెష్\u003C/span\u003E మొత్తం మెష్‌ను సమాన త్రిభుజాలుగా మళ్ళీ చేస్తుంది, రంధ్రాలను మూసివేసి అతివ్యాప్తి చెందుతున్న ముక్కలను కలుపుతుంది. వివరమైన మెష్‌పై శుభ్రమైన మెష్ నిర్మించడానికి, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరీటోపాలజీ\u003C/span\u003E కింద దాన్ని ఎంచుకోండి: మీరు కదిపే పాయింట్లు దాని ఉపరితలంపై చేరతాయి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit#remesh\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-hash=\"remesh\"\u003Eరీమెష్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఅంచు లేదా కర్వ్‌ను విభజించడం.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిభజించు\u003C/span\u003E టూల్ మీరు అంచు వెంట కదిలేటప్పుడు కోతలను గుర్తిస్తుంది మరియు ఒక క్లిక్ లేదా టైప్ చేసిన సంఖ్యతో విభజిస్తుంది; అంచు, ఆర్క్ లేదా వృత్తం ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు \u003Ccode\u003E/5\u003C/code\u003E టైప్ చేయండి; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంటిటీ సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిభజించు…\u003C/span\u003E టాబ్లెట్‌ల కోసం సంఖ్యను అడుగుతుంది. ఆర్క్ లేదా వృత్తం నిజమైన ఆర్క్‌పై సమాన కోణాల ద్వారా విభజించబడుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/divide\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/divide\"\u003Eఅంచు లేదా కర్వ్‌ను సమాన భాగాలుగా విభజించడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవిలువ పెట్టెలో లెక్కలు.\u003C/strong\u003E ఏ పొడవు కోసమైనా \u003Ccode\u003E2.5/3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;+3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E లేదా \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E, మరియు కోణం కోసం \u003Ccode\u003E90/2\u003C/code\u003E టైప్ చేయండి. చేయలేని లెక్క ఏమి తప్పు మరియు ఎక్కడ తప్పు అని చెబుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box#calculations\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\" data-hash=\"calculations\"\u003Eలెక్కలు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eరెండరింగ్\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eగ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై పాత్ ట్రేసింగ్.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిత్రం రెండర్ చేయి…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅధిక నాణ్యత (పాత్ ట్రేస్డ్)\u003C/span\u003E బ్రౌజర్‌కు WebGPU ఉన్న చోట CadCraft యొక్క స్వంత పాత్ ట్రేసర్‌ను గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై నడుపుతుంది; చిత్రం కింద ఉన్న లైన్ ఏ ఇంజిన్ దాన్ని తయారు చేసిందో చెబుతుంది. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆపు\u003C/span\u003E చిత్రాన్ని ఉంచుతుంది, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొనసాగించు\u003C/span\u003E అక్కడి నుండి కొనసాగిస్తుంది. WebGPU లేకుండా, చిత్రం ముందులాగే WebGL తో పాత్ ట్రేస్ చేయబడుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#high-quality\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"high-quality\"\u003Eఅధిక నాణ్యత\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవీక్షణలో రెండర్.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరెండర్\u003C/span\u003E వర్క్‌స్పేస్ వీక్షణను స్వయంగా పాత్ ట్రేస్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు మీరు ఆర్బిట్ చేసినప్పుడు లేదా సవరించినప్పుడు మళ్ళీ మొదలుపెడుతుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రాంతం\u003C/span\u003E ను రెండర్ చేయండి, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫోకస్\u003C/span\u003E చేయడానికి క్లిక్ చేయండి, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవివరం\u003C/span\u003E సెట్ చేయండి. దానికి WebGPU అవసరం. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#render-in-the-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"render-in-the-view\"\u003Eవీక్షణలో రెండర్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమెటీరియల్‌లు, లైట్లు మరియు ఆకాశాలు.\u003C/strong\u003E అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలు ప్రతి ఉపరితలాన్ని OpenPBR మెటీరియల్ మోడల్‌తో షేడ్ చేస్తాయి (కోట్‌లు, ఫజ్, థిన్ ఫిల్మ్‌లు, రంగు గాజు, గ్లో). WebGPU తో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eభౌతిక ఆకాశం\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపొగమంచు\u003C/span\u003E ఉంటుంది, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆకాశ చిత్రం (HDRI)\u003C/span\u003E కూడా, మరియు లాంప్‌ల కోసం తయారీదారుల IES లైట్ ప్రొఫైల్‌లు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#skies-and-light-profiles-on-the-graphics-card\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"skies-and-light-profiles-on-the-graphics-card\"\u003Eఆకాశాలు మరియు లైట్ ప్రొఫైల్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఫోటోగ్రాఫ్ లాంటి కెమెరా.\u003C/strong\u003E ప్రతి మోడ్‌లో f-స్టాప్, షటర్ మరియు ISO నుండి ఎక్స్‌పోజర్; WebGPU తో డెప్త్ ఆఫ్ ఫీల్డ్, రెండు-పాయింట్ పర్స్‌పెక్టివ్, వైట్ బ్యాలెన్స్, 360° పనోరమాలు (స్టీరియోలో కూడా) మరియు క్యూబ్ మ్యాప్‌లు కూడా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#the-camera\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"the-camera\"\u003Eకెమెరా\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరెండర్ తర్వాత చిత్రాన్ని పూర్తి చేయండి.\u003C/strong\u003E దానికి గ్రేడ్ ఇవ్వండి (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EACES ఫిల్మిక్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫిల్మిక్\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రామాణికం\u003C/span\u003E, కాంట్రాస్ట్, బ్లూమ్, గ్లేర్, విగ్నెట్, \u003Ccode\u003E.cube\u003C/code\u003E కలర్ టేబుల్), ఆకాశం, సూర్యుడు మరియు ప్రతి లాంప్ గ్రూప్‌ను కలపండి, మరియు రెండర్ ఎలిమెంట్లను ఒక్కొక్కటిగా చూడండి, అన్నీ మళ్ళీ రెండర్ చేయకుండా. మిగిలిన గ్రెయిన్‌ను మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌లోని డెనాయిజర్ తొలగిస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#finish-the-picture\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"finish-the-picture\"\u003Eచిత్రాన్ని పూర్తి చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#mix-the-lights-after-the-render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"mix-the-lights-after-the-render\"\u003Eరెండర్ తర్వాత లైట్లను కలపడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరెండర్‌ను సేవ్ చేయడానికి మరిన్ని మార్గాలు.\u003C/strong\u003E 16-బిట్ PNG, ప్రతి రెండర్ ఎలిమెంట్ మరియు లైట్ గ్రూప్‌తో మల్టీ-లేయర్ EXR, మరియు దాని మిక్స్ మరియు గ్రేడ్‌తో మళ్ళీ తెరుచుకునే రెండర్ ఫైల్ (\u003Ccode\u003E.ccrender\u003C/code\u003E). చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#save-the-picture\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"save-the-picture\"\u003Eచిత్రాన్ని సేవ్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eపొగమంచు, పొగ మరియు కాస్టిక్స్.\u003C/strong\u003E సూర్యుడు మరియు లాంప్‌లచే ప్రకాశించే పొగమంచు మరియు హేజ్, లైట్ షాఫ్ట్‌లతో; ఒక పొగ స్తంభం లేదా మేఘం; గాజు మరియు అద్దాల కింద కాస్టిక్స్; ఇప్పటికే శుభ్రంగా ఉన్న పిక్సెల్స్‌పై ఆగే అడాప్టివ్ సాంప్లింగ్. గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై మాత్రమే. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#fog-smoke-noise-and-caustics\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"fog-smoke-noise-and-caustics\"\u003Eపొగమంచు, పొగ, నాయిస్ మరియు కాస్టిక్స్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరెండర్ క్యూ మరియు MP4.\u003C/strong\u003E అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలు మరియు మొత్తం యానిమేషన్లను \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరెండర్ క్యూ\u003C/span\u003E కు జోడించండి; అవి ఒకదాని తర్వాత ఒకటి రెండర్ అవుతాయి, మరియు పేజీ మూసివేసినా క్యూ ఉంటుంది. దానికి WebGPU అవసరం. బ్రౌజర్ రాయగలిగితే వీడియోలను MP4 గా కూడా సేవ్ చేయవచ్చు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#the-render-queue\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"the-render-queue\"\u003Eరెండర్ క్యూ\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation#render-the-video\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\" data-hash=\"render-the-video\"\u003Eవీడియో రెండర్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eలైవ్ వ్యూ మరియు ప్రెజెంటేషన్‌లు\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eలైవ్ వ్యూ.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలైవ్ వీక్షణ\u003C/span\u003E మోడల్‌ను వీక్షణ పక్కన రియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో చూపుతుంది, ప్రతి సవరణ మరియు కెమెరాను అనుసరిస్తుంది: సూర్య నీడలు, మెటీరియల్‌లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు గోడలపై తిరిగి పడే కాంతి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనడక\u003C/span\u003E ద్వారా దానిలో నడవండి, సూర్యుడిని కదపడానికి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసమయం\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనెల\u003C/span\u003E లాగండి, లేదా దాన్ని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండో\u003C/span\u003E కు తరలించండి. బ్రౌజర్‌కు ఉన్నప్పుడు ఇది WebGPU ను, లేకపోతే WebGL 2 ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\"\u003Eలైవ్ వ్యూ\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eస్కాటర్.\u003C/strong\u003E గడ్డి, పువ్వులు, రాళ్లు, చెట్లు మరియు మనుషులను ముఖాలపై లేదా బ్రష్‌తో చెదరగొట్టండి, ఏడు కొత్త ఎంటూరేజ్ అంశాలు మరియు లైబ్రరీలోని మిగిలిన వాటి నుండి; సెట్‌లు ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ అవుతాయి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view#scatter-plants-rocks-and-people\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\" data-hash=\"scatter-plants-rocks-and-people\"\u003Eమొక్కలు, రాళ్లు మరియు మనుషులను స్కాటర్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eషేర్.\u003C/strong\u003E 360° పనోరమా, స్పాట్‌ల మధ్య బాణాలతో పనోరమా టూర్, స్వతంత్ర వ్యూయర్ (ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో తెరుచుకుని ఇంటర్నెట్ నుండి ఏమీ లోడ్ చేయని ఒకే వెబ్ పేజీ), మరియు కెమెరా మార్గం యొక్క వీడియో, అన్నీ రియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view#share-panoramas-tours-a-viewer-a-video\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\" data-hash=\"share-panoramas-tours-a-viewer-a-video\"\u003Eషేర్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో VR.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVRలో సమీక్షించు\u003C/span\u003E మోడల్‌ను దాని మెటీరియల్‌లు, ఆకాశం, సూర్య నీడ, లాంప్‌లు, చెదరగొట్టిన అంశాలు మరియు బౌన్స్ కాంతితో చూపిస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/vr-review#what-it-looks-like\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/vr-review\" data-hash=\"what-it-looks-like\"\u003Eఎలా ఉంటుంది\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eమెటీరియల్‌లు\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eనోడ్‌లతో చేసిన మెటీరియల్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eషేడింగ్\u003C/span\u003E వర్క్‌స్పేస్ ఫైల్ యొక్క స్వంత మెటీరియల్‌లను నోడ్ గ్రాఫ్‌లుగా నిర్మిస్తుంది: చిత్రాలు, నాయిస్ మరియు చెక్కర్లు, వొరోనాయ్ కణాలు, అలలు, ఇటుకలు, టైల్స్, కలప, పాలరాయి, గ్రేడియంట్‌లు, మట్టి మరియు అంచు అరుగుదల, రంగు పని, గణితం, బంప్ మరియు నార్మల్ మ్యాప్‌లు, పూర్తి OpenPBR మెటీరియల్‌లోకి. వీక్షణ ప్రతి మార్పును ఒకేసారి చూపుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\"\u003Eనోడ్‌లతో మెటీరియల్‌లు తయారు చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమెటీరియల్ లైబ్రరీ మరియు మీ స్వంత టెక్చర్ సెట్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E షేడింగ్ ప్యానెల్ లైబ్రరీ మెటీరియల్‌లను జాబితా చేస్తుంది: పదహారు CC0 టెక్చర్ సెట్‌లు, ప్రతిదానికి క్రెడిట్. ఉపయోగించడానికి ఒకదానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఒక ముఖంపై లాగండి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిత్రాలు లేదా జిప్…\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఒక ఫోల్డర్…\u003C/span\u003E మీ స్వంత మ్యాప్‌ల నుండి మెటీరియల్‌ను తయారు చేస్తుంది, ఫైల్ పేర్ల ద్వారా కనుగొనబడుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#materials-from-the-library\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"materials-from-the-library\"\u003Eలైబ్రరీ నుండి మెటీరియల్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterialX లోపలికి మరియు బయటికి.\u003C/strong\u003E మెటీరియల్‌ను MaterialX గా ఎగుమతి చేయండి (OpenPBR Surface లేదా Standard Surface, టెక్చర్‌లు PNG గా), మరియు MaterialX ఫైళ్లను వాటి చిత్రాలతో దిగుమతి చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#materialx\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"materialx\"\u003EMaterialX\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eడికాల్స్ మరియు టెక్చర్ పెయింటింగ్.\u003C/strong\u003E ఉపరితలంపై చిత్రాన్ని దాని స్వంత మెటీరియల్‌తో డికాల్‌గా ఉంచండి, మరియు ఒక గుండ్రని బ్రష్‌తో మెటీరియల్ చిత్రంపై పెయింట్ చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#decals\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"decals\"\u003Eడికాల్స్\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#paint-on-the-texture\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"paint-on-the-texture\"\u003Eటెక్చర్‌పై పెయింట్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eటెక్చర్ బేకింగ్.\u003C/strong\u003E యాంబియంట్ ఆక్లూజన్, వక్రత, లైటింగ్ మరియు వివరమైన ఆబ్జెక్ట్ యొక్క నార్మల్‌లను మెష్ UVల వెంట చిత్రాలుగా బేక్ చేయండి, కంప్యూటర్ ప్రాసెసర్ కోర్లపై. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#bake-textures\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"bake-textures\"\u003Eటెక్చర్‌లను బేక్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-7\"\u003Eయానిమేషన్\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eయానిమేట్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్.\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక ఆబ్జెక్ట్ యొక్క స్థానం, తిరుగుదల మరియు పరిమాణం, ప్రతి లాంప్ యొక్క ప్రకాశం, వ్యాప్తి మరియు రంగు, మరియు లెన్స్ కోణాన్ని కీలుగా ఉంచండి, నేరుగా ప్యానెల్‌లోనే. టైమ్‌లైన్‌కు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవక్రాలు\u003C/span\u003E వీక్షణ (బెజియర్ హ్యాండిల్స్, పునరావృతాలు) మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eక్లిప్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E వీక్షణ వస్తాయి, ఇది కీలను మళ్ళీ ఉంచడానికి మరియు మిక్స్ చేయడానికి చర్యలుగా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; అనేక వరుసలపై కీలను తరలించవచ్చు, స్ట్రెచ్ చేయవచ్చు, కాపీ చేయవచ్చు మరియు పేస్ట్ చేయవచ్చు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\"\u003Eఏ ప్రాపర్టీనైనా యానిమేట్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eషేప్ కీలు మరియు డ్రైవర్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E మెష్ తన స్వంత ఇతర ఆకారాలను కలిగి ఉంటుంది, కీ చేయగల స్లైడర్‌ల ద్వారా కలిపి, లేదా ఒక చిన్న వ్యక్తీకరణ ద్వారా ఇతర విలువల నుండి సెట్ చేయబడి ఉంటుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate#shape-keys-morphs\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\" data-hash=\"shape-keys-morphs\"\u003Eషేప్ కీలు\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate#drivers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\" data-hash=\"drivers\"\u003Eడ్రైవర్లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఆర్మేచర్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E మెష్‌లో బోన్‌లు ఉంచండి, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eస్వయంచాలక వెయిట్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E పాయింట్లను వాటి మధ్య పంచనివ్వండి లేదా వెయిట్‌లను పెయింట్ చేయండి, మరియు బోన్‌లను పోజ్ చేసి కీ చేయండి; లీనియర్ లేదా డ్యూయల్ క్వాటర్నియన్ స్కిన్నింగ్. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate#armatures-bones-that-bend-a-mesh\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\" data-hash=\"armatures-bones-that-bend-a-mesh\"\u003Eఆర్మేచర్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIK మరియు కన్‌స్ట్రెయింట్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E పోల్‌తో రీచ్ (IK), లుక్ ఎట్, ట్రాన్స్‌ఫారమ్‌లను కాపీ చేయడం, పరిమితులు, చైల్డ్ ఆఫ్ మరియు ఫాలో పాత్, బోన్‌లు మరియు ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌ల కోసం. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate#ik-and-constraints\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\" data-hash=\"ik-and-constraints\"\u003EIK మరియు కన్‌స్ట్రెయింట్లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఫిజిక్స్.\u003C/strong\u003E గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు బాక్స్‌లు లేదా బంతులుగా పడతాయి, పేరుకుపోతాయి మరియు ఢీకొంటాయి, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫిజిక్స్ బేక్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఫలితాన్ని కీలుగా ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate#physics-drop-stack-and-collide\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\" data-hash=\"physics-drop-stack-and-collide\"\u003Eఫిజిక్స్\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-8\"\u003Eఫైళ్లు\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUSD.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఇలా సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) మోడల్‌ను \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E లేదా \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ModelCraft సేవ్ చేసిన USD ఫైల్ ఏదీ కోల్పోకుండా మళ్ళీ తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల USD మెష్‌లు, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు కెమెరాలుగా తెరుచుకుంటుంది లేదా దిగుమతి అవుతుంది. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఇప్పటికీ \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E రాస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD ను సేవ్ చేయడం, తెరవడం మరియు ఎగుమతి చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EglTF, పూర్తిగా.\u003C/strong\u003E glTF ఫైళ్లు వాటి నార్మల్‌లు, UVs, వర్టెక్స్ రంగులు మరియు టెక్చర్‌లతో సవరించగలిగే మెష్‌లుగా వస్తాయి, నోడ్‌లు గ్రూప్‌లుగా, లైట్లు లాంప్‌లుగా, మరియు కెమెరాలు దృశ్యాలుగా; Draco మరియు meshopt ఫైళ్లు కూడా. \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E దాని ఫైళ్లతో కలిపి వదిలినప్పుడు తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ఎగుమతి మెష్‌లు, టెక్చర్‌లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు కెమెరాలు రాస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#gltf-glb-gltf\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"gltf-glb-gltf\"\u003EglTF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFBX, PLY, STL మరియు Alembic దిగుమతులు.\u003C/strong\u003E FBX 2011 మరియు కొత్తవి (మెష్‌లు, UV సెట్‌లు, మెటీరియల్‌లు, ఎంబెడెడ్ టెక్చర్‌లు, లైట్లు, కెమెరాలు), వాటి రంగులతో PLY మరియు STL, మరియు Alembic కాష్‌లు వాటి మొదటి నమూనాలో. OBJ దాని \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E మరియు చిత్రాలతో వస్తుంది, మీరు వాటిని కలిపి వదిలినప్పుడు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003Eఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమరిన్ని ఎగుమతులు.\u003C/strong\u003E రంగులతో PLY, పెయింట్ చేసిన ముఖాల రంగులతో STL, మరియు దాని టెక్చర్‌లతో OBJ ఒకే zip గా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003Eఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTEP ఫైళ్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E STEP (AP203, AP214, AP242) భాగాలు మరియు అసెంబ్లీలను వాటి నిజమైన పరిమాణంలో మూసిన మెష్‌లుగా చదువుతుంది, ప్రతి భాగం ఫైల్ దాన్ని ఉంచిన చోట. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#step-step-stp\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"step-step-stp\"\u003ESTEP\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E.3dm ఫైళ్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E మోడల్‌లను చదువుతుంది: మెష్‌లు, పాలీసర్ఫేస్‌లు మరియు ఎక్స్‌ట్రూజన్‌లు (ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ చేసిన మెష్‌ల నుండి, లేకపోతే ModelCraft టెస్సలేట్ చేస్తుంది), వక్రాలు అంచులుగా, బ్లాక్‌లు కాంపోనెంట్లుగా మరియు లేయర్‌లు ట్యాగ్‌లుగా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-3dm-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-3dm-files\"\u003E.3dm మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG మరియు DXF నుండి 3D సాలిడ్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E డ్రాయింగ్‌ను దిగుమతి చేయడం ఇప్పుడు దాని 3D సాలిడ్‌లు, రీజియన్‌లు మరియు బాడీలను కూడా తెస్తుంది, ప్రతిదీ దాని స్వంత గ్రూప్‌గా, మూసినవి ఘనపదార్థాలుగా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan#what-comes-in\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\" data-hash=\"what-comes-in\"\u003Eసైట్ ప్లాన్‌పై మోడల్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"3d\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eప్రింట్ టూల్స్.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003Eప్రింట్ టూల్స్\u003C/strong\u003E లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసపోర్ట్‌ల ప్రివ్యూ\u003C/span\u003E ఉంది (ఓవర్‌హాంగ్‌లు, స్తంభాలు లేదా చెట్టు సపోర్ట్‌లు, ఎంత మెటీరియల్ అనే అంచనా), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగోడ మందం\u003C/span\u003E (ప్రింట్ చేయడానికి చాలా సన్నని గోడలు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచూపించు\u003C/span\u003E తో జాబితా), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eబోలు\u003C/span\u003E (మీరు ఇచ్చిన మందం గోడలు, డ్రెయిన్ రంధ్రాలతో) మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమరమ్మతు\u003C/span\u003E (ప్రతి పరిష్కారం జాబితా; అతివ్యాప్తి భాగాలు మీరు కోరినప్పుడు మాత్రమే కలుస్తాయి). బోలు మరియు మరమ్మతు, వాటి ఫ్లాట్ వైపులు ఒకే ముఖాలుగా ఉండే గ్రూప్‌ను ఇస్తాయి, లేదా ఫలితం మూసుకోనప్పుడు లేదా ముఖాలు చాలా ఎక్కువైనప్పుడు మెష్‌ను ఇస్తాయి; స్టేటస్ లైన్ ఏది చెబుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\"\u003Eసపోర్ట్‌లు, గోడ మందం, బోలుగా చేయడం మరియు మరమ్మతు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eప్రారంభకుడు శైలి.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రారంభకుడు\u003C/span\u003E: ఆకారాలు మరియు రంధ్రాలను వదలడానికి, తరలించడానికి, అమర్చడానికి మరియు రంధ్రాలు కోయేలా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్\u003C/span\u003E చేయడానికి పెద్ద బటన్లు, తర్వాత ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను తనిఖీ చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/beginner\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/beginner\"\u003Eప్రారంభకుడు శైలి\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ai\"\u003EAI సహాయకులు మరియు మీ యంత్రం\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAI సహాయకుల కోసం మరిన్ని.\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక సహాయకుడు ఇప్పుడు ముఖాలు మరియు మెటీరియల్‌లను జాబితా చేయగలడు, push/pull చేయగలడు, పెయింట్ చేయగలడు, గ్రూప్‌లు చేయగలడు, దృశ్యాలను జోడించి చూపగలడు, మెష్‌లు జోడించగలడు, మాడిఫైయర్ స్టాక్‌లను సవరించగలడు, USD ఎగుమతి చేయగలడు, చిత్రం రెండర్ చేయగలడు మరియు రెండర్ క్యూ నింపగలడు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\"\u003EModelCraft ను AI సహాయకులతో ఉపయోగించడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమీ యంత్రం.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరెండరింగ్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమీ యంత్రం\u003C/span\u003E ఈ బ్రౌజర్ యాప్‌కు అనుమతించే గ్రాఫిక్స్, ప్రాసెసర్ కోర్లు మరియు మెమరీని చూపుతుంది, మరియు లైవ్ వ్యూ ఎక్కడ ప్రారంభమవుతుందో సెట్ చేసే స్థాయిని. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/your-machine\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/your-machine\"\u003Eమీ యంత్రం ఎలా రెండర్ చేస్తుంది\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-9\"\u003Eపరిష్కరించినవి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసాలిడ్స్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eయూనియన్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతీసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఖండన\u003C/span\u003E ఏ పరిమాణంలోనైనా మూసి లేని గ్రూప్‌ను ఇవ్వవచ్చు. ఇప్పుడు అవి మూసివేస్తాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసాలిడ్స్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతీసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఏదీ ఎంచుకోకుండా బూడిద రంగులో ఉండేది, అయినప్పటికీ మీరు దాన్ని ఎంచుకున్న తర్వాత అది దాని రెండు ముక్కలను వంతులవారీగా ఎంచుకుంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eయానిమేషన్ టైమ్‌లైన్ లోపల, టైప్ చేసిన కీలు ఎక్కడికీ వెళ్ళలేదు, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E కూడా. అక్కడ \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E టైమ్‌లైన్ స్వంత అన్‌డూ మరియు రీడూ; ఇతర \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-10\"\u003Eఅన్ని యాప్‌లలో\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eయూనిట్లు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eయూనిట్లు\u003C/span\u003E పొడవు, వైశాల్యం, ఘనపరిమాణం, కోణం మరియు వాలు కోసం ఒక్కో జాబితా కలిగి ఉంది, యూనిట్ల నిర్వచనాలు ఇచ్చే ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలతో (US సర్వే అడుగు దాని స్వంత యూనిట్). మీరు ఎప్పుడూ ఉపయోగించని యూనిట్లను ఆఫ్ చేయండి, అవి యూనిట్ జాబితాల నుండి బయటకు వెళ్తాయి; ఒకదాన్ని స్టార్ చేస్తే కొత్త పని దానిలో మొదలవుతుంది. ఫైల్ ఎల్లప్పుడూ తన స్వంత యూనిట్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. ModelCraft లో విలువ పెట్టె పక్కన ఉన్న యూనిట్ జాబితా ఈ స్విచ్‌లను అనుసరిస్తుంది (మోడల్ స్వంత యూనిట్ ఎల్లప్పుడూ అందులోనే ఉంటుంది), స్టార్ కొత్త మోడల్ యూనిట్‌ను సెట్ చేస్తుంది (ModelCraft ఆ యూనిట్ కలిగి ఉంటే), మరియు ఏ యూనిట్ గుర్తుతోనైనా టైప్ చేసిన పొడవు ఆ యూనిట్‌లో చదవబడుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003Eయూనిట్లు, టైప్ చేసిన విలువలు మరియు యూనిట్ జాబితాలు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమరింత సాంద్రమైన లేఅవుట్ మరియు సర్దుబాటయ్యే ప్యానెల్‌లు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ సాంద్రత\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపాక్ట్\u003C/span\u003E స్క్రీన్‌పై ఎక్కువ అమర్చుతుంది (టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌లు మరియు ఫోన్‌లు తమ పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతాయి). ప్రతి సైడ్ ప్యానెల్‌కు సన్నని శీర్షిక బార్ ఉంటుంది: దాన్ని లాగి ప్యానెల్‌ను డాక్ లేదా ఫ్లోట్ చేయండి, మరో ప్యానెల్ బార్‌పై వదిలి ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ఒకే ఫ్రేమ్‌ను పంచుకోండి, అంచుకు జారిపోయేలా పిన్ చేయండి, లేదా దాచి అంచు నుండి తిరిగి తీసుకురండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003Eప్యానెల్‌లను అమర్చడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eడైలాగ్‌లు కదులుతాయి.\u003C/strong\u003E డ్రాయింగ్‌కు అడ్డు తప్పించడానికి డైలాగ్‌ను దాని శీర్షిక బార్ ద్వారా లాగండి; మీరు వదిలిన చోటికి అది తిరిగి వస్తుంది. మళ్ళీ మధ్యలోకి తీసుకురావడానికి శీర్షిక బార్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003Eడైలాగ్‌ను తరలించడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAI సహాయకుల కోసం వంటకాలు.\u003C/strong\u003E మీ స్వంత సహాయకుడి కోసం పది పని చేసిన అభ్యర్థనలు (డ్రాయింగ్ చక్కదిద్దడం, సెట్ ప్లాట్ చేయడం, డ్రాయింగ్‌లు తనిఖీ చేయడం, మోడల్‌ను ప్రశ్నలు అడగడం, టేకాఫ్ మొత్తం చేయడం, పార్ట్ వేరియంట్ ప్రయత్నించడం...), ప్రతిదీ దాని పిలిచే టూల్స్ మరియు అవసరమైన అనుమతులతో, మరియు ప్రతిదీ మా పరీక్షల ద్వారా మళ్ళీ ప్లే చేయబడుతుంది. యాప్‌లలో AI లేదు. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003EAI సహాయకుల కోసం వంటకాలు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eభాషలు.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E లో కొత్తది. అది, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E మరియు \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E ఇప్పుడు యాప్‌ల ప్రతి పదాన్ని కలిగి ఉన్నాయి, స్థానిక వక్త సమీక్షించని ముసాయిదాలుగా. సహాయ అంశాలు ఇంగ్లీషులో ఉన్నాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E అప్‌డేట్ తనిఖీ ఎందుకు విఫలమైందో చెబుతుంది (సిస్టమ్‌కు ఫైల్ ఇంకా లేదు, నెట్‌వర్క్ లేదు, చెడ్డ సంతకం) &quot;పూర్తయింది&quot; అని మాత్రమే కాకుండా, మీరు మూసిన &quot;కొత్తవి ఏమిటి&quot; కార్డ్‌ను కొత్త విడుదల వచ్చే వరకు తిరిగి తీసుకురాదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-11\"\u003Eఈ వెర్షన్‌లో ఇంకా లేనివి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0.7 కోడ్-సైన్ చేయబడలేదు.\u003C/strong\u003E సైనింగ్, మరియు Microsoft Store ప్యాకేజీ 0.8 కి మారాయి, ఎందుకంటే సర్టిఫికేట్ ధృవీకరణ ఇంకా రాలేదు. getcadcraft.com/download పై ప్రతి ఫైల్ పక్కన ఉన్న SHA-256 తనిఖీ చేయండి. Windows లో SmartScreen ఇన్‌స్టాలర్‌ను ఆపితే, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమరింత సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకుని, తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఏమైనప్పటికీ రన్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. Mac లో, మొదటిసారి, CadCraft Studio పై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేసి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతెరువు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. CadCraft Studio ఇంకా యాప్ స్టోర్‌లో లేదు, మరియు ఇంకా iPad లేదా iPhone బిల్డ్ లేదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eయూనిట్లు\u003C/span\u003E లోని కోణం మరియు వాలు జాబితాలు సేవ్ అవుతాయి, కానీ ఇంకా ఏ బాక్స్ వాటిని చదవదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eగ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై రెండరింగ్ (WebGPU) ఇక్కడ కేవలం సాఫ్ట్‌వేర్ స్టాండ్-ఇన్‌తో పరీక్షించబడింది, ఇంకా నిజమైన గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌లపై కాదు, కాబట్టి ముందుగా మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌లో ఏదైనా చిన్నది రెండర్ చేయండి. లైవ్ వ్యూ కూడా అంతే, మరియు VR ఇంకా హెడ్‌సెట్ ఎమ్యులేటర్‌తో మాత్రమే ప్రయత్నించబడింది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWebGPU లేకుండా రెండర్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేదా రెండర్ క్యూ ఉండదు, మరియు అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలకు డెప్త్ ఆఫ్ ఫీల్డ్, భౌతిక ఆకాశం, ఆకాశ చిత్రం, పొగమంచు, పొగ, కాస్టిక్స్ లేదా లైట్ మిక్సింగ్ ఉండవు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిత్రం రెండర్ చేయి…\u003C/span\u003E ఇంకా చెదరగొట్టిన అంశాలను గీయదు; అవి లైవ్ వ్యూ, దాని పనోరమాలు, వ్యూయర్ మరియు వీడియోలో కనిపిస్తాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eలాంప్‌లు వీక్షణలో యానిమేట్ అవుతాయి, కానీ రెండర్ చేసిన వీడియో ప్రారంభమైనప్పుడు ఉన్న లాంప్‌లను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఎముకలు మరియు యానిమేషన్ ఇంకా glTF, FBX లేదా USD ఫైళ్ల నుండి చదవబడవు లేదా వాటికి రాయబడవు (స్కిన్ చేసిన మోడల్‌లు వాటి వెయిట్‌లతో విశ్రాంతి స్థితిలో వస్తాయి), మరియు base color లేదా టెక్చర్ దాటి glTF మరియు FBX మెటీరియల్‌లు ఇంకా ఉంచబడవు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESTEP రంగులు మరియు అసెంబ్లీ యొక్క భాగాల పేర్లు ఇంకా చదవబడవు. \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E దిగుమతి ఫార్మాట్ యొక్క టూల్‌కిట్ రాసిన ఫైళ్లతో పరీక్షించబడింది, మోడలింగ్ ప్రోగ్రామ్ నుండి వచ్చిన ఫైళ్లతో ఇంకా కాదు; SubD ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు, టెక్స్ట్ మరియు డైమెన్షన్‌లు వదిలివేయబడ్డాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECivilCraft నుండి మొక్కల లైబ్రరీ ModelCraft లో ఉంది, కానీ దాని నుండి మొక్కలను ఉంచడం ఇంకా నిర్మించబడలేదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపుష్/పుల్ ముఖాలపై టెక్చర్ పెయింట్ చేయడం మెటీరియల్ టైల్‌తో పునరావృతమవుతుంది; ఒక ముఖాన్ని మాత్రమే పెయింట్ చేయడానికి, దాన్ని మెష్‌గా మార్చి అన్‌రాప్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇంకా మిగిలినవి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003Eఇంకా నిర్మించబడలేదు\u003C/a\u003E లో ఉన్నాయి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft 0.6 తో పోలిస్తే 0.7 లో ఏమి మారిందో ఇది. ModelCraft ఇప్పుడు నెట్టు/లాగు జ్యామితితో పాటు మెష్‌లను కూడా మోడల్ చేస్తుంది, గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై రెండర్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ప్రతి రకమైన పనికి ఒక వర్క్‌స్పేస్ కలిగి ఉంది: టూల్‌బార్‌కు ఎడమవైపు ఉన్న వర్క్‌స్పేస్ జాబితా నుండి దాన్ని ఎంచుకోండి. కొత్తవి మెష్‌లు మెష్‌లు. ఒక గ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్‌ను ఎంచుకుని ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం లో మెష్‌గా మార్చు పై క్లిక్ చేయండి: అది ఏ సంఖ్యలోనైనా మూలలున్న ముఖాలతో మెష్ అవుతుంది, అవి టెక్చర్ కోఆర్డినేట్లు, నార్మల్‌లు, రంగులు మరియు వెయిట్‌లను కలిగి ఉండగలవు. జ్యామితిగా మార్చు దాన్ని తిరిగి మారుస్తుంది. మెష్‌పై అన్‌డూ ప్రతి దశ మార్చినదాన్ని మాత్రమే ఉంచుతుంది. మెష్ ఉన్న ఫైల్‌ను తెరవడానికి ModelCraft 0.7 అవసరం. చూడండి మెష్‌లు. మెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్. ఒక మెష్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా వర్క్‌స్పేస్ జాబితాలో మెష్ ఎంచుకోండి, దాని శీర్షాలు, అంచులు మరియు ముఖాలను సవరించడానికి: లూప్‌లు మరియు రింగ్‌లు, అనుపాత సవరణ, ఎక్స్‌ట్రూడ్, ఇన్‌సెట్, బెవెల్, లూప్ కట్, నైఫ్, బైసెక్ట్, స్పిన్, మెర్జ్, డిజాల్వ్, ఫిల్, గ్రిడ్ ఫిల్, బ్రిడ్జ్ మరియు స్లైడ్; చివరి ఆపరేషన్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు తర్వాత ప్యానెల్‌లో మార్చబడతాయి. ప్లేన్, క్యూబ్, గోళం, టోరస్ మరియు ఇతరాల నుండి ప్రారంభించండి. నెట్టు/లాగు కీలు మరియు మోడెలర్ కీలు రెండూ పనిచేస్తాయి. చూడండి మెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో మెష్‌ను సవరించడం. మాడిఫైయర్లు. మిర్రర్, అర్రే, సబ్‌డివిజన్ సర్ఫేస్, బెవెల్, సాలిడిఫై, బూలియన్, డిస్‌ప్లేస్, వెల్డ్, డెసిమేట్, స్మూత్, లాటిస్, కర్వ్ డిఫార్మ్ మరియు ష్రింక్‌ర్యాప్ ఒక మెష్ ఏమి చూపుతుందో మారుస్తాయి, దాని జ్యామితిని మార్చకుండా. వాటిని పేర్చండి, ప్రతిదాన్ని వీక్షణ కోసం మరియు రెండర్‌ల కోసం ఆన్ లేదా ఆఫ్ చేయండి, మరియు శాశ్వతంగా కావాలనుకున్నప్పుడు ఒకదాన్ని వర్తింపజేయి చేయండి. చూడండి మెష్‌పై మాడిఫైయర్లు. క్రీజ్‌లతో సబ్‌డివిజన్. మెష్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లోని క్రీజ్ ఎంచుకున్న అంచులను సబ్‌డివిజన్ ఉపరితలం కింద పదునుగా ఉంచుతుంది. రెండర్‌లు వీక్షణ కంటే ఎక్కువ లెవల్‌లను ఉపయోగించవచ్చు, మరియు పెద్ద మెష్‌పై వీక్షణ తక్కువ లెవల్ చూపుతుంది మరియు అలా చెబుతుంది. చూడండి సబ్‌డివిజన్ సర్ఫేస్. కర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు. బెజియర్, NURBS మరియు సరళరేఖ కర్వ్‌లు ట్యూబ్, బ్యాండ్, టేపర్ లేదా నింపిన ప్లేట్‌ను, మరియు కర్వ్‌గా టెక్స్ట్‌ను తయారు చేస్తాయి. వక్రరేఖ వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో వాటి పాయింట్లను సవరించండి; ఆకారం సరైనప్పుడు మెష్‌కు. చూడండి కర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు. UV వర్క్‌స్పేస్. సీమ్‌లను గుర్తించండి (లేదా పదునైన అంచుల వద్ద ModelCraft గుర్తించనివ్వండి), అన్‌రాప్ మరియు ప్యాక్ చేయండి, లేదా బాక్స్, సిలిండర్, గోళం లేదా వీక్షణ ద్వారా ప్రొజెక్ట్ చేయండి. 3D వీక్షణ పక్కన ఉన్న UV ఎడిటర్ ముక్కలను కదిలిస్తుంది, తిప్పుతుంది మరియు స్కేల్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ఒక చెక్కర్ మరియు స్ట్రెచ్ రంగులు ఫలితాన్ని చూపుతాయి. చూడండి మెష్ UVలను అమర్చడం. శిల్పం. శిల్పం వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో డ్రా, క్లే, స్మూత్, గ్రాబ్, ఇన్‌ఫ్లేట్, క్రీజ్, పించ్, ఫ్లాటెన్ మరియు మాస్క్ బ్రష్‌లు ఉన్నాయి, X అంతటా మిర్రరింగ్, మరియు మీరు శిల్పం చేసే చోట త్రిభుజాలను జోడించే వివరం. పెన్ ఒత్తిడి బ్రష్ పరిమాణం మరియు బలాన్ని సెట్ చేస్తుంది. ప్రతి స్ట్రోక్ ఒక అన్‌డూ దశ. చూడండి మెష్‌ను శిల్పం చేయడం. రీమెష్ మరియు రీటోపాలజీ. రీమెష్ మొత్తం మెష్‌ను సమాన త్రిభుజాలుగా మళ్ళీ చేస్తుంది, రంధ్రాలను మూసివేసి అతివ్యాప్తి చెందుతున్న ముక్కలను కలుపుతుంది. వివరమైన మెష్‌పై శుభ్రమైన మెష్ నిర్మించడానికి, రీటోపాలజీ కింద దాన్ని ఎంచుకోండి: మీరు కదిపే పాయింట్లు దాని ఉపరితలంపై చేరతాయి. చూడండి రీమెష్. డ్రాయింగ్ అంచు లేదా కర్వ్‌ను విభజించడం. విభజించు టూల్ మీరు అంచు వెంట కదిలేటప్పుడు కోతలను గుర్తిస్తుంది మరియు ఒక క్లిక్ లేదా టైప్ చేసిన సంఖ్యతో విభజిస్తుంది; అంచు, ఆర్క్ లేదా వృత్తం ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు /5 టైప్ చేయండి; ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం › విభజించు… టాబ్లెట్‌ల కోసం సంఖ్యను అడుగుతుంది. ఆర్క్ లేదా వృత్తం నిజమైన ఆర్క్‌పై సమాన కోణాల ద్వారా విభజించబడుతుంది. చూడండి అంచు లేదా కర్వ్‌ను సమాన భాగాలుగా విభజించడం. విలువ పెట్టెలో లెక్కలు. ఏ పొడవు కోసమైనా 2.5/3, 12'6\"+3/4\", (24'-0\")/3 లేదా 2*450mm, మరియు కోణం కోసం 90/2 టైప్ చేయండి. చేయలేని లెక్క ఏమి తప్పు మరియు ఎక్కడ తప్పు అని చెబుతుంది. చూడండి లెక్కలు. రెండరింగ్ గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై పాత్ ట్రేసింగ్. చిత్రం రెండర్ చేయి… › అధిక నాణ్యత (పాత్ ట్రేస్డ్) బ్రౌజర్‌కు WebGPU ఉన్న చోట CadCraft యొక్క స్వంత పాత్ ట్రేసర్‌ను గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై నడుపుతుంది; చిత్రం కింద ఉన్న లైన్ ఏ ఇంజిన్ దాన్ని తయారు చేసిందో చెబుతుంది. ఆపు చిత్రాన్ని ఉంచుతుంది, మరియు కొనసాగించు అక్కడి నుండి కొనసాగిస్తుంది. WebGPU లేకుండా, చిత్రం ముందులాగే WebGL తో పాత్ ట్రేస్ చేయబడుతుంది. చూడండి అధిక నాణ్యత. వీక్షణలో రెండర్. రెండర్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్ వీక్షణను స్వయంగా పాత్ ట్రేస్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు మీరు ఆర్బిట్ చేసినప్పుడు లేదా సవరించినప్పుడు మళ్ళీ మొదలుపెడుతుంది: ప్రాంతం ను రెండర్ చేయండి, ఫోకస్ చేయడానికి క్లిక్ చేయండి, వివరం సెట్ చేయండి. దానికి WebGPU అవసరం. చూడండి వీక్షణలో రెండర్. మెటీరియల్‌లు, లైట్లు మరియు ఆకాశాలు. అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలు ప్రతి ఉపరితలాన్ని OpenPBR మెటీరియల్ మోడల్‌తో షేడ్ చేస్తాయి (కోట్‌లు, ఫజ్, థిన్ ఫిల్మ్‌లు, రంగు గాజు, గ్లో). WebGPU తో భౌతిక ఆకాశం మరియు పొగమంచు ఉంటుంది, ఆకాశ చిత్రం (HDRI) కూడా, మరియు లాంప్‌ల కోసం తయారీదారుల IES లైట్ ప్రొఫైల్‌లు. చూడండి ఆకాశాలు మరియు లైట్ ప్రొఫైల్‌లు. ఫోటోగ్రాఫ్ లాంటి కెమెరా. ప్రతి మోడ్‌లో f-స్టాప్, షటర్ మరియు ISO నుండి ఎక్స్‌పోజర్; WebGPU తో డెప్త్ ఆఫ్ ఫీల్డ్, రెండు-పాయింట్ పర్స్‌పెక్టివ్, వైట్ బ్యాలెన్స్, 360° పనోరమాలు (స్టీరియోలో కూడా) మరియు క్యూబ్ మ్యాప్‌లు కూడా. చూడండి కెమెరా. రెండర్ తర్వాత చిత్రాన్ని పూర్తి చేయండి. దానికి గ్రేడ్ ఇవ్వండి (ACES ఫిల్మిక్, AgX, ఫిల్మిక్ లేదా ప్రామాణికం, కాంట్రాస్ట్, బ్లూమ్, గ్లేర్, విగ్నెట్, .cube కలర్ టేబుల్), ఆకాశం, సూర్యుడు మరియు ప్రతి లాంప్ గ్రూప్‌ను కలపండి, మరియు రెండర్ ఎలిమెంట్లను ఒక్కొక్కటిగా చూడండి, అన్నీ మళ్ళీ రెండర్ చేయకుండా. మిగిలిన గ్రెయిన్‌ను మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌లోని డెనాయిజర్ తొలగిస్తుంది. చూడండి చిత్రాన్ని పూర్తి చేయడం మరియు రెండర్ తర్వాత లైట్లను కలపడం. రెండర్‌ను సేవ్ చేయడానికి మరిన్ని మార్గాలు. 16-బిట్ PNG, ప్రతి రెండర్ ఎలిమెంట్ మరియు లైట్ గ్రూప్‌తో మల్టీ-లేయర్ EXR, మరియు దాని మిక్స్ మరియు గ్రేడ్‌తో మళ్ళీ తెరుచుకునే రెండర్ ఫైల్ (.ccrender). చూడండి చిత్రాన్ని సేవ్ చేయడం. పొగమంచు, పొగ మరియు కాస్టిక్స్. సూర్యుడు మరియు లాంప్‌లచే ప్రకాశించే పొగమంచు మరియు హేజ్, లైట్ షాఫ్ట్‌లతో; ఒక పొగ స్తంభం లేదా మేఘం; గాజు మరియు అద్దాల కింద కాస్టిక్స్; ఇప్పటికే శుభ్రంగా ఉన్న పిక్సెల్స్‌పై ఆగే అడాప్టివ్ సాంప్లింగ్. గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై మాత్రమే. చూడండి పొగమంచు, పొగ, నాయిస్ మరియు కాస్టిక్స్. రెండర్ క్యూ మరియు MP4. అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలు మరియు మొత్తం యానిమేషన్లను రెండర్ క్యూ కు జోడించండి; అవి ఒకదాని తర్వాత ఒకటి రెండర్ అవుతాయి, మరియు పేజీ మూసివేసినా క్యూ ఉంటుంది. దానికి WebGPU అవసరం. బ్రౌజర్ రాయగలిగితే వీడియోలను MP4 గా కూడా సేవ్ చేయవచ్చు. చూడండి రెండర్ క్యూ మరియు వీడియో రెండర్ చేయడం. లైవ్ వ్యూ మరియు ప్రెజెంటేషన్‌లు లైవ్ వ్యూ. ఎగుమతి › లైవ్ వీక్షణ మోడల్‌ను వీక్షణ పక్కన రియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో చూపుతుంది, ప్రతి సవరణ మరియు కెమెరాను అనుసరిస్తుంది: సూర్య నీడలు, మెటీరియల్‌లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు గోడలపై తిరిగి పడే కాంతి. నడక ద్వారా దానిలో నడవండి, సూర్యుడిని కదపడానికి సమయం మరియు నెల లాగండి, లేదా దాన్ని కొత్త విండో కు తరలించండి. బ్రౌజర్‌కు ఉన్నప్పుడు ఇది WebGPU ను, లేకపోతే WebGL 2 ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. చూడండి లైవ్ వ్యూ. స్కాటర్. గడ్డి, పువ్వులు, రాళ్లు, చెట్లు మరియు మనుషులను ముఖాలపై లేదా బ్రష్‌తో చెదరగొట్టండి, ఏడు కొత్త ఎంటూరేజ్ అంశాలు మరియు లైబ్రరీలోని మిగిలిన వాటి నుండి; సెట్‌లు ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ అవుతాయి. చూడండి మొక్కలు, రాళ్లు మరియు మనుషులను స్కాటర్ చేయడం. షేర్. 360° పనోరమా, స్పాట్‌ల మధ్య బాణాలతో పనోరమా టూర్, స్వతంత్ర వ్యూయర్ (ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో తెరుచుకుని ఇంటర్నెట్ నుండి ఏమీ లోడ్ చేయని ఒకే వెబ్ పేజీ), మరియు కెమెరా మార్గం యొక్క వీడియో, అన్నీ రియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో. చూడండి షేర్. రియల్-టైమ్ రూపంలో VR. VRలో సమీక్షించు మోడల్‌ను దాని మెటీరియల్‌లు, ఆకాశం, సూర్య నీడ, లాంప్‌లు, చెదరగొట్టిన అంశాలు మరియు బౌన్స్ కాంతితో చూపిస్తుంది. చూడండి ఎలా ఉంటుంది. మెటీరియల్‌లు నోడ్‌లతో చేసిన మెటీరియల్‌లు. షేడింగ్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్ ఫైల్ యొక్క స్వంత మెటీరియల్‌లను నోడ్ గ్రాఫ్‌లుగా నిర్మిస్తుంది: చిత్రాలు, నాయిస్ మరియు చెక్కర్లు, వొరోనాయ్ కణాలు, అలలు, ఇటుకలు, టైల్స్, కలప, పాలరాయి, గ్రేడియంట్‌లు, మట్టి మరియు అంచు అరుగుదల, రంగు పని, గణితం, బంప్ మరియు నార్మల్ మ్యాప్‌లు, పూర్తి OpenPBR మెటీరియల్‌లోకి. వీక్షణ ప్రతి మార్పును ఒకేసారి చూపుతుంది. చూడండి నోడ్‌లతో మెటీరియల్‌లు తయారు చేయడం. మెటీరియల్ లైబ్రరీ మరియు మీ స్వంత టెక్చర్ సెట్‌లు. షేడింగ్ ప్యానెల్ లైబ్రరీ మెటీరియల్‌లను జాబితా చేస్తుంది: పదహారు CC0 టెక్చర్ సెట్‌లు, ప్రతిదానికి క్రెడిట్. ఉపయోగించడానికి ఒకదానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఒక ముఖంపై లాగండి. చిత్రాలు లేదా జిప్… లేదా ఒక ఫోల్డర్… మీ స్వంత మ్యాప్‌ల నుండి మెటీరియల్‌ను తయారు చేస్తుంది, ఫైల్ పేర్ల ద్వారా కనుగొనబడుతుంది. చూడండి లైబ్రరీ నుండి మెటీరియల్‌లు. MaterialX లోపలికి మరియు బయటికి. మెటీరియల్‌ను MaterialX గా ఎగుమతి చేయండి (OpenPBR Surface లేదా Standard Surface, టెక్చర్‌లు PNG గా), మరియు MaterialX ఫైళ్లను వాటి చిత్రాలతో దిగుమతి చేయండి. చూడండి MaterialX. డికాల్స్ మరియు టెక్చర్ పెయింటింగ్. ఉపరితలంపై చిత్రాన్ని దాని స్వంత మెటీరియల్‌తో డికాల్‌గా ఉంచండి, మరియు ఒక గుండ్రని బ్రష్‌తో మెటీరియల్ చిత్రంపై పెయింట్ చేయండి. చూడండి డికాల్స్ మరియు టెక్చర్‌పై పెయింట్ చేయడం. టెక్చర్ బేకింగ్. యాంబియంట్ ఆక్లూజన్, వక్రత, లైటింగ్ మరియు వివరమైన ఆబ్జెక్ట్ యొక్క నార్మల్‌లను మెష్ UVల వెంట చిత్రాలుగా బేక్ చేయండి, కంప్యూటర్ ప్రాసెసర్ కోర్లపై. చూడండి టెక్చర్‌లను బేక్ చేయడం. యానిమేషన్ యానిమేట్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్. ఒక ఆబ్జెక్ట్ యొక్క స్థానం, తిరుగుదల మరియు పరిమాణం, ప్రతి లాంప్ యొక్క ప్రకాశం, వ్యాప్తి మరియు రంగు, మరియు లెన్స్ కోణాన్ని కీలుగా ఉంచండి, నేరుగా ప్యానెల్‌లోనే. టైమ్‌లైన్‌కు వక్రాలు వీక్షణ (బెజియర్ హ్యాండిల్స్, పునరావృతాలు) మరియు క్లిప్‌లు వీక్షణ వస్తాయి, ఇది కీలను మళ్ళీ ఉంచడానికి మరియు మిక్స్ చేయడానికి చర్యలుగా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; అనేక వరుసలపై కీలను తరలించవచ్చు, స్ట్రెచ్ చేయవచ్చు, కాపీ చేయవచ్చు మరియు పేస్ట్ చేయవచ్చు. చూడండి ఏ ప్రాపర్టీనైనా యానిమేట్ చేయడం. షేప్ కీలు మరియు డ్రైవర్లు. మెష్ తన స్వంత ఇతర ఆకారాలను కలిగి ఉంటుంది, కీ చేయగల స్లైడర్‌ల ద్వారా కలిపి, లేదా ఒక చిన్న వ్యక్తీకరణ ద్వారా ఇతర విలువల నుండి సెట్ చేయబడి ఉంటుంది. చూడండి షేప్ కీలు మరియు డ్రైవర్లు. ఆర్మేచర్‌లు. మెష్‌లో బోన్‌లు ఉంచండి, స్వయంచాలక వెయిట్‌లు పాయింట్లను వాటి మధ్య పంచనివ్వండి లేదా వెయిట్‌లను పెయింట్ చేయండి, మరియు బోన్‌లను పోజ్ చేసి కీ చేయండి; లీనియర్ లేదా డ్యూయల్ క్వాటర్నియన్ స్కిన్నింగ్. చూడండి ఆర్మేచర్‌లు. IK మరియు కన్‌స్ట్రెయింట్లు. పోల్‌తో రీచ్ (IK), లుక్ ఎట్, ట్రాన్స్‌ఫారమ్‌లను కాపీ చేయడం, పరిమితులు, చైల్డ్ ఆఫ్ మరియు ఫాలో పాత్, బోన్‌లు మరియు ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌ల కోసం. చూడండి IK మరియు కన్‌స్ట్రెయింట్లు. ఫిజిక్స్. గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు బాక్స్‌లు లేదా బంతులుగా పడతాయి, పేరుకుపోతాయి మరియు ఢీకొంటాయి, మరియు ఫిజిక్స్ బేక్ చేయి ఫలితాన్ని కీలుగా ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి ఫిజిక్స్. ఫైళ్లు USD. ఇలా సేవ్ చేయి (Ctrl+Shift+S) మోడల్‌ను .usda, .usdc లేదా .usdz గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు ModelCraft సేవ్ చేసిన USD ఫైల్ ఏదీ కోల్పోకుండా మళ్ళీ తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల USD మెష్‌లు, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు కెమెరాలుగా తెరుచుకుంటుంది లేదా దిగుమతి అవుతుంది. సేవ్ చేయి ఇప్పటికీ .modelcraft రాస్తుంది. చూడండి USD ను సేవ్ చేయడం, తెరవడం మరియు ఎగుమతి చేయడం. glTF, పూర్తిగా. glTF ఫైళ్లు వాటి నార్మల్‌లు, UVs, వర్టెక్స్ రంగులు మరియు టెక్చర్‌లతో సవరించగలిగే మెష్‌లుగా వస్తాయి, నోడ్‌లు గ్రూప్‌లుగా, లైట్లు లాంప్‌లుగా, మరియు కెమెరాలు దృశ్యాలుగా; Draco మరియు meshopt ఫైళ్లు కూడా. .gltf దాని ఫైళ్లతో కలిపి వదిలినప్పుడు తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ఎగుమతి మెష్‌లు, టెక్చర్‌లు, లాంప్‌లు మరియు కెమెరాలు రాస్తుంది. చూడండి glTF. FBX, PLY, STL మరియు Alembic దిగుమతులు. FBX 2011 మరియు కొత్తవి (మెష్‌లు, UV సెట్‌లు, మెటీరియల్‌లు, ఎంబెడెడ్ టెక్చర్‌లు, లైట్లు, కెమెరాలు), వాటి రంగులతో PLY మరియు STL, మరియు Alembic కాష్‌లు వాటి మొదటి నమూనాలో. OBJ దాని .mtl మరియు చిత్రాలతో వస్తుంది, మీరు వాటిని కలిపి వదిలినప్పుడు. చూడండి ఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా. మరిన్ని ఎగుమతులు. రంగులతో PLY, పెయింట్ చేసిన ముఖాల రంగులతో STL, మరియు దాని టెక్చర్‌లతో OBJ ఒకే zip గా. చూడండి ఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా. STEP ఫైళ్లు. దిగుమతి STEP (AP203, AP214, AP242) భాగాలు మరియు అసెంబ్లీలను వాటి నిజమైన పరిమాణంలో మూసిన మెష్‌లుగా చదువుతుంది, ప్రతి భాగం ఫైల్ దాన్ని ఉంచిన చోట. చూడండి STEP. .3dm ఫైళ్లు. దిగుమతి .3dm మోడల్‌లను చదువుతుంది: మెష్‌లు, పాలీసర్ఫేస్‌లు మరియు ఎక్స్‌ట్రూజన్‌లు (ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ చేసిన మెష్‌ల నుండి, లేకపోతే ModelCraft టెస్సలేట్ చేస్తుంది), వక్రాలు అంచులుగా, బ్లాక్‌లు కాంపోనెంట్లుగా మరియు లేయర్‌లు ట్యాగ్‌లుగా. చూడండి .3dm మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం. DWG మరియు DXF నుండి 3D సాలిడ్‌లు. డ్రాయింగ్‌ను దిగుమతి చేయడం ఇప్పుడు దాని 3D సాలిడ్‌లు, రీజియన్‌లు మరియు బాడీలను కూడా తెస్తుంది, ప్రతిదీ దాని స్వంత గ్రూప్‌గా, మూసినవి ఘనపదార్థాలుగా. చూడండి సైట్ ప్లాన్‌పై మోడల్ చేయడం. 3D ప్రింటింగ్ ప్రింట్ టూల్స్. 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)… › ప్రింట్ టూల్స్ లో సపోర్ట్‌ల ప్రివ్యూ ఉంది (ఓవర్‌హాంగ్‌లు, స్తంభాలు లేదా చెట్టు సపోర్ట్‌లు, ఎంత మెటీరియల్ అనే అంచనా), గోడ మందం (ప్రింట్ చేయడానికి చాలా సన్నని గోడలు, చూపించు తో జాబితా), బోలు (మీరు ఇచ్చిన మందం గోడలు, డ్రెయిన్ రంధ్రాలతో) మరియు మరమ్మతు (ప్రతి పరిష్కారం జాబితా; అతివ్యాప్తి భాగాలు మీరు కోరినప్పుడు మాత్రమే కలుస్తాయి). బోలు మరియు మరమ్మతు, వాటి ఫ్లాట్ వైపులు ఒకే ముఖాలుగా ఉండే గ్రూప్‌ను ఇస్తాయి, లేదా ఫలితం మూసుకోనప్పుడు లేదా ముఖాలు చాలా ఎక్కువైనప్పుడు మెష్‌ను ఇస్తాయి; స్టేటస్ లైన్ ఏది చెబుతుంది. చూడండి సపోర్ట్‌లు, గోడ మందం, బోలుగా చేయడం మరియు మరమ్మతు. ప్రారంభకుడు శైలి. సెట్టింగ్‌లు › ModelCraft › మెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్ › ప్రారంభకుడు: ఆకారాలు మరియు రంధ్రాలను వదలడానికి, తరలించడానికి, అమర్చడానికి మరియు రంధ్రాలు కోయేలా గ్రూప్ చేయడానికి పెద్ద బటన్లు, తర్వాత ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను తనిఖీ చేయండి. చూడండి ప్రారంభకుడు శైలి. AI సహాయకులు మరియు మీ యంత్రం AI సహాయకుల కోసం మరిన్ని. ఒక సహాయకుడు ఇప్పుడు ముఖాలు మరియు మెటీరియల్‌లను జాబితా చేయగలడు, push/pull చేయగలడు, పెయింట్ చేయగలడు, గ్రూప్‌లు చేయగలడు, దృశ్యాలను జోడించి చూపగలడు, మెష్‌లు జోడించగలడు, మాడిఫైయర్ స్టాక్‌లను సవరించగలడు, USD ఎగుమతి చేయగలడు, చిత్రం రెండర్ చేయగలడు మరియు రెండర్ క్యూ నింపగలడు. చూడండి ModelCraft ను AI సహాయకులతో ఉపయోగించడం. మీ యంత్రం. సెట్టింగ్‌లు › ModelCraft › రెండరింగ్ › మీ యంత్రం ఈ బ్రౌజర్ యాప్‌కు అనుమతించే గ్రాఫిక్స్, ప్రాసెసర్ కోర్లు మరియు మెమరీని చూపుతుంది, మరియు లైవ్ వ్యూ ఎక్కడ ప్రారంభమవుతుందో సెట్ చేసే స్థాయిని. చూడండి మీ యంత్రం ఎలా రెండర్ చేస్తుంది. పరిష్కరించినవి సాలిడ్స్ › యూనియన్, తీసివేయి మరియు ఖండన ఏ పరిమాణంలోనైనా మూసి లేని గ్రూప్‌ను ఇవ్వవచ్చు. ఇప్పుడు అవి మూసివేస్తాయి. సాలిడ్స్ › తీసివేయి ఏదీ ఎంచుకోకుండా బూడిద రంగులో ఉండేది, అయినప్పటికీ మీరు దాన్ని ఎంచుకున్న తర్వాత అది దాని రెండు ముక్కలను వంతులవారీగా ఎంచుకుంటుంది. యానిమేషన్ టైమ్‌లైన్ లోపల, టైప్ చేసిన కీలు ఎక్కడికీ వెళ్ళలేదు, Ctrl+S కూడా. అక్కడ Ctrl+Z మరియు Ctrl+Y టైమ్‌లైన్ స్వంత అన్‌డూ మరియు రీడూ; ఇతర Ctrl కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి. అన్ని యాప్‌లలో యూనిట్లు. సెట్టింగ్‌లు › General › యూనిట్లు పొడవు, వైశాల్యం, ఘనపరిమాణం, కోణం మరియు వాలు కోసం ఒక్కో జాబితా కలిగి ఉంది, యూనిట్ల నిర్వచనాలు ఇచ్చే ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలతో (US సర్వే అడుగు దాని స్వంత యూనిట్). మీరు ఎప్పుడూ ఉపయోగించని యూనిట్లను ఆఫ్ చేయండి, అవి యూనిట్ జాబితాల నుండి బయటకు వెళ్తాయి; ఒకదాన్ని స్టార్ చేస్తే కొత్త పని దానిలో మొదలవుతుంది. ఫైల్ ఎల్లప్పుడూ తన స్వంత యూనిట్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. ModelCraft లో విలువ పెట్టె పక్కన ఉన్న యూనిట్ జాబితా ఈ స్విచ్‌లను అనుసరిస్తుంది (మోడల్ స్వంత యూనిట్ ఎల్లప్పుడూ అందులోనే ఉంటుంది), స్టార్ కొత్త మోడల్ యూనిట్‌ను సెట్ చేస్తుంది (ModelCraft ఆ యూనిట్ కలిగి ఉంటే), మరియు ఏ యూనిట్ గుర్తుతోనైనా టైప్ చేసిన పొడవు ఆ యూనిట్‌లో చదవబడుతుంది. చూడండి యూనిట్లు, టైప్ చేసిన విలువలు మరియు యూనిట్ జాబితాలు. మరింత సాంద్రమైన లేఅవుట్ మరియు సర్దుబాటయ్యే ప్యానెల్‌లు. సెట్టింగ్‌లు › General › లేఅవుట్ సాంద్రత › కాంపాక్ట్ స్క్రీన్‌పై ఎక్కువ అమర్చుతుంది (టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌లు మరియు ఫోన్‌లు తమ పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతాయి). ప్రతి సైడ్ ప్యానెల్‌కు సన్నని శీర్షిక బార్ ఉంటుంది: దాన్ని లాగి ప్యానెల్‌ను డాక్ లేదా ఫ్లోట్ చేయండి, మరో ప్యానెల్ బార్‌పై వదిలి ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ఒకే ఫ్రేమ్‌ను పంచుకోండి, అంచుకు జారిపోయేలా పిన్ చేయండి, లేదా దాచి అంచు నుండి తిరిగి తీసుకురండి. చూడండి ప్యానెల్‌లను అమర్చడం. డైలాగ్‌లు కదులుతాయి. డ్రాయింగ్‌కు అడ్డు తప్పించడానికి డైలాగ్‌ను దాని శీర్షిక బార్ ద్వారా లాగండి; మీరు వదిలిన చోటికి అది తిరిగి వస్తుంది. మళ్ళీ మధ్యలోకి తీసుకురావడానికి శీర్షిక బార్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. చూడండి డైలాగ్‌ను తరలించడం. AI సహాయకుల కోసం వంటకాలు. మీ స్వంత సహాయకుడి కోసం పది పని చేసిన అభ్యర్థనలు (డ్రాయింగ్ చక్కదిద్దడం, సెట్ ప్లాట్ చేయడం, డ్రాయింగ్‌లు తనిఖీ చేయడం, మోడల్‌ను ప్రశ్నలు అడగడం, టేకాఫ్ మొత్తం చేయడం, పార్ట్ వేరియంట్ ప్రయత్నించడం...), ప్రతిదీ దాని పిలిచే టూల్స్ మరియు అవసరమైన అనుమతులతో, మరియు ప్రతిదీ మా పరీక్షల ద్వారా మళ్ళీ ప్లే చేయబడుతుంది. యాప్‌లలో AI లేదు. చూడండి AI సహాయకుల కోసం వంటకాలు. భాషలు. Português (Portugal) సెట్టింగ్‌లు › General › Language లో కొత్తది. అది, Português (Brasil), Español (México) మరియు Bahasa Indonesia ఇప్పుడు యాప్‌ల ప్రతి పదాన్ని కలిగి ఉన్నాయి, స్థానిక వక్త సమీక్షించని ముసాయిదాలుగా. సహాయ అంశాలు ఇంగ్లీషులో ఉన్నాయి. CadCraft Studio అప్‌డేట్ తనిఖీ ఎందుకు విఫలమైందో చెబుతుంది (సిస్టమ్‌కు ఫైల్ ఇంకా లేదు, నెట్‌వర్క్ లేదు, చెడ్డ సంతకం) \"పూర్తయింది\" అని మాత్రమే కాకుండా, మీరు మూసిన \"కొత్తవి ఏమిటి\" కార్డ్‌ను కొత్త విడుదల వచ్చే వరకు తిరిగి తీసుకురాదు. ఈ వెర్షన్‌లో ఇంకా లేనివి 0.7 కోడ్-సైన్ చేయబడలేదు. సైనింగ్, మరియు Microsoft Store ప్యాకేజీ 0.8 కి మారాయి, ఎందుకంటే సర్టిఫికేట్ ధృవీకరణ ఇంకా రాలేదు. getcadcraft.com/download పై ప్రతి ఫైల్ పక్కన ఉన్న SHA-256 తనిఖీ చేయండి. Windows లో SmartScreen ఇన్‌స్టాలర్‌ను ఆపితే, మరింత సమాచారం ఎంచుకుని, తర్వాత ఏమైనప్పటికీ రన్ చేయి ఎంచుకోండి. Mac లో, మొదటిసారి, CadCraft Studio పై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేసి తెరువు ఎంచుకోండి. CadCraft Studio ఇంకా యాప్ స్టోర్‌లో లేదు, మరియు ఇంకా iPad లేదా iPhone బిల్డ్ లేదు. యూనిట్లు లోని కోణం మరియు వాలు జాబితాలు సేవ్ అవుతాయి, కానీ ఇంకా ఏ బాక్స్ వాటిని చదవదు. గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌పై రెండరింగ్ (WebGPU) ఇక్కడ కేవలం సాఫ్ట్‌వేర్ స్టాండ్-ఇన్‌తో పరీక్షించబడింది, ఇంకా నిజమైన గ్రాఫిక్స్ కార్డ్‌లపై కాదు, కాబట్టి ముందుగా మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌లో ఏదైనా చిన్నది రెండర్ చేయండి. లైవ్ వ్యూ కూడా అంతే, మరియు VR ఇంకా హెడ్‌సెట్ ఎమ్యులేటర్‌తో మాత్రమే ప్రయత్నించబడింది. WebGPU లేకుండా రెండర్ వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేదా రెండర్ క్యూ ఉండదు, మరియు అధిక నాణ్యత చిత్రాలకు డెప్త్ ఆఫ్ ఫీల్డ్, భౌతిక ఆకాశం, ఆకాశ చిత్రం, పొగమంచు, పొగ, కాస్టిక్స్ లేదా లైట్ మిక్సింగ్ ఉండవు. చిత్రం రెండర్ చేయి… ఇంకా చెదరగొట్టిన అంశాలను గీయదు; అవి లైవ్ వ్యూ, దాని పనోరమాలు, వ్యూయర్ మరియు వీడియోలో కనిపిస్తాయి. లాంప్‌లు వీక్షణలో యానిమేట్ అవుతాయి, కానీ రెండర్ చేసిన వీడియో ప్రారంభమైనప్పుడు ఉన్న లాంప్‌లను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. ఎముకలు మరియు యానిమేషన్ ఇంకా glTF, FBX లేదా USD ఫైళ్ల నుండి చదవబడవు లేదా వాటికి రాయబడవు (స్కిన్ చేసిన మోడల్‌లు వాటి వెయిట్‌లతో విశ్రాంతి స్థితిలో వస్తాయి), మరియు base color లేదా టెక్చర్ దాటి glTF మరియు FBX మెటీరియల్‌లు ఇంకా ఉంచబడవు. STEP రంగులు మరియు అసెంబ్లీ యొక్క భాగాల పేర్లు ఇంకా చదవబడవు. .3dm దిగుమతి ఫార్మాట్ యొక్క టూల్‌కిట్ రాసిన ఫైళ్లతో పరీక్షించబడింది, మోడలింగ్ ప్రోగ్రామ్ నుండి వచ్చిన ఫైళ్లతో ఇంకా కాదు; SubD ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు, టెక్స్ట్ మరియు డైమెన్షన్‌లు వదిలివేయబడ్డాయి. CivilCraft నుండి మొక్కల లైబ్రరీ ModelCraft లో ఉంది, కానీ దాని నుండి మొక్కలను ఉంచడం ఇంకా నిర్మించబడలేదు. పుష్/పుల్ ముఖాలపై టెక్చర్ పెయింట్ చేయడం మెటీరియల్ టైల్‌తో పునరావృతమవుతుంది; ఒక ముఖాన్ని మాత్రమే పెయింట్ చేయడానికి, దాన్ని మెష్‌గా మార్చి అన్‌రాప్ చేయండి. ఇంకా మిగిలినవి ఇంకా నిర్మించబడలేదు లో ఉన్నాయి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"modelcraft","title":"ModelCraft ను ప్రారంభించడం","summary":"ModelCraft దేనికోసం, దాన్ని ఎలా తెరవాలి, మరియు ఒక దీర్ఘచతురస్రం నుండి సేవ్ చేసిన మోడల్ వరకు మీ మొదటి బాక్స్.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["ప్రారంభం","ప్రారంభించు","మొదటి మోడల్","ప్రారంభ","ట్యుటోరియల్","పరిచయం","బాక్స్","మాస్సింగ్","3d","నెట్టు లాగు","త్వరిత ప్రారంభం","మార్గదర్శక","మార్గదర్శి","ప్రారంభకుడు","3d కి కొత్త"],"headings":[{"id":"modelcraft","text":"ModelCraft తెరవడం","level":2},{"id":"section","text":"ముందుగా యూనిట్లను తనిఖీ చేయండి","level":2},{"id":"modelcraft-2","text":"మొదటి మోడల్‌కు ModelCraft మార్గనిర్దేశం చేయనివ్వండి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"మీ మొదటి బాక్స్ గీయండి","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"చుట్టూ చూడండి","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"అన్‌డూ","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"మీ మోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయండి","level":2},{"id":"section-6","text":"సహాయం పొందండి","level":2},{"id":"section-7","text":"తర్వాత ఎక్కడికి వెళ్ళాలి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft ప్రారంభ డిజైన్ కోసం ఒక 3D మోడలర్: మాస్సింగ్ అధ్యయనాలు, గది లేఅవుట్‌లు, త్వరిత భవన రూపాలు మరియు అవి వేసే నీడలు. మీరు ఫ్లాట్ ఆకారాలను గీస్తారు, వాటిని ఘనపదార్థాలుగా పైకి లాగుతారు, మరియు వాటి ముఖాలపై గీయడం ద్వారా వాటిపై పని కొనసాగిస్తారు. ఇది మీ వెబ్ బ్రౌజర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, మరియు మీ మోడల్‌లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESketchUp ఉపయోగించినట్లయితే, మీకు తెలిసినవి చాలావరకు పనికొస్తాయి: అదే టూల్ అక్షరాలు, నెట్టు/లాగు, ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాల కోసం విలువ పెట్టె, గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/coming-from\"\u003Eఇతర మోడలర్‌ల నుండి వచ్చినవారు\u003C/a\u003E ఏమి భిన్నమో జాబితా చేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft\"\u003EModelCraft తెరవడం\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChrome, Edge, Firefox లేదా Safari యొక్క ప్రస్తుత వెర్షన్‌లో ModelCraft తెరవండి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు కంప్యూటర్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D వీక్షణ కనిపించే వరకు వేచి ఉండండి: మధ్యలో ఒక కాంతి (లేదా చీకటి) ప్రాంతం, &quot;మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు&quot; అనే శీర్షిక గల కార్డ్‌తో.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eస్క్రీన్‌పై పైభాగంలో టూల్‌బార్ ఉంది, ఎడమవైపు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅవుట్‌లైనర్\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపోనెంట్లు\u003C/span\u003E, మధ్యలో 3D వీక్షణ, కుడివైపు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంటిటీ సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eశైలి\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదృశ్యాలు\u003C/span\u003E, మరియు కింద స్టేటస్ బార్, అందులో విలువ పెట్టె మరియు యూనిట్లు. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/workspace\"\u003EModelCraft స్క్రీన్\u003C/a\u003E ప్రతి భాగాన్ని వివరిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft కు WebGL అవసరం, అంటే మీ బ్రౌజర్ యొక్క 3D డ్రాయింగ్ ఫీచర్. స్క్రీన్ మధ్యభాగం ఖాళీగా ఉండి, టూల్స్ ఏమీ చేయకపోతే, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#the-3d-view-is-blank\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"the-3d-view-is-blank\"\u003Eట్రబుల్‌షూటింగ్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eముందుగా యూనిట్లను తనిఖీ చేయండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకుడి కింది మూలను చూడండి. యూనిట్ల ఎంపిక పొడవులు ఏ యూనిట్లలో కొలవబడతాయో చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీ బ్రౌజర్ యునైటెడ్ స్టేట్స్‌కు సెట్ చేయబడినప్పుడు (లైబీరియా మరియు మయన్మార్ కూడా), కొత్త మోడల్ \u003Cstrong\u003Eఅడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలు\u003C/strong\u003E లో ప్రారంభమవుతుంది, మరియు మిగతా చోట్ల \u003Cstrong\u003Eమిల్లీమీటర్లు\u003C/strong\u003E లో.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమార్చడానికి, ఎంపికలో మరో యూనిట్ ఎంచుకోండి: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eft మరియు in\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ein\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Em\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ecm\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Emm\u003C/span\u003E. మోడల్ స్వయంగా పరిమాణం మారదు; కేవలం పొడవులు ఎలా చూపబడతాయి మరియు యూనిట్ గుర్తు లేని సంఖ్యలు ఎలా చదవబడతాయి మాత్రమే మారుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eయూనిట్ గుర్తు లేకుండా మీరు టైప్ చేసే సంఖ్య మోడల్ యూనిట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. అడుగులు-అంగుళాల మోడల్‌లో సాధారణ సంఖ్య \u003Cstrong\u003Eఅంగుళాలు\u003C/strong\u003E, కాబట్టి \u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E అంటే 10 అడుగులు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft-2\"\u003Eమొదటి మోడల్‌కు ModelCraft మార్గనిర్దేశం చేయనివ్వండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E3D కి కొత్తవారా? \u003Cstrong\u003Eమోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు\u003C/strong\u003E కార్డ్‌పై, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమార్గదర్శక మొదటి మోడల్\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి (ఇది \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవీక్షణలు\u003C/span\u003E లో కూడా ఉంటుంది). ఒక చిన్న కార్డ్ నాలుగు దశల ద్వారా మిమ్మల్ని నడిపిస్తుంది, ఫ్లాట్ దీర్ఘచతురస్రం నుండి 3D ప్రింటర్‌కు సిద్ధమైన మోడల్ వరకు:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eనేలపై ఒక దీర్ఘచతురస్రం గీయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌గా పైకి లాగండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eబాక్స్‌ను ఒకే వస్తువుగా చేయండి (ఒక గ్రూప్).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి, తర్వాత 3MF లేదా STL ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకార్డ్ బటన్లను కాకుండా మోడల్‌ను చూస్తుంది: దశ పూర్తయినప్పుడు అది ముందుకు వెళ్తుంది, మీరు ఎలా చేసినా సరే, మరియు మీరు అన్‌డూ చేసినప్పుడు ఒక దశ వెనక్కి వెళ్తుంది. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌ను ఎంచుకో\u003C/span\u003E మీ కోసం టూల్‌ను ఎంచుకుంటుంది. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E తో ఎప్పుడైనా మూసివేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eమీ మొదటి బాక్స్ గీయండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదీర్ఘచతురస్రం టూల్ కోసం \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా టూల్‌బార్‌లోని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగీయి\u003C/span\u003E గ్రూప్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదీర్ఘచతురస్రం\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eవీక్షణ మధ్యకు దగ్గరగా నేలపై ఒకసారి క్లిక్ చేయండి. అది మొదటి మూల.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపాయింటర్‌ను దూరంగా కదపండి. ఒక దీర్ఘచతురస్రం దాన్ని అనుసరిస్తుంది, మరియు కింద ఉన్న విలువ పెట్టె (దానిపై \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపరిమాణాలు\u003C/span\u003E అని రాసి ఉంటుంది) దాని పరిమాణాన్ని చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపరిమాణం టైప్ చేసి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి: ఉదాహరణకు \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, లేదా అడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలలో \u003Ccode\u003E16&#039;,12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E. మీరు ముందుగా విలువ పెట్టెపై క్లిక్ చేయనక్కర్లేదు; సంఖ్య టైప్ చేస్తే అది అక్కడికి వెళ్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీకు నేలపై ఒక ఫ్లాట్ దీర్ఘచతురస్రం వస్తుంది. అది \u003Cstrong\u003Eముఖం\u003C/strong\u003E: చుట్టూ అంచులు ఉన్న ఉపరితలం.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనెట్టు/లాగు\u003C/span\u003E కోసం \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదీర్ఘచతురస్రంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, పాయింటర్‌ను పైకి కదపండి, మళ్ళీ క్లిక్ చేయండి. లేదా దీర్ఘచతురస్రంపై క్లిక్ చేసి ఎత్తు టైప్ చేయండి, ఉదాహరణకు \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E లేదా \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eదీర్ఘచతురస్రం పైభాగం, అడుగుభాగం మరియు నాలుగు వైపులతో ఒక బాక్స్ అవుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంచుకో\u003C/span\u003E కి తిరిగి రావడానికి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, తర్వాత బాక్స్ పైభాగంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. కుడివైపు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంటిటీ సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E దాని వైశాల్యం చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరెండుసార్లు క్లిక్ చేయడానికి బదులుగా లాగవచ్చు: మొదటి మూలపై నొక్కి, లాగి, రెండవ మూలపై విడుదల చేయండి. ప్రతి గీత టూల్‌కు రెండు పద్ధతులు పనిచేస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eచుట్టూ చూడండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఆర్బిట్:\u003C/strong\u003E మధ్య మౌస్ బటన్ (వీల్)తో లాగండి, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కి ఎడమ బటన్‌తో లాగండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eప్యాన్:\u003C/strong\u003E కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగండి, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పట్టుకుని మధ్య బటన్‌తో లాగండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eజూమ్:\u003C/strong\u003E వీల్ తిప్పండి. అది పాయింటర్ కింద ఉన్నదానిపైకి జూమ్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఅన్నీ చూడండి:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిస్తీర్ణం\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్ లేదా టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌పై సంజ్ఞలు భిన్నంగా ఉంటాయి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003Eఆర్బిట్, ప్యాన్ మరియు జూమ్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eఅన్‌డూ\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కి అన్‌డూ చేయండి మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కి మళ్ళీ చేయండి, లేదా టూల్‌బార్‌లోని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచర్య రద్దు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమళ్ళీ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఉపయోగించండి. మోడల్ తెరిచి ఉన్నంత వరకు ModelCraft చివరి 200 దశలను గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది. చరిత్ర ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ కాదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eమీ మోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/span\u003E గ్రూప్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ మీ మోడల్ పేరు మీద ఒక ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E. స్టేటస్ బార్ అది ఎక్కడికి వెళ్ళిందో చెబుతుంది: &quot;Untitled.modelcraft మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లకు సేవ్ చేయబడింది.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eసేవ్ చేయడం డౌన్‌లోడ్.\u003C/strong\u003E ప్రతి సేవ్ మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్‌లో కొత్త కాపీని చేస్తుంది; మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్‌ను ఓవర్‌రైట్ చేయదు. చక్కని ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫోల్డర్ కోసం, ఫైల్‌ను అక్కడికి మీరే తరలించండి లేదా పేరు మార్చండి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\"\u003Eఫైల్ తెరవడం, సేవ్ చేయడం, దిగుమతి మరియు ఎగుమతి\u003C/a\u003E ఎందుకు మరియు ఇంకా ఏమి చేయవచ్చో వివరిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft తనంతట తానే సేవ్ చేయదు. సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులతో ట్యాబ్ మూసివేస్తే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (&quot;సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?&quot;). ఉండడానికి ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత సేవ్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eసహాయం పొందండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి లేదా యాప్ బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసహాయం\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేసి ఈ సహాయాన్ని మోడల్ పక్కన తెరవండి. ఇది కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది. సహాయం మూసివేయడానికి మళ్ళీ \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా సహాయం లోపల \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003Eఈ సహాయాన్ని ఉపయోగించడం\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-7\"\u003Eతర్వాత ఎక్కడికి వెళ్ళాలి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003Eగీతలు, దీర్ఘచతురస్రాలు మరియు వృత్తాలతో ముఖాలు గీయండి\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\"\u003Eముఖాలను నెట్టి లాగి ఘనపదార్థాలుగా చేయండి\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003Eవిలువ పెట్టెలో ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలు టైప్ చేయండి\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\"\u003Eప్రతి కీ మరియు మౌస్ చర్య\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"ModelCraft ప్రారంభ డిజైన్ కోసం ఒక 3D మోడలర్: మాస్సింగ్ అధ్యయనాలు, గది లేఅవుట్‌లు, త్వరిత భవన రూపాలు మరియు అవి వేసే నీడలు. మీరు ఫ్లాట్ ఆకారాలను గీస్తారు, వాటిని ఘనపదార్థాలుగా పైకి లాగుతారు, మరియు వాటి ముఖాలపై గీయడం ద్వారా వాటిపై పని కొనసాగిస్తారు. ఇది మీ వెబ్ బ్రౌజర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, మరియు మీ మోడల్‌లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి. SketchUp ఉపయోగించినట్లయితే, మీకు తెలిసినవి చాలావరకు పనికొస్తాయి: అదే టూల్ అక్షరాలు, నెట్టు/లాగు, ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాల కోసం విలువ పెట్టె, గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు. ఇతర మోడలర్‌ల నుండి వచ్చినవారు ఏమి భిన్నమో జాబితా చేస్తుంది. ModelCraft తెరవడం Chrome, Edge, Firefox లేదా Safari యొక్క ప్రస్తుత వెర్షన్‌లో ModelCraft తెరవండి. బ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు కంప్యూటర్‌లు చూడండి. 3D వీక్షణ కనిపించే వరకు వేచి ఉండండి: మధ్యలో ఒక కాంతి (లేదా చీకటి) ప్రాంతం, \"మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు\" అనే శీర్షిక గల కార్డ్‌తో. స్క్రీన్‌పై పైభాగంలో టూల్‌బార్ ఉంది, ఎడమవైపు అవుట్‌లైనర్ మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు, మధ్యలో 3D వీక్షణ, కుడివైపు ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం, శైలి మరియు దృశ్యాలు, మరియు కింద స్టేటస్ బార్, అందులో విలువ పెట్టె మరియు యూనిట్లు. ModelCraft స్క్రీన్ ప్రతి భాగాన్ని వివరిస్తుంది. ModelCraft కు WebGL అవసరం, అంటే మీ బ్రౌజర్ యొక్క 3D డ్రాయింగ్ ఫీచర్. స్క్రీన్ మధ్యభాగం ఖాళీగా ఉండి, టూల్స్ ఏమీ చేయకపోతే, ట్రబుల్‌షూటింగ్ చూడండి. ముందుగా యూనిట్లను తనిఖీ చేయండి కుడి కింది మూలను చూడండి. యూనిట్ల ఎంపిక పొడవులు ఏ యూనిట్లలో కొలవబడతాయో చూపిస్తుంది. మీ బ్రౌజర్ యునైటెడ్ స్టేట్స్‌కు సెట్ చేయబడినప్పుడు (లైబీరియా మరియు మయన్మార్ కూడా), కొత్త మోడల్ అడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలు లో ప్రారంభమవుతుంది, మరియు మిగతా చోట్ల మిల్లీమీటర్లు లో. మార్చడానికి, ఎంపికలో మరో యూనిట్ ఎంచుకోండి: ft మరియు in, in, m, cm లేదా mm. మోడల్ స్వయంగా పరిమాణం మారదు; కేవలం పొడవులు ఎలా చూపబడతాయి మరియు యూనిట్ గుర్తు లేని సంఖ్యలు ఎలా చదవబడతాయి మాత్రమే మారుతుంది. యూనిట్ గుర్తు లేకుండా మీరు టైప్ చేసే సంఖ్య మోడల్ యూనిట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. అడుగులు-అంగుళాల మోడల్‌లో సాధారణ సంఖ్య అంగుళాలు, కాబట్టి 120 అంటే 10 అడుగులు. మొదటి మోడల్‌కు ModelCraft మార్గనిర్దేశం చేయనివ్వండి 3D కి కొత్తవారా? మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు కార్డ్‌పై, మార్గదర్శక మొదటి మోడల్ పై క్లిక్ చేయండి (ఇది వీక్షణలు లో కూడా ఉంటుంది). ఒక చిన్న కార్డ్ నాలుగు దశల ద్వారా మిమ్మల్ని నడిపిస్తుంది, ఫ్లాట్ దీర్ఘచతురస్రం నుండి 3D ప్రింటర్‌కు సిద్ధమైన మోడల్ వరకు: నేలపై ఒక దీర్ఘచతురస్రం గీయండి. దాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌గా పైకి లాగండి. బాక్స్‌ను ఒకే వస్తువుగా చేయండి (ఒక గ్రూప్). 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి, తర్వాత 3MF లేదా STL ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయండి. కార్డ్ బటన్లను కాకుండా మోడల్‌ను చూస్తుంది: దశ పూర్తయినప్పుడు అది ముందుకు వెళ్తుంది, మీరు ఎలా చేసినా సరే, మరియు మీరు అన్‌డూ చేసినప్పుడు ఒక దశ వెనక్కి వెళ్తుంది. టూల్‌ను ఎంచుకో మీ కోసం టూల్‌ను ఎంచుకుంటుంది. × తో ఎప్పుడైనా మూసివేయండి. మీ మొదటి బాక్స్ గీయండి దీర్ఘచతురస్రం టూల్ కోసం R నొక్కండి, లేదా టూల్‌బార్‌లోని గీయి గ్రూప్‌లో దీర్ఘచతురస్రం పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. వీక్షణ మధ్యకు దగ్గరగా నేలపై ఒకసారి క్లిక్ చేయండి. అది మొదటి మూల. పాయింటర్‌ను దూరంగా కదపండి. ఒక దీర్ఘచతురస్రం దాన్ని అనుసరిస్తుంది, మరియు కింద ఉన్న విలువ పెట్టె (దానిపై పరిమాణాలు అని రాసి ఉంటుంది) దాని పరిమాణాన్ని చూపిస్తుంది. పరిమాణం టైప్ చేసి Enter నొక్కండి: ఉదాహరణకు 4m,3m, లేదా అడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలలో 16',12'. మీరు ముందుగా విలువ పెట్టెపై క్లిక్ చేయనక్కర్లేదు; సంఖ్య టైప్ చేస్తే అది అక్కడికి వెళ్తుంది. మీకు నేలపై ఒక ఫ్లాట్ దీర్ఘచతురస్రం వస్తుంది. అది ముఖం: చుట్టూ అంచులు ఉన్న ఉపరితలం. నెట్టు/లాగు కోసం P నొక్కండి. దీర్ఘచతురస్రంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, పాయింటర్‌ను పైకి కదపండి, మళ్ళీ క్లిక్ చేయండి. లేదా దీర్ఘచతురస్రంపై క్లిక్ చేసి ఎత్తు టైప్ చేయండి, ఉదాహరణకు 3m లేదా 10', తర్వాత Enter నొక్కండి. దీర్ఘచతురస్రం పైభాగం, అడుగుభాగం మరియు నాలుగు వైపులతో ఒక బాక్స్ అవుతుంది. ఎంచుకో కి తిరిగి రావడానికి Space నొక్కండి, తర్వాత బాక్స్ పైభాగంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. కుడివైపు ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం దాని వైశాల్యం చూపిస్తుంది. రెండుసార్లు క్లిక్ చేయడానికి బదులుగా లాగవచ్చు: మొదటి మూలపై నొక్కి, లాగి, రెండవ మూలపై విడుదల చేయండి. ప్రతి గీత టూల్‌కు రెండు పద్ధతులు పనిచేస్తాయి. చుట్టూ చూడండి ఆర్బిట్: మధ్య మౌస్ బటన్ (వీల్)తో లాగండి, లేదా O నొక్కి ఎడమ బటన్‌తో లాగండి. ప్యాన్: కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగండి, లేదా Shift పట్టుకుని మధ్య బటన్‌తో లాగండి. జూమ్: వీల్ తిప్పండి. అది పాయింటర్ కింద ఉన్నదానిపైకి జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. అన్నీ చూడండి: Shift+Z నొక్కండి, లేదా విస్తీర్ణం పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్ లేదా టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌పై సంజ్ఞలు భిన్నంగా ఉంటాయి. ఆర్బిట్, ప్యాన్ మరియు జూమ్ చూడండి. అన్‌డూ Ctrl+Z నొక్కి అన్‌డూ చేయండి మరియు Ctrl+Y నొక్కి మళ్ళీ చేయండి, లేదా టూల్‌బార్‌లోని చర్య రద్దు మరియు మళ్ళీ చేయి ఉపయోగించండి. మోడల్ తెరిచి ఉన్నంత వరకు ModelCraft చివరి 200 దశలను గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది. చరిత్ర ఫైల్‌లో సేవ్ కాదు. మీ మోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయండి ఫైల్ గ్రూప్‌లో సేవ్ చేయి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా Ctrl+S నొక్కండి. బ్రౌజర్ మీ మోడల్ పేరు మీద ఒక ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు Untitled.modelcraft. స్టేటస్ బార్ అది ఎక్కడికి వెళ్ళిందో చెబుతుంది: \"Untitled.modelcraft మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లకు సేవ్ చేయబడింది.\" సేవ్ చేయడం డౌన్‌లోడ్. ప్రతి సేవ్ మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్‌లో కొత్త కాపీని చేస్తుంది; మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్‌ను ఓవర్‌రైట్ చేయదు. చక్కని ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫోల్డర్ కోసం, ఫైల్‌ను అక్కడికి మీరే తరలించండి లేదా పేరు మార్చండి. ఫైల్ తెరవడం, సేవ్ చేయడం, దిగుమతి మరియు ఎగుమతి ఎందుకు మరియు ఇంకా ఏమి చేయవచ్చో వివరిస్తుంది. ModelCraft తనంతట తానే సేవ్ చేయదు. సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులతో ట్యాబ్ మూసివేస్తే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (\"సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?\"). ఉండడానికి ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత సేవ్ చేయండి. సహాయం పొందండి F1 నొక్కండి లేదా యాప్ బార్‌లో సహాయం పై క్లిక్ చేసి ఈ సహాయాన్ని మోడల్ పక్కన తెరవండి. ఇది కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది. సహాయం మూసివేయడానికి మళ్ళీ F1 నొక్కండి, లేదా సహాయం లోపల Esc నొక్కండి. ఈ సహాయాన్ని ఉపయోగించడం చూడండి. తర్వాత ఎక్కడికి వెళ్ళాలి గీతలు, దీర్ఘచతురస్రాలు మరియు వృత్తాలతో ముఖాలు గీయండి ముఖాలను నెట్టి లాగి ఘనపదార్థాలుగా చేయండి విలువ పెట్టెలో ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలు టైప్ చేయండి ప్రతి కీ మరియు మౌస్ చర్య","lang":"te"},{"slug":"beginner","scope":"modelcraft","title":"ప్రారంభకుడు శైలి","summary":"3D మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించడానికి కొన్ని పెద్ద బటన్లు: ఆకారాలను వదలండి, రంధ్రాలు చేయండి, తరలించి అమర్చండి, రంధ్రాలు కత్తిరించేలా గ్రూప్ చేయండి, మరియు ఫలితాన్ని 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి.","section":"start","order":12,"keywords":["ప్రారంభకుడు","ప్రారంభకులు","సులభం","సరళం","మొదటి మోడల్","పిల్లలు","పాఠశాల","ఆకారాలు","బాక్స్","సిలిండర్","గోళం","కోన్","పైకప్పు","రంధ్రం","రంధ్రాలు","కత్తిరించు","తీసివేయి","అమర్చు","గ్రూప్","నేలపైకి దించు","మెనూ శైలి","రిబ్బన్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"ఆకారాలతో నిర్మించండి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"రంధ్రాలు చేయండి","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"వరుసగా అమర్చండి","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"ఇతర ట్యాబ్‌లు","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E3D కి కొత్తగా ఉన్నారా? \u003Cstrong\u003Eప్రారంభకుడు\u003C/strong\u003E మెనూ శైలి మొదటి ట్యాబ్‌లో కొన్ని పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతుంది, కాబట్టి ముఖాలు గీయడం మరియు వాటిని పైకి నెట్టడం నేర్చుకునే ముందే మీరు ఆకారాలు మరియు రంధ్రాలతో వస్తువులు నిర్మించవచ్చు. దీన్ని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రారంభకుడు\u003C/span\u003E లో ఎంచుకోండి. ఇతర ప్రతి శైలి ఉన్నట్లే ఉంటుంది; ఎప్పుడైనా తిరిగి మారవచ్చు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eఆకారాలతో నిర్మించండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమేక్\u003C/span\u003E ట్యాబ్‌లోని ఒక ఆకారంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eబాక్స్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసిలిండర్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగోళం\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకోన్\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపైకప్పు\u003C/span\u003E. ప్రతిదీ 20 mm వ్యాసంలో ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఅది ఎక్కడికి వెళ్లాలో క్లిక్ చేయండి: నేలపై, లేదా మరో ఆకారం యొక్క ముఖంపై (అది ఆ ముఖంపై నిలబడుతుంది). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ఆపుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఆకారాన్ని ఎంచుకోవడానికి దానిపై క్లిక్ చేసి, తర్వాత దాన్ని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతరలించు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతిప్పు\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eస్కేల్\u003C/span\u003E చేయండి. ఖచ్చితంగా ఉండేందుకు తరలించేటప్పుడు లేదా స్కేల్ చేసేటప్పుడు పరిమాణం టైప్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eరంధ్రాలు చేయండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరంధ్రం అంటే తాను గ్రూప్ చేయబడిన ఆకారాలలోకి కోసే ఆకారం.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపెట్టె రంధ్రం\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగుండ్రని రంధ్రం\u003C/span\u003E ను వదలండి (అవి బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటాయి), లేదా ఏదైనా ఆకారాన్ని ఎంచుకుని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదీన్ని రంధ్రంగా చేయి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదీన్ని ఘనంగా చేయి\u003C/span\u003E రంధ్రాన్ని తిరిగి ఆకారంగా మారుస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eరంధ్రం ఎక్కడ కోయాలో అక్కడ ఉంచండి: ఒక ఆకారం గుండా, లేదా దానిలో పాక్షికంగా.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఆకారాలను మరియు రంధ్రాలను ఎంచుకోండి (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+క్లిక్ ఒకటి జోడిస్తుంది), తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. రంధ్రాలు కత్తిరించబడతాయి మరియు ఫలితం ఒకే భాగం అవుతుంది. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E దాన్ని మళ్ళీ విడదీస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఎంపికలో రంధ్రం లేకపోతే, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్\u003C/span\u003E ఒక సాధారణ గ్రూప్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eవరుసగా అమర్చండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరెండు లేదా ఎక్కువ ఆకారాలను ఎంచుకుని, అమర్చు బటన్లను ఉపయోగించండి: వాటి బాణాలు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎడమకు అమర్చు\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమధ్యలు అమర్చు (ఎరుపు)\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకుడికి అమర్చు\u003C/span\u003E ను జాబితా చేస్తాయి, అలాగే ఆకుపచ్చ (ముందు, మధ్య, వెనుక) మరియు నీలం (అడుగులు, మధ్యలు, పైభాగాలు) అక్షాల వెంట కూడా అదే. అవి మొత్తం ఎంపిక యొక్క బయటి భాగంతో అమరతాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనేలపైకి దించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకున్న ప్రతి ఆకారాన్ని తిరిగి నేలపై ఉంచుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమేక్\u003C/span\u003E ట్యాబ్‌లోని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E మోడల్‌ను తనిఖీ చేసి, మీ ప్రింటర్ కోసం డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను సిద్ధం చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\"\u003Eసపోర్ట్‌లు, గోడ మందం, బోలుగా చేయడం మరియు మరమ్మతు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eఇతర ట్యాబ్‌లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవీక్షణ\u003C/span\u003E కెమెరాను కలిగి ఉంటుంది: ఆర్బిట్, ప్యాన్, జూమ్, అన్నింటికీ జూమ్, మరియు ప్రామాణిక వీక్షణలు. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమరిన్ని టూల్స్\u003C/span\u003E ఇతర అన్ని ModelCraft టూల్స్‌ను చిన్నవిగా కలిగి ఉంటుంది. ఈ శైలితో \u003Cstrong\u003Eకుడి\u003C/strong\u003E మౌస్ బటన్ ఆర్బిట్ చేస్తుంది మరియు \u003Cstrong\u003Eమధ్య\u003C/strong\u003E బటన్ ప్యాన్ చేస్తుంది; వీల్ జూమ్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీ స్వంత మొదటి భాగాన్ని గీయడంలో ModelCraft మీకు మార్గనిర్దేశం చేయాలనుకుంటున్నారా? బదులుగా \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/getting-started#let-modelcraft-guide-your-first-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/getting-started\" data-hash=\"let-modelcraft-guide-your-first-model\"\u003Eమార్గదర్శక మొదటి మోడల్\u003C/a\u003E ఉపయోగించండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eసంబంధితం: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003Eమెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Eగ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"3D కి కొత్తగా ఉన్నారా? ప్రారంభకుడు మెనూ శైలి మొదటి ట్యాబ్‌లో కొన్ని పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతుంది, కాబట్టి ముఖాలు గీయడం మరియు వాటిని పైకి నెట్టడం నేర్చుకునే ముందే మీరు ఆకారాలు మరియు రంధ్రాలతో వస్తువులు నిర్మించవచ్చు. దీన్ని సెట్టింగ్‌లు (Ctrl+,) › ModelCraft › మెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్ › ప్రారంభకుడు లో ఎంచుకోండి. ఇతర ప్రతి శైలి ఉన్నట్లే ఉంటుంది; ఎప్పుడైనా తిరిగి మారవచ్చు. ఆకారాలతో నిర్మించండి మేక్ ట్యాబ్‌లోని ఒక ఆకారంపై క్లిక్ చేయండి: బాక్స్, సిలిండర్, గోళం, కోన్ లేదా పైకప్పు. ప్రతిదీ 20 mm వ్యాసంలో ఉంటుంది. అది ఎక్కడికి వెళ్లాలో క్లిక్ చేయండి: నేలపై, లేదా మరో ఆకారం యొక్క ముఖంపై (అది ఆ ముఖంపై నిలబడుతుంది). Esc ఆపుతుంది. ఆకారాన్ని ఎంచుకోవడానికి దానిపై క్లిక్ చేసి, తర్వాత దాన్ని తరలించు, తిప్పు లేదా స్కేల్ చేయండి. ఖచ్చితంగా ఉండేందుకు తరలించేటప్పుడు లేదా స్కేల్ చేసేటప్పుడు పరిమాణం టైప్ చేయండి. రంధ్రాలు చేయండి రంధ్రం అంటే తాను గ్రూప్ చేయబడిన ఆకారాలలోకి కోసే ఆకారం. పెట్టె రంధ్రం లేదా గుండ్రని రంధ్రం ను వదలండి (అవి బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటాయి), లేదా ఏదైనా ఆకారాన్ని ఎంచుకుని దీన్ని రంధ్రంగా చేయి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. దీన్ని ఘనంగా చేయి రంధ్రాన్ని తిరిగి ఆకారంగా మారుస్తుంది. రంధ్రం ఎక్కడ కోయాలో అక్కడ ఉంచండి: ఒక ఆకారం గుండా, లేదా దానిలో పాక్షికంగా. ఆకారాలను మరియు రంధ్రాలను ఎంచుకోండి (Shift+క్లిక్ ఒకటి జోడిస్తుంది), తర్వాత గ్రూప్ పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. రంధ్రాలు కత్తిరించబడతాయి మరియు ఫలితం ఒకే భాగం అవుతుంది. Ctrl+Z దాన్ని మళ్ళీ విడదీస్తుంది. ఎంపికలో రంధ్రం లేకపోతే, గ్రూప్ ఒక సాధారణ గ్రూప్ చేస్తుంది. వరుసగా అమర్చండి రెండు లేదా ఎక్కువ ఆకారాలను ఎంచుకుని, అమర్చు బటన్లను ఉపయోగించండి: వాటి బాణాలు ఎడమకు అమర్చు / మధ్యలు అమర్చు (ఎరుపు) / కుడికి అమర్చు ను జాబితా చేస్తాయి, అలాగే ఆకుపచ్చ (ముందు, మధ్య, వెనుక) మరియు నీలం (అడుగులు, మధ్యలు, పైభాగాలు) అక్షాల వెంట కూడా అదే. అవి మొత్తం ఎంపిక యొక్క బయటి భాగంతో అమరతాయి. నేలపైకి దించు ఎంచుకున్న ప్రతి ఆకారాన్ని తిరిగి నేలపై ఉంచుతుంది. ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ చేయండి మేక్ ట్యాబ్‌లోని 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)… మోడల్‌ను తనిఖీ చేసి, మీ ప్రింటర్ కోసం డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది. చూడండి 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను సిద్ధం చేయడం మరియు సపోర్ట్‌లు, గోడ మందం, బోలుగా చేయడం మరియు మరమ్మతు. ఇతర ట్యాబ్‌లు వీక్షణ కెమెరాను కలిగి ఉంటుంది: ఆర్బిట్, ప్యాన్, జూమ్, అన్నింటికీ జూమ్, మరియు ప్రామాణిక వీక్షణలు. మరిన్ని టూల్స్ ఇతర అన్ని ModelCraft టూల్స్‌ను చిన్నవిగా కలిగి ఉంటుంది. ఈ శైలితో కుడి మౌస్ బటన్ ఆర్బిట్ చేస్తుంది మరియు మధ్య బటన్ ప్యాన్ చేస్తుంది; వీల్ జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. మీ స్వంత మొదటి భాగాన్ని గీయడంలో ModelCraft మీకు మార్గనిర్దేశం చేయాలనుకుంటున్నారా? బదులుగా మార్గదర్శక మొదటి మోడల్ ఉపయోగించండి. సంబంధితం: మెనూలు, కీలు మరియు మౌస్ · గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లు","lang":"te"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"modelcraft","title":"ModelCraft స్క్రీన్","summary":"టూల్‌బార్, ప్రతి వైపు ఉన్న ప్యానెల్‌లు, 3D వీక్షణ మరియు స్టేటస్ బార్ యొక్క పర్యటన, వాటి తెర పేర్లతో.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["స్క్రీన్","లేఅవుట్","టూల్‌బార్","ప్యానెల్‌లు","అవుట్‌లైనర్","ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం","శైలి","దృశ్యాలు","స్టేటస్ బార్","విలువ పెట్టె","యూనిట్లు","ఇంటర్‌ఫేస్","పర్యటన"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"యాప్ బార్","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"టూల్‌బార్","level":2},{"id":"3d","text":"3D వీక్షణ","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌లు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"కుడి ప్యానెల్‌లు","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"స్టేటస్ బార్","level":2},{"id":"section-6","text":"చిన్న స్క్రీన్‌పై","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eపైనుండి కిందికి: యాప్ బార్, టూల్‌బార్, మూడు కాలమ్‌లు (ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌లు, 3D వీక్షణ, కుడి ప్యానెల్‌లు) మరియు స్టేటస్ బార్.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eయాప్ బార్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఅత్యంత పైన ఉన్న సన్నని బార్ ప్రతి CadCraft యాప్‌కు చెందుతుంది. దానిలో యాప్ పేరు మరియు వెర్షన్, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft గురించి\u003C/span\u003E, స్క్రీన్-మోడ్ బటన్ మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాగితం\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమోడల్\u003C/span\u003E స్విచ్ ఉన్నాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాగితం\u003C/span\u003E తేలికపాటి థీమ్, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమోడల్\u003C/span\u003E చీకటి థీమ్. 3D వీక్షణ దాని రంగులను సరిపోల్చుతుంది; మీ మోడల్ మారదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eస్క్రీన్-మోడ్ బటన్ (మొదట \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపూర్తి స్క్రీన్\u003C/span\u003E అని చదువుతుంది) మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మూడు మోడ్‌ల ద్వారా తిరుగుతాయి: సాధారణం, పూర్తి స్క్రీన్, మరియు బార్‌లు దాచిన పూర్తి స్క్రీన్. బార్‌లు దాచినప్పుడు, వాటిని తిరిగి తెచ్చుకోవడానికి స్క్రీన్ పైఅంచుకు పాయింటర్ పెట్టండి (లేదా తాకండి). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ నుండి బయటకు వస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో Control కీని ఉపయోగిస్తుంది, Mac లో కూడా. iPhone లో, బ్రౌజర్‌లకు పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ లేనందున, ఈ బటన్ కేవలం బార్‌లను దాచి చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eటూల్‌బార్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eటూల్‌బార్ లేబుల్ గల గ్రూప్‌లుగా విభజించబడింది. బటన్‌పై హోవర్ చేస్తే దాని పేరు మరియు కీ కనిపిస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఎడమవైపున, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E జాబితా టూల్‌బార్ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెల్ దేనికోసమో మార్చుతుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమోడల్\u003C/span\u003E (నెట్టు/లాగు మోడలింగ్, క్రింది పర్యటన), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమెష్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003Eమెష్‌ను సవరించు\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవక్రరేఖ\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/curves\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/curves\"\u003Eకర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUV\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/uv\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/uv\"\u003EUVs ను అమర్చు\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eశిల్పం\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sculpt\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sculpt\"\u003Eశిల్పం\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eషేడింగ్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\"\u003Eనోడ్‌లతో చేసిన మెటీరియల్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eయానిమేట్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\"\u003Eయానిమేట్\u003C/a\u003E) మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరెండర్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#render-in-the-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"render-in-the-view\"\u003Eవీక్షణలో రెండర్\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eగ్రూప్\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eబటన్లు\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్తది\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతెరువు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E (ఫార్మాట్‌ల మెనూ)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eగీయి\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరేఖ\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదీర్ఘచతురస్రం\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవృత్తం\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eబహుభుజి\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eసవరించు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంచుకో\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనెట్టు/లాగు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతరలించు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతిప్పు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eస్కేల్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎరేజర్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటేప్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eనిర్వహించు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపోనెంట్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిడదీయి\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eమార్పు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచర్య రద్దు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమళ్ళీ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాపీ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅతికించు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమరిన్ని\u003C/span\u003E (ఒక మెనూ: కట్, కాపీ, అతికించు, ఉన్న చోటే అతికించు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, నకలు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, తొలగించు, అన్నీ ఎంచుకో, ఏదీ ఎంచుకోవద్దు, మరియు కీ శైలి)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకెమెరా\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆర్బిట్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యాన్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eజూమ్\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిస్తీర్ణం\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవీక్షణలు\u003C/span\u003E (ఒక మెనూ)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eచూపిన కీలు నెట్టు/లాగు కీలు; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌ల\u003C/a\u003Eలో మోడెలర్ శైలి ఎంచుకుంటే, టూల్‌టిప్‌లు బదులుగా ఆ కీలను చూపిస్తాయి. మీరు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న టూల్ నొక్కినట్లుగా చూపబడుతుంది. ఇరుకైన స్క్రీన్‌పై టూల్‌బార్ పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అవుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవీక్షణలు\u003C/span\u003E మెనూలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఐసో\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపైనుండి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eముందు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకుడి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవెనుక\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎడమ\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకిందినుండి\u003C/span\u003E ఉన్నాయి, ప్రతి ఒక్కటి మోడల్‌కు సరిపోయేలా అమర్చబడి, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపర్స్‌పెక్టివ్\u003C/span\u003E, ఇది పర్స్‌పెక్టివ్ మరియు సమాంతర ప్రొజెక్షన్ మధ్య మారుస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d\"\u003E3D వీక్షణ\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eస్క్రీన్ మధ్యభాగం. ఎరుపు, ఆకుపచ్చ మరియు నీలం గీతలు అక్షాలు: ఎరుపు మరియు ఆకుపచ్చ నేలపై ఉంటాయి, నీలం పైకి చూపిస్తుంది, మరియు \u003Cstrong\u003Eఉత్తరం ఆకుపచ్చ అక్షం\u003C/strong\u003E. అవి కలిసే చోటు మూలం.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీరు ప్రారంభించినప్పుడు, &quot;మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు&quot; అనే శీర్షిక గల కార్డ్ మొదటి కొన్ని కీలను జాబితా చేస్తుంది. మీరు గీయే టూల్ ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు లేదా వీక్షణలో క్లిక్ చేసినప్పుడు అది వెళ్ళిపోతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eటూల్ పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు, వీక్షణ \u003Cstrong\u003Eఇన్‌ఫరెన్స్ మార్కర్లు\u003C/strong\u003E చూపిస్తుంది: పాయింటర్ స్నాప్ అయ్యే చిన్న రంగు ఆకారాలు (ఎండ్‌పాయింట్ వద్ద ఆకుపచ్చ వృత్తం, మధ్యబిందువు వద్ద సియాన్, అంచుపై ఎరుపు చతురస్రం, ముఖంపై నీలం వజ్రం). \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003Eఇన్‌ఫరెన్స్ మరియు స్నాపింగ్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఎంచుకున్న వస్తువులు నీలంగా మారతాయి. వెనుక నుండి చూసిన ముఖం నీలం-బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీరు సవరించడానికి ఒక గ్రూప్ తెరిచినప్పుడు, దాని వెలుపల ఉన్నవన్నీ మసకబారతాయి, మరియు అక్షాలు గ్రూప్ స్వంత అక్షాలకు మారతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమోడల్‌కు దృశ్యాలు ఉన్నప్పుడు, వీక్షణ పైభాగంలో దృశ్య ట్యాబ్‌ల వరుస కనిపిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eతెరవడానికి లేదా దిగుమతి చేయడానికి వీక్షణపై ఎక్కడైనా ఫైల్‌ను వదలండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅవుట్‌లైనర్\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E మోడల్‌లోని గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లను, మోడల్‌లో ఎలా గూడుగా ఉన్నాయో అలాగే జాబితా చేస్తుంది. ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువసార్లు ఉపయోగించిన కాంపోనెంట్ ఎన్నిసార్లు అని చూపిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు &quot;Window (4)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపేరు క్లిక్ చేస్తే ఎంచుకుంటుంది. ఎడిట్ చేయడానికి తెరవడానికి డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eపేరుతో వెతకండి\u003C/strong\u003E (రెండు లేదా ఎక్కువ గ్రూప్‌లు ఉన్నప్పుడు చూపబడుతుంది) పేరు సరిపోయే వరుసలను మాత్రమే ఉంచుతుంది, వాటిలో ఉన్న గ్రూప్‌లతో. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E దాన్ని క్లియర్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్రతి వరుస చివర ఉన్న \u003Cstrong\u003Eకన్ను\u003C/strong\u003E ఆ గ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్‌ను దాచుతుంది లేదా చూపిస్తుంది (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసవరించు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమరిన్ని\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదాచు\u003C/span\u003E చేసినట్లే). ట్యాగ్ ఐకాన్ అంటే అది దాని \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003Eట్యాగ్\u003C/a\u003E వల్ల దాచబడింది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఅక్కడికక్కడే పేరు మార్చండి:\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక వరుసపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, తర్వాత \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, టైప్ చేయండి, మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పాత పేరును ఉంచుతుంది).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవరుసను లాగండి\u003C/strong\u003E మరో గ్రూప్ వరుస మధ్యలోకి దాన్ని అందులోకి తరలించడానికి, లేదా ఒక వరుస పై లేదా కింది అంచుపైకి దాన్ని ఆ వరుసకు ముందు లేదా తర్వాత ఉంచడానికి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅవుట్‌లైనర్\u003C/span\u003E శీర్షికపైకి దాన్ని పై స్థాయికి తరలించడానికి. అది మోడల్‌లో తన చోటులోనే ఉంటుంది, మరియు తన రంగు, ట్యాగ్ మరియు పేరును ఉంచుతుంది. ఒక గ్రూప్ తనలోనే వెళ్లలేదు. ప్రతి తరలింపు ఒక అన్‌డూ దశ. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components#rearrange-in-the-outliner\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\" data-hash=\"rearrange-in-the-outliner\"\u003Eఅవుట్‌లైనర్‌లో మళ్ళీ అమర్చడం\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eగ్రూప్ లోపల సవరిస్తున్నప్పుడు, &quot;సవరిస్తున్నారు: మోడల్ › …&quot; అని ఒక లైన్ చదువుతుంది, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్ మూసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E బటన్ కనిపిస్తుంది. అది ఒకేసారి తెరిచి ఉన్న ప్రతి స్థాయిని మూసివేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపోనెంట్లు\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E ప్రతి కాంపోనెంట్ నిర్వచనాన్ని, ఎన్ని కాపీలు ఉంచబడ్డాయో (&quot;3×&quot;) మరియు మీరు క్లిక్ చేసిన చోట మరొక కాపీని జోడించే \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E బటన్‌ను జాబితా చేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eకుడి ప్యానెల్‌లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎంటిటీ సమాచారం\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E ఎంచుకున్నది వివరిస్తుంది. దాని శీర్షిక అది ఏమిటో చెప్పడానికి మారుతుంది:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eశీర్షిక\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eచూపేది\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eబటన్లు\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eమోడల్ (ఏదీ ఎంచుకోలేదు)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eముఖాలు, అంచులు, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు ఎన్ని ఉంచబడ్డాయి, గైడ్‌లు, మొత్తం పరిమాణం\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅన్ని గైడ్‌లు తొలగించు\u003C/span\u003E (గైడ్‌లు ఉన్నప్పుడు)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eముఖం\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eవైశాల్యం, రంధ్రాల సంఖ్య\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eముఖాన్ని తిరగేయి\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅంచు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eపొడవు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఏదీ లేదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eగ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eపేరు (పేరు మార్చడానికి టైప్ చేయండి), ఎన్నిసార్లు ఉంచబడింది, అది మూసిన ఘనమో కాదో, దాని ఘనపరిమాణం, దాని పరిమాణం\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసవరించు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిడదీయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రత్యేకంగా చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువసార్లు ఉంచిన కాంపోనెంట్), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపోనెంట్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (ఒక గ్రూప్)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eగైడ్ పాయింట్ లేదా గైడ్ లైన్\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఏమీ లేదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతొలగించు\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅనేక అంశాలు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఎన్ని ముఖాలు, అంచులు, గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు గైడ్‌లు, ముఖాల మొత్తం వైశాల్యం\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపోనెంట్ చేయి (G)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్ చేయి (Shift+G)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eశైలి\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E మోడల్ ఎలా గీయబడుతుందో సెట్ చేస్తుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eముఖాలు\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eషేడెడ్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదాచిన రేఖ\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవైర్‌ఫ్రేమ్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎక్స్-రే\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅంచులు చూపించు\u003C/span\u003E, మరియు సూర్యుని తేదీ, సమయం మరియు స్థానంతో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసూర్య నీడలు\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\"\u003Eశైలులు మరియు సూర్య నీడలు\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదృశ్యాలు\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E వీక్షణలను సేవ్ చేస్తుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదృశ్యం జోడించు\u003C/span\u003E, దృశ్యాల జాబితా, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమునుపటిది\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్లే\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతదుపరిది\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Eవీక్షణలను దృశ్యాలుగా సేవ్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eస్టేటస్ బార్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eదిగువన, ఎడమ నుండి కుడికి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eచిట్కా\u003C/strong\u003E: టూల్ తదుపరి ఏమి కోరుకుంటుందో, లేదా పాయింటర్ ఏ దానికి స్నాప్ అవుతోందో (&quot;ఎండ్‌పాయింట్&quot;, &quot;ఎరుపు అక్షంపై&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eసందేశాలు\u003C/strong\u003E: ఇప్పుడే ఏమి జరిగింది (&quot;నెట్టు/లాగు రద్దు చేయబడింది.&quot;, &quot;site.dxf దిగుమతి చేయబడింది: నేలపై 1697 అంచులు, ఒక గ్రూప్‌లో.&quot;) లేదా ఏదైనా ఎందుకు పని చేయలేదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవిలువ పెట్టె\u003C/strong\u003E: దాని లేబుల్ టూల్‌తో మారుతుంది (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపొడవు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపరిమాణాలు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవ్యాసార్ధం\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eభుజాలు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదూరం\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకోణం\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eస్కేల్\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌సెట్\u003C/span\u003E). మీరు పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు ప్రత్యక్ష పరిమాణం చూపిస్తుంది, మరియు మీరు టైప్ చేసేది తీసుకుంటుంది. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003Eవిలువ పెట్టెలో ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలు టైప్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eకీ శైలి\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకీలు: నెట్టు-లాగు\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకీలు: మోడెలర్\u003C/span\u003E కీబోర్డ్ షార్ట్‌కట్‌లను మరియు మౌస్ బటన్లను ఒకేసారి మారుస్తుంది (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌ల\u003C/a\u003Eలో ఉన్నట్లే).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eస్నాప్\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఢీకొనడం\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E: పాయింట్లు, అంచులు మరియు అక్షాలకు స్నాపింగ్, మరియు ఘనపదార్థాలు తాకిన చోట ఆగడం. దాటి గీసినది = ఆఫ్. మారడానికి క్లిక్ చేయండి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eయూనిట్ల ఎంపిక\u003C/strong\u003E: పొడవులు చూపబడే యూనిట్. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/getting-started#check-the-units-first\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/getting-started\" data-hash=\"check-the-units-first\"\u003Eప్రారంభించడం\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eచిన్న స్క్రీన్‌పై\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eసుమారు 760 పిక్సెల్‌ల కంటే ఇరుకైన స్క్రీన్ (ఫోన్, లేదా ఇరుకైన విండో) కింద, 3D వీక్షణ స్క్రీన్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. టూల్‌బార్ పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అయ్యే ఒకే వరుస, మరియు ప్యానెల్‌లు స్క్రీన్ దిగువన ఉన్న బార్ నుండి షీట్‌లుగా తెరుచుకుంటాయి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"పైనుండి కిందికి: యాప్ బార్, టూల్‌బార్, మూడు కాలమ్‌లు (ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌లు, 3D వీక్షణ, కుడి ప్యానెల్‌లు) మరియు స్టేటస్ బార్. యాప్ బార్ అత్యంత పైన ఉన్న సన్నని బార్ ప్రతి CadCraft యాప్‌కు చెందుతుంది. దానిలో యాప్ పేరు మరియు వెర్షన్, ModelCraft గురించి, స్క్రీన్-మోడ్ బటన్ మరియు కాగితం / మోడల్ స్విచ్ ఉన్నాయి. కాగితం తేలికపాటి థీమ్, మోడల్ చీకటి థీమ్. 3D వీక్షణ దాని రంగులను సరిపోల్చుతుంది; మీ మోడల్ మారదు. స్క్రీన్-మోడ్ బటన్ (మొదట పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ అని చదువుతుంది) మరియు Ctrl+Shift+F మూడు మోడ్‌ల ద్వారా తిరుగుతాయి: సాధారణం, పూర్తి స్క్రీన్, మరియు బార్‌లు దాచిన పూర్తి స్క్రీన్. బార్‌లు దాచినప్పుడు, వాటిని తిరిగి తెచ్చుకోవడానికి స్క్రీన్ పైఅంచుకు పాయింటర్ పెట్టండి (లేదా తాకండి). Esc పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ నుండి బయటకు వస్తుంది. Ctrl+Shift+F ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో Control కీని ఉపయోగిస్తుంది, Mac లో కూడా. iPhone లో, బ్రౌజర్‌లకు పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ లేనందున, ఈ బటన్ కేవలం బార్‌లను దాచి చూపిస్తుంది. టూల్‌బార్ టూల్‌బార్ లేబుల్ గల గ్రూప్‌లుగా విభజించబడింది. బటన్‌పై హోవర్ చేస్తే దాని పేరు మరియు కీ కనిపిస్తాయి. ఎడమవైపున, వర్క్‌స్పేస్ జాబితా టూల్‌బార్ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెల్ దేనికోసమో మార్చుతుంది: మోడల్ (నెట్టు/లాగు మోడలింగ్, క్రింది పర్యటన), మెష్ (మెష్‌ను సవరించు), వక్రరేఖ (కర్వ్ ఆబ్జెక్ట్‌లు), UV (UVs ను అమర్చు), శిల్పం (శిల్పం), షేడింగ్ (నోడ్‌లతో చేసిన మెటీరియల్‌లు), యానిమేట్ (యానిమేట్) మరియు రెండర్ (వీక్షణలో రెండర్). గ్రూప్ బటన్లు ఫైల్ కొత్తది, తెరువు, సేవ్ చేయి, దిగుమతి, ఎగుమతి (ఫార్మాట్‌ల మెనూ) గీయి రేఖ (L), దీర్ఘచతురస్రం (R), వృత్తం (C), బహుభుజి (N) సవరించు ఎంచుకో (Space), నెట్టు/లాగు (P), తరలించు (M), తిప్పు (Q), స్కేల్ (S), ఎరేజర్ (E), టేప్ (T) నిర్వహించు కాంపోనెంట్ (G), గ్రూప్ (Shift+G), విడదీయి మార్పు చర్య రద్దు (Ctrl+Z), మళ్ళీ చేయి (Ctrl+Y), కాపీ చేయి (Ctrl+C), అతికించు (Ctrl+V), మరిన్ని (ఒక మెనూ: కట్, కాపీ, అతికించు, ఉన్న చోటే అతికించు Ctrl+Shift+V, నకలు Ctrl+D, తొలగించు, అన్నీ ఎంచుకో, ఏదీ ఎంచుకోవద్దు, మరియు కీ శైలి) కెమెరా ఆర్బిట్ (O), ప్యాన్ (H), జూమ్ (Z), విస్తీర్ణం (Shift+Z), వీక్షణలు (ఒక మెనూ) చూపిన కీలు నెట్టు/లాగు కీలు; ModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌లలో మోడెలర్ శైలి ఎంచుకుంటే, టూల్‌టిప్‌లు బదులుగా ఆ కీలను చూపిస్తాయి. మీరు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న టూల్ నొక్కినట్లుగా చూపబడుతుంది. ఇరుకైన స్క్రీన్‌పై టూల్‌బార్ పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అవుతుంది. వీక్షణలు మెనూలో ఐసో, పైనుండి, ముందు, కుడి, వెనుక, ఎడమ మరియు కిందినుండి ఉన్నాయి, ప్రతి ఒక్కటి మోడల్‌కు సరిపోయేలా అమర్చబడి, మరియు పర్స్‌పెక్టివ్, ఇది పర్స్‌పెక్టివ్ మరియు సమాంతర ప్రొజెక్షన్ మధ్య మారుస్తుంది. 3D వీక్షణ స్క్రీన్ మధ్యభాగం. ఎరుపు, ఆకుపచ్చ మరియు నీలం గీతలు అక్షాలు: ఎరుపు మరియు ఆకుపచ్చ నేలపై ఉంటాయి, నీలం పైకి చూపిస్తుంది, మరియు ఉత్తరం ఆకుపచ్చ అక్షం. అవి కలిసే చోటు మూలం. మీరు ప్రారంభించినప్పుడు, \"మోడలింగ్ ప్రారంభించు\" అనే శీర్షిక గల కార్డ్ మొదటి కొన్ని కీలను జాబితా చేస్తుంది. మీరు గీయే టూల్ ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు లేదా వీక్షణలో క్లిక్ చేసినప్పుడు అది వెళ్ళిపోతుంది. టూల్ పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు, వీక్షణ ఇన్‌ఫరెన్స్ మార్కర్లు చూపిస్తుంది: పాయింటర్ స్నాప్ అయ్యే చిన్న రంగు ఆకారాలు (ఎండ్‌పాయింట్ వద్ద ఆకుపచ్చ వృత్తం, మధ్యబిందువు వద్ద సియాన్, అంచుపై ఎరుపు చతురస్రం, ముఖంపై నీలం వజ్రం). ఇన్‌ఫరెన్స్ మరియు స్నాపింగ్ చూడండి. ఎంచుకున్న వస్తువులు నీలంగా మారతాయి. వెనుక నుండి చూసిన ముఖం నీలం-బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది. మీరు సవరించడానికి ఒక గ్రూప్ తెరిచినప్పుడు, దాని వెలుపల ఉన్నవన్నీ మసకబారతాయి, మరియు అక్షాలు గ్రూప్ స్వంత అక్షాలకు మారతాయి. మోడల్‌కు దృశ్యాలు ఉన్నప్పుడు, వీక్షణ పైభాగంలో దృశ్య ట్యాబ్‌ల వరుస కనిపిస్తుంది. తెరవడానికి లేదా దిగుమతి చేయడానికి వీక్షణపై ఎక్కడైనా ఫైల్‌ను వదలండి. ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌లు అవుట్‌లైనర్ మోడల్‌లోని గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు కాంపోనెంట్లను, మోడల్‌లో ఎలా గూడుగా ఉన్నాయో అలాగే జాబితా చేస్తుంది. ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువసార్లు ఉపయోగించిన కాంపోనెంట్ ఎన్నిసార్లు అని చూపిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు \"Window (4)\". పేరు క్లిక్ చేస్తే ఎంచుకుంటుంది. ఎడిట్ చేయడానికి తెరవడానికి డబల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. పేరుతో వెతకండి (రెండు లేదా ఎక్కువ గ్రూప్‌లు ఉన్నప్పుడు చూపబడుతుంది) పేరు సరిపోయే వరుసలను మాత్రమే ఉంచుతుంది, వాటిలో ఉన్న గ్రూప్‌లతో. Esc దాన్ని క్లియర్ చేస్తుంది. ప్రతి వరుస చివర ఉన్న కన్ను ఆ గ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్‌ను దాచుతుంది లేదా చూపిస్తుంది (సవరించు › మరిన్ని › దాచు చేసినట్లే). ట్యాగ్ ఐకాన్ అంటే అది దాని ట్యాగ్ వల్ల దాచబడింది. అక్కడికక్కడే పేరు మార్చండి: ఒక వరుసపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, తర్వాత F2 నొక్కండి, టైప్ చేయండి, మరియు Enter నొక్కండి (Esc పాత పేరును ఉంచుతుంది). వరుసను లాగండి మరో గ్రూప్ వరుస మధ్యలోకి దాన్ని అందులోకి తరలించడానికి, లేదా ఒక వరుస పై లేదా కింది అంచుపైకి దాన్ని ఆ వరుసకు ముందు లేదా తర్వాత ఉంచడానికి, లేదా అవుట్‌లైనర్ శీర్షికపైకి దాన్ని పై స్థాయికి తరలించడానికి. అది మోడల్‌లో తన చోటులోనే ఉంటుంది, మరియు తన రంగు, ట్యాగ్ మరియు పేరును ఉంచుతుంది. ఒక గ్రూప్ తనలోనే వెళ్లలేదు. ప్రతి తరలింపు ఒక అన్‌డూ దశ. అవుట్‌లైనర్‌లో మళ్ళీ అమర్చడం చూడండి. గ్రూప్ లోపల సవరిస్తున్నప్పుడు, \"సవరిస్తున్నారు: మోడల్ › …\" అని ఒక లైన్ చదువుతుంది, మరియు గ్రూప్ మూసివేయి బటన్ కనిపిస్తుంది. అది ఒకేసారి తెరిచి ఉన్న ప్రతి స్థాయిని మూసివేస్తుంది. కాంపోనెంట్లు ప్రతి కాంపోనెంట్ నిర్వచనాన్ని, ఎన్ని కాపీలు ఉంచబడ్డాయో (\"3×\") మరియు మీరు క్లిక్ చేసిన చోట మరొక కాపీని జోడించే ఉంచు బటన్‌ను జాబితా చేస్తుంది. కుడి ప్యానెల్‌లు ఎంటిటీ సమాచారం ఎంచుకున్నది వివరిస్తుంది. దాని శీర్షిక అది ఏమిటో చెప్పడానికి మారుతుంది: శీర్షిక చూపేది బటన్లు మోడల్ (ఏదీ ఎంచుకోలేదు) ముఖాలు, అంచులు, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు ఎన్ని ఉంచబడ్డాయి, గైడ్‌లు, మొత్తం పరిమాణం అన్ని గైడ్‌లు తొలగించు (గైడ్‌లు ఉన్నప్పుడు) ముఖం వైశాల్యం, రంధ్రాల సంఖ్య ముఖాన్ని తిరగేయి అంచు పొడవు ఏదీ లేదు గ్రూప్ లేదా కాంపోనెంట్ పేరు (పేరు మార్చడానికి టైప్ చేయండి), ఎన్నిసార్లు ఉంచబడింది, అది మూసిన ఘనమో కాదో, దాని ఘనపరిమాణం, దాని పరిమాణం సవరించు, విడదీయి, ప్రత్యేకంగా చేయి (ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువసార్లు ఉంచిన కాంపోనెంట్), కాంపోనెంట్ చేయి (ఒక గ్రూప్) గైడ్ పాయింట్ లేదా గైడ్ లైన్ ఏమీ లేదు తొలగించు అనేక అంశాలు ఎన్ని ముఖాలు, అంచులు, గ్రూప్‌లు మరియు గైడ్‌లు, ముఖాల మొత్తం వైశాల్యం కాంపోనెంట్ చేయి (G), గ్రూప్ చేయి (Shift+G) శైలి మోడల్ ఎలా గీయబడుతుందో సెట్ చేస్తుంది: ముఖాలు (షేడెడ్, దాచిన రేఖ, వైర్‌ఫ్రేమ్, ఎక్స్-రే), అంచులు చూపించు, మరియు సూర్యుని తేదీ, సమయం మరియు స్థానంతో సూర్య నీడలు. శైలులు మరియు సూర్య నీడలు చూడండి. దృశ్యాలు వీక్షణలను సేవ్ చేస్తుంది: దృశ్యం జోడించు, దృశ్యాల జాబితా, మరియు మునుపటిది, ప్లే మరియు తదుపరిది. వీక్షణలను దృశ్యాలుగా సేవ్ చేయడం చూడండి. స్టేటస్ బార్ దిగువన, ఎడమ నుండి కుడికి: చిట్కా: టూల్ తదుపరి ఏమి కోరుకుంటుందో, లేదా పాయింటర్ ఏ దానికి స్నాప్ అవుతోందో (\"ఎండ్‌పాయింట్\", \"ఎరుపు అక్షంపై\"). సందేశాలు: ఇప్పుడే ఏమి జరిగింది (\"నెట్టు/లాగు రద్దు చేయబడింది.\", \"site.dxf దిగుమతి చేయబడింది: నేలపై 1697 అంచులు, ఒక గ్రూప్‌లో.\") లేదా ఏదైనా ఎందుకు పని చేయలేదు. విలువ పెట్టె: దాని లేబుల్ టూల్‌తో మారుతుంది (పొడవు, పరిమాణాలు, వ్యాసార్ధం, భుజాలు, దూరం, కోణం, స్కేల్, ఆఫ్‌సెట్). మీరు పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు ప్రత్యక్ష పరిమాణం చూపిస్తుంది, మరియు మీరు టైప్ చేసేది తీసుకుంటుంది. విలువ పెట్టెలో ఖచ్చితమైన పరిమాణాలు టైప్ చేయడం చూడండి. కీ శైలి: కీలు: నెట్టు-లాగు లేదా కీలు: మోడెలర్ కీబోర్డ్ షార్ట్‌కట్‌లను మరియు మౌస్ బటన్లను ఒకేసారి మారుస్తుంది (ModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌లలో ఉన్నట్లే). స్నాప్ మరియు ఢీకొనడం: పాయింట్లు, అంచులు మరియు అక్షాలకు స్నాపింగ్, మరియు ఘనపదార్థాలు తాకిన చోట ఆగడం. దాటి గీసినది = ఆఫ్. మారడానికి క్లిక్ చేయండి. ModelCraft సెట్టింగ్‌లు చూడండి. యూనిట్ల ఎంపిక: పొడవులు చూపబడే యూనిట్. ప్రారంభించడం చూడండి. చిన్న స్క్రీన్‌పై సుమారు 760 పిక్సెల్‌ల కంటే ఇరుకైన స్క్రీన్ (ఫోన్, లేదా ఇరుకైన విండో) కింద, 3D వీక్షణ స్క్రీన్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. టూల్‌బార్ పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అయ్యే ఒకే వరుస, మరియు ప్యానెల్‌లు స్క్రీన్ దిగువన ఉన్న బార్ నుండి షీట్‌లుగా తెరుచుకుంటాయి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"files","scope":"modelcraft","title":"తెరువు, సేవ్, దిగుమతి మరియు ఎగుమతి","summary":"ModelCraft ఫైల్, మీరు తీసుకురాగల మరియు పంపగల వాటిని, ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయో, మరియు ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో మరియు బ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య ఏమి మారుతుందో.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["తెరువు","సేవ్","దిగుమతి","ఎగుమతి","ఫైల్","3dm","skp","తిరిగి పొందు","ఆటోసేవ్","ఇటీవలివి","డెస్క్‌టాప్","3D లో తెరువు","హ్యాండాఫ్","డౌన్‌లోడ్","modelcraft ఫైల్","dae","collada","obj","gltf","glb","stl","step","stp","ifc","dwg","dxf","ఫార్మాట్‌లు","ఆఫ్‌లైన్","ఇలా సేవ్ చేయి"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"ఫార్మాట్‌ల ఒక చూపు","level":2},{"id":"modelcraft","text":"ModelCraft ఫైల్","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"మోడల్‌ను తెరవడం","level":3},{"id":"section-3","text":"మోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయడం","level":3},{"id":"section-4","text":"కొత్త మోడల్ ప్రారంభించడం","level":3},{"id":"section-5","text":"మీరు సేవ్ చేయడానికి ముందు బ్రౌజర్ మూసివేస్తే","level":3},{"id":"cadcraft-modelcraft","text":"CadCraft నుండి: ModelCraft లో తెరువు","level":3},{"id":"section-6","text":"దిగుమతి","level":2},{"id":"section-7","text":"ఎగుమతి","level":2},{"id":"section-8","text":"ఆన్‌లైన్ మరియు ఆఫ్‌లైన్","level":2},{"id":"section-9","text":"డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఫైళ్లను చదువుతుంది మరియు రాస్తుంది. ఏదీ అప్‌లోడ్ కాదు: మీరు తెరిచే ఫైల్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల చదవబడుతుంది, మరియు మీరు సేవ్ చేసే ఫైల్ ఒక డౌన్‌లోడ్. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003Eమీ ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయి\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eఫార్మాట్‌ల ఒక చూపు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eఫార్మాట్\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eతెరువు\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eదేనికోసం\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft (\u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eమీ పని ఫైల్. ప్రతిదీ ఉంచుతుంది.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUSD (\u003Ccode\u003E.usd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఇలా సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eరెండరింగ్ మరియు సీన్-అసెంబ్లీ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు. ModelCraft యొక్క స్వంత USD ప్రతిదీ ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD ను సేవ్ చేయడం, తెరవడం మరియు ఎగుమతి చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketchUp (\u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketchUp నుండి మోడల్‌లు తీసుకురావడం. గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, రంగులు, ముఖాలపై టెక్చర్‌లు, ట్యాగ్‌లు మరియు మృదువైన అంచులను ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\"\u003ESKP మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECollada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఇతర 3D మోడలర్‌లతో మోడల్‌లను మార్పిడి చేయడం. గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు రంగులను ఉంచుతుంది.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOBJ (\u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eరెండరింగ్ మరియు విజువలైజేషన్ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF (\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E మాత్రమే\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eవెబ్ వ్యూయర్‌లు, గేమ్ ఇంజిన్‌లు, AR.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL (\u003Ccode\u003E.stl\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ (మిల్లీమీటర్లలో).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPLY (\u003Ccode\u003E.ply\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eరంగులతో మెష్‌లు: స్కాన్‌లు, రంగు ప్రింటింగ్.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFBX (\u003Ccode\u003E.fbx\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eయానిమేషన్ మరియు గేమ్ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల నుండి మోడల్‌లు (FBX 2011 మరియు కొత్తవి).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlembic (\u003Ccode\u003E.abc\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eజ్యామితి కాష్‌లు, వాటి మొదటి నమూనాలో.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP (\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eపార్ట్ మోడలర్‌లు మరియు సరఫరాదారుల నుండి భాగాలు, వాటి నిజమైన పరిమాణంలో మూసిన మెష్‌లుగా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003Eఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENURBS మోడల్‌లు: పాలీసర్ఫేస్‌లు, మెష్‌లు, వక్రాలు, బ్లాక్‌లు మరియు లేయర్‌లు, మెష్‌లు, అంచులు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు ట్యాగ్‌లుగా. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-3dm-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-3dm-files\"\u003E.3dm మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3MF (\u003Ccode\u003E.3mf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eరంగులతో 3D ప్రింటింగ్ (మిల్లీమీటర్లలో). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E నుండి: చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003E3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను సిద్ధం చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC (\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBIM ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు, మరియు BimCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG (\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eమోడల్ చేయడానికి సైట్ ప్లాన్ లేదా ఫ్లోర్ ప్లాన్; మోడల్‌ను CAD ప్రోగ్రామ్‌కు పంపడం.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF (\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eకాదు\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅవును\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅదే, ఏ CAD ప్రోగ్రామ్‌కైనా.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003Eఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా\u003C/a\u003E ప్రతి ఫార్మాట్ ఏమి ఉంచుతుందో మరియు ఏమి వదిలేస్తుందో ఖచ్చితంగా జాబితా చేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft\"\u003EModelCraft ఫైల్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్ మొత్తం మోడల్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది: జ్యామితి, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, గైడ్‌లు, దృశ్యాలు, యూనిట్లు, మరియు మీరు సేవ్ చేసినప్పుడు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న కెమెరా మరియు శైలి. అది చర్య రద్దు చరిత్రను ఉంచదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eమోడల్‌ను తెరవడం\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/span\u003E గ్రూప్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతెరువు\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eతెరపై ఉన్న మోడల్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉంటే, ModelCraft అడుగుతుంది: &quot;ఈ మోడల్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉన్నాయి. ముందుగా ఒక కాపీని సేవ్ చేయాలా?&quot; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఒక కాపీని సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమార్పులను విస్మరించు\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరద్దు చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్‌ను ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమోడల్ అది సేవ్ చేయబడిన యూనిట్లలో, అది సేవ్ చేయబడిన కెమెరాతో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. మీరు \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్‌ను 3D వీక్షణపై కూడా వదలవచ్చు, లేదా ప్రారంభ కార్డ్‌లోని ఇటీవలి ఫైళ్ల జాబితాలో ఒకదాన్ని క్లిక్ చేయవచ్చు (ఫైల్‌ను మళ్ళీ తెరవగల బ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eడెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్‌లో, \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే అది ModelCraft లో తెరుచుకుంటుంది, అలాగే Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG లేదా DXF ఫైల్‌పై \u003Cstrong\u003Eదీనితో తెరువు\u003C/strong\u003E కూడా (అది కొత్త మోడల్‌లోకి దిగుమతి అవుతుంది).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eమోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయడం\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి. బ్రౌజర్ \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, ఇక్కడ పేరు మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్ పేరు, లేదా కొత్త మోడల్ కోసం &quot;Untitled&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eడెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్‌లో, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్‌లోనే రాస్తుంది. కొత్త మోడల్ ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఇలా సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ఒక కాపీని \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్‌గా లేదా \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/a\u003E గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; బ్రౌజర్ చేయగలిగితే, అది పేరు మరియు స్థలాన్ని అడుగుతుంది. లేకపోతే డౌన్‌లోడ్ అయిన ఫైల్ పేరు మార్చండి: పేరు మార్చిన ఫైల్‌ను తెరిచిన తర్వాత వచ్చే తదుపరి సేవ్ కొత్త పేరును ఉపయోగిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్రతి సేవ్ ఒక కొత్త డౌన్‌లోడ్. బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లను బట్టి, అది నేరుగా మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్‌కు వెళ్తుంది లేదా ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగుతుంది. ఆ పేరుతో ఫైల్ ఇప్పటికే అక్కడ ఉంటే, చాలా బ్రౌజర్‌లు ఒక సంఖ్యను జోడిస్తాయి, ఉదాహరణకు \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (1).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్‌ను ఎంచుకోవడానికి, బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, &quot;downloads&quot; అని వెతికి, &quot;డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేసే ముందు ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగు&quot; ను ఆన్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్‌ను ఎంచుకోవడానికి, \u003Cstrong\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/strong\u003E తెరవండి, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003Eసాధారణం\u003C/strong\u003E, మరియు &quot;డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు&quot; కింద &quot;ఫైళ్లను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో ఎల్లప్పుడూ అడుగు&quot; ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eడౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయో మార్చడానికి, \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari\u003C/strong\u003E తెరిచి, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/strong\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003Eసాధారణం\u003C/strong\u003E, మరియు &quot;ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ స్థానం&quot; ను సెట్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eకొత్త మోడల్ ప్రారంభించడం\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్తది\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉంటే, ModelCraft ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది. కొత్త మోడల్ ఖాళీగా ప్రారంభమవుతుంది, మీ లొకేల్ యూనిట్‌లో (యునైటెడ్ స్టేట్స్‌లో అడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలు, ఇతర చోట్ల మిల్లీమీటర్లు), దృశ్యాలు లేకుండా.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eమీరు సేవ్ చేయడానికి ముందు బ్రౌజర్ మూసివేస్తే\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft ప్రతి మార్పు తర్వాత కొన్ని సెకన్లకు మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని పనిని ఉంచుతుంది. మీరు సేవ్ చేయకముందే ట్యాబ్ లేదా బ్రౌజర్ మూసివేస్తే, మీరు తదుపరిసారి ModelCraft తెరిచినప్పుడు అది &quot;మీ పనిని తిరిగి పొందాలా?&quot; అని అడుగుతుంది. తిరిగి పొందడం మోడల్‌ను ఉన్నట్లుగానే తెరుస్తుంది, కానీ ఇంకా ఒక ఫైల్‌కు సేవ్ చేయబడలేదు: దాన్ని ఉంచడానికి సేవ్ చేయండి. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003Eమీ పనిని తిరిగి పొందడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cadcraft-modelcraft\"\u003ECadCraft నుండి: ModelCraft లో తెరువు\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft డ్రాఫ్టింగ్ యాప్‌లో, \u003Cstrong\u003Eవీక్షణ\u003C/strong\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003Eమరిన్ని\u003C/strong\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003EModelCraft లో తెరువు\u003C/strong\u003E తెరిచిన డ్రాయింగ్‌ను కొత్త ట్యాబ్‌లో DWG గా ModelCraft కు అందిస్తుంది. అది దిగుమతి చేయబడిన DWG లాగా వస్తుంది: నేలపై గీతలు, ఒక గ్రూప్‌లో, మోడల్ చేయడానికి సిద్ధంగా. ఏదీ అప్‌లోడ్ కాదు; ఫైల్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ స్వంత స్టోరేజ్ ద్వారా వెళ్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eదిగుమతి చేయడం తెరిచి ఉన్న మోడల్‌కు ఫైల్‌ను జోడిస్తుంది; అది దాన్ని భర్తీ చేయదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/span\u003E గ్రూప్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదిగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఫైల్‌ను 3D వీక్షణపై వదలండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketchUp, Collada, OBJ, glTF, FBX, PLY, STL, Alembic, STEP, \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E, USD, DWG లేదా DXF ఫైల్‌ను ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఫైల్ \u003Cstrong\u003Eఒక గ్రూప్\u003C/strong\u003Eగా వస్తుంది, ఎంచుకోబడి, మరియు వీక్షణ దాన్ని చూపించడానికి జూమ్ అవుతుంది. స్టేటస్ బార్ ఏమి వచ్చిందో చెబుతుంది, మరియు తీసుకురాలేనివి జాబితా చేస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు &quot;దిగుమతి కాలేదు: 2 టెక్చర్&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదాని జ్యామితిని నేరుగా పని చేయడానికి, గ్రూప్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేసి లోపల సవరించండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవిడదీయి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకొన్ని విషయాలు తెలుసుకోవాలి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eపరిమాణం మరియు పైకి దిశ:\u003C/strong\u003E Collada ఫైళ్లు తమ యూనిట్ మరియు ఏ అక్షం పైకి ఉందో చెబుతాయి, మరియు ModelCraft వాటిని మారుస్తుంది. glTF ఎల్లప్పుడూ మీటర్లలో ఉంటుంది. OBJ ఫైళ్లలో యూనిట్ ఉండదు: ModelCraft వాటిని మీటర్లుగా చదువుతుంది, Y పైకి. OBJ 1000 రెట్లు చాలా పెద్దదిగా లేదా చిన్నదిగా వస్తే, \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E లేదా \u003Ccode\u003E1000\u003C/code\u003E వంటి కారకంతో \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003Eస్కేల్\u003C/a\u003E ఉపయోగించండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG మరియు DXF\u003C/strong\u003E నేలపై గీతలు (అంచులు)గా వస్తాయి, ముఖాలుగా కాదు. డ్రాయింగ్ దాని యూనిట్ చెప్పకపోతే, &quot;డ్రాయింగ్ యూనిట్లు&quot; అనే శీర్షిక గల డైలాగ్ ఒక డ్రాయింగ్ యూనిట్ అంటే ఏమిటో అడుగుతుంది. చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003Eసైట్ ప్లాన్‌పై మోడల్ చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eపరిమితులు:\u003C/strong\u003E 300 MB కంటే పెద్ద ఫైళ్లు దిగుమతికి తిరస్కరించబడతాయి, మరియు తెరవడానికి 200 MB కంటే పెద్దవి. 2 మిలియన్ కంటే ఎక్కువ శీర్షాలు లేదా ముఖాలున్న ఫైళ్లు చాలా పెద్దవిగా తిరస్కరించబడతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-7\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/span\u003E గ్రూప్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఒక ఫార్మాట్ ఎంచుకోండి: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF బైనరీ (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ (.obj)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రింటింగ్ కోసం STL (.stl, మిల్లీమీటర్లు)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరంగులతో PLY (.ply)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft కోసం IFC (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD కోసం DWG (.dwg, 2004)\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD కోసం DXF (.dxf, R12)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, మోడల్ పేరు పెట్టబడినట్లు. మొత్తం మోడల్ ఎగుమతి అవుతుంది, కేవలం ఎంపిక కాదు. ఖాళీ మోడల్ ఎగుమతి కాదు: &quot;మోడల్ ఖాళీగా ఉంది: ముందుగా ఏదైనా గీయండి.&quot; చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\"\u003Eఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల కోసం మోడల్‌ను ఎగుమతి చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-8\"\u003Eఆన్‌లైన్ మరియు ఆఫ్‌లైన్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft లోని ప్రతిదీ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, కాబట్టి యాప్ లోడ్ అయిన తర్వాత మోడలింగ్, తెరవడం, సేవ్ చేయడం, దిగుమతి మరియు ఎగుమతి అన్నీ కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా పనిచేస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఎలాంటి కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా ModelCraft తెరవడానికి, ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడే ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయండి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఎగువన ఉన్న యాప్ బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eసేవ్ అవుతున్నప్పుడు బటన్ లెక్కిస్తుంది (&quot;212 లో 40 సేవ్ అవుతోంది&quot;). ModelCraft తన స్వంత ఫైళ్లను (దాని DWG రీడర్‌తో సహా), ఈ సహాయాన్ని మరియు 3D లైబ్రరీని సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, కాబట్టి కొంత సమయం పట్టవచ్చు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eబటన్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం\u003C/span\u003E అని చదివినప్పుడు, ModelCraft కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా అదే చిరునామా నుండి తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు DWG దిగుమతి కూడా ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో పనిచేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eతర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం\u003C/span\u003E పై క్లిక్ చేస్తే \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమళ్ళీ సేవ్ చేయి (కొత్త వెర్షన్ పొందండి)\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ కాపీని తీసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E కనిపిస్తాయి. బటన్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసేవ్ చేయలేకపోయాం\u003C/span\u003E అని చదివితే, కొన్ని ఫైళ్లు డౌన్‌లోడ్ కాలేదు: కనెక్షన్ తనిఖీ చేసి మళ్ళీ ప్రయత్నించడానికి దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ అందుబాటులో లేదు\u003C/span\u003E అంటే ఈ బ్రౌజర్ విండో కాపీని ఉంచలేదు (ప్రైవేట్ విండోలు మరియు కొన్ని నిర్వహించబడే కంప్యూటర్‌లు). చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో పని చేయడం\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eక్లౌడ్ సేవింగ్ ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేదు. ప్రతి మోడల్ మీరు సేవ్ చేసిన చోటే ఉంటుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నారు. మీరు మోడలింగ్ మరియు సేవ్ చేయడం కొనసాగించవచ్చు. యాప్ బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం\u003C/span\u003E కనిపించకపోతే, మీరు మళ్ళీ ఆన్‌లైన్‌కు వచ్చే వరకు ఈ ట్యాబ్‌ను రీలోడ్ చేయవద్దు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-9\"\u003Eడెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు CadCraft Studio లో ModelCraft ఉపయోగిస్తున్నారు, మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లతో కూడిన డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్. ఫైళ్లు మీరు ఎంచుకున్న చోట తెరుచుకుంటాయి మరియు సేవ్ అవుతాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:web\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EIn the browser\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft Mac, Windows మరియు Linux లో బ్రౌజర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, మరియు CadCraft Studio లో కూడా నడుస్తుంది, ఒకే ఇన్‌స్టాల్‌లో మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లతో కూడిన డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్, getcadcraft.com/download నుండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"ModelCraft మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఫైళ్లను చదువుతుంది మరియు రాస్తుంది. ఏదీ అప్‌లోడ్ కాదు: మీరు తెరిచే ఫైల్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల చదవబడుతుంది, మరియు మీరు సేవ్ చేసే ఫైల్ ఒక డౌన్‌లోడ్. చూడండి మీ ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయి. ఫార్మాట్‌ల ఒక చూపు ఫార్మాట్ తెరువు దిగుమతి ఎగుమతి దేనికోసం ModelCraft (.modelcraft) అవును కాదు సేవ్ చేయి మీ పని ఫైల్. ప్రతిదీ ఉంచుతుంది. USD (.usd, .usda, .usdc, .usdz) అవును అవును ఇలా సేవ్ చేయి, ఎగుమతి రెండరింగ్ మరియు సీన్-అసెంబ్లీ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు. ModelCraft యొక్క స్వంత USD ప్రతిదీ ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి USD ను సేవ్ చేయడం, తెరవడం మరియు ఎగుమతి చేయడం. SketchUp (.skp) కాదు అవును కాదు SketchUp నుండి మోడల్‌లు తీసుకురావడం. గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, రంగులు, ముఖాలపై టెక్చర్‌లు, ట్యాగ్‌లు మరియు మృదువైన అంచులను ఉంచుతుంది. చూడండి SKP మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం. Collada (.dae) కాదు అవును అవును ఇతర 3D మోడలర్‌లతో మోడల్‌లను మార్పిడి చేయడం. గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు రంగులను ఉంచుతుంది. OBJ (.obj) కాదు అవును అవును రెండరింగ్ మరియు విజువలైజేషన్ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు. glTF (.glb, .gltf) కాదు అవును .glb మాత్రమే వెబ్ వ్యూయర్‌లు, గేమ్ ఇంజిన్‌లు, AR. STL (.stl) కాదు అవును అవును 3D ప్రింటింగ్ (మిల్లీమీటర్లలో). PLY (.ply) కాదు అవును అవును రంగులతో మెష్‌లు: స్కాన్‌లు, రంగు ప్రింటింగ్. FBX (.fbx) కాదు అవును కాదు యానిమేషన్ మరియు గేమ్ ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల నుండి మోడల్‌లు (FBX 2011 మరియు కొత్తవి). Alembic (.abc) కాదు అవును కాదు జ్యామితి కాష్‌లు, వాటి మొదటి నమూనాలో. STEP (.step, .stp) కాదు అవును కాదు పార్ట్ మోడలర్‌లు మరియు సరఫరాదారుల నుండి భాగాలు, వాటి నిజమైన పరిమాణంలో మూసిన మెష్‌లుగా. చూడండి ఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు. .3dm కాదు అవును కాదు NURBS మోడల్‌లు: పాలీసర్ఫేస్‌లు, మెష్‌లు, వక్రాలు, బ్లాక్‌లు మరియు లేయర్‌లు, మెష్‌లు, అంచులు, కాంపోనెంట్లు మరియు ట్యాగ్‌లుగా. చూడండి .3dm మోడల్‌ను తీసుకురావడం. 3MF (.3mf) కాదు కాదు అవును రంగులతో 3D ప్రింటింగ్ (మిల్లీమీటర్లలో). 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం తనిఖీ (STL లేదా 3MF)… నుండి: చూడండి 3D ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం మోడల్‌ను సిద్ధం చేయడం. IFC (.ifc) కాదు కాదు అవును BIM ప్రోగ్రామ్‌లు, మరియు BimCraft. DWG (.dwg) కాదు అవును అవును మోడల్ చేయడానికి సైట్ ప్లాన్ లేదా ఫ్లోర్ ప్లాన్; మోడల్‌ను CAD ప్రోగ్రామ్‌కు పంపడం. DXF (.dxf) కాదు అవును అవును అదే, ఏ CAD ప్రోగ్రామ్‌కైనా. ఫైల్ ఫార్మాట్‌లు వివరంగా ప్రతి ఫార్మాట్ ఏమి ఉంచుతుందో మరియు ఏమి వదిలేస్తుందో ఖచ్చితంగా జాబితా చేస్తుంది. ModelCraft ఫైల్ .modelcraft ఫైల్ మొత్తం మోడల్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది: జ్యామితి, గ్రూప్‌లు, కాంపోనెంట్లు, గైడ్‌లు, దృశ్యాలు, యూనిట్లు, మరియు మీరు సేవ్ చేసినప్పుడు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న కెమెరా మరియు శైలి. అది చర్య రద్దు చరిత్రను ఉంచదు. మోడల్‌ను తెరవడం ఫైల్ గ్రూప్‌లో తెరువు పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా Ctrl+O నొక్కండి. తెరపై ఉన్న మోడల్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉంటే, ModelCraft అడుగుతుంది: \"ఈ మోడల్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉన్నాయి. ముందుగా ఒక కాపీని సేవ్ చేయాలా?\" ఒక కాపీని సేవ్ చేయి, మార్పులను విస్మరించు లేదా రద్దు చేయి ఎంచుకోండి. .modelcraft ఫైల్‌ను ఎంచుకోండి. మోడల్ అది సేవ్ చేయబడిన యూనిట్లలో, అది సేవ్ చేయబడిన కెమెరాతో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. మీరు .modelcraft ఫైల్‌ను 3D వీక్షణపై కూడా వదలవచ్చు, లేదా ప్రారంభ కార్డ్‌లోని ఇటీవలి ఫైళ్ల జాబితాలో ఒకదాన్ని క్లిక్ చేయవచ్చు (ఫైల్‌ను మళ్ళీ తెరవగల బ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్). డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్‌లో, .modelcraft ఫైల్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే అది ModelCraft లో తెరుచుకుంటుంది, అలాగే Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG లేదా DXF ఫైల్‌పై దీనితో తెరువు కూడా (అది కొత్త మోడల్‌లోకి దిగుమతి అవుతుంది). మోడల్‌ను సేవ్ చేయడం సేవ్ చేయి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి లేదా Ctrl+S నొక్కండి. బ్రౌజర్ \u003Cname\u003E.modelcraft ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, ఇక్కడ పేరు మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్ పేరు, లేదా కొత్త మోడల్ కోసం \"Untitled\". డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్‌లో, సేవ్ చేయి మీరు తెరిచిన ఫైల్‌లోనే రాస్తుంది. కొత్త మోడల్ ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగుతుంది. ఇలా సేవ్ చేయి (Ctrl+Shift+S) ఒక కాపీని .modelcraft ఫైల్‌గా లేదా USD గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; బ్రౌజర్ చేయగలిగితే, అది పేరు మరియు స్థలాన్ని అడుగుతుంది. లేకపోతే డౌన్‌లోడ్ అయిన ఫైల్ పేరు మార్చండి: పేరు మార్చిన ఫైల్‌ను తెరిచిన తర్వాత వచ్చే తదుపరి సేవ్ కొత్త పేరును ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. ప్రతి సేవ్ ఒక కొత్త డౌన్‌లోడ్. బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లను బట్టి, అది నేరుగా మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్‌కు వెళ్తుంది లేదా ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగుతుంది. ఆ పేరుతో ఫైల్ ఇప్పటికే అక్కడ ఉంటే, చాలా బ్రౌజర్‌లు ఒక సంఖ్యను జోడిస్తాయి, ఉదాహరణకు Untitled (1).modelcraft. ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్‌ను ఎంచుకోవడానికి, బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, \"downloads\" అని వెతికి, \"డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేసే ముందు ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగు\" ను ఆన్ చేయండి. ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్‌ను ఎంచుకోవడానికి, సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరవండి, తర్వాత సాధారణం, మరియు \"డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు\" కింద \"ఫైళ్లను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో ఎల్లప్పుడూ అడుగు\" ఎంచుకోండి. డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయో మార్చడానికి, Safari తెరిచి, తర్వాత సెట్టింగ్‌లు, తర్వాత సాధారణం, మరియు \"ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ స్థానం\" ను సెట్ చేయండి. కొత్త మోడల్ ప్రారంభించడం కొత్తది పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. సేవ్ చేయని మార్పులు ఉంటే, ModelCraft ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది. కొత్త మోడల్ ఖాళీగా ప్రారంభమవుతుంది, మీ లొకేల్ యూనిట్‌లో (యునైటెడ్ స్టేట్స్‌లో అడుగులు మరియు అంగుళాలు, ఇతర చోట్ల మిల్లీమీటర్లు), దృశ్యాలు లేకుండా. మీరు సేవ్ చేయడానికి ముందు బ్రౌజర్ మూసివేస్తే ModelCraft ప్రతి మార్పు తర్వాత కొన్ని సెకన్లకు మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో సేవ్ చేయని పనిని ఉంచుతుంది. మీరు సేవ్ చేయకముందే ట్యాబ్ లేదా బ్రౌజర్ మూసివేస్తే, మీరు తదుపరిసారి ModelCraft తెరిచినప్పుడు అది \"మీ పనిని తిరిగి పొందాలా?\" అని అడుగుతుంది. తిరిగి పొందడం మోడల్‌ను ఉన్నట్లుగానే తెరుస్తుంది, కానీ ఇంకా ఒక ఫైల్‌కు సేవ్ చేయబడలేదు: దాన్ని ఉంచడానికి సేవ్ చేయండి. చూడండి మీ పనిని తిరిగి పొందడం. CadCraft నుండి: ModelCraft లో తెరువు CadCraft డ్రాఫ్టింగ్ యాప్‌లో, వీక్షణ, తర్వాత మరిన్ని, తర్వాత ModelCraft లో తెరువు తెరిచిన డ్రాయింగ్‌ను కొత్త ట్యాబ్‌లో DWG గా ModelCraft కు అందిస్తుంది. అది దిగుమతి చేయబడిన DWG లాగా వస్తుంది: నేలపై గీతలు, ఒక గ్రూప్‌లో, మోడల్ చేయడానికి సిద్ధంగా. ఏదీ అప్‌లోడ్ కాదు; ఫైల్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ స్వంత స్టోరేజ్ ద్వారా వెళ్తుంది. దిగుమతి దిగుమతి చేయడం తెరిచి ఉన్న మోడల్‌కు ఫైల్‌ను జోడిస్తుంది; అది దాన్ని భర్తీ చేయదు. ఫైల్ గ్రూప్‌లో దిగుమతి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఫైల్‌ను 3D వీక్షణపై వదలండి. SketchUp, Collada, OBJ, glTF, FBX, PLY, STL, Alembic, STEP, .3dm, USD, DWG లేదా DXF ఫైల్‌ను ఎంచుకోండి. ఫైల్ ఒక గ్రూప్గా వస్తుంది, ఎంచుకోబడి, మరియు వీక్షణ దాన్ని చూపించడానికి జూమ్ అవుతుంది. స్టేటస్ బార్ ఏమి వచ్చిందో చెబుతుంది, మరియు తీసుకురాలేనివి జాబితా చేస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు \"దిగుమతి కాలేదు: 2 టెక్చర్\". దాని జ్యామితిని నేరుగా పని చేయడానికి, గ్రూప్‌పై డబల్-క్లిక్ చేసి లోపల సవరించండి, లేదా విడదీయి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. కొన్ని విషయాలు తెలుసుకోవాలి: పరిమాణం మరియు పైకి దిశ: Collada ఫైళ్లు తమ యూనిట్ మరియు ఏ అక్షం పైకి ఉందో చెబుతాయి, మరియు ModelCraft వాటిని మారుస్తుంది. glTF ఎల్లప్పుడూ మీటర్లలో ఉంటుంది. OBJ ఫైళ్లలో యూనిట్ ఉండదు: ModelCraft వాటిని మీటర్లుగా చదువుతుంది, Y పైకి. OBJ 1000 రెట్లు చాలా పెద్దదిగా లేదా చిన్నదిగా వస్తే, 0.001 లేదా 1000 వంటి కారకంతో స్కేల్ ఉపయోగించండి. DWG మరియు DXF నేలపై గీతలు (అంచులు)గా వస్తాయి, ముఖాలుగా కాదు. డ్రాయింగ్ దాని యూనిట్ చెప్పకపోతే, \"డ్రాయింగ్ యూనిట్లు\" అనే శీర్షిక గల డైలాగ్ ఒక డ్రాయింగ్ యూనిట్ అంటే ఏమిటో అడుగుతుంది. చూడండి సైట్ ప్లాన్‌పై మోడల్ చేయడం. పరిమితులు: 300 MB కంటే పెద్ద ఫైళ్లు దిగుమతికి తిరస్కరించబడతాయి, మరియు తెరవడానికి 200 MB కంటే పెద్దవి. 2 మిలియన్ కంటే ఎక్కువ శీర్షాలు లేదా ముఖాలున్న ఫైళ్లు చాలా పెద్దవిగా తిరస్కరించబడతాయి. ఎగుమతి ఫైల్ గ్రూప్‌లో ఎగుమతి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ఒక ఫార్మాట్ ఎంచుకోండి: glTF బైనరీ (.glb), Collada (.dae), OBJ (.obj), ప్రింటింగ్ కోసం STL (.stl, మిల్లీమీటర్లు), రంగులతో PLY (.ply), USD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…, BimCraft కోసం IFC (.ifc), CAD కోసం DWG (.dwg, 2004) లేదా CAD కోసం DXF (.dxf, R12). బ్రౌజర్ ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేస్తుంది, మోడల్ పేరు పెట్టబడినట్లు. మొత్తం మోడల్ ఎగుమతి అవుతుంది, కేవలం ఎంపిక కాదు. ఖాళీ మోడల్ ఎగుమతి కాదు: \"మోడల్ ఖాళీగా ఉంది: ముందుగా ఏదైనా గీయండి.\" చూడండి ఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల కోసం మోడల్‌ను ఎగుమతి చేయడం. ఆన్‌లైన్ మరియు ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ModelCraft లోని ప్రతిదీ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, కాబట్టి యాప్ లోడ్ అయిన తర్వాత మోడలింగ్, తెరవడం, సేవ్ చేయడం, దిగుమతి మరియు ఎగుమతి అన్నీ కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా పనిచేస్తాయి. ఎలాంటి కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా ModelCraft తెరవడానికి, ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడే ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయండి: ఎగువన ఉన్న యాప్ బార్‌లో ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. సేవ్ అవుతున్నప్పుడు బటన్ లెక్కిస్తుంది (\"212 లో 40 సేవ్ అవుతోంది\"). ModelCraft తన స్వంత ఫైళ్లను (దాని DWG రీడర్‌తో సహా), ఈ సహాయాన్ని మరియు 3D లైబ్రరీని సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, కాబట్టి కొంత సమయం పట్టవచ్చు. బటన్ ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం అని చదివినప్పుడు, ModelCraft కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా అదే చిరునామా నుండి తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు DWG దిగుమతి కూడా ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో పనిచేస్తుంది. తర్వాత ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం పై క్లిక్ చేస్తే మళ్ళీ సేవ్ చేయి (కొత్త వెర్షన్ పొందండి) లేదా ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కాపీని తీసివేయి కనిపిస్తాయి. బటన్ సేవ్ చేయలేకపోయాం అని చదివితే, కొన్ని ఫైళ్లు డౌన్‌లోడ్ కాలేదు: కనెక్షన్ తనిఖీ చేసి మళ్ళీ ప్రయత్నించడానికి దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ఆఫ్‌లైన్ అందుబాటులో లేదు అంటే ఈ బ్రౌజర్ విండో కాపీని ఉంచలేదు (ప్రైవేట్ విండోలు మరియు కొన్ని నిర్వహించబడే కంప్యూటర్‌లు). చూడండి ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో పని చేయడం. క్లౌడ్ సేవింగ్ ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేదు. ప్రతి మోడల్ మీరు సేవ్ చేసిన చోటే ఉంటుంది. మీరు ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నారు. మీరు మోడలింగ్ మరియు సేవ్ చేయడం కొనసాగించవచ్చు. యాప్ బార్‌లో ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం కనిపించకపోతే, మీరు మళ్ళీ ఆన్‌లైన్‌కు వచ్చే వరకు ఈ ట్యాబ్‌ను రీలోడ్ చేయవద్దు. డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్ మీరు CadCraft Studio లో ModelCraft ఉపయోగిస్తున్నారు, మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లతో కూడిన డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్. ఫైళ్లు మీరు ఎంచుకున్న చోట తెరుచుకుంటాయి మరియు సేవ్ అవుతాయి. ModelCraft Mac, Windows మరియు Linux లో బ్రౌజర్‌లో నడుస్తుంది, మరియు CadCraft Studio లో కూడా నడుస్తుంది, ఒకే ఇన్‌స్టాల్‌లో మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లతో కూడిన డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్, getcadcraft.com/download నుండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"orbit-pan-zoom","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Orbit, pan and zoom","summary":"Move around the model with a mouse, a trackpad, a touch screen or the keyboard, and jump to standard views.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["orbit","pan","zoom","long press","three fingers","two fingers","tap","navigate","camera","view","rotate view","trackpad","touch","pinch","middle button","zoom extents","perspective","parallel","top view","iso"],"headings":[{"id":"with-a-three-button-mouse","text":"With a three-button mouse","level":2},{"id":"without-a-middle-button","text":"Without a middle button","level":2},{"id":"on-a-trackpad","text":"On a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"on-a-touch-screen","text":"On a touch screen","level":2},{"id":"standard-views","text":"Standard views","level":2},{"id":"perspective-and-parallel-projection","text":"Perspective and parallel projection","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EYou can move the camera at any time, even in the middle of drawing a line or pushing a face: the tool waits where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-a-three-button-mouse\"\u003EWith a three-button mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button (press the wheel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the middle button. Pressing or letting go of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E during a drag switches between orbit and pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the wheel. It zooms toward the face under the pointer, or toward the point under it at the depth of the view&#039;s center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOrbit turns the view around the part of the model under the pointer, so that point stays where it is while everything turns around it. Over empty space it turns around the center of the view. Pan keeps the point you grabbed under the pointer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo always orbit around the center of the view, or to make orbit and pan faster or slower, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, \u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit and pan\u003C/strong\u003E. The speeds apply to the mouse, the trackpad, touch and the arrow keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA right-click without dragging does nothing; ModelCraft has no right-click menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-a-middle-button\"\u003EWithout a middle button\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse the camera tools in the toolbar, or the keyboard:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E) and drag with the left button to orbit. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E during the drag to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E) and drag to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E) and drag up to zoom in, down to zoom out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, or press the key of the tool you want next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera tools replace the tool you were using. To look around in the middle of an operation without losing it, use the keyboard instead:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe arrow keys orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the arrow keys pans.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a drawing tool is in the middle of an operation (after its first click), the arrow keys lock directions instead of orbiting. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#lock-a-direction\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"lock-a-direction\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOn a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe easiest way: open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set Scrolling to \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E (or leave it on &quot;Work it out&quot;). Then swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom — no camera buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Scrolling set to \u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers, or pinch. Both zoom toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E press with two fingers (a secondary click) and keep the fingers down while you drag. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with one finger, or use the arrow keys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHolding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click may not count as a right-click in ModelCraft, depending on the browser: it can act as a click with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held instead. Use the two-finger press to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers. Pinch zooms too where the touchpad driver sends it as a scroll with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E drag with the right button (on many touchpads, press the lower-right corner and drag), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or use the arrow keys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Mouse wheel, two-finger scrolling zooms; it does not pan. That is the opposite of many web pages, and it is how the mouse wheel behaves in most 3D modelers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool in ModelCraft. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging does not pan, as it does in some other apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-touch-screen\"\u003EOn a touch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne finger\u003C/strong\u003E uses the current tool: \u003Cstrong\u003Etap\u003C/strong\u003E to click, \u003Cstrong\u003Epress and drag\u003C/strong\u003E to draw or drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile a drawing tool&#039;s finger is down, a \u003Cstrong\u003Eloupe\u003C/strong\u003E above it shows what is under the finger, so you can see the endpoint, midpoint or edge it snaps to before you lift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHold still\u003C/strong\u003E for half a second to finish what the tool is doing, as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does: a curve, a line chain. With nothing going on, holding still and then dragging the same finger \u003Cstrong\u003Eorbits\u003C/strong\u003E until you lift it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/strong\u003E dragged together \u003Cstrong\u003Epan\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003Epinch\u003C/strong\u003E to zoom. Both work in one gesture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThree fingers\u003C/strong\u003E dragged together \u003Cstrong\u003Eorbit\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo swap them (two fingers orbit, three pan), open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, Touch screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPutting a second finger down \u003Cstrong\u003Ecancels\u003C/strong\u003E what the first finger started (a rectangle half drawn, a push/pull in progress). Lift your fingers and start again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small bar at the bottom of the view has \u003Cstrong\u003EDone\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cstrong\u003ECancel\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E, for when there is no keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnapping and picking reach a little further than with a mouse, and the buttons are bigger, so a finger can hit them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, one finger uses the current tool (tap to click, hold still to finish, or hold still and drag to orbit), two fingers pan and pinch to zoom, three fingers orbit, and a small bar offers Done, Cancel and Undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"standard-views\"\u003EStandard views\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E at the right end of the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view turns to that direction and fits the whole model. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E looks north, along the green axis; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E looks straight down with north up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube at the top right of the view does the same in one click, plus edges and corners, a compass ring to turn the model and a home button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"perspective-and-parallel-projection\"\u003EPerspective and parallel projection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E. A check mark means perspective is on. With it off, the view is a parallel (orthographic) projection: lines that are parallel in the model stay parallel on screen, which is what you want for elevations from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Perspective.&quot; or &quot;Parallel projection.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach scene remembers whether it was in perspective. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"You can move the camera at any time, even in the middle of drawing a line or pushing a face: the tool waits where it was. With a three-button mouse To Do this Orbit Drag with the middle button (press the wheel) Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift and drag with the middle button. Pressing or letting go of Shift during a drag switches between orbit and pan Zoom Turn the wheel. It zooms toward the face under the pointer, or toward the point under it at the depth of the view's center See the whole model Shift+Z or Extents Orbit turns the view around the part of the model under the pointer, so that point stays where it is while everything turns around it. Over empty space it turns around the center of the view. Pan keeps the point you grabbed under the pointer. To always orbit around the center of the view, or to make orbit and pan faster or slower, open Settings (Ctrl+,), ModelCraft tab, Orbit and pan. The speeds apply to the mouse, the trackpad, touch and the arrow keys. A right-click without dragging does nothing; ModelCraft has no right-click menu. Without a middle button Use the camera tools in the toolbar, or the keyboard: Press O (or click Orbit) and drag with the left button to orbit. Hold Shift during the drag to pan. Press H (or click Pan) and drag to pan. Press Z (or click Zoom) and drag up to zoom in, down to zoom out. Press Space to go back to Select, or press the key of the tool you want next. The camera tools replace the tool you were using. To look around in the middle of an operation without losing it, use the keyboard instead: The arrow keys orbit. Shift with the arrow keys pans. While a drawing tool is in the middle of an operation (after its first click), the arrow keys lock directions instead of orbiting. See Inference and snapping. On a trackpad The easiest way: open Settings (Ctrl+,), ModelCraft tab, and set Scrolling to Trackpad (or leave it on \"Work it out\"). Then swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom — no camera buttons. See ModelCraft settings. With Scrolling set to Mouse wheel: Zoom: scroll with two fingers, or pinch. Both zoom toward the pointer. Pan: press with two fingers (a secondary click) and keep the fingers down while you drag. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Orbit: press O and drag with one finger, or use the arrow keys. Holding Ctrl while you click may not count as a right-click in ModelCraft, depending on the browser: it can act as a click with Ctrl held instead. Use the two-finger press to pan. Zoom: scroll with two fingers. Pinch zooms too where the touchpad driver sends it as a scroll with Ctrl. Pan: drag with the right button (on many touchpads, press the lower-right corner and drag), or press H and drag. Orbit: press O and drag, or use the arrow keys. With Mouse wheel, two-finger scrolling zooms; it does not pan. That is the opposite of many web pages, and it is how the mouse wheel behaves in most 3D modelers. Space picks the Select tool in ModelCraft. Holding Space and dragging does not pan, as it does in some other apps. On a touch screen One finger uses the current tool: tap to click, press and drag to draw or drag. While a drawing tool's finger is down, a loupe above it shows what is under the finger, so you can see the endpoint, midpoint or edge it snaps to before you lift. Hold still for half a second to finish what the tool is doing, as Enter does: a curve, a line chain. With nothing going on, holding still and then dragging the same finger orbits until you lift it. Two fingers dragged together pan; pinch to zoom. Both work in one gesture. Three fingers dragged together orbit. To swap them (two fingers orbit, three pan), open Settings, ModelCraft tab, Touch screen. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first finger started (a rectangle half drawn, a push/pull in progress). Lift your fingers and start again. A small bar at the bottom of the view has Done (Enter), Cancel (Esc) and Undo, for when there is no keyboard. Snapping and picking reach a little further than with a mouse, and the buttons are bigger, so a finger can hit them. On a touch screen, one finger uses the current tool (tap to click, hold still to finish, or hold still and drag to orbit), two fingers pan and pinch to zoom, three fingers orbit, and a small bar offers Done, Cancel and Undo. Standard views Click Views at the right end of the toolbar. Choose Iso, Top, Front, Right, Back, Left or Bottom. The view turns to that direction and fits the whole model. Front looks north, along the green axis; Top looks straight down with north up. The view cube at the top right of the view does the same in one click, plus edges and corners, a compass ring to turn the model and a home button. See The view cube. Perspective and parallel projection Choose Views, then Perspective. A check mark means perspective is on. With it off, the view is a parallel (orthographic) projection: lines that are parallel in the model stay parallel on screen, which is what you want for elevations from Front or Right. The status bar says \"Perspective.\" or \"Parallel projection.\" Each scene remembers whether it was in perspective. See Save views as scenes.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"view-cube","scope":"modelcraft","title":"The view cube","summary":"Click a face, edge or corner of the cube in the corner of the view to look from there, drag it to orbit, turn the compass ring, step around a face-on view, and go home.","section":"tasks","order":11,"keywords":["view cube","viewcube","cube","compass","north","home view","standard views","top","front","right","iso","corner","edge","orbit","rotate view","axis icon","corner indicator","navigation"],"headings":[{"id":"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner","text":"Look from a side, an edge or a corner","level":2},{"id":"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube","text":"Orbit by dragging the cube","level":2},{"id":"the-compass-ring","text":"The compass ring","level":2},{"id":"face-on-views-arrows","text":"Face-on views: arrows","level":2},{"id":"home","text":"Home","level":2},{"id":"with-the-keyboard","text":"With the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead","text":"Axis icon or nothing instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view. It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E is up, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E faces south (you look north, along the green axis, when you look at it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E faces east (red axis). It stays see-through until you point at it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner\"\u003ELook from a side, an edge or a corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are 26 places to click: 6 faces (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E is the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E still zooms to the whole model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube\"\u003EOrbit by dragging the cube\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-compass-ring\"\u003EThe compass ring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag the ring\u003C/strong\u003E to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a letter\u003C/strong\u003E to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"face-on-views-arrows\"\u003EFace-on views: arrows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe four \u003Cstrong\u003Etriangles\u003C/strong\u003E bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe two \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved arrows\u003C/strong\u003E at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"home\"\u003EHome\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E view and fits the whole model in. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-the-keyboard\"\u003EWith the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the cube, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a higher or lower one, up to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or down to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elook from the one chosen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift →\"\u003E⇧→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe home view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eleave the cube\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start ModelCraft tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead\"\u003EAxis icon or nothing instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. This browser remembers the choice. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#corner-indicator\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"corner-indicator\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft has no roll: away from straight-down and straight-up views, up on the screen is always up in the model. The cube and the curved arrows can turn a plan view; they cannot tip a side view onto its edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view. It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: Top is up, Front faces south (you look north, along the green axis, when you look at it), Right faces east (red axis). It stays see-through until you point at it. Look from a side, an edge or a corner Point at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces). Click it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom. There are 26 places to click: 6 faces (Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces. Clicking Front is the same as Views, Front in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit. Views still zooms to the whole model. Orbit by dragging the cube Drag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere. The compass ring The ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W. Drag the ring to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer. Click a letter to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen. Face-on views: arrows When you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube: The four triangles bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on. The two curved arrows at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen. Home The house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter Iso view and fits the whole model in. Home does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus. With the keyboard Tab to the cube, then: Key Does ← / → choose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which) ↑ / ↓ choose a higher or lower one, up to Top or down to Bottom Enter or Space look from the one chosen Shift+← / Shift+→ turn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis Home the home view Esc leave the cube Keys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start ModelCraft tools. Axis icon or nothing instead Open Settings (Ctrl+,), the ModelCraft tab, and set Corner indicator to View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. This browser remembers the choice. See ModelCraft settings. ModelCraft has no roll: away from straight-down and straight-up views, up on the screen is always up in the model. The cube and the curved arrows can turn a plan view; they cannot tip a side view onto its edge.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"draw-faces","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Draw faces with lines, rectangles, circles and arcs","summary":"Make flat faces on the ground or on other faces, split them with lines, and draw circles and polygons with the number of sides you want.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["draw","line","rectangle","circle","polygon","offset","inset","frame","border","arc","curve","spline","smooth","cable","path","bulge","radius","curve","face","floor plan","footprint","split","sides","segments","outline","pencil"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-rectangle","text":"Draw a rectangle","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-circle-or-a-polygon","text":"Draw a circle or a polygon","level":2},{"id":"draw-an-arc","text":"Draw an arc","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-smooth-curve","text":"Draw a smooth curve","level":2},{"id":"offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face","text":"Offset: a border inside or outside a face","level":2},{"id":"arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves","text":"Arcs, circles and polygons are curves","level":2},{"id":"draw-with-lines","text":"Draw with lines","level":2},{"id":"where-a-line-goes-when-it-touches-nothing","text":"Where a line goes when it touches nothing","level":3},{"id":"split-a-face","text":"Split a face","level":2},{"id":"faces-facing-the-wrong-way","text":"Faces facing the wrong way","level":2},{"id":"what-you-see-while-drawing","text":"What you see while drawing","level":2},{"id":"if-the-shape-is-not-where-you-expected","text":"If the shape is not where you expected","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEverything in ModelCraft starts as a flat \u003Cstrong\u003Eface\u003C/strong\u003E: a surface closed by edges. You can draw a face directly with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, or outline one edge at a time with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E. Faces are what \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/a\u003E turns into solids.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-rectangle\"\u003EDraw a rectangle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first corner: on the ground, or on any face of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer. The rectangle stays on the ground (or on the face you started on), and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E) shows its width and depth. The tip says &quot;Square&quot; when the sides are equal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo draw on another plane, point at it: a face that runs through the first corner puts the rectangle on that face, and snapping to a point off the plane (an endpoint, an edge, the blue axis) stands the rectangle up through both corners. Snap back to a point on the first plane and it lies down again. To keep one plane, press an arrow key: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the rectangle level (the blue plane), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the red plane, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the green plane. Press the same key again to unlock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner, or type the two sizes separated by a comma and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E4000,3000\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model, or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;,10&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E in feet and inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face appears with four edges around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe typed sizes go in the direction you were pointing: if the pointer is to the left of the first corner when you type, the rectangle grows to the left.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe comma separates the two sizes, so it cannot be a decimal mark. Type \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, never \u003Ccode\u003E2,5m\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-circle-or-a-polygon\"\u003EDraw a circle or a polygon\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a circle or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a polygon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the number of sides, type it before you click: \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E (or just \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A circle starts with 24 sides and a polygon with 6. The tip shows the current number, and ModelCraft keeps it for the next circle until you change it or switch tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center, on the ground or on a face. To center it on a face, point at the face&#039;s middle (&quot;Center of face&quot;) or at the middle of one of its edges (&quot;Midpoint&quot;) with the pointer just inside that face: the circle is drawn on that face, even on the narrow end of a long box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to set the radius, or type it (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E in the value box) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA circle is a polygon with many sides. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened so the cylinder reads as one smooth surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-an-arc\"\u003EDraw an arc\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the arc starts, then where it ends (or type the length between them).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer to bulge the arc out, and click. The tip says &quot;Half circle&quot; when the bulge is half the distance between the ends. Or type how far the middle stands off: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo draw an arc around a point instead, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc center\u003C/span\u003E: click the center, then where the arc starts (or type the radius), then where it ends (or type the angle in degrees, such as \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E). It snaps to 15° steps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn arc started on a face is drawn on that face; otherwise it lies on the ground, or stands upright when its two ends are at different heights. An arc has 12 segments; type \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E while drawing for more. Arcs close faces just as lines do: an arc across the corner of a rectangle splits it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-smooth-curve\"\u003EDraw a smooth curve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E draws a smooth line that passes through every point you click: a cable, a handrail&#039;s path, a garden edge. It can leave the ground: points snap to corners, edges and faces anywhere, and the arrow keys lock red, green or blue as with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the points the curve goes through. Type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;0,0,1&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a point 1 above the last one, \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E for an exact place, or a length for a point that far toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or double-click to finish. Click the first point again to close the curve. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the last point back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA curve selects as one piece. To change it, select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit points\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E: its points show as squares. Click one, then click where it goes (or type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts a point back, and ends editing. A closed flat curve makes a face; a curve that outlines a face can no longer be edited.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face\"\u003EOffset: a border inside or outside a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E copies a face&#039;s outline a set distance inside it (or outside it), for a frame, a border, a sill or the glass of a screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the face, or select the face first and then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer into the face for an inner outline, or out of it for an outer one. The new outline follows, and the tip says how far.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe face splits in two: the inner face and a frame around it (or a new ring outside). Push/pull either one straight away: for a screen, draw the box, push it to its thickness, offset the front face 30 mm inward and push the inner face in a little for the glass. Holes in the face are offset too, and an arc or a circle stays one (smaller or larger) curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDouble-click\u003C/strong\u003E a face with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E to offset it by the last distance again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges:\u003C/strong\u003E select a chain of connected edges in one plane (for example the two lines of an L), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move to the side you want and type the distance: a parallel copy is drawn, with mitred corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the distance is more than the face allows, ModelCraft says so and draws nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves\"\u003EArcs, circles and polygons are curves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick any segment of an arc, a circle or a polygon and the whole curve is selected. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E then shows its radius, its segments, its angle (arcs) and its length. Type a new radius there to resize it about its center; faces drawn in its plane follow. Once a curve&#039;s face has been pushed or pulled, its radius stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-with-lines\"\u003EDraw with lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the line starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it ends, or type a length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The typed length goes in the direction the pointer shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep clicking. Each click starts the next line from the end of the last one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the outline by clicking the first point again. When the lines close a flat loop, it fills with a face and the tool stops chaining. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop at any time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines follow the red, green and blue directions when you get close to them; the line turns that color and the tip says &quot;On red axis&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E for how to lock a direction and snap to points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"where-a-line-goes-when-it-touches-nothing\"\u003EWhere a line goes when it touches nothing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line that starts on a face stays in that face&#039;s plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line that starts on the ground, or in empty space, stays level with its start point. If you look at the model edge-on, it goes in the vertical plane facing you instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo draw a line to an exact place, type a point: \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E for a point in the model, or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for an offset from the start. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"split-a-face\"\u003ESplit a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw a line across a face, from one edge to another (or from edge to edge through other lines). The face splits into two faces. Each keeps its side and color. The same works with a rectangle or circle drawn on a face: the shape becomes its own face inside the bigger one, which now has a hole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is how you lay out rooms on a floor slab, a window on a wall, or a step on a stair: draw the outline on the face, then push or pull the new face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"faces-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EFaces facing the wrong way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery face has a front and a back. The back shows blue-grey. Faces drawn on the ground face up; faces drawn on another face face the same way it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a face shows its back where you expect the front, select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-see-while-drawing\"\u003EWhat you see while drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA colored marker where the pointer snaps, and its name in the tip at the bottom left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview of the line or shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe live size in the value box. You can type over it at any time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-shape-is-not-where-you-expected\"\u003EIf the shape is not where you expected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a touch screen, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a line snapped to something far behind, orbit a little: points hidden behind a face never snap, but points on its edge do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to undo the last shape; one undo removes one line or one shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Everything in ModelCraft starts as a flat face: a surface closed by edges. You can draw a face directly with Rectangle, Circle or Polygon, or outline one edge at a time with Line. Faces are what Push/pull turns into solids. Draw a rectangle Press R. Click the first corner: on the ground, or on any face of the model. Move the pointer. The rectangle stays on the ground (or on the face you started on), and the value box (Dimensions) shows its width and depth. The tip says \"Square\" when the sides are equal. To draw on another plane, point at it: a face that runs through the first corner puts the rectangle on that face, and snapping to a point off the plane (an endpoint, an edge, the blue axis) stands the rectangle up through both corners. Snap back to a point on the first plane and it lies down again. To keep one plane, press an arrow key: ↑ keeps the rectangle level (the blue plane), → on the red plane, ← on the green plane. Press the same key again to unlock. Click the opposite corner, or type the two sizes separated by a comma and press Enter: 4m,3m, 4000,3000 in a millimeter model, or 12',10'6\" in feet and inches. A face appears with four edges around it. The typed sizes go in the direction you were pointing: if the pointer is to the left of the first corner when you type, the rectangle grows to the left. The comma separates the two sizes, so it cannot be a decimal mark. Type 2.5m,3m, never 2,5m. Draw a circle or a polygon Press C for a circle or N for a polygon. To change the number of sides, type it before you click: 12s (or just 12) and Enter. A circle starts with 24 sides and a polygon with 6. The tip shows the current number, and ModelCraft keeps it for the next circle until you change it or switch tools. Click the center, on the ground or on a face. To center it on a face, point at the face's middle (\"Center of face\") or at the middle of one of its edges (\"Midpoint\") with the pointer just inside that face: the circle is drawn on that face, even on the narrow end of a long box. Click to set the radius, or type it (Radius in the value box) and press Enter. A circle is a polygon with many sides. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened so the cylinder reads as one smooth surface. Draw an arc Press A (Arc). Click where the arc starts, then where it ends (or type the length between them). Move the pointer to bulge the arc out, and click. The tip says \"Half circle\" when the bulge is half the distance between the ends. Or type how far the middle stands off: 2 and Enter. To draw an arc around a point instead, use Arc center: click the center, then where the arc starts (or type the radius), then where it ends (or type the angle in degrees, such as 90). It snaps to 15° steps. An arc started on a face is drawn on that face; otherwise it lies on the ground, or stands upright when its two ends are at different heights. An arc has 12 segments; type 24s while drawing for more. Arcs close faces just as lines do: an arc across the corner of a rectangle splits it. Draw a smooth curve Curve draws a smooth line that passes through every point you click: a cable, a handrail's path, a garden edge. It can leave the ground: points snap to corners, edges and faces anywhere, and the arrow keys lock red, green or blue as with Line. Click Curve in the Draw group. Click the points the curve goes through. Type \u003C0,0,1\u003E and Enter for a point 1 above the last one, [x,y,z] for an exact place, or a length for a point that far toward the pointer. Press Enter or double-click to finish. Click the first point again to close the curve. Backspace takes the last point back. A curve selects as one piece. To change it, select it and click Edit points in Entity info: its points show as squares. Click one, then click where it goes (or type \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E). Esc puts a point back, and ends editing. A closed flat curve makes a face; a curve that outlines a face can no longer be edited. Offset: a border inside or outside a face Offset copies a face's outline a set distance inside it (or outside it), for a frame, a border, a sill or the glass of a screen. Press F and click the face, or select the face first and then press F. Move the pointer into the face for an inner outline, or out of it for an outer one. The new outline follows, and the tip says how far. Click, or type the distance and press Enter. The face splits in two: the inner face and a frame around it (or a new ring outside). Push/pull either one straight away: for a screen, draw the box, push it to its thickness, offset the front face 30 mm inward and push the inner face in a little for the glass. Holes in the face are offset too, and an arc or a circle stays one (smaller or larger) curve. Double-click a face with Offset to offset it by the last distance again. Edges: select a chain of connected edges in one plane (for example the two lines of an L), press F, move to the side you want and type the distance: a parallel copy is drawn, with mitred corners. When the distance is more than the face allows, ModelCraft says so and draws nothing. Arcs, circles and polygons are curves Click any segment of an arc, a circle or a polygon and the whole curve is selected. Entity info then shows its radius, its segments, its angle (arcs) and its length. Type a new radius there to resize it about its center; faces drawn in its plane follow. Once a curve's face has been pushed or pulled, its radius stays as it is. Draw with lines Press L. Click where the line starts. Click where it ends, or type a length and press Enter. The typed length goes in the direction the pointer shows. Keep clicking. Each click starts the next line from the end of the last one. Close the outline by clicking the first point again. When the lines close a flat loop, it fills with a face and the tool stops chaining. Press Esc to stop at any time. Lines follow the red, green and blue directions when you get close to them; the line turns that color and the tip says \"On red axis\". See Inference and snapping for how to lock a direction and snap to points. Where a line goes when it touches nothing A line that starts on a face stays in that face's plane. A line that starts on the ground, or in empty space, stays level with its start point. If you look at the model edge-on, it goes in the vertical plane facing you instead. To draw a line to an exact place, type a point: [x,y,z] for a point in the model, or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for an offset from the start. See Type exact sizes. Split a face Draw a line across a face, from one edge to another (or from edge to edge through other lines). The face splits into two faces. Each keeps its side and color. The same works with a rectangle or circle drawn on a face: the shape becomes its own face inside the bigger one, which now has a hole. This is how you lay out rooms on a floor slab, a window on a wall, or a step on a stair: draw the outline on the face, then push or pull the new face. Faces facing the wrong way Every face has a front and a back. The back shows blue-grey. Faces drawn on the ground face up; faces drawn on another face face the same way it does. If a face shows its back where you expect the front, select it and click Reverse face in Entity info. What you see while drawing A colored marker where the pointer snaps, and its name in the tip at the bottom left. A preview of the line or shape. The live size in the value box. You can type over it at any time. If the shape is not where you expected On a touch screen, see Troubleshooting. If a line snapped to something far behind, orbit a little: points hidden behind a face never snap, but points on its edge do. Press Ctrl+Z to undo the last shape; one undo removes one line or one shape.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"push-pull","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Push and pull faces into solids","summary":"Turn a face into a box, make a building taller or shorter, add a bay or a recess, and repeat a distance with a double-click.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["push pull","extrude","height","solid","box","mass","taller","recess","bump out","repeat","double-click","volume","level","on face","flush","same height"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-solid-from-a-face","text":"Make a solid from a face","level":2},{"id":"pull-to-the-height-of-something-else","text":"Pull to the height of something else","level":2},{"id":"make-a-box-taller-or-shorter","text":"Make a box taller or shorter","level":2},{"id":"add-a-bay-or-a-recess","text":"Add a bay or a recess","level":2},{"id":"push-all-the-way-through","text":"Push all the way through","level":2},{"id":"type-a-distance","text":"Type a distance","level":2},{"id":"repeat-the-last-distance","text":"Repeat the last distance","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) moves a face along its own direction: up and down for a floor, in and out for a wall. What happens depends on the face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-solid-from-a-face\"\u003EMake a solid from a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a face, for example a rectangle on the ground (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003EDraw faces\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHover the face. It highlights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the face, then move the pointer. The face rises (or sinks) and sides grow from its edges. The value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E) and the tip show the distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2700\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have a closed solid. Select it all and group it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E then shows &quot;Solid: Yes&quot; and its volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA lone face can go either way: pull it up for a mass above the ground, push it down for a pit or a foundation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of clicking twice, press on the face, drag, and release. On a touch screen that is the natural way: press, drag, lift.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pull-to-the-height-of-something-else\"\u003EPull to the height of something else\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you pull, hover a corner, a midpoint, an edge or a guide of another part of the model. The face snaps to that height and a marker shows the point. Use it to make two blocks the same height, or to stop at a guide line you placed with the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Etape measure\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also hover a face that is parallel to the one you are pushing, such as the top of another block or the top of the box a post stands on. The tip says &quot;On face&quot;, and the face you are pushing ends level with it. For example, push the inner face of a pipe down until it is level with the box under the pipe.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face at an angle to the one you are pushing doesn&#039;t set a level. The distance follows the pointer. So does a face behind the face you are pushing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a pushed face ends flush with the faces around it again, its sides go away. No zero-height sides are left over, and the outline you drew stays on the face, so you can push it again later. To make it one face again, erase the outline&#039;s edges with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E: the two faces join.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-box-taller-or-shorter\"\u003EMake a box taller or shorter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPush/pull the top of a box. Because the sides already run straight up from it, the top simply moves: the box gets taller or shorter, and no new edges are made.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPushing the top all the way down to the bottom removes the box. ModelCraft stops at zero height; it never turns the box inside out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe same goes for any face whose neighbors all run along its direction, such as the end of a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-bay-or-a-recess\"\u003EAdd a bay or a recess\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle on the side of a box (start it on the face; it stays in the face&#039;s plane).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and pull the new rectangle \u003Cstrong\u003Eout\u003C/strong\u003E. It adds material: a bay, a chimney, a canopy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr push it \u003Cstrong\u003Ein\u003C/strong\u003E. It cuts a recess: a porch, a niche, a set-back entrance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the sides of the new part line up with faces already there, they merge into one face, so you don&#039;t get stray lines on a flat wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-all-the-way-through\"\u003EPush all the way through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPushing a face into a solid stops at the first face behind it. When it reaches that face, it cuts an opening through. That is how you make windows and doors in a wall with thickness. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/openings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/openings\"\u003ECut an opening through a wall\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-distance\"\u003EType a distance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter clicking the face, type the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe typed distance goes the way you were moving. If you haven&#039;t moved yet, it goes toward the front of the face (out of a solid, up from the ground).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a negative number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E-500\u003C/code\u003E, to go the other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you change your mind before clicking, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The face goes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"repeat-the-last-distance\"\u003ERepeat the last distance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter one push/pull, double-click another face with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E still selected. It moves by the same distance. This is the fast way to give several walls or several floors the same thickness or height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft remembers the distance until you switch to another tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull works on one face at a time. If you click something that is not a face, the status bar says &quot;Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo push/pull a face inside a group or component, open it first (double-click it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other: their faces just pass through. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Push/pull (P) moves a face along its own direction: up and down for a floor, in and out for a wall. What happens depends on the face. Make a solid from a face Draw a face, for example a rectangle on the ground (Draw faces). Press P. Hover the face. It highlights. Click the face, then move the pointer. The face rises (or sinks) and sides grow from its edges. The value box (Distance) and the tip show the distance. Click to finish, or type a distance and press Enter: 3m, 10', 2700 in a millimeter model. You now have a closed solid. Select it all and group it (see Groups and components): Entity info then shows \"Solid: Yes\" and its volume. A lone face can go either way: pull it up for a mass above the ground, push it down for a pit or a foundation. Instead of clicking twice, press on the face, drag, and release. On a touch screen that is the natural way: press, drag, lift. Pull to the height of something else While you pull, hover a corner, a midpoint, an edge or a guide of another part of the model. The face snaps to that height and a marker shows the point. Use it to make two blocks the same height, or to stop at a guide line you placed with the tape measure. You can also hover a face that is parallel to the one you are pushing, such as the top of another block or the top of the box a post stands on. The tip says \"On face\", and the face you are pushing ends level with it. For example, push the inner face of a pipe down until it is level with the box under the pipe. A face at an angle to the one you are pushing doesn't set a level. The distance follows the pointer. So does a face behind the face you are pushing. When a pushed face ends flush with the faces around it again, its sides go away. No zero-height sides are left over, and the outline you drew stays on the face, so you can push it again later. To make it one face again, erase the outline's edges with the Eraser: the two faces join. Make a box taller or shorter Push/pull the top of a box. Because the sides already run straight up from it, the top simply moves: the box gets taller or shorter, and no new edges are made. Pushing the top all the way down to the bottom removes the box. ModelCraft stops at zero height; it never turns the box inside out. The same goes for any face whose neighbors all run along its direction, such as the end of a wall. Add a bay or a recess Draw a rectangle on the side of a box (start it on the face; it stays in the face's plane). Press P and pull the new rectangle out. It adds material: a bay, a chimney, a canopy. Or push it in. It cuts a recess: a porch, a niche, a set-back entrance. When the sides of the new part line up with faces already there, they merge into one face, so you don't get stray lines on a flat wall. Push all the way through Pushing a face into a solid stops at the first face behind it. When it reaches that face, it cuts an opening through. That is how you make windows and doors in a wall with thickness. See Cut an opening through a wall. Type a distance After clicking the face, type the distance and press Enter. The typed distance goes the way you were moving. If you haven't moved yet, it goes toward the front of the face (out of a solid, up from the ground). Type a negative number, such as -500, to go the other way. If you change your mind before clicking, press Esc. The face goes back. Repeat the last distance After one push/pull, double-click another face with Push/pull still selected. It moves by the same distance. This is the fast way to give several walls or several floors the same thickness or height. ModelCraft remembers the distance until you switch to another tool. Things to know Push/pull works on one face at a time. If you click something that is not a face, the status bar says \"Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.\" To push/pull a face inside a group or component, open it first (double-click it). Two solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other: their faces just pass through. See Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"divide","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Divide an edge or curve into equal parts","summary":"Split a line into equal pieces, or give an arc or circle a new number of segments, with the Divide tool.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["divide","divide edge","equal parts","equal segments","split edge","segments","subdivide","subdivide edges","arc segments","circle segments","points along a line"],"headings":[{"id":"divide-an-edge","text":"Divide an edge","level":2},{"id":"divide-an-arc-or-a-circle","text":"Divide an arc or a circle","level":2},{"id":"with-the-modeler-keys","text":"With the modeler keys","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDivide\u003C/span\u003E cuts an edge into equal pieces, with a corner at each cut. Use the corners to snap to, to start lines from, or to push and pull parts of a face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"divide-an-edge\"\u003EDivide an edge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDivide\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or select an edge and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDivide…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the edge. Marks show where the cuts will go: over the middle of the edge it is two parts; move toward an end for more.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to divide it into the parts shown. Or type a number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E5s\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003E/5\u003C/code\u003E), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: it divides at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pieces are left selected, and it is one undo step. A face the edge belongs to keeps its shape and gains the new corners.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"divide-an-arc-or-a-circle\"\u003EDivide an arc or a circle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoint at any segment of an arc or circle: it divides as a whole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn arc or circle whose corners nothing else uses, lying flat on its face, is \u003Cstrong\u003Edrawn again with that many segments\u003C/strong\u003E on the true arc. Its ends stay where they are, and it stays one curve, so you can still select it with one click and change its radius in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. A 24-sided circle divided into 8 becomes an octagon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAny other curve — the rim of a pulled cylinder, a curve drawn with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E, an arc with lines joined to its corners — gets new corners at equal lengths along it, and nothing moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-the-modeler-keys\"\u003EWith the modeler keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool is the same with the modeler keys (Settings › ModelCraft › Keys, or the Modeler menu style), where it is in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMesh\u003C/span\u003E menu as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubdivide edges\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETyped numbers go from 2 to 1000; moving the pointer gives up to 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA curve drawn with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E keeps its points: after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit points\u003C/span\u003E, it is drawn again from them and the new corners are gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003EDraw faces with lines, rectangles, circles and arcs\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes in the value box\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Divide cuts an edge into equal pieces, with a corner at each cut. Use the corners to snap to, to start lines from, or to push and pull parts of a face. Divide an edge Click Divide in the toolbar, or select an edge and click Divide… in Entity info. Point at the edge. Marks show where the cuts will go: over the middle of the edge it is two parts; move toward an end for more. Click to divide it into the parts shown. Or type a number, such as 5 (or 5s, or /5), and press Enter: it divides at once. The pieces are left selected, and it is one undo step. A face the edge belongs to keeps its shape and gains the new corners. Divide an arc or a circle Point at any segment of an arc or circle: it divides as a whole. An arc or circle whose corners nothing else uses, lying flat on its face, is drawn again with that many segments on the true arc. Its ends stay where they are, and it stays one curve, so you can still select it with one click and change its radius in Entity info. A 24-sided circle divided into 8 becomes an octagon. Any other curve — the rim of a pulled cylinder, a curve drawn with Curve, an arc with lines joined to its corners — gets new corners at equal lengths along it, and nothing moves. With the modeler keys The tool is the same with the modeler keys (Settings › ModelCraft › Keys, or the Modeler menu style), where it is in the Mesh menu as Subdivide edges. Things to know Typed numbers go from 2 to 1000; moving the pointer gives up to 100. A curve drawn with Curve keeps its points: after Edit points, it is drawn again from them and the new corners are gone. See also Draw faces with lines, rectangles, circles and arcs and Type exact sizes in the value box.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scale-factors","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Scale by factors and shrinkage allowance","summary":"Scale a selection by a factor for each axis about a point you choose, or make a pattern bigger by the percent a cast part will shrink.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["scale","non-uniform","factor","percent","stretch","shrinkage","shrink","casting","pattern","foundry","allowance","resize"],"headings":[{"id":"shrinkage-allowance","text":"Shrinkage allowance","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe Scale tool (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) scales evenly or drags a grip to stretch one or two directions. \u003Cstrong\u003EScale by factors…\u003C/strong\u003E (Tools menu, or the Modify group) is the typed form: a number for each of the three axes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect edges, faces, groups or components.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale by factors…\u003C/span\u003E. Type a factor for red, green and blue. A factor can be \u003Ccode\u003E1.015\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E101.5%\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E+1.5%\u003C/code\u003E (and \u003Ccode\u003E-2%\u003C/code\u003E for smaller). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESame on all three axes\u003C/span\u003E copies the first to the other two.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what \u003Cstrong\u003Estays put\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle of the selection, its lowest corner, the middle of its bottom, or the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line under the fields shows the size now and after. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E does it in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe axes are those of the group you have open (the model&#039;s, at the top level). A group or component that is turned comes out sheared if its factors differ; scale the group that holds it instead. The contents of a component are not touched, so its other copies stay as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shrinkage-allowance\"\u003EShrinkage allowance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cast part shrinks as it cools, so its pattern is made bigger. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EShrinkage allowance\u003C/strong\u003E type the percent for each axis (your foundry or pattern maker gives it for your metal; 1.5 % below is only an example) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse as the factors\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe percent itself\u003C/strong\u003E makes the factor 1 plus the percent: 1.5 % gives 1.015, what a shrink rule marks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExactly enough\u003C/strong\u003E makes the factor 1 divided by one minus the percent: 1.5 % gives 1.0152, so the casting comes out at the part&#039;s size after it shrinks that much.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour last percents are remembered in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe percent is yours to give: ModelCraft knows nothing about your metal, mould or process.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The Scale tool (S) scales evenly or drags a grip to stretch one or two directions. Scale by factors… (Tools menu, or the Modify group) is the typed form: a number for each of the three axes. Select edges, faces, groups or components. Open Scale by factors…. Type a factor for red, green and blue. A factor can be 1.015, 101.5% or +1.5% (and -2% for smaller). Same on all three axes copies the first to the other two. Choose what stays put: the middle of the selection, its lowest corner, the middle of its bottom, or the origin. The line under the fields shows the size now and after. Scale does it in one undo step. The axes are those of the group you have open (the model's, at the top level). A group or component that is turned comes out sheared if its factors differ; scale the group that holds it instead. The contents of a component are not touched, so its other copies stay as they are. Shrinkage allowance A cast part shrinks as it cools, so its pattern is made bigger. Under Shrinkage allowance type the percent for each axis (your foundry or pattern maker gives it for your metal; 1.5 % below is only an example) and press Use as the factors. The percent itself makes the factor 1 plus the percent: 1.5 % gives 1.015, what a shrink rule marks. Exactly enough makes the factor 1 divided by one minus the percent: 1.5 % gives 1.0152, so the casting comes out at the part's size after it shrinks that much. Your last percents are remembered in this browser. The percent is yours to give: ModelCraft knows nothing about your metal, mould or process.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"follow-me","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Follow me: sweep a face along a path","summary":"Make pipes, cables, handrails, moldings and rings by sweeping a face along edges, arcs or a curve.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["follow me","sweep","path","pipe","cable","tube","handrail","molding","moulding","gutter","ring","torus","lathe","extrude along"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-cable","text":"Make a cable","level":2},{"id":"without-selecting-the-path-first","text":"Without selecting the path first","level":2},{"id":"what-to-know","text":"What to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E moves a face along a path and builds the solid it passes through. The face keeps its shape and stays square to the path, and at each corner of the path the pieces meet on a clean mitre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-cable\"\u003EMake a cable\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the path: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E through the points the cable passes (it can rise and fall), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003Es and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003Es joined end to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the start of the path, draw the cable&#039;s section: a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E across the path. Start the circle on the path&#039;s end point and turn it to face along the path; or draw it on a face that crosses the path there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the path (one click selects a whole curve or arc; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds more edges).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E group, then click the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tube is a closed solid, with smooth (soft) sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-selecting-the-path-first\"\u003EWithout selecting the path first\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E, click the face, then click any edge of the path. The path is the chain of edges through that edge, up to where three or more edges meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-know\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClosed paths\u003C/strong\u003E (a circle, a closed curve, a rectangle&#039;s outline) make a ring: the face is used up and the ring closes on itself. Draw a circle on the ground for the path and a small circle standing across it for a donut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOff the path:\u003C/strong\u003E the face does not have to touch the path. A profile drawn beside a path sweeps along beside it, like a molding along the top of a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe path must not branch\u003C/strong\u003E, and must be one connected chain. ModelCraft says so when it is not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA face lying along the path\u003C/strong\u003E (in the same plane as the path&#039;s first edge) cannot be swept: turn it to cross the path.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Follow me moves a face along a path and builds the solid it passes through. The face keeps its shape and stays square to the path, and at each corner of the path the pieces meet on a clean mitre. Make a cable Draw the path: a Curve through the points the cable passes (it can rise and fall), or Lines and Arcs joined end to end. At the start of the path, draw the cable's section: a small Circle across the path. Start the circle on the path's end point and turn it to face along the path; or draw it on a face that crosses the path there. Select the path (one click selects a whole curve or arc; Ctrl+click adds more edges). Click Follow me in the Modify group, then click the circle. The tube is a closed solid, with smooth (soft) sides. Without selecting the path first Click Follow me, click the face, then click any edge of the path. The path is the chain of edges through that edge, up to where three or more edges meet. What to know Closed paths (a circle, a closed curve, a rectangle's outline) make a ring: the face is used up and the ring closes on itself. Draw a circle on the ground for the path and a small circle standing across it for a donut. Off the path: the face does not have to touch the path. A profile drawn beside a path sweeps along beside it, like a molding along the top of a wall. The path must not branch, and must be one connected chain. ModelCraft says so when it is not. A face lying along the path (in the same plane as the path's first edge) cannot be swept: turn it to cross the path. It is one undo step.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"text3d","scope":"modelcraft","title":"3D text","summary":"Add lettering to a model as faces, flat or solid, standing on any face: a sign, a house number, a name on a door.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["3d text","text","letters","lettering","sign","signage","house number","label","engrave","font","words"],"headings":[{"id":"add-text","text":"Add text","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D Text\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group makes lettering out of faces, which you can paint, push/pull or cut into a solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-text\"\u003EAdd text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D Text\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the text. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the box for a second line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELetter height\u003C/span\u003E (the height of a capital letter, such as \u003Ccode\u003E30cm\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1&#039;\u003C/code\u003E) and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Cstrong\u003Eflat letters\u003C/strong\u003E: faces lying on whatever you place them on, ready to paint or to push/pull.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA depth such as \u003Ccode\u003E5cm\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Cstrong\u003Esolid letters\u003C/strong\u003E, closed solids you can use with the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E, then click where the text goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a face, the text lies on that face, reading left to right: on a wall it stands upright; on the ground or a table it lies flat along red. Away from any face it lies on the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E named after it, so every copy changes if you edit one, and it shows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E list to place again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe letters are ModelCraft&#039;s own single-stroke font, thickened: clean and readable, with no choice of typeface yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELetters, digits and common signs (including accented letters) are drawn; a character the font does not have shows as a small box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the words, delete the text and add it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo cut lettering into a surface, make solid letters, place them half-way into a solid group, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esubtract\u003C/a\u003E them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"3D Text in the Draw group makes lettering out of faces, which you can paint, push/pull or cut into a solid. Add text Click 3D Text. Type the text. Press Enter in the box for a second line. Set the Letter height (the height of a capital letter, such as 30cm or 1') and the Depth: 0 gives flat letters: faces lying on whatever you place them on, ready to paint or to push/pull. A depth such as 5cm gives solid letters, closed solids you can use with the solid tools. Click Place, then click where the text goes. On a face, the text lies on that face, reading left to right: on a wall it stands upright; on the ground or a table it lies flat along red. Away from any face it lies on the ground. The text is a component named after it, so every copy changes if you edit one, and it shows in the Components list to place again. Things to know The letters are ModelCraft's own single-stroke font, thickened: clean and readable, with no choice of typeface yet. Letters, digits and common signs (including accented letters) are drawn; a character the font does not have shows as a small box. To change the words, delete the text and add it again. To cut lettering into a surface, make solid letters, place them half-way into a solid group, and subtract them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"starter","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Starter library","summary":"About thirty ready-made things to place in a model: doors and windows, furniture, kitchen and bath, columns and stairs, trees, people and a car.","section":"tasks","order":39,"keywords":["furnish","furnish a room","furniture layout","bedroom","living room","kitchen","bathroom","office","rendering","library","starter","entourage","trees","people","cars","plants","shrubs","hedge","components","furniture","door","window","chair","table","sofa","bed","kitchen","bathroom","toilet","stair","column","tree","person","car","warehouse","ready-made"],"headings":[{"id":"place-one","text":"Place one","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-it","text":"What is in it","level":2},{"id":"furnish-a-room-for-a-rendering","text":"Furnish a room for a rendering","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/span\u003E gives you everyday things at real sizes, so a model reads at a glance: a door to show the scale of a room, a car in a driveway, people and trees around a building.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-one\"\u003EPlace one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E on the left, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a thing, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChair\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a floor, a table or the ground, it stands on it. \u003Cstrong\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/strong\u003E are made to go in walls: click on a wall and they stand in it, facing out. (They sit on the wall&#039;s face; they do not cut an opening. To make one, draw a rectangle on the wall where the window goes and push it through.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach thing is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E: picking the same one again places another copy, and every copy changes if you edit one. It also shows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E list, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E to add more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-it\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThings\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoor, double door, window, wide window, garage door\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFurniture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDining table, round table, chair, bar stool, sofa, armchair, double and single bed, desk, bookshelf, wardrobe\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKitchen and bath\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase and wall cabinet, kitchen counter, fridge, stove, sink unit, toilet, bathtub, shower, washbasin\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilding\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight stair (15 risers of 18 cm), round and square column, beam\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESite and people\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETree, shrub, person (1.75 m), car\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEntourage: trees, people and cars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeciduous trees (4, 6 and 10 m), a conifer, a columnar tree, round and small shrubs, a hedge, a potted plant, people standing and walking, a child, a sedan and an SUV: rounder shapes in their own colors, for \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Erendered pictures\u003C/a\u003E and site plans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are simple shapes, made of solids you can paint, scale or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Emake unique\u003C/a\u003E and change. The wall cabinet hangs at 1.45 m above where you click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe entourage is drawn by CadCraft and free to use for anything (CC0); the same trees, people and cars are in the Library library for BimCraft and CadCraft. A car&#039;s windows are painted with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETinted glass\u003C/span\u003E material, so they are see-through in pictures.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"furnish-a-room-for-a-rendering\"\u003EFurnish a room for a rendering\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the floor of a room (one level face looking up, in the model or inside a group) and use \u003Cstrong\u003EOrganize › Furnish a room…\u003C/strong\u003E (the \u003Cstrong\u003EFurnish\u003C/strong\u003E button). Pick what the room is — bedroom, living room, dining room, kitchen, bathroom or office — or let it guess from the size. The starter furniture goes against the walls from the longest wall round, nothing overlaps, and the swing of any starter door placed in the room&#039;s walls (and the width of its windows, in front of tall pieces) stays free. It is a default layout by rules for you to review, not a design: the same room always gets the same layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything comes in \u003Cstrong\u003Eone group\u003C/strong\u003E on the tag \u003Cstrong\u003EFurniture\u003C/strong\u003E, as one undo step: Undo, delete the group or hide the tag to take it away. A piece that does not fit is named in the status line and listed in \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E as a note. BimCraft&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EFurnish a room\u003C/strong\u003E uses the same rules.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Starter library gives you everyday things at real sizes, so a model reads at a glance: a door to show the scale of a room, a car in a driveway, people and trees around a building. Place one In Components on the left, click Starter library. Pick a thing, such as Chair. Click where it goes. On a floor, a table or the ground, it stands on it. Doors and windows are made to go in walls: click on a wall and they stand in it, facing out. (They sit on the wall's face; they do not cut an opening. To make one, draw a rectangle on the wall where the window goes and push it through.) Each thing is a component: picking the same one again places another copy, and every copy changes if you edit one. It also shows in the Components list, with Place to add more. What is in it Group Things Doors and windows Door, double door, window, wide window, garage door Furniture Dining table, round table, chair, bar stool, sofa, armchair, double and single bed, desk, bookshelf, wardrobe Kitchen and bath Base and wall cabinet, kitchen counter, fridge, stove, sink unit, toilet, bathtub, shower, washbasin Building Straight stair (15 risers of 18 cm), round and square column, beam Site and people Tree, shrub, person (1.75 m), car Entourage: trees, people and cars Deciduous trees (4, 6 and 10 m), a conifer, a columnar tree, round and small shrubs, a hedge, a potted plant, people standing and walking, a child, a sedan and an SUV: rounder shapes in their own colors, for rendered pictures and site plans They are simple shapes, made of solids you can paint, scale or make unique and change. The wall cabinet hangs at 1.45 m above where you click. The entourage is drawn by CadCraft and free to use for anything (CC0); the same trees, people and cars are in the Library library for BimCraft and CadCraft. A car's windows are painted with the Tinted glass material, so they are see-through in pictures. Furnish a room for a rendering Select the floor of a room (one level face looking up, in the model or inside a group) and use Organize › Furnish a room… (the Furnish button). Pick what the room is — bedroom, living room, dining room, kitchen, bathroom or office — or let it guess from the size. The starter furniture goes against the walls from the longest wall round, nothing overlaps, and the swing of any starter door placed in the room's walls (and the width of its windows, in front of tall pieces) stays free. It is a default layout by rules for you to review, not a design: the same room always gets the same layout. Everything comes in one group on the tag Furniture, as one undo step: Undo, delete the group or hide the tag to take it away. A piece that does not fit is named in the status line and listed in Problems as a note. BimCraft's Furnish a room uses the same rules.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"openings","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Cut an opening through a wall","summary":"Draw a window or door on a wall with thickness and push it through, so the wall gets a real hole.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["window","door","opening","hole","wall","cut","push through","void","thickness","facade"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-wall-to-practice-on","text":"Make a wall to practice on","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-opening","text":"Draw the opening","level":2},{"id":"push-it-through","text":"Push it through","level":2},{"id":"a-door","text":"A door","level":2},{"id":"many-windows-of-the-same-size","text":"Many windows of the same size","level":2},{"id":"if-it-did-not-cut-through","text":"If it did not cut through","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn opening needs a wall with two sides: a box, or a wall you made by pulling a long thin rectangle up. A single face standing on its own has nothing to push through to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-wall-to-practice-on\"\u003EMake a wall to practice on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a corner on the ground and type \u003Ccode\u003E6m,0.3m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E20&#039;,12&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click the rectangle and type \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou have a wall 300 mm (12 in) thick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-opening\"\u003EDraw the opening\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrbit so you can see the long side of the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHover the wall&#039;s face, then click where the window&#039;s lower corner goes. The tip says &quot;On face&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the window&#039;s width and height, such as \u003Ccode\u003E1.2m,1.5m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E4&#039;,5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe window is a face of its own inside the wall face, which now has a hole around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo place it at an exact distance from the corner and the floor, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Eguides\u003C/a\u003E first: with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E, drag guide lines off the wall&#039;s bottom and end edges, then start the rectangle where two guides cross (the tip says &quot;On guide&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-it-through\"\u003EPush it through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the window face and push it into the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove until it stops at the back face, then click. Or type the wall thickness, \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft stops at the face behind and opens it: the wall now has a hole straight through, with four side faces (jambs, head and sill) lining it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E still calls the wall a closed solid when it is grouped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you push less than the full thickness, you get a recess instead of a hole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-door\"\u003EA door\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the rectangle starting on the bottom edge of the wall face (the tip says &quot;On edge&quot; or &quot;Endpoint&quot;), so the opening reaches the floor. Push it through the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"many-windows-of-the-same-size\"\u003EMany windows of the same size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the first opening.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor the next ones, draw the rectangles, then double-click each one with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E: a double-click repeats the last distance. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull#repeat-the-last-distance\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\" data-hash=\"repeat-the-last-distance\"\u003EPush and pull\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-did-not-cut-through\"\u003EIf it did not cut through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe rectangle must be drawn \u003Cstrong\u003Eon\u003C/strong\u003E the wall&#039;s face. If it was drawn in the air in front of it, it is a separate face: undo and draw it again, starting on the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe push stops at the first face behind the opening. If something else is in the way inside the wall (an inner face left from earlier drawing), it stops there; erase that face&#039;s edges and try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGeometry in a separate group is not cut. Open the group that holds the wall and draw the opening inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An opening needs a wall with two sides: a box, or a wall you made by pulling a long thin rectangle up. A single face standing on its own has nothing to push through to. Make a wall to practice on Press R, click a corner on the ground and type 6m,0.3m (or 20',12\"), then Enter. Press P, click the rectangle and type 3m (or 10'), then Enter. You have a wall 300 mm (12 in) thick. Draw the opening Orbit so you can see the long side of the wall. Press R. Hover the wall's face, then click where the window's lower corner goes. The tip says \"On face\". Type the window's width and height, such as 1.2m,1.5m (or 4',5'), and press Enter. The window is a face of its own inside the wall face, which now has a hole around it. To place it at an exact distance from the corner and the floor, use guides first: with Tape, drag guide lines off the wall's bottom and end edges, then start the rectangle where two guides cross (the tip says \"On guide\"). Push it through Press P. Click the window face and push it into the wall. Move until it stops at the back face, then click. Or type the wall thickness, 300 (or 12), and press Enter. ModelCraft stops at the face behind and opens it: the wall now has a hole straight through, with four side faces (jambs, head and sill) lining it. Entity info still calls the wall a closed solid when it is grouped. If you push less than the full thickness, you get a recess instead of a hole. A door Draw the rectangle starting on the bottom edge of the wall face (the tip says \"On edge\" or \"Endpoint\"), so the opening reaches the floor. Push it through the same way. Many windows of the same size Make the first opening. For the next ones, draw the rectangles, then double-click each one with Push/pull: a double-click repeats the last distance. See Push and pull. If it did not cut through The rectangle must be drawn on the wall's face. If it was drawn in the air in front of it, it is a separate face: undo and draw it again, starting on the face. The push stops at the first face behind the opening. If something else is in the way inside the wall (an inner face left from earlier drawing), it stops there; erase that face's edges and try again. Geometry in a separate group is not cut. Open the group that holds the wall and draw the opening inside it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"stretch","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Components that stretch","summary":"Make one copy of a component wider, deeper or taller while its parts keep their sizes: a longer table with the same legs, a taller bookshelf with its shelves spread out, a railing whose posts repeat.","section":"tasks","order":41,"keywords":["dynamic component","stretch","resize component","parametric","named sizes","repeat","array","legs","shelves","railing","rules","variables"],"headings":[{"id":"stretch-a-copy","text":"Stretch a copy","level":2},{"id":"the-rules","text":"The rules","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EScaling a component makes everything in it bigger, legs and rails too. \u003Cstrong\u003EStretch this copy\u003C/strong\u003E changes a component&#039;s width, depth or height while each part follows a rule: a table top grows, its legs keep their thickness and stay at the ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works on components made of parts, that is, groups or components inside the component, as every \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/starter\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/starter\"\u003Estarter library\u003C/a\u003E thing is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stretch-a-copy\"\u003EStretch a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch this copy…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (red)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth (green)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight (blue)\u003C/span\u003E, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly that copy changes: it becomes a component of its own, which remembers the original it came from. Stretch it again at any time, and it is worked out afresh from the original, so nothing drifts. Other copies of the original stay as they were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-rules\"\u003EThe rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the component (double-click it) and select one of its parts. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhen the component stretches\u003C/span\u003E, with a rule along red, green and blue:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERule\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the part does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFor example\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrows with the component; its distances to both sides stay\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA table top\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStay at the start\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStay at the end\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps its size and its distance to that side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELegs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove in proportion\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps its size; its middle moves in proportion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShelves, a door handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale in proportion\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts place and size both in proportion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe steps of a stair\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies of it, every so often (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery\u003C/span\u003E), from where it starts to the far side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe posts of a railing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic\u003C/span\u003E (the start) works one out and shows which: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E when the part spans the component, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove in proportion\u003C/span\u003E when it sits about in the middle, otherwise it stays at the side it is nearer to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe parts&#039; own shapes are shared, so a stretched copy is light. Editing a part changes it in every copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA stretched copy does not follow later changes to the original&#039;s rules or layout: stretch it again to bring them in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces drawn loose in the component (not in a part) keep their size; make them a part to have them follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERules and what each copy was stretched from are saved in the ModelCraft file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Scaling a component makes everything in it bigger, legs and rails too. Stretch this copy changes a component's width, depth or height while each part follows a rule: a table top grows, its legs keep their thickness and stay at the ends. It works on components made of parts, that is, groups or components inside the component, as every starter library thing is. Stretch a copy Select the component. In Entity info, click Stretch this copy…. Type the new Width (red), Depth (green) and Height (blue), and click Stretch. Only that copy changes: it becomes a component of its own, which remembers the original it came from. Stretch it again at any time, and it is worked out afresh from the original, so nothing drifts. Other copies of the original stay as they were. The rules Open the component (double-click it) and select one of its parts. Entity info shows When the component stretches, with a rule along red, green and blue: Rule What the part does For example Stretch Grows with the component; its distances to both sides stay A table top Stay at the start / Stay at the end Keeps its size and its distance to that side Legs Move in proportion Keeps its size; its middle moves in proportion Shelves, a door handle Scale in proportion Its place and size both in proportion The steps of a stair Repeat Copies of it, every so often (Every), from where it starts to the far side The posts of a railing Automatic (the start) works one out and shows which: Stretch when the part spans the component, Move in proportion when it sits about in the middle, otherwise it stays at the side it is nearer to. Things to know The parts' own shapes are shared, so a stretched copy is light. Editing a part changes it in every copy. A stretched copy does not follow later changes to the original's rules or layout: stretch it again to bring them in. Faces drawn loose in the component (not in a part) keep their size; make them a part to have them follow. Rules and what each copy was stretched from are saved in the ModelCraft file.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"paint","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Paint with colors and materials","summary":"Give faces, groups and components a solid color or a material such as brick, siding, shingles or wood, and keep them when you export.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["paint","color","colour","material","materials","texture","textures","brick","siding","shingles","roofing","wood","stone","tile","concrete","stucco","grass","gravel","glass","metal","paint bucket","fill","sample","eyedropper","default","glb","obj","mtl","collada"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-a-color","text":"Pick a color","level":2},{"id":"paint","text":"Paint","level":2},{"id":"paint-with-a-material","text":"Paint with a material","level":2},{"id":"components-and-colors","text":"Components and colors","level":2},{"id":"colors-in-files","text":"Colors in files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EColors help you tell parts of a model apart, and \u003Cstrong\u003Ematerials\u003C/strong\u003E (brick, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, grass, glass, metal) make it look like what it is built of. Both paint the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-a-color\"\u003EPick a color\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColors\u003C/span\u003E section on the right shows a row of solid colors, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E (the one crossed by a line: no color) and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E swatch for any other color. Click one to make it the current color. The Paint tool starts with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint\"\u003EPaint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E), or click a color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a face to paint it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a group or a component to paint that copy: its faces that have no color of their own take this color. Faces you painted inside it keep theirs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo paint several things at once, select them first and then click a color, or click any of them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E. It is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHold\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAnd click a face\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat gets painted\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery face connected to it that has the same color\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery face of that color in the group you are working in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: you pick up that color instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo take a face back to no color, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E and click it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint-with-a-material\"\u003EPaint with a material\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E section, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColors\u003C/span\u003E, lists the material library by kind: brick and block, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, ground, glass and metal. Hover a swatch for its name. Click one, then paint with it exactly as with a color: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click faces, groups or components; or select first and click the material.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003EShaded\u003C/strong\u003E style the faces show the material&#039;s texture. Hidden line, wireframe and x-ray show its color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETextures run \u003Cstrong\u003Elevel along each face\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eup it\u003C/strong\u003E: brick courses stay level on every wall, shingles run down a roof&#039;s slope, boards on a floor follow the red axis. They are at their real size (a brick is about 215 mm long), so they look right at any distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Erendered picture\u003C/a\u003E materials keep their texture, roughness and, for glass and metal, their shine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E picks up a face&#039;s material too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe textures are drawn by ModelCraft itself, so they work offline and are free to use for anything (CC0).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"components-and-colors\"\u003EComponents and colors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA color painted on a face \u003Cstrong\u003Einside\u003C/strong\u003E a component shows on every copy, because the copies share one definition. A color painted on a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecopy\u003C/strong\u003E from outside (with the component closed) belongs to that copy only, so two copies of one component can have different colors. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows the color of a selected face, group or component.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"colors-in-files\"\u003EColors in files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ModelCraft file keeps every color and every material.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther formats get a \u003Cstrong\u003Ematerial&#039;s color\u003C/strong\u003E (its look from afar) in place of the texture: glTF, Collada, OBJ, DWG and DXF, and the 3D printing check. In glTF, Collada and OBJ the material keeps its name, such as \u003Ccode\u003EMaterial_brick-red\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EglTF (.glb)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/strong\u003E: each color becomes a material. A component shown in different colors is written once per color. glTF also writes imported textures as textures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ\u003C/strong\u003E: exporting downloads two files, the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E and a \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E materials file with the colors. Keep them in the same folder. With imported textures, the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E, the \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E and the pictures come as one \u003Ccode\u003E.zip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the exact colors; \u003Cstrong\u003EDXF\u003C/strong\u003E uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTL\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EPLY\u003C/strong\u003E carry colors: STL in the VisCAM / SolidView convention (programs that don&#039;t read it ignore it), PLY as a color on each point. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003EFile formats in detail\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo model a screen, paint the frame charcoal and the glass blue after you \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003Eoffset\u003C/a\u003E the front face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Colors help you tell parts of a model apart, and materials (brick, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, grass, glass, metal) make it look like what it is built of. Both paint the same way. Pick a color The Colors section on the right shows a row of solid colors, Default (the one crossed by a line: no color) and a + swatch for any other color. Click one to make it the current color. The Paint tool starts with it. Paint Press B (Paint), or click a color. Click a face to paint it. Click a group or a component to paint that copy: its faces that have no color of their own take this color. Faces you painted inside it keep theirs. To paint several things at once, select them first and then click a color, or click any of them with Paint. It is one undo step. Hold And click a face What gets painted — That face Ctrl Every face connected to it that has the same color Shift Every face of that color in the group you are working in Alt Nothing: you pick up that color instead To take a face back to no color, choose Default and click it. Paint with a material The Materials section, under Colors, lists the material library by kind: brick and block, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, ground, glass and metal. Hover a swatch for its name. Click one, then paint with it exactly as with a color: B, click faces, groups or components; or select first and click the material. In the Shaded style the faces show the material's texture. Hidden line, wireframe and x-ray show its color. Textures run level along each face and up it: brick courses stay level on every wall, shingles run down a roof's slope, boards on a floor follow the red axis. They are at their real size (a brick is about 215 mm long), so they look right at any distance. In a rendered picture materials keep their texture, roughness and, for glass and metal, their shine. Alt-click with Paint picks up a face's material too. The textures are drawn by ModelCraft itself, so they work offline and are free to use for anything (CC0). Components and colors A color painted on a face inside a component shows on every copy, because the copies share one definition. A color painted on a copy from outside (with the component closed) belongs to that copy only, so two copies of one component can have different colors. Entity info shows the color of a selected face, group or component. Colors in files The ModelCraft file keeps every color and every material. Other formats get a material's color (its look from afar) in place of the texture: glTF, Collada, OBJ, DWG and DXF, and the 3D printing check. In glTF, Collada and OBJ the material keeps its name, such as Material_brick-red. glTF (.glb) and Collada (.dae): each color becomes a material. A component shown in different colors is written once per color. glTF also writes imported textures as textures. OBJ: exporting downloads two files, the .obj and a .mtl materials file with the colors. Keep them in the same folder. With imported textures, the .obj, the .mtl and the pictures come as one .zip. DWG keeps the exact colors; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors. STL and PLY carry colors: STL in the VisCAM / SolidView convention (programs that don't read it ignore it), PLY as a color on each point. See File formats in detail. To model a screen, paint the frame charcoal and the glass blue after you offset the front face.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"select","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Select things","summary":"Click, double-click and triple-click, drag a window or a crossing box, add and remove with modifier keys, and delete.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["select","selection","window","crossing","add to selection","deselect","select all","triple click","double click","delete","erase"],"headings":[{"id":"click","text":"Click","level":2},{"id":"drag-a-box","text":"Drag a box","level":2},{"id":"add-and-remove","text":"Add and remove","level":2},{"id":"clear-the-selection","text":"Clear the selection","level":2},{"id":"delete-what-is-selected","text":"Delete what is selected","level":2},{"id":"select-inside-a-group","text":"Select inside a group","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is the tool ModelCraft starts with, and the one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings you back to. Selected things turn blue, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E on the right says what they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"click\"\u003EClick\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face or an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat face or edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face and its edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe edge and the faces on either side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens it for editing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriple-click a face or edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything connected to it: the whole object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing (clears the selection)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click empty space while editing a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloses the group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdges are easier to hit than faces: when the pointer is within a few pixels of an edge, the click takes the edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-a-box\"\u003EDrag a box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeft to right\u003C/strong\u003E draws a solid box: a \u003Cstrong\u003Ewindow\u003C/strong\u003E selection. Only things wholly inside it are selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight to left\u003C/strong\u003E draws a dashed box: a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecrossing\u003C/strong\u003E selection. Anything the box touches is selected, and a face is selected when the box sits entirely inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group or component counts as one thing: a window must hold all of it, a crossing only has to touch it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-and-remove\"\u003EAdd and remove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold a key while you click or drag:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEffect\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToggles: adds what is not selected, removes what is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo select everything in the model (or everything in the group you are editing), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clear-the-selection\"\u003EClear the selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E as the tool), or click empty space.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-what-is-selected\"\u003EDelete what is selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Faces, edges, groups and guides in the selection are removed in one step; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeleting an edge removes the faces that need it, unless the edge only separated two faces in the same plane: then the two faces join into one. Deleting a face leaves its edges. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/erase\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/erase\"\u003EErase and clean up\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-inside-a-group\"\u003ESelect inside a group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking a group selects the whole group. To select a face inside it, double-click the group to open it, then click. While a group is open, clicks only reach what is inside it; everything else is faded. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with nothing selected, or double-click empty space, to close it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E on the left selects groups and components by name, which helps when they are hidden behind other things.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Select (Space) is the tool ModelCraft starts with, and the one Esc brings you back to. Selected things turn blue, and Entity info on the right says what they are. Click Do this Selects Click a face or an edge That face or edge Click a group or component The whole group or component Double-click a face The face and its edges Double-click an edge The edge and the faces on either side Double-click a group or component Opens it for editing (see Groups and components) Triple-click a face or edge Everything connected to it: the whole object Click empty space Nothing (clears the selection) Double-click empty space while editing a group Closes the group Edges are easier to hit than faces: when the pointer is within a few pixels of an edge, the click takes the edge. Drag a box Left to right draws a solid box: a window selection. Only things wholly inside it are selected. Right to left draws a dashed box: a crossing selection. Anything the box touches is selected, and a face is selected when the box sits entirely inside it. A group or component counts as one thing: a window must hold all of it, a crossing only has to touch it. Add and remove Hold a key while you click or drag: Key Effect Shift Toggles: adds what is not selected, removes what is Ctrl Adds Shift and Ctrl together Removes To select everything in the model (or everything in the group you are editing), press Ctrl+A. Clear the selection Press Esc (with Select as the tool), or click empty space. Delete what is selected Press Delete or Backspace. Faces, edges, groups and guides in the selection are removed in one step; Ctrl+Z brings them back. Deleting an edge removes the faces that need it, unless the edge only separated two faces in the same plane: then the two faces join into one. Deleting a face leaves its edges. See Erase and clean up. Select inside a group Clicking a group selects the whole group. To select a face inside it, double-click the group to open it, then click. While a group is open, clicks only reach what is inside it; everything else is faded. Press Esc with nothing selected, or double-click empty space, to close it. The Outliner on the left selects groups and components by name, which helps when they are hidden behind other things.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"solids","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Solid tools: union, subtract, intersect","summary":"Join closed groups and components into one, cut one out of another, or keep only what they share.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["solid","solids","solid inspector","not solid","fix solid","hole","stray edge","inside out","union","join","combine","merge","subtract","difference","cut","hole","intersect","intersection","boolean","outer shell"],"headings":[{"id":"when-something-is-not-a-solid","text":"When something is not a solid","level":2},{"id":"union","text":"Union","level":2},{"id":"subtract","text":"Subtract","level":2},{"id":"intersect","text":"Intersect","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe solid tools combine \u003Cstrong\u003Esolids\u003C/strong\u003E: groups and components whose faces close a volume, with every edge joining exactly two faces. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolid\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYes\u003C/span\u003E for one, and shows its volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E menu in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-something-is-not-a-solid\"\u003EWhen something is not a solid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the group or component. When it is not a solid, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says why, for example &quot;Not a solid: 4 edges border a hole · 1 stray edge&quot;:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProblem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges border a hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face is missing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges shared by three faces or more\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face divides the inside, or two shapes touch along an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces inside the shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall left inside the volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStray edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines that bound no face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces turned inside out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir front faces in, against their neighbours\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow problems\u003C/span\u003E opens it with those edges and faces selected, so you can see them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E mends the simple cases in one undo step: it removes stray edges and faces inside the shape, turns faces the right way out, and closes holes whose rim lies flat. Anything it cannot mend safely (a hole that is not flat, an edge shared by many faces) it leaves for you, and says what is left.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"union\"\u003EUnion\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect two or more solids (click one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click the others).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnion\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey become one solid, in one group. Where they overlapped, the faces inside are gone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"subtract\"\u003ESubtract\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubtract\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the solid to \u003Cstrong\u003Etake away\u003C/strong\u003E (the cutter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the solid to \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is left of the second solid stays; the cutter is used up. For example, to make a hole through a block, draw a post through it, make the post a group, then subtract the post from the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect\"\u003EIntersect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect two or more solids, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect\u003C/span\u003E. Only the part they all share is kept.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe result is a new group. It keeps the name, color and tag of the first solid (for Subtract: the one you cut). Colors painted on its faces are not kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlat sides of the result are single faces. Round sides come out as the flat facets they were drawn with.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a group or component is not a solid, the tool says which one and changes nothing. Look for a missing face or a stray face inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInside an open group, the tools work on the groups and components in that group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach tool is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first time you use a solid tool, its engine (manifold-3d) loads, which can take a moment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The solid tools combine solids: groups and components whose faces close a volume, with every edge joining exactly two faces. Entity info says Solid: Yes for one, and shows its volume. They are in the Solids menu in the Organize group. When something is not a solid Select the group or component. When it is not a solid, Entity info says why, for example \"Not a solid: 4 edges border a hole · 1 stray edge\": Problem What it means Edges border a hole A face is missing Edges shared by three faces or more A face divides the inside, or two shapes touch along an edge Faces inside the shape A wall left inside the volume Stray edges Lines that bound no face Faces turned inside out Their front faces in, against their neighbours Show problems opens it with those edges and faces selected, so you can see them. Fix mends the simple cases in one undo step: it removes stray edges and faces inside the shape, turns faces the right way out, and closes holes whose rim lies flat. Anything it cannot mend safely (a hole that is not flat, an edge shared by many faces) it leaves for you, and says what is left. Union Select two or more solids (click one, Ctrl+click the others). Solids › Union. They become one solid, in one group. Where they overlapped, the faces inside are gone. Subtract Solids › Subtract. Click the solid to take away (the cutter). Click the solid to cut. What is left of the second solid stays; the cutter is used up. For example, to make a hole through a block, draw a post through it, make the post a group, then subtract the post from the block. Intersect Select two or more solids, then Solids › Intersect. Only the part they all share is kept. Things to know The result is a new group. It keeps the name, color and tag of the first solid (for Subtract: the one you cut). Colors painted on its faces are not kept. Flat sides of the result are single faces. Round sides come out as the flat facets they were drawn with. If a group or component is not a solid, the tool says which one and changes nothing. Look for a missing face or a stray face inside it. Inside an open group, the tools work on the groups and components in that group. Each tool is one undo step. The first time you use a solid tool, its engine (manifold-3d) loads, which can take a moment.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"hide-isolate","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Hide, isolate and unhide","summary":"Put things out of the way to work between them, or show only what you are working on; hidden things stay hidden in the file.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["hide","unhide","unhide all","isolate","hide others","show","hidden","local view","visibility","out of the way"],"headings":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide","level":2},{"id":"isolate","text":"Isolate","level":2},{"id":"unhide-all","text":"Unhide all","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHiding puts things out of the way without erasing them. A hidden face, edge, group or component is not drawn, and you cannot snap to it, click it or select it, so you can work on what is behind or between things.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hide\"\u003EHide\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what to hide: faces, edges, groups or components.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor example, to work in the narrow gap between two groups standing close together, select the front one and hide it: clicks and snaps now reach the face behind it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"isolate\"\u003EIsolate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect what you want to keep in view, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIsolate (hide the rest)\u003C/span\u003E. Everything else in the model hides — or, while you are editing inside a group, everything else in that group. A selected face keeps its edges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unhide-all\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E shows everything again, in every group. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says how many things are hidden when nothing is selected, with an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E button there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows hidden groups and components in italics, with a crossed-out eye; click the eye to show one again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Modeler keys, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hides, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E isolates and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option H\"\u003E⌥H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E unhides all. (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E chooses the key style.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat is hidden is saved in the ModelCraft file, and comes back hidden when you open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden things are still in the model: exports include them, and so do volumes and sizes in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach hide, isolate or unhide is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Hiding puts things out of the way without erasing them. A hidden face, edge, group or component is not drawn, and you cannot snap to it, click it or select it, so you can work on what is behind or between things. Hide Select what to hide: faces, edges, groups or components. Edit › More › Hide. For example, to work in the narrow gap between two groups standing close together, select the front one and hide it: clicks and snaps now reach the face behind it. Isolate Select what you want to keep in view, then Edit › More › Isolate (hide the rest). Everything else in the model hides — or, while you are editing inside a group, everything else in that group. A selected face keeps its edges. Unhide all Edit › More › Unhide all shows everything again, in every group. Entity info says how many things are hidden when nothing is selected, with an Unhide all button there too. The Outliner shows hidden groups and components in italics, with a crossed-out eye; click the eye to show one again. With Modeler keys, H hides, Shift+H isolates and Alt+H unhides all. (Settings › ModelCraft chooses the key style.) Things to know What is hidden is saved in the ModelCraft file, and comes back hidden when you open it. Hidden things are still in the model: exports include them, and so do volumes and sizes in Entity info. Each hide, isolate or unhide is one undo step.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"tags","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Tags: show and hide sets of things","summary":"Give groups and components a tag, such as Furniture or Walls, and hide or show everything with that tag at once.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["tags","tag","layers","layer","show","hide","visibility","organize","furniture","walls","untagged","rename tag","delete tag"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-tag","text":"Make a tag","level":2},{"id":"tag-groups-and-components","text":"Tag groups and components","level":2},{"id":"hide-and-show","text":"Hide and show","level":2},{"id":"delete-a-tag","text":"Delete a tag","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA tag is a name you give groups and components, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EFurniture\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ESite\u003C/strong\u003E, so that you can hide or show everything with that tag at once. Tags are what CAD programs call layers. For hiding one thing at a time, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and unhide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-tag\"\u003EMake a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E on the right, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd tag\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType its name and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo rename a tag, click its name in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E, type the new one and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Two tags cannot share a name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tag-groups-and-components\"\u003ETag groups and components\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more groups or components.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, choose the tag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew tag…\u003C/span\u003E makes a new one and tags the selection with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E takes the tag away. The number next to each tag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E says how many groups and components have it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETag groups and components, not loose faces and edges: draw the things you want to tag as groups first (select them, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hide-and-show\"\u003EHide and show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the eye next to a tag. Everything with that tag goes out of view, wherever it is in the model (also inside other groups), and clicks and snaps go past it. Click the eye again to show it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E things are always shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you are editing inside a group whose tag you hide, the group closes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-a-tag\"\u003EDelete a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the bin next to it. What had the tag becomes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E, and shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETags, their names and which are hidden are saved in the ModelCraft file. Copying a tagged group into another model brings its tag along.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHiding and showing a tag, and every other change to tags, is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports include everything, hidden tags too, as they do for hidden things.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers in DWG and DXF site plans do not come in as tags yet: the whole drawing comes in as one group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A tag is a name you give groups and components, such as Furniture, Walls or Site, so that you can hide or show everything with that tag at once. Tags are what CAD programs call layers. For hiding one thing at a time, see Hide, isolate and unhide. Make a tag In Tags on the right, click Add tag. Type its name and press Enter. To rename a tag, click its name in Tags, type the new one and press Enter. Two tags cannot share a name. Tag groups and components Select one or more groups or components. In Entity info, choose the tag in Tag. New tag… makes a new one and tags the selection with it. Untagged takes the tag away. The number next to each tag in Tags says how many groups and components have it. Tag groups and components, not loose faces and edges: draw the things you want to tag as groups first (select them, Shift+G). Hide and show Click the eye next to a tag. Everything with that tag goes out of view, wherever it is in the model (also inside other groups), and clicks and snaps go past it. Click the eye again to show it. Untagged things are always shown. If you are editing inside a group whose tag you hide, the group closes. Delete a tag Click the bin next to it. What had the tag becomes Untagged, and shows. Things to know Tags, their names and which are hidden are saved in the ModelCraft file. Copying a tagged group into another model brings its tag along. Hiding and showing a tag, and every other change to tags, is one undo step. Exports include everything, hidden tags too, as they do for hidden things. Layers in DWG and DXF site plans do not come in as tags yet: the whole drawing comes in as one group.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sections","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Section planes: cut the model open","summary":"Place a section plane to see inside the model, slide it through, reverse it or turn the cut off.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["section","section plane","section cut","cut","cutaway","clip","inside","floor plan","slice","cross section"],"headings":[{"id":"place-one","text":"Place one","level":2},{"id":"slide-it-reverse-it-turn-it-off","text":"Slide it, reverse it, turn it off","level":2},{"id":"show-and-hide","text":"Show and hide","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA section plane cuts the model open, so you can see and work inside it: a floor plan of a house, the inside of a cabinet. The model is not changed; the cut is only a view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-one\"\u003EPlace one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a face. The plane is placed on that face, with four arrows pointing at you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick away from any face for a plane square to the direction you look in most. Press an arrow key first to stand it square to red (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), green (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or blue (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe side the arrows point at is \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut away\u003C/strong\u003E. A plane placed on a face cuts nothing yet: slide it into the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"slide-it-reverse-it-turn-it-off\"\u003ESlide it, reverse it, turn it off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the plane&#039;s outline to select it. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlong its arrows\u003C/span\u003E says where it sits. Type a new distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to slide it through the model; typing \u003Ccode\u003E-1m\u003C/code\u003E on a plane you placed on a front face moves it 1 m into the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns the arrows round, so the other side is cut away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActive (cuts the model)\u003C/span\u003E: one plane cuts at a time. Tick another plane&#039;s box, or place a new one, and that one cuts instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E; delete it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the plane passes through a closed solid, the cut is filled in a dark color. Geometry that is not closed (a single face, an open box) is cut but not filled.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-and-hide\"\u003EShow and hide\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow section planes\u003C/span\u003E hides the planes&#039; outlines and arrows (the cut stays).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow section cuts\u003C/span\u003E turns the cut off and shows the whole model, without deleting the planes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/a\u003E hides the outlines too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat is cut away cannot be clicked or snapped to, so you can draw on what the cut shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESection planes, which one is active and the two switches are saved in the ModelCraft file. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports always contain the whole model: a section is a view, not an edit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A section plane cuts the model open, so you can see and work inside it: a floor plan of a house, the inside of a cabinet. The model is not changed; the cut is only a view. Place one Click Section in the Camera group. Click a face. The plane is placed on that face, with four arrows pointing at you. Click away from any face for a plane square to the direction you look in most. Press an arrow key first to stand it square to red (→), green (←) or blue (↑) instead. The side the arrows point at is cut away. A plane placed on a face cuts nothing yet: slide it into the model. Slide it, reverse it, turn it off Click the plane's outline to select it. In Entity info: Along its arrows says where it sits. Type a new distance and press Enter to slide it through the model; typing -1m on a plane you placed on a front face moves it 1 m into the model. Reverse turns the arrows round, so the other side is cut away. Active (cuts the model): one plane cuts at a time. Tick another plane's box, or place a new one, and that one cuts instead. Rename it in Name; delete it with Delete or the Eraser. Where the plane passes through a closed solid, the cut is filled in a dark color. Geometry that is not closed (a single face, an open box) is cut but not filled. Show and hide In Views: Show section planes hides the planes' outlines and arrows (the cut stays). Show section cuts turns the cut off and shows the whole model, without deleting the planes. Presentation view hides the outlines too. Things to know What is cut away cannot be clicked or snapped to, so you can draw on what the cut shows. Section planes, which one is active and the two switches are saved in the ModelCraft file. Each change is one undo step. Exports always contain the whole model: a section is a view, not an edit.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"move-copy","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Move, copy and make arrays","summary":"Move things by clicking or by an exact distance, copy them with a modifier key, and make a row of evenly spaced copies.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["move","copy","duplicate","array","row","spacing","divide","offset","stretch","columns","repeat","multiple copy"],"headings":[{"id":"move-something","text":"Move something","level":2},{"id":"moving-part-of-a-shape-stretches-it","text":"Moving part of a shape stretches it","level":2},{"id":"copy-something","text":"Copy something","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-paste-and-duplicate","text":"Copy, cut, paste and duplicate","level":2},{"id":"a-row-of-copies","text":"A row of copies","level":2},{"id":"example-columns-every-6-meters","text":"Example: columns every 6 meters","level":3},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) moves whatever is selected from one point to another. Holding a modifier key makes it a copy, and after a copy you can type how many more you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-something\"\u003EMove something\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to move (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/select\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/select\"\u003ESelect things\u003C/a\u003E). If nothing is selected, Move takes the thing you click on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point to move from. Pick something meaningful, such as a corner: that point will land exactly where you click next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer. The selection follows. It snaps to points, edges and the red, green and blue directions like a line does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the point goes, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s tip returns to &quot;Click a point to move from…&quot; for the next move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA typed distance goes in the direction you are pointing. You can also type an exact offset, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;2m,0,0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (2 m along red), or an exact destination for the point you picked, \u003Ccode\u003E[0,0,0]\u003C/code\u003E (the origin). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move along one axis only, press an arrow key after the first click: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E locks to red, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to green, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to blue. Press the same arrow again to unlock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-part-of-a-shape-stretches-it\"\u003EMoving part of a shape stretches it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGeometry that is not grouped is connected. If you select one face or a few edges and move them, the faces attached to them stretch to follow. Move the top face of a box to the side and the box leans; move one edge of the roof and the roof changes pitch. Faces that can no longer stay flat fold into triangles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move a whole object without stretching it, select all of it first (triple-click it), or better, make it a group or component (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-something\"\u003ECopy something\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the point to move from. On a Mac, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same. The tip says &quot;Ctrl moves instead&quot;, which means you are copying.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also press and release \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the first click to switch between moving and copying.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the copy goes, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe original stays where it was, and the copy is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, cut, paste and duplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the selection, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cuts it (copies, then erases), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E are also buttons in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E beside them has cut, paste in place and duplicate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes: an outline of the copy follows the pointer, snapping like any tool, and a click puts it down with its lowest corner there. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes in place, exactly where the copy came from — useful for moving something into a group you have opened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the Modeler style) duplicates: a copy is made and moves with the pointer at once; click to put it down, type a distance, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to drop it. Right after, type \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E for more copies in a row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pasted group becomes a new group. A pasted component is another placement of the same component, so editing one still changes them all. Copies also paste into another ModelCraft tab in this browser (they come in as new definitions there).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-row-of-copies\"\u003EA row of copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after a copy, while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E is still the tool:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for three copies in a row, each at the same spacing as the first copy. You get three new copies besides the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to divide instead: three copies spread evenly from the original to where you put the first copy, the last one at that spot. Use this to fill a span: place one copy at the far end, then divide.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can type a different number right after: \u003Ccode\u003E5x\u003C/code\u003E replaces the \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E row. The tip reminds you: &quot;Copied. Type 3x for more copies in a row, or /3 to divide the distance.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"example-columns-every-6-meters\"\u003EExample: columns every 6 meters\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a column and make it a component (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), so all copies change together later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the column&#039;s base corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove along the red axis and type \u003Ccode\u003E6m\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4x\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You have five columns, 6 m apart.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the second click to cancel; everything goes back where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove works inside the group you are editing. To move a whole group, close it first and select it from outside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ECollide\u003C/strong\u003E on (bottom right, and in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#solids-stop-where-they-touch\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"solids-stop-where-they-touch\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E), a move stops where the selection touches another solid instead of passing through it. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollide\u003C/span\u003E to turn it off for a move that should go through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo put a copy inside another group: copy it, open the other group, and paste (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps it in the same place).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Move (M) moves whatever is selected from one point to another. Holding a modifier key makes it a copy, and after a copy you can type how many more you want. Move something Select what you want to move (see Select things). If nothing is selected, Move takes the thing you click on. Press M. Click a point to move from. Pick something meaningful, such as a corner: that point will land exactly where you click next. Move the pointer. The selection follows. It snaps to points, edges and the red, green and blue directions like a line does. Click where the point goes, or type a distance and press Enter. The status bar's tip returns to \"Click a point to move from…\" for the next move. A typed distance goes in the direction you are pointing. You can also type an exact offset, \u003C2m,0,0\u003E (2 m along red), or an exact destination for the point you picked, [0,0,0] (the origin). See Type exact sizes. To move along one axis only, press an arrow key after the first click: → locks to red, ← to green, ↑ to blue. Press the same arrow again to unlock. Moving part of a shape stretches it Geometry that is not grouped is connected. If you select one face or a few edges and move them, the faces attached to them stretch to follow. Move the top face of a box to the side and the box leans; move one edge of the roof and the roof changes pitch. Faces that can no longer stay flat fold into triangles. To move a whole object without stretching it, select all of it first (triple-click it), or better, make it a group or component (see Groups and components). Copy something Select what you want to copy. Press M. Hold Ctrl (or Alt) and click the point to move from. On a Mac, Ctrl or Alt does the same. The tip says \"Ctrl moves instead\", which means you are copying. You can also press and release Ctrl or Alt after the first click to switch between moving and copying. Click where the copy goes, or type a distance and press Enter. The original stays where it was, and the copy is selected. Copy, cut, paste and duplicate Ctrl+C copies the selection, Ctrl+X cuts it (copies, then erases), Copy and Paste are also buttons in the toolbar's Edit group, and More beside them has cut, paste in place and duplicate. Ctrl+V pastes: an outline of the copy follows the pointer, snapping like any tool, and a click puts it down with its lowest corner there. Esc cancels. Ctrl+Shift+V pastes in place, exactly where the copy came from — useful for moving something into a group you have opened. Ctrl+D (Shift+D in the Modeler style) duplicates: a copy is made and moves with the pointer at once; click to put it down, type a distance, or press Esc to drop it. Right after, type 3x for more copies in a row. A pasted group becomes a new group. A pasted component is another placement of the same component, so editing one still changes them all. Copies also paste into another ModelCraft tab in this browser (they come in as new definitions there). A row of copies Right after a copy, while Move is still the tool: Type 3x (or *3) and press Enter for three copies in a row, each at the same spacing as the first copy. You get three new copies besides the original. Type /3 and press Enter to divide instead: three copies spread evenly from the original to where you put the first copy, the last one at that spot. Use this to fill a span: place one copy at the far end, then divide. You can type a different number right after: 5x replaces the 3x row. The tip reminds you: \"Copied. Type 3x for more copies in a row, or /3 to divide the distance.\" Example: columns every 6 meters Make a column and make it a component (G), so all copies change together later. Press M, hold Ctrl and click the column's base corner. Move along the red axis and type 6m, then Enter. Type 4x and Enter. You have five columns, 6 m apart. Things to know Press Esc before the second click to cancel; everything goes back where it was. Move works inside the group you are editing. To move a whole group, close it first and select it from outside. With Collide on (bottom right, and in ModelCraft settings), a move stops where the selection touches another solid instead of passing through it. Click Collide to turn it off for a move that should go through. To put a copy inside another group: copy it, open the other group, and paste (Ctrl+Shift+V keeps it in the same place).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"soften","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Soften and smooth edges","summary":"Hide the edges between faces that meet at a shallow angle, so curved shapes read as one smooth surface, in ModelCraft and in exported files.","section":"tasks","order":52,"keywords":["soften","smooth","soft edges","smooth shading","hide edges","facets","faceted","curved","normals","harden","angle"],"headings":[{"id":"soften-by-angle","text":"Soften by angle","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA curved shape in ModelCraft is made of flat faces. \u003Cstrong\u003ESoft\u003C/strong\u003E edges between those faces are not drawn, and the faces they join are shaded as one smooth surface, so a 24-sided cylinder looks round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you pull a circle up with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E, its sides are softened for you. For other shapes, soften by angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"soften-by-angle\"\u003ESoften by angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what to soften: faces and edges, or a group or component (all of its edges).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, type an angle in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESoften up to\u003C/span\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESoften\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery edge in the selection whose two faces turn by that angle or less becomes soft; edges with a sharper turn become hard. A 12-sided prism&#039;s sides turn by 30° and its rims by 90°, so \u003Ccode\u003E31\u003C/code\u003E softens only the sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHarden all\u003C/span\u003E makes every edge in the selection hard again. Each is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly edges between exactly two faces are softened. Loose edges stay as they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmooth shading works across soft edges only: a face with no soft edge shades flat.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESoft edges are kept in the ModelCraft file. \u003Cstrong\u003EglTF (.glb)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ\u003C/strong\u003E exports carry the smooth shading (a normal per corner), so other programs show the same surface. Collada and STL export flat faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESoft edges are hidden in every face style, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E too. To see them, select the shape and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHarden all\u003C/span\u003E; undo brings the softening back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A curved shape in ModelCraft is made of flat faces. Soft edges between those faces are not drawn, and the faces they join are shaded as one smooth surface, so a 24-sided cylinder looks round. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened for you. For other shapes, soften by angle. Soften by angle Select what to soften: faces and edges, or a group or component (all of its edges). In Entity info, type an angle in Soften up to, such as 20, and click Soften. Every edge in the selection whose two faces turn by that angle or less becomes soft; edges with a sharper turn become hard. A 12-sided prism's sides turn by 30° and its rims by 90°, so 31 softens only the sides. Harden all makes every edge in the selection hard again. Each is one undo step. Things to know Only edges between exactly two faces are softened. Loose edges stay as they are. Smooth shading works across soft edges only: a face with no soft edge shades flat. Soft edges are kept in the ModelCraft file. glTF (.glb) and OBJ exports carry the smooth shading (a normal per corner), so other programs show the same surface. Collada and STL export flat faces. Soft edges are hidden in every face style, X-ray and Wireframe too. To see them, select the shape and click Harden all; undo brings the softening back.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"intersect","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Intersect faces","summary":"Draw the lines where faces cut through each other, so you can push, erase or paint the pieces.","section":"tasks","order":53,"keywords":["intersect","intersect faces","intersect with model","intersect with selection","cut","opening","hole","join","lines where faces cross","boolean"],"headings":[{"id":"intersect-with-model","text":"Intersect with model","level":2},{"id":"intersect-with-selection","text":"Intersect with selection","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lines-go","text":"Where the lines go","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen one shape passes through another, ModelCraft does not draw the line where their faces meet: they just overlap. \u003Cstrong\u003EIntersect\u003C/strong\u003E draws those lines, as edges, so the faces split there. Then you can push/pull, erase or paint the pieces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth commands are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect-with-model\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECuts what you select against \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery face on screen\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor example, a beam (a group) passes through a wall (another group), and you want an opening in the wall:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the wall to open it, and select all of its faces (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control A\"\u003E⌃A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe beam&#039;s outline is drawn on both faces of the wall, as two new faces. Push one of them through the wall to make the opening, then close the wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect-with-selection\"\u003EIntersect with selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECuts the selected faces, groups and components \u003Cstrong\u003Eonly against each other\u003C/strong\u003E. Select at least two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lines-go\"\u003EWhere the lines go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe lines are always drawn in the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopen context\u003C/strong\u003E: in the model when no group is open, or in the group you are editing. Groups and components that are not open are not changed. So to cut a group&#039;s faces, open it first, as in the example above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces that lie in the same plane are not intersected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines that close a loop on a face make a new face, as drawn lines do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach intersect is one undo step. If nothing crosses, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor closed shapes, the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esolid tools\u003C/a\u003E (Union, Subtract) do the whole job in one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"When one shape passes through another, ModelCraft does not draw the line where their faces meet: they just overlap. Intersect draws those lines, as edges, so the faces split there. Then you can push/pull, erase or paint the pieces. Both commands are in Edit › More. Intersect with model Cuts what you select against every face on screen. For example, a beam (a group) passes through a wall (another group), and you want an opening in the wall: Double-click the wall to open it, and select all of its faces (Ctrl+A). Edit › More › Intersect with model. The beam's outline is drawn on both faces of the wall, as two new faces. Push one of them through the wall to make the opening, then close the wall. Intersect with selection Cuts the selected faces, groups and components only against each other. Select at least two. Where the lines go The lines are always drawn in the open context: in the model when no group is open, or in the group you are editing. Groups and components that are not open are not changed. So to cut a group's faces, open it first, as in the example above. Things to know Faces that lie in the same plane are not intersected. Lines that close a loop on a face make a new face, as drawn lines do. Each intersect is one undo step. If nothing crosses, nothing changes. For closed shapes, the solid tools (Union, Subtract) do the whole job in one step.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"axes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Move and turn the drawing axes","summary":"Turn the red, green and blue axes to match a building that is not square to them, or align them to a face, so drawing and typing follow it.","section":"tasks","order":54,"keywords":["axes","drawing axes","origin","align axes","rotate axes","turn axes","reset axes","angled building","skewed","ucs","coordinate system"],"headings":[{"id":"place-axes","text":"Place axes","level":2},{"id":"align-axes-to-a-face","text":"Align axes to a face","level":2},{"id":"reset-axes","text":"Reset axes","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe red, green and blue axes guide everything you draw: rectangles line up with them, lines lock to them, and the ground is the red–green plane. When a building sits at an angle (a site plan that is not square to north, a wing turned 30°), move the axes onto it, so drawing follows its walls. The model itself does not move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three commands are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-axes\"\u003EPlace axes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace axes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the origin goes, such as a corner of the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point along the new red axis, such as further along a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point toward the new green axis. Green is made square to red, and blue stands square to both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe axes are drawn in their new place. A rectangle you now draw on the ground, or type as \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, runs along the turned red and green; the arrow keys lock to them; and the origin snap is at the new origin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"align-axes-to-a-face\"\u003EAlign axes to a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one face, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign axes to face\u003C/span\u003E. Blue then points out of the face, red runs along its longest edge, and the origin is at that edge&#039;s start. Use it to draw on a sloping roof as if it were the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reset-axes\"\u003EReset axes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset axes\u003C/span\u003E puts them back where the model began.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInside an open group or component, its own axes are used (they turn with it); the model&#039;s axes apply at the top level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe axes are saved in the ModelCraft file, and each change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports and the standard views (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E…) still use the model&#039;s own directions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The red, green and blue axes guide everything you draw: rectangles line up with them, lines lock to them, and the ground is the red–green plane. When a building sits at an angle (a site plan that is not square to north, a wing turned 30°), move the axes onto it, so drawing follows its walls. The model itself does not move. All three commands are in Edit › More. Place axes Edit › More › Place axes. Click where the origin goes, such as a corner of the building. Click a point along the new red axis, such as further along a wall. Click a point toward the new green axis. Green is made square to red, and blue stands square to both. The axes are drawn in their new place. A rectangle you now draw on the ground, or type as 4m,3m, runs along the turned red and green; the arrow keys lock to them; and the origin snap is at the new origin. Align axes to a face Select one face, then Edit › More › Align axes to face. Blue then points out of the face, red runs along its longest edge, and the origin is at that edge's start. Use it to draw on a sloping roof as if it were the ground. Reset axes Edit › More › Reset axes puts them back where the model began. Things to know Inside an open group or component, its own axes are used (they turn with it); the model's axes apply at the top level. The axes are saved in the ModelCraft file, and each change is one undo step. Exports and the standard views (Top, Front…) still use the model's own directions.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"terrain","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Terrain: ground from contours, and sculpting it","summary":"Make ground from contour lines or as a grid, then raise and lower it with a soft brush.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["terrain","ground","landscape","topography","contours","contour lines","site","slope","hill","mound","sandbox","smoove","sculpt","grade","TIN"],"headings":[{"id":"from-contour-lines","text":"From contour lines","level":2},{"id":"a-grid-to-sculpt","text":"A grid to sculpt","level":2},{"id":"sculpt-raise-and-lower-the-ground","text":"Sculpt: raise and lower the ground","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETerrain is ground made of triangles, so it can follow any shape. It is a group: double-click it to edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-contour-lines\"\u003EFrom contour lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGet the contour lines at their heights: draw them, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003Eimport a site plan\u003C/a\u003E and move each contour up to its level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect them (all the edges of all the contours).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain from selected contours\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft joins the contour points with triangles (a TIN), and softens every edge inside so the ground shades smooth. The lines themselves stay; erase or hide them when you no longer need them. Very dense contours are thinned to 8 000 points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-grid-to-sculpt\"\u003EA grid to sculpt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain grid…\u003C/span\u003E asks for a width, a depth and a cell size, then places a flat grid of triangles where you click its corner. Small cells sculpt smoother but make more faces (at most 200 × 200 cells).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sculpt-raise-and-lower-the-ground\"\u003ESculpt: raise and lower the ground\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the terrain to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESculpt terrain\u003C/span\u003E. A circle shows the brush on the ground under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag up to raise the ground under the brush, or down to lower it. The middle moves most and the ground eases to nothing at the circle&#039;s edge, so mounds and hollows come out smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a mound an exact height, click once, then type the height, such as \u003Ccode\u003E1.5m\u003C/code\u003E (a minus lowers it), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. To change the brush, type a length before you click: \u003Ccode\u003E5m\u003C/code\u003E gives a brush 5 m across its radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sculpt is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESculpt moves only triangle faces, so every face stays flat. Faces drawn by hand (rectangles, polygons) are not terrain: the tool says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESculpting moves points straight up and down; it does not add points. For finer detail, make a grid with smaller cells.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETerrain exports like any other group. In IFC it is a building element proxy, like everything else.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Terrain is ground made of triangles, so it can follow any shape. It is a group: double-click it to edit it. From contour lines Get the contour lines at their heights: draw them, or import a site plan and move each contour up to its level. Select them (all the edges of all the contours). Edit › More › Terrain from selected contours. ModelCraft joins the contour points with triangles (a TIN), and softens every edge inside so the ground shades smooth. The lines themselves stay; erase or hide them when you no longer need them. Very dense contours are thinned to 8 000 points. A grid to sculpt Edit › More › Terrain grid… asks for a width, a depth and a cell size, then places a flat grid of triangles where you click its corner. Small cells sculpt smoother but make more faces (at most 200 × 200 cells). Sculpt: raise and lower the ground Double-click the terrain to open it. Edit › More › Sculpt terrain. A circle shows the brush on the ground under the pointer. Drag up to raise the ground under the brush, or down to lower it. The middle moves most and the ground eases to nothing at the circle's edge, so mounds and hollows come out smooth. To make a mound an exact height, click once, then type the height, such as 1.5m (a minus lowers it), and press Enter. To change the brush, type a length before you click: 5m gives a brush 5 m across its radius. Each sculpt is one undo step. Things to know Sculpt moves only triangle faces, so every face stays flat. Faces drawn by hand (rectangles, polygons) are not terrain: the tool says so. Sculpting moves points straight up and down; it does not add points. For finer detail, make a grid with smaller cells. Terrain exports like any other group. In IFC it is a building element proxy, like everything else.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"meshes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Meshes","summary":"Turn a group or component into a mesh — the kind of object 3D content tools model with — and back into push/pull geometry.","section":"tasks","order":58,"keywords":["mesh","meshes","polygon","polygons","n-gon","quad","quads","vertices","half-edge","uv","uvs","convert","convert to mesh","convert to geometry","subdivision","sculpt","modeling","3d content"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-a-group-or-component-into-a-mesh","text":"Turn a group or component into a mesh","level":2},{"id":"what-entity-info-shows","text":"What Entity info shows","level":2},{"id":"turn-a-mesh-back-into-push-pull-geometry","text":"Turn a mesh back into push/pull geometry","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft has two kinds of geometry. \u003Cstrong\u003EPush/pull geometry\u003C/strong\u003E is what you draw: flat faces that stick together, cut each other and heal. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emesh\u003C/strong\u003E is the kind of object 3D content tools model with: faces with any number of corners, each corner able to carry its own texture coordinates (UVs), normals and colors, and each vertex values such as weights. A mesh is placed like a group or component, and sits beside push/pull geometry in the same model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-a-group-or-component-into-a-mesh\"\u003ETurn a group or component into a mesh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a group or component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConvert to mesh\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts faces become the mesh&#039;s faces (a face with holes comes in as triangles), hard edges stay sharp, soft edges shade smoothly, and paint, materials and imported textures stay on their faces. Groups and components inside it are joined into the one mesh. Loose edges are left out: a mesh keeps faces. Every placement of a component shows the mesh. It is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-entity-info-shows\"\u003EWhat Entity info shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a mesh to see its faces, vertices and edges, whether it is a closed solid (and its volume), and its size as placed. A mesh with open edges says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-a-mesh-back-into-push-pull-geometry\"\u003ETurn a mesh back into push/pull geometry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the mesh and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConvert to geometry\u003C/span\u003E. Flat faces come back as they were; a face whose corners are not in one plane comes back as triangles. Sharp edges are hard, the others soft. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E puts a mesh&#039;s faces straight into the group around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a mesh to \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003Eedit its vertices, edges and faces\u003C/a\u003E in the Mesh workspace. Convert it to geometry to edit it with push/pull; move, rotate, scale, copy, paint, hide and tag it like any group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports, 3D printing, renders and VR see a mesh as its faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file that holds a mesh is saved as a newer version of the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file, so ModelCraft 0.6 and older can&#039;t open it; files without a mesh open in them as before. Convert meshes to geometry first to share a file with someone on an older version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUndo on a mesh keeps only what each step changed, so even a very large mesh undoes quickly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft has two kinds of geometry. Push/pull geometry is what you draw: flat faces that stick together, cut each other and heal. A mesh is the kind of object 3D content tools model with: faces with any number of corners, each corner able to carry its own texture coordinates (UVs), normals and colors, and each vertex values such as weights. A mesh is placed like a group or component, and sits beside push/pull geometry in the same model. Turn a group or component into a mesh Select a group or component. In Entity info, click Convert to mesh. Its faces become the mesh's faces (a face with holes comes in as triangles), hard edges stay sharp, soft edges shade smoothly, and paint, materials and imported textures stay on their faces. Groups and components inside it are joined into the one mesh. Loose edges are left out: a mesh keeps faces. Every placement of a component shows the mesh. It is one undo step. What Entity info shows Select a mesh to see its faces, vertices and edges, whether it is a closed solid (and its volume), and its size as placed. A mesh with open edges says how many. Turn a mesh back into push/pull geometry Select the mesh and click Convert to geometry. Flat faces come back as they were; a face whose corners are not in one plane comes back as triangles. Sharp edges are hard, the others soft. Explode puts a mesh's faces straight into the group around it. Things to know Double-click a mesh to edit its vertices, edges and faces in the Mesh workspace. Convert it to geometry to edit it with push/pull; move, rotate, scale, copy, paint, hide and tag it like any group. Exports, 3D printing, renders and VR see a mesh as its faces. A file that holds a mesh is saved as a newer version of the .modelcraft file, so ModelCraft 0.6 and older can't open it; files without a mesh open in them as before. Convert meshes to geometry first to share a file with someone on an older version. Undo on a mesh keeps only what each step changed, so even a very large mesh undoes quickly. See also Groups and components and Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"mesh-edit","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Edit a mesh in the Mesh workspace","summary":"Select a mesh's vertices, edges and faces, move them, and extrude, inset, bevel, cut, merge, fill and bridge them, with either key style.","section":"tasks","order":59,"keywords":["mesh","edit mode","mesh workspace","vertex","vertices","edge","edges","face","faces","extrude","inset","bevel","loop cut","knife","bisect","spin","merge","dissolve","fill","grid fill","bridge","subdivide","slide","proportional editing","falloff","workspace"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"select","text":"Select","level":2},{"id":"move-rotate-scale","text":"Move, rotate, scale","level":2},{"id":"change-the-shape","text":"Change the shape","level":2},{"id":"remesh","text":"Remesh","level":2},{"id":"retopology","text":"Retopology","level":2},{"id":"adjust-the-last-operation","text":"Adjust the last operation","level":2},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EMesh\u003C/strong\u003E workspace edits a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E point by point: its vertices, edges and faces. Push/pull geometry stays in the \u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E workspace.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMesh\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWorkspace\u003C/span\u003E list at the left of the toolbar. The selected mesh opens for editing; a selected group or component is turned into a mesh first (one undo step).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr double-click a mesh, or, with the modeler keys, select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith nothing selected, the panel offers meshes to add: plane, grid, cube, circle, cylinder, cone, sphere, ico sphere and torus. A new one goes where the view is looking and opens for editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to Model\u003C/span\u003E (or the list, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the modeler keys, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with nothing selected with the push/pull keys) goes back to Model with the mesh selected. While the Mesh workspace is open, the rest of the model is faded and Model&#039;s drawing tools are put away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select\"\u003ESelect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"1\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"2\"\u003E2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect vertices, edges or faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds or removes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect what is inside and seen; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E adds, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click an edge, or double-click it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts edge loop (a face loop in face mode; the whole border on an open edge)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts edge ring\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriple-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything joined to it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E group also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinked\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELess\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-rotate-scale\"\u003EMove, rotate, scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E start from the pointer: move it, then click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put the selection down, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or the arrow keys) lock an axis. Type a distance, an angle or a factor for an exact one — \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box#calculations\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\" data-hash=\"calculations\"\u003Ecalculations\u003C/a\u003E work too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProportional editing\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProportional\u003C/span\u003E, or the panel): vertices near the selection follow a move, less the further they are, up to the reach you set, with the falloff you choose. Turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly along the mesh\u003C/span\u003E to measure along edges rather than straight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlide\u003C/span\u003E moves a selected edge loop along the edges beside it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-shape\"\u003EChange the shape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPulls the selected faces out along their normal, with new side faces (or makes faces from open border edges). Type the distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInset\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA band inside the selected faces&#039; outline; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for each face on its own\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBevel\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECuts the selected edges (or, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, vertices) at a width; the wheel or the plus and minus keys add segments for a rounded edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoop cut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint at an edge: new loops across the ring of four-sided faces; the wheel sets how many; click to cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKnife\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick points on edges or corners; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cuts straight across the faces between them, as you see them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBisect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a line on screen: the selected faces (or all) are cut along it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpin\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns the selected faces or open edges round the blue axis in steps (the wheel sets them); a full turn closes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubdivide\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECuts the selected edges in two; whole faces become a grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMerge\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected vertices become one, at their centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDissolve\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes the selection out but keeps the surface: faces join\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face across a selected open border (or between two open edges)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid fill\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFills an open border with an even number of edges with a grid of quads\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBridge\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoins two selected open edge loops with faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrease\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow sharp the selected edges stay when the mesh is smoothed by a Subdivision surface: move away from the centre, use the wheel (steps of 0.1) or type 0 to 1. The panel shows the selected edges&#039; crease and takes a typed value too. Creased edges are drawn in magenta\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn faces over; smooth vertices toward their neighbours; delete by the select mode\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach is one undo step. Texture coordinates, colours and weights follow onto the new faces and vertices.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remesh\"\u003ERemesh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemesh\u003C/span\u003E makes the whole mesh again as even triangles of one size, asked for when you press it (smaller is finer and slower). It closes holes, joins pieces that overlap, and evens out a mesh that sculpting has stretched. Sharp edges come out very slightly rounded. UVs, paint and creases start again. One undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"retopology\"\u003ERetopology\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo build a new, cleaner mesh over a detailed one (a sculpt, a scan), choose the detailed one under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERetopology\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EBuild on\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESnap moved points onto it:\u003C/strong\u003E points you move, and edges you extrude, land on its surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPut the selected points on it\u003C/strong\u003E moves the selected points onto it now (one undo step).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow this mesh&#039;s edges in front\u003C/strong\u003E draws the mesh you are building over the surface, so you can see it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"adjust-the-last-operation\"\u003EAdjust the last operation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInset\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBevel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoop cut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubdivide\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid fill\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpin\u003C/span\u003E, the panel shows its settings (width, segments, profile, cuts …). Change one and the operation is done again from where it started, still one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys\"\u003EKeys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPush/pull keys\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EModeler keys\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove, rotate, scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E twice slides)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude, inset\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrease\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift E\"\u003E⇧E\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+E\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect all, none, invert\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control A\"\u003E⌃A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control I\"\u003E⌃I\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+I\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option A\"\u003E⌥A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control I\"\u003E⌃I\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+I\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control B\"\u003E⌃B\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+B\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bevel, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control R\"\u003E⌃R\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+R\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E loop cut, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E knife\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe same, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fill, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E merge, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control X\"\u003E⌃X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E dissolve, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control L\"\u003E⌃L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+L\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E linked, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option N\"\u003E⌥N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E flip, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E proportional, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift V\"\u003E⇧V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E vertex slide\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003EMeshes\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003EModifiers on a mesh\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Mesh workspace edits a mesh point by point: its vertices, edges and faces. Push/pull geometry stays in the Model workspace. Open it Pick Mesh in the Workspace list at the left of the toolbar. The selected mesh opens for editing; a selected group or component is turned into a mesh first (one undo step). Or double-click a mesh, or, with the modeler keys, select it and press Tab. With nothing selected, the panel offers meshes to add: plane, grid, cube, circle, cylinder, cone, sphere, ico sphere and torus. A new one goes where the view is looking and opens for editing. Back to Model (or the list, Tab with the modeler keys, Esc with nothing selected with the push/pull keys) goes back to Model with the mesh selected. While the Mesh workspace is open, the rest of the model is faded and Model's drawing tools are put away. Select Do To 1 2 3 Select vertices, edges or faces Click Select one; Shift+click adds or removes Drag a box Select what is inside and seen; Shift adds, Ctrl removes Alt+click an edge, or double-click it Its edge loop (a face loop in face mode; the whole border on an open edge) Ctrl+Alt+click an edge Its edge ring Triple-click Everything joined to it The Select group also has All, Linked, Loop, Ring, More and Less. Move, rotate, scale Move, Rotate and Scale start from the pointer: move it, then click or press Enter to put the selection down, or Esc to cancel. X, Y or Z (or the arrow keys) lock an axis. Type a distance, an angle or a factor for an exact one — calculations work too. Proportional editing (Proportional, or the panel): vertices near the selection follow a move, less the further they are, up to the reach you set, with the falloff you choose. Turn on Only along the mesh to measure along edges rather than straight. Slide moves a selected edge loop along the edges beside it. Change the shape Tool Does Extrude Pulls the selected faces out along their normal, with new side faces (or makes faces from open border edges). Type the distance Inset A band inside the selected faces' outline; press I again for each face on its own Bevel Cuts the selected edges (or, Ctrl+Shift+B, vertices) at a width; the wheel or the plus and minus keys add segments for a rounded edge Loop cut Point at an edge: new loops across the ring of four-sided faces; the wheel sets how many; click to cut Knife Click points on edges or corners; Enter cuts straight across the faces between them, as you see them Bisect Draw a line on screen: the selected faces (or all) are cut along it Spin Turns the selected faces or open edges round the blue axis in steps (the wheel sets them); a full turn closes Subdivide Cuts the selected edges in two; whole faces become a grid Merge The selected vertices become one, at their centre Dissolve Takes the selection out but keeps the surface: faces join Fill A face across a selected open border (or between two open edges) Grid fill Fills an open border with an even number of edges with a grid of quads Bridge Joins two selected open edge loops with faces Crease How sharp the selected edges stay when the mesh is smoothed by a Subdivision surface: move away from the centre, use the wheel (steps of 0.1) or type 0 to 1. The panel shows the selected edges' crease and takes a typed value too. Creased edges are drawn in magenta Flip, Smooth, Delete Turn faces over; smooth vertices toward their neighbours; delete by the select mode Each is one undo step. Texture coordinates, colours and weights follow onto the new faces and vertices. Remesh Remesh makes the whole mesh again as even triangles of one size, asked for when you press it (smaller is finer and slower). It closes holes, joins pieces that overlap, and evens out a mesh that sculpting has stretched. Sharp edges come out very slightly rounded. UVs, paint and creases start again. One undo step. Retopology To build a new, cleaner mesh over a detailed one (a sculpt, a scan), choose the detailed one under Retopology › Build on in the panel, then: Snap moved points onto it: points you move, and edges you extrude, land on its surface. Put the selected points on it moves the selected points onto it now (one undo step). Show this mesh's edges in front draws the mesh you are building over the surface, so you can see it. Adjust the last operation After Inset, Bevel, Loop cut, Subdivide, Smooth, Grid fill or Spin, the panel shows its settings (width, segments, profile, cuts …). Change one and the operation is done again from where it started, still one undo step. Keys Push/pull keys Modeler keys Move, rotate, scale M Q S G R S (G twice slides) Extrude, inset P, F E, I Delete E or Delete X or Delete Crease Shift+C Shift+E Select all, none, invert Ctrl+A, Esc, Ctrl+I A, Alt+A, Ctrl+I Both Ctrl+B bevel, Ctrl+R loop cut, K knife the same, and F fill, M merge, Ctrl+X dissolve, L / Ctrl+L linked, Alt+N flip, O proportional, Shift+V vertex slide See also Meshes, Modifiers on a mesh and Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"modifiers","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Modifiers on a mesh, a group or a component","summary":"Scale, mirror, array, smooth, bevel, cut and bend a mesh without changing it, and repeat or scale a group or component with the same stack; make it permanent when you are ready.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["modifier","modifiers","stack","group","component","scale","bake","edit source","non-destructive","mirror","array","radial array","subdivision","subdivision surface","smooth","bevel","solidify","thickness","boolean","cut","displace","noise","weld","decimate","simplify","lattice","cage","curve deform","bend","shrinkwrap","apply"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-modifier","text":"Add a modifier","level":2},{"id":"the-buttons-on-each-modifier","text":"The buttons on each modifier","level":2},{"id":"the-modifiers","text":"The modifiers","level":2},{"id":"on-a-group-or-a-component","text":"On a group or a component","level":2},{"id":"subdivision-surface","text":"Subdivision surface","level":2},{"id":"bend-with-a-lattice-cage","text":"Bend with a lattice cage","level":2},{"id":"big-meshes","text":"Big meshes","level":2},{"id":"what-modifiers-do-not-do-yet","text":"What modifiers do not do yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Emodifier\u003C/strong\u003E changes what a mesh shows without changing the mesh itself. Mirror one half of a part and model only that half; round a box with a subdivision surface and keep editing the box. Modifiers sit in a \u003Cstrong\u003Estack\u003C/strong\u003E and work in order, top to bottom. What you see, export, print and render is the mesh with every modifier that is on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-modifier\"\u003EAdd a modifier\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a mesh. In the Model workspace its \u003Cstrong\u003EModifiers\u003C/strong\u003E section is in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E; in the Mesh workspace it is in the panel on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick one from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a modifier…\u003C/span\u003E. It goes to the bottom of the stack.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange its settings below its name. Lengths take the same values as the Value box, so \u003Ccode\u003E600mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E(6m)/4\u003C/code\u003E all work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-buttons-on-each-modifier\"\u003EThe buttons on each modifier\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe checkbox turns it on or off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe screen button shows or hides it in the view (it stays in the stack).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe camera button shows or hides it in rendered pictures and videos, so a modifier can be for renders only (or for the view only).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe arrows move it up or down the stack. Order matters: mirror then subdivide gives a smooth seam, subdivide then mirror does not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E makes the shape part of the mesh: the modifiers above it and this one are done to the mesh itself and leave the stack. It is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cross removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-modifiers\"\u003EThe modifiers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EModifier\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScales about the object&#039;s origin, a factor for red, green and blue. On a group or component too.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds a mirrored copy across red, green or blue through the mesh&#039;s origin, and joins the halves where they meet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArray\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies in a line (a step of its own size times the numbers you give, plus a fixed step), round an axis, or along a curve in the model. A group or component takes the line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubdivision surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmooths the mesh by Catmull-Clark subdivision. Sharp edges stay creased. Renders can use more levels than the view.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBevel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERounds every edge sharper than the angle you give.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolidify\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGives a surface a thickness and closes the rim.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoolean\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECuts away, joins or keeps the overlap with another object in the model. Both need to be closed solids.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDisplace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves the surface in or out by smooth noise. The same seed gives the same bumps every time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeld\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoins vertices closer than a distance.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps a share of the triangles, removing the ones that change the shape least.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmooth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelaxes the vertices toward their neighbours.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELattice\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBends the mesh with a cage: move the cage&#039;s points and the mesh follows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECurve deform\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBends the mesh along a curve in the model, along red, green or blue.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShrinkwrap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves every vertex onto the nearest point of another object.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-group-or-a-component\"\u003EOn a group or a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group or component can keep a recipe too, so a lamp&#039;s shade can be a \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E and an \u003Cstrong\u003EArray\u003C/strong\u003E of one shade, and changing a number changes the lamp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a group or component. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E its \u003Cstrong\u003EModifiers\u003C/strong\u003E section lists the stack (empty at first).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a modifier…\u003C/span\u003E. The group becomes a component that is \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe original, placed by the stack\u003C/strong\u003E. The original is kept: edit it and every copy follows at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScale takes a factor for red, green and blue (\u003Ccode\u003E1, 1, 2\u003C/code\u003E doubles the height). Array takes the number of copies, a step as a share of the result&#039;s size so far, and an extra step in lengths.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stack runs top to bottom on the original&#039;s own axes about its origin, so order matters: an Array of three copies 3 m apart, then Scale 2 on red, puts the copies 6 m apart; Scale first and then the Array keeps them 3 m apart. The checkbox, the arrows and the cross work as on a mesh; the view and camera buttons and Apply on one modifier do not, because a stack on a group is one thing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit source\u003C/span\u003E opens the original in place. Changes show in every copy; closing it returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBake modifiers\u003C/span\u003E makes the result plain edges and faces and takes the stack away, in one undo step. Copies that overlap stay as separate geometry: Bake is not a union.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies of the component share the recipe; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E separates it (the original stays shared).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe stack is saved in the file, carried by copy and paste and by undo, and Purge unused keeps the original. A program that does not know modifiers still shows the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntil Bake, the result cannot be entered, exploded, resized or stretched: it would be rebuilt on the next change. Each says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe limits are 32 modifiers and 1,000 copies. A component made to stretch (Stretch) and a stack cannot share a definition yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"subdivision-surface\"\u003ESubdivision surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESubdivision smooths a mesh by Catmull-Clark: every face becomes four-sided faces, and each level makes four times as many. A box with two or three levels already reads as a rounded shape, and you keep editing the box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELevels in the view\u003C/strong\u003E sets how smooth it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELevels in renders\u003C/strong\u003E sets how smooth rendered pictures and videos are; renders can use more levels than the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenders on the true smooth surface\u003C/strong\u003E puts a rendered mesh&#039;s points on the limit surface the levels tend to, so even two levels render as smooth as many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECreases\u003C/strong\u003E keep edges sharp: in the Mesh workspace select edges and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrease\u003C/span\u003E (1 stays sharp, 0.5 rounds a little, 0 is smooth). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003EEdit a mesh\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELower the view level on a big mesh:\u003C/strong\u003E when the levels you ask for would give more faces than this computer&#039;s view handles well, the view shows a lower level and says which. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E always uses every level. How many faces is set by your machine&#039;s class in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › Rendering › Your machine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen borders keep their corners and stay on their edges&#039; line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bend-with-a-lattice-cage\"\u003EBend with a lattice cage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELattice\u003C/span\u003E modifier and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a lattice cage round the mesh\u003C/span\u003E. A cage of 3 × 3 × 3 points appears round the mesh, drawn as wires.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the cage in the Mesh workspace (double-click it) and move its points. The mesh follows smoothly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cage is a mesh like any other: hide it with its tag when you are done.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"big-meshes\"\u003EBig meshes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESmall stacks are worked out at once. A big one is worked out in the background on every core of your computer: the view keeps showing the last result until the new one is ready. Booleans load the solid modeler the first time; the view says so for a moment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-modifiers-do-not-do-yet\"\u003EWhat modifiers do not do yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWeights per vertex (a falloff painted on the mesh) for Displace, Smooth and Shrinkwrap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EModifiers other than Scale and the linear Array on push/pull geometry (groups and components): turn it into a mesh first (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConvert to mesh\u003C/span\u003E). Mirror, a round or curve Array, Bend and Taper on groups come later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A modifier changes what a mesh shows without changing the mesh itself. Mirror one half of a part and model only that half; round a box with a subdivision surface and keep editing the box. Modifiers sit in a stack and work in order, top to bottom. What you see, export, print and render is the mesh with every modifier that is on. Add a modifier Select a mesh. In the Model workspace its Modifiers section is in Entity info; in the Mesh workspace it is in the panel on the right. Pick one from Add a modifier…. It goes to the bottom of the stack. Change its settings below its name. Lengths take the same values as the Value box, so 600mm, 2'6\" and (6m)/4 all work. Every change is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. The buttons on each modifier The checkbox turns it on or off. The screen button shows or hides it in the view (it stays in the stack). The camera button shows or hides it in rendered pictures and videos, so a modifier can be for renders only (or for the view only). The arrows move it up or down the stack. Order matters: mirror then subdivide gives a smooth seam, subdivide then mirror does not. Apply makes the shape part of the mesh: the modifiers above it and this one are done to the mesh itself and leave the stack. It is one undo step. The cross removes it. The modifiers Modifier What it does Scale Scales about the object's origin, a factor for red, green and blue. On a group or component too. Mirror Adds a mirrored copy across red, green or blue through the mesh's origin, and joins the halves where they meet. Array Copies in a line (a step of its own size times the numbers you give, plus a fixed step), round an axis, or along a curve in the model. A group or component takes the line. Subdivision surface Smooths the mesh by Catmull-Clark subdivision. Sharp edges stay creased. Renders can use more levels than the view. Bevel Rounds every edge sharper than the angle you give. Solidify Gives a surface a thickness and closes the rim. Boolean Cuts away, joins or keeps the overlap with another object in the model. Both need to be closed solids. Displace Moves the surface in or out by smooth noise. The same seed gives the same bumps every time. Weld Joins vertices closer than a distance. Decimate Keeps a share of the triangles, removing the ones that change the shape least. Smooth Relaxes the vertices toward their neighbours. Lattice Bends the mesh with a cage: move the cage's points and the mesh follows. Curve deform Bends the mesh along a curve in the model, along red, green or blue. Shrinkwrap Moves every vertex onto the nearest point of another object. On a group or a component A group or component can keep a recipe too, so a lamp's shade can be a Scale and an Array of one shade, and changing a number changes the lamp. Select a group or component. In Entity info its Modifiers section lists the stack (empty at first). Pick Scale or Array from Add a modifier…. The group becomes a component that is the original, placed by the stack. The original is kept: edit it and every copy follows at once. Scale takes a factor for red, green and blue (1, 1, 2 doubles the height). Array takes the number of copies, a step as a share of the result's size so far, and an extra step in lengths. The stack runs top to bottom on the original's own axes about its origin, so order matters: an Array of three copies 3 m apart, then Scale 2 on red, puts the copies 6 m apart; Scale first and then the Array keeps them 3 m apart. The checkbox, the arrows and the cross work as on a mesh; the view and camera buttons and Apply on one modifier do not, because a stack on a group is one thing. Edit source opens the original in place. Changes show in every copy; closing it returns to where you were. Bake modifiers makes the result plain edges and faces and takes the stack away, in one undo step. Copies that overlap stay as separate geometry: Bake is not a union. Copies of the component share the recipe; Make unique separates it (the original stays shared). The stack is saved in the file, carried by copy and paste and by undo, and Purge unused keeps the original. A program that does not know modifiers still shows the copies. Until Bake, the result cannot be entered, exploded, resized or stretched: it would be rebuilt on the next change. Each says so. The limits are 32 modifiers and 1,000 copies. A component made to stretch (Stretch) and a stack cannot share a definition yet. Subdivision surface Subdivision smooths a mesh by Catmull-Clark: every face becomes four-sided faces, and each level makes four times as many. A box with two or three levels already reads as a rounded shape, and you keep editing the box. Levels in the view sets how smooth it is. Levels in renders sets how smooth rendered pictures and videos are; renders can use more levels than the view. Renders on the true smooth surface puts a rendered mesh's points on the limit surface the levels tend to, so even two levels render as smooth as many. Creases keep edges sharp: in the Mesh workspace select edges and use Crease (1 stays sharp, 0.5 rounds a little, 0 is smooth). See Edit a mesh. Lower the view level on a big mesh: when the levels you ask for would give more faces than this computer's view handles well, the view shows a lower level and says which. Apply always uses every level. How many faces is set by your machine's class in Settings › Rendering › Your machine. Open borders keep their corners and stay on their edges' line. Bend with a lattice cage Add a Lattice modifier and press Add a lattice cage round the mesh. A cage of 3 × 3 × 3 points appears round the mesh, drawn as wires. Open the cage in the Mesh workspace (double-click it) and move its points. The mesh follows smoothly. A cage is a mesh like any other: hide it with its tag when you are done. Big meshes Small stacks are worked out at once. A big one is worked out in the background on every core of your computer: the view keeps showing the last result until the new one is ready. Booleans load the solid modeler the first time; the view says so for a moment. What modifiers do not do yet Weights per vertex (a falloff painted on the mesh) for Displace, Smooth and Shrinkwrap. Modifiers other than Scale and the linear Array on push/pull geometry (groups and components): turn it into a mesh first (Convert to mesh). Mirror, a round or curve Array, Bend and Taper on groups come later.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"rotate-scale","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Rotate and scale","summary":"Turn things about a point by clicking or by typing degrees, and resize them evenly by a factor.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["scale grips","stretch","non-uniform","per axis","flip","mirror","mirrored copy","flip along","rotate","turn","angle","degrees","protractor","scale","resize","enlarge","shrink","factor","uniform scale"],"headings":[{"id":"rotate","text":"Rotate","level":2},{"id":"scale","text":"Scale","level":2},{"id":"stretch-or-scale-with-the-grips","text":"Stretch or scale with the grips","level":3},{"id":"scale-about-any-point","text":"Scale about any point","level":3},{"id":"scale-to-an-exact-size","text":"Scale to an exact size","level":3},{"id":"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size","text":"Fix an import that came in the wrong size","level":3},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"flip","text":"Flip","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"rotate\"\u003ERotate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) turns the selection about a center point. It takes three clicks: the center, a starting direction, and the end direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to turn. If nothing is selected, Rotate takes the thing you click first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A ring follows the pointer, showing the plane you will turn in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center of rotation.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the ground or in empty space, the ring lies flat (blue): the turn is about a vertical axis, like turning a building on its site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a face, the ring lies in that face&#039;s plane: the turn is about the face&#039;s own direction. Click on a wall to turn something about a horizontal axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point that sets where the angle starts, for example along an edge of the thing you are turning.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer around the center. The angle shows in the tip and in the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E). It clicks into steps of 15° when you get close to one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type the angle in degrees and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-30\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPositive angles turn counterclockwise, seen from the side the ring faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELike Move, rotating part of connected geometry twists the faces attached to it. Group things first to turn them whole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tip says arrow keys pick the rotation axis, but in this build they orbit the view instead before the first click. To choose the axis, click the center on a face that faces the way you want. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale\"\u003EScale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) resizes the selection. Select what you want to resize first, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: small squares (grips) appear on the corners, the edges and the middles of the sides of its box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"stretch-or-scale-with-the-grips\"\u003EStretch or scale with the grips\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecorner\u003C/strong\u003E grip scales evenly in every direction, about the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grip in the middle of a \u003Cstrong\u003Eside\u003C/strong\u003E stretches along that direction only, about the opposite side: make a box longer without making it taller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grip in the middle of a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebox edge\u003C/strong\u003E stretches in the two directions across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when you click a grip to scale about the middle of the box instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a grip, move the pointer, and click again; or click the grip and type a factor (\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E doubles, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E halves) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. To give each direction its own factor, type three, for red, green and blue: \u003Ccode\u003E2,1,0.5\u003C/code\u003E. Inside an open group, the directions are the group&#039;s own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-about-any-point\"\u003EScale about any point\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick anywhere other than a grip to scale evenly about a point of your own. It takes three clicks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to resize. If nothing is selected, Scale takes the thing you click first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the point that stays put (not on a grip), for example a bottom corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a reference point. Its distance from the first point counts as 1×.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer toward or away from the first point. The factor shows in the tip and in the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type a factor and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E doubles the size, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E halves it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-to-an-exact-size\"\u003EScale to an exact size\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make something an exact length, work out the factor: new length divided by current length. The value box does not do arithmetic, so work it out first: to make a 3.6 m wall 4.2 m long, 4.2 divided by 3.6 is about 1.1667, so type \u003Ccode\u003E1.1667\u003C/code\u003E. Select the edge first to read its current length in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003EFix an import that came in the wrong size\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOBJ files have no unit, so a model drawn in millimeters comes in 1000 times too big. Select the imported group, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a corner, click any other point, and type \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E. For a model drawn in inches, type \u003Ccode\u003E0.0254\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScale is uniform only: there is no stretching in one direction and no grips on a box around the selection. To make a box longer in one direction, push/pull its end, or move its end face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScaling a group or component changes the copy you scaled; other copies of a component keep their size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the last click to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flip\"\u003EFlip\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along red\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along green\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along blue\u003C/span\u003E mirrors the selection in place, through the middle of its box: a left-hand stair becomes a right-hand one. Faces keep facing out, and a group or component is mirrored as a whole (its insides are not rewritten).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlipped copy along red\u003C/span\u003E (and green, blue) leaves the original where it is and puts a mirrored copy beside it, across the far side of the selection&#039;s box: the other door of a pair, the other wing of a building. Loose geometry touching there joins the original, as touching geometry always does; make the original a group first to keep them apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInside an open group, red, green and blue are the group&#039;s own axes.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Rotate Rotate (Q) turns the selection about a center point. It takes three clicks: the center, a starting direction, and the end direction. Select what you want to turn. If nothing is selected, Rotate takes the thing you click first. Press Q. A ring follows the pointer, showing the plane you will turn in. Click the center of rotation. On the ground or in empty space, the ring lies flat (blue): the turn is about a vertical axis, like turning a building on its site. On a face, the ring lies in that face's plane: the turn is about the face's own direction. Click on a wall to turn something about a horizontal axis. Click a point that sets where the angle starts, for example along an edge of the thing you are turning. Move the pointer around the center. The angle shows in the tip and in the value box (Angle). It clicks into steps of 15° when you get close to one. Click to finish, or type the angle in degrees and press Enter: 90, 45, -30. Positive angles turn counterclockwise, seen from the side the ring faces. Like Move, rotating part of connected geometry twists the faces attached to it. Group things first to turn them whole. The tip says arrow keys pick the rotation axis, but in this build they orbit the view instead before the first click. To choose the axis, click the center on a face that faces the way you want. See Not built yet. Scale Scale (S) resizes the selection. Select what you want to resize first, then press S: small squares (grips) appear on the corners, the edges and the middles of the sides of its box. Stretch or scale with the grips A corner grip scales evenly in every direction, about the opposite corner. The grip in the middle of a side stretches along that direction only, about the opposite side: make a box longer without making it taller. The grip in the middle of a box edge stretches in the two directions across it. Hold Ctrl when you click a grip to scale about the middle of the box instead. Click a grip, move the pointer, and click again; or click the grip and type a factor (2 doubles, 0.5 halves) and press Enter. To give each direction its own factor, type three, for red, green and blue: 2,1,0.5. Inside an open group, the directions are the group's own. Scale about any point Click anywhere other than a grip to scale evenly about a point of your own. It takes three clicks: Select what you want to resize. If nothing is selected, Scale takes the thing you click first. Press S. Click the point that stays put (not on a grip), for example a bottom corner. Click a reference point. Its distance from the first point counts as 1×. Move the pointer toward or away from the first point. The factor shows in the tip and in the value box (Scale). Click to finish, or type a factor and press Enter: 2 doubles the size, 0.5 halves it. Scale to an exact size To make something an exact length, work out the factor: new length divided by current length. The value box does not do arithmetic, so work it out first: to make a 3.6 m wall 4.2 m long, 4.2 divided by 3.6 is about 1.1667, so type 1.1667. Select the edge first to read its current length in Entity info. Fix an import that came in the wrong size OBJ files have no unit, so a model drawn in millimeters comes in 1000 times too big. Select the imported group, press S, click a corner, click any other point, and type 0.001. For a model drawn in inches, type 0.0254. Things to know Scale is uniform only: there is no stretching in one direction and no grips on a box around the selection. To make a box longer in one direction, push/pull its end, or move its end face. Scaling a group or component changes the copy you scaled; other copies of a component keep their size. Press Esc before the last click to cancel. Flip Edit › More › Flip along red, Flip along green or Flip along blue mirrors the selection in place, through the middle of its box: a left-hand stair becomes a right-hand one. Faces keep facing out, and a group or component is mirrored as a whole (its insides are not rewritten). Flipped copy along red (and green, blue) leaves the original where it is and puts a mirrored copy beside it, across the far side of the selection's box: the other door of a pair, the other wing of a building. Loose geometry touching there joins the original, as touching geometry always does; make the original a group first to keep them apart. Inside an open group, red, green and blue are the group's own axes.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"uv","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Lay out a mesh's UVs","summary":"Mark seams, unwrap, project and pack a mesh's texture coordinates in the UV workspace, and check them with a checker and the stretch colours.","section":"tasks","order":61,"keywords":["uv","uvs","unwrap","unwrapping","seam","seams","texture","texture coordinates","atlas","pack","packing","island","islands","checker","stretch","distortion","box projection","cylinder projection","sphere projection","conformal","angle based","lscm","abf"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"mark-seams","text":"Mark seams","level":2},{"id":"unwrap","text":"Unwrap","level":2},{"id":"project","text":"Project","level":2},{"id":"arrange","text":"Arrange","level":2},{"id":"check-them","text":"Check them","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA texture is a flat picture; a mesh is not flat. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EUVs\u003C/strong\u003E say where each corner of each face sits on the picture. The UV workspace makes them: you cut the mesh open along \u003Cstrong\u003Eseams\u003C/strong\u003E, lay the pieces flat (\u003Cstrong\u003Eunwrap\u003C/strong\u003E), and fit them into the square (\u003Cstrong\u003Epack\u003C/strong\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a mesh and pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUV\u003C/span\u003E in the Workspace list. A group or component becomes a mesh first, as in the Mesh workspace. The 3D view moves to the left half; the \u003Cstrong\u003EUV editor\u003C/strong\u003E on the right shows the mesh&#039;s faces laid out on the texture square. Selecting works as in the Mesh workspace, and what you select in one view is selected in the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mark-seams\"\u003EMark seams\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeams are where the mesh may be cut open; they show red in both views.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect edges (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdge\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"2\"\u003E2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMark seam\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control E\"\u003E⌃E\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+E\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear seam\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift E\"\u003E⌃⇧E\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+E\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) takes them off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeams at sharp\u003C/span\u003E marks every sharp edge and every edge where faces meet at more than 60°: a box falls into its six sides.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeams from UVs\u003C/span\u003E marks seams where the UVs already break, for example after a projection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA closed piece needs seams to lie flat: a ball needs at least one cut, a tube a cut along its length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unwrap\"\u003EUnwrap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the faces to lay out (or none, for the whole mesh) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnwrap\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Each piece between seams is laid flat, keeping its angles as true as it can (angle-based flattening), then the pieces are packed into the square. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConformal\u003C/span\u003E unwraps by least-squares conformal maps instead: quicker, a little less true on strongly curved pieces. A shape that can be made from a flat sheet, such as a cylinder&#039;s side cut once, unwraps with no stretch at all.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"project\"\u003EProject\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor simple shapes a projection is often enough:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProjection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach face onto the side of a box it faces most\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECylinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound the blue axis: across is the angle round, up is the height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESphere\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the centre: across is the angle round, up is the latitude\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom view\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs the faces are seen in the 3D view now\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face across the cylinder&#039;s or the sphere&#039;s join is kept in one piece, so its UVs can run a little past the right edge of the square; textures repeat there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrange\"\u003EArrange\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPack\u003C/span\u003E fits the pieces (islands) into the square at one texel size, each turned to take the least room. The panel sets the space left between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E turn or mirror the selected faces&#039; UVs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the UV editor, drag a selected face to move the selection&#039;s UVs, or point at the editor and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E then move to turn or scale them (a click keeps it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E moves without dragging. Double-click a face (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to select its whole island. The wheel zooms; drag with the middle or right button to pan; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fits the square again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-them\"\u003ECheck them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecker\u003C/span\u003E shows a labelled checker on the mesh in its UVs: even, square checks mean the texture lies evenly; the labels show which way it runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E colours each face by how much its UVs stretch it: blue none, through green, to red a lot. The panel chooses whether that measures \u003Cstrong\u003Eangles bent\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Esize changed\u003C/strong\u003E, and shows the average.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003EEdit a mesh\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003EMeshes\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003EModifiers on a mesh\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A texture is a flat picture; a mesh is not flat. Its UVs say where each corner of each face sits on the picture. The UV workspace makes them: you cut the mesh open along seams, lay the pieces flat (unwrap), and fit them into the square (pack). Open it Select a mesh and pick UV in the Workspace list. A group or component becomes a mesh first, as in the Mesh workspace. The 3D view moves to the left half; the UV editor on the right shows the mesh's faces laid out on the texture square. Selecting works as in the Mesh workspace, and what you select in one view is selected in the other. Mark seams Seams are where the mesh may be cut open; they show red in both views. Select edges (Edge, or 2) and press Mark seam (Ctrl+E). Clear seam (Ctrl+Shift+E) takes them off. Seams at sharp marks every sharp edge and every edge where faces meet at more than 60°: a box falls into its six sides. Seams from UVs marks seams where the UVs already break, for example after a projection. A closed piece needs seams to lie flat: a ball needs at least one cut, a tube a cut along its length. Unwrap Select the faces to lay out (or none, for the whole mesh) and press Unwrap (U). Each piece between seams is laid flat, keeping its angles as true as it can (angle-based flattening), then the pieces are packed into the square. Conformal unwraps by least-squares conformal maps instead: quicker, a little less true on strongly curved pieces. A shape that can be made from a flat sheet, such as a cylinder's side cut once, unwraps with no stretch at all. Project For simple shapes a projection is often enough: Projection What it does Box Each face onto the side of a box it faces most Cylinder Round the blue axis: across is the angle round, up is the height Sphere From the centre: across is the angle round, up is the latitude From view As the faces are seen in the 3D view now A face across the cylinder's or the sphere's join is kept in one piece, so its UVs can run a little past the right edge of the square; textures repeat there. Arrange Pack fits the pieces (islands) into the square at one texel size, each turned to take the least room. The panel sets the space left between them. Turn 90° and Mirror turn or mirror the selected faces' UVs. In the UV editor, drag a selected face to move the selection's UVs, or point at the editor and press R or S then move to turn or scale them (a click keeps it, Esc cancels). G moves without dragging. Double-click a face (or L) to select its whole island. The wheel zooms; drag with the middle or right button to pan; F fits the square again. Every change is one undo step. Check them Checker shows a labelled checker on the mesh in its UVs: even, square checks mean the texture lies evenly; the labels show which way it runs. Stretch colours each face by how much its UVs stretch it: blue none, through green, to red a lot. The panel chooses whether that measures angles bent or size changed, and shows the average. See also Edit a mesh, Meshes and Modifiers on a mesh.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"curves","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Curve objects","summary":"Draw Bézier, NURBS and straight-segment curves, give them a thickness, a width or a taper, fill flat ones, set text as a curve, and turn them into meshes.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["curve","curves","bezier","nurbs","spline","splines","path","tube","pipe","cable","rope","bevel","extrude","taper","tilt","radius","fill","profile","text","letters","logo","curve to mesh"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-curve","text":"Add a curve","level":2},{"id":"edit-the-points","text":"Edit the points","level":2},{"id":"shape","text":"Shape","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-into-a-mesh","text":"Turn it into a mesh","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurve object\u003C/strong\u003E is a curve that makes a shape: a tube along it (bevel), a band (extrude), a tapering horn (taper), or — when it is flat and closed — a filled plate or letters. The shape follows the curve: move a point and the tube moves with it. When the shape is right, turn it into a mesh.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-curve\"\u003EAdd a curve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E in the Workspace list and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBézier curve, Bézier circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPasses through its points; each point has two handles that set the curve&#039;s direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENURBS curve, NURBS circle, Path\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmooth, drawn towards its points (it starts and ends on its end points); each point has a weight\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour text as flat, filled letter outlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new curve gets a thin tube so it can be seen and picked; set its bevel depth to 0 for a bare curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also start from something drawn: select an arc, a circle, a polygon or a drawn curve in the Model workspace and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake a curve object\u003C/span\u003E in Entity info. The drawn lines stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-the-points\"\u003EEdit the points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click a curve object (or select it and pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E) to edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point or handle (Shift adds or removes); drag it to move it. Drag on empty space to select with a box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtend\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click) adds a point after the selected end, at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubdivide\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) puts a point between each pair of selected neighbours; a Bézier keeps its shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E move the selected points with the pointer (modeler keys \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, push/pull keys \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E); click to keep, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) closes an open spline or opens a closed one; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift R\"\u003E⇧R\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+R\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) runs it the other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBézier handles: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) places them for a smooth curve, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift V\"\u003E⇧V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) points them at the neighbours, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option V\"\u003E⌥V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) leaves them where you put them. Dragging a handle makes it free.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBézier\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENURBS\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoly\u003C/span\u003E change the selected splines&#039; type, keeping the points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shape\"\u003EShape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings are in the panel (and in Entity info in the Model workspace):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShape:\u003C/strong\u003E 3D curves go any way; a flat curve stays in its own plane and can be filled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFill closed splines\u003C/strong\u003E (flat): the outlines become a plate; an outline inside another is a hole, so letters keep their holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExtrude:\u003C/strong\u003E half the band&#039;s width (open or 3D curves), or half the plate&#039;s thickness (filled).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBevel depth\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eroundness:\u003C/strong\u003E a round tube along the curve (with extrude: a rounded band); on a filled shape, a rounded edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClose the ends:\u003C/strong\u003E caps on an open tube.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETaper along it:\u003C/strong\u003E scales evenly along each spline, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1, 0.2\u003C/code\u003E for a horn, \u003Ccode\u003E0, 1, 0\u003C/code\u003E for a spindle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESmoothness\u003C/strong\u003E and, for NURBS, \u003Cstrong\u003Eorder\u003C/strong\u003E: how finely it is drawn, and how smooth (2 straight … 6 smoothest).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith points selected: \u003Cstrong\u003Eradius\u003C/strong\u003E (the tube&#039;s thickness there, times the bevel), \u003Cstrong\u003Etilt\u003C/strong\u003E (turns the band about the curve) and, for NURBS, \u003Cstrong\u003Eweight\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModifiers work on top of a curve object as on a mesh: an Array along it, a Mirror, a Subdivision surface. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003EModifiers on a mesh\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-into-a-mesh\"\u003ETurn it into a mesh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo mesh\u003C/span\u003E (in the Curve workspace or in Entity info, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve to mesh\u003C/span\u003E) makes the curve object the mesh it shows now; its modifiers stay on top. Then edit it in the Mesh workspace. Undo turns it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003EMeshes\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003EEdit a mesh\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A curve object is a curve that makes a shape: a tube along it (bevel), a band (extrude), a tapering horn (taper), or — when it is flat and closed — a filled plate or letters. The shape follows the curve: move a point and the tube moves with it. When the shape is right, turn it into a mesh. Add a curve Pick Curve in the Workspace list and press Add: Kind What it is Bézier curve, Bézier circle Passes through its points; each point has two handles that set the curve's direction NURBS curve, NURBS circle, Path Smooth, drawn towards its points (it starts and ends on its end points); each point has a weight Text Your text as flat, filled letter outlines A new curve gets a thin tube so it can be seen and picked; set its bevel depth to 0 for a bare curve. You can also start from something drawn: select an arc, a circle, a polygon or a drawn curve in the Model workspace and press Make a curve object in Entity info. The drawn lines stay. Edit the points Double-click a curve object (or select it and pick Curve) to edit it. Click a point or handle (Shift adds or removes); drag it to move it. Drag on empty space to select with a box. Extend (E, or Ctrl+click) adds a point after the selected end, at the pointer. Subdivide (W) puts a point between each pair of selected neighbours; a Bézier keeps its shape. Move, Turn, Scale move the selected points with the pointer (modeler keys G R S, push/pull keys M Q S); click to keep, Esc to cancel. Close (C) closes an open spline or opens a closed one; Reverse (Shift+R) runs it the other way. Bézier handles: Smooth (V) places them for a smooth curve, Corner (Shift+V) points them at the neighbours, Free (Alt+V) leaves them where you put them. Dragging a handle makes it free. Bézier, NURBS and Poly change the selected splines' type, keeping the points. Every change is one undo step. Shape The settings are in the panel (and in Entity info in the Model workspace): Shape: 3D curves go any way; a flat curve stays in its own plane and can be filled. Fill closed splines (flat): the outlines become a plate; an outline inside another is a hole, so letters keep their holes. Extrude: half the band's width (open or 3D curves), or half the plate's thickness (filled). Bevel depth and roundness: a round tube along the curve (with extrude: a rounded band); on a filled shape, a rounded edge. Close the ends: caps on an open tube. Taper along it: scales evenly along each spline, for example 1, 0.2 for a horn, 0, 1, 0 for a spindle. Smoothness and, for NURBS, order: how finely it is drawn, and how smooth (2 straight … 6 smoothest). With points selected: radius (the tube's thickness there, times the bevel), tilt (turns the band about the curve) and, for NURBS, weight. Modifiers work on top of a curve object as on a mesh: an Array along it, a Mirror, a Subdivision surface. See Modifiers on a mesh. Turn it into a mesh To mesh (in the Curve workspace or in Entity info, Curve to mesh) makes the curve object the mesh it shows now; its modifiers stay on top. Then edit it in the Mesh workspace. Undo turns it back. See also Meshes and Edit a mesh.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"shading","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Make materials with nodes","summary":"Build a material from textures, pictures and maths in the Shading workspace, see it in the view as you go, and paint faces with it.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["shading","bake","baking","texture baking","ambient occlusion","ao","curvature","normal bake","high poly","low poly","light map","decal","decals","sticker","logo","label","texture painting","paint texture","brush","material","materials","materialx","mtlx","openpbr","standard surface","material library","librarycraft","texture set","pbr","import textures","zip","cc0","drag","node","nodes","node editor","shader","texture","procedural","noise","checker","voronoi","cells","waves","bricks","tiles","wood","marble","gradient","dirt","edge wear","color ramp","mix","bump","normal map","roughness","metalness","opacity","cut-out","emission","glow","displacement","box mapping","triplanar","uv","picture","image texture","openpbr"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"materials-from-the-library","text":"Materials from the library","level":2},{"id":"import-a-texture-set","text":"Import a texture set","level":2},{"id":"materialx","text":"MaterialX","level":2},{"id":"decals","text":"Decals","level":2},{"id":"paint-on-the-texture","text":"Paint on the texture","level":2},{"id":"bake-textures","text":"Bake textures","level":2},{"id":"build-a-graph","text":"Build a graph","level":2},{"id":"the-material-output","text":"The Material output","level":2},{"id":"in-high-quality-pictures","text":"In high-quality pictures","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA material made in the \u003Cstrong\u003EShading\u003C/strong\u003E workspace belongs to the file. It is a small graph: \u003Cstrong\u003Enodes\u003C/strong\u003E that make textures, pictures and numbers, linked into the \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial output\u003C/strong\u003E, whose inputs are the surface&#039;s look — base color, roughness, metalness, normal, opacity, emission and the rest of the OpenPBR model. You paint faces with it like any library material, and every change shows in the view at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShading\u003C/span\u003E in the Workspace list. The 3D view keeps the top of the screen and the \u003Cstrong\u003Enode editor\u003C/strong\u003E takes the bottom. The panel on the right lists the file&#039;s own materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew material\u003C/span\u003E makes a grey one with only its Material output.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMake an editable copy of a library material\u003C/strong\u003E turns brick, wood, tile and the rest into a graph you can change: its texture comes through a Library texture node, its bumps through a Bump node.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint with it\u003C/span\u003E paints the selected faces or groups, or picks the material for the Paint tool. The Paint tool&#039;s materials also list the file&#039;s own under \u003Cstrong\u003EIn this file\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E work on the material you are editing. Deleting cannot be undone: faces painted with it show the default color until you paint them again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"materials-from-the-library\"\u003EMaterials from the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial library (Library)\u003C/strong\u003E lists the library&#039;s materials, each with a preview rendered by CadCraft&#039;s path tracer. They are texture sets released under CC0, credited to the people who made them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one to bring it into the file (its pictures come with it) and, with faces or groups selected, paint them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag one onto a face or a group in the view to paint just that.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt becomes one of the file&#039;s materials: change its nodes like any other. Its credit shows in the panel and stays in the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-a-texture-set\"\u003EImport a texture set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPictures or a zip…\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA folder…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EImport a texture set\u003C/strong\u003E makes a material from a set of maps. The maps are found by the words in their file names: color, albedo, diffuse or basecolor; roughness (or gloss, turned into roughness); metalness or metallic; normal (OpenGL or DirectX, from \u003Ccode\u003Egl\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003Edx\u003C/code\u003E in the name); height or displacement; ambient occlusion; opacity; emission. Packed maps (ARM, ORM) are left out. The tile starts at 1 × 1 m: set its real size on the Material output. Height goes to displacement with its scale at 0 — raise the scale to use it on meshes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"materialx\"\u003EMaterialX\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaterialX is the open format other 3D and rendering programs use to pass materials along.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as MaterialX…\u003C/span\u003E saves the material you are editing: its values, and every texture as a PNG picture beside the \u003Ccode\u003E.mtlx\u003C/code\u003E file (in a zip). Choose the shader it is written as: \u003Cstrong\u003EOpenPBR Surface\u003C/strong\u003E (MaterialX 1.39, the same model ModelCraft uses) or \u003Cstrong\u003EStandard Surface\u003C/strong\u003E (MaterialX 1.38, which more programs read today). The textures are placed by UV coordinates in metres; box and triplanar placement are written as UV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport MaterialX…\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom a folder…\u003C/span\u003E) reads \u003Ccode\u003E.mtlx\u003C/code\u003E files with OpenPBR, Standard Surface, UsdPreviewSurface or glTF PBR shaders. Give it the pictures as well, or a zip of everything: a picture is found by its path, its name, or its name with another picture type (a \u003Ccode\u003E.exr\u003C/code\u003E the file names can come as a \u003Ccode\u003E.jpg\u003C/code\u003E beside it). Textures, normal maps, arithmetic, mixes and displacement come in as nodes; anything else keeps its value, and the status line says what was left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"decals\"\u003EDecals\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA decal is a picture laid onto a surface — a logo, a sign, a label, a stain — with a material of its own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EWidth of a new decal\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace a decal…\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecal\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar), choose a PNG, JPEG or WebP picture and click a face. The picture is projected onto the surfaces there, keeping its proportions; transparent parts of a PNG stay see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt belongs to the group or component you clicked in and moves with it. It covers the faces inside its box that face the same way, and stops at the edges of what it lies on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach decal gets its own material, named \u003Cstrong\u003EDecal: &lt;picture&gt;\u003C/strong\u003E, in the file&#039;s list: edit it like any other (roughness, a bump, a glow). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E beside the decal opens it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBeside each decal: its width (the height follows the picture), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E (a quarter turn) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E. Decals are kept with the file&#039;s materials, outside Undo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThey show in the shaded view, in fast renders and in high-quality pictures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint-on-the-texture\"\u003EPaint on the texture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint texture\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EPaint on the texture\u003C/strong\u003E) turns the pointer into a round brush. Drag on a face that wears one of this file&#039;s materials: the brush paints the picture behind its base color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a mesh it paints along the mesh&#039;s UVs (unwrap it in the UV workspace first); on other faces it paints across the face, so the painting repeats with the material&#039;s tile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA material whose base color is a plain color gets a new 512 × 512 picture of that color the first time you paint it. A material whose base color comes from other nodes needs a Picture node linked to Base color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the brush color, size (in picture pixels), strength and hardness in the panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo stroke\u003C/span\u003E or Ctrl+Z (while painting) takes back a stroke, up to the last 20.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe painted picture is saved in the file. Library materials can&#039;t be painted: make an editable copy first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bake-textures\"\u003EBake textures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBake textures\u003C/strong\u003E writes what the shape and the scene give a surface into pictures along a mesh&#039;s UVs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a mesh that has UVs (unwrap it in the UV workspace), tick the maps and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBake\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAmbient occlusion\u003C/strong\u003E — how open each point is: corners, creases and places under other things get darker. Everything shown in the model shades it, up to the occlusion distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurvature\u003C/strong\u003E — bright along outside edges, dark in inside corners, mid grey on flat and gently curved parts, over the edge width you set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENormals from the high-detail object\u003C/strong\u003E — select a detailed copy too: its surface detail is written as a normal map for the simpler one (the object with fewer triangles is baked onto). The search distance is how far apart the two surfaces may be.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELighting\u003C/strong\u003E — the sun of the model&#039;s place and date (Style) with its shadows, plus the sky, as a light map.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe pictures join the file and appear in Picture nodes. If the material you are editing has a \u003Cstrong\u003EDirt and edge wear\u003C/strong\u003E node, it reads the new occlusion and curvature at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMore rays per texel make smoother occlusion and lighting and take longer. Baking runs on the computer&#039;s processor cores in the background; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop baking\u003C/span\u003E stops it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"build-a-graph\"\u003EBuild a graph\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a node…\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift A\"\u003E⇧A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the node editor) adds a node in the middle of the editor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag from an output dot (on a node&#039;s right) to an input dot (on a node&#039;s left) to link them. A second link into an input replaces the first; drag a linked input away to unlink it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInputs that are not linked show their value: type a number or pick a color. Settings sit at the bottom of each node.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a node by its title to move it, drag empty space to pan, and use the wheel to zoom. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the selected nodes or link, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E duplicates, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E shows every node. Every change is one undo step of the node editor. The model&#039;s own undo does not change materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENodes\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat they make\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETexture coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere on the tile a point is: repeat it, offset it, turn it a quarter.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EValue, Color\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA number, a color.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the file (one that came with an imported model, or one you added with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a picture…\u003C/span\u003E). Choose \u003Cstrong\u003EColors\u003C/strong\u003E for a photo and \u003Cstrong\u003EData\u003C/strong\u003E for a roughness or normal map.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELibrary texture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA library material&#039;s texture, roughness and height.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChecker, Noise\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquares; smooth fractal noise with its detail, roughness and seed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVoronoi cells, Waves, Gradient\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECells (distance to the nearest point, the edges between cells, a color per cell); bands across, up or diagonal, as sine, saw or triangle, bent by noise; linear, quadratic, radial and spherical gradients.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBricks, Tiles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERows of bricks with a running offset, or a grid of tiles: colors, mortar or grout, a gap and a bevel in metres, a height for bumps, a random number per piece.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWood, Marble\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERings along the grain with fine fibres; veins bent by turbulence.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDirt and edge wear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWears convex edges and dirties hollows from a mesh&#039;s curvature and occlusion pictures (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#bake-textures\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"bake-textures\"\u003EBake textures\u003C/a\u003E makes them; or set its two picture settings, in the mesh&#039;s UV layout: place the material by UV coordinates). Without them it changes nothing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMix colors, Color ramp, Hue/saturation/value, Brightness and contrast, Invert, Gamma, Separate and Combine RGB\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColor work.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMath, Map range\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArithmetic on numbers (add, multiply, power, smooth step and more).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBump, Normal map\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA normal from a height (Bump) or from a normal-map picture (Normal map: pick the green channel&#039;s direction).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-material-output\"\u003EThe Material output\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts settings say how the texture lies on surfaces:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETile width\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETile height\u003C/strong\u003E: how many metres one tile of the texture covers. Everything a graph makes repeats across the tile with no seam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlaced by\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EUV coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E follow faces (push/pull faces: along each face; meshes: their UVs, one UV unit = one tile), \u003Cstrong\u003EBox\u003C/strong\u003E projects from the nearest of the three axes, \u003Cstrong\u003ETriplanar\u003C/strong\u003E blends the three projections on surfaces that lean (\u003Cstrong\u003EBlend sharpness\u003C/strong\u003E: higher is a harder blend).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENormal strength\u003C/strong\u003E scales the normal input. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplacement scale\u003C/strong\u003E sets how far the displacement input moves a mesh&#039;s vertices.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore inputs\u003C/span\u003E shows the rest of the OpenPBR inputs: coat, fuzz, subsurface, thin film, transmission and their colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-high-quality-pictures\"\u003EIn high-quality pictures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe path tracer reads the base color, roughness, metalness, opacity, normal and emission from the graph as they change across the surface. Any other input you link (a coat that varies, for example) is used at its average; the panel says which. A cut-out from an opacity texture shows its shape, and its shadow is evenly see-through. A glowing texture lights what it faces, without being sampled as a lamp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDisplacement\u003C/strong\u003E moves the vertices of meshes only, so add a Subdivision modifier for detail (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003EModifiers\u003C/a\u003E). Push/pull faces keep their shape and get the bumps from the normal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a computer with WebGPU the graph is worked out on the graphics card; without it, on the processor. The result is the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A material made in the Shading workspace belongs to the file. It is a small graph: nodes that make textures, pictures and numbers, linked into the Material output, whose inputs are the surface's look — base color, roughness, metalness, normal, opacity, emission and the rest of the OpenPBR model. You paint faces with it like any library material, and every change shows in the view at once. Open it Pick Shading in the Workspace list. The 3D view keeps the top of the screen and the node editor takes the bottom. The panel on the right lists the file's own materials. New material makes a grey one with only its Material output. Make an editable copy of a library material turns brick, wood, tile and the rest into a graph you can change: its texture comes through a Library texture node, its bumps through a Bump node. Paint with it paints the selected faces or groups, or picks the material for the Paint tool. The Paint tool's materials also list the file's own under In this file. Duplicate and Delete work on the material you are editing. Deleting cannot be undone: faces painted with it show the default color until you paint them again. Materials from the library The panel's Material library (Library) lists the library's materials, each with a preview rendered by CadCraft's path tracer. They are texture sets released under CC0, credited to the people who made them. Click one to bring it into the file (its pictures come with it) and, with faces or groups selected, paint them. Drag one onto a face or a group in the view to paint just that. It becomes one of the file's materials: change its nodes like any other. Its credit shows in the panel and stays in the file. Import a texture set Pictures or a zip… or A folder… under Import a texture set makes a material from a set of maps. The maps are found by the words in their file names: color, albedo, diffuse or basecolor; roughness (or gloss, turned into roughness); metalness or metallic; normal (OpenGL or DirectX, from gl / dx in the name); height or displacement; ambient occlusion; opacity; emission. Packed maps (ARM, ORM) are left out. The tile starts at 1 × 1 m: set its real size on the Material output. Height goes to displacement with its scale at 0 — raise the scale to use it on meshes. MaterialX MaterialX is the open format other 3D and rendering programs use to pass materials along. Export as MaterialX… saves the material you are editing: its values, and every texture as a PNG picture beside the .mtlx file (in a zip). Choose the shader it is written as: OpenPBR Surface (MaterialX 1.39, the same model ModelCraft uses) or Standard Surface (MaterialX 1.38, which more programs read today). The textures are placed by UV coordinates in metres; box and triplanar placement are written as UV. Import MaterialX… (or From a folder…) reads .mtlx files with OpenPBR, Standard Surface, UsdPreviewSurface or glTF PBR shaders. Give it the pictures as well, or a zip of everything: a picture is found by its path, its name, or its name with another picture type (a .exr the file names can come as a .jpg beside it). Textures, normal maps, arithmetic, mixes and displacement come in as nodes; anything else keeps its value, and the status line says what was left out. Decals A decal is a picture laid onto a surface — a logo, a sign, a label, a stain — with a material of its own. Set Width of a new decal, then Place a decal… (or Decal on the toolbar), choose a PNG, JPEG or WebP picture and click a face. The picture is projected onto the surfaces there, keeping its proportions; transparent parts of a PNG stay see-through. It belongs to the group or component you clicked in and moves with it. It covers the faces inside its box that face the same way, and stops at the edges of what it lies on. Each decal gets its own material, named Decal: \u003Cpicture\u003E, in the file's list: edit it like any other (roughness, a bump, a glow). Material beside the decal opens it. Beside each decal: its width (the height follows the picture), Turn (a quarter turn) and Delete. Decals are kept with the file's materials, outside Undo. They show in the shaded view, in fast renders and in high-quality pictures. Paint on the texture Paint texture (or Paint under Paint on the texture) turns the pointer into a round brush. Drag on a face that wears one of this file's materials: the brush paints the picture behind its base color. On a mesh it paints along the mesh's UVs (unwrap it in the UV workspace first); on other faces it paints across the face, so the painting repeats with the material's tile. A material whose base color is a plain color gets a new 512 × 512 picture of that color the first time you paint it. A material whose base color comes from other nodes needs a Picture node linked to Base color. Set the brush color, size (in picture pixels), strength and hardness in the panel. Undo stroke or Ctrl+Z (while painting) takes back a stroke, up to the last 20. The painted picture is saved in the file. Library materials can't be painted: make an editable copy first. Bake textures Bake textures writes what the shape and the scene give a surface into pictures along a mesh's UVs. Select a mesh that has UVs (unwrap it in the UV workspace), tick the maps and press Bake: Ambient occlusion — how open each point is: corners, creases and places under other things get darker. Everything shown in the model shades it, up to the occlusion distance. Curvature — bright along outside edges, dark in inside corners, mid grey on flat and gently curved parts, over the edge width you set. Normals from the high-detail object — select a detailed copy too: its surface detail is written as a normal map for the simpler one (the object with fewer triangles is baked onto). The search distance is how far apart the two surfaces may be. Lighting — the sun of the model's place and date (Style) with its shadows, plus the sky, as a light map. The pictures join the file and appear in Picture nodes. If the material you are editing has a Dirt and edge wear node, it reads the new occlusion and curvature at once. More rays per texel make smoother occlusion and lighting and take longer. Baking runs on the computer's processor cores in the background; Stop baking stops it. Build a graph Add a node… (or Shift+A in the node editor) adds a node in the middle of the editor. Drag from an output dot (on a node's right) to an input dot (on a node's left) to link them. A second link into an input replaces the first; drag a linked input away to unlink it. Inputs that are not linked show their value: type a number or pick a color. Settings sit at the bottom of each node. Drag a node by its title to move it, drag empty space to pan, and use the wheel to zoom. Delete removes the selected nodes or link, Ctrl+Z undoes, Ctrl+D duplicates, F shows every node. Every change is one undo step of the node editor. The model's own undo does not change materials. Nodes What they make Texture coordinates Where on the tile a point is: repeat it, offset it, turn it a quarter. Value, Color A number, a color. Picture A picture of the file (one that came with an imported model, or one you added with Add a picture…). Choose Colors for a photo and Data for a roughness or normal map. Library texture A library material's texture, roughness and height. Checker, Noise Squares; smooth fractal noise with its detail, roughness and seed. Voronoi cells, Waves, Gradient Cells (distance to the nearest point, the edges between cells, a color per cell); bands across, up or diagonal, as sine, saw or triangle, bent by noise; linear, quadratic, radial and spherical gradients. Bricks, Tiles Rows of bricks with a running offset, or a grid of tiles: colors, mortar or grout, a gap and a bevel in metres, a height for bumps, a random number per piece. Wood, Marble Rings along the grain with fine fibres; veins bent by turbulence. Dirt and edge wear Wears convex edges and dirties hollows from a mesh's curvature and occlusion pictures (Bake textures makes them; or set its two picture settings, in the mesh's UV layout: place the material by UV coordinates). Without them it changes nothing. Mix colors, Color ramp, Hue/saturation/value, Brightness and contrast, Invert, Gamma, Separate and Combine RGB Color work. Math, Map range Arithmetic on numbers (add, multiply, power, smooth step and more). Bump, Normal map A normal from a height (Bump) or from a normal-map picture (Normal map: pick the green channel's direction). The Material output Its settings say how the texture lies on surfaces: Tile width and Tile height: how many metres one tile of the texture covers. Everything a graph makes repeats across the tile with no seam. Placed by: UV coordinates follow faces (push/pull faces: along each face; meshes: their UVs, one UV unit = one tile), Box projects from the nearest of the three axes, Triplanar blends the three projections on surfaces that lean (Blend sharpness: higher is a harder blend). Normal strength scales the normal input. Displacement scale sets how far the displacement input moves a mesh's vertices. More inputs shows the rest of the OpenPBR inputs: coat, fuzz, subsurface, thin film, transmission and their colors. In high-quality pictures The path tracer reads the base color, roughness, metalness, opacity, normal and emission from the graph as they change across the surface. Any other input you link (a coat that varies, for example) is used at its average; the panel says which. A cut-out from an opacity texture shows its shape, and its shadow is evenly see-through. A glowing texture lights what it faces, without being sampled as a lamp. Displacement moves the vertices of meshes only, so add a Subdivision modifier for detail (Modifiers). Push/pull faces keep their shape and get the bumps from the normal. On a computer with WebGPU the graph is worked out on the graphics card; without it, on the processor. The result is the same.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sculpt","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Sculpt a mesh","summary":"Shape a mesh like clay with brushes — draw, clay, smooth, grab, inflate, crease, pinch, flatten — protect parts with a mask, mirror across X, and add detail where you sculpt.","section":"tasks","order":63,"keywords":["sculpt","sculpting","brush","brushes","clay","draw","smooth","grab","inflate","crease","pinch","flatten","mask","symmetry","mirror","detail","dynamic topology","pen","pressure","tablet","stylus"],"headings":[{"id":"start","text":"Start","level":2},{"id":"brushes","text":"Brushes","level":2},{"id":"size-and-strength","text":"Size and strength","level":2},{"id":"mirror-mask-and-detail","text":"Mirror, mask and detail","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe Sculpt workspace shapes a mesh with brushes, as with clay: drag across the surface and it rises, sinks, smooths or follows the pointer under a round brush, strongest in the middle and fading to nothing at its rim.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start\"\u003EStart\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a mesh and pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESculpt\u003C/span\u003E in the Workspace list. A group or component becomes a mesh first, as in the Mesh workspace. Brushes work on the mesh&#039;s vertices, so a mesh with more of them takes finer shapes: subdivide it first (the Mesh workspace&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubdivide\u003C/span\u003E, or a Subdivision surface modifier you Apply), or turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"brushes\"\u003EBrushes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrush\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERaises the surface under the brush\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClay\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilds up flat layers, filling dips first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvens out bumps; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to smooth with any brush\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPulls the surface along with the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInflate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwells the surface out along its own direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrease\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECuts a sharp groove\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraws the surface towards the brush&#039;s middle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlatten\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPresses the surface towards its average plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMask\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaints a mask that protects the surface from the other brushes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while stroking to work the other way (lower instead of raise, a ridge instead of a groove, remove the mask), or turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake away\u003C/span\u003E on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"size-and-strength\"\u003ESize and strength\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe brush&#039;s size is in pixels on the screen, so it feels the same however far you zoom: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"[\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"]\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E make it smaller and bigger, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift [\"\u003E⇧[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ]\"\u003E⇧]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E change its strength. The panel shows both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a pen on a tablet, pressing harder makes the brush bigger and stronger; the panel turns either off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror-mask-and-detail\"\u003EMirror, mask and detail\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror X\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) sculpts both sides across red at once, for faces and figures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe mask shows darker while you sculpt. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear mask\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option M\"\u003E⌥M\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+M\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) takes it off, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvert mask\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control I\"\u003E⌃I\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+I\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) masks what was free and frees what was masked. The mask is kept with the mesh.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E adds triangles where you sculpt: edges longer than the size set in the panel (pixels on the screen) are split under the brush. It needs a mesh of triangles, and asks to make one first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach stroke is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetail only adds triangles; it does not take away ones that became tiny. A Remesh (the Mesh workspace) evens the whole mesh out again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003EEdit a mesh\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003EMeshes\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003EModifiers on a mesh\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Sculpt workspace shapes a mesh with brushes, as with clay: drag across the surface and it rises, sinks, smooths or follows the pointer under a round brush, strongest in the middle and fading to nothing at its rim. Start Select a mesh and pick Sculpt in the Workspace list. A group or component becomes a mesh first, as in the Mesh workspace. Brushes work on the mesh's vertices, so a mesh with more of them takes finer shapes: subdivide it first (the Mesh workspace's Subdivide, or a Subdivision surface modifier you Apply), or turn Detail on. Brushes Brush Key What it does Draw D Raises the surface under the brush Clay C Builds up flat layers, filling dips first Smooth S Evens out bumps; hold Shift to smooth with any brush Grab G Pulls the surface along with the pointer Inflate I Swells the surface out along its own direction Crease Shift+C Cuts a sharp groove Pinch P Draws the surface towards the brush's middle Flatten T Presses the surface towards its average plane Mask M Paints a mask that protects the surface from the other brushes Hold Ctrl while stroking to work the other way (lower instead of raise, a ridge instead of a groove, remove the mask), or turn Take away on. Size and strength The brush's size is in pixels on the screen, so it feels the same however far you zoom: [ and ] make it smaller and bigger, Shift+[ and Shift+] change its strength. The panel shows both. With a pen on a tablet, pressing harder makes the brush bigger and stronger; the panel turns either off. Mirror, mask and detail Mirror X (X) sculpts both sides across red at once, for faces and figures. The mask shows darker while you sculpt. Clear mask (Alt+M) takes it off, Invert mask (Ctrl+I) masks what was free and frees what was masked. The mask is kept with the mesh. Detail adds triangles where you sculpt: edges longer than the size set in the panel (pixels on the screen) are split under the brush. It needs a mesh of triangles, and asks to make one first. Each stroke is one undo step. Detail only adds triangles; it does not take away ones that became tiny. A Remesh (the Mesh workspace) evens the whole mesh out again. See also Edit a mesh, Meshes and Modifiers on a mesh.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"groups-components","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Groups and components","summary":"Keep objects from sticking together, edit one copy to change them all, and use the Outliner and Components panels.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["group","component","make group","make component","explode","make unique","outliner","instances","copies","block","nested","edit group","place"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-group-or-a-component","text":"Make a group or a component","level":2},{"id":"edit-inside-a-group-or-component","text":"Edit inside a group or component","level":2},{"id":"place-more-copies-of-a-component","text":"Place more copies of a component","level":2},{"id":"rearrange-in-the-outliner","text":"Rearrange in the Outliner","level":2},{"id":"make-one-copy-different","text":"Make one copy different","level":2},{"id":"turn-a-group-into-a-component","text":"Turn a group into a component","level":2},{"id":"explode","text":"Explode","level":2},{"id":"solid-and-volume","text":"Solid and volume","level":2},{"id":"when-the-last-copy-goes","text":"When the last copy goes","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELoose geometry in ModelCraft sticks together: lines that touch split each other, and faces that share an edge move together. That is what makes drawing on faces work, but it also means a box drawn against another box merges with it. \u003Cstrong\u003EGroups\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponents\u003C/strong\u003E keep things apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Egroup\u003C/strong\u003E wraps some geometry so it acts as one object. Nothing outside it can stick to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E is a group that can be placed many times. Every copy shares the same contents: edit one and they all change. Use components for anything repeated: windows, columns, desks, trees.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-group-or-a-component\"\u003EMake a group or a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the geometry. A triple-click on a face selects the whole connected object; a window drag selects everything in the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a group, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a component. You can also click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponent\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake group (Shift+G)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component (G)\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Grouped as Group#1.&quot; or &quot;Made component Component#1.&quot;, the new object is selected, and it appears in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo name it, type a name in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E field of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelecting a face brings its edges along. If nothing is selected, the status bar says &quot;Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also group groups: select several groups and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows the nesting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe key letters match SketchUp&#039;s defaults: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a component, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E a group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-inside-a-group-or-component\"\u003EEdit inside a group or component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click it (in the view or in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E), or select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rest of the model fades, the axes move to the group&#039;s own axes, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E reads &quot;Editing: Model › Group#1&quot;. The status bar says &quot;Editing Group#1. Esc or double-click outside closes it.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw, push/pull, move and erase as usual. Everything you do stays inside the group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose it: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (twice if something is selected), double-click empty space, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose group\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a component is open, every copy of it changes as you work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-more-copies-of-a-component\"\u003EPlace more copies of a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\"\u003EMove and copy\u003C/a\u003E). Each copy is another placement of the same component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E next to it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel, then click where it goes. Its origin, the lowest corner of the box around what you selected when you made it, lands where you click. The tool then returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E shows how many copies are placed (&quot;4×&quot;), and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows the count after the name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rearrange-in-the-outliner\"\u003ERearrange in the Outliner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a row in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E to change where it belongs. Nothing moves in the view: a group keeps its place in the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInto a group:\u003C/strong\u003E drop it on the middle of a group&#039;s row (the row gets a dashed outline). It goes inside, last.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn a different order:\u003C/strong\u003E drop it on the top or bottom edge of a row (a line shows there). It goes just before or after that row, inside the same group as that row, so this also puts it into a group at a chosen place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOut to the top level:\u003C/strong\u003E drop it on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E heading. It goes last.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach drop is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E during a drag drops nothing. A group can&#039;t go inside itself or inside a group it holds. Dragging is off while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind by name\u003C/span\u003E has text in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold a finger on a row for a moment, then drag it. A quick swipe scrolls the list instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-one-copy-different\"\u003EMake one copy different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the copy and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E (shown when the component is placed more than once). That copy gets its own definition, named like the original with a new number. Edit it without touching the others.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-a-group-into-a-component\"\u003ETurn a group into a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the group and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. It gets a new name, such as Component#2, even if you had named the group; type the name again in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"explode\"\u003EExplode\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a group or component and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E (in the toolbar or in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E). Its contents become loose geometry in the place it was, and stick to what they touch. Groups inside it stay groups.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solid-and-volume\"\u003ESolid and volume\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you select a group or component, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cstrong\u003ESolid: Yes\u003C/strong\u003E if its faces close up with no gaps (every edge is shared by exactly two faces) and there are no groups nested inside it. Then it also shows the \u003Cstrong\u003Evolume\u003C/strong\u003E. A box, a wall with a window cut through it, and a pulled cylinder are solids; a box with its top erased is not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-last-copy-goes\"\u003EWhen the last copy goes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EErasing the last copy of a component also removes it from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies of a \u003Cstrong\u003Egroup\u003C/strong\u003E made with Move share their contents in this build, like component copies do: editing one changes the others. To make one independent, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComponents do not cut openings in walls or glue to faces, and there is no library of ready-made components. You make your own, or import them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImported files arrive as a group. Collada files keep their own groups and components inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Loose geometry in ModelCraft sticks together: lines that touch split each other, and faces that share an edge move together. That is what makes drawing on faces work, but it also means a box drawn against another box merges with it. Groups and components keep things apart. A group wraps some geometry so it acts as one object. Nothing outside it can stick to it. A component is a group that can be placed many times. Every copy shares the same contents: edit one and they all change. Use components for anything repeated: windows, columns, desks, trees. Make a group or a component Select the geometry. A triple-click on a face selects the whole connected object; a window drag selects everything in the box. Press Shift+G for a group, or G for a component. You can also click Group or Component in the toolbar's Organize group, or Make group (Shift+G) or Make component (G) in Entity info. The status bar says \"Grouped as Group#1.\" or \"Made component Component#1.\", the new object is selected, and it appears in the Outliner. To name it, type a name in the Name field of Entity info and press Enter or click away. Selecting a face brings its edges along. If nothing is selected, the status bar says \"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\" You can also group groups: select several groups and press Shift+G. The Outliner shows the nesting. The key letters match SketchUp's defaults: G makes a component, Shift+G a group. Edit inside a group or component Double-click it (in the view or in the Outliner), or select it and click Edit in Entity info. The rest of the model fades, the axes move to the group's own axes, and the Outliner reads \"Editing: Model › Group#1\". The status bar says \"Editing Group#1. Esc or double-click outside closes it.\" Draw, push/pull, move and erase as usual. Everything you do stays inside the group. Close it: press Esc (twice if something is selected), double-click empty space, or click Close group in the Outliner. While a component is open, every copy of it changes as you work. Place more copies of a component Copy it with Move and Ctrl (see Move and copy). Each copy is another placement of the same component. Or click Place next to it in the Components panel, then click where it goes. Its origin, the lowest corner of the box around what you selected when you made it, lands where you click. The tool then returns to Select. Components shows how many copies are placed (\"4×\"), and the Outliner shows the count after the name. Rearrange in the Outliner Drag a row in the Outliner to change where it belongs. Nothing moves in the view: a group keeps its place in the model. Into a group: drop it on the middle of a group's row (the row gets a dashed outline). It goes inside, last. In a different order: drop it on the top or bottom edge of a row (a line shows there). It goes just before or after that row, inside the same group as that row, so this also puts it into a group at a chosen place. Out to the top level: drop it on the Outliner heading. It goes last. Each drop is one step: Ctrl+Z puts it back. Esc during a drag drops nothing. A group can't go inside itself or inside a group it holds. Dragging is off while Find by name has text in it. Hold a finger on a row for a moment, then drag it. A quick swipe scrolls the list instead. Make one copy different Select the copy and click Make unique in Entity info (shown when the component is placed more than once). That copy gets its own definition, named like the original with a new number. Edit it without touching the others. Turn a group into a component Select the group and click Make component in Entity info. It gets a new name, such as Component#2, even if you had named the group; type the name again in Name. Explode Select a group or component and click Explode (in the toolbar or in Entity info). Its contents become loose geometry in the place it was, and stick to what they touch. Groups inside it stay groups. Solid and volume When you select a group or component, Entity info shows Solid: Yes if its faces close up with no gaps (every edge is shared by exactly two faces) and there are no groups nested inside it. Then it also shows the volume. A box, a wall with a window cut through it, and a pulled cylinder are solids; a box with its top erased is not. When the last copy goes Erasing the last copy of a component also removes it from the Components panel. Ctrl+Z brings it back. Things to know Copies of a group made with Move share their contents in this build, like component copies do: editing one changes the others. To make one independent, click Make component, then Make unique. See Troubleshooting. Components do not cut openings in walls or glue to faces, and there is no library of ready-made components. You make your own, or import them. Imported files arrive as a group. Collada files keep their own groups and components inside it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"guides-measure","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Measure and place guides","summary":"Read lengths and areas, measure between two points with the tape, and lay out guide lines and points to draw against.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["measure","tape measure","protractor","angle","angled guide","guide","guideline","construction line","distance","dimension","length","area","offset","layout","guide point"],"headings":[{"id":"read-sizes-without-measuring","text":"Read sizes without measuring","level":2},{"id":"measure-between-two-points","text":"Measure between two points","level":2},{"id":"place-a-guide-line","text":"Place a guide line","level":2},{"id":"a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor","text":"A guide at an angle: Protractor","level":2},{"id":"draw-against-guides","text":"Draw against guides","level":2},{"id":"remove-guides","text":"Remove guides","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"read-sizes-without-measuring\"\u003ERead sizes without measuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect something and look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn edge shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Elength\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA face shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E and how many holes it has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESeveral faces show their \u003Cstrong\u003Etotal area\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA group or component shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Esize\u003C/strong\u003E (width × depth × height of the box around it) and, if it is a closed solid, its \u003Cstrong\u003Evolume\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows the size of the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas use the unit in the picker at the bottom right. A millimeter model shows areas of 0.01 m² and up in square meters (a 4 × 3 m room reads &quot;12 m²&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you draw, the value box shows the live length, size, radius or distance, so you rarely need to measure afterward.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change a size after drawing, select an edge or a group and retype its length or size in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. Or place a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Edimension\u003C/a\u003E and type a new length into it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-between-two-points\"\u003EMeasure between two points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point: a corner, a midpoint, a point on a face, or the ground. (Clicking an edge does something else; see below.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the second point. The tip and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E) show the distance. It snaps to points and to the red, green and blue directions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The status bar says &quot;Measured 4&#039; 6&quot;.&quot; and a \u003Cstrong\u003Eguide point\u003C/strong\u003E is left at the second point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo leave the guide point at an exact distance instead, type it after the first click and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: it goes in the direction you are pointing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-guide-line\"\u003EPlace a guide line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA guide line is an endless dashed line, parallel to an edge, at a distance you choose. Use guides to lay out a setback, a floor height, or where a window starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on an \u003Cstrong\u003Eedge\u003C/strong\u003E (not its end). The tip says &quot;On edge&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove off the edge, to the side the guide should go. A dashed line follows, parallel to the edge, and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E) shows the distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type the offset and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E1.5m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Guide line 1.5 m from the edge.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStarting on an existing guide line makes another guide parallel to it, so you can step a grid out from one guide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you type an offset without moving off the edge first, ModelCraft cannot tell which side you mean: &quot;Move off the edge first to show which side.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor\"\u003EA guide at an angle: Protractor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProtractor\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center: the point the guide passes through. On a face, the protractor lies on that face; elsewhere it lies flat. After this click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stands it square to red, green or blue instead (press again to undo).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point that sets 0°, for example along an edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the angle you want and click: it snaps to 15° steps. Or type the angle in degrees, such as \u003Ccode\u003E22.5\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the guide goes to the side the pointer is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe guide line passes through the center at that angle. Draw along it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E for a roof pitch, a chamfer or a splayed wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-against-guides\"\u003EDraw against guides\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrawing tools snap to guide lines (&quot;On guide&quot;) and guide points (&quot;Guide point&quot;), and to where a guide line crosses an axis direction. Start a rectangle where two guides cross to place it exactly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-guides\"\u003ERemove guides\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne guide: click it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click it with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll guides: with nothing selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all guides\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuides are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file. They are not exported to other formats.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Read sizes without measuring Select something and look at Entity info: An edge shows its length. A face shows its area and how many holes it has. Several faces show their total area. A group or component shows its size (width × depth × height of the box around it) and, if it is a closed solid, its volume. With nothing selected, Entity info shows the size of the whole model. Lengths and areas use the unit in the picker at the bottom right. A millimeter model shows areas of 0.01 m² and up in square meters (a 4 × 3 m room reads \"12 m²\"). While you draw, the value box shows the live length, size, radius or distance, so you rarely need to measure afterward. To change a size after drawing, select an edge or a group and retype its length or size in Entity info. Or place a dimension and type a new length into it. Measure between two points Press T for the Tape measure. Click a point: a corner, a midpoint, a point on a face, or the ground. (Clicking an edge does something else; see below.) Move to the second point. The tip and the value box (Length) show the distance. It snaps to points and to the red, green and blue directions. Click. The status bar says \"Measured 4' 6\".\" and a guide point is left at the second point. To leave the guide point at an exact distance instead, type it after the first click and press Enter: it goes in the direction you are pointing. Place a guide line A guide line is an endless dashed line, parallel to an edge, at a distance you choose. Use guides to lay out a setback, a floor height, or where a window starts. Press T. Click on an edge (not its end). The tip says \"On edge\". Move off the edge, to the side the guide should go. A dashed line follows, parallel to the edge, and the value box (Offset) shows the distance. Click, or type the offset and press Enter: 1.5m, 5'. The status bar says \"Guide line 1.5 m from the edge.\" Starting on an existing guide line makes another guide parallel to it, so you can step a grid out from one guide. If you type an offset without moving off the edge first, ModelCraft cannot tell which side you mean: \"Move off the edge first to show which side.\" A guide at an angle: Protractor Click Protractor in the Modify group. Click the center: the point the guide passes through. On a face, the protractor lies on that face; elsewhere it lies flat. After this click, →, ← or ↑ stands it square to red, green or blue instead (press again to undo). Click a point that sets 0°, for example along an edge. Move to the angle you want and click: it snaps to 15° steps. Or type the angle in degrees, such as 22.5, and press Enter; the guide goes to the side the pointer is on. The guide line passes through the center at that angle. Draw along it with Line for a roof pitch, a chamfer or a splayed wall. Draw against guides Drawing tools snap to guide lines (\"On guide\") and guide points (\"Guide point\"), and to where a guide line crosses an axis direction. Start a rectangle where two guides cross to place it exactly. Remove guides One guide: click it with Select and press Delete, or click it with the Eraser. All guides: with nothing selected, click Delete all guides in Entity info. Guides are saved in the .modelcraft file. They are not exported to other formats.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"dimensions","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Dimension and resize what you drew","summary":"Place dimensions between corners, on arcs and circles, or between two edges; type a new length to resize the model. Retype an edge's length or a group's width, depth and height in Entity info.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["dimension","dimensions","resize","change size","edit size","length","width","depth","height","stretch","driving dimension","annotate","measure","after the fact","radius","diameter","angle","angular","arc","circle","R","Ø"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-dimension","text":"Place a dimension","level":2},{"id":"radius-diameter-and-angle","text":"Radius, diameter and angle","level":2},{"id":"resize-the-model-with-a-dimension","text":"Resize the model with a dimension","level":2},{"id":"what-moves","text":"What moves","level":3},{"id":"when-a-resize-is-refused","text":"When a resize is refused","level":3},{"id":"retype-a-size-in-entity-info","text":"Retype a size in Entity info","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-follow-the-model","text":"Dimensions follow the model","level":2},{"id":"remove-dimensions","text":"Remove dimensions","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESizes in ModelCraft are not fixed once something is drawn. A dimension shows a length on the model, and typing a new length into it resizes the model to match. You can also retype an edge&#039;s length, or a group&#039;s width, depth and height, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-dimension\"\u003EPlace a dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a corner to measure from. It snaps to corners, midpoints and edges, as drawing does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr click once on an \u003Cstrong\u003Eedge\u003C/strong\u003E (the tip says &quot;On edge&quot;) to dimension that whole edge in one click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer to where the dimension line should go, and click. You can also type the offset, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0.5m\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dimension line stays level for a level edge, unless you are looking at it from the side. For an upright edge it stays in the upright plane that faces you best.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Dimension 4 m.&quot; Dimensions show their length in the model&#039;s unit and update when you change the unit in the picker.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"radius-diameter-and-angle\"\u003ERadius, diameter and angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E tool dimensions round things and angles:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn arc:\u003C/strong\u003E click the arc. The tip says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E. Move the pointer to where the text should go and click: the dimension reads \u003Cstrong\u003ER 1.5 m\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA circle:\u003C/strong\u003E click the circle. You get a \u003Cstrong\u003Ediameter\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cstrong\u003EØ 3 m\u003C/strong\u003E) with a line across the circle through its centre. Polygons work the same way, with their corners on the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe angle between two edges:\u003C/strong\u003E click one edge, then click a second edge that meets it at a corner (while you are over it, the tip says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle between the edges\u003C/span\u003E). Then move into the angle you want, and click. Inside the corner you get the angle between the edges; move to one side and you get the angle beside it (for example 150° instead of 30°). How far from the corner you click sets where the arc goes; or type that distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo switch a radius to a diameter or back, select the dimension and choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow as\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResizing with them:\u003C/strong\u003E type a new radius or diameter right after placing one, or select it later and type. The arc or circle keeps its centre and changes size; a face it bounds (a disc, the end of a slot) changes with it. Two things only measure:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn angle dimension\u003C/strong\u003E never changes the model. To change an angle, rotate the edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe circle at the end of a cylinder\u003C/strong\u003E (a circle you pulled with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E) cannot be resized from its dimension, because its sides would have to change too. Scale the cylinder instead, about its axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"resize-the-model-with-a-dimension\"\u003EResize the model with a dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after placing a dimension, type a new length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E6m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3500\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model. The model changes to that size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELater, at any time:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, click the dimension&#039;s text or line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension\u003C/strong\u003E and shows its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You can type straight into the value box, or into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E box in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-moves\"\u003EWhat moves\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe second corner you clicked moves, and so does everything beyond it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoth corners on one piece\u003C/strong\u003E (a box&#039;s width, a wall&#039;s height): the side at the moving corner slides out or in, and faces joined to it stretch. A box stays a closed solid, and anything sitting wholly on that side, such as a window component, moves with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe corners on two separate pieces\u003C/strong\u003E (the gap between two boxes): the moving corner&#039;s piece moves whole, and the gap changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe moving corner inside a group or component\u003C/strong\u003E that the other corner is not in: the whole group moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoth corners inside the same group or component\u003C/strong\u003E: it is stretched inside. A component changes everywhere it is placed, as editing it always does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change which end moves, select the dimension and choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMoves\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESecond end\u003C/span\u003E (the default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end you clicked second moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst end\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end you clicked first moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoth ends (centered)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach end moves half the change, so the middle stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"when-a-resize-is-refused\"\u003EWhen a resize is refused\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft changes nothing and says why in the status bar:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a face would bend out of its plane&quot;: the dimension runs at a slant across the shape. Dimension along an edge, or along red, green or blue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a face would shrink to nothing or turn inside out&quot;: the new length is too short for the shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;the end that moves is not on a corner of the model&quot;: that end was placed in empty space or on the middle of an edge. Choose the other end in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMoves\u003C/span\u003E, or place the dimension on two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a length must be above zero&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery resize is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"retype-a-size-in-entity-info\"\u003ERetype a size in Entity info\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou don&#039;t always need a dimension:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge:\u003C/strong\u003E select it. Its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E is a box you can type into. Typing a length into the value box works too. The end further along red, then green, then blue moves, and what is joined to it stretches. For example, retyping the front bottom edge of a box makes the box wider to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA group or component:\u003C/strong\u003E select it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (red)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth (green)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight (blue)\u003C/span\u003E are boxes. Its far side (right, back, top) moves. These are the group&#039;s own axes, before any rotation you gave it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to apply, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put the old value back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-follow-the-model\"\u003EDimensions follow the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension is attached to the two corners it was placed on (an angle: to its corner and the far ends of its two edges; a radius or diameter: to its arc or circle). Move, push/pull or resize the geometry, and the dimension follows and shows the new length. It stays attached when you make the geometry into a group or component.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf an end was placed somewhere that isn&#039;t a corner (on a face, or in empty space), or its corner is erased, that end stays where it was. The dimension still measures, but it can only resize from its other end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-dimensions\"\u003ERemove dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne dimension: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click it with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll dimensions: with nothing selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all dimensions\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimensions are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file. They are not exported to other formats, which carry only the 3D model. A file with dimensions still opens in an older ModelCraft, without them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Sizes in ModelCraft are not fixed once something is drawn. A dimension shows a length on the model, and typing a new length into it resizes the model to match. You can also retype an edge's length, or a group's width, depth and height, in Entity info. Place a dimension Press D, or click Dimension in the toolbar. Click a corner to measure from. It snaps to corners, midpoints and edges, as drawing does. Click the second corner. Or click once on an edge (the tip says \"On edge\") to dimension that whole edge in one click. Move the pointer to where the dimension line should go, and click. You can also type the offset, such as 0.5m, and press Enter. The dimension line stays level for a level edge, unless you are looking at it from the side. For an upright edge it stays in the upright plane that faces you best. The status bar says \"Dimension 4 m.\" Dimensions show their length in the model's unit and update when you change the unit in the picker. Radius, diameter and angle The same Dimension tool dimensions round things and angles: An arc: click the arc. The tip says Radius. Move the pointer to where the text should go and click: the dimension reads R 1.5 m. A circle: click the circle. You get a diameter (Ø 3 m) with a line across the circle through its centre. Polygons work the same way, with their corners on the circle. The angle between two edges: click one edge, then click a second edge that meets it at a corner (while you are over it, the tip says Angle between the edges). Then move into the angle you want, and click. Inside the corner you get the angle between the edges; move to one side and you get the angle beside it (for example 150° instead of 30°). How far from the corner you click sets where the arc goes; or type that distance. To switch a radius to a diameter or back, select the dimension and choose in Show as in Entity info. Resizing with them: type a new radius or diameter right after placing one, or select it later and type. The arc or circle keeps its centre and changes size; a face it bounds (a disc, the end of a slot) changes with it. Two things only measure: An angle dimension never changes the model. To change an angle, rotate the edges with Rotate. The circle at the end of a cylinder (a circle you pulled with Push/pull) cannot be resized from its dimension, because its sides would have to change too. Scale the cylinder instead, about its axis. Resize the model with a dimension Right after placing a dimension, type a new length and press Enter: 6m, 12', 3500 in a millimeter model. The model changes to that size. Later, at any time: With Select, click the dimension's text or line. Entity info says Dimension and shows its Length. Type the new length and press Enter. You can type straight into the value box, or into the Length box in Entity info. What moves The second corner you clicked moves, and so does everything beyond it: Both corners on one piece (a box's width, a wall's height): the side at the moving corner slides out or in, and faces joined to it stretch. A box stays a closed solid, and anything sitting wholly on that side, such as a window component, moves with it. The corners on two separate pieces (the gap between two boxes): the moving corner's piece moves whole, and the gap changes. The moving corner inside a group or component that the other corner is not in: the whole group moves. Both corners inside the same group or component: it is stretched inside. A component changes everywhere it is placed, as editing it always does. To change which end moves, select the dimension and choose in Moves in Entity info: Choice What happens Second end (the default) The end you clicked second moves First end The end you clicked first moves Both ends (centered) Each end moves half the change, so the middle stays put When a resize is refused ModelCraft changes nothing and says why in the status bar: \"a face would bend out of its plane\": the dimension runs at a slant across the shape. Dimension along an edge, or along red, green or blue. \"a face would shrink to nothing or turn inside out\": the new length is too short for the shape. \"the end that moves is not on a corner of the model\": that end was placed in empty space or on the middle of an edge. Choose the other end in Moves, or place the dimension on two corners. \"a length must be above zero\". Every resize is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Retype a size in Entity info You don't always need a dimension: An edge: select it. Its Length in Entity info is a box you can type into. Typing a length into the value box works too. The end further along red, then green, then blue moves, and what is joined to it stretches. For example, retyping the front bottom edge of a box makes the box wider to the right. A group or component: select it. Width (red), Depth (green) and Height (blue) are boxes. Its far side (right, back, top) moves. These are the group's own axes, before any rotation you gave it. Press Enter to apply, or Esc to put the old value back. Dimensions follow the model A dimension is attached to the two corners it was placed on (an angle: to its corner and the far ends of its two edges; a radius or diameter: to its arc or circle). Move, push/pull or resize the geometry, and the dimension follows and shows the new length. It stays attached when you make the geometry into a group or component. If an end was placed somewhere that isn't a corner (on a face, or in empty space), or its corner is erased, that end stays where it was. The dimension still measures, but it can only resize from its other end. Remove dimensions One dimension: select it and press Delete, or click it with the Eraser. All dimensions: with nothing selected, click Delete all dimensions in Entity info. Dimensions are saved in the .modelcraft file. They are not exported to other formats, which carry only the 3D model. A file with dimensions still opens in an older ModelCraft, without them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"erase","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Erase and clean up","summary":"Erase edges, groups and guides with the eraser or the Delete key, heal split faces, and fix faces that face the wrong way.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["erase","eraser","delete","remove","heal","merge faces","clean up","stray lines","reverse face","back face","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"erase-with-the-eraser","text":"Erase with the eraser","level":2},{"id":"what-happens-to-the-faces","text":"What happens to the faces","level":2},{"id":"erase-with-the-delete-key","text":"Erase with the Delete key","level":2},{"id":"clean-up-stray-lines-on-a-flat-face","text":"Clean up stray lines on a flat face","level":2},{"id":"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way","text":"A face facing the wrong way","level":2},{"id":"undo-a-mistake","text":"Undo a mistake","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-with-the-eraser\"\u003EErase with the eraser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an edge, a group or component, or a guide. It is erased.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo erase several, press and drag across them. Everything you pass over highlights, and all of it goes when you release.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe eraser does not pick faces: a face goes when you erase the edges it needs. Faces are what the eraser looks through to find edges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-happens-to-the-faces\"\u003EWhat happens to the faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge between two faces in the same plane\u003C/strong\u003E (a line you drew across a floor, for example) disappears and the two faces \u003Cstrong\u003Eheal\u003C/strong\u003E into one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge that a face needs to stay closed\u003C/strong\u003E (the edge of a box) takes that face with it. Erase one top edge of a box and the top and one side go, leaving an open box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoose ends\u003C/strong\u003E (points no edge uses any more) are cleaned up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EErasing the last copy of a component also removes the component from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-with-the-delete-key\"\u003EErase with the Delete key\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to remove with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis removes faces too. A face deleted this way leaves its edges behind, so you can delete the top of a box and keep its outline, then draw something else there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clean-up-stray-lines-on-a-flat-face\"\u003EClean up stray lines on a flat face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a wall or floor shows lines you don&#039;t want (left over from drawing), erase them with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E. Lines lying in the face heal away; the face stays.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EA face facing the wrong way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face seen from behind is blue-grey. If a face shows blue-grey on the outside of your model:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFace direction matters most when you export: renderers, game engines and 3D printers may hide or misread faces that point inward.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-a-mistake\"\u003EUndo a mistake\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes one step at a time, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) redoes. A drag with the eraser is one step, however many things it took. The status bar names what was undone: &quot;Undid erase.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Erase with the eraser Press E for the Eraser. Click an edge, a group or component, or a guide. It is erased. To erase several, press and drag across them. Everything you pass over highlights, and all of it goes when you release. The eraser does not pick faces: a face goes when you erase the edges it needs. Faces are what the eraser looks through to find edges. What happens to the faces An edge between two faces in the same plane (a line you drew across a floor, for example) disappears and the two faces heal into one. An edge that a face needs to stay closed (the edge of a box) takes that face with it. Erase one top edge of a box and the top and one side go, leaving an open box. Loose ends (points no edge uses any more) are cleaned up. Erasing the last copy of a component also removes the component from the Components panel. Erase with the Delete key Select what you want to remove with Select. Press Delete or Backspace. This removes faces too. A face deleted this way leaves its edges behind, so you can delete the top of a box and keep its outline, then draw something else there. Clean up stray lines on a flat face If a wall or floor shows lines you don't want (left over from drawing), erase them with the Eraser. Lines lying in the face heal away; the face stays. A face facing the wrong way A face seen from behind is blue-grey. If a face shows blue-grey on the outside of your model: Select the face. Click Reverse face in Entity info. Face direction matters most when you export: renderers, game engines and 3D printers may hide or misread faces that point inward. Undo a mistake Ctrl+Z undoes one step at a time, Ctrl+Y (or Ctrl+Shift+Z) redoes. A drag with the eraser is one step, however many things it took. The status bar names what was undone: \"Undid erase.\"","lang":"en"},{"slug":"site-plan","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Model on a site plan from a DWG or DXF","summary":"Bring a CAD site or floor plan in as lines on the ground, answer the units question, and trace it into faces and masses.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["site plan","dwg","dxf","cad","import","sketch","sketch photo","hand sketch","napkin sketch","photo","paper","pen colours","dynamic blocks","visibility states","text","hatch","spline","ellipse","3d face","trace","floor plan","lot","property line","units","underlay","context"],"headings":[{"id":"import-the-drawing","text":"Import the drawing","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-in","text":"What comes in","level":2},{"id":"trace-it-into-faces","text":"Trace it into faces","level":2},{"id":"troubles","text":"Troubles","level":2},{"id":"from-a-photo-of-a-hand-sketch","text":"From a photo of a hand sketch","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft can bring in the lines of a 2D CAD drawing, a site plan or a floor plan, and lay them on the ground so you can model on top of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-the-drawing\"\u003EImport the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or drop the file on the 3D view).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA large DWG takes a moment: the status bar says &quot;Reading plan.dwg…&quot; until it is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the drawing does not say what its units are, a dialog titled &quot;Drawing units&quot; asks: &quot;What is one drawing unit?&quot; Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimeters\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeters\u003C/span\u003E. Most US site plans are drawn in feet, architectural plans in inches, and metric plans in millimeters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing arrives as a group of edges on the ground, selected, and the view zooms to it. The status bar says how many edges came in, for example &quot;Imported site.dwg: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group. Explode it to draw on it directly.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-comes-in\"\u003EWhat comes in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, polylines (including their arcs), circles and arcs, and blocks with everything in them, from \u003Cstrong\u003Emodel space\u003C/strong\u003E only. Curves become short straight pieces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses and splines land flat on the ground, as edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHatches\u003C/strong\u003E become faces on the ground, with their islands as holes, so you can push/pull a hatched area straight away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D faces\u003C/strong\u003E and solid fills become faces; 3D faces keep their heights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E (single-line text, multiline text and attribute values) is drawn as edges in a simple single-stroke lettering, at the text&#039;s own height, angle and alignment. Its font is not the drawing&#039;s, so the words may run a little longer or shorter. Characters the lettering has no shape for are drawn as small boxes and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimensions\u003C/strong\u003E are drawn as the drawing shows them (their lines, arrowheads and text, as edges and faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDynamic blocks\u003C/strong\u003E come in as the drawing shows them: each placed block in the visibility state it was saved with, and only that state&#039;s lines. The status bar lists them with the states shown and the other states each one has, for example &quot;Receptacle shown as Duplex ×38, GFI ×7 (states: Duplex, GFI)&quot;. ModelCraft does not switch states.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D solids, regions and bodies\u003C/strong\u003E (the drawing&#039;s ACIS data) come in as their own groups: a closed solid as a solid, a region or open body as faces. Flat faces come in whole; curved faces (cylinders, cones, spheres, tori, simple spline surfaces) as small triangles with soft edges between them, so only the solid&#039;s own edges show. A face on another kind of surface is left out and listed: &quot;Not drawn yet (3D solids): …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot imported: points, tables, multileaders, images and other object types. The status bar lists them by type, such as &quot;Not imported: 12 POINT.&quot; Anything on a layout (paper space) is left out too, without being listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers are not kept: all lines come in alike.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the drawing is far from its origin (state plane coordinates, for instance), it still imports, but it may land far from ModelCraft&#039;s origin. Drawings reaching more than 10,000 km from their origin are refused: move the drawing nearer to 0,0 in the CAD program first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trace-it-into-faces\"\u003ETrace it into faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe imported lines rarely close neatly into faces by themselves. Two ways to work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep the plan as an underlay (recommended)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave the plan as its own group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw your building on top with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, snapping to the plan&#039;s corners (&quot;Endpoint&quot;) and edges (&quot;On edge&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull your faces up. The plan stays untouched underneath, and you can erase the whole group later with one click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnapping works across groups: you can snap to the plan&#039;s corners without opening it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDraw on the plan&#039;s own lines\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the plan group and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E. Its lines become loose edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere a closed outline should be a face, draw one line over any of its edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, from corner to corner. When the outline is closed and flat, the face usually fills in. If it doesn&#039;t, look for a gap or an overlap in the outline and draw over it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works best on a clean plan. On a busy survey, tracing on top is faster.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubles\"\u003ETroubles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.&quot; Save the DXF again from the CAD program as ASCII (text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;…has no lines, arcs or circles in model space to bring in.&quot; The drawing is probably all on layouts, or all text and hatches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…&quot; The brackets say why, for example &quot;DWG version AC1009 is not supported&quot;. Save it as a DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan is the wrong size: you picked the wrong unit. Undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and import again, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003Escale\u003C/a\u003E the group by the right factor (12 between feet and inches, 25.4 between inches and millimeters).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo turn contour lines into ground you can sculpt, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/terrain\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/terrain\"\u003ETerrain\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-a-photo-of-a-hand-sketch\"\u003EFrom a photo of a hand sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFile › Import a sketch photo…\u003C/strong\u003E takes a photo of a sketch on paper (JPEG, PNG or TIFF, straight from a phone is fine) and makes the same kind of edges on the ground. Say which paper it is (or let it find the sheet, taken as A4) and the sketch&#039;s scale, 1 : 100 for example. The paper&#039;s edge is found and the photo straightened, the ink is traced into lines and arcs, and each pen colour (black, red, blue, green) becomes a group of its own with a tag (\u003Cstrong\u003ESketch black\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch red\u003C/strong\u003E, …), all inside one group named after the photo. It is one undo step, and everything happens on this computer: nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen no paper edge can be found the whole photo is taken as the sheet and the status line says so: measure something you know and scale the group if it is off. Trace the lines into faces as above.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft can bring in the lines of a 2D CAD drawing, a site plan or a floor plan, and lay them on the ground so you can model on top of them. Import the drawing Click Import in the File group (or drop the file on the 3D view). Pick a .dwg or .dxf file. A large DWG takes a moment: the status bar says \"Reading plan.dwg…\" until it is in. If the drawing does not say what its units are, a dialog titled \"Drawing units\" asks: \"What is one drawing unit?\" Choose Inches, Feet, Millimeters or Meters. Most US site plans are drawn in feet, architectural plans in inches, and metric plans in millimeters. The drawing arrives as a group of edges on the ground, selected, and the view zooms to it. The status bar says how many edges came in, for example \"Imported site.dwg: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group. Explode it to draw on it directly.\" What comes in Lines, polylines (including their arcs), circles and arcs, and blocks with everything in them, from model space only. Curves become short straight pieces. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses and splines land flat on the ground, as edges. Hatches become faces on the ground, with their islands as holes, so you can push/pull a hatched area straight away. 3D faces and solid fills become faces; 3D faces keep their heights. Text (single-line text, multiline text and attribute values) is drawn as edges in a simple single-stroke lettering, at the text's own height, angle and alignment. Its font is not the drawing's, so the words may run a little longer or shorter. Characters the lettering has no shape for are drawn as small boxes and counted. Dimensions are drawn as the drawing shows them (their lines, arrowheads and text, as edges and faces). Dynamic blocks come in as the drawing shows them: each placed block in the visibility state it was saved with, and only that state's lines. The status bar lists them with the states shown and the other states each one has, for example \"Receptacle shown as Duplex ×38, GFI ×7 (states: Duplex, GFI)\". ModelCraft does not switch states. 3D solids, regions and bodies (the drawing's ACIS data) come in as their own groups: a closed solid as a solid, a region or open body as faces. Flat faces come in whole; curved faces (cylinders, cones, spheres, tori, simple spline surfaces) as small triangles with soft edges between them, so only the solid's own edges show. A face on another kind of surface is left out and listed: \"Not drawn yet (3D solids): …\". Not imported: points, tables, multileaders, images and other object types. The status bar lists them by type, such as \"Not imported: 12 POINT.\" Anything on a layout (paper space) is left out too, without being listed. Layers are not kept: all lines come in alike. If the drawing is far from its origin (state plane coordinates, for instance), it still imports, but it may land far from ModelCraft's origin. Drawings reaching more than 10,000 km from their origin are refused: move the drawing nearer to 0,0 in the CAD program first. Trace it into faces The imported lines rarely close neatly into faces by themselves. Two ways to work: Keep the plan as an underlay (recommended) Leave the plan as its own group. Draw your building on top with Rectangle and Line, snapping to the plan's corners (\"Endpoint\") and edges (\"On edge\"). Push/pull your faces up. The plan stays untouched underneath, and you can erase the whole group later with one click. Snapping works across groups: you can snap to the plan's corners without opening it. Draw on the plan's own lines Select the plan group and click Explode. Its lines become loose edges. Where a closed outline should be a face, draw one line over any of its edges with Line, from corner to corner. When the outline is closed and flat, the face usually fills in. If it doesn't, look for a gap or an overlap in the outline and draw over it. Push/pull it. This works best on a clean plan. On a busy survey, tracing on top is faster. Troubles \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.\" Save the DXF again from the CAD program as ASCII (text). \"…has no lines, arcs or circles in model space to bring in.\" The drawing is probably all on layouts, or all text and hatches. \"This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…\" The brackets say why, for example \"DWG version AC1009 is not supported\". Save it as a DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program. The plan is the wrong size: you picked the wrong unit. Undo (Ctrl+Z) and import again, or scale the group by the right factor (12 between feet and inches, 25.4 between inches and millimeters). To turn contour lines into ground you can sculpt, see Terrain. From a photo of a hand sketch File › Import a sketch photo… takes a photo of a sketch on paper (JPEG, PNG or TIFF, straight from a phone is fine) and makes the same kind of edges on the ground. Say which paper it is (or let it find the sheet, taken as A4) and the sketch's scale, 1 : 100 for example. The paper's edge is found and the photo straightened, the ink is traced into lines and arcs, and each pen colour (black, red, blue, green) becomes a group of its own with a tag (Sketch black, Sketch red, …), all inside one group named after the photo. It is one undo step, and everything happens on this computer: nothing is sent anywhere. When no paper edge can be found the whole photo is taken as the sheet and the status line says so: measure something you know and scale the group if it is off. Trace the lines into faces as above.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"import-sketchup-files","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Bring in an SKP model","summary":"Import a .skp file with its groups, components, colors, textures, tags and soft edges, see what came in and what did not, and carry on modeling.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["skp","import","open skp","components","groups","tags","layers","materials","colors","textures","hidden","soft edges","3d warehouse","migrate","switching"],"headings":[{"id":"import-the-file","text":"Import the file","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-in","text":"What comes in","level":2},{"id":"what-does-not-come-in","text":"What does not come in","level":2},{"id":"problems","text":"Problems","level":2},{"id":"take-the-model-back","text":"Take the model back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft reads SketchUp files (\u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E) with its own reader, in the page. Nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Files from SketchUp 3 up to 2026 have been tested.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-the-file\"\u003EImport the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or drop the \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E file on the 3D view).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Reading house.skp…&quot; while it reads, then what came in, for example &quot;Imported house.skp (SketchUp 25.0.575): 1240 faces, 6 components, in a group.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole model arrives as one group named after the file, selected, and the view zooms to it. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all away again, tags included.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExplode the import group (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E) when you want its contents loose in your model rather than in one group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-comes-in\"\u003EWhat comes in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaces\u003C/strong\u003E with their holes, and \u003Cstrong\u003Eedges\u003C/strong\u003E that are not part of a face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroups\u003C/strong\u003E come in as groups, and \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponents\u003C/strong\u003E as components: every copy of a component is a placement of one definition, so editing one copy changes them all, as in SketchUp. Names come with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors.\u003C/strong\u003E A face&#039;s own material gives it its color. A material painted on a group or component becomes that placement&#039;s paint, so faces inside it with no color of their own take it, as in SketchUp. A component painted red in one place and blue in another stays one component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETextures.\u003C/strong\u003E A face painted with a textured material comes in with its texture, laid on the face as in the file, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShaded\u003C/span\u003E style shows it. The image is kept in the ModelCraft file when you save, and a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Erendered picture\u003C/a\u003E shows it too. A textured material painted on a whole group or component gives that copy the material&#039;s color only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETags.\u003C/strong\u003E A group or component on a SketchUp tag gets a ModelCraft tag of the same name, listed in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E, shown or hidden as it was in SketchUp. If your model already has a tag with that name, that tag is used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden things come in hidden:\u003C/strong\u003E faces, edges, groups and components, and faces and edges on a hidden tag. You see what SketchUp showed, and nothing is lost: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E shows them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and unhide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESoft and smooth edges\u003C/strong\u003E stay soft, so curved surfaces still look smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESizes\u003C/strong\u003E come out right in any unit: SketchUp files are in inches inside, and ModelCraft converts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-does-not-come-in\"\u003EWhat does not come in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says what was left out, with counts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESome textures\u003C/strong\u003E keep only their material&#039;s color: a texture missing from the file, an image in a format browsers can&#039;t show (such as a Photoshop file), or a texture without a size. Faces with a projected texture come in with the texture placed approximately.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETags on loose faces and edges.\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft tags groups and components only, so a face or edge with its own tag comes in untagged, and the status bar counts them. Make such faces into a group (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and tag the group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImages\u003C/strong\u003E placed in the model, text, dimensions, guides, section planes, scenes and styles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"problems\"\u003EProblems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is not a SketchUp file.&quot; or &quot;This SketchUp file is damaged: …&quot;: the file is cut off or is something else with a \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E name. Save it again from SketchUp, or ask for a fresh copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file from a SketchUp version newer than ModelCraft knows is refused with the version it found.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA model that is all images, text or dimensions says it has nothing to show.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-the-model-back\"\u003ETake the model back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft cannot save \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E files. Export Collada (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E) and import that in SketchUp: groups, components and colors go across; textures go as their material&#039;s color. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\"\u003EExport your model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft reads SketchUp files (.skp) with its own reader, in the page. Nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Files from SketchUp 3 up to 2026 have been tested. Import the file Click Import in the File group (or drop the .skp file on the 3D view). Pick the file. The status bar says \"Reading house.skp…\" while it reads, then what came in, for example \"Imported house.skp (SketchUp 25.0.575): 1240 faces, 6 components, in a group.\" The whole model arrives as one group named after the file, selected, and the view zooms to it. One Ctrl+Z takes it all away again, tags included. Explode the import group (Entity info › Explode) when you want its contents loose in your model rather than in one group. What comes in Faces with their holes, and edges that are not part of a face. Groups come in as groups, and components as components: every copy of a component is a placement of one definition, so editing one copy changes them all, as in SketchUp. Names come with them. Colors. A face's own material gives it its color. A material painted on a group or component becomes that placement's paint, so faces inside it with no color of their own take it, as in SketchUp. A component painted red in one place and blue in another stays one component. Textures. A face painted with a textured material comes in with its texture, laid on the face as in the file, and the Shaded style shows it. The image is kept in the ModelCraft file when you save, and a rendered picture shows it too. A textured material painted on a whole group or component gives that copy the material's color only. Tags. A group or component on a SketchUp tag gets a ModelCraft tag of the same name, listed in Tags, shown or hidden as it was in SketchUp. If your model already has a tag with that name, that tag is used. Hidden things come in hidden: faces, edges, groups and components, and faces and edges on a hidden tag. You see what SketchUp showed, and nothing is lost: Edit › More › Unhide all shows them. See Hide, isolate and unhide. Soft and smooth edges stay soft, so curved surfaces still look smooth. Sizes come out right in any unit: SketchUp files are in inches inside, and ModelCraft converts them. What does not come in The status bar says what was left out, with counts. Some textures keep only their material's color: a texture missing from the file, an image in a format browsers can't show (such as a Photoshop file), or a texture without a size. Faces with a projected texture come in with the texture placed approximately. Tags on loose faces and edges. ModelCraft tags groups and components only, so a face or edge with its own tag comes in untagged, and the status bar counts them. Make such faces into a group (Shift+G) and tag the group. Images placed in the model, text, dimensions, guides, section planes, scenes and styles. Problems \"This is not a SketchUp file.\" or \"This SketchUp file is damaged: …\": the file is cut off or is something else with a .skp name. Save it again from SketchUp, or ask for a fresh copy. A file from a SketchUp version newer than ModelCraft knows is refused with the version it found. A model that is all images, text or dimensions says it has nothing to show. Take the model back ModelCraft cannot save .skp files. Export Collada (Export › .dae) and import that in SketchUp: groups, components and colors go across; textures go as their material's color. See Export your model.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"site-from-civilcraft","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Take a site from CivilCraft","summary":"Open the address-to-site file CivilCraft makes and add the buildings and the parcel round it as masses and a site line, only the ones you tick.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["site","civilcraft","address","parcel","neighbouring buildings","masses","context","ccsite","open data","openstreetmap"],"headings":[{"id":"bring-the-file-in","text":"Bring the file in","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft can look up an address and save what the open data sources know round it: neighbouring buildings, the parcel, and flood, zoning and soil outlines. ModelCraft takes that file as suggestions. Nothing is added until you say so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-the-file-in\"\u003EBring the file in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft, send the site to ModelCraft, or save the file (it ends in \u003Ccode\u003E.ccsite.json\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn ModelCraft, open or drop the file. A list opens with a tick box for each building and for the parcel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a height for the buildings the source gave none for. A line beside each says where its height came from: its number of levels at 3 m each, or the height you typed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the ticked ones\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ticked buildings become extruded masses in one component, and the parcel becomes a line round the site. All of it is one undo step. The address point sits at the origin, in metres east and north of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFlood, zoning and soil outlines are listed for information only; they are not drawn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe data comes from open sources and carries its attribution at the bottom of the list. Heights that were not given are assumptions, so check them before you rely on them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CivilCraft can look up an address and save what the open data sources know round it: neighbouring buildings, the parcel, and flood, zoning and soil outlines. ModelCraft takes that file as suggestions. Nothing is added until you say so. Bring the file in In CivilCraft, send the site to ModelCraft, or save the file (it ends in .ccsite.json). In ModelCraft, open or drop the file. A list opens with a tick box for each building and for the parcel. Type a height for the buildings the source gave none for. A line beside each says where its height came from: its number of levels at 3 m each, or the height you typed. Press Add the ticked ones. The ticked buildings become extruded masses in one component, and the parcel becomes a line round the site. All of it is one undo step. The address point sits at the origin, in metres east and north of it. Flood, zoning and soil outlines are listed for information only; they are not drawn. The data comes from open sources and carries its attribution at the bottom of the list. Heights that were not given are assumptions, so check them before you rely on them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"import-3dm-files","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Bring in a .3dm model","summary":"Import a .3dm file with its meshes, surfaces, curves, blocks, layers and colors; where the triangles come from, and what is left out.","section":"tasks","order":102,"keywords":["3dm","import","nurbs","polysurface","brep","surfaces","render mesh","extrusion","curves","blocks","layers","tags","colors","units","switching","migrate"],"headings":[{"id":"import-the-file","text":"Import the file","level":2},{"id":"where-the-triangles-come-from","text":"Where the triangles come from","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-in","text":"What comes in","level":2},{"id":"what-is-left-out-and-said","text":"What is left out (and said)","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft reads \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E files with its own reader, in the page. Nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Files saved in the version 5, 6, 7 and 8 layouts have been tested; older ones (versions 2 to 4) should open too, but have not been tried yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-the-file\"\u003EImport the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or drop the \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E file on the 3D view).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Reading chair.3dm…&quot; while it reads, then what came in, for example &quot;Imported chair.3dm: 14 meshes (8330 faces), 8 curves, 5 block placements, 3 layers as tags. Surfaces: 7 faces from the meshes saved in the file, 29 tessellated by CadCraft.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole model arrives as one group named after the file, selected, and the view zooms to it. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all away again, tags included.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-triangles-come-from\"\u003EWhere the triangles come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft models with faces and meshes, so every surface in the file comes in as a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E of small flat triangles. For each face of a polysurface (and each extrusion) ModelCraft takes, in this order:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe render mesh saved in the file.\u003C/strong\u003E Most modeling programs save the mesh they showed on screen with each surface; when it is there, ModelCraft uses it as it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIts own tessellation\u003C/strong\u003E, when the file has no saved mesh: planes, NURBS surfaces, surfaces of revolution (spheres, cones, tori, lathe shapes), extruded curves and extrusions (with their holes and end caps), trimmed by their edges. Faces of one polysurface share their edge points, so a closed polysurface comes in as a closed mesh. Curved areas are split until the triangles lie within about 0.15 % of the object&#039;s size of the true surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing\u003C/strong\u003E, for a face on a kind of surface ModelCraft does not read yet. The status bar says how many faces, and of what kind.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s &quot;Surfaces:&quot; part says how many faces came from saved meshes and how many ModelCraft tessellated. Edges inside one face of the file are soft and edges between two faces sharp, so a curved face shades smoothly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor the most faithful shapes, save the file with its render meshes in the program that made it (that is usually the default; a &quot;save small&quot; option leaves them out).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-comes-in\"\u003EWhat comes in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeshes\u003C/strong\u003E with their vertex colors and texture coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPolysurfaces, surfaces and extrusions\u003C/strong\u003E, as described above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E (lines, polylines, arcs, circles, NURBS curves and curves joined from them) as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/curves\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/curves\"\u003Eedges\u003C/a\u003E, one group of edges per layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlocks\u003C/strong\u003E become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Ecomponents\u003C/a\u003E: every placement of a block is a placement of one component, so editing one copy changes them all. Blocks inside blocks are kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayers\u003C/strong\u003E become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003Etags\u003C/a\u003E with the layer&#039;s full name (&quot;Furniture::Chairs&quot;), hidden when the layer was off. Each object&#039;s placement carries its layer&#039;s tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors.\u003C/strong\u003E An object&#039;s color comes from where the file says: its own color, its layer&#039;s, or its material&#039;s base color. It becomes the paint of its faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden objects come in hidden\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E shows them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits:\u003C/strong\u003E the file says its unit; the model comes in at its true size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-left-out-and-said\"\u003EWhat is left out (and said)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s &quot;Notes:&quot; part names everything that did not come in, with how many:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Epoints and point clouds, text, text dots, dimensions, leaders, hatches, lights, clipping planes and layout details;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESubD objects: turn them into meshes or polysurfaces in the program that made the file first;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efaces on offset surfaces and other surface kinds ModelCraft does not read yet, when the file saved no mesh for them;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eblocks linked from other files (only the file you import is read);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eextrusions with slanted (mitered) ends come in with square ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaterial textures, render settings, named views, groups and user text are not read; a material gives its base color only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFiles up to 300 MB. A damaged file says what is wrong and adds nothing; one object that cannot be read is left out and counted, and the rest comes in.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft reads .3dm files with its own reader, in the page. Nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Files saved in the version 5, 6, 7 and 8 layouts have been tested; older ones (versions 2 to 4) should open too, but have not been tried yet. Import the file Click Import in the File group (or drop the .3dm file on the 3D view). Pick the file. The status bar says \"Reading chair.3dm…\" while it reads, then what came in, for example \"Imported chair.3dm: 14 meshes (8330 faces), 8 curves, 5 block placements, 3 layers as tags. Surfaces: 7 faces from the meshes saved in the file, 29 tessellated by CadCraft.\" The whole model arrives as one group named after the file, selected, and the view zooms to it. One Ctrl+Z takes it all away again, tags included. Where the triangles come from ModelCraft models with faces and meshes, so every surface in the file comes in as a mesh of small flat triangles. For each face of a polysurface (and each extrusion) ModelCraft takes, in this order: The render mesh saved in the file. Most modeling programs save the mesh they showed on screen with each surface; when it is there, ModelCraft uses it as it is. Its own tessellation, when the file has no saved mesh: planes, NURBS surfaces, surfaces of revolution (spheres, cones, tori, lathe shapes), extruded curves and extrusions (with their holes and end caps), trimmed by their edges. Faces of one polysurface share their edge points, so a closed polysurface comes in as a closed mesh. Curved areas are split until the triangles lie within about 0.15 % of the object's size of the true surface. Nothing, for a face on a kind of surface ModelCraft does not read yet. The status bar says how many faces, and of what kind. The status bar's \"Surfaces:\" part says how many faces came from saved meshes and how many ModelCraft tessellated. Edges inside one face of the file are soft and edges between two faces sharp, so a curved face shades smoothly. For the most faithful shapes, save the file with its render meshes in the program that made it (that is usually the default; a \"save small\" option leaves them out). What comes in Meshes with their vertex colors and texture coordinates. Polysurfaces, surfaces and extrusions, as described above. Curves (lines, polylines, arcs, circles, NURBS curves and curves joined from them) as edges, one group of edges per layer. Blocks become components: every placement of a block is a placement of one component, so editing one copy changes them all. Blocks inside blocks are kept. Layers become tags with the layer's full name (\"Furniture::Chairs\"), hidden when the layer was off. Each object's placement carries its layer's tag. Colors. An object's color comes from where the file says: its own color, its layer's, or its material's base color. It becomes the paint of its faces. Hidden objects come in hidden; Unhide all shows them. Units: the file says its unit; the model comes in at its true size. What is left out (and said) The status bar's \"Notes:\" part names everything that did not come in, with how many: points and point clouds, text, text dots, dimensions, leaders, hatches, lights, clipping planes and layout details; SubD objects: turn them into meshes or polysurfaces in the program that made the file first; faces on offset surfaces and other surface kinds ModelCraft does not read yet, when the file saved no mesh for them; blocks linked from other files (only the file you import is read); extrusions with slanted (mitered) ends come in with square ends. Material textures, render settings, named views, groups and user text are not read; a material gives its base color only. Limits Files up to 300 MB. A damaged file says what is wrong and adds nothing; one object that cannot be read is left out and counted, and the rest comes in.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"print-3d","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Get a model ready for 3D printing","summary":"Check that a model is closed and faces the right way, see its size, base and overhangs, fix what is wrong, and download it as STL or 3MF for your slicer.","section":"tasks","order":102,"keywords":["3d print","3d printing","printer","print","slicer","stl","3mf","watertight","closed","manifold","solid","holes","inside out","overhang","supports","base","bed","millimeters","export","fix","repair","hollow","bed size","beginner","wall thickness","print tools"],"headings":[{"id":"check-and-download","text":"Check and download","level":2},{"id":"what-the-check-looks-for","text":"What the check looks for","level":2},{"id":"fix-the-problems","text":"Fix the problems","level":2},{"id":"a-hollow-part","text":"A hollow part","level":2},{"id":"your-printer-s-bed","text":"Your printer's bed","level":2},{"id":"print-tools","text":"Print tools","level":2},{"id":"not-checked","text":"Not checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA 3D printer&#039;s slicer needs a closed shape: no holes, every face pointing out, nothing passing through itself. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E looks for exactly that before you download the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-and-download\"\u003ECheck and download\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to print: one or more groups or components, or some faces. With nothing selected, everything shown prints.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog starts with one line: &quot;Ready to print&quot;, how many things to fix before printing, or how many to look at first. Each problem has three parts: what is wrong, why it matters, and what to do. Then come the size in millimeters, the volume, the number of triangles and your printer bed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the dialog lists things to fix, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix what can be fixed\u003C/span\u003E (see below). The check runs again by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload 3MF\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload STL\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat prints is what you see: hidden faces, hidden groups and components, and anything on a hidden tag are left out. Sizes are always in millimeters, the unit slicers expect, whatever unit the model shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPrefer 3MF when your slicer reads it: it keeps the colors and the units. STL has neither, and slicers assume millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-check-looks-for\"\u003EWhat the check looks for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese stop a print or spoil it, and make the dialog say &quot;Not ready to print&quot;:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProblem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face on one side only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA hole: an edge has a face on one side and nothing on the other\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the missing face (a line across the gap often closes it), or use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E (below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShared by three or more faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces meet in a fin or a wall inside a solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the inner face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces facing opposite ways\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome faces point in, some out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the faces that face in and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInside out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA whole closed part points inward\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect its faces and reverse them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPasses through itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne part folds through itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/span\u003E, then erase what is inside. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003EIntersect faces\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are warnings: the model prints, but have a look first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeparate parts overlap\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Emeet face to face\u003C/strong\u003E: slicers usually merge them into one. For a cleaner file, join them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnion\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003ESolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing lies flat at the bottom\u003C/strong\u003E: turn the model so a face rests on the bed, or the slicer will add supports or a raft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeans out more than 45° from upright\u003C/strong\u003E: those faces need support. The dialog says how much of the surface does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnder 1 mm across\u003C/strong\u003E: probably modeled in the wrong unit. Check the unit in the status bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA hollow part (a closed shell inside another) is fine: its faces point into the hollow, and its volume is taken away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-the-problems\"\u003EFix the problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere the problems are\u003C/strong\u003E, the dialog names each group or component that has something wrong (loose faces not in a group are listed as &quot;Faces not in a group&quot;), with what is wrong in it. A component placed several times is listed once: fixing it fixes every copy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E opens that group and selects the edges and faces that are wrong, so they show highlighted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix what can be fixed\u003C/span\u003E fixes all of them at once: it closes holes whose edges lie flat, removes faces inside the shape and stray edges, and turns faces the right way out. It is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it. The check then runs again and shows what is left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat it can&#039;t fix (a hole whose edges don&#039;t lie flat, a part passing through itself) stays in the list with what to do by hand. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepair\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint tools\u003C/strong\u003E goes further: it closes holes that aren&#039;t flat, removes stray pieces and doubled faces, and lists every fix. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#repair\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"repair\"\u003ESupports, wall thickness, hollowing and repair\u003C/a\u003E. You can also fix one group at a time: select it, then use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow problems\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003ESolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-hollow-part\"\u003EA hollow part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint tools\u003C/strong\u003E hollows a part to the wall thickness you give, with drain holes where you click. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#hollow\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"hollow\"\u003EHollow\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also draw the hollow yourself: a closed group inside the part, not touching it, with all its faces reversed so they face into the hollow. The check counts it as a hollow and takes its volume away; it is not listed as something to fix.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-printer-s-bed\"\u003EYour printer&#039;s bed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose your printer&#039;s bed size in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D printer bed\u003C/span\u003E. The check then warns when the model is larger than the bed, turned either way on it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#3d-printer-bed\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"3d-printer-bed\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print-tools\"\u003EPrint tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the dialog&#039;s findings, \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint tools\u003C/strong\u003E opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupports preview\u003C/span\u003E (where supports go and about how much), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall thickness\u003C/span\u003E (walls too thin to print), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepair\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\"\u003ESupports, wall thickness, hollowing and repair\u003C/a\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit for printing\u003C/span\u003E cuts a model too big for the bed into pieces with connectors and numbered joints: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-split\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-split\"\u003ESplit a big model for printing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-checked\"\u003ENot checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe check does not slice: tiny details your nozzle cannot draw and the real supports are your slicer&#039;s. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet#3d-printing\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-hash=\"3d-printing\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A 3D printer's slicer needs a closed shape: no holes, every face pointing out, nothing passing through itself. Export › Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)… looks for exactly that before you download the file. Check and download Select what you want to print: one or more groups or components, or some faces. With nothing selected, everything shown prints. Click Export in the File group, then Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…. The dialog starts with one line: \"Ready to print\", how many things to fix before printing, or how many to look at first. Each problem has three parts: what is wrong, why it matters, and what to do. Then come the size in millimeters, the volume, the number of triangles and your printer bed. If the dialog lists things to fix, click Fix what can be fixed (see below). The check runs again by itself. Click Download 3MF or Download STL. What prints is what you see: hidden faces, hidden groups and components, and anything on a hidden tag are left out. Sizes are always in millimeters, the unit slicers expect, whatever unit the model shows. Prefer 3MF when your slicer reads it: it keeps the colors and the units. STL has neither, and slicers assume millimeters. What the check looks for These stop a print or spoil it, and make the dialog say \"Not ready to print\": Problem What it means What to do A face on one side only A hole: an edge has a face on one side and nothing on the other Draw the missing face (a line across the gap often closes it), or use Fix (below) Shared by three or more faces Faces meet in a fin or a wall inside a solid Erase the inner face Faces facing opposite ways Some faces point in, some out Select the faces that face in and click Reverse face in Entity info Inside out A whole closed part points inward Select its faces and reverse them Passes through itself One part folds through itself Edit › More › Intersect with model, then erase what is inside. See Intersect faces These are warnings: the model prints, but have a look first. Separate parts overlap, or meet face to face: slicers usually merge them into one. For a cleaner file, join them with Solids › Union. See Solid tools. Nothing lies flat at the bottom: turn the model so a face rests on the bed, or the slicer will add supports or a raft. Leans out more than 45° from upright: those faces need support. The dialog says how much of the surface does. Under 1 mm across: probably modeled in the wrong unit. Check the unit in the status bar. A hollow part (a closed shell inside another) is fine: its faces point into the hollow, and its volume is taken away. Fix the problems Under Where the problems are, the dialog names each group or component that has something wrong (loose faces not in a group are listed as \"Faces not in a group\"), with what is wrong in it. A component placed several times is listed once: fixing it fixes every copy. Show opens that group and selects the edges and faces that are wrong, so they show highlighted. Fix what can be fixed fixes all of them at once: it closes holes whose edges lie flat, removes faces inside the shape and stray edges, and turns faces the right way out. It is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. The check then runs again and shows what is left. What it can't fix (a hole whose edges don't lie flat, a part passing through itself) stays in the list with what to do by hand. Repair under Print tools goes further: it closes holes that aren't flat, removes stray pieces and doubled faces, and lists every fix. See Supports, wall thickness, hollowing and repair. You can also fix one group at a time: select it, then use Show problems and Fix in Entity info. See Solid tools. A hollow part Hollow under Print tools hollows a part to the wall thickness you give, with drain holes where you click. See Hollow. You can also draw the hollow yourself: a closed group inside the part, not touching it, with all its faces reversed so they face into the hollow. The check counts it as a hollow and takes its volume away; it is not listed as something to fix. Your printer's bed Choose your printer's bed size in Settings › ModelCraft › 3D printer bed. The check then warns when the model is larger than the bed, turned either way on it. See Settings. Print tools Under the dialog's findings, Print tools opens Supports preview (where supports go and about how much), Wall thickness (walls too thin to print), Hollow and Repair. See Supports, wall thickness, hollowing and repair. Split for printing cuts a model too big for the bed into pieces with connectors and numbered joints: see Split a big model for printing. Not checked The check does not slice: tiny details your nozzle cannot draw and the real supports are your slicer's. See Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"print-tools","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Supports, wall thickness, hollowing and repair","summary":"See where a print needs supports and about how much, find walls too thin to print, hollow a part to a wall thickness with drain holes, and repair a mesh with every fix listed.","section":"tasks","order":103,"keywords":["3d print","supports","support preview","overhang","tree supports","column supports","wall thickness","thin walls","too thin","nozzle","hollow","hollowing","shell","drain hole","resin","repair","fix mesh","non-manifold","flipped faces","holes","small shells","self-intersection","overlap","join"],"headings":[{"id":"supports-preview","text":"Supports preview","level":2},{"id":"wall-thickness","text":"Wall thickness","level":2},{"id":"hollow","text":"Hollow","level":2},{"id":"repair","text":"Repair","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EFour tools for getting a model ready for a 3D printer. Open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E; the dialog&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint tools\u003C/strong\u003E line has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupports preview\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall thickness\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepair\u003C/span\u003E. Each opens as a card over the view; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E (the cross) takes it and its marks away. One card shows at a time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach tool works on what prints: the selection, or everything shown when nothing is selected. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepair\u003C/span\u003E need one group, component or mesh selected, because they change it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"supports-preview\"\u003ESupports preview\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe faces that lean out more than the \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverhang angle\u003C/strong\u003E from upright are shaded red, and simple supports are drawn from them (orange) down to the bed, or to the part of the model below them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOverhang angle\u003C/strong\u003E: 45° is what most printers manage without support. Raise it for a printer that bridges well.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E: a straight column every 3 mm under each overhang. The estimate counts them as a 15% support pattern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETree\u003C/strong\u003E: nearby points join one trunk under their middle; no branch leans more than 45°. Trees usually take less material, and touch the bed in fewer places.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card says how much surface needs support, how many support points there are and about how much material they take. Supports that stand on the model (not the bed) are counted: they leave marks there. With a printer bed chosen in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, the bed is drawn under the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a preview, not a slicer: your slicer decides the real supports. On a very large overhang the support points are spread wider so the preview stays quick; the card says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wall-thickness\"\u003EWall thickness\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach face is measured straight through the solid to the other side. Walls under the \u003Cstrong\u003Ethinnest wall to allow\u003C/strong\u003E are red, and walls under one and a half times it are orange: they print, but may come out weak.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin areas are listed, thinnest first, with how thin and how large each is. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E turns the camera to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA printer can&#039;t make a wall thinner than about two widths of its nozzle: 0.8 mm for the common 0.4 mm nozzle (the start). Resin printers manage less; check your printer&#039;s guide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a very large model only some faces cast a ray, and the others take their neighbours&#039; thickness; the card says how many were measured.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hollow\"\u003EHollow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHollowing keeps the outside as it is and takes out the inside, leaving walls as thick as you ask. Resin prints use less resin and pull less on the build plate; filament prints use less plastic than infill would.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the group, component or mesh, then open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cstrong\u003Ewall\u003C/strong\u003E (2 mm to start) and how wide the \u003Cstrong\u003Edrain holes\u003C/strong\u003E are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a resin print, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace a drain hole\u003C/span\u003E, then click the model&#039;s outside where it goes: low down, near the build plate, so the liquid resin runs out. Place as many as you need; each shows as a blue circle and is listed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreview\u003C/span\u003E says how much the hollowing takes out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHollow it\u003C/span\u003E makes the change: one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is one closed solid that the 3D-print check accepts: ModelCraft checks it first and changes nothing if it would not pass. Where the model is thinner than two walls, it stays solid. The result is a group whose flat sides are single faces, so you can keep modeling it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E; a curved part hollowed on the fine grid usually has too many faces for that and becomes a mesh instead, and the status line says which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor most shapes the inside is the outside moved in by exactly the wall. Where corners or thin parts make that impossible, ModelCraft follows the outside at the wall&#039;s distance on a fine grid instead; the card then says how close it is (to within a fraction of a millimeter).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"repair\"\u003ERepair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepair\u003C/span\u003E looks at the selected part the way a printer&#039;s slicer will, fixes what it can fix safely and lists every fix:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt fixes\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat that means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles with no area\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eleft out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA triangle drawn twice\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eone copy kept; a face drawn back to back with itself (no thickness) is removed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoose pieces under 0.5 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eremoved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles facing the wrong way\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturned to match their neighbours\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles, flat or not\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eclosed with a fan of new triangles from the middle of each hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA piece inside out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturned outward (a hollow inside another piece is left as it is: it is meant to face in)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix the safe ones\u003C/span\u003E makes all of them in one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes, and the status line lists each fix. The fixed part becomes a group whose flat sides are single faces. If it still does not close up (something under \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft for you\u003C/strong\u003E remains), or it would have too many faces for a group, it becomes a mesh instead: its faces stay triangles, and flat sides still look flat. The status line says which. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin overlapping parts\u003C/span\u003E works the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat can&#039;t be fixed safely is listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft for you\u003C/strong\u003E and marked in purple in the view, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges where three or more faces meet\u003C/strong\u003E: decide which face belongs and erase the others.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA piece passing through itself\u003C/strong\u003E: move or redraw that part. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003EIntersect faces\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHoles too big or too tangled to close\u003C/strong\u003E: draw faces across them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeparate pieces that overlap\u003C/strong\u003E: slicers merge them anyway. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin overlapping parts\u003C/span\u003E makes them one closed body (it rebuilds the faces where they meet, so it is a separate button, never done for you).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor push/pull groups you can also fix one at a time in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow problems\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E, which keeps the faces you drew. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003ESolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003EGet a model ready for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/beginner\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/beginner\"\u003EThe Beginner style\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet#3d-printing\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-hash=\"3d-printing\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Four tools for getting a model ready for a 3D printer. Open Export › Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…; the dialog's Print tools line has Supports preview, Wall thickness, Hollow and Repair. Each opens as a card over the view; Close (the cross) takes it and its marks away. One card shows at a time. Each tool works on what prints: the selection, or everything shown when nothing is selected. Hollow and Repair need one group, component or mesh selected, because they change it. Supports preview The faces that lean out more than the overhang angle from upright are shaded red, and simple supports are drawn from them (orange) down to the bed, or to the part of the model below them. Overhang angle: 45° is what most printers manage without support. Raise it for a printer that bridges well. Columns: a straight column every 3 mm under each overhang. The estimate counts them as a 15% support pattern. Tree: nearby points join one trunk under their middle; no branch leans more than 45°. Trees usually take less material, and touch the bed in fewer places. The card says how much surface needs support, how many support points there are and about how much material they take. Supports that stand on the model (not the bed) are counted: they leave marks there. With a printer bed chosen in Settings, the bed is drawn under the model. This is a preview, not a slicer: your slicer decides the real supports. On a very large overhang the support points are spread wider so the preview stays quick; the card says so. Wall thickness Each face is measured straight through the solid to the other side. Walls under the thinnest wall to allow are red, and walls under one and a half times it are orange: they print, but may come out weak. The thin areas are listed, thinnest first, with how thin and how large each is. Show turns the camera to it. A printer can't make a wall thinner than about two widths of its nozzle: 0.8 mm for the common 0.4 mm nozzle (the start). Resin printers manage less; check your printer's guide. On a very large model only some faces cast a ray, and the others take their neighbours' thickness; the card says how many were measured. Hollow Hollowing keeps the outside as it is and takes out the inside, leaving walls as thick as you ask. Resin prints use less resin and pull less on the build plate; filament prints use less plastic than infill would. Select the group, component or mesh, then open Hollow. Set the wall (2 mm to start) and how wide the drain holes are. For a resin print, click Place a drain hole, then click the model's outside where it goes: low down, near the build plate, so the liquid resin runs out. Place as many as you need; each shows as a blue circle and is listed with Remove. Preview says how much the hollowing takes out. Hollow it makes the change: one step that Ctrl+Z undoes. The result is one closed solid that the 3D-print check accepts: ModelCraft checks it first and changes nothing if it would not pass. Where the model is thinner than two walls, it stays solid. The result is a group whose flat sides are single faces, so you can keep modeling it with Push/pull; a curved part hollowed on the fine grid usually has too many faces for that and becomes a mesh instead, and the status line says which. For most shapes the inside is the outside moved in by exactly the wall. Where corners or thin parts make that impossible, ModelCraft follows the outside at the wall's distance on a fine grid instead; the card then says how close it is (to within a fraction of a millimeter). Repair Repair looks at the selected part the way a printer's slicer will, fixes what it can fix safely and lists every fix: It fixes What that means Triangles with no area left out A triangle drawn twice one copy kept; a face drawn back to back with itself (no thickness) is removed Loose pieces under 0.5 mm removed Triangles facing the wrong way turned to match their neighbours Holes, flat or not closed with a fan of new triangles from the middle of each hole A piece inside out turned outward (a hollow inside another piece is left as it is: it is meant to face in) Fix the safe ones makes all of them in one step that Ctrl+Z undoes, and the status line lists each fix. The fixed part becomes a group whose flat sides are single faces. If it still does not close up (something under Left for you remains), or it would have too many faces for a group, it becomes a mesh instead: its faces stay triangles, and flat sides still look flat. The status line says which. Join overlapping parts works the same way. What can't be fixed safely is listed under Left for you and marked in purple in the view, with Show: Edges where three or more faces meet: decide which face belongs and erase the others. A piece passing through itself: move or redraw that part. See Intersect faces. Holes too big or too tangled to close: draw faces across them. Separate pieces that overlap: slicers merge them anyway. Join overlapping parts makes them one closed body (it rebuilds the faces where they meet, so it is a separate button, never done for you). For push/pull groups you can also fix one at a time in Entity info › Show problems / Fix, which keeps the faces you drew. See Solid tools. Related: Get a model ready for 3D printing · The Beginner style · Not built yet","lang":"en"},{"slug":"print-split","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Split a big model for printing","summary":"Cut a model too big for your printer into pieces that fit the bed, with pegs, keys, dovetails, dowel or magnet holes and the joint's number on both faces, and download every piece and an assembly sheet in one zip.","section":"tasks","order":104,"keywords":["3d print","split","slant","slanted cut","angled cut","oblique","splitter","cut","too big","bed size","build volume","pieces","parts","connectors","pegs","keys","dovetail","dowel","magnet","clearance","tolerance","part numbers","assembly","exploded view","zip","stl","3mf","licence","license","usb key","dongle"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-cut","text":"How to cut","level":2},{"id":"connectors","text":"Connectors","level":2},{"id":"part-numbers","text":"Part numbers","level":2},{"id":"preview-download-split-the-model","text":"Preview, download, split the model","level":2},{"id":"move-the-connectors-yourself","text":"Move the connectors yourself","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen a model is bigger than your printer&#039;s bed, \u003Cstrong\u003ESplit for printing\u003C/strong\u003E cuts it into pieces that fit, adds connectors so the pieces line up when you put them together, and engraves the same number on both faces of every joint. Open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E; the dialog&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint tools\u003C/strong\u003E line has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit for printing\u003C/span\u003E. It opens as a card over the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works on what prints: the selection, or everything shown when nothing is selected. What prints must be a closed solid; if the check finds holes, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#repair\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"repair\"\u003ERepair\u003C/a\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt runs in ModelCraft, on your computer: no licence server, no USB key, nothing uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-to-cut\"\u003EHow to cut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit the bed, fewest pieces\u003C/strong\u003E: give the bed&#039;s width, depth and height (they start from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › ModelCraft › 3D printer). The card says how many pieces it takes and how big each is; it cuts the model into equal pieces, as few as fit. A piece may lie on its side in the files if that is what makes it fit. The connectors that stand out of a piece count toward its size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAt places I give\u003C/strong\u003E: pick the axis to cut across and type where, in millimetres from the low end of the model, with commas between (\u003Ccode\u003E100, 200\u003C/code\u003E makes three pieces). \u003Cstrong\u003ESlant\u003C/strong\u003E turns the cuts by up to 60° about one of the two other axes (a cut across Z slanted about X, for example): each slanting cut runs through the model&#039;s middle line at its place, gives the pieces more gluing area and hides the joint better on a sloping surface. Connectors stand square to the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cut planes are drawn round the model as dashed lines while the card is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connectors\"\u003EConnectors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach place where two pieces touch gets connectors, placed by themselves where there is the most room: at least the \u003Cstrong\u003Eleast wall\u003C/strong\u003E plus the \u003Cstrong\u003Eclearance\u003C/strong\u003E plus their own size away from the outside, and far enough from every other cut that two holes never meet. On a long face two connectors are spread along it, so the pieces can&#039;t turn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERound pegs\u003C/strong\u003E: a peg on one piece (chamfered at the tip), a hole on the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESquare keys\u003C/strong\u003E: the same, square, so a piece can only go on one way round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDovetails\u003C/strong\u003E: a tongue that slides into a slot from the side. Where one end of the slot would meet another cut, the dovetail stops short and slides in from the open end. Dovetails suit cuts in one direction (a row of slices); in a grid, use pegs or dowels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDowel holes\u003C/strong\u003E: a hole on each side; the pins are printed too (in the zip).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMagnet pockets\u003C/strong\u003E: a pocket on each side for a round magnet of the diameter and thickness you give. Check the poles before gluing them in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENone (glue)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Eclearance\u003C/strong\u003E is the gap on each side between a peg and its hole (0.2 mm to start). It is true to the number you give: round holes are made a little larger so their flat sides, not their corners, keep that gap. Raise it if your printer&#039;s parts come out tight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a joint has no room for a connector (a thin wall, a small touching area), the card says so: glue that one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"part-numbers\"\u003EPart numbers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave the joint numbers on both faces\u003C/strong\u003E on, each joint gets the number of its two pieces, such as \u003Ccode\u003E3-4\u003C/code\u003E, engraved 0.6 mm deep on both faces, reading the right way round on each. The numbers start 5 mm high and get smaller to fit, down to 2.5 mm; a joint with no room for its number is listed. Pieces are numbered from the bottom layer up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"preview-download-split-the-model\"\u003EPreview, download, split the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreview\u003C/span\u003E cuts the model and says how many pieces, joints and connectors there are, whether every piece passes the 3D-print check and fits the bed, and lists each piece&#039;s size. A ring marks each connector and number in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the pieces (.zip)\u003C/span\u003E saves one zip: a file per piece (STL or 3MF, as you choose under \u003Cstrong\u003EFiles\u003C/strong\u003E), each standing on the bed; the dowel pins; and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eassembly sheet\u003C/strong\u003E — a picture of the pieces pulled apart with their numbers (\u003Ccode\u003Eassembly.svg\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eassembly.png\u003C/code\u003E) and a list of the pieces and joints (\u003Ccode\u003Eassembly.txt\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit the model\u003C/span\u003E puts the pieces in the model in place of the selected groups or components, named as on the assembly sheet, in one step: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E puts the original back. It needs the groups or components to split selected; the download works on whatever prints.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-the-connectors-yourself\"\u003EMove the connectors yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a preview, \u003Cstrong\u003EConnectors by hand\u003C/strong\u003E lets you correct where the connectors go:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove one\u003C/span\u003E: click a red ring in the view (it turns green), then click where it should go on the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd one\u003C/span\u003E: click in the view where a connector should go. It goes on the cut plane your click reaches, between the two pieces there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove one\u003C/span\u003E: click a red ring to take that connector away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is checked again like the automatic ones: a spot too near the outline, another cut or another connector is not made, and the card says so. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic again\u003C/span\u003E forgets your places. They are kept while the model, the selection and the cuts stay as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA preview stays until the model, the selection or a setting changes; then the card asks you to preview again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery piece is checked as the 3D-print check would check it before you download. Your slicer still decides how each piece prints: look at each one there, and print one joint first to see how your printer handles the clearance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"When a model is bigger than your printer's bed, Split for printing cuts it into pieces that fit, adds connectors so the pieces line up when you put them together, and engraves the same number on both faces of every joint. Open Export › Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…; the dialog's Print tools line has Split for printing. It opens as a card over the view. It works on what prints: the selection, or everything shown when nothing is selected. What prints must be a closed solid; if the check finds holes, use Repair first. It runs in ModelCraft, on your computer: no licence server, no USB key, nothing uploaded. How to cut Fit the bed, fewest pieces: give the bed's width, depth and height (they start from Settings › ModelCraft › 3D printer). The card says how many pieces it takes and how big each is; it cuts the model into equal pieces, as few as fit. A piece may lie on its side in the files if that is what makes it fit. The connectors that stand out of a piece count toward its size. At places I give: pick the axis to cut across and type where, in millimetres from the low end of the model, with commas between (100, 200 makes three pieces). Slant turns the cuts by up to 60° about one of the two other axes (a cut across Z slanted about X, for example): each slanting cut runs through the model's middle line at its place, gives the pieces more gluing area and hides the joint better on a sloping surface. Connectors stand square to the cut. The cut planes are drawn round the model as dashed lines while the card is open. Connectors Each place where two pieces touch gets connectors, placed by themselves where there is the most room: at least the least wall plus the clearance plus their own size away from the outside, and far enough from every other cut that two holes never meet. On a long face two connectors are spread along it, so the pieces can't turn. Round pegs: a peg on one piece (chamfered at the tip), a hole on the other. Square keys: the same, square, so a piece can only go on one way round. Dovetails: a tongue that slides into a slot from the side. Where one end of the slot would meet another cut, the dovetail stops short and slides in from the open end. Dovetails suit cuts in one direction (a row of slices); in a grid, use pegs or dowels. Dowel holes: a hole on each side; the pins are printed too (in the zip). Magnet pockets: a pocket on each side for a round magnet of the diameter and thickness you give. Check the poles before gluing them in. None (glue). The clearance is the gap on each side between a peg and its hole (0.2 mm to start). It is true to the number you give: round holes are made a little larger so their flat sides, not their corners, keep that gap. Raise it if your printer's parts come out tight. When a joint has no room for a connector (a thin wall, a small touching area), the card says so: glue that one. Part numbers With Engrave the joint numbers on both faces on, each joint gets the number of its two pieces, such as 3-4, engraved 0.6 mm deep on both faces, reading the right way round on each. The numbers start 5 mm high and get smaller to fit, down to 2.5 mm; a joint with no room for its number is listed. Pieces are numbered from the bottom layer up. Preview, download, split the model Preview cuts the model and says how many pieces, joints and connectors there are, whether every piece passes the 3D-print check and fits the bed, and lists each piece's size. A ring marks each connector and number in the view. Download the pieces (.zip) saves one zip: a file per piece (STL or 3MF, as you choose under Files), each standing on the bed; the dowel pins; and the assembly sheet — a picture of the pieces pulled apart with their numbers (assembly.svg and assembly.png) and a list of the pieces and joints (assembly.txt). Split the model puts the pieces in the model in place of the selected groups or components, named as on the assembly sheet, in one step: Undo puts the original back. It needs the groups or components to split selected; the download works on whatever prints. Move the connectors yourself After a preview, Connectors by hand lets you correct where the connectors go: Move one: click a red ring in the view (it turns green), then click where it should go on the cut. Add one: click in the view where a connector should go. It goes on the cut plane your click reaches, between the two pieces there. Remove one: click a red ring to take that connector away. Each change is checked again like the automatic ones: a spot too near the outline, another cut or another connector is not made, and the card says so. Automatic again forgets your places. They are kept while the model, the selection and the cuts stay as they are. A preview stays until the model, the selection or a setting changes; then the card asks you to preview again. Every piece is checked as the 3D-print check would check it before you download. Your slicer still decides how each piece prints: look at each one there, and print one joint first to see how your printer handles the clearance.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"nurbs-surfaces","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Freeform surfaces (NURBS)","summary":"Smooth curves and surfaces for product shells, car bodies and boat hulls — loft, sweep, curve networks, four-edge patches, blends — with G0, G1 and G2 continuity measured on the exact surfaces, zebra and curvature views, and STEP or IGES files.","section":"tasks","order":106,"keywords":["nurbs","trim","trimmed","hole","cut","freeform","free-form","surface","surfaces","b-spline","bspline","curve","spline","loft","sweep","rail","network","coons","patch","blend","match","continuity","g0","g1","g2","tangent","curvature","zebra","control points","knots","degree","step","iges","class a"],"headings":[{"id":"how-a-command-works","text":"How a command works","level":2},{"id":"curves","text":"Curves","level":2},{"id":"surfaces","text":"Surfaces","level":2},{"id":"checking-the-quality","text":"Checking the quality","level":2},{"id":"trim-a-surface","text":"Trim a surface","level":2},{"id":"undo-the-file-step-and-iges","text":"Undo, the file, STEP and IGES","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ESurfaces\u003C/strong\u003E panel makes smooth curves and surfaces of the kind used for product shells, car bodies and boat hulls: NURBS, the same maths other surface modellers use. Everything is measured on the exact surfaces, never on the triangles you see. Sizes in the panel are in \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimetres\u003C/strong\u003E, whatever unit the model shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it from the right-hand panel: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurfaces\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the Surfaces panel\u003C/span\u003E. A model that already has surfaces opens it by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-a-command-works\"\u003EHow a command works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress a command (for example \u003Cstrong\u003ELoft\u003C/strong\u003E). The panel says what it still needs: points, curves, a surface edge or a curve end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick them: click them in the 3D view, or press \u003Cstrong\u003EPick\u003C/strong\u003E (or the edge and end buttons) beside each one in the list. For an edge, click near that edge of the surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the options (degree, tolerance, continuity, …) and press \u003Cstrong\u003EMake\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurves from points take the points typed one per line (\u003Ccode\u003Ex, y, z\u003C/code\u003E in mm), or \u003Cstrong\u003EPick points in the view\u003C/strong\u003E: each click uses the same snapping as the drawing tools; press &lt;kbd&gt;Enter&lt;/kbd&gt; or double-click to finish, &lt;kbd&gt;Esc&lt;/kbd&gt; to stop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"curves\"\u003ECurves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurve through points\u003C/strong\u003E passes through every point. \u003Cstrong\u003ECurve by control points\u003C/strong\u003E follows a cage of points instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInsert knot\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERaise degree\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELower degree\u003C/strong\u003E change how a curve is described without moving it (lowering refuses when the curve would move more than the tolerance). \u003Cstrong\u003ERebuild curve\u003C/strong\u003E gives it a new number of control points and says how far it moved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMatch curve\u003C/strong\u003E makes one curve&#039;s end meet another&#039;s with G0 (touching), G1 (same direction) or G2 (same curvature too). \u003Cstrong\u003EBlend curve\u003C/strong\u003E adds a new curve between two ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJoin curves\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESplit curve\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"surfaces\"\u003ESurfaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoft\u003C/strong\u003E through two or more curves, \u003Cstrong\u003ERuled surface\u003C/strong\u003E between two, \u003Cstrong\u003EExtrude curve\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERevolve curve\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESweep along one rail\u003C/strong\u003E carries a profile along a path without twisting it; \u003Cstrong\u003ESweep along two rails\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the profile&#039;s ends on both rails. Sweeps are fitted: the status line says how close it stayed to the true sweep.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurface from a curve network\u003C/strong\u003E passes through a grid of curves (the first picks are the profiles, the rest the guides). \u003Cstrong\u003EPatch from four edges\u003C/strong\u003E fills four curves that meet at their corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMatch surface\u003C/strong\u003E makes a surface edge meet a neighbour with G0, G1 or G2; \u003Cstrong\u003EBlend surface\u003C/strong\u003E adds a surface between two edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset surface\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EExtend surface\u003C/strong\u003E (the surface carries on as it was going), \u003Cstrong\u003ERebuild surface\u003C/strong\u003E, and knots and degree for surfaces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checking-the-quality\"\u003EChecking the quality\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EShared edges\u003C/strong\u003E table lists every edge two surfaces share, with the gap (mm), the angle between them (°) and the curvature jump (1/mm), and whether that makes it G0, G1 or G2. An edge worse than you asked for is also in \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E (the ⚠ button in the app bar) with a \u003Cstrong\u003EMatch\u003C/strong\u003E fix.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow as\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EZebra stripes\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECurvature (Gaussian)\u003C/strong\u003E come from the exact surface: stripes that run on unbroken across an edge mean G1; stripes that also keep their width mean G2. Curvature is blue where the surface is a saddle, red where it is a dome and white where it is flat. \u003Cstrong\u003EControl points\u003C/strong\u003E draws the control nets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EG0–G2 are measured at 33 points per edge. \u003Cstrong\u003EG3 and &quot;class A&quot; quality are not measured\u003C/strong\u003E and are not claimed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-a-surface\"\u003ETrim a surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ETrim\u003C/strong\u003E group cuts a surface without changing its shape: the surface keeps all its control points, and only the part inside the outline is shown, counted and written. \u003Cstrong\u003ETrim to a rectangle\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETrim with a circle\u003C/strong\u003E work on a share (0 to 1) of the surface in each direction, so they need no picking in the view; \u003Cstrong\u003ETrim by a curve\u003C/strong\u003E takes a closed curve that lies on the surface (it is projected into the surface, and refused in words when it is open or more than the tolerance away). Tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECut a hole\u003C/strong\u003E to remove the inside of the outline; leave it off to keep only the inside. A surface can have one kept outline and any number of holes; an outline that crosses another is refused. \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove the trim\u003C/strong\u003E shows the whole surface again. The cut edges follow the outline exactly; the panel&#039;s Undo takes a trim back like any other step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESTEP carries the trim as the face&#039;s bounds and reads it back. IGES in this panel holds the whole patches only: it says so when you save trimmed surfaces. Solid Booleans (joining trimmed surfaces into a solid) are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-the-file-step-and-iges\"\u003EUndo, the file, STEP and IGES\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel has its own \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ERedo\u003C/strong\u003E (one step per command). The surfaces are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file with the model; they are not part of the model&#039;s own undo, like the file&#039;s materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTEP…\u003C/strong\u003E (AP242) and \u003Cstrong\u003EIGES…\u003C/strong\u003E save the curves and surfaces exactly as they are (B-spline curves and surfaces, nothing turned into triangles). Each surface is its own face (with its trim, if it has one): surfaces are not stitched into solids yet. \u003Cstrong\u003EImport…\u003C/strong\u003E reads STEP and IGES curves and surfaces written this way, and \u003Ccode\u003E.ccnurbs\u003C/code\u003E patch sets; other programs&#039; STEP solids open with File › Import as meshes.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Surfaces panel makes smooth curves and surfaces of the kind used for product shells, car bodies and boat hulls: NURBS, the same maths other surface modellers use. Everything is measured on the exact surfaces, never on the triangles you see. Sizes in the panel are in millimetres, whatever unit the model shows. Open it from the right-hand panel: Surfaces › Open the Surfaces panel. A model that already has surfaces opens it by itself. How a command works Press a command (for example Loft). The panel says what it still needs: points, curves, a surface edge or a curve end. Pick them: click them in the 3D view, or press Pick (or the edge and end buttons) beside each one in the list. For an edge, click near that edge of the surface. Check the options (degree, tolerance, continuity, …) and press Make. Curves from points take the points typed one per line (x, y, z in mm), or Pick points in the view: each click uses the same snapping as the drawing tools; press \u003Ckbd\u003EEnter\u003C/kbd\u003E or double-click to finish, \u003Ckbd\u003EEsc\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop. Curves Curve through points passes through every point. Curve by control points follows a cage of points instead. Insert knot, Raise degree and Lower degree change how a curve is described without moving it (lowering refuses when the curve would move more than the tolerance). Rebuild curve gives it a new number of control points and says how far it moved. Match curve makes one curve's end meet another's with G0 (touching), G1 (same direction) or G2 (same curvature too). Blend curve adds a new curve between two ends. Join curves and Split curve. Surfaces Loft through two or more curves, Ruled surface between two, Extrude curve, Revolve curve. Sweep along one rail carries a profile along a path without twisting it; Sweep along two rails keeps the profile's ends on both rails. Sweeps are fitted: the status line says how close it stayed to the true sweep. Surface from a curve network passes through a grid of curves (the first picks are the profiles, the rest the guides). Patch from four edges fills four curves that meet at their corners. Match surface makes a surface edge meet a neighbour with G0, G1 or G2; Blend surface adds a surface between two edges. Offset surface, Extend surface (the surface carries on as it was going), Rebuild surface, and knots and degree for surfaces. Checking the quality The Shared edges table lists every edge two surfaces share, with the gap (mm), the angle between them (°) and the curvature jump (1/mm), and whether that makes it G0, G1 or G2. An edge worse than you asked for is also in Problems (the ⚠ button in the app bar) with a Match fix. Show as › Zebra stripes and Curvature (Gaussian) come from the exact surface: stripes that run on unbroken across an edge mean G1; stripes that also keep their width mean G2. Curvature is blue where the surface is a saddle, red where it is a dome and white where it is flat. Control points draws the control nets. G0–G2 are measured at 33 points per edge. G3 and \"class A\" quality are not measured and are not claimed. Trim a surface The Trim group cuts a surface without changing its shape: the surface keeps all its control points, and only the part inside the outline is shown, counted and written. Trim to a rectangle and Trim with a circle work on a share (0 to 1) of the surface in each direction, so they need no picking in the view; Trim by a curve takes a closed curve that lies on the surface (it is projected into the surface, and refused in words when it is open or more than the tolerance away). Tick Cut a hole to remove the inside of the outline; leave it off to keep only the inside. A surface can have one kept outline and any number of holes; an outline that crosses another is refused. Remove the trim shows the whole surface again. The cut edges follow the outline exactly; the panel's Undo takes a trim back like any other step. STEP carries the trim as the face's bounds and reads it back. IGES in this panel holds the whole patches only: it says so when you save trimmed surfaces. Solid Booleans (joining trimmed surfaces into a solid) are not built yet. Undo, the file, STEP and IGES The panel has its own Undo and Redo (one step per command). The surfaces are saved in the .modelcraft file with the model; they are not part of the model's own undo, like the file's materials. STEP… (AP242) and IGES… save the curves and surfaces exactly as they are (B-spline curves and surfaces, nothing turned into triangles). Each surface is its own face (with its trim, if it has one): surfaces are not stitched into solids yet. Import… reads STEP and IGES curves and surfaces written this way, and .ccnurbs patch sets; other programs' STEP solids open with File › Import as meshes.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"nodes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Nodes: build a model by wiring","summary":"Wire numbers into points, points into curves, curves into surfaces. Change a number and everything downstream rebuilds. Every node is also a line of CraftScript, and the graph is saved in the file.","section":"tasks","order":107,"keywords":["nodes","node editor","visual scripting","parametric","graph","wires","ports","free-form","freeform","nurbs","loft","extrude","revolve","circle","curve","surface","series","range","random","list","script","craftscript","grasshopper"],"headings":[{"id":"wiring","text":"Wiring","level":2},{"id":"types-and-units","text":"Types and units","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Errors","level":2},{"id":"every-node-is-a-line-of-script","text":"Every node is a line of script","level":2},{"id":"saved-with-the-file","text":"Saved with the file","level":2},{"id":"bake-to-model","text":"Bake to model","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ENodes\u003C/strong\u003E editor builds geometry by wiring boxes together. Each box (a node) does one small job: make a number, a point, a circle, a loft. You connect the result of one to the input of the next, and the model follows when you change a number at the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it from the right-hand panel: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENodes\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENodes…\u003C/span\u003E. A file that already has a graph opens it by itself. It is a window over the 3D view: drag its title bar to move it, drag its corner to resize it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wiring\"\u003EWiring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a node from the list on the left (type in the search box to narrow it, &lt;kbd&gt;Enter&lt;/kbd&gt; adds the first match). It appears in the middle of the canvas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag from a round \u003Cstrong\u003Eoutput\u003C/strong\u003E dot on the right of one node to a round \u003Cstrong\u003Einput\u003C/strong\u003E dot on the left of another. The dot turns green when it fits and red when it does not; point at it for the reason.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn input with nothing connected shows a box for a typed value. Type a number and press &lt;kbd&gt;Tab&lt;/kbd&gt;: the result rebuilds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe small circle in a node&#039;s title bar is its \u003Cstrong\u003Eeye\u003C/strong\u003E. A node with the eye on is drawn in the 3D view, as lines for curves, shaded for surfaces, dots for points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a node by its title bar. Drag the background to pan, turn the wheel to zoom. &lt;kbd&gt;Delete&lt;/kbd&gt; removes the selected node. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETidy\u003C/span\u003E arranges the nodes in columns, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E brings them all into view, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E step through your edits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"types-and-units\"\u003ETypes and units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPorts have a type: number, yes/no, point, vector, curve, surface, mesh, or &quot;geometry&quot; for any of those. A point can feed a vector input and back. Numbers carry a unit: a \u003Cstrong\u003Elength\u003C/strong\u003E is in millimetres and an \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle\u003C/strong\u003E is in degrees. A length cannot feed an angle, and a length times a length (an area) is refused, so a wrong wire is stopped when you make it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA square dot is a \u003Cstrong\u003Elist\u003C/strong\u003E. When a list reaches a single-value input the node runs once for each item, and its result becomes a list. The shorter list repeats its last item. For example, a \u003Cstrong\u003ESeries\u003C/strong\u003E of heights into \u003Cstrong\u003EMove\u003C/strong\u003E makes a stack of copies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"errors\"\u003EErrors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA node that cannot do its job says why under its name, in red (&quot;The radius must be above zero.&quot;), and the nodes after it wait. The same message is a Problem in the Problems panel, so you can find it from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-node-is-a-line-of-script\"\u003EEvery node is a line of script\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScript\u003C/span\u003E to see the graph as CraftScript: one line per node, such as \u003Ccode\u003En3 = nd_circle(n2, n1, [0 0 1]);\u003C/code\u003E. Edit the text and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake the graph from this script\u003C/span\u003E to bring it back as a graph. The same \u003Ccode\u003End_\u003C/code\u003E functions work in a CraftScript in PartCraft, so a graph can be run, looped or tested there. Random numbers always take a \u003Cstrong\u003Eseed\u003C/strong\u003E, so the same graph gives the same result on every computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saved-with-the-file\"\u003ESaved with the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe graph is saved in the model file. The nodes make curves, surfaces and meshes of their own: they do not change the model&#039;s faces until you ask.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bake-to-model\"\u003EBake to model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch on the eye of a node that makes a surface or a mesh, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBake to model\u003C/span\u003E in the Nodes window. Each shown surface or mesh becomes a group of faces in the model, in metres, that you can edit like any other. It is one undo step. The graph stays, but the copy no longer follows it: change a number and bake again for a new one. Points and curves are not solids, so they are left out, and the message says how many.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Nodes editor builds geometry by wiring boxes together. Each box (a node) does one small job: make a number, a point, a circle, a loft. You connect the result of one to the input of the next, and the model follows when you change a number at the start. Open it from the right-hand panel: Nodes › Nodes…. A file that already has a graph opens it by itself. It is a window over the 3D view: drag its title bar to move it, drag its corner to resize it. Wiring Pick a node from the list on the left (type in the search box to narrow it, \u003Ckbd\u003EEnter\u003C/kbd\u003E adds the first match). It appears in the middle of the canvas. Drag from a round output dot on the right of one node to a round input dot on the left of another. The dot turns green when it fits and red when it does not; point at it for the reason. An input with nothing connected shows a box for a typed value. Type a number and press \u003Ckbd\u003ETab\u003C/kbd\u003E: the result rebuilds. The small circle in a node's title bar is its eye. A node with the eye on is drawn in the 3D view, as lines for curves, shaded for surfaces, dots for points. Drag a node by its title bar. Drag the background to pan, turn the wheel to zoom. \u003Ckbd\u003EDelete\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the selected node. Tidy arranges the nodes in columns, Fit brings them all into view, Undo and Redo step through your edits. Types and units Ports have a type: number, yes/no, point, vector, curve, surface, mesh, or \"geometry\" for any of those. A point can feed a vector input and back. Numbers carry a unit: a length is in millimetres and an angle is in degrees. A length cannot feed an angle, and a length times a length (an area) is refused, so a wrong wire is stopped when you make it. A square dot is a list. When a list reaches a single-value input the node runs once for each item, and its result becomes a list. The shorter list repeats its last item. For example, a Series of heights into Move makes a stack of copies. Errors A node that cannot do its job says why under its name, in red (\"The radius must be above zero.\"), and the nodes after it wait. The same message is a Problem in the Problems panel, so you can find it from there. Every node is a line of script Press Script to see the graph as CraftScript: one line per node, such as n3 = nd_circle(n2, n1, [0 0 1]);. Edit the text and press Make the graph from this script to bring it back as a graph. The same nd_ functions work in a CraftScript in PartCraft, so a graph can be run, looped or tested there. Random numbers always take a seed, so the same graph gives the same result on every computer. Saved with the file The graph is saved in the model file. The nodes make curves, surfaces and meshes of their own: they do not change the model's faces until you ask. Bake to model Switch on the eye of a node that makes a surface or a mesh, then press Bake to model in the Nodes window. Each shown surface or mesh becomes a group of faces in the model, in metres, that you can edit like any other. It is one undo step. The graph stays, but the copy no longer follows it: change a number and bake again for a new one. Points and curves are not solids, so they are left out, and the message says how many.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"styles-shadows","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Styles and sun shadows","summary":"Show the model shaded, as hidden lines, as a wireframe or see-through, and study the shadows it casts at any date, time and place.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["presentation view","clean view","hide guides","hide axes","style","shaded","hidden line","wireframe","x-ray","edges","shadows","sun","sun study","solar","date","time","latitude","longitude","time zone","north"],"headings":[{"id":"faces","text":"Faces","level":2},{"id":"turn-on-sun-shadows","text":"Turn on sun shadows","level":2},{"id":"point-the-model-north","text":"Point the model north","level":2},{"id":"a-sun-study","text":"A sun study","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"presentation-view","text":"Presentation view","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right changes how the model is drawn. Nothing in it changes the model itself, and it is not part of undo. Each \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E remembers its own style, and the file remembers the style you had when you saved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"faces\"\u003EFaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose one in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E list:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELooks like\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShaded\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolid faces, lit softly. Faces seen from behind are blue-grey. Imported colors show.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden line\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces in the background color with edges in ink: a clean line drawing with hidden lines removed. White on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, dark on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges only. Faces are not drawn, but they still count when you click and snap: you cannot pick through them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee-through faces, to see what is behind or inside. As in wireframe, clicks and snaps still stop at the nearest face.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow edges\u003C/span\u003E turns the edges on or off in every style except \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E. With it off, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden line\u003C/span\u003E shows almost nothing, since its faces match the background. Soft edges, such as the sides of a pulled circle, are never drawn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-on-sun-shadows\"\u003ETurn on sun shadows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E. The sun settings below it become active, and the model casts shadows on itself and on the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the place:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELatitude\u003C/span\u003E: north is positive, south negative (New York is about 40.7, Sydney about -33.9).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELongitude\u003C/span\u003E: east is positive, west negative (New York is about -74.0).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUTC offset\u003C/span\u003E: the site&#039;s offset from UTC in hours, such as \u003Ccode\u003E-5\u003C/code\u003E for New York in winter. ModelCraft does not apply daylight saving time; use \u003Ccode\u003E-4\u003C/code\u003E for New York in summer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDate\u003C/span\u003E (month and day) and drag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime\u003C/span\u003E slider. The time moves in 15-minute steps and shows next to the slider, such as &quot;3:00 pm&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line under the settings says where the sun is, such as &quot;Sun 52° up, 250° from north. North is the green axis.&quot; At night it says &quot;The sun is down at this time.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new model starts with the sun on June 21 at 3:00 pm, at latitude 40, longitude -105, UTC offset -7.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-the-model-north\"\u003EPoint the model north\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft&#039;s north is always the \u003Cstrong\u003Egreen axis\u003C/strong\u003E. To study a real site, turn the model so its north side faces along green: select everything (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a center on the ground, and turn it by the site&#039;s angle from north (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-sun-study\"\u003EA sun study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the place and the date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a scene (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scene\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the time slider, then add another scene. Repeat for each hour you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E to step through them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShadows can look jagged on a very large model, since one shadow map covers the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShadows are not drawn in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sun position follows the NOAA solar calculation and is accurate to a fraction of a degree, which is plenty for massing studies. Atmospheric refraction is ignored, so very low sun angles near sunrise and sunset can be off slightly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows only the model: the axes, guide lines and guide points, section planes&#039; outlines (the cut stays), lamps&#039; marks, the box of an open group, the snapping marks and tips, and the selection highlight all go. Use it to look at the model clearly, or before a screenshot. Tools still work, and still snap; you just do not see the marks. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to bring them back. Dimensions stay, as they are part of the drawing. While you are editing inside a group, the rest of the model no longer fades: it shows as it is, textures included.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Style panel on the right changes how the model is drawn. Nothing in it changes the model itself, and it is not part of undo. Each scene remembers its own style, and the file remembers the style you had when you saved. Faces Choose one in the Faces list: Style Looks like Shaded Solid faces, lit softly. Faces seen from behind are blue-grey. Imported colors show. Hidden line Faces in the background color with edges in ink: a clean line drawing with hidden lines removed. White on Paper, dark on Model. Wireframe Edges only. Faces are not drawn, but they still count when you click and snap: you cannot pick through them. X-ray See-through faces, to see what is behind or inside. As in wireframe, clicks and snaps still stop at the nearest face. Show edges turns the edges on or off in every style except Wireframe. With it off, Hidden line shows almost nothing, since its faces match the background. Soft edges, such as the sides of a pulled circle, are never drawn. Turn on sun shadows Tick Sun shadows. The sun settings below it become active, and the model casts shadows on itself and on the ground. Set the place: Latitude: north is positive, south negative (New York is about 40.7, Sydney about -33.9). Longitude: east is positive, west negative (New York is about -74.0). UTC offset: the site's offset from UTC in hours, such as -5 for New York in winter. ModelCraft does not apply daylight saving time; use -4 for New York in summer. Set the Date (month and day) and drag the Time slider. The time moves in 15-minute steps and shows next to the slider, such as \"3:00 pm\". A line under the settings says where the sun is, such as \"Sun 52° up, 250° from north. North is the green axis.\" At night it says \"The sun is down at this time.\" A new model starts with the sun on June 21 at 3:00 pm, at latitude 40, longitude -105, UTC offset -7. Point the model north ModelCraft's north is always the green axis. To study a real site, turn the model so its north side faces along green: select everything (Ctrl+A), press Q, click a center on the ground, and turn it by the site's angle from north (see Rotate and scale). A sun study Set up the place and the date. Add a scene (Add scene in Scenes). Move the time slider, then add another scene. Repeat for each hour you want. Click Play to step through them. See Save views as scenes. Things to know Shadows can look jagged on a very large model, since one shadow map covers the whole model. Shadows are not drawn in Wireframe. The sun position follows the NOAA solar calculation and is accurate to a fraction of a degree, which is plenty for massing studies. Atmospheric refraction is ignored, so very low sun angles near sunrise and sunset can be off slightly. Presentation view Views › Presentation view (or Shift+P) shows only the model: the axes, guide lines and guide points, section planes' outlines (the cut stays), lamps' marks, the box of an open group, the snapping marks and tips, and the selection highlight all go. Use it to look at the model clearly, or before a screenshot. Tools still work, and still snap; you just do not see the marks. Press Shift+P again to bring them back. Dimensions stay, as they are part of the drawing. While you are editing inside a group, the rest of the model no longer fades: it shows as it is, textures included.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Render a picture of the model","summary":"Make a picture that looks like daylight: sky, sun from your model's place and date, soft shadows, glass and metal looks, then save it as a PNG.","section":"tasks","order":112,"keywords":["render","rendering","path tracing","path traced","ray tracing","global illumination","reflections","refraction","high quality","photo","photoreal","realistic","picture","image","png","still","sky","sun","daylight","shadows","soft shadows","ambient occlusion","glass","metal","glossy","materials","exposure","brightness","overcast","dusk","studio","presentation","fog","haze","mist","smoke","cloud","volume","light shafts","god rays","caustics","adaptive sampling","noise"],"headings":[{"id":"render-the-view","text":"Render the view","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"make-colors-look-like-glass-or-metal","text":"Make colors look like glass or metal","level":2},{"id":"high-quality","text":"High quality","level":2},{"id":"render-in-the-view","text":"Render in the view","level":2},{"id":"skies-and-light-profiles-on-the-graphics-card","text":"Skies and light profiles on the graphics card","level":2},{"id":"the-camera","text":"The camera","level":2},{"id":"finish-the-picture","text":"Finish the picture","level":2},{"id":"save-the-picture","text":"Save the picture","level":2},{"id":"mix-the-lights-after-the-render","text":"Mix the lights after the render","level":2},{"id":"the-render-queue","text":"The render queue","level":2},{"id":"fog-smoke-noise-and-caustics","text":"Fog, smoke, noise and caustics","level":2},{"id":"how-long-it-takes","text":"How long it takes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E makes a picture of the view that looks like daylight rather than a drawing: a sky, the sun where your model&#039;s place and date put it, soft shadows, light from the whole sky that darkens corners and gaps, and colors that can look like glass, metal or gloss paint. The model is not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"render-the-view\"\u003ERender the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the view the way you want the picture: orbit, zoom, pick a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E, hide what should not show. The picture uses the view&#039;s own camera.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality\u003C/span\u003E, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture fills in as it renders and gets smoother with each pass. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ends it early and keeps the picture so far.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E to download it (a PNG unless \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E says otherwise).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe view&#039;s size\u003C/span\u003E to check the camera and the look, then render \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E at a bigger size for the final picture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view&#039;s size, twice the view&#039;s size, Full HD, 4K or a square. Fixed sizes keep the view&#039;s camera and show more or less at the sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMode\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E (below) with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime limit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many passes (or, in high quality, samples) are averaged: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E 16, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E 64, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E 256. More passes give smoother shadows and edges and take longer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESky\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear sky\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOvercast\u003C/span\u003E (soft light, faint shadows), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDusk\u003C/span\u003E (low, warm light) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhite studio\u003C/span\u003E (a plain white background)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sun comes from the date, time and place in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E › sun. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another date and time for this picture\u003C/span\u003E to try another time without changing the model&#039;s style\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELighter or darker, in steps (stops)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGround under the model\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ground that takes the shadows and fades into the horizon. Turn it off for a model that floats\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw edges\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraws the model&#039;s edges thinly over the picture, for a drawn look\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the sun is below the horizon at the chosen time it is night: the sky goes dark and the model&#039;s \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/lights\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/lights\"\u003Elamps\u003C/a\u003E light it. The dialog says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file, so the next picture of the same model starts where you left off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-colors-look-like-glass-or-metal\"\u003EMake colors look like glass or metal\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E, every color on screen has a look: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatte\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESatin\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlossy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolished metal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlass\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrosted glass\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnpainted faces\u003C/span\u003E are the faces with no color of their own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make windows glass, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/paint\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/paint\"\u003Epaint\u003C/a\u003E the window panes one color, then set that color to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlass\u003C/span\u003E. Glass lets the sky and what is behind it show through, tinted by its color, and reflects the sky.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E mode, glass and metal reflect the sky, not the rest of the model, and light does not bend through glass. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E mode below does both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"high-quality\"\u003EHigh quality\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMode\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E for a picture that follows light the way a camera sees it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Elight bounces between surfaces, so a room lit through a window, or by a lamp, is lit all round and colors bleed onto their neighbors;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eglass is real glass: light bends through it and it reflects the model;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Emetal and polished surfaces reflect the rest of the model;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Elight panels light as panels, with soft shadows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is much slower than \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E: each pass traces every pixel&#039;s light again. The picture starts grainy and clears as samples add up, so start small (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe view&#039;s size\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E) and choose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime limit\u003C/span\u003E: when it runs out, the picture so far is kept. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E keeps it too. The first pass waits while the browser prepares the path tracer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the browser has WebGPU, the path tracer is CadCraft&#039;s own and runs on the graphics card; the line under the picture says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath traced on the graphics card (WebGPU)\u003C/span\u003E. After \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E or the time limit, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResume\u003C/span\u003E carries on with the same picture until it has all its samples; changing a setting or pressing \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E again starts afresh. Without WebGPU the picture is path traced with WebGL instead (the line says so) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResume\u003C/span\u003E is not offered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ELinux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox and most Chrome setups on Linux have no WebGPU yet, so high quality uses WebGL there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn this mode edges are not drawn, library textures show their color and roughness but not their bumps (a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#in-high-quality-pictures\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"in-high-quality-pictures\"\u003Ematerial made with nodes\u003C/a\u003E shows its normal and bumps), and a section cut is made on the model before the picture starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDenoise (clears the grain left after the samples)\u003C/span\u003E is on by default: when the picture is done, or stopped, the grain that is left is cleared by a neural network made for this (Intel Open Image Denoise&#039;s published network, run on your own computer: on the graphics card, else on the processor). A picture with few samples comes out clean; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe picture before denoising\u003C/span\u003E shows it as rendered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"render-in-the-view\"\u003ERender in the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E workspace runs the path tracer in the view itself: orbit, edit the model or change a light and it starts again at once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERegion\u003C/span\u003E: drag a rectangle and only it renders; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole view\u003C/span\u003E goes back. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFocus\u003C/span\u003E: click what should be sharp, and depth of field focuses there. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E sets how many of the view&#039;s pixels it renders (fewer is faster). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPause\u003C/span\u003E stops and goes on. It needs WebGPU: without it the workspace says so, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E still works.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skies-and-light-profiles-on-the-graphics-card\"\u003ESkies and light profiles on the graphics card\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith WebGPU, high quality has two more skies:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhysical sky\u003C/span\u003E works the daylight out from the sun&#039;s height: a deep blue overhead, a pale band at the horizon, and a sun that turns warmer and dimmer as it gets low. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHaze\u003C/span\u003E goes from a very clear day (2) to a hazy one (10).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESky picture (HDRI)\u003C/span\u003E lights the model with a panorama picture of a real place. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a picture…\u003C/span\u003E takes an equirectangular \u003Ccode\u003E.hdr\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.exr\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E turns it round the model and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPicture brightness\u003C/span\u003E makes it brighter or darker. The picture carries its own sun, so the model&#039;s sun is not added. It is kept only while the page is open: after reopening, choose it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach lamp in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELamps\u003C/span\u003E list can take a maker&#039;s light profile: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIES profile…\u003C/span\u003E reads an IES (LM-63) file and the lamp then throws its light the way that fitting does (a narrow downlight, a wall washer). Every lamp with that name uses it, and the lamp keeps its own brightness. Click the profile&#039;s name to stop using it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWindows of clear glass help the sky&#039;s light find its way into a room, so indoor pictures under a sky clear sooner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E mode and the WebGL path tracer show the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhysical sky\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESky picture (HDRI)\u003C/span\u003E as a clear sky and ignore light profiles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-camera\"\u003EThe camera\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera\u003C/span\u003E sets the picture up like a photograph:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExposure\u003C/span\u003E: keep the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness slider\u003C/span\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera settings\u003C/span\u003E and set the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAperture (f-stop)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShutter speed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EISO\u003C/span\u003E as on a camera; the EV next to them is the usual exposure value. f/16 at 1/100 s and ISO 100 matches a sunny day; a darker scene wants a wider aperture, a slower shutter or a higher ISO. This works in every mode.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth of field\u003C/span\u003E blurs what is nearer or farther than the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFocus distance (m)\u003C/span\u003E as a lens of that f-stop would (leave it empty to focus on what is in the middle of the view); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAperture shape\u003C/span\u003E gives out-of-focus highlights a round or bladed shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-point perspective (vertical lines stay vertical)\u003C/span\u003E levels the camera and shifts the picture, as an architectural tilt-shift lens does, so walls do not lean when you look up or down at a building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhite balance\u003C/span\u003E sets which light colour shows as white: lower (for example 3000 K) for warm lamp light, higher for shade and overcast days.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProjection\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E360° panorama\u003C/span\u003E makes a 2:1 picture of everything round the camera for panorama viewers; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E360° stereo panorama\u003C/span\u003E stacks a left-eye and a right-eye panorama for headsets; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECube map (6 faces)\u003C/span\u003E makes six square views in a 3 × 2 grid. The width comes from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProjection, depth of field, two-point perspective and white balance need the path tracer on the graphics card (WebGPU), so they show only in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"finish-the-picture\"\u003EFinish the picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPicture\u003C/span\u003E grades the high-quality picture like a photograph, and every control changes the finished picture at once, without rendering again:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELook (view transform)\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EACES filmic\u003C/span\u003E (the picture as before), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgX\u003C/span\u003E (softer, keeps colour in very bright light), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFilmic\u003C/span\u003E (a gentle film curve) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E (plain, bright light clips to white).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContrast\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight compression\u003C/span\u003E (brings back detail in very bright parts).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBloom\u003C/span\u003E (a soft glow round bright light), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlare\u003C/span\u003E with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlare streaks\u003C/span\u003E (star rays from the brightest points) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVignette\u003C/span\u003E (darker corners).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour table (.cube)…\u003C/span\u003E applies a colour grade from a \u003Ccode\u003E.cube\u003C/code\u003E file, with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour table strength\u003C/span\u003E; like a sky picture it is kept only while the page is open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-picture\"\u003ESave the picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E next to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E chooses the file: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJPEG\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWebP\u003C/span\u003E for sharing (JPEG and WebP only where the browser can write them), and after a high-quality render on the graphics card also:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG, 16 bits\u003C/span\u003E: the graded picture with finer steps between shades, for printing or further editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEXR, every layer (32-bit float)\u003C/span\u003E: the picture as rendered (linear light, no grade) with every render element and light group as its own layer, for compositing programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender file (.ccrender, to grade again later)\u003C/span\u003E: everything the dialog needs to mix and grade the picture again. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a render file…\u003C/span\u003E brings it back, with its mix and its grade, without rendering.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mix-the-lights-after-the-render\"\u003EMix the lights after the render\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELight mixing (change each light&#039;s colour and brightness after the render)\u003C/span\u003E before you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E. When the picture is done (or stopped), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMix the picture\u003C/span\u003E lists its lights in groups: the sky, the sun, and the lamps by name (every lamp with the same name is one group). Switch a group off, give it a colour, or make it brighter or darker, and the picture changes at once, without rendering again. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave mix\u003C/span\u003E keeps the mix under a name to choose again later; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs rendered\u003C/span\u003E goes back to the picture as it was made. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E saves the mixed picture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender elements (albedo, normals, depth, diffuse, specular, transmission, emission, IDs)\u003C/span\u003E keeps the picture&#039;s parts as well. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E switches the view to one of them: the surface colours (albedo), the normals, the depth, the diffuse, specular and transmitted light (each direct and indirect), what glows and the sky, and a colour per object or per material for making masks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth keep extra layers on the graphics card. At large sizes a graphics card may not have room; the dialog says so and a smaller size works.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-render-queue\"\u003EThe render queue\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a high-quality setup, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this picture to the queue\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender queue\u003C/span\u003E keeps the picture as it is set now; the video dialog&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this animation to the queue\u003C/span\u003E does the same for a whole animation, path traced frame by frame. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart the queue\u003C/span\u003E renders them one after another on this computer, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E beside each finished one downloads it. Closing the page keeps the queue: open the same model and it goes on where it stopped. The queue needs WebGPU.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fog-smoke-noise-and-caustics\"\u003EFog, smoke, noise and caustics\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese live in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFog, smoke and noise\u003C/span\u003E and work in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality\u003C/span\u003E on the graphics card; the other engines leave them out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFog and haze\u003C/span\u003E fills the air up to a height with a mist that the sun, the sky and lamps light. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow far you can see, in meters\u003C/span\u003E sets how thick it is (about 100 m is a light haze, 20 m a thick fog), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFog height above the ground, in meters\u003C/span\u003E is where it ends, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELight shafts\u003C/span\u003E is how much the mist glows toward the light. Sunlight leaves the fog at its top, so where sun comes through a window or between buildings you see it as shafts of light.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmoke or cloud\u003C/span\u003E adds one volume of smoke or cloud lit like the fog. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E is a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmoke column\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom a volume file (.ccvol)\u003C/span\u003E, CadCraft&#039;s own grid format. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVariation\u003C/span\u003E change a made cloud or column, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize of its longest side, in meters (0 = the size of the model)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove X (m)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove Y (m)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove Z (m)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E place it (it starts in the middle of the model), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness\u003C/span\u003E makes it denser, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward glow\u003C/span\u003E makes it brighter toward the light, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E tints it. A volume file is kept only while the page is open, like a sky picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop early where the picture is clean (adaptive sampling)\u003C/span\u003E gives no more samples to pixels that have settled, so the rest of the picture finishes sooner. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENoise to accept\u003C/span\u003E is how much grain is left when a pixel stops. The line under the picture says how many samples a pixel got on average.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECaustics (light bent by glass or bounced by mirrors)\u003C/span\u003E traces light from the lamps and the sun, so the bright patch under a glass ball or a mirror&#039;s light on a wall comes out clean instead of grainy. It is off while fog, smoke, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMix the lights after the render\u003C/span\u003E or render elements are on, and it is made for pictures, not for 360° pictures, animations or the Render workspace.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-long-it-takes\"\u003EHow long it takes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is made on your own computer, by its graphics card; nothing is uploaded. How long it takes depends on that card. Time grows with the passes and the pixels: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E has 16 times the passes of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E, and a 4K picture has about four times the pixels of Full HD. A computer without a graphics card still renders, more slowly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the dialog says the browser can&#039;t render pictures, the browser has no WebGL 2. Update it, or turn on hardware acceleration in its settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Render a picture… makes a picture of the view that looks like daylight rather than a drawing: a sky, the sun where your model's place and date put it, soft shadows, light from the whole sky that darkens corners and gaps, and colors that can look like glass, metal or gloss paint. The model is not changed. Render the view Set up the view the way you want the picture: orbit, zoom, pick a scene, hide what should not show. The picture uses the view's own camera. Click Export in the File group, then Render a picture…. Choose the Size and the Quality, then click Render. The picture fills in as it renders and gets smoother with each pass. Stop (or Esc) ends it early and keeps the picture so far. Click Save to download it (a PNG unless Save as says otherwise). Start with Draft at The view's size to check the camera and the look, then render Good or Best at a bigger size for the final picture. Settings Setting What it does Size The view's size, twice the view's size, Full HD, 4K or a square. Fixed sizes keep the view's camera and show more or less at the sides Mode Fast, or High quality (path traced) (below) with its Time limit Quality How many passes (or, in high quality, samples) are averaged: Draft 16, Good 64, Best 256. More passes give smoother shadows and edges and take longer Sky Clear sky, Overcast (soft light, faint shadows), Dusk (low, warm light) or White studio (a plain white background) Sun The sun comes from the date, time and place in Style › sun. Tick Use another date and time for this picture to try another time without changing the model's style Brightness Lighter or darker, in steps (stops) Ground under the model A ground that takes the shadows and fades into the horizon. Turn it off for a model that floats Draw edges Draws the model's edges thinly over the picture, for a drawn look When the sun is below the horizon at the chosen time it is night: the sky goes dark and the model's lamps light it. The dialog says so. The settings are saved in the .modelcraft file, so the next picture of the same model starts where you left off. Make colors look like glass or metal Under Materials, every color on screen has a look: Matte, Satin, Glossy, Metal, Polished metal, Glass or Frosted glass. Unpainted faces are the faces with no color of their own. To make windows glass, paint the window panes one color, then set that color to Glass. Glass lets the sky and what is behind it show through, tinted by its color, and reflects the sky. In the Fast mode, glass and metal reflect the sky, not the rest of the model, and light does not bend through glass. The High quality (path traced) mode below does both. High quality Choose Mode › High quality (path traced) for a picture that follows light the way a camera sees it: light bounces between surfaces, so a room lit through a window, or by a lamp, is lit all round and colors bleed onto their neighbors; glass is real glass: light bends through it and it reflects the model; metal and polished surfaces reflect the rest of the model; light panels light as panels, with soft shadows. It is much slower than Fast: each pass traces every pixel's light again. The picture starts grainy and clears as samples add up, so start small (The view's size, Draft) and choose a Time limit: when it runs out, the picture so far is kept. Stop keeps it too. The first pass waits while the browser prepares the path tracer. Where the browser has WebGPU, the path tracer is CadCraft's own and runs on the graphics card; the line under the picture says Path traced on the graphics card (WebGPU). After Stop or the time limit, Resume carries on with the same picture until it has all its samples; changing a setting or pressing Render again starts afresh. Without WebGPU the picture is path traced with WebGL instead (the line says so) and Resume is not offered. Firefox and most Chrome setups on Linux have no WebGPU yet, so high quality uses WebGL there. In this mode edges are not drawn, library textures show their color and roughness but not their bumps (a material made with nodes shows its normal and bumps), and a section cut is made on the model before the picture starts. Denoise (clears the grain left after the samples) is on by default: when the picture is done, or stopped, the grain that is left is cleared by a neural network made for this (Intel Open Image Denoise's published network, run on your own computer: on the graphics card, else on the processor). A picture with few samples comes out clean; Show › The picture before denoising shows it as rendered. Render in the view The Render workspace runs the path tracer in the view itself: orbit, edit the model or change a light and it starts again at once. Region: drag a rectangle and only it renders; Whole view goes back. Focus: click what should be sharp, and depth of field focuses there. Detail sets how many of the view's pixels it renders (fewer is faster). Pause stops and goes on. It needs WebGPU: without it the workspace says so, and Export › Render a picture… still works. Skies and light profiles on the graphics card With WebGPU, high quality has two more skies: Physical sky works the daylight out from the sun's height: a deep blue overhead, a pale band at the horizon, and a sun that turns warmer and dimmer as it gets low. Haze goes from a very clear day (2) to a hazy one (10). Sky picture (HDRI) lights the model with a panorama picture of a real place. Choose a picture… takes an equirectangular .hdr or .exr file; Turn turns it round the model and Picture brightness makes it brighter or darker. The picture carries its own sun, so the model's sun is not added. It is kept only while the page is open: after reopening, choose it again. Each lamp in the Lamps list can take a maker's light profile: IES profile… reads an IES (LM-63) file and the lamp then throws its light the way that fitting does (a narrow downlight, a wall washer). Every lamp with that name uses it, and the lamp keeps its own brightness. Click the profile's name to stop using it. Windows of clear glass help the sky's light find its way into a room, so indoor pictures under a sky clear sooner. The Fast mode and the WebGL path tracer show the Physical sky and Sky picture (HDRI) as a clear sky and ignore light profiles. The camera Camera sets the picture up like a photograph: Exposure: keep the Brightness slider, or choose Camera settings and set the Aperture (f-stop), Shutter speed and ISO as on a camera; the EV next to them is the usual exposure value. f/16 at 1/100 s and ISO 100 matches a sunny day; a darker scene wants a wider aperture, a slower shutter or a higher ISO. This works in every mode. Depth of field blurs what is nearer or farther than the Focus distance (m) as a lens of that f-stop would (leave it empty to focus on what is in the middle of the view); Aperture shape gives out-of-focus highlights a round or bladed shape. Two-point perspective (vertical lines stay vertical) levels the camera and shifts the picture, as an architectural tilt-shift lens does, so walls do not lean when you look up or down at a building. White balance sets which light colour shows as white: lower (for example 3000 K) for warm lamp light, higher for shade and overcast days. Projection: 360° panorama makes a 2:1 picture of everything round the camera for panorama viewers; 360° stereo panorama stacks a left-eye and a right-eye panorama for headsets; Cube map (6 faces) makes six square views in a 3 × 2 grid. The width comes from Size. Projection, depth of field, two-point perspective and white balance need the path tracer on the graphics card (WebGPU), so they show only in High quality. Finish the picture Picture grades the high-quality picture like a photograph, and every control changes the finished picture at once, without rendering again: Look (view transform): ACES filmic (the picture as before), AgX (softer, keeps colour in very bright light), Filmic (a gentle film curve) or Standard (plain, bright light clips to white). Contrast and Highlight compression (brings back detail in very bright parts). Bloom (a soft glow round bright light), Glare with its Glare streaks (star rays from the brightest points) and Vignette (darker corners). Colour table (.cube)… applies a colour grade from a .cube file, with its Colour table strength; like a sky picture it is kept only while the page is open. Save the picture Save as next to Save chooses the file: PNG, JPEG or WebP for sharing (JPEG and WebP only where the browser can write them), and after a high-quality render on the graphics card also: PNG, 16 bits: the graded picture with finer steps between shades, for printing or further editing. EXR, every layer (32-bit float): the picture as rendered (linear light, no grade) with every render element and light group as its own layer, for compositing programs. Render file (.ccrender, to grade again later): everything the dialog needs to mix and grade the picture again. Open a render file… brings it back, with its mix and its grade, without rendering. Mix the lights after the render Turn on Light mixing (change each light's colour and brightness after the render) before you press Render. When the picture is done (or stopped), Mix the picture lists its lights in groups: the sky, the sun, and the lamps by name (every lamp with the same name is one group). Switch a group off, give it a colour, or make it brighter or darker, and the picture changes at once, without rendering again. Save mix keeps the mix under a name to choose again later; As rendered goes back to the picture as it was made. Save saves the mixed picture. Render elements (albedo, normals, depth, diffuse, specular, transmission, emission, IDs) keeps the picture's parts as well. Show switches the view to one of them: the surface colours (albedo), the normals, the depth, the diffuse, specular and transmitted light (each direct and indirect), what glows and the sky, and a colour per object or per material for making masks. Both keep extra layers on the graphics card. At large sizes a graphics card may not have room; the dialog says so and a smaller size works. The render queue After a high-quality setup, Add this picture to the queue under Render queue keeps the picture as it is set now; the video dialog's Add this animation to the queue does the same for a whole animation, path traced frame by frame. Start the queue renders them one after another on this computer, and Save beside each finished one downloads it. Closing the page keeps the queue: open the same model and it goes on where it stopped. The queue needs WebGPU. Fog, smoke, noise and caustics These live in Fog, smoke and noise and work in High quality on the graphics card; the other engines leave them out. Fog and haze fills the air up to a height with a mist that the sun, the sky and lamps light. How far you can see, in meters sets how thick it is (about 100 m is a light haze, 20 m a thick fog), Fog height above the ground, in meters is where it ends, and Light shafts is how much the mist glows toward the light. Sunlight leaves the fog at its top, so where sun comes through a window or between buildings you see it as shafts of light. Smoke or cloud adds one volume of smoke or cloud lit like the fog. Kind is a Cloud, a Smoke column or From a volume file (.ccvol), CadCraft's own grid format. Detail and Variation change a made cloud or column, Size of its longest side, in meters (0 = the size of the model), Move X (m), Move Y (m), Move Z (m) and Turn place it (it starts in the middle of the model), Thickness makes it denser, Forward glow makes it brighter toward the light, and Color tints it. A volume file is kept only while the page is open, like a sky picture. Stop early where the picture is clean (adaptive sampling) gives no more samples to pixels that have settled, so the rest of the picture finishes sooner. Noise to accept is how much grain is left when a pixel stops. The line under the picture says how many samples a pixel got on average. Caustics (light bent by glass or bounced by mirrors) traces light from the lamps and the sun, so the bright patch under a glass ball or a mirror's light on a wall comes out clean instead of grainy. It is off while fog, smoke, Mix the lights after the render or render elements are on, and it is made for pictures, not for 360° pictures, animations or the Render workspace. How long it takes The picture is made on your own computer, by its graphics card; nothing is uploaded. How long it takes depends on that card. Time grows with the passes and the pixels: Best has 16 times the passes of Draft, and a 4K picture has about four times the pixels of Full HD. A computer without a graphics card still renders, more slowly. If the dialog says the browser can't render pictures, the browser has no WebGL 2. Update it, or turn on hardware acceleration in its settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render-engines","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Choose which program draws the picture","summary":"The Engine choice in the Render dialog: our own two engines, other programs in CadCraft Studio, and what the web does instead.","section":"tasks","order":113,"keywords":["render","engine","renderer","render engine","plug-in","plugin","studio","outside program","drawn by","queue","add-on engine"],"headings":[{"id":"how-the-choice-behaves","text":"How the choice behaves","level":2},{"id":"what-every-engine-is-given","text":"What every engine is given","level":2},{"id":"the-render-queue","text":"The render queue","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E has an \u003Cstrong\u003EEngine\u003C/strong\u003E choice above Mode. Our own two engines are always there and work everywhere; add-ons can add more, and in CadCraft Studio the list also holds the programs that take the scene over.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt-in: Fast\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe quick still: daylight, soft shadows, glass and metal looks, in seconds\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser and Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt-in: Path tracer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELight bouncing between surfaces on your graphics card or processor, the render queue and the other computers on your network\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser and Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn add-on&#039;s engine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt draws and hands the picture back: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-other-program\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-other-program\"\u003EDraw the picture with another program\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser (an engine written in JavaScript) or Studio (an engine that runs a program)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA program that takes the scene over\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStudio puts the scene in its folder and keeps it in step\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStudio\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen in …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaves the scene and opens it in a program; nothing comes back: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-open-in\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-open-in\"\u003EOpen the scene in another program\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe web saves the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-choice-behaves\"\u003EHow the choice behaves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPicking Fast or the Path tracer changes Mode with it, and the other way round. Nothing else about the dialog changes for people who never use another engine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn engine that is not available stays in the list with the reason (&quot;not found&quot;, &quot;needs a newer version&quot;); you cannot pick it. In the browser an engine that only runs in Studio shows &quot;saves a file&quot;: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E then saves the scene and its settings to a file you take to that program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choice is saved with the file&#039;s render settings, and the engine you chose last is the starting engine for files that have no choice of their own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA picture from another program says which program drew it and its version, in the dialog and in the render queue. The promises of the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender\u003C/a\u003E page cover \u003Cstrong\u003Eour own\u003C/strong\u003E engines only: another program lights, tones and samples in its own way, so two pictures of one scene are alike in camera, sun, lamps and exposure, not pixel for pixel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-every-engine-is-given\"\u003EWhat every engine is given\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scene goes to another program as a \u003Cstrong\u003Epackage\u003C/strong\u003E: the model as a USD file (the same file \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003ESave as USD\u003C/a\u003E writes), and a settings file that holds what a USD importer cannot be trusted with: the exact material values, the lamps that are on with their power and light profiles, the sun, the sky, the exposure and the camera as the dialog would draw it. Nothing is uploaded anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-render-queue\"\u003EThe render queue\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA picture or an animation for another program can wait in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#the-render-queue\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"the-render-queue\"\u003Ethe render queue\u003C/a\u003E like our own; its job remembers which engine it is for. An animation goes to the program in one run.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Render a picture… has an Engine choice above Mode. Our own two engines are always there and work everywhere; add-ons can add more, and in CadCraft Studio the list also holds the programs that take the scene over. Engine What it does Where Built-in: Fast The quick still: daylight, soft shadows, glass and metal looks, in seconds The browser and Studio Built-in: Path tracer Light bouncing between surfaces on your graphics card or processor, the render queue and the other computers on your network The browser and Studio An add-on's engine It draws and hands the picture back: see Draw the picture with another program The browser (an engine written in JavaScript) or Studio (an engine that runs a program) A program that takes the scene over Studio puts the scene in its folder and keeps it in step Studio Open in … Saves the scene and opens it in a program; nothing comes back: see Open the scene in another program The web saves the file How the choice behaves Picking Fast or the Path tracer changes Mode with it, and the other way round. Nothing else about the dialog changes for people who never use another engine. An engine that is not available stays in the list with the reason (\"not found\", \"needs a newer version\"); you cannot pick it. In the browser an engine that only runs in Studio shows \"saves a file\": Render a picture… then saves the scene and its settings to a file you take to that program. The choice is saved with the file's render settings, and the engine you chose last is the starting engine for files that have no choice of their own. A picture from another program says which program drew it and its version, in the dialog and in the render queue. The promises of the Render page cover our own engines only: another program lights, tones and samples in its own way, so two pictures of one scene are alike in camera, sun, lamps and exposure, not pixel for pixel. What every engine is given The scene goes to another program as a package: the model as a USD file (the same file Save as USD writes), and a settings file that holds what a USD importer cannot be trusted with: the exact material values, the lamps that are on with their power and light profiles, the sun, the sky, the exposure and the camera as the dialog would draw it. Nothing is uploaded anywhere. The render queue A picture or an animation for another program can wait in the render queue like our own; its job remembers which engine it is for. An animation goes to the program in one run.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lights","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Light a model with lamps","summary":"Put lamps, spotlights and light panels in a model so rendered pictures can show rooms at night or lit interiors, with soft shadows.","section":"tasks","order":113,"keywords":["light","lights","lamp","lamps","lighting","bulb","ceiling light","spotlight","downlight","light panel","area light","lumens","brightness","color temperature","warm","night","interior","render"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-lamp","text":"Add a lamp","level":2},{"id":"change-a-lamp","text":"Change a lamp","level":2},{"id":"pictures-at-night","text":"Pictures at night","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELamps light \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Erendered pictures\u003C/a\u003E. They don&#039;t change the 3D view, where each lamp shows as a small mark in its own color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-lamp\"\u003EAdd a lamp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELights\u003C/span\u003E section on the right, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELamp\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpotlight\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELight panel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes. Snapping works as when drawing.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a ceiling or wall a \u003Cstrong\u003Elamp\u003C/strong\u003E hangs 25 cm off the face, on the side you are looking from; a \u003Cstrong\u003Elight panel\u003C/strong\u003E lies on the face and shines away from it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Espotlight\u003C/strong\u003E takes two clicks: where it is, then what it points at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new lamp is selected in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELights\u003C/span\u003E, ready to change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-lamp\"\u003EChange a lamp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a lamp&#039;s name in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELights\u003C/span\u003E, or its mark in the view with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness (lumens)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow much light it gives. A household bulb is about 800 lumens; a bright ceiling panel 3000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWarm\u003C/span\u003E (a home bulb), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENeutral\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDaylight\u003C/span\u003E, or any color\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam (degrees)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA spotlight&#039;s cone, edge to edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBulb size\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBigger bulbs and panels give softer shadows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERed (x)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGreen (y)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere it is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tick beside a lamp&#039;s name switches it on or off. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete the light\u003C/span\u003E removes it. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it. Lamps are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pictures-at-night\"\u003EPictures at night\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another date and time for this picture\u003C/span\u003E and pick an evening hour. With the sun down the sky goes dark and only the lamps light the model. Raise \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog if the picture is too dark, or add lamps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a room seen from inside, put the camera inside the room (orbit and zoom in, or a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E), add a lamp on the ceiling and render at night. In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E mode corners away from a lamp stay darker than in a photo; the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#high-quality\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"high-quality\"\u003Ehigh-quality mode\u003C/a\u003E lets the light bounce off the walls.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Lamps light rendered pictures. They don't change the 3D view, where each lamp shows as a small mark in its own color. Add a lamp In the Lights section on the right, click Lamp, Spotlight or Light panel. Click where it goes. Snapping works as when drawing. On a ceiling or wall a lamp hangs 25 cm off the face, on the side you are looking from; a light panel lies on the face and shines away from it. A spotlight takes two clicks: where it is, then what it points at. The new lamp is selected in Lights, ready to change. Change a lamp Click a lamp's name in Lights, or its mark in the view with Select. Then: Setting What it does Brightness (lumens) How much light it gives. A household bulb is about 800 lumens; a bright ceiling panel 3000 Color Warm (a home bulb), Neutral or Daylight, or any color Beam (degrees) A spotlight's cone, edge to edge Bulb size, Width, Depth Bigger bulbs and panels give softer shadows Red (x), Green (y), Height Where it is The tick beside a lamp's name switches it on or off. Delete the light removes it. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Lamps are saved in the .modelcraft file. Pictures at night In Render a picture…, tick Use another date and time for this picture and pick an evening hour. With the sun down the sky goes dark and only the lamps light the model. Raise Brightness in the dialog if the picture is too dark, or add lamps. For a room seen from inside, put the camera inside the room (orbit and zoom in, or a scene), add a lamp on the ceiling and render at night. In the Fast mode corners away from a lamp stay darker than in a photo; the high-quality mode lets the light bounce off the walls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"live-view","scope":"modelcraft","title":"The live view","summary":"A window beside the model that shows it in the real-time look, with sun and shadows, and follows every change you make; walk through it, and set the time of day and the month.","section":"tasks","order":113,"keywords":["share","panorama","360","tour","virtual tour","standalone viewer","offline viewer","send to a client","video","walkthrough video","scatter","scattering","grass","meadow","forest","rocks","plants","trees","crowd","paint plants","live view","real time","realtime","real-time render","walkthrough","walk","walk through","first person","presentation","client","second screen","second window","monitor","time of day","sun","season","month","shadows","ambient occlusion","reflections","bounce light","global illumination","webgpu","webgl"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"the-controls","text":"The controls","level":2},{"id":"walk-through-the-model","text":"Walk through the model","level":2},{"id":"scatter-plants-rocks-and-people","text":"Scatter plants, rocks and people","level":2},{"id":"share-panoramas-tours-a-viewer-a-video","text":"Share: panoramas, tours, a viewer, a video","level":2},{"id":"quality","text":"Quality","level":2},{"id":"bounce-light","text":"Bounce light","level":2},{"id":"brightness-and-colours","text":"Brightness and colours","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive view\u003C/span\u003E opens a panel over the app that shows the model in the real-time look: the sky and the sun from your model&#039;s place and date, the sun&#039;s shadows, the looks and materials you gave the faces, lamps, and light bounced off walls and floors. It follows what you do: turn the view and it turns too, change the model and it shows the change a moment later. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive view\u003C/span\u003E. The panel opens in the bottom right corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag its title bar to move it, pull its corner to make it bigger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew window\u003C/span\u003E moves it to a window of its own, for a second screen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive view in a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens it there straight away. If your browser blocks pop-up windows, it opens as the panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E closes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-controls\"\u003EThe controls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EControl\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow the view\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn: the live view looks where ModelCraft&#039;s view looks. Walking turns it off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWalk through the model (below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGravity\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhile walking: stand on floors and climb stairs. Off: fly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hour of the day, from 05:00 to 21:00. The sun moves as you drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMonth\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe month (the 21st of it), so you can see the sun in winter and summer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor this computer\u003C/span\u003E (what \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/your-machine\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/your-machine\"\u003Eyour machine\u003C/a\u003E gets), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMedium\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line at the bottom says what draws the view (WebGPU or WebGL 2), the quality, and the bounce light.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk-through-the-model\"\u003EWalk through the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the main view to where you want to stand and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E. You stand on the floor below the view&#039;s eye point, 1.6 m tall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the live view, then use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the arrow keys. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run. Drag to look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESteps up to 40 cm are climbed (stairs); anything higher is a wall. Walls stop you and you slide along them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGravity\u003C/span\u003E off you fly: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes up, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops walking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scatter-plants-rocks-and-people\"\u003EScatter plants, rocks and people\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScatter\u003C/span\u003E in the live view&#039;s bar opens the scatter panel beside the picture. A scatter set spreads items from the entourage library (trees, shrubs, grass, flowers, rocks, people, cars) over part of the model. The items are drawn in the live view only: they are not geometry in the model, and the main view does not show them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFill faces:\u003C/strong\u003E select faces (or a group, such as a terrain) in ModelCraft, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill selection\u003C/span\u003E. The set covers them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaint:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E, then drag over the live view. With faces selected the paint lands only on them; with nothing selected it lands on anything below the brush, the ground included. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrush size\u003C/span\u003E sets the brush; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E takes painted spots away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick what to scatter. The number beside each is its weight: 3 and 1 makes three of the first for every one of the second.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EControl\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPer m²\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many items on each square metre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep away (m)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo items closer than this to other objects, and none under a roof or a table. 0 turns it off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn (°)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far each item is turned at random about the vertical\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteepest (°)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo items where the surface is steeper\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe smallest and largest size, as a factor of the item&#039;s own size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight from, to (m)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly between these heights (blank = any)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELean with the surface\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 keeps items upright; 1 stands them square to a slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShuffle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same seed always gives the same scatter; Shuffle picks another\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sets are saved in the .modelcraft file and follow the faces they cover when you change them. How many items the live view draws at most is set by \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/your-machine\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/your-machine\"\u003Eyour machine\u003C/a\u003E&#039;s level: 20 000 for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProcessor only\u003C/span\u003E, 100 000 for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics card, basic\u003C/span\u003E, a million for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics card, high\u003C/span\u003E. The panel says how many are placed of how many the density asks for: fewer are placed when the budget is reached, or where objects keep them out. Items close to the camera are drawn as themselves; far ones as simple shapes in their colours.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScattered items are in the live view (and its walk, panoramas and the standalone viewer). Render a picture does not draw them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-panoramas-tours-a-viewer-a-video\"\u003EShare: panoramas, tours, a viewer, a video\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare\u003C/span\u003E in the live view&#039;s bar makes files from the live view. Everything is made on this computer; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E360° panorama\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA 360° picture from where the view stands (or where you are walking), as an equirectangular PNG that panorama viewers and most photo sites open\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this spot to the tour\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the tour\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne web page with a panorama at each spot and arrows to the spots each one can see (a wall in between hides the arrow)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a viewer\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne web page with the model in this real-time look: turn it, walk through it, jump to its saved scenes. It opens offline in a browser with WebGPU or WebGL 2, from a folder, a USB stick or an email, with no install and no account, and it loads nothing from the internet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVideo of the path\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe timeline&#039;s camera path (or the saved scenes in order) as a video in this real-time look, at the live view&#039;s size: WebM or MP4 where the browser can make them, else numbered pictures in a ZIP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panorama&#039;s size follows the quality: 1024, 2048 or 4096 pixels wide on Low, Medium and High. A viewer file is about 5 MB plus the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tour and the viewer are made for showing, not editing: they keep the model as it is when you save them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quality\"\u003EQuality\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELow\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMedium\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHigh\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESun shadows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eone shadow map\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethree cascades\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efour cascades\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAmbient occlusion (contact shadows)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E✓\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E✓, finer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReflections in metal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E✓\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBloom (when Render a picture sets it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E✓\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E✓\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges smoothed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFXAA\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eover several frames (TAA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eover several frames (TAA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBounce light\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecoarse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efiner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efinest\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor this computer\u003C/span\u003E picks Low on a computer with no usable graphics card, Medium with a built-in graphics chip, High with a separate graphics card. Low is meant for an 8 GB laptop with no graphics card.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bounce-light\"\u003EBounce light\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELight that bounces off a sunlit wall onto the floor, and the darker inside of a room, are worked out by the same path tracer that makes \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Erendered pictures\u003C/a\u003E: it renders a small panorama at points through the model, in the background on every processor core. While it works the bottom line says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBounce light: 40 %\u003C/span\u003E; when it is ready the view takes it in. After a change it starts again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is an approximation for a live view, not a rendered picture: small details between the points get the bounce light of the points near them. For a finished picture use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"brightness-and-colours\"\u003EBrightness and colours\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe live view uses the same brightness, white balance, contrast and view transform (ACES, AgX, filmic or standard) as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E: set them there and the live view follows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrowsers differ. Where the browser has WebGPU (Chrome and Edge on Windows, macOS and ChromeOS, Safari 26) the live view uses it; elsewhere (most browsers on Linux, older Apple systems) it uses WebGL 2 and looks the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Export › Live view opens a panel over the app that shows the model in the real-time look: the sky and the sun from your model's place and date, the sun's shadows, the looks and materials you gave the faces, lamps, and light bounced off walls and floors. It follows what you do: turn the view and it turns too, change the model and it shows the change a moment later. Nothing is uploaded. Open it Choose Export › Live view. The panel opens in the bottom right corner. Drag its title bar to move it, pull its corner to make it bigger. New window moves it to a window of its own, for a second screen. Export › Live view in a new window opens it there straight away. If your browser blocks pop-up windows, it opens as the panel. Close closes it. The controls Control What it does Follow the view On: the live view looks where ModelCraft's view looks. Walking turns it off Walk Walk through the model (below) Gravity While walking: stand on floors and climb stairs. Off: fly Time The hour of the day, from 05:00 to 21:00. The sun moves as you drag Month The month (the 21st of it), so you can see the sun in winter and summer Quality For this computer (what your machine gets), or Low, Medium or High The line at the bottom says what draws the view (WebGPU or WebGL 2), the quality, and the bounce light. Walk through the model Turn the main view to where you want to stand and choose Walk. You stand on the floor below the view's eye point, 1.6 m tall. Click the live view, then use W A S D or the arrow keys. Hold Shift to run. Drag to look round. Steps up to 40 cm are climbed (stairs); anything higher is a wall. Walls stop you and you slide along them. With Gravity off you fly: E goes up, Q down. Esc stops walking. Scatter plants, rocks and people Scatter in the live view's bar opens the scatter panel beside the picture. A scatter set spreads items from the entourage library (trees, shrubs, grass, flowers, rocks, people, cars) over part of the model. The items are drawn in the live view only: they are not geometry in the model, and the main view does not show them. Fill faces: select faces (or a group, such as a terrain) in ModelCraft, then choose Fill selection. The set covers them. Paint: choose Paint, then drag over the live view. With faces selected the paint lands only on them; with nothing selected it lands on anything below the brush, the ground included. Brush size sets the brush; Eraser takes painted spots away. Tick what to scatter. The number beside each is its weight: 3 and 1 makes three of the first for every one of the second. Control What it does Per m² How many items on each square metre Keep away (m) No items closer than this to other objects, and none under a roof or a table. 0 turns it off Turn (°) How far each item is turned at random about the vertical Steepest (°) No items where the surface is steeper Size The smallest and largest size, as a factor of the item's own size Height from, to (m) Only between these heights (blank = any) Lean with the surface 0 keeps items upright; 1 stands them square to a slope Seed, Shuffle The same seed always gives the same scatter; Shuffle picks another The sets are saved in the .modelcraft file and follow the faces they cover when you change them. How many items the live view draws at most is set by your machine's level: 20 000 for Processor only, 100 000 for Graphics card, basic, a million for Graphics card, high. The panel says how many are placed of how many the density asks for: fewer are placed when the budget is reached, or where objects keep them out. Items close to the camera are drawn as themselves; far ones as simple shapes in their colours. Scattered items are in the live view (and its walk, panoramas and the standalone viewer). Render a picture does not draw them yet. Share: panoramas, tours, a viewer, a video Share in the live view's bar makes files from the live view. Everything is made on this computer; nothing is uploaded. Choice What you get 360° panorama A 360° picture from where the view stands (or where you are walking), as an equirectangular PNG that panorama viewers and most photo sites open Add this spot to the tour, then Save the tour One web page with a panorama at each spot and arrows to the spots each one can see (a wall in between hides the arrow) Save a viewer One web page with the model in this real-time look: turn it, walk through it, jump to its saved scenes. It opens offline in a browser with WebGPU or WebGL 2, from a folder, a USB stick or an email, with no install and no account, and it loads nothing from the internet Video of the path The timeline's camera path (or the saved scenes in order) as a video in this real-time look, at the live view's size: WebM or MP4 where the browser can make them, else numbered pictures in a ZIP The panorama's size follows the quality: 1024, 2048 or 4096 pixels wide on Low, Medium and High. A viewer file is about 5 MB plus the model. The tour and the viewer are made for showing, not editing: they keep the model as it is when you save them. Quality Low Medium High Sun shadows one shadow map three cascades four cascades Ambient occlusion (contact shadows) ✓ ✓, finer Reflections in metal ✓ Bloom (when Render a picture sets it) ✓ ✓ Edges smoothed FXAA over several frames (TAA) over several frames (TAA) Bounce light coarse finer finest For this computer picks Low on a computer with no usable graphics card, Medium with a built-in graphics chip, High with a separate graphics card. Low is meant for an 8 GB laptop with no graphics card. Bounce light Light that bounces off a sunlit wall onto the floor, and the darker inside of a room, are worked out by the same path tracer that makes rendered pictures: it renders a small panorama at points through the model, in the background on every processor core. While it works the bottom line says Bounce light: 40 %; when it is ready the view takes it in. After a change it starts again. This is an approximation for a live view, not a rendered picture: small details between the points get the bounce light of the points near them. For a finished picture use Render a picture. Brightness and colours The live view uses the same brightness, white balance, contrast and view transform (ACES, AgX, filmic or standard) as Render a picture: set them there and the live view follows. Browsers differ. Where the browser has WebGPU (Chrome and Edge on Windows, macOS and ChromeOS, Safari 26) the live view uses it; elsewhere (most browsers on Linux, older Apple systems) it uses WebGL 2 and looks the same.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"animation","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Animate the camera and objects, and render a video","summary":"Set keys for the camera and for groups and components on a timeline, play them in the view, and render the animation to a WebM video with the renderer's sky, sun and materials.","section":"tasks","order":114,"keywords":["animation","animate","timeline","keyframe","keys","key frame","camera path","fly through","flythrough","walkthrough","walk through","video","movie","film","webm","render video","frames","frame rate","fps","playhead","auto key","ease","smooth","hold","scenes","png sequence","motion"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-timeline","text":"Open the timeline","level":2},{"id":"move-the-camera","text":"Move the camera","level":2},{"id":"move-a-group-or-component","text":"Move a group or component","level":2},{"id":"change-keys","text":"Change keys","level":2},{"id":"render-the-video","text":"Render the video","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Etimeline\u003C/strong\u003E moves the camera and any group or component over time. You set \u003Cstrong\u003Ekeys\u003C/strong\u003E (where something is at a given moment); ModelCraft moves it smoothly from key to key. Play it in the view, then render it to a video with the same sky, sun, materials and lamps as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003Ea rendered picture\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-timeline\"\u003EOpen the timeline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera\u003C/span\u003E group, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnimation timeline\u003C/span\u003E. The timeline opens under the view: the controls along the top, then a \u003Cstrong\u003ECamera\u003C/strong\u003E row and a row for each group or component that moves. The diamonds are keys; the red line is the \u003Cstrong\u003Eplayhead\u003C/strong\u003E, the moment shown in the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the timeline opens under the view as well. Its controls are one row: swipe it sideways to reach the rest; the button that closes the timeline stays at its end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the slider (or click in a row) to move the playhead. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E plays from the playhead; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious key\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENext key\u003C/span\u003E jump between keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-the-camera\"\u003EMove the camera\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the playhead to where the shot starts (0 s), set up the view, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey camera\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the playhead later (say 4 s), orbit, pan or walk the view to the next position, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey camera\u003C/span\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd as many keys as you like, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera passes smoothly through every key and starts and ends at rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlready have \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escenes\u003C/a\u003E? \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom scenes\u003C/span\u003E makes the camera&#039;s path from them: it holds at each scene, then moves to the next. If the timeline has no keys at all, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video…\u003C/span\u003E does this by itself, so two scenes or more are enough for a walkthrough video.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-group-or-component\"\u003EMove a group or component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the group or component (only whole groups and components move; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Emake a group\u003C/a\u003E first).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt 0 s, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey selected\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the playhead later, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\"\u003Emove, rotate or scale\u003C/a\u003E the group to where it should be, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey selected\u003C/span\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt slides, turns and grows between its keys. Turns take the short way round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAuto key\u003C/strong\u003E saves step 3&#039;s click: while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto key\u003C/span\u003E is on, a selected group or component you move, rotate or scale gets a key at the playhead by itself (and, the first time, a key at 0 s where it was).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving an animated group without a key only lasts until the playhead moves: the next time you play or scrub, it goes back where its keys put it. The status bar says so. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey selected\u003C/span\u003E to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-keys\"\u003EChange keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChoose a key\u003C/strong\u003E by clicking its diamond: the playhead goes to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a key\u003C/strong\u003E by dragging its diamond along its row, or with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (one frame; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, ten).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete key\u003C/span\u003E (the chosen key, or every key at the playhead) or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a chosen diamond.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHow it moves on\u003C/strong\u003E, for the chosen key: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E (a curve through the keys around it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteady\u003C/span\u003E (a straight line at one speed), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlow in and out\u003C/span\u003E (comes to rest at this key) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHold\u003C/span\u003E (stays put until the next key).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength (s)\u003C/strong\u003E sets how long the animation is; a key set after the end makes it longer. To shorten it, move or delete the keys past the new end first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003E×\u003C/strong\u003E on a group&#039;s row stops animating it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe timeline has its own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E: the model&#039;s undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) changes the model, not the keys. The animation is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file. Closing the timeline puts moving groups back at their first pose.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"render-the-video\"\u003ERender the video\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video\u003C/span\u003E on the timeline (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video…\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame rate\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality of each frame\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video\u003C/span\u003E. Each frame shows as it is made, with the time left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen it is done, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave video\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ends early; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave video\u003C/span\u003E then keeps the frames already made as a shorter video.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sky, sun, materials, lamps and brightness are the ones set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E: set them there first, checking a still. Each frame is rendered on this computer; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E480p, 720p HD, 1080p Full HD, or the view&#039;s own shape (up to 1920 pixels wide)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame rate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24, 25, 30 or 60 frames a second. Twice the frames take twice as long\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality of each frame\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick\u003C/span\u003E (4 passes), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E (16) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E (64): more passes give smoother shadows and edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWebM video\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMP4 video (H.264)\u003C/span\u003E where the browser can write it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG pictures, one per frame (ZIP)\u003C/span\u003E for video editors (and for browsers with no video encoder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 10-second video at 30 frames a second is 301 frames. Try \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick\u003C/span\u003E at 480p first to check the motion, then render \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E at a bigger size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMP4 video (H.264)\u003C/span\u003E is offered where the browser can write it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video\u003C/span\u003E uses the fast renderer; to path trace every frame, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this animation to the queue\u003C/span\u003E in the same dialog: the frames render in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#the-render-queue\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"the-render-queue\"\u003Erender queue\u003C/a\u003E, which needs WebGPU.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERendering a video needs a browser that encodes video itself (WebCodecs): recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari 17 do. Without it, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG pictures, one per frame (ZIP)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly whole groups and components move. Faces, edges, the sun&#039;s time and the style do not change over time yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003EScenes\u003C/a\u003E still records the view as it is on screen, in real time; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video\u003C/span\u003E renders each frame with sky, shadows and materials.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The timeline moves the camera and any group or component over time. You set keys (where something is at a given moment); ModelCraft moves it smoothly from key to key. Play it in the view, then render it to a video with the same sky, sun, materials and lamps as a rendered picture. Open the timeline Click Views in the Camera group, then Animation timeline. The timeline opens under the view: the controls along the top, then a Camera row and a row for each group or component that moves. The diamonds are keys; the red line is the playhead, the moment shown in the view. On a phone the timeline opens under the view as well. Its controls are one row: swipe it sideways to reach the rest; the button that closes the timeline stays at its end. Drag the slider (or click in a row) to move the playhead. Play plays from the playhead; Previous key and Next key jump between keys. Move the camera Move the playhead to where the shot starts (0 s), set up the view, and click Key camera. Move the playhead later (say 4 s), orbit, pan or walk the view to the next position, and click Key camera again. Add as many keys as you like, then Play. The camera passes smoothly through every key and starts and ends at rest. Already have scenes? From scenes makes the camera's path from them: it holds at each scene, then moves to the next. If the timeline has no keys at all, Render video… does this by itself, so two scenes or more are enough for a walkthrough video. Move a group or component Select the group or component (only whole groups and components move; make a group first). At 0 s, click Key selected. Move the playhead later, move, rotate or scale the group to where it should be, and click Key selected again. It slides, turns and grows between its keys. Turns take the short way round. Auto key saves step 3's click: while Auto key is on, a selected group or component you move, rotate or scale gets a key at the playhead by itself (and, the first time, a key at 0 s where it was). Moving an animated group without a key only lasts until the playhead moves: the next time you play or scrub, it goes back where its keys put it. The status bar says so. Press Key selected to keep it. Change keys Choose a key by clicking its diamond: the playhead goes to it. Move a key by dragging its diamond along its row, or with ← and → (one frame; with Shift, ten). Delete with Delete key (the chosen key, or every key at the playhead) or Delete on a chosen diamond. How it moves on, for the chosen key: Smooth (a curve through the keys around it), Steady (a straight line at one speed), Slow in and out (comes to rest at this key) or Hold (stays put until the next key). Length (s) sets how long the animation is; a key set after the end makes it longer. To shorten it, move or delete the keys past the new end first. The × on a group's row stops animating it. The timeline has its own Undo and Redo: the model's undo (Ctrl+Z) changes the model, not the keys. The animation is saved in the .modelcraft file. Closing the timeline puts moving groups back at their first pose. Render the video Click Render video on the timeline (or Export › Render video…). Choose the Size, the Frame rate, the Quality of each frame and the File. Click Render video. Each frame shows as it is made, with the time left. When it is done, click Save video. Stop (or Esc) ends early; Save video then keeps the frames already made as a shorter video. The sky, sun, materials, lamps and brightness are the ones set in Render a picture…: set them there first, checking a still. Each frame is rendered on this computer; nothing is uploaded. Setting What it does Size 480p, 720p HD, 1080p Full HD, or the view's own shape (up to 1920 pixels wide) Frame rate 24, 25, 30 or 60 frames a second. Twice the frames take twice as long Quality of each frame Quick (4 passes), Draft (16) or Good (64): more passes give smoother shadows and edges File WebM video, MP4 video (H.264) where the browser can write it, or PNG pictures, one per frame (ZIP) for video editors (and for browsers with no video encoder) A 10-second video at 30 frames a second is 301 frames. Try Quick at 480p first to check the motion, then render Draft or Good at a bigger size. WebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it. MP4 video (H.264) is offered where the browser can write it. Render video uses the fast renderer; to path trace every frame, choose Add this animation to the queue in the same dialog: the frames render in the render queue, which needs WebGPU. Rendering a video needs a browser that encodes video itself (WebCodecs): recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari 17 do. Without it, choose PNG pictures, one per frame (ZIP). Things to know Only whole groups and components move. Faces, edges, the sun's time and the style do not change over time yet. Export animation under Scenes still records the view as it is on screen, in real time; Render video renders each frame with sky, shadows and materials.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render-other-program","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Draw the picture with another program","summary":"Our own path tracer draws the Render picture; another program can draw it only through a render-engine add-on in CadCraft Studio. What is carried over, and why some programs are never offered.","section":"tasks","order":114,"keywords":["render","engine","outside program","add-on engine","studio","background","queue","usd","gltf","drawn by","blender","gpl"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-picture-with-an-add-on-s-engine","text":"Draw a picture with an add-on's engine","level":2},{"id":"what-is-carried-over","text":"What is carried over","level":2},{"id":"programs-cadcraft-never-runs","text":"Programs CadCraft never runs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe picture in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E is drawn by \u003Cstrong\u003Eour own engines\u003C/strong\u003E: Fast and the Path tracer, in the browser and in CadCraft Studio. Another program can draw it only when a \u003Cstrong\u003Erender-engine add-on\u003C/strong\u003E brings it: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-engine-addons\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-engine-addons\"\u003EAdd a render engine with an add-on\u003C/a\u003E. To take the scene to another program and keep working there, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-open-in\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-open-in\"\u003EOpen the scene in another program\u003C/a\u003E or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-unreal\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-unreal\"\u003ESend the scene to a game engine\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-picture-with-an-add-on-s-engine\"\u003EDraw a picture with an add-on&#039;s engine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Its engine appears in the Engine list of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose it. Its own settings show under the Engine list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E. In Studio, the first time an add-on runs a program, Studio asks you in its own dialog. Progress shows while it draws; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E ends it. The picture appears marked with the engine that drew it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn engine written in JavaScript runs in the browser too. An engine that runs a program on this computer runs only in Studio; in the browser it shows as a file to save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-carried-over\"\u003EWhat is carried over\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe engine gets a \u003Cstrong\u003Epackage\u003C/strong\u003E: the model as a USD file (and a glTF file when it asks for one), and a settings file with the exact material values, the sun and the sky, every lamp that is on with its power and light profile, the camera and the exposure. Each program lights, tones and samples in its own way, so pictures are alike in camera, sun, lamps and exposure, not pixel for pixel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"programs-cadcraft-never-runs\"\u003EPrograms CadCraft never runs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft never starts a program under the GNU GPL licence, and never lists one as an engine or an Open in target. Blender is one of them, so it is not in the Engine list. A USD or glTF file saved from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E still opens in it like in any program that reads those files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanorama and stereo cameras and a sky picture (the sky&#039;s colors are used instead) are not carried over to another program. Nothing done in another program comes back to the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The picture in Render a picture… is drawn by our own engines: Fast and the Path tracer, in the browser and in CadCraft Studio. Another program can draw it only when a render-engine add-on brings it: see Add a render engine with an add-on. To take the scene to another program and keep working there, use Open the scene in another program or Send the scene to a game engine. Draw a picture with an add-on's engine Turn the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Its engine appears in the Engine list of Render a picture…. Choose it. Its own settings show under the Engine list. Click Render. In Studio, the first time an add-on runs a program, Studio asks you in its own dialog. Progress shows while it draws; Stop ends it. The picture appears marked with the engine that drew it. An engine written in JavaScript runs in the browser too. An engine that runs a program on this computer runs only in Studio; in the browser it shows as a file to save. What is carried over The engine gets a package: the model as a USD file (and a glTF file when it asks for one), and a settings file with the exact material values, the sun and the sky, every lamp that is on with its power and light profile, the camera and the exposure. Each program lights, tones and samples in its own way, so pictures are alike in camera, sun, lamps and exposure, not pixel for pixel. Programs CadCraft never runs CadCraft never starts a program under the GNU GPL licence, and never lists one as an engine or an Open in target. Blender is one of them, so it is not in the Engine list. A USD or glTF file saved from Render a picture… still opens in it like in any program that reads those files. Panorama and stereo cameras and a sky picture (the sky's colors are used instead) are not carried over to another program. Nothing done in another program comes back to the model.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"animate","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Animate any property, shape it as curves, and mix clips","summary":"The Animate workspace keys an object's place, turn and size, a lamp's brightness, spread and colour, the lens and a mesh's shape keys; armatures bend a mesh with bones and painted weights; IK and constraints make bones and objects reach, look at, copy, stay within limits, hang from or follow others; rigid bodies drop, stack and collide and are baked to keys; drivers set a value from others; the timeline's Curves view shapes each change with Bézier handles and cycles, and its Clips view saves animations as actions you place again and mix.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["physics","rigid body","rigid bodies","simulation","simulate","drop","fall","gravity","collide","collision","stack","bounce","friction","bake","baked","ik","inverse kinematics","constraint","constraints","look at","track to","aim","copy transforms","copy location","copy rotation","limit rotation","limits","child of","parent","follow path","path","pole","fabrik","chain","armature","armatures","rig","rigging","bones","bone","skeleton","skin","skinning","weights","weight paint","weight painting","vertex groups","automatic weights","bone heat","pose","pose mode","dual quaternion","linear blend","shape keys","shape key","morph","morphs","morph targets","blend shapes","blendshapes","facial","expression","driver","drivers","expressions","animate workspace","animate","animation","keyframe","key","keys","property","properties","transform","location","rotation","scale","lamp","light","brightness","colour","color","lens","field of view","fov","graph editor","curves","curve","bezier","handles","tangent","interpolation","cycle","cycles","repeat","loop","ping pong","offset","dope sheet","select keys","box select","copy keys","paste keys","stretch keys","scale keys","clips","actions","nla","mix","blend","influence","stash"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"key-a-property","text":"Key a property","level":2},{"id":"shape-keys-morphs","text":"Shape keys (morphs)","level":2},{"id":"armatures-bones-that-bend-a-mesh","text":"Armatures: bones that bend a mesh","level":2},{"id":"ik-and-constraints","text":"IK and constraints","level":2},{"id":"physics-drop-stack-and-collide","text":"Physics: drop, stack and collide","level":2},{"id":"drivers","text":"Drivers","level":2},{"id":"choose-and-change-keys-keys-view","text":"Choose and change keys (Keys view)","level":2},{"id":"shape-the-curves-curves-view","text":"Shape the curves (Curves view)","level":2},{"id":"reuse-animations-clips-view","text":"Reuse animations (Clips view)","level":2},{"id":"what-plays-where","text":"What plays where","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003Etimeline\u003C/a\u003E moves the camera and whole groups and components. The \u003Cstrong\u003EAnimate\u003C/strong\u003E workspace keys more than that, and the timeline gets two more views to shape and reuse what you keyed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnimate\u003C/span\u003E in the workspace list on the toolbar. The timeline opens under the view, the toolbar shows the Animate groups (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETimeline\u003C/span\u003E) and the panel on the right lists what you can key at the playhead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"key-a-property\"\u003EKey a property\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel lists, at the playhead:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChosen object\u003C/strong\u003E: its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn (°)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E, three numbers each. Type a number and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: it is keyed at the playhead and the object moves there. The diamond beside a row keys the value as it is now; a filled diamond means there is a key at the playhead already.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELamps\u003C/strong\u003E: each lamp&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness (lm)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpread (°)\u003C/span\u003E (spot lamps) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECamera\u003C/strong\u003E: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELens angle (°)\u003C/span\u003E, for a zoom while the camera moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey transform\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) keys the place, turn and size of every chosen group or component at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey camera\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) keys the camera. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto key\u003C/span\u003E on, a group whose place, turn and size are keyed this way gets a new key whenever you move it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurns are three angles, about X, then Y, then Z. A turn keyed past 180° keeps going the same way round (190° stays 190°, not −170°), so a wheel can spin several times.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group keyed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey selected\u003C/span\u003E on the timeline has whole-placement keys. The curve button on its row turns them into place, turn and size curves (the same placement at every key) so you can shape them in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurves\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shape-keys-morphs\"\u003EShape keys (morphs)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eshape key\u003C/strong\u003E is another shape of the same \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E: a smile on a face, a door&#039;s bulge, a bent leaf. Its slider blends from the mesh as it is (0) to that shape (1); several keys add up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a mesh in the Animate workspace: the panel shows its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShape keys\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd shape key\u003C/span\u003E. ModelCraft goes to the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\"\u003EMesh workspace\u003C/a\u003E with the new key being edited: move points, extrude, sculpt — change the mesh into the shape you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECome back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnimate\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E. The mesh goes back as it was, and the key holds your change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThen drag the key&#039;s slider (or type a value) to blend it in. The diamond keys the value at the playhead, so a key can grow and fade over time; once it has a track, a new value is keyed at the playhead. The pencil edits the key&#039;s shape again (the mesh shows it fully until \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E leaves the key as it was), the eye turns the key off, the bin removes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA vertex you add while editing a key belongs to the mesh itself: it stays where you made it, in every shape.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShape keys move the mesh before its \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003Emodifiers\u003C/a\u003E: a Subdivision modifier smooths the blended shape. Rendered pictures and videos show the keys at each frame.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"armatures-bones-that-bend-a-mesh\"\u003EArmatures: bones that bend a mesh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Earmature\u003C/strong\u003E is a set of bones inside a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E. Pose the bones and the mesh follows, each point as much as its \u003Cstrong\u003Eweights\u003C/strong\u003E say.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the mesh in the Animate workspace and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd an armature\u003C/span\u003E in the panel (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd bone\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERig\u003C/span\u003E group): the first bone runs up the middle of the mesh from its foot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a bone chosen, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a child bone\u003C/span\u003E adds the next one from its tip. Set any bone&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHead\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETip\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll (°)\u003C/span\u003E and what it \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHangs from\u003C/span\u003E in the panel; a bone that hangs from another moves with it. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMoves the mesh\u003C/span\u003E for a bone that only steers others.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic weights\u003C/span\u003E: each bone takes hold of the points nearest to it, and the hold spreads smoothly over the mesh, so the points between two bones share them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENearest bones only\u003C/span\u003E is a quicker, harder split.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPose\u003C/strong\u003E the bones with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPose\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click a bone to choose it, drag to turn it about the view&#039;s axis, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-drag to move it. Or type its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn (°)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPose\u003C/span\u003E; turns are in the bone&#039;s own axes, from where it rests. The diamonds and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey the whole pose\u003C/span\u003E key the pose at the playhead; once a bone has keys, posing it keys it there. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset this bone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the pose\u003C/span\u003E put the bones back at rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaint weights\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) for the chosen bone: the mesh shows at rest, each face coloured by how much that bone holds it — red all, blue none. Paint to add hold (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it away, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E smooths); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize (px)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrength\u003C/span\u003E set the brush. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep each point&#039;s weights adding up to 1\u003C/span\u003E, giving one bone more takes the same from the others. Each stroke is one step of the model&#039;s undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkin\u003C/span\u003E chooses how a point between bones moves: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (fast)\u003C/span\u003E blends the bones&#039; moves, and pinches a bent joint a little; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDual quaternion (keeps volume at joints)\u003C/span\u003E turns the point round the joint instead, so an elbow or a knee keeps its thickness (a bone&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E then doesn&#039;t stretch the skin).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShape keys come first, then the armature, then the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\"\u003Emodifiers\u003C/a\u003E. The view, pictures and videos all show the posed mesh; a mesh&#039;s weights are saved with it, the bones and their pose with the animation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ik-and-constraints\"\u003EIK and constraints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Econstraint\u003C/strong\u003E moves a bone or an object after its keys, by a rule. Choose the object (or, in an armature, the bone) and add one under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstraints of …\u003C/span\u003E; several run in the order listed (the arrows change it), and each has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow much\u003C/span\u003E (0 to 1, keyed with its diamond like any property) and an on/off switch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EConstraint\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFor\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReach (IK)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBones\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe bone and the bones it hangs from (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBones in the chain\u003C/span\u003E) bend so its tip reaches the target. With two bones and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPole (the bend points to it)\u003C/span\u003E, the elbow or knee points towards the pole; longer chains bend smoothly. Out of reach, the chain points straight at the target.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELook at\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns so the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis that points\u003C/span\u003E faces the target, keeping the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis kept up\u003C/span\u003E as near to up as it can.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy transforms\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes the target&#039;s place, turn or size — the ones ticked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELimits\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps its own place, turn (degrees) or size between a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Emin\u003C/span\u003E and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Emax\u003C/span\u003E per axis; leave a pair empty to leave that axis free.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChild of\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves with the target as if it hung from it, from where both were when you chose the target.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow path\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESits on a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/curves\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/curves\"\u003Ecurve object\u003C/a\u003E at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlong the curve\u003C/span\u003E (0 its start, 1 its end; key it to travel), and with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn along the curve\u003C/span\u003E faces along it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA target is another object in the open group, a bone of the same armature, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA point\u003C/span\u003E you type (an object&#039;s point is in the model&#039;s coordinates, a bone&#039;s in its mesh&#039;s). For an IK leg, put the target where the foot should be and key the target, or key \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow much\u003C/span\u003E to hand the leg between the keys and the IK.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn object that looks at one that follows a path sees it on the path: ModelCraft works out the targets first, whatever the order of the objects.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"physics-drop-stack-and-collide\"\u003EPhysics: drop, stack and collide\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELet objects fall and knock into each other, then keep the result as keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a group, component or mesh and set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERigid body\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhysics\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFalls and collides\u003C/span\u003E (it moves) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStays put, gets in the way\u003C/span\u003E (a table, a wall, a ramp). Give a falling one its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShape\u003C/span\u003E — \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox (its bounds)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBall (round its bounds)\u003C/span\u003E — its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMass (kg)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFriction\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBounce\u003C/span\u003E (0 thuds, 1 bounces back as high).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe whole model\u003C/span\u003E, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom (s)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo (s)\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGravity (m/s², down)\u003C/span\u003E and whether there is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA ground to land on\u003C/span\u003E (and its height).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBake physics\u003C/span\u003E (in the panel or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhysics\u003C/span\u003E group). Each falling object gets a place, turn and size key on every frame between the two times; play it like any animation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBaking again replaces those keys; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove baked keys\u003C/span\u003E takes them off. The objects start at rest, where their keys, curves and constraints put them at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom (s)\u003C/span\u003E (a keyed movement before it doesn&#039;t carry on into the fall). More \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteps a frame\u003C/span\u003E keeps tall stacks steadier; the status bar says when something is still moving at the end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShapes are boxes and balls round each object&#039;s bounds; a box touches with its corners, edge middles and face middles, so boxes stack and tip over as you would expect. Cloth, fluids, joints between objects and exact shapes are not built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drivers\"\u003EDrivers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Edriver\u003C/strong\u003E sets a shape key from other things instead of by hand: open a key&#039;s driver (the calculator button), write an \u003Cstrong\u003Eexpression\u003C/strong\u003E, and give each name in it what it reads (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReads\u003C/span\u003E): the time in seconds, the frame, a placement&#039;s place, turn or size along X, Y or Z, a lamp&#039;s brightness, or another shape key. For example, with \u003Ccode\u003Ev\u003C/code\u003E reading a door&#039;s turn about Z, \u003Ccode\u003Eclamp(v / 90, 0, 1)\u003C/code\u003E opens a shape as the door opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExpressions use numbers, the names, \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / % ^\u003C/code\u003E and brackets, and the functions \u003Ccode\u003Esin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 abs sqrt exp log floor ceil round sign pow min max clamp lerp smoothstep rad deg\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ee\u003C/code\u003E. A mistake is pointed out and changes nothing. A driven key&#039;s slider shows the driver&#039;s value; the driver wins over the key&#039;s track until you remove it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-and-change-keys-keys-view\"\u003EChoose and change keys (Keys view)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys\u003C/span\u003E shows a row per track: the camera, each moving group, and each property (&quot;Lamp 2 · Brightness (lm)&quot;; property keys are round).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a key to choose it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click adds or removes keys from the choice. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-drag over the rows draws a box that adds every key in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a chosen key to move them all; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move them a frame (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: ten).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the chosen keys, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes them from the playhead onto the same tracks (another ModelCraft tab can paste them too).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E spreads the chosen keys out from the playhead by the number beside it (2 = twice as far apart; 0.5 pulls them in).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ease list sets how every chosen key moves on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shape-the-curves-curves-view\"\u003EShape the curves (Curves view)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurves\u003C/span\u003E is a graph of each number over time: time along, value up. Tick the curves to show in the list on the left (X, Y and Z are red, green and blue).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a key (a square) to choose it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click for more, or drag a box. Drag a chosen key to change its time and value; with several chosen, drag moves them along in time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA chosen key shows its two \u003Cstrong\u003Ehandles\u003C/strong\u003E. Drag one to shape the curve on that side. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHandles\u003C/span\u003E says how they behave: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic\u003C/span\u003E (a smooth curve through the keys around it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPointing at the next key\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAligned\u003C/span\u003E (the two stay in a straight line, so the curve has no corner) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom the chosen keys\u003C/span\u003E sets how each chosen key moves on to the next: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve (handles)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmooth\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteady\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlow in and out\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHold\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBefore\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAfter\u003C/span\u003E set what a curve does outside its keys: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHold\u003C/span\u003E (stays at the first or last value), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack and forth\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat and carry on\u003C/span\u003E (each repeat starts where the last one ended: a wheel keeps rolling, a walker keeps walking).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe wheel zooms (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: values only, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: time only); drag with the middle button or with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to pan; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fits every shown curve. Click on empty space to move the playhead there. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the chosen keys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHandles are kept from crossing each other in time, so a curve never runs backwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reuse-animations-clips-view\"\u003EReuse animations (Clips view)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eclip\u003C/strong\u003E plays a saved animation (an \u003Cstrong\u003Eaction\u003C/strong\u003E) on the timeline. Clips are mixed in order, the lower rows over the upper ones; the timeline&#039;s own tracks go on top of every clip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKey the groups and lamps as usual, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake a clip\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClips\u003C/span\u003E and give the action a name. The tracks become the action and a clip plays it where they were (the camera stays on the timeline).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo use it again, choose the action and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd clip\u003C/span\u003E: a new clip starts at the playhead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a clip along its row to move it; click it to set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart (s)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E (2 = twice as fast), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeats\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow much\u003C/span\u003E (0 to 1), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMix\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd on top\u003C/span\u003E of the clips before it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutside the clip\u003C/span\u003E (nothing, hold its end, or hold both ends) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMute\u003C/span\u003E. The arrows change the order; the bin removes it (an action no clip uses any more goes with it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA clip at less than full \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow much\u003C/span\u003E blends from what is under it: the clip before it, or the lamp or placement as it is without the animation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-plays-where\"\u003EWhat plays where\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the view\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn a rendered video\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECamera keys, lens angle with camera keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace, turn and size (keys, curves, clips)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELamp brightness, spread and colour\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: the video uses the lamps as they are when it starts (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003Ewhat isn&#039;t built yet\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELens angle with no camera keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: key the camera too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShape keys and drivers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArmatures (posed meshes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIK and constraints\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBaked physics (keys)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file with the rest of the animation, and the timeline&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E take back any of it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The timeline moves the camera and whole groups and components. The Animate workspace keys more than that, and the timeline gets two more views to shape and reuse what you keyed. Open it Choose Animate in the workspace list on the toolbar. The timeline opens under the view, the toolbar shows the Animate groups (Keys, Play, Timeline) and the panel on the right lists what you can key at the playhead. Key a property The panel lists, at the playhead: Chosen object: its Place, Turn (°) and Size, three numbers each. Type a number and press Enter: it is keyed at the playhead and the object moves there. The diamond beside a row keys the value as it is now; a filled diamond means there is a key at the playhead already. Lamps: each lamp's Brightness (lm), Spread (°) (spot lamps) and Colour. Camera: the Lens angle (°), for a zoom while the camera moves. Key transform (or I) keys the place, turn and size of every chosen group or component at once; Key camera (or K) keys the camera. With Auto key on, a group whose place, turn and size are keyed this way gets a new key whenever you move it. Turns are three angles, about X, then Y, then Z. A turn keyed past 180° keeps going the same way round (190° stays 190°, not −170°), so a wheel can spin several times. A group keyed with Key selected on the timeline has whole-placement keys. The curve button on its row turns them into place, turn and size curves (the same placement at every key) so you can shape them in Curves. Shape keys (morphs) A shape key is another shape of the same mesh: a smile on a face, a door's bulge, a bent leaf. Its slider blends from the mesh as it is (0) to that shape (1); several keys add up. Choose a mesh in the Animate workspace: the panel shows its Shape keys. Click Add shape key. ModelCraft goes to the Mesh workspace with the new key being edited: move points, extrude, sculpt — change the mesh into the shape you want. Come back to Animate and click Done. The mesh goes back as it was, and the key holds your change. Then drag the key's slider (or type a value) to blend it in. The diamond keys the value at the playhead, so a key can grow and fade over time; once it has a track, a new value is keyed at the playhead. The pencil edits the key's shape again (the mesh shows it fully until Done; Cancel leaves the key as it was), the eye turns the key off, the bin removes it. A vertex you add while editing a key belongs to the mesh itself: it stays where you made it, in every shape. Shape keys move the mesh before its modifiers: a Subdivision modifier smooths the blended shape. Rendered pictures and videos show the keys at each frame. Armatures: bones that bend a mesh An armature is a set of bones inside a mesh. Pose the bones and the mesh follows, each point as much as its weights say. Choose the mesh in the Animate workspace and click Add an armature in the panel (or Add bone in the Rig group): the first bone runs up the middle of the mesh from its foot. With a bone chosen, Add a child bone adds the next one from its tip. Set any bone's Head, Tip, Roll (°) and what it Hangs from in the panel; a bone that hangs from another moves with it. Untick Moves the mesh for a bone that only steers others. Click Automatic weights: each bone takes hold of the points nearest to it, and the hold spreads smoothly over the mesh, so the points between two bones share them. Nearest bones only is a quicker, harder split. Pose the bones with Pose (P): click a bone to choose it, drag to turn it about the view's axis, Shift-drag to move it. Or type its Place, Turn (°) and Size under Pose; turns are in the bone's own axes, from where it rests. The diamonds and Key the whole pose key the pose at the playhead; once a bone has keys, posing it keys it there. Reset this bone and Reset the pose put the bones back at rest. Paint weights (W) for the chosen bone: the mesh shows at rest, each face coloured by how much that bone holds it — red all, blue none. Paint to add hold (Ctrl takes it away, Shift smooths); Size (px) and Strength set the brush. With Keep each point's weights adding up to 1, giving one bone more takes the same from the others. Each stroke is one step of the model's undo. Skin chooses how a point between bones moves: Linear (fast) blends the bones' moves, and pinches a bent joint a little; Dual quaternion (keeps volume at joints) turns the point round the joint instead, so an elbow or a knee keeps its thickness (a bone's Size then doesn't stretch the skin). Shape keys come first, then the armature, then the modifiers. The view, pictures and videos all show the posed mesh; a mesh's weights are saved with it, the bones and their pose with the animation. IK and constraints A constraint moves a bone or an object after its keys, by a rule. Choose the object (or, in an armature, the bone) and add one under Constraints of …; several run in the order listed (the arrows change it), and each has How much (0 to 1, keyed with its diamond like any property) and an on/off switch. Constraint For What it does Reach (IK) Bones The bone and the bones it hangs from (Bones in the chain) bend so its tip reaches the target. With two bones and a Pole (the bend points to it), the elbow or knee points towards the pole; longer chains bend smoothly. Out of reach, the chain points straight at the target. Look at Both Turns so the Axis that points faces the target, keeping the Axis kept up as near to up as it can. Copy transforms Both Takes the target's place, turn or size — the ones ticked. Limits Both Keeps its own place, turn (degrees) or size between a min and a max per axis; leave a pair empty to leave that axis free. Child of Both Moves with the target as if it hung from it, from where both were when you chose the target. Follow path Objects Sits on a curve object at Along the curve (0 its start, 1 its end; key it to travel), and with Turn along the curve faces along it. A target is another object in the open group, a bone of the same armature, or A point you type (an object's point is in the model's coordinates, a bone's in its mesh's). For an IK leg, put the target where the foot should be and key the target, or key How much to hand the leg between the keys and the IK. An object that looks at one that follows a path sees it on the path: ModelCraft works out the targets first, whatever the order of the objects. Physics: drop, stack and collide Let objects fall and knock into each other, then keep the result as keys. Choose a group, component or mesh and set its Rigid body under Physics: Falls and collides (it moves) or Stays put, gets in the way (a table, a wall, a ramp). Give a falling one its Shape — Box (its bounds) or Ball (round its bounds) — its Mass (kg), Friction and Bounce (0 thuds, 1 bounces back as high). Under The whole model, set From (s) and To (s), the Gravity (m/s², down) and whether there is A ground to land on (and its height). Click Bake physics (in the panel or the Physics group). Each falling object gets a place, turn and size key on every frame between the two times; play it like any animation. Baking again replaces those keys; Remove baked keys takes them off. The objects start at rest, where their keys, curves and constraints put them at From (s) (a keyed movement before it doesn't carry on into the fall). More Steps a frame keeps tall stacks steadier; the status bar says when something is still moving at the end. Shapes are boxes and balls round each object's bounds; a box touches with its corners, edge middles and face middles, so boxes stack and tip over as you would expect. Cloth, fluids, joints between objects and exact shapes are not built. Drivers A driver sets a shape key from other things instead of by hand: open a key's driver (the calculator button), write an expression, and give each name in it what it reads (Reads): the time in seconds, the frame, a placement's place, turn or size along X, Y or Z, a lamp's brightness, or another shape key. For example, with v reading a door's turn about Z, clamp(v / 90, 0, 1) opens a shape as the door opens. Expressions use numbers, the names, + - * / % ^ and brackets, and the functions sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 abs sqrt exp log floor ceil round sign pow min max clamp lerp smoothstep rad deg with pi and e. A mistake is pointed out and changes nothing. A driven key's slider shows the driver's value; the driver wins over the key's track until you remove it. Choose and change keys (Keys view) Keys shows a row per track: the camera, each moving group, and each property (\"Lamp 2 · Brightness (lm)\"; property keys are round). Click a key to choose it; Shift-click or Ctrl-click adds or removes keys from the choice. Shift-drag over the rows draws a box that adds every key in it. Drag a chosen key to move them all; ← / → move them a frame (Shift: ten). Ctrl+C copies the chosen keys, Ctrl+V pastes them from the playhead onto the same tracks (another ModelCraft tab can paste them too). Stretch spreads the chosen keys out from the playhead by the number beside it (2 = twice as far apart; 0.5 pulls them in). The ease list sets how every chosen key moves on. Shape the curves (Curves view) Curves is a graph of each number over time: time along, value up. Tick the curves to show in the list on the left (X, Y and Z are red, green and blue). Click a key (a square) to choose it, Shift-click for more, or drag a box. Drag a chosen key to change its time and value; with several chosen, drag moves them along in time. A chosen key shows its two handles. Drag one to shape the curve on that side. Handles says how they behave: Automatic (a smooth curve through the keys around it), Pointing at the next key, Aligned (the two stay in a straight line, so the curve has no corner) or Free. From the chosen keys sets how each chosen key moves on to the next: Curve (handles), Smooth, Steady, Slow in and out or Hold. Before and After set what a curve does outside its keys: Hold (stays at the first or last value), Repeat, Back and forth, or Repeat and carry on (each repeat starts where the last one ended: a wheel keeps rolling, a walker keeps walking). The wheel zooms (Ctrl: values only, Shift: time only); drag with the middle button or with Alt to pan; Show all or Home fits every shown curve. Click on empty space to move the playhead there. Delete deletes the chosen keys. Handles are kept from crossing each other in time, so a curve never runs backwards. Reuse animations (Clips view) A clip plays a saved animation (an action) on the timeline. Clips are mixed in order, the lower rows over the upper ones; the timeline's own tracks go on top of every clip. Key the groups and lamps as usual, then click Make a clip in Clips and give the action a name. The tracks become the action and a clip plays it where they were (the camera stays on the timeline). To use it again, choose the action and click Add clip: a new clip starts at the playhead. Drag a clip along its row to move it; click it to set Start (s), Speed (2 = twice as fast), Repeats, How much (0 to 1), Mix (Replace, or Add on top of the clips before it), Outside the clip (nothing, hold its end, or hold both ends) and Mute. The arrows change the order; the bin removes it (an action no clip uses any more goes with it). A clip at less than full How much blends from what is under it: the clip before it, or the lamp or placement as it is without the animation. What plays where What In the view In a rendered video Camera keys, lens angle with camera keys Yes Yes Place, turn and size (keys, curves, clips) Yes Yes Lamp brightness, spread and colour Yes Not yet: the video uses the lamps as they are when it starts (see what isn't built yet) Lens angle with no camera keys Yes Not yet: key the camera too Shape keys and drivers Yes Yes Armatures (posed meshes) Yes Yes IK and constraints Yes Yes Baked physics (keys) Yes Yes Everything is saved in the .modelcraft file with the rest of the animation, and the timeline's Undo and Redo take back any of it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render-open-in","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Open the scene in another program","summary":"Save the scene as a USD file or a glTF file for any program that reads them, or open it from Studio in a program on this computer.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["open in","usd","gltf","glb","export","rendering program","archviz","studio","usdview","send","hand-off","package","unreal folder"],"headings":[{"id":"a-file-for-any-program","text":"A file for any program","level":2},{"id":"in-studio-open-it-in-a-program","text":"In Studio: open it in a program","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EChoose an \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen in\u003C/strong\u003E entry in the Engine list of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E to take the scene to another program. Nothing comes back to the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-for-any-program\"\u003EA file for any program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUSD file\u003C/strong\u003E saves the scene as \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E when the model carries pictures): the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003Esame file as Save as USD\u003C/a\u003E, with materials, lamps and cameras. It keeps instancing and the exact material values. Most renderers, game engines and viewers read USD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EglTF file (.glb)\u003C/strong\u003E saves the lighter glTF 2.0 file with materials, textures and lamps; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\"\u003EExport a model for other programs\u003C/a\u003E for the formats ModelCraft writes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnreal project folder (.zip)\u003C/strong\u003E saves the folder \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-unreal\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-unreal\"\u003ESend the scene to a game engine\u003C/a\u003E writes, for a project on any computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three work in the browser and in Studio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-studio-open-it-in-a-program\"\u003EIn Studio: open it in a program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStudio lists the programs it finds on this computer that can open the scene: usdview if it is on your search path, and the computer&#039;s own program for the file type. Choose one, choose USD or glTF where it reads both, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E. Studio writes the package into a new folder under Documents (\u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft hand-off\u003C/code\u003E; nothing is overwritten, each send gets its own folder) and starts the program on it, then says where the files are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EReads best\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eusdview\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUSD\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe computer&#039;s own program\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhatever the computer has for the file type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStudio only starts programs from this fixed list; it does not run anything the page asks for by name, and it never starts a program under the GNU GPL licence (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-other-program#programs-cadcraft-never-runs\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-other-program\" data-hash=\"programs-cadcraft-never-runs\"\u003EDraw the picture with another program\u003C/a\u003E). Any program that reads USD or glTF opens the file from \u003Cstrong\u003EA file for any program\u003C/strong\u003E above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwinmotion, Enscape and other visualization programs are not in the list: CadCraft has not been checked with them and makes no claim about them. A glTF or USD file from here is the route in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Choose an Open in entry in the Engine list of Render a picture… to take the scene to another program. Nothing comes back to the model. A file for any program USD file saves the scene as .usdc (or .usdz when the model carries pictures): the same file as Save as USD, with materials, lamps and cameras. It keeps instancing and the exact material values. Most renderers, game engines and viewers read USD. glTF file (.glb) saves the lighter glTF 2.0 file with materials, textures and lamps; see Export a model for other programs for the formats ModelCraft writes. Unreal project folder (.zip) saves the folder Send the scene to a game engine writes, for a project on any computer. All three work in the browser and in Studio. In Studio: open it in a program Studio lists the programs it finds on this computer that can open the scene: usdview if it is on your search path, and the computer's own program for the file type. Choose one, choose USD or glTF where it reads both, and click Render. Studio writes the package into a new folder under Documents (CadCraft hand-off; nothing is overwritten, each send gets its own folder) and starts the program on it, then says where the files are. Program Reads best usdview USD The computer's own program Whatever the computer has for the file type Studio only starts programs from this fixed list; it does not run anything the page asks for by name, and it never starts a program under the GNU GPL licence (see Draw the picture with another program). Any program that reads USD or glTF opens the file from A file for any program above. Twinmotion, Enscape and other visualization programs are not in the list: CadCraft has not been checked with them and makes no claim about them. A glTF or USD file from here is the route in.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"vr-review","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Review the model in a VR headset","summary":"Walk the model at full size in a VR headset: jump around, turn, measure, cut it open, place issues with a picture, see it as a table-top model, and meet the others of a live session.","section":"tasks","order":116,"keywords":["vr","virtual reality","headset","webxr","xr","immersive","quest","oculus","vive","index","pico","walk through","walkthrough","teleport","design review","review","measure","section","cut","issue","issues","bcf","table top","scale model","presence","avatars","live","together"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"start","text":"Start","level":2},{"id":"what-it-looks-like","text":"What it looks like","level":2},{"id":"move-around","text":"Move around","level":2},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure","level":2},{"id":"cut-it-open","text":"Cut it open","level":2},{"id":"place-an-issue","text":"Place an issue","level":2},{"id":"see-it-as-a-table-top-model","text":"See it as a table-top model","level":2},{"id":"with-others-live","text":"With others, live","level":2},{"id":"leave","text":"Leave","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReview in VR\u003C/span\u003E puts you inside the model with a VR headset, at full size. You stand where the view&#039;s camera is, on the floor below it, facing the same way. Nothing is uploaded: the headset shows what the browser draws on this computer (or in the headset&#039;s own browser).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA headset the browser can drive (WebXR): a standalone headset&#039;s own browser, or Chrome or Edge on a PC with a headset connected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EModelCraft open on a secure page (https), as it is on this site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen there is no headset, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReview in VR\u003C/span\u003E is greyed out and says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start\"\u003EStart\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the view where you want to start: in a room, or outside looking at the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera\u003C/span\u003E group, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReview in VR\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPut on the headset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe menu is on the back of your left hand. Point at it with the right controller and pull the trigger to pick a button. (With one hand only, the menu floats in front of where you started.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-looks-like\"\u003EWhat it looks like\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe headset shows the model in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\"\u003Elive view&#039;s\u003C/a\u003E look: the looks and materials you gave the faces, the sky and the sun from your model&#039;s place and date with its shadow, lamps, scattered plants and rocks, and light bounced off walls and floors (worked out on this computer while you look round, and following you as you move). Brightness and the view transform (ACES or AgX) are those of \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E. How much it draws depends on what the headset&#039;s browser has, as on the desktop; ambient occlusion, reflections and bloom stay on the desktop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-around\"\u003EMove around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJump:\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, point at a floor and pull the trigger; you land there. Or push the stick forward to aim, and let go to jump. You can only land on floors and ground (surfaces facing up), never on a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETurn:\u003C/strong\u003E push the stick left or right: you turn 30° each time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalk:\u003C/strong\u003E in your play area, simply walk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E, then pull the trigger on two points. The distance shows between them, in the model&#039;s units. A point near a corner snaps to the corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-it-open\"\u003ECut it open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E and pull the trigger on a surface:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a wall (or anything not a floor): everything between you and that surface is cut away, so you see in. This works best from outside, or across a room;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a floor, from above: a plan cut 1.2 m over that floor, so the walls show their lower part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E takes the cut and the measurements away. If a section plane cuts the model in the view when you start, the headset starts with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-an-issue\"\u003EPlace an issue\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssue\u003C/span\u003E and pull the trigger where the problem is. A numbered pin marks it, and ModelCraft keeps the issue with a picture of what you saw and where you stood. When you leave VR, the issues are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E list on the right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe eye button shows the issue from where it was placed;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as BCF\u003C/span\u003E downloads them all as a BCF file, which BIM review programs open (camera and picture included);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethey are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd a comment to an issue after you leave VR, in a BCF program: typing in the headset is not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-it-as-a-table-top-model\"\u003ESee it as a table-top model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETable top\u003C/span\u003E shrinks the model to fit a table in front of you; walk round it, or turn it with the stick. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull size\u003C/span\u003E puts you back where you were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-others-live\"\u003EWith others, live\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/work-live\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/work-live\"\u003Elive session\u003C/a\u003E, everyone in VR sees the others as a head and two hands in their own color, with their name, and a dot where each one points. Edits anyone makes reach your headset within a moment. People at the desk are not shown in the headset yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"leave\"\u003ELeave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave VR\u003C/span\u003E (or take the headset off and end it there). The view on the screen moves to where your head was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETested with a headset emulator only, not yet with a real headset. A large model can make the headset stutter: hide what you don&#039;t need first (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003Ehide and isolate\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Review in VR puts you inside the model with a VR headset, at full size. You stand where the view's camera is, on the floor below it, facing the same way. Nothing is uploaded: the headset shows what the browser draws on this computer (or in the headset's own browser). What you need A headset the browser can drive (WebXR): a standalone headset's own browser, or Chrome or Edge on a PC with a headset connected. ModelCraft open on a secure page (https), as it is on this site. When there is no headset, Review in VR is greyed out and says why. Start Set up the view where you want to start: in a room, or outside looking at the building. Click Views in the Camera group, then Review in VR. Put on the headset. The menu is on the back of your left hand. Point at it with the right controller and pull the trigger to pick a button. (With one hand only, the menu floats in front of where you started.) What it looks like The headset shows the model in the live view's look: the looks and materials you gave the faces, the sky and the sun from your model's place and date with its shadow, lamps, scattered plants and rocks, and light bounced off walls and floors (worked out on this computer while you look round, and following you as you move). Brightness and the view transform (ACES or AgX) are those of Render a picture. How much it draws depends on what the headset's browser has, as on the desktop; ambient occlusion, reflections and bloom stay on the desktop. Move around Jump: in Move, point at a floor and pull the trigger; you land there. Or push the stick forward to aim, and let go to jump. You can only land on floors and ground (surfaces facing up), never on a wall. Turn: push the stick left or right: you turn 30° each time. Walk: in your play area, simply walk. Measure Pick Measure, then pull the trigger on two points. The distance shows between them, in the model's units. A point near a corner snaps to the corner. Cut it open Pick Section and pull the trigger on a surface: on a wall (or anything not a floor): everything between you and that surface is cut away, so you see in. This works best from outside, or across a room; on a floor, from above: a plan cut 1.2 m over that floor, so the walls show their lower part. Clear takes the cut and the measurements away. If a section plane cuts the model in the view when you start, the headset starts with it. Place an issue Pick Issue and pull the trigger where the problem is. A numbered pin marks it, and ModelCraft keeps the issue with a picture of what you saw and where you stood. When you leave VR, the issues are in the Issues list on the right: the eye button shows the issue from where it was placed; Save as BCF downloads them all as a BCF file, which BIM review programs open (camera and picture included); they are saved in the .modelcraft file too. Add a comment to an issue after you leave VR, in a BCF program: typing in the headset is not built yet. See it as a table-top model Table top shrinks the model to fit a table in front of you; walk round it, or turn it with the stick. Full size puts you back where you were. With others, live In a live session, everyone in VR sees the others as a head and two hands in their own color, with their name, and a dot where each one points. Edits anyone makes reach your headset within a moment. People at the desk are not shown in the headset yet. Leave Pick Leave VR (or take the headset off and end it there). The view on the screen moves to where your head was. Tested with a headset emulator only, not yet with a real headset. A large model can make the headset stutter: hide what you don't need first (hide and isolate).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render-unreal","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Send the scene to a game engine","summary":"Studio can write the scene into an Unreal Engine project and keep it in step on every send; your own edits live in a layer CadCraft never writes. The plug-in that opens it is not released yet.","section":"tasks","order":116,"keywords":["unreal","unreal engine","game engine","link","send","usd stage","project","live","cine camera","movie render queue"],"headings":[{"id":"send-the-scene","text":"Send the scene","level":2},{"id":"your-own-edits-are-safe","text":"Your own edits are safe","level":2},{"id":"without-studio","text":"Without Studio","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio the Engine list can hold a \u003Cstrong\u003Elink\u003C/strong\u003E to an Unreal Engine project. One way only: from CadCraft to Unreal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-the-scene\"\u003ESend the scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the Unreal entry in the Engine list. The first time, Studio asks you to pick your project&#039;s folder (the one with the \u003Ccode\u003E.uproject\u003C/code\u003E file); it remembers it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend\u003C/span\u003E. Studio writes \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft/&lt;file name&gt;/\u003C/code\u003E inside the project: the scene as a USD file, the settings file, light profiles, and two small layer files.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the model, save and send again. Only the scene and the settings file are replaced.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-own-edits-are-safe\"\u003EYour own edits are safe\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stage Unreal opens is a small root layer made once, with two layers under it: \u003Cstrong\u003Eyours\u003C/strong\u003E on top (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.user.usda\u003C/code\u003E, empty at first, never rewritten by CadCraft) and CadCraft&#039;s scene below. Edits you save into your own layer stay when the scene is sent again. Lights and other actors you place in the Unreal level are outside the stage and are not touched either.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-studio\"\u003EWithout Studio\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the browser, or on a computer where Studio does not find Unreal, choose \u003Cstrong\u003EUnreal project folder (.zip)\u003C/strong\u003E in the Engine list and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E. It saves the same folder as a zip: unzip it into your project&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft\u003C/code\u003E folder. Leave \u003Cstrong\u003EInclude the stage files\u003C/strong\u003E on the first time; turn it off for later saves, so the zip holds only the scene and the settings file and your own layer is never replaced.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Unreal plug-in that opens the stage, applies the sun, lamps, cameras and exposure, follows each send and renders a camera is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot released yet\u003C/strong\u003E and has not been run in Unreal. Until it is, Send puts the files in the project and Unreal&#039;s own USD tools can open them. Windows is the first system; macOS follows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"In CadCraft Studio the Engine list can hold a link to an Unreal Engine project. One way only: from CadCraft to Unreal. Send the scene Choose the Unreal entry in the Engine list. The first time, Studio asks you to pick your project's folder (the one with the .uproject file); it remembers it. Click Send. Studio writes CadCraft/\u003Cfile name\u003E/ inside the project: the scene as a USD file, the settings file, light profiles, and two small layer files. Change the model, save and send again. Only the scene and the settings file are replaced. Your own edits are safe The stage Unreal opens is a small root layer made once, with two layers under it: yours on top (\u003Cname\u003E.user.usda, empty at first, never rewritten by CadCraft) and CadCraft's scene below. Edits you save into your own layer stay when the scene is sent again. Lights and other actors you place in the Unreal level are outside the stage and are not touched either. Without Studio In the browser, or on a computer where Studio does not find Unreal, choose Unreal project folder (.zip) in the Engine list and click Render. It saves the same folder as a zip: unzip it into your project's CadCraft folder. Leave Include the stage files on the first time; turn it off for later saves, so the zip holds only the scene and the settings file and your own layer is never replaced. The Unreal plug-in that opens the stage, applies the sun, lamps, cameras and exposure, follows each send and renders a camera is not released yet and has not been run in Unreal. Until it is, Send puts the files in the project and Unreal's own USD tools can open them. Windows is the first system; macOS follows.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"ar-on-site","scope":"modelcraft","title":"See the model on site with an Android phone (AR)","summary":"Hold up an Android phone with Chrome and see the model at full size over the camera, lined up on the building by two points you stand at, with an x-ray lens and a quick re-check on the next visit. For looking, not for setting out.","section":"tasks","order":117,"keywords":["ar","augmented reality","phone","android","chrome","webxr","on site","site","camera","full size","x-ray","align","line up","re-check","arcore"],"headings":[{"id":"line-the-model-up","text":"Line the model up","level":2},{"id":"x-ray-lens","text":"X-ray lens","level":2},{"id":"the-next-visit","text":"The next visit","level":2},{"id":"leave","text":"Leave","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView on site (AR, phone)\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu shows the model at its real size over the phone&#039;s camera. It works on an \u003Cstrong\u003EAndroid phone with Chrome\u003C/strong\u003E, on a secure (https) page, where the phone supports AR. On an iPhone or iPad, a computer, or an Android app that only shows a web page, the item says why it cannot start; use the 3D view there, or open this page in Chrome on the phone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA phone is for looking.\u003C/strong\u003E Do not measure, set out, or cut anything from what the screen shows: the phone drifts as you walk, and the pointing error grows with distance from the two points you lined up on (a few centimetres near them, more far away). Nothing here is survey grade.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-the-model-up\"\u003ELine the model up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the model on the phone and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView on site (AR, phone)\u003C/span\u003E. Allow the camera.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe panel at the bottom names the first point (a ground corner of the model). Stand there on site, aim the green ring at the ground, and tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace point\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalk to the second point, at least half a metre away (the further apart, the better the fit), and do the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model appears at full size, turned and moved to fit the two places. The panel gives how well the two points agree.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart over\u003C/span\u003E clears the points. The alignment is kept in the model file, so the next time you open the model on site it starts lined up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"x-ray-lens\"\u003EX-ray lens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray lens\u003C/span\u003E shows the whole model inside a box in front of the phone (about a metre and a half wide and three metres deep) and only a faint ghost of it elsewhere, so you can look at what is behind a wall without losing the real wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-next-visit\"\u003EThe next visit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStand at the first point and tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERe-check\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace point\u003C/span\u003E. Under 5 centimetres off, the lining up is kept. Up to half a metre off, the model moves by the difference. More than that, the panel asks you to point at the two places again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"leave\"\u003ELeave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave AR\u003C/span\u003E ends the session. The camera picture stays in the browser: nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"View on site (AR, phone) in the Views menu shows the model at its real size over the phone's camera. It works on an Android phone with Chrome, on a secure (https) page, where the phone supports AR. On an iPhone or iPad, a computer, or an Android app that only shows a web page, the item says why it cannot start; use the 3D view there, or open this page in Chrome on the phone. A phone is for looking. Do not measure, set out, or cut anything from what the screen shows: the phone drifts as you walk, and the pointing error grows with distance from the two points you lined up on (a few centimetres near them, more far away). Nothing here is survey grade. Line the model up Open the model on the phone and choose View on site (AR, phone). Allow the camera. The panel at the bottom names the first point (a ground corner of the model). Stand there on site, aim the green ring at the ground, and tap Place point. Walk to the second point, at least half a metre away (the further apart, the better the fit), and do the same. The model appears at full size, turned and moved to fit the two places. The panel gives how well the two points agree. Start over clears the points. The alignment is kept in the model file, so the next time you open the model on site it starts lined up. X-ray lens X-ray lens shows the whole model inside a box in front of the phone (about a metre and a half wide and three metres deep) and only a faint ghost of it elsewhere, so you can look at what is behind a wall without losing the real wall. The next visit Stand at the first point and tap Re-check, then Place point. Under 5 centimetres off, the lining up is kept. Up to half a metre off, the model moves by the difference. More than that, the panel asks you to point at the two places again. Leave Leave AR ends the session. The camera picture stays in the browser: nothing is uploaded.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scenes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Save views as scenes","summary":"Save a camera and style as a scene, update and rename it, and step or play through scenes to present a design.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["scene","view","saved view","camera","presentation","animation","play","slideshow","page up","page down","tabs","walkthrough"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-scene","text":"Add a scene","level":2},{"id":"go-to-a-scene","text":"Go to a scene","level":2},{"id":"change-rename-or-delete-a-scene","text":"Change, rename or delete a scene","level":2},{"id":"lock-a-scene-s-camera","text":"Lock a scene's camera","level":2},{"id":"present","text":"Present","level":2},{"id":"what-a-scene-shows","text":"What a scene shows","level":2},{"id":"export-an-animation","text":"Export an animation","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Escene\u003C/strong\u003E remembers a camera position, a style and what is hidden: where you are looking from, perspective or parallel, the face style, edges, the sun settings, hidden groups, tags turned off and the section cut. Scenes are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-scene\"\u003EAdd a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the view: orbit, zoom, pick a style, set the sun.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scene\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scene appears in the list, named &quot;Scene 1&quot;, &quot;Scene 2&quot; and so on, and as a tab across the top of the 3D view. The status bar says &quot;Scene 1 saved: this camera, style and what is hidden.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"go-to-a-scene\"\u003EGo to a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick its tab over the 3D view, or its name in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next scene and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the previous one. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENext\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E under the list do the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera glides to the scene in a little over half a second. If your computer is set to reduce motion, it jumps there instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-rename-or-delete-a-scene\"\u003EChange, rename or delete a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach scene in the list has three small buttons after its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUpdate\u003C/strong\u003E (the circular arrows): saves the current camera, style and what is hidden into that scene. &quot;Scene 2 updated.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename\u003C/strong\u003E (the pencil): opens a &quot;Rename scene&quot; dialog. Type the new name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E (the trash can): removes the scene. &quot;Scene 2 deleted. Ctrl+Z brings it back.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdding, updating, renaming and deleting scenes are steps in undo, like changes to the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lock-a-scene-s-camera\"\u003ELock a scene&#039;s camera\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you render the same view again and again as the design changes, lock that scene&#039;s camera so the view never moves by accident.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E list, click the \u003Cstrong\u003Elock\u003C/strong\u003E button (the open padlock) after the scene&#039;s name. It closes, and the scene&#039;s tab over the 3D view shows a small padlock. The status bar says &quot;Scene 2: camera locked.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGo to the scene whenever you want to render: the camera goes back to exactly the saved view, so pictures made from it line up with the earlier ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the camera is locked, \u003Cstrong\u003EUpdate\u003C/strong\u003E still saves the style and what is hidden into the scene, but the camera stays where it was, even if you have orbited away. Click the padlock again to unlock it; Update then saves the current view again. Locking and unlocking are steps in undo, and the lock is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"present\"\u003EPresent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd two or more scenes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E under the list. ModelCraft moves to the next scene every few seconds and goes back to the first after the last.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (the same button) or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a sun study, add one scene per hour with only the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime\u003C/span\u003E changed, then play them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#a-sun-study\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"a-sun-study\"\u003EStyles and sun shadows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo present without the bars, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E twice: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden. The scene tabs stay over the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-scene-shows\"\u003EWhat a scene shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scene remembers what was hidden when you saved it: groups and components you \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003Ehid\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003Etags\u003C/a\u003E you turned off, and which \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sections\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sections\"\u003Esection plane\u003C/a\u003E cuts. Going to the scene hides and shows them again. So one scene can show the whole house, the next the house with the roof&#039;s tag off, the next a section through it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHidden loose faces and edges are not part of scenes. Going to a scene does not make an undo step. Scenes saved by an earlier build of ModelCraft leave what is hidden as it is; click Update to save it into them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-an-animation\"\u003EExport an animation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E, under the list, plays the scenes from the first to the last, moving the camera smoothly between them, and saves the film as a \u003Cstrong\u003EWebM\u003C/strong\u003E video (two seconds&#039; move and a short pause per scene). It records the view as you see it, at its size on screen, in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003Epresentation view\u003C/a\u003E (no axes, guides or selection). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click the button again to stop without saving. WebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a rendered walkthrough (sky, sun, shadows, materials) at a size you choose, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender video…\u003C/span\u003E instead: with no animation keys it takes the scenes, in order, as the camera&#039;s path. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003EAnimate the camera and objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChanging the style or moving the camera after going to a scene does not change the scene until you click Update.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScenes are not exported to Collada, OBJ, glTF, STL or IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A scene remembers a camera position, a style and what is hidden: where you are looking from, perspective or parallel, the face style, edges, the sun settings, hidden groups, tags turned off and the section cut. Scenes are saved in the .modelcraft file. Add a scene Set up the view: orbit, zoom, pick a style, set the sun. In the Scenes panel on the right, click Add scene. The scene appears in the list, named \"Scene 1\", \"Scene 2\" and so on, and as a tab across the top of the 3D view. The status bar says \"Scene 1 saved: this camera, style and what is hidden.\" Go to a scene Click its tab over the 3D view, or its name in the Scenes list. Or press Page Down for the next scene and Page Up for the previous one. Next and Previous under the list do the same. The camera glides to the scene in a little over half a second. If your computer is set to reduce motion, it jumps there instead. Change, rename or delete a scene Each scene in the list has three small buttons after its name: Update (the circular arrows): saves the current camera, style and what is hidden into that scene. \"Scene 2 updated.\" Rename (the pencil): opens a \"Rename scene\" dialog. Type the new name and click Save. Delete (the trash can): removes the scene. \"Scene 2 deleted. Ctrl+Z brings it back.\" Adding, updating, renaming and deleting scenes are steps in undo, like changes to the model. Lock a scene's camera When you render the same view again and again as the design changes, lock that scene's camera so the view never moves by accident. In the Scenes list, click the lock button (the open padlock) after the scene's name. It closes, and the scene's tab over the 3D view shows a small padlock. The status bar says \"Scene 2: camera locked.\" Go to the scene whenever you want to render: the camera goes back to exactly the saved view, so pictures made from it line up with the earlier ones. While the camera is locked, Update still saves the style and what is hidden into the scene, but the camera stays where it was, even if you have orbited away. Click the padlock again to unlock it; Update then saves the current view again. Locking and unlocking are steps in undo, and the lock is saved in the .modelcraft file. Present Add two or more scenes. Click Play under the list. ModelCraft moves to the next scene every few seconds and goes back to the first after the last. Click Stop (the same button) or press Esc to stop. For a sun study, add one scene per hour with only the Time changed, then play them. See Styles and sun shadows. To present without the bars, press Ctrl+Shift+F twice: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden. The scene tabs stay over the view. What a scene shows A scene remembers what was hidden when you saved it: groups and components you hid, tags you turned off, and which section plane cuts. Going to the scene hides and shows them again. So one scene can show the whole house, the next the house with the roof's tag off, the next a section through it. Hidden loose faces and edges are not part of scenes. Going to a scene does not make an undo step. Scenes saved by an earlier build of ModelCraft leave what is hidden as it is; click Update to save it into them. Export an animation Export animation, under the list, plays the scenes from the first to the last, moving the camera smoothly between them, and saves the film as a WebM video (two seconds' move and a short pause per scene). It records the view as you see it, at its size on screen, in presentation view (no axes, guides or selection). Press Esc or click the button again to stop without saving. WebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it. For a rendered walkthrough (sky, sun, shadows, materials) at a size you choose, use Render video… instead: with no animation keys it takes the scenes, in order, as the camera's path. See Animate the camera and objects. Things to know Changing the style or moving the camera after going to a scene does not change the scene until you click Update. Scenes are not exported to Collada, OBJ, glTF, STL or IFC.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Use ModelCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script look at the model and build simple solids: permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","modeling","box","cylinder","render","render queue","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with ModelCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). ModelCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use ModelCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, ModelCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset ModelCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it (for example &quot;Assistant: box&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodel_describe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model&#039;s unit, size, and every group and component with where it is, whether it is a closed solid and its volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eview_screenshot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the 3D view as shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebox_add\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecylinder_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA box or a cylinder as a new group, at a place and size you or it give (&quot;3m&quot;, &quot;10&#039;&quot; or a number in the model&#039;s unit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Einstance_move\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einstances_erase\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves or erases groups and components\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efaces_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ematerials_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe faces of a group or of the loose geometry (each one&#039;s direction, area and centre); the materials and colours it can paint with\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epush_pull\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPushes or pulls a face, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/Pull\u003C/span\u003E does\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epaint\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaints faces or a whole group with a colour or a material\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Egroup_make\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes a group or a component of loose faces and groups\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Escene_add\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Escene_show\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaves the view as a scene; goes to a saved scene\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emesh_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA mesh object to start from (cube, sphere, cylinder …), as the Mesh workspace adds it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emodifiers_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodifier_add\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodifier_set\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodifier_remove\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead / Edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA mesh&#039;s modifier stack: mirror, array, subdivision, bevel and the rest; nothing is applied for good\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model as OpenUSD (.usda, .usdc or .usdz), as saving the model as USD writes it (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/a\u003E); to a folder you allowed in Studio, else a download\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Erender_still\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA rendered picture of the view or of a saved scene, as the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E dialog&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E button makes it, with your Render settings unless it asks for others (fast or high quality, size, samples, sky); a PNG it can look at, or a PNG, EXR or render file in a folder you allowed in Studio (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Erender_queue\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds high-quality pictures to the Render dialog&#039;s queue (the view, or saved scenes) and runs the queue; the finished pictures go to a folder you allowed, or stay in the Render dialog&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender queue\u003C/span\u003E for you. Needs WebGPU\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes back its own steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolids an assistant makes come in as groups, so they never merge with what you drew. There is no tool that saves the model: saving stays with you, and exporting or rendering needs \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E ticked. A render changes nothing in the model; rendering a saved scene moves the view to it, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no ModelCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with ModelCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. ModelCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). ModelCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the ModelCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use ModelCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, ModelCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; Reset ModelCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it (for example \"Assistant: box\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › ModelCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, model_describe Read The model's unit, size, and every group and component with where it is, whether it is a closed solid and its volume view_screenshot Read A picture of the 3D view as shown box_add, cylinder_add Edit A box or a cylinder as a new group, at a place and size you or it give (\"3m\", \"10'\" or a number in the model's unit) instance_move, instances_erase Edit Moves or erases groups and components faces_list, materials_list Read The faces of a group or of the loose geometry (each one's direction, area and centre); the materials and colours it can paint with push_pull Edit Pushes or pulls a face, as Push/Pull does paint Edit Paints faces or a whole group with a colour or a material group_make Edit Makes a group or a component of loose faces and groups scene_add, scene_show Edit Saves the view as a scene; goes to a saved scene mesh_add Edit A mesh object to start from (cube, sphere, cylinder …), as the Mesh workspace adds it modifiers_list, modifier_add, modifier_set, modifier_remove Read / Edit A mesh's modifier stack: mirror, array, subdivision, bevel and the rest; nothing is applied for good usd_export Export The model as OpenUSD (.usda, .usdc or .usdz), as saving the model as USD writes it (USD); to a folder you allowed in Studio, else a download render_still Export A rendered picture of the view or of a saved scene, as the Render dialog's Render button makes it, with your Render settings unless it asks for others (fast or high quality, size, samples, sky); a PNG it can look at, or a PNG, EXR or render file in a folder you allowed in Studio (Render a picture) render_queue Export Adds high-quality pictures to the Render dialog's queue (the view, or saved scenes) and runs the queue; the finished pictures go to a folder you allowed, or stay in the Render dialog's Render queue for you. Needs WebGPU undo Edit Takes back its own steps Solids an assistant makes come in as groups, so they never merge with what you drew. There is no tool that saves the model: saving stays with you, and exporting or rendering needs Export ticked. A render changes nothing in the model; rendering a saved scene moves the view to it, as Scenes does. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no ModelCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\": tick Edit under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"export-model","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Export a model for other programs","summary":"Choose the right export for rendering, the web, 3D printing, BIM and BimCraft, and check what each one keeps.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["print","3mf","pdf","paper","image","png","picture","screenshot","render","save view","export","share","render","rendering","gltf","glb","collada","dae","obj","stl","3d print","ifc","bim","bimcraft","send","other software"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"which-format","text":"Which format","level":2},{"id":"before-you-export","text":"Before you export","level":2},{"id":"send-a-model-to-bimcraft","text":"Send a model to BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"lines-without-faces","text":"Lines without faces","level":2},{"id":"save-the-view-as-an-image","text":"Save the view as an image","level":2},{"id":"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf","text":"Print the view, or save it as a PDF","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EExport makes a copy of the model in another format. Keep your \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file too: it is the only file that keeps everything, and the only one ModelCraft opens directly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave first (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), so your working file is safe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the format. The browser downloads it, named like the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole model is exported. To export one part, save, erase the rest, export, then undo or reopen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-format\"\u003EWhich format\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou want to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoose\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in another 3D modeler\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps groups, nested groups, components (shared, not copied), names and colors. Z up, meters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERender it, or use it in a visualization program\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ (.obj)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF binary (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOBJ is the most widely read; it writes each group placement as a separate object, but has no colors and no shared components. glTF keeps components and colors.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it on the web, in a game engine or in AR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF binary (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne file with everything in it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint it in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChecks the model is closed and faces the right way, then writes STL or 3MF in millimeters. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003EGet a model ready for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL for printing (.stl, millimeters)\u003C/span\u003E writes the whole model without the check.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in BimCraft or another BIM program\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach top-level group or component becomes one building element.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar confirms each export, for example &quot;Exported House.stl (millimeters).&quot; or &quot;Exported House.dae (Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters; components stay shared).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-export\"\u003EBefore you export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroup your objects.\u003C/strong\u003E Collada, glTF and IFC keep groups and components as separate objects. Loose geometry goes out as one lump; in IFC it becomes one element called &quot;Loose geometry&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName them.\u003C/strong\u003E Group and component names go into Collada and IFC, so a wall called &quot;North wall&quot; arrives as &quot;North wall&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck face direction.\u003C/strong\u003E Faces showing blue-grey on the outside will point inward in the export. Fix them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/erase#a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/erase\" data-hash=\"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EErase and clean up\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFor printing,\u003C/strong\u003E make the parts closed solids: select each group and check that \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says &quot;Solid: Yes&quot;. Then scale the model to the printed size, or scale it in the printer software.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-model-to-bimcraft\"\u003ESend a model to BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake each building part a group or component and name it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the downloaded \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file in BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no direct link between the two apps yet. The IFC has one storey, and every element is a generic building element proxy with no type or properties: it carries shape and name only. Closed groups become solid shapes; open ones become surfaces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lines-without-faces\"\u003ELines without faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdges that are not part of any face (guide-like lines, an imported site plan) go into OBJ and Collada as lines. glTF and STL have no place for them and leave them out. Guides and scenes are not exported to any format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-view-as-an-image\"\u003ESave the view as an image\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage of the view (.png)\u003C/span\u003E saves exactly what the 3D view shows, at its size on screen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage at twice the size (.png)\u003C/span\u003E saves it twice as wide and twice as tall (for print or a sharp slide), with dimensions and marks redrawn at that size rather than stretched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a picture that looks like daylight (sky, sun, soft shadows, glass), use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E instead: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a clean picture, turn on \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003Epresentation view\u003C/a\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) first: the axes, guides, section outlines and the selection are left out. Set up the camera, style and shadows the way you want them, or go to a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E; to save a moving film of your scenes, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\"\u003EPrint the view, or save it as a PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF of the view (.pdf)\u003C/span\u003E saves a one-page PDF: the view at twice its screen size, with its dimensions, on an A4 page (Letter when the model is in feet and inches), landscape, with a line under it giving the file name, the date and the unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint the view…\u003C/span\u003E puts the same page in front of your browser&#039;s print dialog. Choose a printer, or &quot;Save as PDF&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe page shows the view as it is: there is no scale on a perspective view. For a drawing to scale, switch \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E off and look from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, and add \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Edimensions\u003C/a\u003E for the sizes that matter. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/a\u003E leaves out the axes, guides and the selection; dimensions stay.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Export makes a copy of the model in another format. Keep your .modelcraft file too: it is the only file that keeps everything, and the only one ModelCraft opens directly. Export Save first (Ctrl+S), so your working file is safe. Click Export in the File group. Choose the format. The browser downloads it, named like the model. The whole model is exported. To export one part, save, erase the rest, export, then undo or reopen. Which format You want to Choose Notes Open it in another 3D modeler Collada (.dae) Keeps groups, nested groups, components (shared, not copied), names and colors. Z up, meters. Render it, or use it in a visualization program OBJ (.obj) or glTF binary (.glb) OBJ is the most widely read; it writes each group placement as a separate object, but has no colors and no shared components. glTF keeps components and colors. Put it on the web, in a game engine or in AR glTF binary (.glb) One file with everything in it. Print it in 3D Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)… Checks the model is closed and faces the right way, then writes STL or 3MF in millimeters. See Get a model ready for 3D printing. STL for printing (.stl, millimeters) writes the whole model without the check. Open it in BimCraft or another BIM program IFC for BimCraft (.ifc) Each top-level group or component becomes one building element. The status bar confirms each export, for example \"Exported House.stl (millimeters).\" or \"Exported House.dae (Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters; components stay shared).\" Before you export Group your objects. Collada, glTF and IFC keep groups and components as separate objects. Loose geometry goes out as one lump; in IFC it becomes one element called \"Loose geometry\". Name them. Group and component names go into Collada and IFC, so a wall called \"North wall\" arrives as \"North wall\". Check face direction. Faces showing blue-grey on the outside will point inward in the export. Fix them with Reverse face (see Erase and clean up). For printing, make the parts closed solids: select each group and check that Entity info says \"Solid: Yes\". Then scale the model to the printed size, or scale it in the printer software. Send a model to BimCraft Make each building part a group or component and name it. Choose Export, then IFC for BimCraft (.ifc). Open the downloaded .ifc file in BimCraft. There is no direct link between the two apps yet. The IFC has one storey, and every element is a generic building element proxy with no type or properties: it carries shape and name only. Closed groups become solid shapes; open ones become surfaces. Lines without faces Edges that are not part of any face (guide-like lines, an imported site plan) go into OBJ and Collada as lines. glTF and STL have no place for them and leave them out. Guides and scenes are not exported to any format. Save the view as an image Export › Image of the view (.png) saves exactly what the 3D view shows, at its size on screen. Image at twice the size (.png) saves it twice as wide and twice as tall (for print or a sharp slide), with dimensions and marks redrawn at that size rather than stretched. For a picture that looks like daylight (sky, sun, soft shadows, glass), use Export › Render a picture… instead: see Render a picture. For a clean picture, turn on presentation view (Shift+P) first: the axes, guides, section outlines and the selection are left out. Set up the camera, style and shadows the way you want them, or go to a scene; to save a moving film of your scenes, use Export animation in the Scenes panel. Print the view, or save it as a PDF Export › PDF of the view (.pdf) saves a one-page PDF: the view at twice its screen size, with its dimensions, on an A4 page (Letter when the model is in feet and inches), landscape, with a line under it giving the file name, the date and the unit. Export › Print the view… puts the same page in front of your browser's print dialog. Choose a printer, or \"Save as PDF\". The page shows the view as it is: there is no scale on a perspective view. For a drawing to scale, switch Perspective off and look from Top or Front, and add dimensions for the sizes that matter. Presentation view leaves out the axes, guides and the selection; dimensions stay.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Model together with Live TeamWork","summary":"What changes in ModelCraft while you are in a Live TeamWork session from CadCraft Studio: everyone's groups and components in one model, who can reshape what at once, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["live","live teamwork","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","group","component","push pull","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"work-in-your-own-group","text":"Work in your own group","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a model while several people work on it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share, Join); this page is about the model itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-in-your-own-group\"\u003EWork in your own group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEveryone edits the same model, and your own edits show at once. How changes merge depends on what you change:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMoving, naming, colouring or hiding placed groups and components\u003C/strong\u003E merges: two people moving two groups both keep their moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe geometry inside one group\u003C/strong\u003E (its edges and faces: drawing, push/pull, erasing) is one piece. When two people reshape the same group at the same moment, the second change is taken back and the status bar says so. Different groups never get in each other&#039;s way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoose geometry\u003C/strong\u003E (not in any group) is the model&#039;s own group: only one person can reshape it at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESo agree who works in which group, or make a group for each person&#039;s part before you start. Guides, dimensions and section planes merge one by one; scenes, tags, storeys and the drawing axes go to whoever changed them last.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last step and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. ModelCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s model once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a model while several people work on it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share, Join); this page is about the model itself. Work in your own group Everyone edits the same model, and your own edits show at once. How changes merge depends on what you change: Moving, naming, colouring or hiding placed groups and components merges: two people moving two groups both keep their moves. The geometry inside one group (its edges and faces: drawing, push/pull, erasing) is one piece. When two people reshape the same group at the same moment, the second change is taken back and the status bar says so. Different groups never get in each other's way. Loose geometry (not in any group) is the model's own group: only one person can reshape it at a time. So agree who works in which group, or make a group for each person's part before you start. Guides, dimensions and section planes merge one by one; scenes, tags, storeys and the drawing axes go to whoever changed them last. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last step and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. ModelCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's model once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"usd","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Save, open and export USD","summary":"Save the whole model as USD (.usda, .usdc or .usdz) with nothing lost, open it again, and import USD scenes from other 3D programs.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["usd","usda","usdc","usdz","openusd","universal scene description","save as","export","import","scene assembly","archviz","openpbr","materialx","package"],"headings":[{"id":"save-as-usd","text":"Save as USD","level":2},{"id":"nothing-is-lost","text":"Nothing is lost","level":2},{"id":"open-or-import-someone-else-s-usd","text":"Open or import someone else's USD","level":2},{"id":"not-yet","text":"Not yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EUSD is a scene format many 3D, rendering and archviz programs share. ModelCraft writes and reads it. \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E stays the format \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E writes; USD is a choice in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-as-usd\"\u003ESave as USD\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the format: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText (.usda)\u003C/span\u003E (readable, good for version control), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBinary (.usdc)\u003C/span\u003E (smallest and fastest) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPackage (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E (one file with the textures inside).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file downloads (in the desktop app you choose where it goes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same choices are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-is-lost\"\u003ENothing is lost\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a USD file that ModelCraft saved and you get the model back exactly: faces with their holes, soft and hidden edges, curves, groups and components, tags, scenes, dimensions, guides, section planes, lamps, imported textures and the view. Other programs see the same model as meshes, groups, prototypes for components, materials, lamps and cameras.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf another program changes the geometry of a group or component, ModelCraft opens that part as a mesh with the change kept, and says how many parts it read that way. A placement moved or deleted in another program stays moved or deleted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-or-import-someone-else-s-usd\"\u003EOpen or import someone else&#039;s USD\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E a \u003Ccode\u003E.usd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E file to start a model from it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E one to add it to the open model. Drop the files it refers to (other layers, textures) together with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMeshes come in with their normals, UV coordinates and colours, shared geometry as components, materials as paint colours, lamps as lamps and cameras as scenes, in metres with Z up whatever the file&#039;s units and up axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-yet\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnimation, skeletons and MaterialX node graphs are not read or written as USD yet. ModelCraft&#039;s own animation and node materials are kept in a USD file it saved, for ModelCraft to open again, but other programs don&#039;t see them. Layers a file refers to that ModelCraft can&#039;t read (for example a MaterialX document) are left out with a note.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"USD is a scene format many 3D, rendering and archviz programs share. ModelCraft writes and reads it. .modelcraft stays the format Save writes; USD is a choice in Save as. Save as USD Choose Save as in the File group (or press Ctrl+Shift+S). Pick the format: Text (.usda) (readable, good for version control), Binary (.usdc) (smallest and fastest) or Package (.usdz) (one file with the textures inside). The file downloads (in the desktop app you choose where it goes). The same choices are in Export › USD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…. Nothing is lost Open a USD file that ModelCraft saved and you get the model back exactly: faces with their holes, soft and hidden edges, curves, groups and components, tags, scenes, dimensions, guides, section planes, lamps, imported textures and the view. Other programs see the same model as meshes, groups, prototypes for components, materials, lamps and cameras. If another program changes the geometry of a group or component, ModelCraft opens that part as a mesh with the change kept, and says how many parts it read that way. A placement moved or deleted in another program stays moved or deleted. Open or import someone else's USD Open a .usd, .usda, .usdc or .usdz file to start a model from it. Import one to add it to the open model. Drop the files it refers to (other layers, textures) together with it. Meshes come in with their normals, UV coordinates and colours, shared geometry as components, materials as paint colours, lamps as lamps and cameras as scenes, in metres with Z up whatever the file's units and up axis. Not yet Animation, skeletons and MaterialX node graphs are not read or written as USD yet. ModelCraft's own animation and node materials are kept in a USD file it saved, for ModelCraft to open again, but other programs don't see them. Layers a file refers to that ModelCraft can't read (for example a MaterialX document) are left out with a note.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Coming from other modelers","summary":"How push/pull modeling habits map to ModelCraft, key by key, and the differences that catch people out.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","differences","push pull modeler","skp","shortcuts","habits","compare","measurements box","outliner","entity info"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-that-carry-over","text":"Habits that carry over","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-catch-people-out","text":"Differences that catch people out","level":2},{"id":"saving-is-a-download","text":"Saving is a download","level":3},{"id":"it-imports-skp-files-but-does-not-save-them","text":"It imports .skp files, but does not save them","level":3},{"id":"no-right-click-menu","text":"No right-click menu","level":3},{"id":"units-live-in-the-status-bar","text":"Units live in the status bar","level":3},{"id":"paint-has-its-own-materials","text":"Paint has its own materials","level":3},{"id":"scale-is-uniform-without-grips","text":"Scale is uniform, without grips","level":3},{"id":"rotate-picks-its-plane-from-where-you-click","text":"Rotate picks its plane from where you click","level":3},{"id":"inference-from-points-you-pass-over","text":"Inference from points you pass over","level":3},{"id":"parallel-uses-the-edge-you-passed-over-before-starting","text":"Parallel uses the edge you passed over before starting","level":3},{"id":"copies-of-a-group-are-linked","text":"Copies of a group are linked","level":3},{"id":"space-does-not-pan","text":"Space does not pan","level":3},{"id":"two-finger-scroll-zooms","text":"Two-finger scroll zooms","level":3},{"id":"solids-pushed-into-each-other-do-not-cut","text":"Solids pushed into each other do not cut","level":3},{"id":"what-is-missing","text":"What is missing","level":3},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have modeled in SketchUp, ModelCraft will feel familiar: the same idea of faces and push/pull, the same tool letters, a value box you type into without clicking, inference with red, green and blue axes, groups, components, an Outliner, Entity Info and scenes. This page lists what maps across directly and, more importantly, what does not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-that-carry-over\"\u003EHabits that carry over\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn ModelCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress Space for Select, L Line, R Rectangle, C Circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress P Push/Pull, M Move, Q Rotate, S Scale, E Eraser, T Tape Measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress O Orbit, H Pan, Z Zoom, Shift+Z Zoom Extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress G to make a component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType into the Measurements box without clicking it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, in the value box at the bottom. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E for a rectangle, \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E after a Move copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same; \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E also works\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for a point or offset\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a bare number in an architectural-inch model and get inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-drag to orbit, Shift+middle-drag to pan, wheel to zoom at the cursor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click with Push/Pull to repeat the distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys to lock red, green, blue, and parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E red, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E green, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E blue, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold Shift to lock an inference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl (Option on a Mac) to copy while moving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, on any computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift to toggle, Ctrl to add, Shift+Ctrl to remove from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same keys; on a Mac use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where it says Ctrl\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-to-right window, right-to-left crossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA Dimension tool to label lengths\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and more: type a new length into a dimension and the model resizes to it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a face for face plus edges; triple-click for all connected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a group to edit it; Esc to close\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a line across a face to split it; erase an edge between coplanar faces to heal them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPush a window rectangle through a wall to cut it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack faces in blue-grey; Reverse Faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same color; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScenes as tabs over the view, Page Up and Page Down\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShadows from date, time, latitude and longitude, with north on green\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-catch-people-out\"\u003EDifferences that catch people out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"saving-is-a-download\"\u003ESaving is a download\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Save that overwrites the file you opened, no Save As dialog with a name, no autosave and no backup file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) downloads a fresh \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file every time, usually to your downloads folder. Save often, and rename or move the files yourself. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\"\u003EOpen, save, import and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"it-imports-skp-files-but-does-not-save-them\"\u003EIt imports .skp files, but does not save them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads SketchUp files with ModelCraft&#039;s own reader, in the page: groups, components, colors, textures on faces, tags and soft edges come across. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#skp-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"skp-files\"\u003EFile formats in detail\u003C/a\u003E. ModelCraft cannot save \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E: to go back, export Collada from ModelCraft and import it in SketchUp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-right-click-menu\"\u003ENo right-click menu\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-drag pans; a right-click does nothing. Commands you would find in the context menu are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E) and in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"units-live-in-the-status-bar\"\u003EUnits live in the status bar\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Model Info or template choice. The unit picker is at the bottom right. A new model starts in feet and inches in the US and in millimeters elsewhere, from your browser&#039;s language setting. Decimals always take a period: \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m\u003C/code\u003E, never \u003Ccode\u003E2,5m\u003C/code\u003E, because the comma always separates values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"paint-has-its-own-materials\"\u003EPaint has its own materials\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) paints solid colors and the materials in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E section: brick, siding, roofing, wood, stone and more, drawn by ModelCraft. You can&#039;t load your own image as a material yet; textures that come in with imported files are kept. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/paint\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/paint\"\u003EPaint with colors and materials\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-is-uniform-without-grips\"\u003EScale is uniform, without grips\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScale does not show a box with grips. Click the point that stays put, a reference point, then the new size or a typed factor. It always scales evenly in all directions. To stretch in one direction, push/pull or move a face, retype a group&#039;s width, depth or height in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Eresize with a dimension\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rotate-picks-its-plane-from-where-you-click\"\u003ERotate picks its plane from where you click\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no protractor that sticks to faces as you hover with arrow keys to pick the plane. Click the center on a face to turn in that face&#039;s plane, or on the ground to turn about the vertical. The arrow keys orbit the view before the first click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"inference-from-points-you-pass-over\"\u003EInference from points you pass over\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft snaps to axis directions from \u003Cstrong\u003Eyour start point\u003C/strong\u003E, and to points, edges and faces under the pointer. It does not track a direction from a point you merely hovered (the dotted &quot;from point&quot; inference). To line up with a far corner, lock the axis with an arrow key and hover that corner: the locked point slides to line up with it. Or place a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Eguide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"parallel-uses-the-edge-you-passed-over-before-starting\"\u003EParallel uses the edge you passed over before starting\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe parallel and perpendicular inference uses the last edge the pointer crossed \u003Cstrong\u003Ebefore the first click\u003C/strong\u003E, not edges you hover while drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#parallel-and-perpendicular\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"parallel-and-perpendicular\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copies-of-a-group-are-linked\"\u003ECopies of a group are linked\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopying a group with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E makes a second placement of the same group, so editing one changes both, as with components. SketchUp makes groups independent. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"space-does-not-pan\"\u003ESpace does not pan\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, as in SketchUp. In some other web apps holding Space pans; here it doesn&#039;t. Right-drag pans instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"two-finger-scroll-zooms\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll zooms\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Scrolling to Trackpad: two fingers orbit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and two fingers pan, a pinch zooms. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#on-a-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your hands know Blender&#039;s keys rather than SketchUp&#039;s, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E menu style: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E act on the selection at once, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E extrudes (push/pull), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E duplicates, and a header menu bar (File, Edit, View, Select, Add, Object, Mesh) replaces the toolbar. Coming from SketchUp, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush-pull\u003C/span\u003E style gives a menu bar like its own over the same tool letters. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"solids-pushed-into-each-other-do-not-cut\"\u003ESolids pushed into each other do not cut\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPush/pull cuts through the face behind it in the same object, but two separate solids pushed into each other don&#039;t intersect. There is no Intersect Faces and no Solid Tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-is-missing\"\u003EWhat is missing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFreehand, Trim, Split and Outer Shell, your own image materials, extensions, and a layout tool for sheets are not in this build. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft runs in a browser, so a few shortcuts belong to the browser, not the app. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens a browser window (use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E), and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the tab. If you have unsaved changes, the browser asks before closing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have modeled in SketchUp, ModelCraft will feel familiar: the same idea of faces and push/pull, the same tool letters, a value box you type into without clicking, inference with red, green and blue axes, groups, components, an Outliner, Entity Info and scenes. This page lists what maps across directly and, more importantly, what does not. Habits that carry over You are used to In ModelCraft Press Space for Select, L Line, R Rectangle, C Circle The same: Space, L, R, C Press P Push/Pull, M Move, Q Rotate, S Scale, E Eraser, T Tape Measure The same: P, M, Q, S, E, T Press O Orbit, H Pan, Z Zoom, Shift+Z Zoom Extents The same: O, H, Z, Shift+Z Press G to make a component The same: G. Shift+G makes a group Type into the Measurements box without clicking it The same, in the value box at the bottom. Press Enter Type 4m,3m for a rectangle, 12s for sides The same Type 3x or /3 after a Move copy The same; *3 also works Type [x,y,z] or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for a point or offset The same Type a bare number in an architectural-inch model and get inches The same Middle-drag to orbit, Shift+middle-drag to pan, wheel to zoom at the cursor The same Double-click with Push/Pull to repeat the distance The same Arrow keys to lock red, green, blue, and parallel The same: → red, ← green, ↑ blue, ↓ parallel Hold Shift to lock an inference The same Ctrl (Option on a Mac) to copy while moving Ctrl, Ctrl or Alt, on any computer Shift to toggle, Ctrl to add, Shift+Ctrl to remove from the selection The same keys; on a Mac use Ctrl where it says Ctrl Left-to-right window, right-to-left crossing The same A Dimension tool to label lengths D, and more: type a new length into a dimension and the model resizes to it. See Dimension and resize Double-click a face for face plus edges; triple-click for all connected The same Double-click a group to edit it; Esc to close The same Draw a line across a face to split it; erase an edge between coplanar faces to heal them The same Push a window rectangle through a wall to cut it The same Back faces in blue-grey; Reverse Faces The same color; Reverse face in Entity info Scenes as tabs over the view, Page Up and Page Down The same, with Play in the Scenes panel Shadows from date, time, latitude and longitude, with north on green The same, in the Style panel Differences that catch people out Saving is a download There is no Save that overwrites the file you opened, no Save As dialog with a name, no autosave and no backup file. Save (Ctrl+S) downloads a fresh .modelcraft file every time, usually to your downloads folder. Save often, and rename or move the files yourself. See Open, save, import and export. It imports .skp files, but does not save them Import reads SketchUp files with ModelCraft's own reader, in the page: groups, components, colors, textures on faces, tags and soft edges come across. See File formats in detail. ModelCraft cannot save .skp: to go back, export Collada from ModelCraft and import it in SketchUp. No right-click menu Right-drag pans; a right-click does nothing. Commands you would find in the context menu are in Entity info (Reverse face, Edit, Explode, Make unique, Make component) and in the toolbar's Organize group. Units live in the status bar There is no Model Info or template choice. The unit picker is at the bottom right. A new model starts in feet and inches in the US and in millimeters elsewhere, from your browser's language setting. Decimals always take a period: 2.5m, never 2,5m, because the comma always separates values. Paint has its own materials Paint (B) paints solid colors and the materials in the Materials section: brick, siding, roofing, wood, stone and more, drawn by ModelCraft. You can't load your own image as a material yet; textures that come in with imported files are kept. See Paint with colors and materials. Scale is uniform, without grips Scale does not show a box with grips. Click the point that stays put, a reference point, then the new size or a typed factor. It always scales evenly in all directions. To stretch in one direction, push/pull or move a face, retype a group's width, depth or height in Entity info, or resize with a dimension. Rotate picks its plane from where you click There is no protractor that sticks to faces as you hover with arrow keys to pick the plane. Click the center on a face to turn in that face's plane, or on the ground to turn about the vertical. The arrow keys orbit the view before the first click. Inference from points you pass over ModelCraft snaps to axis directions from your start point, and to points, edges and faces under the pointer. It does not track a direction from a point you merely hovered (the dotted \"from point\" inference). To line up with a far corner, lock the axis with an arrow key and hover that corner: the locked point slides to line up with it. Or place a guide. Parallel uses the edge you passed over before starting The parallel and perpendicular inference uses the last edge the pointer crossed before the first click, not edges you hover while drawing. See Inference and snapping. Copies of a group are linked Copying a group with Move makes a second placement of the same group, so editing one changes both, as with components. SketchUp makes groups independent. See Troubleshooting. Space does not pan Space picks Select, as in SketchUp. In some other web apps holding Space pans; here it doesn't. Right-drag pans instead. Two-finger scroll zooms On a trackpad, set Settings › ModelCraft › Scrolling to Trackpad: two fingers orbit, Shift and two fingers pan, a pinch zooms. See Orbit, pan and zoom. If your hands know Blender's keys rather than SketchUp's, pick the Modeler menu style: G, R and S act on the selection at once, E extrudes (push/pull), Shift+D duplicates, and a header menu bar (File, Edit, View, Select, Add, Object, Mesh) replaces the toolbar. Coming from SketchUp, the Push-pull style gives a menu bar like its own over the same tool letters. See Menus, keys and mouse. Solids pushed into each other do not cut Push/pull cuts through the face behind it in the same object, but two separate solids pushed into each other don't intersect. There is no Intersect Faces and no Solid Tools. What is missing Freehand, Trim, Split and Outer Shell, your own image materials, extensions, and a layout tool for sheets are not in this build. Not built yet lists what to do instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps ModelCraft runs in a browser, so a few shortcuts belong to the browser, not the app. Ctrl+N opens a browser window (use New), and Ctrl+W closes the tab. If you have unsaved changes, the browser asks before closing. See Keys, mice and trackpads.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"modelcraft","title":"ModelCraft settings","summary":"Pick the key style you know, make a trackpad orbit and pan without the camera buttons, and turn snapping and solid collisions on or off.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","controls","key style","keymap","shortcuts","blender","trackpad","touchpad","scroll","orbit","pinch","snap","snapping","collide","collision","overlap","intersect","pass through"],"headings":[{"id":"controls-which-keys-and-mouse-buttons","text":"Controls: which keys and mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad","text":"Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad","level":2},{"id":"snapping","text":"Snapping","level":2},{"id":"solids-stop-where-they-touch","text":"Solids stop where they touch","level":2},{"id":"3d-printer-bed","text":"3D printer bed","level":2},{"id":"orbit-and-pan","text":"Orbit and pan","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). ModelCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-which-keys-and-mouse-buttons\"\u003EControls: which keys and mouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo key styles. Switch them here, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: Push-pull\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: Modeler\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom right of the view, or at the end of the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPush-pull style\u003C/strong\u003E (the start; the letters of push-pull modelers such as SketchUp): a letter picks a tool — \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E push/pull, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scale, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E make component. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse actions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModeler style\u003C/strong\u003E (the keys of mesh modelers such as Blender): select first, then act on the selection straight away. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E moves it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotates it and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scales it from where the pointer is — no first click. While it moves, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E locks the red, green or blue axis, a typed number sets the distance (or degrees, or factor), and a click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it down; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a right-click puts it back. Then you are back in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe key style is the same choice as the menu style: the Modeler key style is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E menu style, and picking one picks the other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E. The full Modeler list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard#modeler-keys\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"modeler-keys\"\u003EKeys and mouse actions\u003C/a\u003E. The tooltips on the toolbar and the start card follow the style you pick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EScrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWork it out\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): ModelCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, orbits; whole wheel notches zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E: swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. You never need the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E buttons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pinch zooms in every choice. If &quot;Work it out&quot; guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the Modeler style you can also orbit by holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging, and pan with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and a drag — handy on a laptop without a middle button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snapping\"\u003ESnapping\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESnap to points, edges and axes\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start) pulls the pointer to endpoints, midpoints, edges, guides and the red, green and blue axes — see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003EInference\u003C/a\u003E. Turn it off when you want to place something freely near other geometry without it jumping. Faces, the ground and the axis locks still work with snapping off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E button at the bottom right of the view does the same for this session&#039;s work (it is saved too). In the Modeler style, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Tab\"\u003E⇧ Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solids-stop-where-they-touch\"\u003ESolids stop where they touch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStop solids where they touch\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start) keeps a wall from being dragged through another wall. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and paste-then-move with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) the selection slides up to the other solid and stops touching it; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E a face stops where it meets another solid. Resting on something and sliding along it are fine — only passing into the inside is stopped. Things that already overlap when you start can still be pulled apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo push something through on purpose, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollide\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom right (or clear the setting), make the move, and click it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECollisions are checked for moves and push/pull. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E do not check them yet, and loose geometry that touches still joins up, as it always has: keep separate things in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Egroups\u003C/a\u003E so they stay separate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-printer-bed\"\u003E3D printer bed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe size of your printer&#039;s bed: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot set\u003C/span\u003E (the start), 180 × 180 × 180, 220 × 220 × 250, 256 × 256 × 256 or 300 × 300 × 400 mm. Pick the size closest to your printer&#039;s build volume. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E then says when a model is larger than the bed; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot set\u003C/span\u003E, the size is not checked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"orbit-and-pan\"\u003EOrbit and pan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit about what is under the cursor\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start): orbit turns around the part of the model under the pointer, and pan keeps that point under the pointer; over empty space both use the center of the view. Turn it off to always orbit around the center of the view. \u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit speed\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EPan speed\u003C/strong\u003E go from 0.25× to 3× (1× is the start) and apply to the mouse, the trackpad, touch and the arrow keys. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat shows in the corner of the 3D view: \u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, ModelCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). ModelCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Controls: which keys and mouse buttons Two key styles. Switch them here, with Keys: Push-pull / Keys: Modeler at the bottom right of the view, or at the end of the toolbar's More menu: Push-pull style (the start; the letters of push-pull modelers such as SketchUp): a letter picks a tool — L line, R rectangle, P push/pull, M move, Q rotate, S scale, G make component. See Keys and mouse actions. Modeler style (the keys of mesh modelers such as Blender): select first, then act on the selection straight away. G moves it, R rotates it and S scales it from where the pointer is — no first click. While it moves, X, Y or Z locks the red, green or blue axis, a typed number sets the distance (or degrees, or factor), and a click or Enter puts it down; Esc or a right-click puts it back. Then you are back in Select. The key style is the same choice as the menu style: the Modeler key style is the Modeler menu style, and picking one picks the other. See Menus, keys and mouse. The full Modeler list is in Keys and mouse actions. The tooltips on the toolbar and the start card follow the style you pick. Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad Work it out (the start): ModelCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, orbits; whole wheel notches zoom. Mouse wheel: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer. Trackpad: swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. You never need the Orbit, Pan or Zoom buttons. A pinch zooms in every choice. If \"Work it out\" guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have. In the Modeler style you can also orbit by holding Alt and dragging, and pan with Alt+Shift and a drag — handy on a laptop without a middle button. Snapping Snap to points, edges and axes (on at the start) pulls the pointer to endpoints, midpoints, edges, guides and the red, green and blue axes — see Inference. Turn it off when you want to place something freely near other geometry without it jumping. Faces, the ground and the axis locks still work with snapping off. The Snap button at the bottom right of the view does the same for this session's work (it is saved too). In the Modeler style, Shift+Tab switches it. Solids stop where they touch Stop solids where they touch (on at the start) keeps a wall from being dragged through another wall. With Move (and G, and paste-then-move with Ctrl+D) the selection slides up to the other solid and stops touching it; with Push/pull a face stops where it meets another solid. Resting on something and sliding along it are fine — only passing into the inside is stopped. Things that already overlap when you start can still be pulled apart. To push something through on purpose, click Collide at the bottom right (or clear the setting), make the move, and click it again. Collisions are checked for moves and push/pull. Rotate and Scale do not check them yet, and loose geometry that touches still joins up, as it always has: keep separate things in groups so they stay separate. 3D printer bed The size of your printer's bed: Not set (the start), 180 × 180 × 180, 220 × 220 × 250, 256 × 256 × 256 or 300 × 300 × 400 mm. Pick the size closest to your printer's build volume. Check for 3D printing then says when a model is larger than the bed; with Not set, the size is not checked. Orbit and pan Orbit about what is under the cursor (on at the start): orbit turns around the part of the model under the pointer, and pan keeps that point under the pointer; over empty space both use the center of the view. Turn it off to always orbit around the center of the view. Orbit speed and Pan speed go from 0.25× to 3× (1× is the start) and apply to the mouse, the trackpad, touch and the arrow keys. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Corner indicator What shows in the corner of the 3D view: View cube (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. See The view cube.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the modeler you already know, give any command your own key, move commands between menus, and set what the wheel and the middle and right mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","menu bar","toolbar","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","key style","keymap","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","orbit","pan","export","import","switching","familiar","push-pull","modeler"],"headings":[{"id":"the-styles","text":"The styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft&#039;s tools are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-styles\"\u003EThe styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft&#039;s own toolbar (File, Draw, Modify, Organize, Edit, Camera) and its single-letter keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E push/pull, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scale, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eraser, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E orbit. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush-pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA menu bar (File, Edit, View, Camera, Draw, Tools, Window) over a row of the main tools, with the same single-letter keys.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA header menu bar (File, Edit, View, Select, Add, Object, Mesh) and the keys of mesh modelers: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E grab, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotate and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scale the selection at once (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E locks an axis while it moves), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E push/pull, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E delete, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select all, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option A\"\u003E⌥A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E none, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E duplicate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command G\"\u003E⌘G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E group, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hide. The middle button orbits; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button pan, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button zoom; the right button cancels what a tool is doing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeginner\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ribbon whose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake\u003C/span\u003E tab is a few big buttons: shapes to drop, holes, move, turn and size, align, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E (holes cut into the shapes) and the check for 3D printing; every other tool on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore tools\u003C/span\u003E tab. The right button orbits, the middle button pans. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/beginner\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/beginner\"\u003EThe Beginner style\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe key style you may know from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom right of the view and from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E is the same choice: picking the modeler keys there picks the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E style, and picking the push-pull keys leaves it for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E style. If you had chosen the modeler keys in an older version, ModelCraft opens in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E style once, by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to the push-pull keys but that your style gives another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In Modeler, Push/pull is E&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning, numbers always go to the value box, and while a tool is moving something its own keys win.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a menu bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel and what the buttons do in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Modeler&quot;); the built-in one never changes. Your copy reads keys the way the style it started from does: one made from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E keeps the grab, rotate and scale keys working at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps ModelCraft&#039;s buttons (the middle button orbits, the right button or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button pan). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always belongs to the tool in use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpads\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScrolling\u003C/span\u003E still decides whether two fingers orbit (trackpad) or zoom (mouse). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at ModelCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush-pull\u003C/span\u003E goes with the other apps&#039; menu-bar styles. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeler\u003C/span\u003E has no counterpart in the other apps, so it stays a ModelCraft choice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse actions\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other modelers\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft's tools are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › ModelCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The styles Style What you get ModelCraft ModelCraft's own toolbar (File, Draw, Modify, Organize, Edit, Camera) and its single-letter keys: L line, R rectangle, P push/pull, M move, Q rotate, S scale, E eraser, O orbit. The start. Push-pull A menu bar (File, Edit, View, Camera, Draw, Tools, Window) over a row of the main tools, with the same single-letter keys. Modeler A header menu bar (File, Edit, View, Select, Add, Object, Mesh) and the keys of mesh modelers: W select, G grab, R rotate and S scale the selection at once (X, Y or Z locks an axis while it moves), E push/pull, B rectangle, X delete, A select all, Alt+A none, Shift+D duplicate, Ctrl+G group, H hide. The middle button orbits; Shift and the middle button pan, Ctrl and the middle button zoom; the right button cancels what a tool is doing. Beginner A ribbon whose Make tab is a few big buttons: shapes to drop, holes, move, turn and size, align, Group (holes cut into the shapes) and the check for 3D printing; every other tool on the More tools tab. The right button orbits, the middle button pans. See The Beginner style. The key style you may know from Keys at the bottom right of the view and from Edit › More is the same choice: picking the modeler keys there picks the Modeler style, and picking the push-pull keys leaves it for the ModelCraft style. If you had chosen the modeler keys in an older version, ModelCraft opens in the Modeler style once, by itself. A key that belongs to the push-pull keys but that your style gives another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In Modeler, Push/pull is E\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning, numbers always go to the value box, and while a tool is moving something its own keys win. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key. Click a key to take it off. With a menu bar, Where it sits moves the command to another menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel and what the buttons do in the 3D view. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Modeler\"); the built-in one never changes. Your copy reads keys the way the style it started from does: one made from Modeler keeps the grab, rotate and scale keys working at once. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Drag in the 3D view: Each view's own keeps ModelCraft's buttons (the middle button orbits, the right button or Shift and the middle button pan). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always belongs to the tool in use. Trackpads: Settings › ModelCraft › Scrolling still decides whether two fingers orbit (trackpad) or zoom (mouse). See Orbit, pan and zoom. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at ModelCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style: Push-pull goes with the other apps' menu-bar styles. Modeler has no counterpart in the other apps, so it stays a ModelCraft choice. Related: Keys and mouse actions · ModelCraft settings · Coming from other modelers","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Keys and mouse actions","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut, mouse action, trackpad gesture and touch gesture in ModelCraft, grouped by what they do.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","keys","hotkeys","mouse","trackpad","touch","gestures","cheat sheet","ctrl","command","arrow keys"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"model-and-file","text":"Model and file","level":2},{"id":"esc","text":"Esc","level":2},{"id":"while-a-tool-is-working","text":"While a tool is working","level":2},{"id":"arrow-keys-at-other-times","text":"Arrow keys at other times","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"modeler-keys","text":"Modeler keys","level":2},{"id":"touch-screen","text":"Touch screen","level":2},{"id":"where-move-lives","text":"Where Move lives","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese are the push-pull keys, the ones ModelCraft starts with. For the other style, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard#modeler-keys\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"modeler-keys\"\u003EModeler keys\u003C/a\u003E below and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts work when the 3D view or a toolbar button has focus. If you just typed in a panel field or changed a list in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E, click in the 3D view first, or your keys go to that field. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E for how keys are written on each computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETool letters work without modifier keys only: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a letter does nothing, except the two below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"model-and-file\"\u003EModel and file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake component from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake group from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents (the whole model)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave (downloads the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect everything (in the open group, if one is open)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the pointer, click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place (where it was copied from)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: a copy moves with the pointer, click to put it down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext / previous scene\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help (also \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E inside the help closes it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScreen mode: full screen, then bars hidden, then normal (Control on a Mac too)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E backs out one level each time you press it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECancels what the tool is in the middle of (a line being drawn, a push/pull, a move). Everything goes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitches to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloses the group you are editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt also stops scenes from playing. In the value box, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears what you typed and returns to the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-a-tool-is-working\"\u003EWhile a tool is working\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA digit, or one of \u003Ccode\u003E. , - &#039; &quot; [ &lt; * /\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarts typing in the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies what you typed in the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the red axis (press again to unlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the green axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the blue axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks parallel to the last edge you hovered before the first click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds the direction you are snapped to, while held\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Move)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitches between moving and copying\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArrow-key locks work in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E (when measuring from a point), after the first click. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E always moves along the face&#039;s direction and has no locks. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E the arrow keys show a &quot;Locked&quot; message after the first click but do not change the turn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrow-keys-at-other-times\"\u003EArrow keys at other times\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen no tool is in the middle of something, the arrow keys move the camera:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with an arrow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUses the current tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress, drag, release\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as two clicks (for drawing tools); a box selection (for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E); erases along the way (for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: face with its edges, edge with its faces, opens a group; empty space closes the group. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E: repeats the last distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriple-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: everything connected\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: toggle in and out of the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: add to the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: remove from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + middle-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no right-click menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a two-finger swipe does depends on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Scrolling:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETrackpad (or worked out)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + two-finger swipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger press and drag (secondary click held)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#on-a-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modeler-keys\"\u003EModeler keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Controls set to Modeler style:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E (extrude)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection at once, from the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate the selection about its middle (blue axis)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale the selection about its middle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while moving or rotating\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock the red / green / blue axis (again to unlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate and move the copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option A\"\u003E⌥A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect all / select nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the selected group or component; again to close it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command G\"\u003E⌘G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command G\"\u003E⇧⌘G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake group / make component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"1\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"7\"\u003E7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront / right / top view (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for back / left / bottom)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"5\"\u003E5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerspective on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\".\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom to the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Tab\"\u003E⇧ Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-button drag / with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit / pan / zoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit / pan (no middle button needed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopy, paste, undo, save and open keep the keys listed above. Typed values, the value box and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E work the same in both styles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screen\"\u003ETouch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA click with the current tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA double-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress, drag, lift\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as press, drag, release with a mouse\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers: pinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers: move together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (Settings › ModelCraft › Touch screen can make it Orbit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThree fingers: move together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit (Pan when two fingers orbit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold one finger still\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinishes the tool, as Enter; with nothing going on, then drag to orbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA second finger during a one-finger action\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels that action\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E tool, then drag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo type a value on a touch screen, tap the value box at the bottom, type, and press the keyboard&#039;s Enter (or Go) key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-move-lives\"\u003EWhere Move lives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove lives in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E tool, key \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: pick what to move, click a base point, then a second point or type a distance. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\"\u003EMove, copy and make arrays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"These are the push-pull keys, the ones ModelCraft starts with. For the other style, see Modeler keys below and ModelCraft settings. Shortcuts work when the 3D view or a toolbar button has focus. If you just typed in a panel field or changed a list in Style, click in the 3D view first, or your keys go to that field. See Keys, mice and trackpads for how keys are written on each computer. Tools Key Tool Space Select L Line R Rectangle C Circle N Polygon A Arc F Offset B Paint P Push/pull M Move Q Rotate S Scale E Eraser T Tape measure D Dimension O Orbit H Pan Z Zoom Tool letters work without modifier keys only: Shift with a letter does nothing, except the two below. Model and file Key Does G Make component from the selection Shift+G Make group from the selection Shift+Z Zoom extents (the whole model) Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Ctrl+S Save (downloads the .modelcraft file) Ctrl+O Open a model Ctrl+A Select everything (in the open group, if one is open) Ctrl+C Copy the selection Ctrl+X Cut the selection Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the pointer, click where it goes Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place (where it was copied from) Ctrl+D Duplicate: a copy moves with the pointer, click to put it down Delete or Backspace Erase the selection Page Down / Page Up Next / previous scene F1 Open or close this help (also Help in the app bar; Esc inside the help closes it) Ctrl+Shift+F Screen mode: full screen, then bars hidden, then normal (Control on a Mac too) Esc Esc backs out one level each time you press it: Cancels what the tool is in the middle of (a line being drawn, a push/pull, a move). Everything goes back. Switches to Select. Clears the selection. Closes the group you are editing. It also stops scenes from playing. In the value box, Esc clears what you typed and returns to the view. While a tool is working Key Does A digit, or one of . , - ' \" [ \u003C * / Starts typing in the value box Enter Applies what you typed in the value box → Locks to the red axis (press again to unlock) ← Locks to the green axis ↑ Locks to the blue axis ↓ Locks parallel to the last edge you hovered before the first click Hold Shift Holds the direction you are snapped to, while held Ctrl, Ctrl or Alt (Move) Switches between moving and copying Arrow-key locks work in Line, Rectangle, Circle, Polygon, Move, Scale and Tape (when measuring from a point), after the first click. Push/pull always moves along the face's direction and has no locks. In Rotate the arrow keys show a \"Locked\" message after the first click but do not change the turn. Arrow keys at other times When no tool is in the middle of something, the arrow keys move the camera: Key Does ← → ↑ ↓ Orbit Shift with an arrow Pan Mouse Action Does Click Uses the current tool Press, drag, release Same as two clicks (for drawing tools); a box selection (for Select); erases along the way (for Eraser) Double-click Select: face with its edges, edge with its faces, opens a group; empty space closes the group. Push/pull: repeats the last distance Triple-click Select: everything connected Shift + click Select: toggle in and out of the selection Ctrl + click Select: add to the selection Shift + Ctrl + click Select: remove from the selection Middle-button drag Orbit, with any tool Shift + middle-button drag Pan, with any tool Right-button drag Pan, with any tool Wheel Zoom toward the pointer There is no right-click menu. Trackpad What a two-finger swipe does depends on Settings › ModelCraft › Scrolling: Gesture Trackpad (or worked out) Mouse wheel Two-finger swipe Orbit Zoom toward the pointer Shift + two-finger swipe Pan Zoom Pinch Zoom toward the pointer Zoom toward the pointer Two-finger press and drag (secondary click held) Pan Pan See Orbit, pan and zoom and ModelCraft settings. Modeler keys With Settings › ModelCraft › Controls set to Modeler style: Key Does W or Space Select L / B / C / N Line / Rectangle / Circle / Polygon E Push/pull (extrude) T Tape measure D Dimension G Move the selection at once, from the pointer R Rotate the selection about its middle (blue axis) S Scale the selection about its middle X / Y / Z while moving or rotating Lock the red / green / blue axis (again to unlock) Click or Enter Put it down Esc or right-click Put it back Shift+D Duplicate and move the copy X or Delete Delete the selection A / Alt+A Select all / select nothing Tab Open the selected group or component; again to close it Ctrl+G / Ctrl+Shift+G Make group / make component Numpad 1 / 3 / 7 Front / right / top view (Ctrl for back / left / bottom) Numpad 5 Perspective on or off Numpad . Zoom to the selection Home or Shift+C Zoom extents Shift+Tab Snapping on or off Middle-button drag / with Shift / with Ctrl Orbit / pan / zoom Alt + drag / Alt+Shift + drag Orbit / pan (no middle button needed) Copy, paste, undo, save and open keep the keys listed above. Typed values, the value box and Push/pull work the same in both styles. Touch screen Gesture Does Tap A click with the current tool Double-tap A double-click Press, drag, lift Same as press, drag, release with a mouse Two fingers: pinch Zoom Two fingers: move together Pan (Settings › ModelCraft › Touch screen can make it Orbit) Three fingers: move together Orbit (Pan when two fingers orbit) Hold one finger still Finishes the tool, as Enter; with nothing going on, then drag to orbit A second finger during a one-finger action Cancels that action Orbit tool, then drag one finger Orbit To type a value on a touch screen, tap the value box at the bottom, type, and press the keyboard's Enter (or Go) key. Where Move lives Move lives in the Move tool, key M: pick what to move, click a base point, then a second point or type a distance. See Move, copy and make arrays.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"tools","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Tool list","summary":"Every ModelCraft tool with its key, what each click does, and what you can type into the value box while it works.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["tools","toolbar","commands","list","line","rectangle","circle","polygon","offset","arc","curve","spline","follow me","sweep","2 point arc","arc from center","select","push pull","move","rotate","scale","eraser","tape","dimension","orbit","pan","zoom","place component"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-tools","text":"Drawing tools","level":2},{"id":"editing-tools","text":"Editing tools","level":2},{"id":"camera-tools","text":"Camera tools","level":2},{"id":"organize","text":"Organize","level":2},{"id":"place-component","text":"Place component","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s tip always says what the current tool wants next. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels what a tool is in the middle of.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-tools\"\u003EDrawing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EClicks\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType in the value box\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart, then each next point. Stops when a face closes, or on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length toward the pointer; \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E a point; \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E an offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst corner (ground or a face), opposite corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth and depth: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter (ground or a face), a point on the edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBefore the first click: sides, \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E24\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESides\u003C/span\u003E). After: the radius (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, a corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as Circle; starts with 6 sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart, end, then the bulge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe chord&#039;s length after the start (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E); the bulge after the end (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBulge\u003C/span\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProtractor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, 0° point, then the angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle in degrees (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a path, then click the face (or click the face, then an edge of the path)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face, group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E— (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E connected, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E all of that color, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pick up a color)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face (or select a face or edges first), then the side and distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe distance (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E); into the face is inward\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints the curve passes through; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a double-click finishes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length along the pointer, \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for the next point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc center\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, start, then the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe radius after the center; the angle in degrees after the start; \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-tools\"\u003EEditing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EClicks\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType in the value box\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick, double-click, triple-click, drag a box. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/select\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/select\"\u003ESelect things\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith one dimension or edge selected: a new length, which resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face, then the distance. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance; negative goes the other way (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom a point, to a point. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance toward the pointer, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E). After a copy: \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter (on a face to turn in its plane), start direction, end direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle in degrees (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point that stays, a reference point, the new size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA factor: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick or drag over edges, groups, guides and dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom an edge: a parallel guide line. From a point: measures and leaves a guide point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E) or the distance (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo corners (or one edge), then where the dimension line goes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E). Right after: a new length, which resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E work on the selection. If nothing is selected, they take what you click first (a face brings its edges).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"camera-tools\"\u003ECamera tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrag to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn around the part of the model under the pointer (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pans)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlide the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom: up is in, down is out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(a button, not a tool) Fit the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou rarely need these with a mouse: middle-drag orbits, right-drag pans and the wheel zooms with any tool. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organize\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponent\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes the selection a component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes the selection a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns the selected groups and components back into loose geometry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-component\"\u003EPlace component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot on the toolbar: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E next to a component in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel. Click once where the component&#039;s origin goes; the tool then returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The status bar's tip always says what the current tool wants next. Esc cancels what a tool is in the middle of. Drawing tools Tool Key Clicks Type in the value box Line L Start, then each next point. Stops when a face closes, or on Esc A length toward the pointer; [x,y,z] a point; \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E an offset (Length) Rectangle R First corner (ground or a face), opposite corner Width and depth: 4m,3m (Dimensions) Circle C Center (ground or a face), a point on the edge Before the first click: sides, 24s or 24 (Sides). After: the radius (Radius) Polygon N Center, a corner Same as Circle; starts with 6 sides Arc A Start, end, then the bulge The chord's length after the start (Length); the bulge after the end (Bulge); 12s for segments Protractor — Center, 0° point, then the angle The angle in degrees (Angle) Follow me — Select a path, then click the face (or click the face, then an edge of the path) — Paint B A face, group or component — (Ctrl connected, Shift all of that color, Alt pick up a color) Offset F A face (or select a face or edges first), then the side and distance The distance (Distance); into the face is inward Curve — Points the curve passes through; Enter or a double-click finishes A length along the pointer, [x,y,z] or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for the next point Arc center — Center, start, then the end The radius after the center; the angle in degrees after the start; 12s for segments Editing tools Tool Key Clicks Type in the value box Select Space Click, double-click, triple-click, drag a box. See Select things With one dimension or edge selected: a new length, which resizes the model Push/pull P The face, then the distance. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance A distance; negative goes the other way (Distance) Move M From a point, to a point. Hold Ctrl or Alt to copy A distance toward the pointer, \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E or [x,y,z] (Distance). After a copy: 3x or /3 Rotate Q Center (on a face to turn in its plane), start direction, end direction The angle in degrees (Angle) Scale S The point that stays, a reference point, the new size A factor: 2, 0.5 (Scale) Eraser E Click or drag over edges, groups, guides and dimensions nothing Tape T From an edge: a parallel guide line. From a point: measures and leaves a guide point The offset (Offset) or the distance (Length) Dimension D Two corners (or one edge), then where the dimension line goes. See Dimension and resize The offset (Offset). Right after: a new length, which resizes the model Move, Rotate and Scale work on the selection. If nothing is selected, they take what you click first (a face brings its edges). Camera tools Tool Key Drag to Orbit O Turn around the part of the model under the pointer (Shift pans) Pan H Slide the view Zoom Z Zoom: up is in, down is out Extents Shift+Z (a button, not a tool) Fit the whole model You rarely need these with a mouse: middle-drag orbits, right-drag pans and the wheel zooms with any tool. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Organize Button Key Does Component G Makes the selection a component Group Shift+G Makes the selection a group Explode none Turns the selected groups and components back into loose geometry Place component Not on the toolbar: click Place next to a component in the Components panel. Click once where the component's origin goes; the tool then returns to Select.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"inference","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Inference and snapping","summary":"What the colored markers mean, which snap wins, and how to lock a direction with the arrow keys or Shift.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["inference","snap","snapping","endpoint","midpoint","center of face","centre","on edge","on face","axis","red axis","green axis","blue axis","parallel","perpendicular","lock","shift","osnap"],"headings":[{"id":"markers-and-tips","text":"Markers and tips","level":2},{"id":"which-snap-wins","text":"Which snap wins","level":2},{"id":"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point","text":"Drawing on a face from a point","level":2},{"id":"parallel-and-perpendicular","text":"Parallel and perpendicular","level":2},{"id":"lock-a-direction","text":"Lock a direction","level":2},{"id":"inside-a-group","text":"Inside a group","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile a drawing or editing tool is working, ModelCraft looks at what is under the pointer and snaps to the most useful thing nearby. A marker shows what it found and the tip at the bottom left names it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"markers-and-tips\"\u003EMarkers and tips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETip\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarker\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESnaps to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end of an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMidpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECyan circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter of face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the face under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGuide point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA guide point left by the tape measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrigin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOchre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the red, green and blue axes meet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn red axis / On green axis / On blue axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line in that color\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line from your start point along that direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel to edge / Perpendicular to edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line from your start point parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge (see below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA direction you locked with an arrow key or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed square\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere along an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn guide\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere along a guide line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue diamond\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere on a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plane you started on, when the pointer is over empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe ground (the red-green plane), when nothing else is there\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen an axis line from your start point crosses an edge or guide, ModelCraft snaps to the crossing and the tip names both, such as &quot;On red axis, on edge&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-snap-wins\"\u003EWhich snap wins\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E within about 12 pixels: endpoints first, then guide points, midpoints, the center of the face under the pointer and the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDirections\u003C/strong\u003E from the start point: the red, green and blue axes, and parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn an edge\u003C/strong\u003E or a guide line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn a face.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing plane\u003C/strong\u003E, or the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThings hidden behind a face never snap. Orbit so you can see the point you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\"\u003EDrawing on a face from a point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E started on a corner, a midpoint, an edge or the center of a face is drawn \u003Cstrong\u003Ein the plane of the face under the pointer\u003C/strong\u003E, and cuts that face. An edge belongs to two faces, so the side of the edge the pointer is on decides: to draw on the end of a long box, start from the middle of one of its edges with the pointer just inside the end face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parallel-and-perpendicular\"\u003EParallel and perpendicular\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft remembers the \u003Cstrong\u003Elast edge the pointer passed over before your first click\u003C/strong\u003E. Starting on a corner or on an edge counts as passing over that edge. Once the tool is working, other edges you hover do not replace it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo draw a line parallel to an existing edge:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and hover the edge you want to match.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the start point, without crossing other edges on the way if you can, and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove in the direction parallel to that edge. The line turns magenta and the tip says &quot;Parallel to edge&quot;. Perpendicular works the same way, within the plane you are drawing on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the wrong edge was remembered, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, hover the right edge, and start again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lock-a-direction\"\u003ELock a direction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first click of a tool, you can keep the next point on one line:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELocks to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe red axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe green axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe blue axis (straight up)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel to the remembered edge (see above)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress the same arrow again to unlock. The status bar says &quot;Locked to the red axis. Press the same arrow to unlock.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile locked, the point slides along the line toward wherever the pointer is, and still lines up with points you hover: hover the corner of another box to draw a line along red exactly as far as that corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E locks whatever direction you are snapped to right now (an axis, or parallel), for as long as you hold it. Let go to unlock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELocks work in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E. Before the first click, the arrow keys orbit the view instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inside-a-group\"\u003EInside a group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you edit inside a group or component, the axes are the group&#039;s own axes (they move with it, and turn with it if it was rotated). Snaps still find points and edges outside the group, so you can line things up with the rest of the model.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"While a drawing or editing tool is working, ModelCraft looks at what is under the pointer and snaps to the most useful thing nearby. A marker shows what it found and the tip at the bottom left names it. Markers and tips Tip Marker Snaps to Endpoint Green circle The end of an edge Midpoint Cyan circle The middle of an edge Center of face Blue circle The middle of the face under the pointer Guide point Grey A guide point left by the tape measure Origin Ochre Where the red, green and blue axes meet On red axis / On green axis / On blue axis A line in that color A line from your start point along that direction Parallel to edge / Perpendicular to edge Magenta line A line from your start point parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge (see below) Locked Magenta line A direction you locked with an arrow key or Shift On edge Red square Anywhere along an edge On guide Grey Anywhere along a guide line On face Blue diamond Anywhere on a face On plane none The plane you started on, when the pointer is over empty space On the ground none The ground (the red-green plane), when nothing else is there When an axis line from your start point crosses an edge or guide, ModelCraft snaps to the crossing and the tip names both, such as \"On red axis, on edge\". Which snap wins Points within about 12 pixels: endpoints first, then guide points, midpoints, the center of the face under the pointer and the origin. Directions from the start point: the red, green and blue axes, and parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge. On an edge or a guide line. On a face. The drawing plane, or the ground. Things hidden behind a face never snap. Orbit so you can see the point you want. Drawing on a face from a point A Rectangle, Circle or Polygon started on a corner, a midpoint, an edge or the center of a face is drawn in the plane of the face under the pointer, and cuts that face. An edge belongs to two faces, so the side of the edge the pointer is on decides: to draw on the end of a long box, start from the middle of one of its edges with the pointer just inside the end face. Parallel and perpendicular ModelCraft remembers the last edge the pointer passed over before your first click. Starting on a corner or on an edge counts as passing over that edge. Once the tool is working, other edges you hover do not replace it. To draw a line parallel to an existing edge: Press L and hover the edge you want to match. Move to the start point, without crossing other edges on the way if you can, and click. Move in the direction parallel to that edge. The line turns magenta and the tip says \"Parallel to edge\". Perpendicular works the same way, within the plane you are drawing on. If the wrong edge was remembered, press Esc, hover the right edge, and start again. Lock a direction After the first click of a tool, you can keep the next point on one line: Key Locks to → The red axis ← The green axis ↑ The blue axis (straight up) ↓ Parallel to the remembered edge (see above) Press the same arrow again to unlock. The status bar says \"Locked to the red axis. Press the same arrow to unlock.\" While locked, the point slides along the line toward wherever the pointer is, and still lines up with points you hover: hover the corner of another box to draw a line along red exactly as far as that corner. Shift locks whatever direction you are snapped to right now (an axis, or parallel), for as long as you hold it. Let go to unlock. Locks work in Line, Rectangle, Circle, Polygon, Move, Scale and Tape. Before the first click, the arrow keys orbit the view instead. Inside a group While you edit inside a group or component, the axes are the group's own axes (they move with it, and turn with it if it was rotated). Snaps still find points and edges outside the group, so you can line things up with the rest of the model.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"value-box","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Type exact sizes in the value box","summary":"Every way to type lengths, sizes, points, offsets, angles, sides and copies, and how units and bare numbers are read.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["value box","measurements box","vcb","type","exact","dimension","length","units","feet","inches","metric","millimeters","coordinates","offset","array","sides","angle","decimal comma"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-type","text":"How to type","level":2},{"id":"lengths","text":"Lengths","level":2},{"id":"calculations","text":"Calculations","level":2},{"id":"per-tool","text":"Per tool","level":2},{"id":"points-and-offsets","text":"Points and offsets","level":2},{"id":"copies-after-a-move","text":"Copies after a move","level":2},{"id":"typed-lengths-need-a-direction","text":"Typed lengths need a direction","level":2},{"id":"the-model-s-unit","text":"The model's unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe value box sits in the status bar at the bottom, with a label that changes with the tool. While you work it shows the live size; at any moment you can type an exact one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-to-type\"\u003EHow to type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart the action with a click (the first corner, the face, the point to move from).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType. You don&#039;t need to click the value box: a digit or one of \u003Ccode\u003E. , - &#039; &quot; [ &lt; * /\u003C/code\u003E sends your typing there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the value box to clear it and go back to the view. On a touch screen, tap the value box to bring up the keyboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf ModelCraft cannot read what you typed, the status bar says &quot;Type a length such as 3m, 10&#039;6&quot; or 4m,3m.&quot; If the tool cannot use it, it says &quot;That did not work: …&quot; with the reason.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lengths\"\u003ELengths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E320cm\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E3200mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeters, centimeters, millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10ft\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10 feet\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6in\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6 inches\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039; 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet and inches, with a fraction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInches with a fraction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1.5ft\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.5m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimals with a unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA bare number, in the model&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA bare number uses the model&#039;s unit\u003C/strong\u003E, as shown in the picker at the bottom right: inches in a feet-and-inches model (so \u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E is 10 feet), millimeters in a millimeter model, and so on. Typing a unit always wins, whatever the model&#039;s unit. Curly quotes and prime marks (’ ” ′ ″) are read as feet and inches too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlways use a \u003Cstrong\u003Eperiod\u003C/strong\u003E for decimals: \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m\u003C/code\u003E. A comma separates two values, so \u003Ccode\u003E2,5\u003C/code\u003E means 2 and 5 (two values), whatever your language settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calculations\"\u003ECalculations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAny length can be a calculation: add \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, subtract \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, multiply \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E, divide \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E, and use brackets. No calculator needed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2.5/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA third of 2.5 (in the model&#039;s unit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;+3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039; 6 3/4&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E900 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 7/16&quot; in a feet-and-inches model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3m-25cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.75 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bare number is in the model&#039;s unit when it is a length (\u003Ccode\u003E2.5/3\u003C/code\u003E in a meter model is 0.833 m), and a plain factor when it multiplies or divides one (\u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E). Angles and factors take calculations too: \u003Ccode\u003E90/2\u003C/code\u003E is 45°. Two lengths multiplied make an area, not a length, so \u003Ccode\u003E2m*3m\u003C/code\u003E is refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf ModelCraft can&#039;t work it out, the status bar says what is wrong and where, for example &quot;Division by zero (character 4).&quot; A bracket also starts the value box, so you can begin with \u003Ccode\u003E(\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese keep their own meaning: \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E after a copy, \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E for sides, and \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E with an edge selected (it \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/divide\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/divide\"\u003Edivides the edge\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"per-tool\"\u003EPer tool\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset (below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lengths: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;,10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E. A semicolon works too: \u003Ccode\u003E4m;3m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESides\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBefore the first click, the number of sides: \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E (3 to 999). After it, the radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length. A negative one goes the other way\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset. After a copy: \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDegrees: \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E22.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA factor above 0: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.25\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDivide\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParts\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many equal parts: \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E5s\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/5\u003C/code\u003E (2 to 1000). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/divide\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/divide\"\u003EDivide an edge or curve into equal parts\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the dimension line goes; right after placing, a new length resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValue\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith one dimension or edge selected, a new length resizes the model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"points-and-offsets\"\u003EPoints and offsets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, after the first click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E goes to that exact point in the model (in the group&#039;s own axes when you are editing inside a group). Example: \u003Ccode\u003E[0,0,3m]\u003C/code\u003E is 3 m straight above the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E goes that far from the first point along red, green and blue. Example: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;0,0,10&#039;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E is 10 feet straight up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach of the three values can have its own unit: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;12&#039;,0,6&quot;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copies-after-a-move\"\u003ECopies after a move\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after a copy with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMakes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 copies in a row at the same spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 copies spread evenly from the original to the first copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to 500 copies. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy#a-row-of-copies\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\" data-hash=\"a-row-of-copies\"\u003EMove, copy and make arrays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typed-lengths-need-a-direction\"\u003ETyped lengths need a direction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA single typed length goes the way the pointer is pointing from the first click. If you type right after clicking, before moving at all, there is no direction yet and nothing happens. Move the pointer a little toward where you want to go, then type.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-model-s-unit\"\u003EThe model&#039;s unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange the unit in the picker at the bottom right: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eft and in\u003C/span\u003E, in, ft, yd, mi, mil, µm, mm, cm, m or km, as far as they are switched on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E (the model&#039;s own unit is always there). A length typed with any unit&#039;s mark is read in that unit. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnits, typed values and the unit lists\u003C/a\u003E. It changes how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read; the model stays the same size. The unit is saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow sizes are shown:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELengths\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAreas\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVolumes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eft and in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E (to 1/16&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E126 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E320 cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3200 mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em² from 0.01 m², else mm²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em³ from 0.01 m³, else mm³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new model starts in feet and inches when the browser&#039;s language is set to a US region (and Liberia and Myanmar), and in millimeters everywhere else. A unit starred in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E is used instead, when ModelCraft has it. Or change the picker after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The value box sits in the status bar at the bottom, with a label that changes with the tool. While you work it shows the live size; at any moment you can type an exact one. How to type Start the action with a click (the first corner, the face, the point to move from). Type. You don't need to click the value box: a digit or one of . , - ' \" [ \u003C * / sends your typing there. Press Enter. Press Esc in the value box to clear it and go back to the view. On a touch screen, tap the value box to bring up the keyboard. If ModelCraft cannot read what you typed, the status bar says \"Type a length such as 3m, 10'6\" or 4m,3m.\" If the tool cannot use it, it says \"That did not work: …\" with the reason. Lengths Type Means 3m 320cm 3200mm Meters, centimeters, millimeters 10' 10ft 10 feet Feet 6\" 6in 6 inches Inches 10'6\" 10' 6\" 10'-6 1/2\" Feet and inches, with a fraction 6 1/2\" 6-1/2\" 3/4\" Inches with a fraction 1.5ft .5m Decimals with a unit 120 A bare number, in the model's unit A bare number uses the model's unit, as shown in the picker at the bottom right: inches in a feet-and-inches model (so 120 is 10 feet), millimeters in a millimeter model, and so on. Typing a unit always wins, whatever the model's unit. Curly quotes and prime marks (’ ” ′ ″) are read as feet and inches too. Always use a period for decimals: 2.5m. A comma separates two values, so 2,5 means 2 and 5 (two values), whatever your language settings. Calculations Any length can be a calculation: add +, subtract -, multiply *, divide /, and use brackets. No calculator needed. Type Means 2.5/3 A third of 2.5 (in the model's unit) 12'6\"+3/4\" 12' 6 3/4\" (24'-0\")/3 8' 2*450mm 900 mm 4+7/16 4 7/16\" in a feet-and-inches model 3m-25cm 2.75 m A bare number is in the model's unit when it is a length (2.5/3 in a meter model is 0.833 m), and a plain factor when it multiplies or divides one (2*450mm). Angles and factors take calculations too: 90/2 is 45°. Two lengths multiplied make an area, not a length, so 2m*3m is refused. If ModelCraft can't work it out, the status bar says what is wrong and where, for example \"Division by zero (character 4).\" A bracket also starts the value box, so you can begin with (. These keep their own meaning: /3, 3x and *3 after a copy, 24s for sides, and /3 with an edge selected (it divides the edge). Per tool Tool Label Type Line Length A length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset (below) Rectangle Dimensions Two lengths: 4m,3m, 12',10'. A semicolon works too: 4m;3m Circle, Polygon Sides, then Radius Before the first click, the number of sides: 12s or 12 (3 to 999). After it, the radius Push/pull Distance A length. A negative one goes the other way Move Distance A length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset. After a copy: 3x, *3, /3 Rotate Angle Degrees: 90, -45, 22.5 Scale Scale A factor above 0: 2, 0.5, 1.25 Tape Offset or Length A length Divide Parts How many equal parts: 5, 5s or /5 (2 to 1000). See Divide an edge or curve into equal parts Dimension Offset, then Length Where the dimension line goes; right after placing, a new length resizes the model Select Value With one dimension or edge selected, a new length resizes the model. See Dimension and resize Points and offsets For Line and Move, after the first click: [x,y,z] goes to that exact point in the model (in the group's own axes when you are editing inside a group). Example: [0,0,3m] is 3 m straight above the origin. \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E goes that far from the first point along red, green and blue. Example: \u003C0,0,10'\u003E is 10 feet straight up. Each of the three values can have its own unit: \u003C12',0,6\"\u003E. Copies after a move Right after a copy with Move: Type Makes 3x or *3 3 copies in a row at the same spacing /3 3 copies spread evenly from the original to the first copy Up to 500 copies. See Move, copy and make arrays. Typed lengths need a direction A single typed length goes the way the pointer is pointing from the first click. If you type right after clicking, before moving at all, there is no direction yet and nothing happens. Move the pointer a little toward where you want to go, then type. The model's unit Change the unit in the picker at the bottom right: ft and in, in, ft, yd, mi, mil, µm, mm, cm, m or km, as far as they are switched on in Settings › General › Units (the model's own unit is always there). A length typed with any unit's mark is read in that unit. See Units, typed values and the unit lists. It changes how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read; the model stays the same size. The unit is saved in the file. How sizes are shown: Unit Lengths Areas Volumes ft and in 10' 6 1/2\" (to 1/16\") sq ft cu ft in 126 1/2\" sq in cu in m 3.2 m m² m³ cm 320 cm cm² cm³ mm 3200 mm m² from 0.01 m², else mm² m³ from 0.01 m³, else mm³ A new model starts in feet and inches when the browser's language is set to a US region (and Liberia and Myanmar), and in millimeters everywhere else. A unit starred in Settings › General › Units is used instead, when ModelCraft has it. Or change the picker after New.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"formats","scope":"modelcraft","title":"File formats in detail","summary":"Exactly what each format ModelCraft opens, imports and exports keeps, converts and leaves out.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["formats","file types","modelcraft","json","3dm","nurbs","skp","collada","dae","obj","mtl","gltf","glb","stl","step","stp","ap214","ap242","ply","alembic","abc","cache","fbx","3mf","ifc","dwg","dxf","units","axes","y up","z up","millimeters","limits"],"headings":[{"id":"modelcraft-modelcraft","text":"ModelCraft (.modelcraft)","level":2},{"id":"usd-usd-usda-usdc-usdz","text":"USD (.usd, .usda, .usdc, .usdz)","level":2},{"id":"collada-dae","text":"Collada (.dae)","level":2},{"id":"obj-obj-mtl","text":"OBJ (.obj, .mtl)","level":2},{"id":"gltf-glb-gltf","text":"glTF (.glb, .gltf)","level":2},{"id":"stl-stl","text":"STL (.stl)","level":2},{"id":"step-step-stp","text":"STEP (.step, .stp)","level":2},{"id":"3dm","text":".3dm","level":2},{"id":"ply-ply","text":"PLY (.ply)","level":2},{"id":"fbx-fbx","text":"FBX (.fbx)","level":2},{"id":"alembic-abc","text":"Alembic (.abc)","level":2},{"id":"3mf-3mf","text":"3MF (.3mf)","level":2},{"id":"dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf","text":"DWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf)","level":2},{"id":"ifc-ifc","text":"IFC (.ifc)","level":2},{"id":"dwg-dwg-and-dxf-dxf","text":"DWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf)","level":2},{"id":"skp-files","text":"SKP files","level":2},{"id":"not-supported","text":"Not supported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft&#039;s model is in meters with Z (blue) up. Every format below is converted to or from that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft-modelcraft\"\u003EModelCraft (.modelcraft)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or drop on the view. Files ending in \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E are accepted too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), as a download\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything: geometry, faces and their colors, materials and imported textures, lamps, soft edges, groups, components, names, guides, scenes, the units, and the last camera and style\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeaves out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe undo history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELimit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E200 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file saved by a newer ModelCraft than yours is refused: &quot;This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft (file version …). Update ModelCraft to open it.&quot; A file that is not a ModelCraft model says &quot;This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).&quot; or &quot;This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usd-usd-usda-usdc-usdz\"\u003EUSD (.usd, .usda, .usdc, .usdz)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen, import, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…\u003C/span\u003E. A USD file ModelCraft saved opens again with nothing lost; other programs&#039; USD files open as meshes, groups, components, lamps and cameras. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003ESave, open and export USD\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"collada-dae\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export. The best format for trading models with other 3D modelers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport:\u003C/strong\u003E Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters. One geometry per group or component definition; components are written once and placed by reference, so they stay shared. Faces with holes or concave outlines are split into triangles. Colors become materials. Loose edges are written as lines. Names that are not valid in the format are adjusted, and the exact name is kept alongside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E triangles, polygons (with holes), triangle fans and strips become faces; lines become edges. Node transforms, the file&#039;s up axis and its unit are applied. A node placed in several places, or a geometry used by several nodes, comes in as a component; other nodes come in as groups. Material colors (diffuse) become face colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot imported:\u003C/strong\u003E textures, skins and morphs, cameras, lights, some transform types (look-at and skew), and references to other files. The status bar counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELost on export:\u003C/strong\u003E which edges are soft. \u003Cstrong\u003EOn import,\u003C/strong\u003E triangulated surfaces stay triangles, with the diagonals softened so flat areas still read as one surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"obj-obj-mtl\"\u003EOBJ (.obj, .mtl)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E drop the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E together with its \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E and texture pictures (or import the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E alone: it comes in with no materials, and the status bar names the \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E it wanted). Read as meters with Y up. Each object (\u003Ccode\u003Eo\u003C/code\u003E, else \u003Ccode\u003Eg\u003C/code\u003E) becomes a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E, its faces kept as they are (quads stay quads). Texture coordinates, normals and per-vertex colors are kept. A material&#039;s color picture (\u003Ccode\u003Emap_Kd\u003C/code\u003E, PNG, JPEG or WebP) comes in as a texture, with its scale and offset options; otherwise its color (\u003Ccode\u003EKd\u003C/code\u003E). Edges where the file&#039;s normals break are hard; with no normals, the file&#039;s smoothing groups decide. Other maps (bump, normal), points, lines and curves are counted and left out. OBJ has no unit, so a model made in millimeters or inches arrives at the wrong size: scale it after import (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport:\u003C/strong\u003E meters, Y up, world coordinates. One object per group placement; components are written out in full at every placement. Meshes keep their own faces, texture coordinates and normals; faces joined by \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003Esoft edges\u003C/a\u003E get a normal per corner. When the model has colors, a \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E downloads beside the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E. When it has imported textures, the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E, the \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E and the pictures download together as one \u003Ccode\u003E.zip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E 2 million vertices or faces; 64 MB of text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gltf-glb-gltf\"\u003EglTF (.glb, .gltf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export, glTF 2.0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E a \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E, or a \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E together with its \u003Ccode\u003E.bin\u003C/code\u003E and texture files: drop them on the view all at once (or pick the \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E). A \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E given alone names the file it needs: &quot;This .gltf file needs &quot;…&quot;: open the .glb version, or drop the .gltf together with its files.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeshes\u003C/strong\u003E come in as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emeshes\u003C/a\u003E, one per mesh in the file, with the file&#039;s normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors kept on them. Edges where the file&#039;s normals break are hard; the rest are soft. A mesh used in several places stays shared.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENodes\u003C/strong\u003E become groups, named as in the file. Instanced meshes (EXT_mesh_gpu_instancing) come in as one mesh placed many times.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterials:\u003C/strong\u003E a base color texture (PNG, JPEG or WebP) comes in as a texture, placed by the file&#039;s texture coordinates (with any texture transform), and is kept when you save; otherwise the base color. Metal, roughness, normal maps, glass, clear coat, sheen and the other material extensions are read but not kept yet: the status bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELights\u003C/strong\u003E (KHR_lights_punctual): point and spot lights become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/lights\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/lights\"\u003Elamps\u003C/a\u003E. Directional lights are counted: the sun lights the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECameras\u003C/strong\u003E become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESkinned characters\u003C/strong\u003E come in standing as the file rests them; their bones and animations, and morph targets, are counted and not kept yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompressed files\u003C/strong\u003E (Draco, meshopt) and quantized data are read. The decoders load only for a file that needs them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints and lines are counted and left out (a mesh holds faces). KTX2 textures are left out; the faces keep their color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E): glTF 2.0 binary, meters, Y up. One mesh per definition, so components stay shared. Meshes keep their normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors; faces joined by \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003Esoft edges\u003C/a\u003E carry smooth normals. Colors and materials become materials (a material as its color); imported textures are written as textures. Lamps that are on become point and spot lights (area lamps have no glTF equivalent and are left out); scenes become cameras. Loose edges are left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E files up to 300 MB; about 130 million numbers of geometry.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stl-stl\"\u003ESTL (.stl)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E binary or ASCII. STL has no unit: it is read as \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimeters\u003C/strong\u003E, the way printers and ModelCraft&#039;s own export write it. Corners at the same point are joined, so a closed part comes in as a closed \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E. Colors in the file (the VisCAM / SolidView and the Materialise conventions) paint the faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport,\u003C/strong\u003E for 3D printing: binary STL, \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimeters\u003C/strong\u003E, Z up. Triangles only: groups and loose edges are left out. Painted faces carry their color in the VisCAM / SolidView convention, which other programs ignore if they don&#039;t read it. Export closed solids for a clean print.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"step-step-stp\"\u003ESTEP (.step, .stp)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport only. STEP is what part modelers and suppliers send: AP203, AP214 and AP242 files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft reads the file itself, in the page, and turns every solid into a closed \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E of triangles: flat faces stay flat, curved faces (cylinders, cones, spheres, tori, B-spline surfaces) become many small flat ones, with the edges inside one curved face soft and the edges between two faces sharp. STEP files say their unit; the model comes in at its true size. An assembly comes in with every part where the file places it, each part its own mesh in a group named after the file. Colors, part names of the assembly tree and tolerances (PMI) are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMessages:\u003C/strong\u003E what was skipped or drawn coarser is added to the status line (&quot;Notes: …&quot;); a file with no solid, a damaged file or one past the limits says why and adds nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E files up to 300 MB; 400 000 faces and 8 million triangles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3dm\"\u003E.3dm\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport only. \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E files hold NURBS models: polysurfaces, surfaces, meshes, curves and blocks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft reads the file itself, in the page. Meshes come in as they are; every face of a polysurface or extrusion comes from the render mesh saved with it in the file, else ModelCraft tessellates it (planes, NURBS surfaces, surfaces of revolution, extruded curves, extrusions; trimmed by their edges, closed polysurfaces as closed meshes). Curves become edges, blocks become components (blocks in blocks kept), layers become tags (hidden when off), the object&#039;s color (its own, its layer&#039;s or its material&#039;s) paints its faces. The file says its unit; the model comes in at its true size. Step by step: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-3dm-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-3dm-files\"\u003EBring in a .3dm model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMessages:\u003C/strong\u003E the status line says how many faces came from saved meshes and how many were tessellated, and names what was left out: points, point clouds, text, dimensions, hatches, lights, SubD objects, faces on surface kinds not read yet, linked blocks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E files up to 300 MB.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ply-ply\"\u003EPLY (.ply)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export, for meshes with colors (scans, 3D printing in color, other modelers).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E ASCII and binary. Faces become a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E with their normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors kept; each face is painted the nearest of 4,096 colors so you see the colors now. PLY has no unit or axis convention: coordinates come in as they are (meters, Z up). A PLY with points only is a point cloud: open it in BimCraft or CivilCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport:\u003C/strong\u003E binary PLY with positions, normals and colors (and texture coordinates when the model has them), in meters, Z up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fbx-fbx\"\u003EFBX (.fbx)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport only. FBX 7 files (from 2011 on), binary or ASCII, read by ModelCraft&#039;s own reader.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeshes come in as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emeshes\u003C/a\u003E with their normals, texture coordinates (every UV set) and vertex colors; a mesh several objects use stays shared. Objects become groups with their transforms (pivots, offsets and pre-rotations included).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMaterials: the diffuse texture — embedded in the file, or a picture dropped together with the \u003Ccode\u003E.fbx\u003C/code\u003E — comes in as a texture (TGA pictures are kept as PNG); otherwise the diffuse color. Other material settings wait for the new material model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file&#039;s up axis and unit are applied: everything comes in Z up, in meters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint and spot lights become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/lights\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/lights\"\u003Elamps\u003C/a\u003E (an FBX intensity of 100 is ModelCraft&#039;s default lamp, 800 lumens: FBX intensities have no unit). Cameras become \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkinned characters come in at rest; bones and animations are counted and not kept yet. Directional and area lights, and geometry other than meshes (NURBS, curves), are counted and left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFBX 6 and older files are refused: save them again as FBX 2011 or newer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alembic-abc\"\u003EAlembic (.abc)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport only: geometry caches from animation and effects programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeshes (polygon and subdivision meshes) come in as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emeshes\u003C/a\u003E, with their texture coordinates and normals; transforms become groups. Alembic is Y up and is turned to Z up; meters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA cache with many samples (an animation) comes in at its first sample; the status bar says how many it has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints, curves, NURBS surfaces, cameras and lights are counted and left out. Files in the old HDF5 layout are refused: save them again as an Ogawa Alembic file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E checks the shape first and writes the selection, or everything shown. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003EGet a model ready for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3mf-3mf\"\u003E3MF (.3mf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExport only, from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E. 3MF core: one mesh in \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimeters\u003C/strong\u003E, Z up, with its colors as materials. Most slicers read it, and unlike STL it says its unit. Hidden things are left out. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-3d\"\u003EGet a model ready for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf\"\u003EDWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor CAD programs. The model is written in its own unit: inches for feet-and-inches models, otherwise millimeters, centimeters or meters (the status bar names it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaces\u003C/strong\u003E become 3D faces (triangles). Lines inside a face, and soft edges, are written invisible, so the faces look like ModelCraft&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoose edges\u003C/strong\u003E become lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroups and components\u003C/strong\u003E become blocks: a component is written once and placed as often as it is used, turned and mirrored copies included. A group that was stretched after it was turned (so it is slanted) is written out in place instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors:\u003C/strong\u003E DWG keeps each face&#039;s color exactly; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVersions:\u003C/strong\u003E DWG is written as AutoCAD 2004 (AC1018), which CAD programs from 2004 on open. DXF is R12 (AC1009), the version every CAD program reads; R12 has no unit field, so tell the CAD program which unit to use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot written:\u003C/strong\u003E guides, dimensions, scenes, and the camera.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ifc-ifc\"\u003EIFC (.ifc)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExport only, for BimCraft and other BIM programs. IFC4 with a project, a site, a building and its storeys. Each top-level group or component becomes one building element proxy with its name; closed ones are written as solid shapes (faceted breps), open ones as surface models; holes in faces are kept. Loose geometry at the top level becomes one element called &quot;Loose geometry&quot;. No types, properties or materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStoreys:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC storeys…\u003C/span\u003E lists them, each with a name and an elevation (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd storey\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuess from the model\u003C/span\u003E, which makes one storey for each height your top-level groups stand on). On export, each group or component goes on the storey at or below its lowest point, and keeps its place. With no storeys, everything goes on one storey, &quot;Level 1&quot;, at 0. The storeys are saved in the ModelCraft file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-dwg-and-dxf-dxf\"\u003EDWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport as lines on the ground (hatches and 3D faces as faces); export as described above. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003EModel on a site plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead:\u003C/strong\u003E lines, polylines (with arcs), 3D polylines, circles, arcs, and block references including arrays, from model space. Invisible objects (such as the hidden states of a dynamic block) are left out. Curves become 48 straight pieces per full turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead since 0.4:\u003C/strong\u003E splines and ellipses (as edges), text, multiline text and attribute values (as edges in a single-stroke lettering), hatches (faces with holes), 3D faces and solid fills (faces), and dimensions (drawn from the drawing&#039;s own dimension blocks).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead since 0.7:\u003C/strong\u003E 3D solids, regions and bodies (their ACIS data, DWG and DXF of every version), each as its own group, closed ones as solids. Faces on surfaces other than planes, cylinders, cones, spheres, tori and simple spline surfaces are left out and listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot read:\u003C/strong\u003E points, tables, multileaders, images, and anything in paper space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits:\u003C/strong\u003E taken from the drawing when it says. If not, ModelCraft asks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF\u003C/strong\u003E must be ASCII. Binary DXF is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E files from AutoCAD R13 up to the current format (AutoCAD 2018 and later; AC1012 to AC1032) are read by ModelCraft&#039;s own DWG reader, in the page; it is part of ModelCraft, so it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Older versions (R12 and before) and badly damaged files cannot be read; save them as DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimits:\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates more than 10,000 km from the origin, or more than 2 million line pieces, are refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skp-files\"\u003ESKP files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E (or drop the file on the view) reads SketchUp files with ModelCraft&#039;s own reader, in the page: nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Tested with files from SketchUp 3, 6, 7, 2014, 2017, 2018, 2020, 2025 and 2026; other versions should open too, but have not been tried yet. The model comes in as one group named after the file. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it away again. Step by step: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\"\u003EBring in an SKP model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKept:\u003C/strong\u003E faces with their holes, groups (as groups) and components (as components, shared), their names and placements, colors, tags on groups and components, edges that are not part of a face, and soft and smooth edges, so curved surfaces stay smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors:\u003C/strong\u003E a face&#039;s own material gives its color. A material painted on a group or component becomes that placement&#039;s paint, so faces inside with no color of their own take it, as in SketchUp; a component painted two colors stays one component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETags\u003C/strong\u003E on groups and components become ModelCraft tags, shown or hidden as they were. Tags on loose faces and edges are not kept (ModelCraft tags groups and components only); the status bar counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden things come in hidden\u003C/strong\u003E (faces, edges, groups, components, and faces and edges on a hidden tag), so you see what SketchUp showed; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E shows them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETextures:\u003C/strong\u003E a face painted with a textured material keeps its texture, placed as in the file, and the ModelCraft file keeps the image. A texture on a whole group or component, a texture missing from the file, or an image browsers can&#039;t show (such as a Photoshop file) keeps only its material&#039;s color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeft out, and counted in the status bar:\u003C/strong\u003E images placed in the model. Text, dimensions, guides, section planes, scenes and styles are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits:\u003C/strong\u003E SketchUp files are in inches inside; ModelCraft converts them, so sizes come out right in any unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E is not built. To take a model back to SketchUp, export Collada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-supported\"\u003ENot supported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft cannot open 3DS or IFC files, and cannot save 3DS, FBX, STEP, \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E or SketchUp (\u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E) files.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft's model is in meters with Z (blue) up. Every format below is converted to or from that. ModelCraft (.modelcraft) What Details Open Open (Ctrl+O) or drop on the view. Files ending in .json are accepted too Save Save (Ctrl+S), as a download Keeps Everything: geometry, faces and their colors, materials and imported textures, lamps, soft edges, groups, components, names, guides, scenes, the units, and the last camera and style Leaves out The undo history Limit 200 MB A file saved by a newer ModelCraft than yours is refused: \"This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft (file version …). Update ModelCraft to open it.\" A file that is not a ModelCraft model says \"This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).\" or \"This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …\". USD (.usd, .usda, .usdc, .usdz) Open, import, and Save as or Export › USD (.usda, .usdc, .usdz)…. A USD file ModelCraft saved opens again with nothing lost; other programs' USD files open as meshes, groups, components, lamps and cameras. See Save, open and export USD. Collada (.dae) Import and export. The best format for trading models with other 3D modelers. Export: Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters. One geometry per group or component definition; components are written once and placed by reference, so they stay shared. Faces with holes or concave outlines are split into triangles. Colors become materials. Loose edges are written as lines. Names that are not valid in the format are adjusted, and the exact name is kept alongside it. Import: triangles, polygons (with holes), triangle fans and strips become faces; lines become edges. Node transforms, the file's up axis and its unit are applied. A node placed in several places, or a geometry used by several nodes, comes in as a component; other nodes come in as groups. Material colors (diffuse) become face colors. Not imported: textures, skins and morphs, cameras, lights, some transform types (look-at and skew), and references to other files. The status bar counts them. Lost on export: which edges are soft. On import, triangulated surfaces stay triangles, with the diagonals softened so flat areas still read as one surface. OBJ (.obj, .mtl) Import and export. Import: drop the .obj together with its .mtl and texture pictures (or import the .obj alone: it comes in with no materials, and the status bar names the .mtl it wanted). Read as meters with Y up. Each object (o, else g) becomes a mesh, its faces kept as they are (quads stay quads). Texture coordinates, normals and per-vertex colors are kept. A material's color picture (map_Kd, PNG, JPEG or WebP) comes in as a texture, with its scale and offset options; otherwise its color (Kd). Edges where the file's normals break are hard; with no normals, the file's smoothing groups decide. Other maps (bump, normal), points, lines and curves are counted and left out. OBJ has no unit, so a model made in millimeters or inches arrives at the wrong size: scale it after import (see Rotate and scale). Export: meters, Y up, world coordinates. One object per group placement; components are written out in full at every placement. Meshes keep their own faces, texture coordinates and normals; faces joined by soft edges get a normal per corner. When the model has colors, a .mtl downloads beside the .obj. When it has imported textures, the .obj, the .mtl and the pictures download together as one .zip. Limit: 2 million vertices or faces; 64 MB of text. glTF (.glb, .gltf) Import and export, glTF 2.0. Import: a .glb, or a .gltf together with its .bin and texture files: drop them on the view all at once (or pick the .glb). A .gltf given alone names the file it needs: \"This .gltf file needs \"…\": open the .glb version, or drop the .gltf together with its files.\" Meshes come in as meshes, one per mesh in the file, with the file's normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors kept on them. Edges where the file's normals break are hard; the rest are soft. A mesh used in several places stays shared. Nodes become groups, named as in the file. Instanced meshes (EXT_mesh_gpu_instancing) come in as one mesh placed many times. Materials: a base color texture (PNG, JPEG or WebP) comes in as a texture, placed by the file's texture coordinates (with any texture transform), and is kept when you save; otherwise the base color. Metal, roughness, normal maps, glass, clear coat, sheen and the other material extensions are read but not kept yet: the status bar says so. Lights (KHR_lights_punctual): point and spot lights become lamps. Directional lights are counted: the sun lights the model. Cameras become scenes. Skinned characters come in standing as the file rests them; their bones and animations, and morph targets, are counted and not kept yet. Compressed files (Draco, meshopt) and quantized data are read. The decoders load only for a file that needs them. Points and lines are counted and left out (a mesh holds faces). KTX2 textures are left out; the faces keep their color. Export (.glb): glTF 2.0 binary, meters, Y up. One mesh per definition, so components stay shared. Meshes keep their normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors; faces joined by soft edges carry smooth normals. Colors and materials become materials (a material as its color); imported textures are written as textures. Lamps that are on become point and spot lights (area lamps have no glTF equivalent and are left out); scenes become cameras. Loose edges are left out. Limit: files up to 300 MB; about 130 million numbers of geometry. STL (.stl) Import and export. Import: binary or ASCII. STL has no unit: it is read as millimeters, the way printers and ModelCraft's own export write it. Corners at the same point are joined, so a closed part comes in as a closed mesh. Colors in the file (the VisCAM / SolidView and the Materialise conventions) paint the faces. Export, for 3D printing: binary STL, millimeters, Z up. Triangles only: groups and loose edges are left out. Painted faces carry their color in the VisCAM / SolidView convention, which other programs ignore if they don't read it. Export closed solids for a clean print. STEP (.step, .stp) Import only. STEP is what part modelers and suppliers send: AP203, AP214 and AP242 files. Import: ModelCraft reads the file itself, in the page, and turns every solid into a closed mesh of triangles: flat faces stay flat, curved faces (cylinders, cones, spheres, tori, B-spline surfaces) become many small flat ones, with the edges inside one curved face soft and the edges between two faces sharp. STEP files say their unit; the model comes in at its true size. An assembly comes in with every part where the file places it, each part its own mesh in a group named after the file. Colors, part names of the assembly tree and tolerances (PMI) are not read. Messages: what was skipped or drawn coarser is added to the status line (\"Notes: …\"); a file with no solid, a damaged file or one past the limits says why and adds nothing. Limit: files up to 300 MB; 400 000 faces and 8 million triangles. .3dm Import only. .3dm files hold NURBS models: polysurfaces, surfaces, meshes, curves and blocks. Import: ModelCraft reads the file itself, in the page. Meshes come in as they are; every face of a polysurface or extrusion comes from the render mesh saved with it in the file, else ModelCraft tessellates it (planes, NURBS surfaces, surfaces of revolution, extruded curves, extrusions; trimmed by their edges, closed polysurfaces as closed meshes). Curves become edges, blocks become components (blocks in blocks kept), layers become tags (hidden when off), the object's color (its own, its layer's or its material's) paints its faces. The file says its unit; the model comes in at its true size. Step by step: Bring in a .3dm model. Messages: the status line says how many faces came from saved meshes and how many were tessellated, and names what was left out: points, point clouds, text, dimensions, hatches, lights, SubD objects, faces on surface kinds not read yet, linked blocks. Limit: files up to 300 MB. PLY (.ply) Import and export, for meshes with colors (scans, 3D printing in color, other modelers). Import: ASCII and binary. Faces become a mesh with their normals, texture coordinates and vertex colors kept; each face is painted the nearest of 4,096 colors so you see the colors now. PLY has no unit or axis convention: coordinates come in as they are (meters, Z up). A PLY with points only is a point cloud: open it in BimCraft or CivilCraft. Export: binary PLY with positions, normals and colors (and texture coordinates when the model has them), in meters, Z up. FBX (.fbx) Import only. FBX 7 files (from 2011 on), binary or ASCII, read by ModelCraft's own reader. Meshes come in as meshes with their normals, texture coordinates (every UV set) and vertex colors; a mesh several objects use stays shared. Objects become groups with their transforms (pivots, offsets and pre-rotations included). Materials: the diffuse texture — embedded in the file, or a picture dropped together with the .fbx — comes in as a texture (TGA pictures are kept as PNG); otherwise the diffuse color. Other material settings wait for the new material model. The file's up axis and unit are applied: everything comes in Z up, in meters. Point and spot lights become lamps (an FBX intensity of 100 is ModelCraft's default lamp, 800 lumens: FBX intensities have no unit). Cameras become scenes. Skinned characters come in at rest; bones and animations are counted and not kept yet. Directional and area lights, and geometry other than meshes (NURBS, curves), are counted and left out. FBX 6 and older files are refused: save them again as FBX 2011 or newer. Alembic (.abc) Import only: geometry caches from animation and effects programs. Meshes (polygon and subdivision meshes) come in as meshes, with their texture coordinates and normals; transforms become groups. Alembic is Y up and is turned to Z up; meters. A cache with many samples (an animation) comes in at its first sample; the status bar says how many it has. Points, curves, NURBS surfaces, cameras and lights are counted and left out. Files in the old HDF5 layout are refused: save them again as an Ogawa Alembic file. Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)… checks the shape first and writes the selection, or everything shown. See Get a model ready for 3D printing. 3MF (.3mf) Export only, from Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…. 3MF core: one mesh in millimeters, Z up, with its colors as materials. Most slicers read it, and unlike STL it says its unit. Hidden things are left out. See Get a model ready for 3D printing. DWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf) For CAD programs. The model is written in its own unit: inches for feet-and-inches models, otherwise millimeters, centimeters or meters (the status bar names it). Faces become 3D faces (triangles). Lines inside a face, and soft edges, are written invisible, so the faces look like ModelCraft's. Loose edges become lines. Groups and components become blocks: a component is written once and placed as often as it is used, turned and mirrored copies included. A group that was stretched after it was turned (so it is slanted) is written out in place instead. Colors: DWG keeps each face's color exactly; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors. Versions: DWG is written as AutoCAD 2004 (AC1018), which CAD programs from 2004 on open. DXF is R12 (AC1009), the version every CAD program reads; R12 has no unit field, so tell the CAD program which unit to use. Not written: guides, dimensions, scenes, and the camera. IFC (.ifc) Export only, for BimCraft and other BIM programs. IFC4 with a project, a site, a building and its storeys. Each top-level group or component becomes one building element proxy with its name; closed ones are written as solid shapes (faceted breps), open ones as surface models; holes in faces are kept. Loose geometry at the top level becomes one element called \"Loose geometry\". No types, properties or materials. Storeys: Export › IFC storeys… lists them, each with a name and an elevation (Add storey, or Guess from the model, which makes one storey for each height your top-level groups stand on). On export, each group or component goes on the storey at or below its lowest point, and keeps its place. With no storeys, everything goes on one storey, \"Level 1\", at 0. The storeys are saved in the ModelCraft file. DWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf) Import as lines on the ground (hatches and 3D faces as faces); export as described above. See Model on a site plan. Read: lines, polylines (with arcs), 3D polylines, circles, arcs, and block references including arrays, from model space. Invisible objects (such as the hidden states of a dynamic block) are left out. Curves become 48 straight pieces per full turn. Read since 0.4: splines and ellipses (as edges), text, multiline text and attribute values (as edges in a single-stroke lettering), hatches (faces with holes), 3D faces and solid fills (faces), and dimensions (drawn from the drawing's own dimension blocks). Read since 0.7: 3D solids, regions and bodies (their ACIS data, DWG and DXF of every version), each as its own group, closed ones as solids. Faces on surfaces other than planes, cylinders, cones, spheres, tori and simple spline surfaces are left out and listed. Not read: points, tables, multileaders, images, and anything in paper space. Units: taken from the drawing when it says. If not, ModelCraft asks. DXF must be ASCII. Binary DXF is refused. DWG files from AutoCAD R13 up to the current format (AutoCAD 2018 and later; AC1012 to AC1032) are read by ModelCraft's own DWG reader, in the page; it is part of ModelCraft, so it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Older versions (R12 and before) and badly damaged files cannot be read; save them as DXF. Limits: coordinates more than 10,000 km from the origin, or more than 2 million line pieces, are refused. SKP files Import (or drop the file on the view) reads SketchUp files with ModelCraft's own reader, in the page: nothing is uploaded, and it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Tested with files from SketchUp 3, 6, 7, 2014, 2017, 2018, 2020, 2025 and 2026; other versions should open too, but have not been tried yet. The model comes in as one group named after the file. One Ctrl+Z takes it away again. Step by step: Bring in an SKP model. Kept: faces with their holes, groups (as groups) and components (as components, shared), their names and placements, colors, tags on groups and components, edges that are not part of a face, and soft and smooth edges, so curved surfaces stay smooth. Colors: a face's own material gives its color. A material painted on a group or component becomes that placement's paint, so faces inside with no color of their own take it, as in SketchUp; a component painted two colors stays one component. Tags on groups and components become ModelCraft tags, shown or hidden as they were. Tags on loose faces and edges are not kept (ModelCraft tags groups and components only); the status bar counts them. Hidden things come in hidden (faces, edges, groups, components, and faces and edges on a hidden tag), so you see what SketchUp showed; Unhide all shows them. Textures: a face painted with a textured material keeps its texture, placed as in the file, and the ModelCraft file keeps the image. A texture on a whole group or component, a texture missing from the file, or an image browsers can't show (such as a Photoshop file) keeps only its material's color. Left out, and counted in the status bar: images placed in the model. Text, dimensions, guides, section planes, scenes and styles are not read. Units: SketchUp files are in inches inside; ModelCraft converts them, so sizes come out right in any unit. Saving .skp is not built. To take a model back to SketchUp, export Collada (.dae). Not supported ModelCraft cannot open 3DS or IFC files, and cannot save 3DS, FBX, STEP, .3dm or SketchUp (.skp) files.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"your-machine","scope":"modelcraft","title":"What your machine renders with","summary":"Settings › Rendering shows the graphics, processor cores and memory this browser lets the app use, and the level that sets where rendering starts.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["your machine","graphics card","gpu","webgpu","webgl","processor","cpu","cores","threads","memory","ram","shared memory","isolation","tier","level","speed","slow","rendering speed","laptop"],"headings":[{"id":"the-level","text":"The level","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), stay on this app&#039;s own tab and scroll to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERendering\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour machine\u003C/span\u003E lists what this browser lets the app use for rendering. Nothing in it leaves your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERow\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe graphics card the browser reports. &quot;WebGPU&quot; means it can do all the rendering work; &quot;WebGL 2 only&quot; means the live view and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E use it (high quality with the WebGL path tracer), while rendering in the view and the render queue, which need WebGPU, are not offered; a software stand-in means everything runs on the processor\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProcessor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many processor cores the browser reports\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMemory\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout how much memory the computer has, when the browser says. Some browsers do not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShared memory\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhether the page may share memory between its workers. On, every core can work on one copy of a scene; off, each needs its own copy, so a large scene fits on fewer cores at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast processor code\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhether the browser runs the faster WebAssembly code. Without it a slower fallback is used\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevel\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics card, high\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics card, basic\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProcessor only\u003C/span\u003E, with the reason under it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-level\"\u003EThe level\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe level sets where the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\"\u003Elive view\u003C/a\u003E starts (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor this computer\u003C/span\u003E), how many scattered items it draws, and how many faces a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/modifiers#subdivision-surface\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/modifiers\" data-hash=\"subdivision-surface\"\u003Esubdivision surface\u003C/a\u003E may show in the view, so that a laptop with a built-in graphics chip or no graphics card at all stays responsive. A built-in or phone graphics chip, or 4 GB of memory or less, never gets \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGraphics card, high\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrowsers differ. The same computer can show WebGPU in one browser and &quot;WebGL 2 only&quot; in another. On Linux most browsers have no WebGPU yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Open Settings (Ctrl+,), stay on this app's own tab and scroll to Rendering. Your machine lists what this browser lets the app use for rendering. Nothing in it leaves your computer. Row What it means Graphics The graphics card the browser reports. \"WebGPU\" means it can do all the rendering work; \"WebGL 2 only\" means the live view and Render a picture… use it (high quality with the WebGL path tracer), while rendering in the view and the render queue, which need WebGPU, are not offered; a software stand-in means everything runs on the processor Processor How many processor cores the browser reports Memory About how much memory the computer has, when the browser says. Some browsers do not Shared memory Whether the page may share memory between its workers. On, every core can work on one copy of a scene; off, each needs its own copy, so a large scene fits on fewer cores at once Fast processor code Whether the browser runs the faster WebAssembly code. Without it a slower fallback is used Level Graphics card, high, Graphics card, basic or Processor only, with the reason under it The level The level sets where the live view starts (For this computer), how many scattered items it draws, and how many faces a subdivision surface may show in the view, so that a laptop with a built-in graphics chip or no graphics card at all stays responsive. A built-in or phone graphics chip, or 4 GB of memory or less, never gets Graphics card, high. Browsers differ. The same computer can show WebGPU in one browser and \"WebGL 2 only\" in another. On Linux most browsers have no WebGPU yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"render-engine-addons","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Add a render engine with an add-on","summary":"What an add-on can add to the Render dialog's Engine list, the permission it needs to run a program, and what Studio checks and asks.","section":"reference","order":117,"keywords":["add-on","addon","engine","render engine","developer","engine.run","permission","program","script","signed","studio","api"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on (Settings › Add-ons) can add an engine to the Engine list of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E. The full text for add-on authors is section 16 of the add-on specification; in short:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe add-on registers an engine with an id that starts with its own, a name, optional settings and \u003Cstrong\u003Eeither\u003C/strong\u003E a program to run \u003Cstrong\u003Eor\u003C/strong\u003E a function in JavaScript that draws the picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn engine that runs a program needs the \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eengine.run\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E permission, shown before install and in Settings. It runs only in Studio; in the browser it shows as a file to save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe add-on names the program (a plain file name: never a shell), a script that is \u003Cstrong\u003Einside the signed add-on\u003C/strong\u003E, and arguments made of placeholders and plain words. Studio finds the program or asks you to point at it once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first time an add-on runs a program, \u003Cstrong\u003EStudio asks you in its own dialog\u003C/strong\u003E: which add-on, which program, and that a script can do anything the program can. Only allow it for an add-on you trust. Your answer is remembered for that add-on and program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe package (the scene as USD and a settings file, with the glTF file when the add-on asks for it) and the add-on&#039;s script go into a fresh temporary folder, the program runs there, and the picture comes back marked with the add-on&#039;s engine name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons can only add render engines from add-on API 1.4. A worked example for an open-source command-line renderer is in its own repository; it has not been run in that renderer yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"An add-on (Settings › Add-ons) can add an engine to the Engine list of Render a picture…. The full text for add-on authors is section 16 of the add-on specification; in short: The add-on registers an engine with an id that starts with its own, a name, optional settings and either a program to run or a function in JavaScript that draws the picture. An engine that runs a program needs the engine.run permission, shown before install and in Settings. It runs only in Studio; in the browser it shows as a file to save. The add-on names the program (a plain file name: never a shell), a script that is inside the signed add-on, and arguments made of placeholders and plain words. Studio finds the program or asks you to point at it once. The first time an add-on runs a program, Studio asks you in its own dialog: which add-on, which program, and that a script can do anything the program can. Only allow it for an add-on you trust. Your answer is remembered for that add-on and program. The package (the scene as USD and a settings file, with the glTF file when the add-on asks for it) and the add-on's script go into a fresh temporary folder, the program runs there, and the picture comes back marked with the add-on's engine name. Add-ons can only add render engines from add-on API 1.4. A worked example for an open-source command-line renderer is in its own repository; it has not been run in that renderer yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Symptoms, causes and fixes, from a blank 3D view to imports at the wrong size, with the messages ModelCraft shows.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","trouble","error","not working","blank","webgl","graphics","slow","message","import failed","wrong size","cannot select","touch","keys not working","lost work"],"headings":[{"id":"the-3d-view-is-blank","text":"The 3D view is blank","level":2},{"id":"keys-do-nothing-or-type-into-a-box","text":"Keys do nothing, or type into a box","level":2},{"id":"typing-a-number-does-nothing","text":"Typing a number does nothing","level":2},{"id":"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen","text":"A shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen","level":2},{"id":"editing-one-group-changed-another","text":"Editing one group changed another","level":2},{"id":"push-pull-does-not-do-what-i-expected","text":"Push/pull does not do what I expected","level":2},{"id":"a-line-or-shape-did-not-make-a-face","text":"A line or shape did not make a face","level":2},{"id":"i-can-t-select-or-erase-a-face","text":"I can't select or erase a face","level":2},{"id":"select-something-to-move-first-or-click-on-it","text":"\"Select something to move first, or click on it.\"","level":2},{"id":"select-some-geometry-first-drag-a-window-or-triple-click-to-select-everything-connected","text":"\"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\"","level":2},{"id":"the-arrow-keys-orbit-instead-of-locking","text":"The arrow keys orbit instead of locking","level":2},{"id":"esc-in-full-screen","text":"Esc in full screen","level":2},{"id":"no-middle-button","text":"No middle button","level":2},{"id":"imports","text":"Imports","level":2},{"id":"the-import-is-the-wrong-size-or-lying-on-its-side","text":"The import is the wrong size or lying on its side","level":3},{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"i-lost-work","text":"I lost work","level":2},{"id":"my-downloads-folder-fills-up-with-copies","text":"My downloads folder fills up with copies","level":2},{"id":"it-gets-slow","text":"It gets slow","level":2},{"id":"no-shadows","text":"No shadows","level":2},{"id":"this-browser-did-not-allow-full-screen","text":"\"This browser did not allow full screen.\"","level":2},{"id":"modelcraft-does-not-open-without-a-connection","text":"ModelCraft does not open without a connection","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-view-is-blank\"\u003EThe 3D view is blank\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E the middle of the screen stays empty, or shows only the background color, and clicking tools does nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft draws with WebGL 2, the browser&#039;s 3D graphics. It is missing when the browser is old, when hardware acceleration is turned off, or when the graphics driver is blocked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the browser&#039;s settings and search for &quot;graphics acceleration&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on &quot;Use graphics acceleration when available&quot; and relaunch the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it is already on, type \u003Ccode\u003Echrome://gpu\u003C/code\u003E in the address bar (\u003Ccode\u003Eedge://gpu\u003C/code\u003E in Edge) and look for &quot;WebGL2&quot; in the list. If it says disabled, update your graphics driver.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, and scroll to &quot;Performance&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick &quot;Use recommended performance settings&quot;, then tick &quot;Use hardware acceleration when available&quot;. Restart Firefox.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it still fails, type \u003Ccode\u003Eabout:support\u003C/code\u003E in the address bar and look at the &quot;Graphics&quot; section for WebGL 2.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUpdate macOS and Safari. Recent versions of Safari always have WebGL 2 on. If you changed experimental features under \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EAdvanced\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EFeature Flags\u003C/strong\u003E), reset them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a work computer, remote desktop or virtual machine, 3D graphics may be blocked by policy. Try another computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-do-nothing-or-type-into-a-box\"\u003EKeys do nothing, or type into a box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the keyboard focus is in a panel field: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E list in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E, a sun setting, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E field in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, or the units picker. Letters and arrow keys then go to that field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E click in the 3D view, then press the key again. Keys also do nothing while a dialog is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-number-does-nothing\"\u003ETyping a number does nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo direction yet.\u003C/strong\u003E A typed length goes the way the pointer points from the first click. Move the pointer a little in the direction you want, then type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWrong moment.\u003C/strong\u003E Most tools take a typed value only after the first click: click the first corner, the face or the point to move from first. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E take a number of sides before the first click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Type a length such as 3m, 10&#039;6&quot; or 4m,3m.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft could not read it. Check for a decimal comma (\u003Ccode\u003E2,5\u003C/code\u003E): use a period.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\"\u003EA shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E with a finger, the first rectangle is fine, but the next rectangle, circle or line starts where your previous touch ended instead of where you touched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E a bug in this build: on a touch screen the tool remembers the last snap point from the previous gesture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E tap the tool&#039;s button in the toolbar again before each new shape. That starts the tool fresh, and the first point lands where you touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003EEditing one group changed another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E you copied a group with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, opened one copy and edited it, and the other copy changed too. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows both with the same name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E in this build, copying a group makes a second placement of the same group, as with a component. ModelCraft&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E button is only offered for components.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E select the copy you want to change, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E. That copy now has its own contents. Or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-pull-does-not-do-what-i-expected\"\u003EPush/pull does not do what I expected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You clicked an edge or empty space. Hover until the face highlights, then click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe face is inside a group.\u003C/strong\u003E Double-click the group to open it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA window did not cut through the wall.\u003C/strong\u003E The rectangle must be drawn on the wall&#039;s face, not in front of it, and the wall must have a back face. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/openings#if-it-did-not-cut-through\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/openings\" data-hash=\"if-it-did-not-cut-through\"\u003ECut an opening through a wall\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo boxes overlap without merging.\u003C/strong\u003E Solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other in this build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-line-or-shape-did-not-make-a-face\"\u003EA line or shape did not make a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face fills in only when the edges close a flat loop. Check that the last point snapped to the first (&quot;Endpoint&quot;), and that all the edges lie in one plane. A loop that is slightly out of plane stays as lines. Draw one of its edges again, snapping to the corners.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-can-t-select-or-erase-a-face\"\u003EI can&#039;t select or erase a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, a click near an edge takes the edge. Click nearer the middle of the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E does not pick faces. Select the face and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or erase one of its edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it belongs to a group or component, the click selects the whole thing. Double-click to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-something-to-move-first-or-click-on-it\"\u003E&quot;Select something to move first, or click on it.&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E need something to work on: select it first, or start the tool by clicking on the thing itself (not on empty space or a guide).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-some-geometry-first-drag-a-window-or-triple-click-to-select-everything-connected\"\u003E&quot;Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E need a selection. Guides cannot be grouped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-arrow-keys-orbit-instead-of-locking\"\u003EThe arrow keys orbit instead of locking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELocks work only after the first click of a drawing or editing tool. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E has no locks, and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E they do not change the turn. At other times the arrow keys orbit the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"esc-in-full-screen\"\u003EEsc in full screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn full screen, the browser may use the first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel the tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"no-middle-button\"\u003ENo middle button\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E and drag, or use the arrow keys. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#without-a-middle-button\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"without-a-middle-button\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"imports\"\u003EImports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;ModelCraft imports SketchUp (.skp), Collada (.dae), OBJ, glTF (.glb/.gltf), FBX, PLY, STL, Alembic (.abc), DWG and DXF. … is none of these.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the model from the other program in one of these formats\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is over 300 MB.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimplify or split the model in the other program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;…too big to model with here (over 2 million vertices).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This .gltf file needs &quot;…&quot;…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop the \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E together with its \u003Ccode\u003E.bin\u003C/code\u003E and texture files, or open the \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This glTF file needs the … extension, which ModelCraft does not read yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport again from the other program without that extension (KTX2 textures, for one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn OBJ comes in without its colors or textures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E together with its \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E and pictures\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This Alembic file uses the old HDF5 layout…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it again from the other program as an Ogawa Alembic file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn FBX comes in without its textures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop the \u003Ccode\u003E.fbx\u003C/code\u003E together with its texture pictures, or embed the media when exporting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This FBX file is version …, which ModelCraft does not read&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport again as FBX 2011 or newer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn STL or PLY comes in 1,000 times too big or too small\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL is read as millimeters and PLY as meters: scale it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a Collada (.dae) file…&quot; or &quot;…not well-formed XML…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or not Collada. Export it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This Collada file has no geometry ModelCraft can import.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt holds only cameras, lights or references to other files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it again from the CAD program as ASCII\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a DWG drawing…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a DWG, or it is damaged\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe brackets give the reason. Save it as DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing reaches more than 10,000 km from its origin…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the drawing near 0,0 in the CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-import-is-the-wrong-size-or-lying-on-its-side\"\u003EThe import is the wrong size or lying on its side\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ:\u003C/strong\u003E OBJ has no unit; ModelCraft reads meters. A millimeter model comes in 1000 times too big: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003Escale\u003C/a\u003E it by \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E; an inch model by \u003Ccode\u003E0.0254\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ on its side:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft expects OBJ files with Y up. A file written with Z up comes in lying down. Rotate it 90° about the red axis: click the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E center on a face that faces along red, or open the file in its program and export with Y up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG or DXF:\u003C/strong\u003E you picked the wrong unit in the &quot;Drawing units&quot; dialog. Undo and import again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E for other formats\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or was changed by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is over 200 MB.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model is too large to open here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-lost-work\"\u003EI lost work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft does not save to a file by itself. When you close or reload a tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot; or &quot;Reload site?&quot;); choose to stay and save. New and Open ask too (&quot;Unsaved changes&quot;). If the browser or computer crashed, open ModelCraft again in the same browser: it offers the unsaved work back (&quot;Recover your work?&quot;; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E). Save often with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"my-downloads-folder-fills-up-with-copies\"\u003EMy downloads folder fills up with copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save is a new download, named \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (1).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (2).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E and so on. Delete the old ones, or set the browser to ask where to save each file and overwrite the old file yourself. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files#save-a-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\" data-hash=\"save-a-model\"\u003EOpen, save, import and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"it-gets-slow\"\u003EIt gets slow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery step of undo keeps a copy of the model (a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E keeps only what changed), so on a very large import of push/pull geometry each edit takes longer. Explode or erase what you don&#039;t need, save, and reopen: reopening also clears the undo history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESun shadows cost the most. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E while you model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"no-shadows\"\u003ENo shadows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E is off, the sun is down at the time you chose (&quot;The sun is down at this time.&quot;), or the style is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E, which draws no shadows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"this-browser-did-not-allow-full-screen\"\u003E&quot;This browser did not allow full screen.&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome browsers and some embedded views refuse full screen. Use the browser&#039;s own full screen instead (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft-does-not-open-without-a-connection\"\u003EModelCraft does not open without a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft opens offline only after it was saved for offline use in that browser: the app bar&#039;s button must read \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. Open it while online and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E. If the button reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E, click it again on a better connection; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E means this window (a private window, or a managed computer) cannot keep a copy. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The 3D view is blank Symptom: the middle of the screen stays empty, or shows only the background color, and clicking tools does nothing. Cause: ModelCraft draws with WebGL 2, the browser's 3D graphics. It is missing when the browser is old, when hardware acceleration is turned off, or when the graphics driver is blocked. Fix: Open the browser's settings and search for \"graphics acceleration\". Turn on \"Use graphics acceleration when available\" and relaunch the browser. If it is already on, type chrome://gpu in the address bar (edge://gpu in Edge) and look for \"WebGL2\" in the list. If it says disabled, update your graphics driver. Open Settings, then General, and scroll to \"Performance\". Untick \"Use recommended performance settings\", then tick \"Use hardware acceleration when available\". Restart Firefox. If it still fails, type about:support in the address bar and look at the \"Graphics\" section for WebGL 2. Update macOS and Safari. Recent versions of Safari always have WebGL 2 on. If you changed experimental features under Safari, then Settings, then Advanced (or Feature Flags), reset them. On a work computer, remote desktop or virtual machine, 3D graphics may be blocked by policy. Try another computer. See Browsers and computers. Keys do nothing, or type into a box Cause: the keyboard focus is in a panel field: the Faces list in Style, a sun setting, the Name field in Entity info, or the units picker. Letters and arrow keys then go to that field. Fix: click in the 3D view, then press the key again. Keys also do nothing while a dialog is open. Typing a number does nothing No direction yet. A typed length goes the way the pointer points from the first click. Move the pointer a little in the direction you want, then type. Wrong moment. Most tools take a typed value only after the first click: click the first corner, the face or the point to move from first. Circle and Polygon take a number of sides before the first click. \"Type a length such as 3m, 10'6\" or 4m,3m.\" ModelCraft could not read it. Check for a decimal comma (2,5): use a period. A shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen Symptom: with a finger, the first rectangle is fine, but the next rectangle, circle or line starts where your previous touch ended instead of where you touched. Cause: a bug in this build: on a touch screen the tool remembers the last snap point from the previous gesture. Fix: tap the tool's button in the toolbar again before each new shape. That starts the tool fresh, and the first point lands where you touch. Editing one group changed another Symptom: you copied a group with Move and Ctrl, opened one copy and edited it, and the other copy changed too. The Outliner shows both with the same name. Cause: in this build, copying a group makes a second placement of the same group, as with a component. ModelCraft's Make unique button is only offered for components. Fix: select the copy you want to change, click Make component in Entity info, then Make unique. That copy now has its own contents. Or Explode it. Push/pull does not do what I expected \"Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.\" You clicked an edge or empty space. Hover until the face highlights, then click. The face is inside a group. Double-click the group to open it first. A window did not cut through the wall. The rectangle must be drawn on the wall's face, not in front of it, and the wall must have a back face. See Cut an opening through a wall. Two boxes overlap without merging. Solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other in this build. A line or shape did not make a face A face fills in only when the edges close a flat loop. Check that the last point snapped to the first (\"Endpoint\"), and that all the edges lie in one plane. A loop that is slightly out of plane stays as lines. Draw one of its edges again, snapping to the corners. I can't select or erase a face With Select, a click near an edge takes the edge. Click nearer the middle of the face. The Eraser does not pick faces. Select the face and press Delete, or erase one of its edges. If it belongs to a group or component, the click selects the whole thing. Double-click to open it. \"Select something to move first, or click on it.\" Move, Rotate and Scale need something to work on: select it first, or start the tool by clicking on the thing itself (not on empty space or a guide). \"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\" G and Shift+G need a selection. Guides cannot be grouped. The arrow keys orbit instead of locking Locks work only after the first click of a drawing or editing tool. Push/pull has no locks, and in Rotate they do not change the turn. At other times the arrow keys orbit the view. Esc in full screen In full screen, the browser may use the first Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel the tool. No middle button Press O for Orbit and drag, or use the arrow keys. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Imports Message What to do \"ModelCraft imports SketchUp (.skp), Collada (.dae), OBJ, glTF (.glb/.gltf), FBX, PLY, STL, Alembic (.abc), DWG and DXF. … is none of these.\" Export the model from the other program in one of these formats \"This file is over 300 MB.\" Simplify or split the model in the other program \"…too big to model with here (over 2 million vertices).\" The same \"This .gltf file needs \"…\"…\" Drop the .gltf together with its .bin and texture files, or open the .glb version \"This glTF file needs the … extension, which ModelCraft does not read yet.\" Export again from the other program without that extension (KTX2 textures, for one) An OBJ comes in without its colors or textures Drop the .obj together with its .mtl and pictures \"This Alembic file uses the old HDF5 layout…\" Save it again from the other program as an Ogawa Alembic file An FBX comes in without its textures Drop the .fbx together with its texture pictures, or embed the media when exporting \"This FBX file is version …, which ModelCraft does not read\" Export again as FBX 2011 or newer An STL or PLY comes in 1,000 times too big or too small STL is read as millimeters and PLY as meters: scale it (see Rotate and scale) \"This is not a Collada (.dae) file…\" or \"…not well-formed XML…\" The file is damaged or not Collada. Export it again \"This Collada file has no geometry ModelCraft can import.\" It holds only cameras, lights or references to other files \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.\" Save it again from the CAD program as ASCII \"This is not a DWG drawing…\" The file is not a DWG, or it is damaged \"This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…\" The brackets give the reason. Save it as DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program \"This drawing reaches more than 10,000 km from its origin…\" Move the drawing near 0,0 in the CAD program The import is the wrong size or lying on its side OBJ: OBJ has no unit; ModelCraft reads meters. A millimeter model comes in 1000 times too big: scale it by 0.001; an inch model by 0.0254. OBJ on its side: ModelCraft expects OBJ files with Y up. A file written with Z up comes in lying down. Rotate it 90° about the red axis: click the Rotate center on a face that faces along red, or open the file in its program and export with Y up. DWG or DXF: you picked the wrong unit in the \"Drawing units\" dialog. Undo and import again. Opening files Message What to do \"This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).\" Use Import for other formats \"This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …\" The file is damaged or was changed by hand \"This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft…\" Open it in the newer version \"This file is over 200 MB.\" The model is too large to open here I lost work ModelCraft does not save to a file by itself. When you close or reload a tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\" or \"Reload site?\"); choose to stay and save. New and Open ask too (\"Unsaved changes\"). If the browser or computer crashed, open ModelCraft again in the same browser: it offers the unsaved work back (\"Recover your work?\"; see Recover your work). Save often with Ctrl+S. My downloads folder fills up with copies Each save is a new download, named Untitled (1).modelcraft, Untitled (2).modelcraft and so on. Delete the old ones, or set the browser to ask where to save each file and overwrite the old file yourself. See Open, save, import and export. It gets slow Every step of undo keeps a copy of the model (a mesh keeps only what changed), so on a very large import of push/pull geometry each edit takes longer. Explode or erase what you don't need, save, and reopen: reopening also clears the undo history. Sun shadows cost the most. Untick Sun shadows while you model. No shadows Sun shadows is off, the sun is down at the time you chose (\"The sun is down at this time.\"), or the style is Wireframe, which draws no shadows. \"This browser did not allow full screen.\" Some browsers and some embedded views refuse full screen. Use the browser's own full screen instead (Control-Command-F / F11). ModelCraft does not open without a connection ModelCraft opens offline only after it was saved for offline use in that browser: the app bar's button must read Offline ready. Open it while online and click Save for offline. If the button reads Could not save, click it again on a better connection; Offline not available means this window (a private window, or a managed computer) cannot keep a copy. See Working offline.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Not built yet","summary":"What this build of ModelCraft does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","not yet","limitations","roadmap","materials","textures","text","layers","trim","split","cloud","desktop"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-tools","text":"Drawing tools","level":2},{"id":"animation","text":"Animation","level":2},{"id":"meshes","text":"Meshes","level":2},{"id":"organizing-and-viewing","text":"Organizing and viewing","level":2},{"id":"rendering","text":"Rendering","level":2},{"id":"materials-and-colors","text":"Materials and colors","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"3d-printing","text":"3D printing","level":2},{"id":"performance","text":"Performance","level":2},{"id":"apps-and-platforms","text":"Apps and platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you don&#039;t go looking for it, and the nearest way to get the job done today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-tools\"\u003EDrawing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreehand drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the points with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an arc&#039;s or circle&#039;s number of segments after drawing it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase it and draw it again after typing \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoosing a typeface for \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/text3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/text3d\"\u003E3D text\u003C/a\u003E, or editing its words after placing it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete it and add the text again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an angle by typing into an angle dimension, or a cylinder&#039;s size from its circle&#039;s dimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate the edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E; scale the cylinder with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions in exported model files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions show in images, prints and PDFs of the view; model files carry geometry only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim, Split and Outer shell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003EUnion, Subtract and Intersect\u003C/a\u003E closed groups, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003Eintersect faces\u003C/a\u003E and erase what you do not need\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoosing the rotation axis with the arrow keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E center on a face that faces the way the axis should point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks on more than one inference at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock one direction with an arrow key, then snap to a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EResizing along a slant, or from a dimension end that is not on a corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace the dimension on two corners, along an edge or along red, green or blue\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"animation\"\u003EAnimation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnimating faces, edges, the sun&#039;s time of day, the style or what is hidden\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake the moving part a group or component; render a separate video for another time of day\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA camera that follows a drawn path, or looks at a moving object\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet more camera keys along the way (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003EAnimate\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESound in videos, and high-quality (path-traced) videos without WebGPU\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd sound in a video editor; without WebGPU, render the video with the fast renderer (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation#render-the-video\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\" data-hash=\"render-the-video\"\u003ERender the video\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnimated lamps (brightness, spread, colour) in a rendered video, a lens angle animated without camera keys in a video, animating materials or render settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELamps animate in the view; for a video, key the camera too for a zoom; render separate videos for different lamp settings (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\"\u003EAnimate any property\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirroring weights or bones from one side to the other, bone envelopes you can see, showing weights shaded point by point (they show per face), seeing the posed mesh while painting weights\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaint both sides, or make one half and use a Mirror modifier before the armature is added; switch between Paint weights and Pose to check\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBones read from glTF, FBX or USD files as an armature, and armatures written to those files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkinned models come in at rest with the file&#039;s weights; add the bones in the file&#039;s joint order, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse the file&#039;s weights\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animate\"\u003EAnimate\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving an IK target or a pole by dragging it in the view, a constraint&#039;s own space (local or world) to choose, stretching IK chains, IK along a curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType the target&#039;s point, or aim at an object and move that; keep chains to their lengths\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloth, fluids, soft bodies, joints and hinges between rigid bodies, collision with an object&#039;s exact shape (only boxes and balls round it), a live simulation while you play, a starting speed or spin (bodies start at rest)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBake rigid bodies and play the keys; build a hinge from keys; split an odd shape into boxes (groups)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExporting the animation to glTF or other model files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERender a video; the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file, and a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD file\u003C/a\u003E ModelCraft saved, keep it for ModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping a comment or a title on an issue in VR, voice notes, moving a section plane by hand in VR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace the issue in VR, then add its words in a BCF program; set a section plane in the view before you go in: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/vr-review\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/vr-review\"\u003EReview the model in a VR headset\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeeing people at the desk in VR (only people in VR show), recording a VR walk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk them to join in VR, or make an \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003Eanimation\u003C/a\u003E of the walk\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"meshes\"\u003EMeshes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESculpting by levels (multiresolution), and Detail taking away triangles that became tiny\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sculpt\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sculpt\"\u003ESculpt\u003C/a\u003E a subdivided mesh; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemesh\u003C/span\u003E to even it out again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA remesh into four-sided faces that follow the shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemesh\u003C/span\u003E (even triangles), then rebuild it with \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit#retopology\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-hash=\"retopology\"\u003ERetopology\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeights painted on vertices for modifiers (Displace, Smooth and Shrinkwrap work on the whole mesh)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApply the modifier, then sculpt or edit the parts that need it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste of vertices, edges or faces, a knife cut that ends inside a face, wire edges with no face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the mesh in Model; knife from edge to edge; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/mesh-edit#change-the-shape\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/mesh-edit\" data-hash=\"change-the-shape\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/a\u003E open edges into faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organizing-and-viewing\"\u003EOrganizing and viewing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers of DWG and DXF site plans as tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe site plan comes in as one group: explode it and group the parts you want to tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral section cuts at once, a section box, or a section&#039;s outline exported as a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sections\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sections\"\u003Esection plane\u003C/a\u003E cuts at a time; export the view as an image\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShowing soft edges dashed, or softening while you drag a slider\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003ESoften by angle\u003C/a\u003E in Entity info, and undo to try another angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn online library of models to search, or components with options to pick (colour, style)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/starter\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/starter\"\u003Estarter library\u003C/a\u003E; import Collada, OBJ or glTF models; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/stretch\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/stretch\"\u003Estretch\u003C/a\u003E a copy to size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlacing plants from the plant library\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view#scatter-plants-rocks-and-people\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\" data-hash=\"scatter-plants-rocks-and-people\"\u003EScatter\u003C/a\u003E plants in the live view, or place a starter-library tree\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIndependent copies of a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake it a component, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rendering\"\u003ERendering\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERendering in the view (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E workspace), the render queue, and the camera, sky-picture, fog and light-mixing options without WebGPU\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality\u003C/span\u003E uses the WebGL path tracer there, without those options (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render#high-quality\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render\" data-hash=\"high-quality\"\u003ERender a picture\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnother program drawing the Render picture without a render-engine add-on (CadCraft never runs a program under the GNU GPL licence); panorama or stereo cameras and a sky picture through another program; the Unreal Engine plug-in (the files are written, the plug-in that opens them is not released)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOur own Fast and Path tracer engines; an add-on&#039;s engine (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/render-other-program\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/render-other-program\"\u003Edraw the picture with another program\u003C/a\u003E); a USD, glTF or Unreal folder file from the Engine list to take to any program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScattered plants and rocks in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender a picture…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/live-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/live-view\"\u003Elive view\u003C/a\u003E, its panoramas, viewer and video\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"materials-and-colors\"\u003EMaterials and colors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInputs other than base color, roughness, metalness, opacity, normal and emission changing across a surface in high-quality pictures (they use their average)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\"\u003EMake materials with nodes\u003C/a\u003E: link them anyway, the view shows them varying\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDisplacement on push/pull faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert the object to a mesh for displacement\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESharing your own materials to the Library from the app, and packed (ARM/ORM) or EXR maps in an imported texture set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep them in the file; import the separate maps as PNG or JPEG\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterials of the file in Live TeamWork sessions (they stay on the computer that made them)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the file and share it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecals bending around curved surfaces, decals in Undo, or painting several faces&#039; textures at once across a seam\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace smaller decals on each part; paint each side of a seam on its own\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBaking onto push/pull faces, baking several objects into one picture, a cage you shape by hand, or baked lighting from lamps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert to a mesh and unwrap it; bake each object; raise the search distance; render a picture for lamp light\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPainting roughness, metalness or height, painting with pictures (stamps) and texture painting larger than 512 × 512\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaint the base color here; make other maps in a picture editor and import them as a texture set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures in Collada, STL, PLY, 3MF, IFC, DWG and DXF exports, and the node graphs of the file&#039;s own materials in exported models\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose formats get each material&#039;s color; glTF and OBJ carry imported textures; export a material on its own as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading#materialx\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\" data-hash=\"materialx\"\u003EMaterialX\u003C/a\u003E; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/a\u003E keeps the whole model for ModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures in Collada files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out on import; textures in \u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E files come in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving SketchUp files, and text and dimensions from them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-sketchup-files\"\u003EImport\u003C/a\u003E keeps faces, colors and textures; export Collada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E) to go back to SketchUp\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions and guides in DWG and DXF exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd them in the CAD program after exporting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out; text comes in as single-stroke lettering, not the drawing&#039;s font\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurfaces, meshes, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF; faces of 3D solids on swept, lofted or other freeform surfaces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out too (3D solids themselves come in). Export those parts from the CAD program as OBJ or Collada and import that\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC types, properties or materials\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC export has \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#ifc-ifc\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"ifc-ifc\"\u003Estoreys\u003C/a\u003E and generic elements; add the rest in a BIM program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending a model straight to BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E and open it in BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoose edges in glTF and STL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse OBJ or Collada for line work\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF and FBX materials beyond the base color (metal, roughness, normal maps, glass, clear coat, sheen…)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are read and listed; ModelCraft keeps the base color or texture. Rebuild the look in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/shading\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/shading\"\u003EShading workspace\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF bones, animations and morph targets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkinned models come in standing as they rest; animate in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003Etimeline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColors and the assembly&#039;s part names from STEP files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParts come in as meshes at their true size; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/paint\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/paint\"\u003Epaint\u003C/a\u003E and name them after import\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubD objects, text, dimensions and lights from \u003Ccode\u003E.3dm\u003C/code\u003E files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/import-3dm-files#what-is-left-out-and-said\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/import-3dm-files\" data-hash=\"what-is-left-out-and-said\"\u003Ewhat is left out\u003C/a\u003E; turn SubD into meshes in the program that made the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKTX2 textures and material variants in glTF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport with PNG or JPEG textures; the default variant is used\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWriting glTF instancing (EXT_mesh_gpu_instancing)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComponents are written once and placed many times, which every viewer reads\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlembic animation (every sample of a cache), Alembic points and curves, writing Alembic\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first sample comes in; animate in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/animation\"\u003Etimeline\u003C/a\u003E; write glTF, USD or OBJ\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBump and normal maps in OBJ materials\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly the color picture (\u003Ccode\u003Emap_Kd\u003C/code\u003E) comes in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFBX bones and animation, FBX export, FBX 6 files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkinned models come in at rest; export glTF; save old files as FBX 2011 or newer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECollada skins, morphs, cameras, lights, look-at and skew transforms, external references\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft out on import and counted in the message\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackup files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) saves another copy. After a crash, ModelCraft offers your unsaved work back (&quot;Recover your work?&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Save files to your computer, and copy them where you need them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-printing\"\u003E3D printing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReal slicer supports (interfaces, raft, the exact support pattern)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#supports-preview\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"supports-preview\"\u003Esupports preview\u003C/a\u003E shows where and about how much; your slicer&#039;s preview shows the real ones\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUntangling a part that passes through itself, or edges where three or more faces meet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#repair\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"repair\"\u003ERepair\u003C/a\u003E lists them with where they are; use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003EIntersect faces\u003C/a\u003E and erase what is inside\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInfill patterns and a lattice inside a hollow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#hollow\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"hollow\"\u003EHollow\u003C/a\u003E leaves it empty; your slicer adds infill to a solid part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetails too small for your nozzle (other than walls)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/print-tools#wall-thickness\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/print-tools\" data-hash=\"wall-thickness\"\u003EWall thickness\u003C/a\u003E finds thin walls; your slicer&#039;s layer preview shows the rest\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"performance\"\u003EPerformance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo keeps a copy of the model for each step (a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/meshes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/meshes\"\u003Emesh\u003C/a\u003E keeps only what changed), so very large imports of push/pull geometry make every edit slower. Keep imports lean, and save and reopen to clear the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"apps-and-platforms\"\u003EApps and platforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDesktop:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux, and in CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install. On Android, CadCraft Studio installs from its APK on getcadcraft.com/download. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch:\u003C/strong\u003E see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E for the finger gestures. A pen&#039;s pressure sets the brush in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sculpt\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sculpt\"\u003ESculpt\u003C/a\u003E; elsewhere pressure and pen tilt are not used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheets:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model#print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\" data-hash=\"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\"\u003Eprints one view per page\u003C/a\u003E; there are no sheet layouts with several views or a title block. Export to CadCraft-friendly DWG/DXF for drawings to scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"ModelCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you don't go looking for it, and the nearest way to get the job done today. Drawing tools Not yet Instead, today Freehand drawing Click the points with Curve Changing an arc's or circle's number of segments after drawing it Erase it and draw it again after typing 24s Choosing a typeface for 3D text, or editing its words after placing it Delete it and add the text again Changing an angle by typing into an angle dimension, or a cylinder's size from its circle's dimension Rotate the edges with Rotate; scale the cylinder with Scale Dimensions in exported model files Dimensions show in images, prints and PDFs of the view; model files carry geometry only Trim, Split and Outer shell Union, Subtract and Intersect closed groups, or intersect faces and erase what you do not need Choosing the rotation axis with the arrow keys Click the Rotate center on a face that faces the way the axis should point Locks on more than one inference at a time Lock one direction with an arrow key, then snap to a point Resizing along a slant, or from a dimension end that is not on a corner Place the dimension on two corners, along an edge or along red, green or blue Animation Not yet Instead, today Animating faces, edges, the sun's time of day, the style or what is hidden Make the moving part a group or component; render a separate video for another time of day A camera that follows a drawn path, or looks at a moving object Set more camera keys along the way (Animate) Sound in videos, and high-quality (path-traced) videos without WebGPU Add sound in a video editor; without WebGPU, render the video with the fast renderer (Render the video) Animated lamps (brightness, spread, colour) in a rendered video, a lens angle animated without camera keys in a video, animating materials or render settings Lamps animate in the view; for a video, key the camera too for a zoom; render separate videos for different lamp settings (Animate any property) Mirroring weights or bones from one side to the other, bone envelopes you can see, showing weights shaded point by point (they show per face), seeing the posed mesh while painting weights Paint both sides, or make one half and use a Mirror modifier before the armature is added; switch between Paint weights and Pose to check Bones read from glTF, FBX or USD files as an armature, and armatures written to those files Skinned models come in at rest with the file's weights; add the bones in the file's joint order, then Use the file's weights (Animate) Moving an IK target or a pole by dragging it in the view, a constraint's own space (local or world) to choose, stretching IK chains, IK along a curve Type the target's point, or aim at an object and move that; keep chains to their lengths Cloth, fluids, soft bodies, joints and hinges between rigid bodies, collision with an object's exact shape (only boxes and balls round it), a live simulation while you play, a starting speed or spin (bodies start at rest) Bake rigid bodies and play the keys; build a hinge from keys; split an odd shape into boxes (groups) Exporting the animation to glTF or other model files Render a video; the .modelcraft file, and a USD file ModelCraft saved, keep it for ModelCraft Typing a comment or a title on an issue in VR, voice notes, moving a section plane by hand in VR Place the issue in VR, then add its words in a BCF program; set a section plane in the view before you go in: see Review the model in a VR headset Seeing people at the desk in VR (only people in VR show), recording a VR walk Ask them to join in VR, or make an animation of the walk Meshes Not yet Instead, today Sculpting by levels (multiresolution), and Detail taking away triangles that became tiny Sculpt a subdivided mesh; Remesh to even it out again A remesh into four-sided faces that follow the shape Remesh (even triangles), then rebuild it with Retopology Weights painted on vertices for modifiers (Displace, Smooth and Shrinkwrap work on the whole mesh) Apply the modifier, then sculpt or edit the parts that need it Copy and paste of vertices, edges or faces, a knife cut that ends inside a face, wire edges with no face Duplicate the mesh in Model; knife from edge to edge; Extrude open edges into faces Organizing and viewing Not yet Instead, today Layers of DWG and DXF site plans as tags The site plan comes in as one group: explode it and group the parts you want to tag Several section cuts at once, a section box, or a section's outline exported as a drawing One section plane cuts at a time; export the view as an image Showing soft edges dashed, or softening while you drag a slider Soften by angle in Entity info, and undo to try another angle An online library of models to search, or components with options to pick (colour, style) The starter library; import Collada, OBJ or glTF models; stretch a copy to size Placing plants from the plant library Scatter plants in the live view, or place a starter-library tree Independent copies of a group Make it a component, then Make unique (see Troubleshooting) Rendering Not yet Instead, today Rendering in the view (the Render workspace), the render queue, and the camera, sky-picture, fog and light-mixing options without WebGPU Render a picture… in High quality uses the WebGL path tracer there, without those options (Render a picture) Another program drawing the Render picture without a render-engine add-on (CadCraft never runs a program under the GNU GPL licence); panorama or stereo cameras and a sky picture through another program; the Unreal Engine plug-in (the files are written, the plug-in that opens them is not released) Our own Fast and Path tracer engines; an add-on's engine (draw the picture with another program); a USD, glTF or Unreal folder file from the Engine list to take to any program Scattered plants and rocks in Render a picture… They show in the live view, its panoramas, viewer and video Materials and colors Not yet Instead, today Inputs other than base color, roughness, metalness, opacity, normal and emission changing across a surface in high-quality pictures (they use their average) Make materials with nodes: link them anyway, the view shows them varying Displacement on push/pull faces Convert the object to a mesh for displacement Sharing your own materials to the Library from the app, and packed (ARM/ORM) or EXR maps in an imported texture set Keep them in the file; import the separate maps as PNG or JPEG Materials of the file in Live TeamWork sessions (they stay on the computer that made them) Save the file and share it Decals bending around curved surfaces, decals in Undo, or painting several faces' textures at once across a seam Place smaller decals on each part; paint each side of a seam on its own Baking onto push/pull faces, baking several objects into one picture, a cage you shape by hand, or baked lighting from lamps Convert to a mesh and unwrap it; bake each object; raise the search distance; render a picture for lamp light Painting roughness, metalness or height, painting with pictures (stamps) and texture painting larger than 512 × 512 Paint the base color here; make other maps in a picture editor and import them as a texture set Textures in Collada, STL, PLY, 3MF, IFC, DWG and DXF exports, and the node graphs of the file's own materials in exported models Those formats get each material's color; glTF and OBJ carry imported textures; export a material on its own as MaterialX; USD keeps the whole model for ModelCraft Textures in Collada files They are counted and left out on import; textures in .skp and .glb files come in Files Not yet Instead, today Saving SketchUp files, and text and dimensions from them Import keeps faces, colors and textures; export Collada (.dae) to go back to SketchUp Dimensions and guides in DWG and DXF exports Add them in the CAD program after exporting Points, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF They are counted and left out; text comes in as single-stroke lettering, not the drawing's font Surfaces, meshes, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF; faces of 3D solids on swept, lofted or other freeform surfaces They are counted and left out too (3D solids themselves come in). Export those parts from the CAD program as OBJ or Collada and import that IFC types, properties or materials The IFC export has storeys and generic elements; add the rest in a BIM program Sending a model straight to BimCraft Export IFC for BimCraft (.ifc) and open it in BimCraft Loose edges in glTF and STL Use OBJ or Collada for line work glTF and FBX materials beyond the base color (metal, roughness, normal maps, glass, clear coat, sheen…) They are read and listed; ModelCraft keeps the base color or texture. Rebuild the look in the Shading workspace glTF bones, animations and morph targets Skinned models come in standing as they rest; animate in the timeline Colors and the assembly's part names from STEP files Parts come in as meshes at their true size; paint and name them after import SubD objects, text, dimensions and lights from .3dm files See what is left out; turn SubD into meshes in the program that made the file KTX2 textures and material variants in glTF Export with PNG or JPEG textures; the default variant is used Writing glTF instancing (EXT_mesh_gpu_instancing) Components are written once and placed many times, which every viewer reads Alembic animation (every sample of a cache), Alembic points and curves, writing Alembic The first sample comes in; animate in the timeline; write glTF, USD or OBJ Bump and normal maps in OBJ materials Only the color picture (map_Kd) comes in FBX bones and animation, FBX export, FBX 6 files Skinned models come in at rest; export glTF; save old files as FBX 2011 or newer Collada skins, morphs, cameras, lights, look-at and skew transforms, external references Left out on import and counted in the message Backup files Save as (Ctrl+Shift+S) saves another copy. After a crash, ModelCraft offers your unsaved work back (\"Recover your work?\") Cloud saving and sync between computers Not available yet. Save files to your computer, and copy them where you need them 3D printing Not yet Instead, today Real slicer supports (interfaces, raft, the exact support pattern) The supports preview shows where and about how much; your slicer's preview shows the real ones Untangling a part that passes through itself, or edges where three or more faces meet Repair lists them with where they are; use Intersect faces and erase what is inside Infill patterns and a lattice inside a hollow Hollow leaves it empty; your slicer adds infill to a solid part Details too small for your nozzle (other than walls) Wall thickness finds thin walls; your slicer's layer preview shows the rest Performance Undo keeps a copy of the model for each step (a mesh keeps only what changed), so very large imports of push/pull geometry make every edit slower. Keep imports lean, and save and reopen to clear the history. Apps and platforms Desktop: ModelCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux, and in CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install. On Android, CadCraft Studio installs from its APK on getcadcraft.com/download. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet. Touch: see Orbit, pan and zoom for the finger gestures. A pen's pressure sets the brush in Sculpt; elsewhere pressure and pen tilt are not used. Sheets: ModelCraft prints one view per page; there are no sheet layouts with several views or a title block. Export to CadCraft-friendly DWG/DXF for drawings to scale.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"ఈ సహాయాన్ని ఉపయోగించడం","summary":"సహాయాన్ని తెరవండి, దానిలో వెతకండి, మరియు మీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం దశలను చూడండి.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["సహాయం","f1","వెతుకు","ట్యుటోరియల్స్","మాన్యువల్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"సహాయం తెరవండి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"టాపిక్‌ను కనుగొను","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"మీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం దశలు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"వెబ్‌సైట్‌లో అదే సహాయం","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి యాప్ తన స్వంత సహాయంతో వస్తుంది. ఇది యాప్‌లో భాగం, కాబట్టి కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు మీరు నడుపుతున్న వెర్షన్‌ను ఎల్లప్పుడూ వివరిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eసహాయం తెరవండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా యాప్ పైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసహాయం\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదాన్ని దాచడానికి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమూసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. మీ డ్రాయింగ్ ఉన్నట్లే ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eచాలా Mac కీబోర్డ్‌లలో పై వరుస ప్రకాశం మరియు ధ్వనిని నియంత్రిస్తుంది. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E తో పాటు \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E కీ (లేదా గ్లోబ్ కీ) నొక్కండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసహాయం\u003C/span\u003E బటన్ ఉపయోగించండి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EMac లో కీలు\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eటాపిక్‌ను కనుగొను\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eసహాయం పైన ఉన్న శోధన బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయండి. మీరు టైప్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు ఫలితాలు మారతాయి, మరియు ఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల కమాండ్ పేర్లను కూడా కలిగి ఉంటాయి, కాబట్టి మీరు మరెక్కడైనా టైప్ చేసేది టైప్ చేస్తే సాధారణంగా సరైన పేజీ దొరుకుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eటాపిక్‌లు ప్రతి యాప్‌లో ఒకే విధంగా వర్గీకరించబడతాయి: ప్రారంభం, వాక్‌త్రూలు, ఇతర సాఫ్ట్‌వేర్ నుండి వచ్చేవారు, రిఫరెన్స్ మరియు ట్రబుల్‌షూటింగ్.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eటాపిక్‌లోని లింక్‌లు అదే ప్యానెల్‌లో తదుపరి టాపిక్‌ను తెరుస్తాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవెనక్కి\u003C/span\u003E మీరు ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి తీసుకువెళ్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eమీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం దశలు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకొన్ని దశలు Mac, Windows మరియు Linux మధ్య, బ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య, మరియు ఆన్‌లైన్ లేదా ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉన్నప్పుడు భిన్నంగా ఉంటాయి. సహాయం మీకు ఏది వర్తిస్తుందో గుర్తించి ఆ దశలను చూపిస్తుంది. టాపిక్ పైభాగంలోని వరుస ఏమి ఎంచుకుందో చెబుతుంది, ఉదాహరణకు &quot;Mac, Chrome లేదా Edge కోసం దశలు చూపిస్తోంది, ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఆ వరుసపై \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమార్చు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, మరో కంప్యూటర్ లేదా బ్రౌజర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి, ఉదాహరణకు సహోద్యోగికి సహాయం చేయడానికి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్రతి దశ యొక్క ప్రతి వెర్షన్‌ను, ప్రతిదానికి లేబుల్‌తో చూడటానికి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅన్నీ చూపించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eషార్ట్‌కట్‌లు మీ కీబోర్డ్ వాటిపై ఎలా లేబుల్ చేస్తుందో అలా రాయబడతాయి: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E Mac లో ⌘S గా, Windows మరియు Linux లో Ctrl+S గా చదవబడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eవెబ్‌సైట్‌లో అదే సహాయం\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eవెబ్‌సైట్ అదే టాపిక్‌లను getcadcraft.com/tutorials వద్ద, ప్రతి ప్లాట్‌ఫామ్ దశలతో చూపిస్తుంది. యాప్‌లో, టాపిక్ దిగువన ఉన్న \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవెబ్‌సైట్‌లో తెరువు\u003C/span\u003E మీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు ఆ పేజీని తెరుస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ప్రతి యాప్ తన స్వంత సహాయంతో వస్తుంది. ఇది యాప్‌లో భాగం, కాబట్టి కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు మీరు నడుపుతున్న వెర్షన్‌ను ఎల్లప్పుడూ వివరిస్తుంది. సహాయం తెరవండి F1 నొక్కండి, లేదా యాప్ పైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో సహాయం ఎంచుకోండి. దాన్ని దాచడానికి Esc నొక్కండి లేదా మూసివేయి ఎంచుకోండి. మీ డ్రాయింగ్ ఉన్నట్లే ఉంటుంది. చాలా Mac కీబోర్డ్‌లలో పై వరుస ప్రకాశం మరియు ధ్వనిని నియంత్రిస్తుంది. F1 తో పాటు fn కీ (లేదా గ్లోబ్ కీ) నొక్కండి, లేదా సహాయం బటన్ ఉపయోగించండి. Mac లో కీలు చూడండి. టాపిక్‌ను కనుగొను సహాయం పైన ఉన్న శోధన బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయండి. మీరు టైప్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు ఫలితాలు మారతాయి, మరియు ఇతర ప్రోగ్రామ్‌ల కమాండ్ పేర్లను కూడా కలిగి ఉంటాయి, కాబట్టి మీరు మరెక్కడైనా టైప్ చేసేది టైప్ చేస్తే సాధారణంగా సరైన పేజీ దొరుకుతుంది. టాపిక్‌లు ప్రతి యాప్‌లో ఒకే విధంగా వర్గీకరించబడతాయి: ప్రారంభం, వాక్‌త్రూలు, ఇతర సాఫ్ట్‌వేర్ నుండి వచ్చేవారు, రిఫరెన్స్ మరియు ట్రబుల్‌షూటింగ్. టాపిక్‌లోని లింక్‌లు అదే ప్యానెల్‌లో తదుపరి టాపిక్‌ను తెరుస్తాయి. వెనక్కి మీరు ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి తీసుకువెళ్తుంది. మీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం దశలు కొన్ని దశలు Mac, Windows మరియు Linux మధ్య, బ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య, మరియు ఆన్‌లైన్ లేదా ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉన్నప్పుడు భిన్నంగా ఉంటాయి. సహాయం మీకు ఏది వర్తిస్తుందో గుర్తించి ఆ దశలను చూపిస్తుంది. టాపిక్ పైభాగంలోని వరుస ఏమి ఎంచుకుందో చెబుతుంది, ఉదాహరణకు \"Mac, Chrome లేదా Edge కోసం దశలు చూపిస్తోంది, ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో\". ఆ వరుసపై మార్చు ఎంచుకోండి, మరో కంప్యూటర్ లేదా బ్రౌజర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి, ఉదాహరణకు సహోద్యోగికి సహాయం చేయడానికి. ప్రతి దశ యొక్క ప్రతి వెర్షన్‌ను, ప్రతిదానికి లేబుల్‌తో చూడటానికి అన్నీ చూపించు ఎంచుకోండి. షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు మీ కీబోర్డ్ వాటిపై ఎలా లేబుల్ చేస్తుందో అలా రాయబడతాయి: Ctrl+S Mac లో ⌘S గా, Windows మరియు Linux లో Ctrl+S గా చదవబడుతుంది. వెబ్‌సైట్‌లో అదే సహాయం వెబ్‌సైట్ అదే టాపిక్‌లను getcadcraft.com/tutorials వద్ద, ప్రతి ప్లాట్‌ఫామ్ దశలతో చూపిస్తుంది. యాప్‌లో, టాపిక్ దిగువన ఉన్న వెబ్‌సైట్‌లో తెరువు మీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు ఆ పేజీని తెరుస్తుంది.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"బ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు కంప్యూటర్‌లు","summary":"యాప్‌లు ఏ బ్రౌజర్‌లలో నడుస్తాయి, వాటి మధ్య తేడా ఏమిటి, మరియు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఏమి కావాలి.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","బ్రౌజర్","mac","windows","linux","ipad","టాబ్లెట్","webgl","అవసరాలు","మెమరీ"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"ఏ బ్రౌజర్","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"బ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య తేడా ఏమిటి","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-linux","text":"Mac, Windows మరియు Linux","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"టాబ్లెట్‌లు మరియు టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌లు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"అతి పాత బ్రౌజర్ మరియు సిస్టమ్","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"మీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఏమి కావాలి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eయాప్‌లు ఒక వెబ్ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల నడుస్తాయి. అవి పని మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌పైనే చేస్తాయి: మీ ఫైళ్లు అక్కడే తెరవబడతాయి మరియు మాకు ఎప్పుడూ పంపబడవు (చూడండి: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003Eమీ ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయి\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eఏ బ్రౌజర్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఈ వాటిలో ఒకదాని తాజా వెర్షన్ ఉపయోగించండి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eఇంజిన్\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eగమనికలు\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eఅత్యంత పూర్తి: ఫోల్డర్ ఎంపిక, ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్, ఫైల్ హ్యాండ్లింగ్.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eపనిచేస్తుంది. మొత్తం ఫోల్డర్ తెరవడానికి సరళమైన పికర్ ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. ప్రైవేట్ విండోలలో ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సేవ్ పనిచేయకపోవచ్చు.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMac మరియు iPad లో పనిచేస్తుంది. మొత్తం ఫోల్డర్ తెరవడానికి సరళమైన పికర్ ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. Safari కొన్ని వారాలుగా వాడని ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కాపీని తొలగించవచ్చు.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమూడు ఇంజిన్‌లూ రోజువారీ పనికి బాగానే ఉంటాయి. ఒక ఇంజిన్‌లో యాప్ భిన్నంగా ఏదైనా చేసినప్పుడు, దాని సహాయం మీ బ్రౌజర్ కోసం దశలను చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య తేడా ఏమిటి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఫోల్డర్ తెరవడం.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium బ్రౌజర్‌లు నిజమైన ఫోల్డర్ పికర్ చూపిస్తాయి మరియు ఫోల్డర్‌ను నేరుగా చదవగలవు. Firefox మరియు Safari మీరు ఒక ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోమని అడిగి, అందులోని ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను చదువుతాయి; పెద్ద ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫోల్డర్‌లకు ఇంకా ఎక్కువ సమయం పడుతుంది. ఫోల్డర్‌ను యాప్‌పైకి లాగడం మూడింటిలోనూ పనిచేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eసేవ్ చేయడం.\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్‌లు ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయడం ద్వారా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి. డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయో, మరియు ముందుగా అడగాలా వద్దా అనేది మీ బ్రౌజర్ నిర్ణయిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome లేదా Edge లో, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eడౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E కింద ఉన్న \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E లో &quot;ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయడానికి ముందు ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగు&quot; ఆన్ చేయండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox లో, సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, జనరల్, తర్వాత డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు తెరిచి, &quot;ఫైల్‌లను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో ఎల్లప్పుడూ అడుగు&quot; ఎంచుకోండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari లో, సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, జనరల్ ఎంచుకుని, &quot;ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ స్థానం&quot; ను &quot;ప్రతి డౌన్‌లోడ్‌కు అడుగు&quot; కు సెట్ చేయండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows మరియు Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eయాప్‌లు ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో ఒకేలా ఉంటాయి. మారేది కీబోర్డ్: షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు మీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం స్వయంచాలకంగా రాయబడతాయి. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003Eకీలు, మౌస్‌లు మరియు ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eటాబ్లెట్‌లు మరియు టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకొన్ని యాప్‌లు టచ్‌ను అంగీకరిస్తాయి (ఒక వేలు గీస్తుంది లేదా ఎంచుకుంటుంది, రెండు వేళ్లు జూమ్ మరియు ప్యాన్ కోసం చిటికెడు). ప్రతి యాప్ సహాయం అది చేస్తుందో లేదో చెబుతుంది. iPad లో, Safari లేదా Chrome ఉపయోగించండి. టైప్ చేసిన కమాండ్‌లు ఉపయోగించే యాప్‌లకు కీబోర్డ్ సహాయపడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eఅతి పాత బ్రౌజర్ మరియు సిస్టమ్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇవి 0.7 యాప్‌ల కోడ్ నుండి చదవబడ్డాయి, ఊహ నుండి కాదు: ప్రతి ఫైల్ విశ్లేషించబడింది మరియు అది ఉపయోగించే ప్రతి భాషా ఫీచర్ బ్రౌజర్‌ల ప్రచురించిన అనుకూలత డేటాలో వెతకబడింది. ఈ వెర్షన్‌ల కంటే తక్కువగా ఉంటే, బ్రౌజర్ కోడ్‌ను అర్థం చేసుకోనందున యాప్‌లు ప్రారంభం కూడా కావు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eయాప్‌లు ప్రారంభం కాగల అతి పాత వెర్షన్\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium 94\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E93\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari (Mac, iPad, iPhone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS and iOS 16.4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft Studio Windows లో తన స్వంత వెబ్ ఇంజిన్‌ను తీసుకువెళ్తుంది, కాబట్టి దానికి Windows 10 లేదా 11 కావాలి; Mac లో అది సిస్టమ్‌దే ఉపయోగిస్తుంది, కాబట్టి macOS 13.3 లేదా అంతకంటే కొత్తది కావాలి. Studio లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅప్‌డేట్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E మీ కాపీ ఏ సిస్టమ్‌పై నడుస్తుందో చూపిస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇది కోడ్ నుండి చదవబడిన, \u003Cem\u003Eప్రారంభమయ్యే\u003C/em\u003E అతి పాత వెర్షన్. ఇది మేము యాప్‌లను నడిపిన పాత బ్రౌజర్‌ల జాబితా కాదు, కాబట్టి ప్రారంభమయ్యే పాతది ఇంకా ఒక ఫీచర్‌ను కోల్పోవచ్చు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eమీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఏమి కావాలి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇవి యాప్‌ల ప్రణాళిక చేసిన కనీస అవసరాలు, కొలిచిన అవసరాలు కాదు:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB మెమరీ (పెద్ద federated మోడల్స్ కోసం 16 GB సిఫార్సు చేయబడుతుంది).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D యాప్‌ల కోసం WebGL 2 ఉన్న గ్రాఫిక్స్ చిప్. 3D వీక్షణ ఖాళీగా ఉంటే, మీ బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లలో హార్డ్‌వేర్ యాక్సిలరేషన్ ఆన్‌లో ఉందో లేదో చూడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపూర్తి టూల్‌బార్ల కోసం కనీసం 1280 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు ఉన్న స్క్రీన్; చిన్న స్క్రీన్‌లు వాటిని వరుసలుగా మడతపెడతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eచాలా పెద్ద ఫైళ్లు (పెద్ద ప్లాన్ సెట్‌లు, పెద్ద మోడల్స్) బ్రౌజర్ ట్యాబ్‌లో చాలా మెమరీ వాడతాయి. యాప్ నెమ్మదిస్తే ఇతర భారీ ట్యాబ్‌లను మూసివేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"యాప్‌లు ఒక వెబ్ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల నడుస్తాయి. అవి పని మీ స్వంత కంప్యూటర్‌పైనే చేస్తాయి: మీ ఫైళ్లు అక్కడే తెరవబడతాయి మరియు మాకు ఎప్పుడూ పంపబడవు (చూడండి: మీ ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయి). ఏ బ్రౌజర్ ఈ వాటిలో ఒకదాని తాజా వెర్షన్ ఉపయోగించండి: బ్రౌజర్ ఇంజిన్ గమనికలు Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium అత్యంత పూర్తి: ఫోల్డర్ ఎంపిక, ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్, ఫైల్ హ్యాండ్లింగ్. Firefox Gecko పనిచేస్తుంది. మొత్తం ఫోల్డర్ తెరవడానికి సరళమైన పికర్ ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. ప్రైవేట్ విండోలలో ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సేవ్ పనిచేయకపోవచ్చు. Safari WebKit Mac మరియు iPad లో పనిచేస్తుంది. మొత్తం ఫోల్డర్ తెరవడానికి సరళమైన పికర్ ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. Safari కొన్ని వారాలుగా వాడని ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కాపీని తొలగించవచ్చు. మూడు ఇంజిన్‌లూ రోజువారీ పనికి బాగానే ఉంటాయి. ఒక ఇంజిన్‌లో యాప్ భిన్నంగా ఏదైనా చేసినప్పుడు, దాని సహాయం మీ బ్రౌజర్ కోసం దశలను చూపిస్తుంది. బ్రౌజర్‌ల మధ్య తేడా ఏమిటి ఫోల్డర్ తెరవడం. Chromium బ్రౌజర్‌లు నిజమైన ఫోల్డర్ పికర్ చూపిస్తాయి మరియు ఫోల్డర్‌ను నేరుగా చదవగలవు. Firefox మరియు Safari మీరు ఒక ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోమని అడిగి, అందులోని ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను చదువుతాయి; పెద్ద ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫోల్డర్‌లకు ఇంకా ఎక్కువ సమయం పడుతుంది. ఫోల్డర్‌ను యాప్‌పైకి లాగడం మూడింటిలోనూ పనిచేస్తుంది. సేవ్ చేయడం. యాప్‌లు ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయడం ద్వారా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి. డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయో, మరియు ముందుగా అడగాలా వద్దా అనేది మీ బ్రౌజర్ నిర్ణయిస్తుంది. Chrome లేదా Edge లో, డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు కింద ఉన్న సెట్టింగ్‌లు లో \"ప్రతి ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయడానికి ముందు ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో అడుగు\" ఆన్ చేయండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి. Firefox లో, సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, జనరల్, తర్వాత డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లు తెరిచి, \"ఫైల్‌లను ఎక్కడ సేవ్ చేయాలో ఎల్లప్పుడూ అడుగు\" ఎంచుకోండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి. Safari లో, సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరిచి, జనరల్ ఎంచుకుని, \"ఫైల్ డౌన్‌లోడ్ స్థానం\" ను \"ప్రతి డౌన్‌లోడ్‌కు అడుగు\" కు సెట్ చేయండి; ప్రతిసారీ ఫోల్డర్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి. Mac, Windows మరియు Linux యాప్‌లు ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో ఒకేలా ఉంటాయి. మారేది కీబోర్డ్: షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు మీ కంప్యూటర్ కోసం స్వయంచాలకంగా రాయబడతాయి. కీలు, మౌస్‌లు మరియు ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌లు చూడండి. టాబ్లెట్‌లు మరియు టచ్ స్క్రీన్‌లు కొన్ని యాప్‌లు టచ్‌ను అంగీకరిస్తాయి (ఒక వేలు గీస్తుంది లేదా ఎంచుకుంటుంది, రెండు వేళ్లు జూమ్ మరియు ప్యాన్ కోసం చిటికెడు). ప్రతి యాప్ సహాయం అది చేస్తుందో లేదో చెబుతుంది. iPad లో, Safari లేదా Chrome ఉపయోగించండి. టైప్ చేసిన కమాండ్‌లు ఉపయోగించే యాప్‌లకు కీబోర్డ్ సహాయపడుతుంది. అతి పాత బ్రౌజర్ మరియు సిస్టమ్ ఇవి 0.7 యాప్‌ల కోడ్ నుండి చదవబడ్డాయి, ఊహ నుండి కాదు: ప్రతి ఫైల్ విశ్లేషించబడింది మరియు అది ఉపయోగించే ప్రతి భాషా ఫీచర్ బ్రౌజర్‌ల ప్రచురించిన అనుకూలత డేటాలో వెతకబడింది. ఈ వెర్షన్‌ల కంటే తక్కువగా ఉంటే, బ్రౌజర్ కోడ్‌ను అర్థం చేసుకోనందున యాప్‌లు ప్రారంభం కూడా కావు. యాప్‌లు ప్రారంభం కాగల అతి పాత వెర్షన్ Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium 94 Firefox 93 Safari (Mac, iPad, iPhone) 16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS and iOS 16.4) CadCraft Studio Windows లో తన స్వంత వెబ్ ఇంజిన్‌ను తీసుకువెళ్తుంది, కాబట్టి దానికి Windows 10 లేదా 11 కావాలి; Mac లో అది సిస్టమ్‌దే ఉపయోగిస్తుంది, కాబట్టి macOS 13.3 లేదా అంతకంటే కొత్తది కావాలి. Studio లో సెట్టింగ్‌లు › అప్‌డేట్‌లు మీ కాపీ ఏ సిస్టమ్‌పై నడుస్తుందో చూపిస్తుంది. ఇది కోడ్ నుండి చదవబడిన, ప్రారంభమయ్యే అతి పాత వెర్షన్. ఇది మేము యాప్‌లను నడిపిన పాత బ్రౌజర్‌ల జాబితా కాదు, కాబట్టి ప్రారంభమయ్యే పాతది ఇంకా ఒక ఫీచర్‌ను కోల్పోవచ్చు. మీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఏమి కావాలి ఇవి యాప్‌ల ప్రణాళిక చేసిన కనీస అవసరాలు, కొలిచిన అవసరాలు కాదు: 8 GB మెమరీ (పెద్ద federated మోడల్స్ కోసం 16 GB సిఫార్సు చేయబడుతుంది). 3D యాప్‌ల కోసం WebGL 2 ఉన్న గ్రాఫిక్స్ చిప్. 3D వీక్షణ ఖాళీగా ఉంటే, మీ బ్రౌజర్ సెట్టింగ్‌లలో హార్డ్‌వేర్ యాక్సిలరేషన్ ఆన్‌లో ఉందో లేదో చూడండి. పూర్తి టూల్‌బార్ల కోసం కనీసం 1280 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు ఉన్న స్క్రీన్; చిన్న స్క్రీన్‌లు వాటిని వరుసలుగా మడతపెడతాయి. చాలా పెద్ద ఫైళ్లు (పెద్ద ప్లాన్ సెట్‌లు, పెద్ద మోడల్స్) బ్రౌజర్ ట్యాబ్‌లో చాలా మెమరీ వాడతాయి. యాప్ నెమ్మదిస్తే ఇతర భారీ ట్యాబ్‌లను మూసివేయండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"కీలు, మౌస్‌లు మరియు ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌లు","summary":"షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఎలా రాయబడతాయి, Mac లో ఫంక్షన్ కీలు, మౌస్ లేకుండా రైట్-క్లిక్ మరియు ప్యాన్ చేయడం.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["కీబోర్డ్","షార్ట్‌కట్","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","ఫంక్షన్ కీలు","ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్","రైట్ క్లిక్","మధ్య బటన్","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఎలా రాయబడతాయి","level":2},{"id":"mac","text":"Mac లో ఫంక్షన్ కీలు","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"బ్రౌజర్ ఉంచే షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై మౌస్ బటన్లు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"టెక్స్ట్ బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయడం","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eషార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఎలా రాయబడతాయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eసహాయం ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్‌ను మీ కీబోర్డ్ దానిని లేబుల్ చేసే విధంగా రాస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Eరాయబడింది\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMac లో\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWindows మరియు Linux లో\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) మరియు S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl మరియు S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) మరియు F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift మరియు F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) మరియు ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt మరియు ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete కీ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn మరియు Delete (ముందుకు delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్లస్ గుర్తు అంటే కీలను కలిసి పట్టుకోవాలి. &quot;తర్వాత&quot; అంటే ఒకదాని తర్వాత ఒకటి నొక్కాలి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eచాలా యాప్ షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు Mac లో Command లేదా Control ఏదైనా అంగీకరిస్తాయి, కాబట్టి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E రెండు విధాలుగా పనిచేస్తుంది. కొన్ని, ఉదాహరణకు పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో Control కీని ఉపయోగిస్తాయి; అది వర్తించే చోట సహాయం చెబుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac\"\u003EMac లో ఫంక్షన్ కీలు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eచాలా Mac కీబోర్డ్‌లలో పై వరుస ప్రకాశం, వాల్యూమ్ మరియు మీడియాను మారుస్తుంది. F1 నుండి F12 పంపడానికి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eకీని నొక్కుతూ \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (లేదా గ్లోబ్ కీ) పట్టుకోండి, లేదా\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E తెరవండి, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, మరియు &quot;F1, F2 మొదలైన కీలను ప్రామాణిక ఫంక్షన్ కీలుగా ఉపయోగించు&quot; ఆన్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకొన్ని F-కీలను macOS కూడా తనే వాడుతుంది (ఉదాహరణకు Mission Control). ఒక కీ యాప్‌లో ఏమీ చేయకపోతే, fn తో ప్రయత్నించండి, లేదా బదులుగా టూల్‌బార్ బటన్ ఉపయోగించండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ ఉంచే షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eవెబ్ పేజీ బ్రౌజర్ నుండి కొన్ని షార్ట్‌కట్‌లను తీసుకోలేదు, అది ఏమి చేసినా. ప్రతి యాప్‌లో:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ఎల్లప్పుడూ బ్రౌజర్ విండోలు మరియు ట్యాబ్‌లను తెరుస్తాయి లేదా మూసివేస్తాయి. యాప్‌లు కొత్త మరియు మూసివేత కోసం బటన్లు ఉపయోగిస్తాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMac లో \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు Windows లో \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E బ్రౌజర్‌ను నిష్క్రమిస్తాయి. సేవ్ చేయని పని ఉంటే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (&quot;సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?&quot;); ఉండటానికి ఎంచుకుని, తర్వాత సేవ్ చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ యొక్క స్వంత పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, లేదా విండోపై ఉన్న ఆకుపచ్చ బటన్\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) ఇంకా పనిచేస్తుంది. యాప్‌లకు కూడా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పై వాటి స్వంత స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు ఉన్నాయి, అవి యాప్ బార్‌లను కూడా దాచవచ్చు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై మౌస్ బటన్లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eయాప్‌లు కుడి బటన్ మరియు మధ్య బటన్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తాయి. మూడు-బటన్ మౌస్ లేకుండా:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరైట్-క్లిక్:\u003C/strong\u003E రెండు వేళ్లతో క్లిక్ చేయండి. రెండు వేళ్ల క్లిక్ ఏమీ చేయకపోతే \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E లో, తర్వాత \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E లో &quot;Secondary click&quot; ఆన్ చేయండి. క్లిక్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పట్టుకుంటే బ్రౌజర్ మెనూ తెరుచుకుంటుంది, కానీ డ్రాయింగ్ లోపల కొన్ని యాప్‌లు దానిని సాధారణ క్లిక్‌గా (Control పట్టుకుని) చదువుతాయి, కాబట్టి రెండు వేళ్ల క్లిక్‌నే ఇష్టపడండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమధ్య-బటన్ లాగడం (ప్యాన్):\u003C/strong\u003E ప్రతి యాప్ ప్యాన్ చేయడానికి మరో మార్గాన్ని అందిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు లాగుతున్నప్పుడు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పట్టుకోవడం లేదా కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగడం. యాప్ కీబోర్డ్ పేజీ ఏది అని చెబుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవీల్ జూమ్:\u003C/strong\u003E ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై రెండు వేళ్ల స్క్రోల్ చాలా యాప్‌లలో జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. యాప్ మద్దతిచ్చే చోట చిటికెడు కూడా జూమ్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరైట్-క్లిక్:\u003C/strong\u003E రెండు వేళ్లతో క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా టచ్‌ప్యాడ్ దిగువ కుడి మూలపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ఇది మీ టచ్‌ప్యాడ్ సెట్టింగ్‌లపై ఆధారపడి ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమధ్య-బటన్ లాగడం (ప్యాన్):\u003C/strong\u003E ప్రతి యాప్ ప్యాన్ చేయడానికి మరో మార్గాన్ని అందిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు లాగుతున్నప్పుడు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E పట్టుకోవడం లేదా కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగడం. యాప్ కీబోర్డ్ పేజీ ఏది అని చెబుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవీల్ జూమ్:\u003C/strong\u003E టచ్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై రెండు వేళ్ల స్క్రోల్ చాలా యాప్‌లలో జూమ్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eటెక్స్ట్ బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయడం\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు ఒక బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు (పేరు, నోట్, శోధన), యాప్ యొక్క ఒక-అక్షర షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఆఫ్ అవుతాయి, కాబట్టి &quot;L&quot; టైప్ చేస్తే L అక్షరమే వస్తుంది. కీలను యాప్‌కు తిరిగి ఇవ్వడానికి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి లేదా డ్రాయింగ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఎలా రాయబడతాయి సహాయం ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్‌ను మీ కీబోర్డ్ దానిని లేబుల్ చేసే విధంగా రాస్తుంది. రాయబడింది Mac లో Windows మరియు Linux లో Ctrl+S Command (⌘) మరియు S Ctrl మరియు S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) మరియు F Ctrl, Shift మరియు F Alt+← Option (⌥) మరియు ← Alt మరియు ← Backspace Delete కీ Backspace Delete fn మరియు Delete (ముందుకు delete) Delete Enter Return Enter ప్లస్ గుర్తు అంటే కీలను కలిసి పట్టుకోవాలి. \"తర్వాత\" అంటే ఒకదాని తర్వాత ఒకటి నొక్కాలి. చాలా యాప్ షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు Mac లో Command లేదా Control ఏదైనా అంగీకరిస్తాయి, కాబట్టి Ctrl+Z రెండు విధాలుగా పనిచేస్తుంది. కొన్ని, ఉదాహరణకు పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ (Ctrl+Shift+F), ప్రతి కంప్యూటర్‌లో Control కీని ఉపయోగిస్తాయి; అది వర్తించే చోట సహాయం చెబుతుంది. Mac లో ఫంక్షన్ కీలు చాలా Mac కీబోర్డ్‌లలో పై వరుస ప్రకాశం, వాల్యూమ్ మరియు మీడియాను మారుస్తుంది. F1 నుండి F12 పంపడానికి: కీని నొక్కుతూ fn (లేదా గ్లోబ్ కీ) పట్టుకోండి, లేదా System Settings తెరవండి, తర్వాత Keyboard, తర్వాత Keyboard Shortcuts, తర్వాత Function Keys, మరియు \"F1, F2 మొదలైన కీలను ప్రామాణిక ఫంక్షన్ కీలుగా ఉపయోగించు\" ఆన్ చేయండి. కొన్ని F-కీలను macOS కూడా తనే వాడుతుంది (ఉదాహరణకు Mission Control). ఒక కీ యాప్‌లో ఏమీ చేయకపోతే, fn తో ప్రయత్నించండి, లేదా బదులుగా టూల్‌బార్ బటన్ ఉపయోగించండి. బ్రౌజర్ ఉంచే షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు వెబ్ పేజీ బ్రౌజర్ నుండి కొన్ని షార్ట్‌కట్‌లను తీసుకోలేదు, అది ఏమి చేసినా. ప్రతి యాప్‌లో: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T మరియు Ctrl+W ఎల్లప్పుడూ బ్రౌజర్ విండోలు మరియు ట్యాబ్‌లను తెరుస్తాయి లేదా మూసివేస్తాయి. యాప్‌లు కొత్త మరియు మూసివేత కోసం బటన్లు ఉపయోగిస్తాయి. Mac లో Ctrl+Q మరియు Windows లో Alt+F4 బ్రౌజర్‌ను నిష్క్రమిస్తాయి. సేవ్ చేయని పని ఉంటే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (\"సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?\"); ఉండటానికి ఎంచుకుని, తర్వాత సేవ్ చేయండి. బ్రౌజర్ యొక్క స్వంత పూర్తి స్క్రీన్ (Control-Command-F, లేదా విండోపై ఉన్న ఆకుపచ్చ బటన్ / F11) ఇంకా పనిచేస్తుంది. యాప్‌లకు కూడా Ctrl+Shift+F పై వాటి స్వంత స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు ఉన్నాయి, అవి యాప్ బార్‌లను కూడా దాచవచ్చు. ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై మౌస్ బటన్లు యాప్‌లు కుడి బటన్ మరియు మధ్య బటన్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తాయి. మూడు-బటన్ మౌస్ లేకుండా: రైట్-క్లిక్: రెండు వేళ్లతో క్లిక్ చేయండి. రెండు వేళ్ల క్లిక్ ఏమీ చేయకపోతే System Settings లో, తర్వాత Trackpad లో \"Secondary click\" ఆన్ చేయండి. క్లిక్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు Ctrl పట్టుకుంటే బ్రౌజర్ మెనూ తెరుచుకుంటుంది, కానీ డ్రాయింగ్ లోపల కొన్ని యాప్‌లు దానిని సాధారణ క్లిక్‌గా (Control పట్టుకుని) చదువుతాయి, కాబట్టి రెండు వేళ్ల క్లిక్‌నే ఇష్టపడండి. మధ్య-బటన్ లాగడం (ప్యాన్): ప్రతి యాప్ ప్యాన్ చేయడానికి మరో మార్గాన్ని అందిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు లాగుతున్నప్పుడు Space పట్టుకోవడం లేదా కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగడం. యాప్ కీబోర్డ్ పేజీ ఏది అని చెబుతుంది. వీల్ జూమ్: ట్రాక్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై రెండు వేళ్ల స్క్రోల్ చాలా యాప్‌లలో జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. యాప్ మద్దతిచ్చే చోట చిటికెడు కూడా జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. రైట్-క్లిక్: రెండు వేళ్లతో క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా టచ్‌ప్యాడ్ దిగువ కుడి మూలపై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ఇది మీ టచ్‌ప్యాడ్ సెట్టింగ్‌లపై ఆధారపడి ఉంటుంది. మధ్య-బటన్ లాగడం (ప్యాన్): ప్రతి యాప్ ప్యాన్ చేయడానికి మరో మార్గాన్ని అందిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు లాగుతున్నప్పుడు Space పట్టుకోవడం లేదా కుడి బటన్‌తో లాగడం. యాప్ కీబోర్డ్ పేజీ ఏది అని చెబుతుంది. వీల్ జూమ్: టచ్‌ప్యాడ్‌పై రెండు వేళ్ల స్క్రోల్ చాలా యాప్‌లలో జూమ్ చేస్తుంది. టెక్స్ట్ బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయడం మీరు ఒక బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేస్తున్నప్పుడు (పేరు, నోట్, శోధన), యాప్ యొక్క ఒక-అక్షర షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు ఆఫ్ అవుతాయి, కాబట్టి \"L\" టైప్ చేస్తే L అక్షరమే వస్తుంది. కీలను యాప్‌కు తిరిగి ఇవ్వడానికి Esc నొక్కండి లేదా డ్రాయింగ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"సాధనం లేదా సెట్టింగ్‌ను కనుగొను","summary":"ఏ సాధనాన్నైనా నడపడానికి, సెట్టింగ్‌ను మార్చడానికి లేదా సహాయ టాపిక్‌ను తెరవడానికి Ctrl+K నొక్కి కొన్ని అక్షరాలు టైప్ చేయండి.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["కనుగొను","వెతుకు","కమాండ్ పాలెట్","త్వరిత తెరువు","కమాండ్ నడుపు","ctrl k","cmd k","సాధనం","సెట్టింగ్","షార్ట్‌కట్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"కనుగొని నడుపు","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"తెలుసుకోవలసినవి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి యాప్‌కు దాని సాధనాలు, దాని సెట్టింగ్‌లు మరియు ఈ సహాయం కోసం ఒక శోధన బాక్స్ ఉంటుంది. సాధనం టూల్‌బార్‌లో ఎక్కడ ఉందో మీకు తెలియాల్సిన అవసరం లేదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eకనుగొని నడుపు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా పైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవెతుకు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీకు కావలసిన దానిలో కొన్ని అక్షరాలు టైప్ చేయండి: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. మీరు టైప్ చేసే ప్రతి పదం సరిపోవాలి, కాబట్టి జాబితాను తగ్గించడానికి రెండవ పదం జోడించండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమొదటి సరిపోలికను నడపడానికి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, లేదా ముందు బాణం కీలతో కదపండి. బాక్స్‌ను మూసివేయడానికి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eసాధనం టూల్‌బార్‌లో క్లిక్ చేసినట్లే నడుస్తుంది. ఒక సెట్టింగ్ ఆ సెట్టింగ్ వద్ద \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E ను తెరుస్తుంది. సహాయ టాపిక్ ఇక్కడ, సహాయ ప్యానెల్‌లో తెరుచుకుంటుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eతెలుసుకోవలసినవి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eచివరిసారి మీరు ఉపయోగించినది తదుపరిసారి మొదట వస్తుంది, కాబట్టి మీరు తరచుగా వాడే సాధనం ఒక కీ నొక్కు దూరంలో ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనాలు, ఉదాహరణకు ఫైల్ తెరవకముందు, జాబితాలో ఉంటాయి కానీ బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eయాడ్-ఆన్ ఆన్ చేసిన తర్వాత, దాని సాధనాలు కూడా జాబితాలో ఉంటాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"ప్రతి యాప్‌కు దాని సాధనాలు, దాని సెట్టింగ్‌లు మరియు ఈ సహాయం కోసం ఒక శోధన బాక్స్ ఉంటుంది. సాధనం టూల్‌బార్‌లో ఎక్కడ ఉందో మీకు తెలియాల్సిన అవసరం లేదు. కనుగొని నడుపు Ctrl+K నొక్కండి, లేదా పైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో వెతుకు ఎంచుకోండి. మీకు కావలసిన దానిలో కొన్ని అక్షరాలు టైప్ చేయండి: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". మీరు టైప్ చేసే ప్రతి పదం సరిపోవాలి, కాబట్టి జాబితాను తగ్గించడానికి రెండవ పదం జోడించండి. మొదటి సరిపోలికను నడపడానికి Enter నొక్కండి, లేదా ముందు బాణం కీలతో కదపండి. బాక్స్‌ను మూసివేయడానికి Esc. సాధనం టూల్‌బార్‌లో క్లిక్ చేసినట్లే నడుస్తుంది. ఒక సెట్టింగ్ ఆ సెట్టింగ్ వద్ద సెట్టింగ్‌లు ను తెరుస్తుంది. సహాయ టాపిక్ ఇక్కడ, సహాయ ప్యానెల్‌లో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. తెలుసుకోవలసినవి చివరిసారి మీరు ఉపయోగించినది తదుపరిసారి మొదట వస్తుంది, కాబట్టి మీరు తరచుగా వాడే సాధనం ఒక కీ నొక్కు దూరంలో ఉంటుంది. ఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనాలు, ఉదాహరణకు ఫైల్ తెరవకముందు, జాబితాలో ఉంటాయి కానీ బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటాయి. యాడ్-ఆన్ ఆన్ చేసిన తర్వాత, దాని సాధనాలు కూడా జాబితాలో ఉంటాయి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"ప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు మరియు రెండవ మానిటర్‌ను ఉపయోగించు","summary":"సైడ్ ప్యానెళ్లను ఎడమ, కుడి లేదా కింద డాక్ చేయి, వాటిని పేర్చు, ట్యాబ్‌లుగా సమూహపరచు, పిన్ చేయి లేదా దాచు, డ్రాయింగ్ పైన తేలియాడించు, లేఅవుట్‌ను మరింత దట్టంగా చేయి, టూల్‌బార్ ఎన్ని వరుసలు వాడాలో ఎంచుకో, లేదా మరో స్క్రీన్‌లో వాటి స్వంత విండోకు తరలించు.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["ప్యానెళ్లు","డాక్","తేలియాడు","ప్యానెల్ తరలించు","లాగు","లేఅవుట్","ట్యాబ్‌లు","ట్యాబ్ సమూహం","పేర్చు","పిన్","ఆటో-దాచు","ప్యానెల్ దాచు","ప్యానెల్ మూసివేయి","కాంపాక్ట్","లేఅవుట్ సాంద్రత","దట్టం","టూల్‌బార్ వరుసలు","ర్యాప్","1 వరుస","2 వరుసలు","రెండవ మానిటర్","రెండు స్క్రీన్‌లు","డ్యూయల్ మానిటర్","బహుళ మానిటర్లు","విండో","పాప్-అప్","లేఅవుట్ రీసెట్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"విండోలో ప్యానెల్‌ను తరలించు","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ప్యానెళ్లను సమూహపరచు","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"పిన్ చేయి, దాచు మరియు తిరిగి తీసుకురా","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"లేఅవుట్‌ను మరింత దట్టంగా చేయి","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"టూల్‌బార్ ఎన్ని వరుసలు వాడాలో ఎంచుకో","level":2},{"id":"section-6","text":"కీబోర్డ్ ఉపయోగించు","level":2},{"id":"section-7","text":"రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై ప్యానెళ్లను ఉంచు","level":2},{"id":"section-8","text":"ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువ ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలు","level":2},{"id":"section-9","text":"డైలాగ్‌ను తరలించు","level":2},{"id":"section-10","text":"ప్యానెళ్లను తిరిగి ఉంచు","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్ పక్కన ఉన్న ప్యానెళ్లను కదపవచ్చు. వాటిని మీకు నచ్చిన చోట ఉంచండి, లేదా రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై వాటికి వాటి స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వండి, తద్వారా డ్రాయింగ్‌కు మొదటి మానిటర్ మొత్తం దక్కుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు టైటిల్ బార్ ఉంటుంది: ప్యానెల్ పేరు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూ, పిన్ మరియు మూసివేత బటన్ ఉన్న సన్నని బార్, ప్యానెల్ పైభాగంలో. 0.7 కంటే ముందు వెర్షన్‌లలో పేరు మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూ మాత్రమే ఉంటాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eవిండోలో ప్యానెల్‌ను తరలించు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పైన ఉన్న పేరు ద్వారా లాగండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eలాగుతున్నప్పుడు, డ్రాయింగ్ యొక్క ఎడమ, కుడి మరియు కింది భాగాల్లో రూపురేఖలు గల ప్రాంతాలు కనిపిస్తాయి. ప్యానెల్‌ను వాటిలో ఒకదానిపై వదిలితే అక్కడ డాక్ అవుతుంది. మీరు ఉన్న ప్రాంతం ముదురుగా మారుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదానిని డ్రాయింగ్‌పై మరెక్కడైనా వదిలితే అది తేలియాడుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెళ్లు ఒకే వైపును పంచుకోవచ్చు: అవి పేర్చబడతాయి, మరియు వాటి మధ్య ఉన్న ప్రతి గీత స్థలాన్ని పంచుకోవడానికి లాగవచ్చు (గీత ఫోకస్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, బాణం కీలు దాన్ని కదుపుతాయి, దానిపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే స్థలం సమానంగా పంచబడుతుంది). డాక్ చేసిన వైపు లోపలి అంచును లాగితే అది వెడల్పుగా లేదా ఇరుకుగా మారుతుంది; అంచుపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే అది తిరిగి ఉంచబడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eతేలియాడే ప్యానెల్ డ్రాయింగ్ పైనే ఉంటుంది. దాన్ని దాని పేరు ద్వారా కదపండి, మరియు దిగువ కుడి మూలను లాగి పరిమాణం మార్చండి (లేదా మూలకు ట్యాబ్ చేసి బాణం కీలు ఉపయోగించండి). దానిని తిరిగి డాక్ చేయడానికి దాని పేరుపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి; అది ఉన్న చోటే డాక్ అవుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెల్ పేరుపై ఒక వేలితో లాగండి. తేలియాడే ప్యానెల్ దిగువ కుడి మూలను ఒక వేలితో లాగితే దాని పరిమాణం మారుతుంది; డాక్ చేసిన ప్యానెళ్ల మధ్య అంచులు కూడా అదే విధంగా. లాగకుండా తేలియాడించడానికి లేదా డాక్ చేయడానికి, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూ ఉపయోగించండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eట్యాబ్‌లుగా ప్యానెళ్లను సమూహపరచు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరెండు ప్యానెళ్లు ఒకే ఫ్రేమ్‌ను పంచుకుని ఒక్కోసారి ఒకటి చూపగలవు. ఒక ప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పేరు ద్వారా మరో ప్యానెల్ టైటిల్ బార్‌పైకి లాగి (మీరు పైన ఉన్నప్పుడు బార్ రూపురేఖలు కనిపిస్తాయి) వదలండి: అప్పుడు ఫ్రేమ్‌కు దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌లో ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు ఒక ట్యాబ్ వస్తుంది. ఆ ప్యానెల్‌ను చూపడానికి ట్యాబ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ట్యాబ్ ఫోకస్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ట్యాబ్‌లను మారుస్తాయి, మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మొదటి మరియు చివరి ట్యాబ్‌కు వెళ్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెల్‌ను సమూహం నుండి బయటకు తీయడానికి, దాని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eట్యాబ్ సమూహం నుండి బయటకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా దానిని మరెక్కడైనా లాగండి. లాగకుండా సమూహపరచడానికి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eట్యాబ్‌తో కలుపు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు ప్యానెల్ పేరును ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eపిన్ చేయి, దాచు మరియు తిరిగి తీసుకురా\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్యానెల్ టైటిల్ బార్‌లోని పిన్ దానిని \u003Cstrong\u003Eఆటో-దాచు\u003C/strong\u003E చేస్తుంది: ప్యానెల్ దాని డాక్ నుండి వెళ్లి ఆ అంచున ఒక చిన్న ట్యాబ్‌గా మారుతుంది. ట్యాబ్‌పై పాయింటర్ ఉంచండి, ఫోకస్ చేయండి లేదా క్లిక్ చేయండి, ప్యానెల్ డ్రాయింగ్ పైకి జారి వస్తుంది; దూరంగా వెళ్లండి (లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి), అది తిరిగి జారిపోతుంది. దానిని శాశ్వతంగా డాక్ చేయడానికి పిన్‌ను మళ్లీ నొక్కండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eపిన్ తీసివేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమూసివేత బటన్, లేదా మెనూలోని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఈ ప్యానెల్‌ను దాచు\u003C/span\u003E, ప్యానెల్‌ను దాస్తుంది మరియు దాని డాక్‌ను మూసివేస్తుంది. అంచున ఉన్న డాష్ గీతల ట్యాబ్ దానిని తిరిగి తెస్తుంది: దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఫోకస్ చేసి \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్‌ను మరింత దట్టంగా చేయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసాధారణం\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ సాంద్రత\u003C/span\u003E లో రెండు ఎంపికలు ఉన్నాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసౌకర్యవంతం\u003C/span\u003E డిఫాల్ట్. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకాంపాక్ట్\u003C/span\u003E ఆ యాప్ యొక్క వరుసల ఎత్తు, ప్యానెల్ పాడింగ్, టూల్‌బార్ బటన్లు మరియు ప్యానెల్ టెక్స్ట్‌ను బిగిస్తుంది, తద్వారా స్క్రీన్‌పై ఎక్కువ సరిపోతుంది. ఇది ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడుతుంది. టచ్ స్క్రీన్ రెండింటిలోనూ పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతుంది, ఫోన్ తన స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E మీరు సెట్ చేసిన సాంద్రతను మార్చదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eటూల్‌బార్ ఎన్ని వరుసలు వాడాలో ఎంచుకో\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసాధారణం\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బార్ వరుసలు\u003C/span\u003E లో మూడు ఎంపికలు ఉన్నాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eర్యాప్\u003C/span\u003E డిఫాల్ట్: ప్రతి టూల్ గ్రూప్ కనిపిస్తుంది, ఎన్ని వరుసలు పట్టినా. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 వరుసలు\u003C/span\u003E టూల్‌బార్‌ను రెండు వరుసలకు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E1 వరుస\u003C/span\u003E ఒక వరుసకు పరిమితం చేస్తుంది, తద్వారా డ్రాయింగ్‌కు ఎత్తు దక్కుతుంది; సరిపోని గ్రూపులు టూల్‌బార్ లోపల పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అవుతాయి (మౌస్ వీల్, రెండు వేళ్ల స్వైప్, లేదా ఒక టూల్‌కు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). రెండు వరుసల్లో ఇప్పటికే సరిపోయే టూల్‌బార్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 వరుసలు\u003C/span\u003E కింద అలాగే ఉంటుంది, ఎందుకంటే గ్రిడ్ దానిని మరింత ఎత్తుగా మాత్రమే చేస్తుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడుతుంది. టచ్ స్క్రీన్ మరియు ఫోన్ తమ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉంచుతాయి. CadCraft కు టూల్‌బార్ కోసం దాని స్వంత సెట్టింగ్ ఉంది, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E కింద.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eకీబోర్డ్ ఉపయోగించు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి ప్యానెల్ యొక్క \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E బటన్ ఒక మెనూ తెరుస్తుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎడమవైపు డాక్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకుడివైపు డాక్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకింద డాక్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్ పైన తేలియాడించు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅంచుకు పిన్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఈ ప్యానెల్‌ను దాచు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eట్యాబ్‌తో కలుపు\u003C/span\u003E మరో ప్యానెల్, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతిరిగి ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E. బటన్‌కు ట్యాబ్ చేయండి, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, బాణం కీలతో కదపండి మరియు మళ్లీ \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మెనూను మూసివేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-7\"\u003Eరెండవ మానిటర్‌పై ప్యానెళ్లను ఉంచు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఒక ప్యానెల్‌పై \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఅన్ని ప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E కూడా ఎంచుకోవచ్చు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదానిలో ప్యానెళ్లతో ఒక చిన్న విండో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. దానిని మీ రెండవ మానిటర్‌కు లాగి, మీకు కావలసినంత పెద్దదిగా చేయండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపనిని కొనసాగించండి: ప్యానెళ్లు ముందులాగే డ్రాయింగ్‌పై పనిచేస్తాయి, మరియు డ్రాయింగ్ ప్రధాన విండోను నింపుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెళ్ల విండో ముందు ఉన్నప్పుడు షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు పనిచేస్తూనే ఉంటాయి: అక్కడ మీరు నొక్కే కీలు, ఒక బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయడం కాకుండా, డ్రాయింగ్‌కు వెళ్తాయి. యాప్ అడిగే ప్రశ్నలు మరియు డైలాగ్‌లు ఇంకా ప్రధాన విండోలోనే తెరుచుకుంటాయి, కాబట్టి ఒకటి కనిపించినప్పుడు అక్కడ చూడండి. థీమ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ టెక్స్ట్ పరిమాణం ప్రధాన విండోను అనుసరిస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome మరియు Edge స్వయంగా విండోను మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై ఉంచగలవు. రెండవ స్క్రీన్ కనెక్ట్ అయి ఉన్నప్పుడు, ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనా మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి (లేదా ప్యానెల్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను నా మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై పెట్టు\u003C/span\u003E). మీ స్క్రీన్‌లను చూడటానికి సైట్‌కు అనుమతి ఉందో లేదో బ్రౌజర్ ఒకసారి అడుగుతుంది; మీరు అనుమతించిన తర్వాత, ప్యానెళ్ల విండో ప్రతిసారీ ఆ స్క్రీన్‌పై తెరుచుకుంటుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెళ్ల విండోను మరో మానిటర్‌కు మీరే లాగండి. తదుపరిసారి అది అదే స్థానంలో మరియు అదే పరిమాణంలో తెరుచుకుంటుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-8\"\u003Eఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువ ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు దాని స్వంత విండో ఉండవచ్చు: గరిష్ఠంగా నాలుగు ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలు, ఉదాహరణకు ప్రతి మానిటర్‌కు ఒకటి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్యానెల్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E మెనూలో, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E ఆ ప్యానెల్ కోసం మరో విండో తెరుస్తుంది, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్ల విండో 2కు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E (మరియు అలా) దానిని ఇప్పటికే తెరిచి ఉన్న ఒక విండోలోకి తరలిస్తుంది. రెండవ విండోను &quot;panels 2&quot; అంటారు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఒక విండో నుండి మరో విండోకు ప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పేరు ద్వారా లాగండి: ప్రధాన విండోపై వదిలితే అక్కడ డాక్ అవుతుంది (మీరు పైన ఉన్నప్పుడు రూపురేఖలు కనిపిస్తాయి) లేదా తేలియాడుతుంది, మరియు మరో ప్యానెళ్ల విండోపై వదిలితే అందులోకి వెళ్తుంది. ఎక్కడైనా మిగతా చోట వదిలితే అది ఉన్న చోటే ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఇక ప్యానెళ్లు లేని ప్యానెళ్ల విండో దానంతట అదే మూసుకుపోతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరీలోడ్ తర్వాత, యాప్ మీ ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలను మళ్లీ అందిస్తుంది. బ్రౌజర్‌లు ఒక క్లిక్‌కు ఒక విండో తెరుస్తాయి, కాబట్టి ప్రతి విండోకు ఒకసారి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతదుపరిది తెరువు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-9\"\u003Eడైలాగ్‌ను తరలించు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి డైలాగ్‌ను డ్రాయింగ్‌కు అడ్డు తొలగించడానికి కదపవచ్చు: దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌ను నొక్కి లాగండి. డైలాగ్ మీరు వదిలిన చోటును గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది, ప్రతి డైలాగ్ విడిగా, మరియు అది తదుపరిసారి తెరిచినప్పుడు ఆ చోటుకే తిరిగి వస్తుంది. మళ్లీ మధ్యలో ఉంచడానికి టైటిల్ బార్‌పై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eడైలాగ్ లోపల ఫోకస్ ఉన్నప్పుడు కీబోర్డ్ నుండి, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+ఒక బాణం కీ నొక్కండి, అది ఒకేసారి 16 పిక్సెల్స్ కదులుతుంది; 64 కోసం \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E కలపండి. చిన్న విండో లేదా స్క్రీన్ తర్వాత స్క్రీన్ నుండి బయట పడిన డైలాగ్ తిరిగి తీసుకురాబడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఫోన్‌పై, డైలాగ్‌లు పూర్తి-వెడల్పు షీట్‌లుగా ఉంటాయి మరియు కదలవు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft Studio లో, CadCraft యొక్క \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF కు ప్లాట్\u003C/span\u003E డైలాగ్ (మీరు పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు తెరిచి ఉంచవచ్చు) దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌లో ఒక బటన్ కలిగి ఉంటుంది: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదాని స్వంత విండోలో తెరువు\u003C/span\u003E. డైలాగ్ తన స్వంత విండోలోకి మారుతుంది, దానిని మరో మానిటర్‌పై ఉంచవచ్చు. డైలాగ్‌ను మూసివేస్తే విండో మూసివేయబడుతుంది, విండోను మూసివేస్తే డైలాగ్ మూసివేయబడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-10\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను తిరిగి ఉంచు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్యానెళ్ల విండోలో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను తిరిగి ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా దానిని మూసివేయండి. ప్యానెళ్లు ఎక్కడ ఉండేవో అక్కడికి తిరిగి వెళ్తాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eప్రధాన విండోను మూసివేయడం లేదా రీలోడ్ చేయడం ప్యానెళ్ల విండోను కూడా మూసివేస్తుంది. యాప్ మళ్లీ తెరిచినప్పుడు మీ ప్యానెళ్లు తమ స్వంత విండోలో ఉన్నాయని చెబుతుంది; ఆ విండోను తిరిగి తీసుకురావడానికి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదాన్ని తెరువు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఇక్కడే ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసాధారణం\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌ను అది మొదట ప్రారంభమైన చోటుకు తిరిగి ఉంచుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించినప్పుడు ఏదీ తెరుచుకోకపోతే, బ్రౌజర్ పాప్-అప్‌ను అడ్డుకుంది. యాప్ అది చెబుతుంది మరియు ప్యానెళ్లను ఉన్న చోటే ఉంచుతుంది. getcadcraft.com కోసం పాప్-అప్‌లను అనుమతించండి, తర్వాత మళ్లీ ప్రయత్నించండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఈ లేఅవుట్ ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లో ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా, మీ ఇతర సెట్టింగ్‌లతో పాటు ఉంచబడుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"డ్రాయింగ్ పక్కన ఉన్న ప్యానెళ్లను కదపవచ్చు. వాటిని మీకు నచ్చిన చోట ఉంచండి, లేదా రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై వాటికి వాటి స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వండి, తద్వారా డ్రాయింగ్‌కు మొదటి మానిటర్ మొత్తం దక్కుతుంది. ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు టైటిల్ బార్ ఉంటుంది: ప్యానెల్ పేరు, ⋯ మెనూ, పిన్ మరియు మూసివేత బటన్ ఉన్న సన్నని బార్, ప్యానెల్ పైభాగంలో. 0.7 కంటే ముందు వెర్షన్‌లలో పేరు మరియు ⋯ మెనూ మాత్రమే ఉంటాయి. విండోలో ప్యానెల్‌ను తరలించు ప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పైన ఉన్న పేరు ద్వారా లాగండి. లాగుతున్నప్పుడు, డ్రాయింగ్ యొక్క ఎడమ, కుడి మరియు కింది భాగాల్లో రూపురేఖలు గల ప్రాంతాలు కనిపిస్తాయి. ప్యానెల్‌ను వాటిలో ఒకదానిపై వదిలితే అక్కడ డాక్ అవుతుంది. మీరు ఉన్న ప్రాంతం ముదురుగా మారుతుంది. దానిని డ్రాయింగ్‌పై మరెక్కడైనా వదిలితే అది తేలియాడుతుంది. ప్యానెళ్లు ఒకే వైపును పంచుకోవచ్చు: అవి పేర్చబడతాయి, మరియు వాటి మధ్య ఉన్న ప్రతి గీత స్థలాన్ని పంచుకోవడానికి లాగవచ్చు (గీత ఫోకస్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, బాణం కీలు దాన్ని కదుపుతాయి, దానిపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే స్థలం సమానంగా పంచబడుతుంది). డాక్ చేసిన వైపు లోపలి అంచును లాగితే అది వెడల్పుగా లేదా ఇరుకుగా మారుతుంది; అంచుపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే అది తిరిగి ఉంచబడుతుంది. తేలియాడే ప్యానెల్ డ్రాయింగ్ పైనే ఉంటుంది. దాన్ని దాని పేరు ద్వారా కదపండి, మరియు దిగువ కుడి మూలను లాగి పరిమాణం మార్చండి (లేదా మూలకు ట్యాబ్ చేసి బాణం కీలు ఉపయోగించండి). దానిని తిరిగి డాక్ చేయడానికి దాని పేరుపై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి; అది ఉన్న చోటే డాక్ అవుతుంది. ప్యానెల్ పేరుపై ఒక వేలితో లాగండి. తేలియాడే ప్యానెల్ దిగువ కుడి మూలను ఒక వేలితో లాగితే దాని పరిమాణం మారుతుంది; డాక్ చేసిన ప్యానెళ్ల మధ్య అంచులు కూడా అదే విధంగా. లాగకుండా తేలియాడించడానికి లేదా డాక్ చేయడానికి, ⋯ మెనూ ఉపయోగించండి. ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ప్యానెళ్లను సమూహపరచు రెండు ప్యానెళ్లు ఒకే ఫ్రేమ్‌ను పంచుకుని ఒక్కోసారి ఒకటి చూపగలవు. ఒక ప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పేరు ద్వారా మరో ప్యానెల్ టైటిల్ బార్‌పైకి లాగి (మీరు పైన ఉన్నప్పుడు బార్ రూపురేఖలు కనిపిస్తాయి) వదలండి: అప్పుడు ఫ్రేమ్‌కు దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌లో ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు ఒక ట్యాబ్ వస్తుంది. ఆ ప్యానెల్‌ను చూపడానికి ట్యాబ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి. ట్యాబ్ ఫోకస్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, ← మరియు → ట్యాబ్‌లను మారుస్తాయి, మరియు Home మరియు End మొదటి మరియు చివరి ట్యాబ్‌కు వెళ్తాయి. ప్యానెల్‌ను సమూహం నుండి బయటకు తీయడానికి, దాని ⋯ మెనూలో ట్యాబ్ సమూహం నుండి బయటకు తరలించు ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా దానిని మరెక్కడైనా లాగండి. లాగకుండా సమూహపరచడానికి ట్యాబ్‌తో కలుపు మరియు ప్యానెల్ పేరును ఎంచుకోండి. పిన్ చేయి, దాచు మరియు తిరిగి తీసుకురా ప్యానెల్ టైటిల్ బార్‌లోని పిన్ దానిని ఆటో-దాచు చేస్తుంది: ప్యానెల్ దాని డాక్ నుండి వెళ్లి ఆ అంచున ఒక చిన్న ట్యాబ్‌గా మారుతుంది. ట్యాబ్‌పై పాయింటర్ ఉంచండి, ఫోకస్ చేయండి లేదా క్లిక్ చేయండి, ప్యానెల్ డ్రాయింగ్ పైకి జారి వస్తుంది; దూరంగా వెళ్లండి (లేదా Esc నొక్కండి), అది తిరిగి జారిపోతుంది. దానిని శాశ్వతంగా డాక్ చేయడానికి పిన్‌ను మళ్లీ నొక్కండి, లేదా పిన్ తీసివేయి ఎంచుకోండి. మూసివేత బటన్, లేదా మెనూలోని ఈ ప్యానెల్‌ను దాచు, ప్యానెల్‌ను దాస్తుంది మరియు దాని డాక్‌ను మూసివేస్తుంది. అంచున ఉన్న డాష్ గీతల ట్యాబ్ దానిని తిరిగి తెస్తుంది: దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా ఫోకస్ చేసి Enter నొక్కండి. లేఅవుట్‌ను మరింత దట్టంగా చేయి సెట్టింగ్‌లు › సాధారణం › లేఅవుట్ సాంద్రత లో రెండు ఎంపికలు ఉన్నాయి. సౌకర్యవంతం డిఫాల్ట్. కాంపాక్ట్ ఆ యాప్ యొక్క వరుసల ఎత్తు, ప్యానెల్ పాడింగ్, టూల్‌బార్ బటన్లు మరియు ప్యానెల్ టెక్స్ట్‌ను బిగిస్తుంది, తద్వారా స్క్రీన్‌పై ఎక్కువ సరిపోతుంది. ఇది ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడుతుంది. టచ్ స్క్రీన్ రెండింటిలోనూ పెద్ద బటన్లను ఉంచుతుంది, ఫోన్ తన స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉంచుతుంది. లేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి మీరు సెట్ చేసిన సాంద్రతను మార్చదు. టూల్‌బార్ ఎన్ని వరుసలు వాడాలో ఎంచుకో సెట్టింగ్‌లు › సాధారణం › టూల్‌బార్ వరుసలు లో మూడు ఎంపికలు ఉన్నాయి. ర్యాప్ డిఫాల్ట్: ప్రతి టూల్ గ్రూప్ కనిపిస్తుంది, ఎన్ని వరుసలు పట్టినా. 2 వరుసలు టూల్‌బార్‌ను రెండు వరుసలకు, 1 వరుస ఒక వరుసకు పరిమితం చేస్తుంది, తద్వారా డ్రాయింగ్‌కు ఎత్తు దక్కుతుంది; సరిపోని గ్రూపులు టూల్‌బార్ లోపల పక్కకు స్క్రోల్ అవుతాయి (మౌస్ వీల్, రెండు వేళ్ల స్వైప్, లేదా ఒక టూల్‌కు Tab). రెండు వరుసల్లో ఇప్పటికే సరిపోయే టూల్‌బార్ 2 వరుసలు కింద అలాగే ఉంటుంది, ఎందుకంటే గ్రిడ్ దానిని మరింత ఎత్తుగా మాత్రమే చేస్తుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడుతుంది. టచ్ స్క్రీన్ మరియు ఫోన్ తమ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉంచుతాయి. CadCraft కు టూల్‌బార్ కోసం దాని స్వంత సెట్టింగ్ ఉంది, సెట్టింగ్‌లు › CadCraft కింద. కీబోర్డ్ ఉపయోగించు ప్రతి ప్యానెల్ యొక్క ⋯ బటన్ ఒక మెనూ తెరుస్తుంది: ఎడమవైపు డాక్ చేయి, కుడివైపు డాక్ చేయి, కింద డాక్ చేయి, డ్రాయింగ్ పైన తేలియాడించు, అంచుకు పిన్ చేయి, ఈ ప్యానెల్‌ను దాచు, ట్యాబ్‌తో కలుపు మరో ప్యానెల్, కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు మరియు తిరిగి ఉంచు. బటన్‌కు ట్యాబ్ చేయండి, Enter నొక్కండి, బాణం కీలతో కదపండి మరియు మళ్లీ Enter నొక్కండి. Esc మెనూను మూసివేస్తుంది. రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై ప్యానెళ్లను ఉంచు ఒక ప్యానెల్‌పై ⋯ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత అన్ని ప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు. సెట్టింగ్‌లు లో ప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు కూడా ఎంచుకోవచ్చు. దానిలో ప్యానెళ్లతో ఒక చిన్న విండో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. దానిని మీ రెండవ మానిటర్‌కు లాగి, మీకు కావలసినంత పెద్దదిగా చేయండి. పనిని కొనసాగించండి: ప్యానెళ్లు ముందులాగే డ్రాయింగ్‌పై పనిచేస్తాయి, మరియు డ్రాయింగ్ ప్రధాన విండోను నింపుతుంది. ప్యానెళ్ల విండో ముందు ఉన్నప్పుడు షార్ట్‌కట్‌లు పనిచేస్తూనే ఉంటాయి: అక్కడ మీరు నొక్కే కీలు, ఒక బాక్స్‌లో టైప్ చేయడం కాకుండా, డ్రాయింగ్‌కు వెళ్తాయి. యాప్ అడిగే ప్రశ్నలు మరియు డైలాగ్‌లు ఇంకా ప్రధాన విండోలోనే తెరుచుకుంటాయి, కాబట్టి ఒకటి కనిపించినప్పుడు అక్కడ చూడండి. థీమ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ టెక్స్ట్ పరిమాణం ప్రధాన విండోను అనుసరిస్తాయి. Chrome మరియు Edge స్వయంగా విండోను మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై ఉంచగలవు. రెండవ స్క్రీన్ కనెక్ట్ అయి ఉన్నప్పుడు, ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలో నా మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై ఉంచు ఎంచుకోండి (లేదా ప్యానెల్ ⋯ మెనూలో ప్యానెళ్లను నా మరో స్క్రీన్‌పై పెట్టు). మీ స్క్రీన్‌లను చూడటానికి సైట్‌కు అనుమతి ఉందో లేదో బ్రౌజర్ ఒకసారి అడుగుతుంది; మీరు అనుమతించిన తర్వాత, ప్యానెళ్ల విండో ప్రతిసారీ ఆ స్క్రీన్‌పై తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ప్యానెళ్ల విండోను మరో మానిటర్‌కు మీరే లాగండి. తదుపరిసారి అది అదే స్థానంలో మరియు అదే పరిమాణంలో తెరుచుకుంటుంది. ఒకటి కంటే ఎక్కువ ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలు ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌కు దాని స్వంత విండో ఉండవచ్చు: గరిష్ఠంగా నాలుగు ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలు, ఉదాహరణకు ప్రతి మానిటర్‌కు ఒకటి. ప్యానెల్ ⋯ మెనూలో, కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు ఆ ప్యానెల్ కోసం మరో విండో తెరుస్తుంది, మరియు ప్యానెళ్ల విండో 2కు తరలించు (మరియు అలా) దానిని ఇప్పటికే తెరిచి ఉన్న ఒక విండోలోకి తరలిస్తుంది. రెండవ విండోను \"panels 2\" అంటారు. ఒక విండో నుండి మరో విండోకు ప్యానెల్‌ను దాని పేరు ద్వారా లాగండి: ప్రధాన విండోపై వదిలితే అక్కడ డాక్ అవుతుంది (మీరు పైన ఉన్నప్పుడు రూపురేఖలు కనిపిస్తాయి) లేదా తేలియాడుతుంది, మరియు మరో ప్యానెళ్ల విండోపై వదిలితే అందులోకి వెళ్తుంది. ఎక్కడైనా మిగతా చోట వదిలితే అది ఉన్న చోటే ఉంటుంది. ఇక ప్యానెళ్లు లేని ప్యానెళ్ల విండో దానంతట అదే మూసుకుపోతుంది. రీలోడ్ తర్వాత, యాప్ మీ ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలను మళ్లీ అందిస్తుంది. బ్రౌజర్‌లు ఒక క్లిక్‌కు ఒక విండో తెరుస్తాయి, కాబట్టి ప్రతి విండోకు ఒకసారి తదుపరిది తెరువు ఎంచుకోండి. డైలాగ్‌ను తరలించు ప్రతి డైలాగ్‌ను డ్రాయింగ్‌కు అడ్డు తొలగించడానికి కదపవచ్చు: దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌ను నొక్కి లాగండి. డైలాగ్ మీరు వదిలిన చోటును గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది, ప్రతి డైలాగ్ విడిగా, మరియు అది తదుపరిసారి తెరిచినప్పుడు ఆ చోటుకే తిరిగి వస్తుంది. మళ్లీ మధ్యలో ఉంచడానికి టైటిల్ బార్‌పై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. డైలాగ్ లోపల ఫోకస్ ఉన్నప్పుడు కీబోర్డ్ నుండి, Alt+Shift+ఒక బాణం కీ నొక్కండి, అది ఒకేసారి 16 పిక్సెల్స్ కదులుతుంది; 64 కోసం Ctrl కలపండి. చిన్న విండో లేదా స్క్రీన్ తర్వాత స్క్రీన్ నుండి బయట పడిన డైలాగ్ తిరిగి తీసుకురాబడుతుంది. ఫోన్‌పై, డైలాగ్‌లు పూర్తి-వెడల్పు షీట్‌లుగా ఉంటాయి మరియు కదలవు. CadCraft Studio లో, CadCraft యొక్క PDF కు ప్లాట్ డైలాగ్ (మీరు పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు తెరిచి ఉంచవచ్చు) దాని టైటిల్ బార్‌లో ఒక బటన్ కలిగి ఉంటుంది: దాని స్వంత విండోలో తెరువు. డైలాగ్ తన స్వంత విండోలోకి మారుతుంది, దానిని మరో మానిటర్‌పై ఉంచవచ్చు. డైలాగ్‌ను మూసివేస్తే విండో మూసివేయబడుతుంది, విండోను మూసివేస్తే డైలాగ్ మూసివేయబడుతుంది. ప్యానెళ్లను తిరిగి ఉంచు ప్యానెళ్ల విండోలో ప్యానెళ్లను తిరిగి ఉంచు ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా దానిని మూసివేయండి. ప్యానెళ్లు ఎక్కడ ఉండేవో అక్కడికి తిరిగి వెళ్తాయి. ప్రధాన విండోను మూసివేయడం లేదా రీలోడ్ చేయడం ప్యానెళ్ల విండోను కూడా మూసివేస్తుంది. యాప్ మళ్లీ తెరిచినప్పుడు మీ ప్యానెళ్లు తమ స్వంత విండోలో ఉన్నాయని చెబుతుంది; ఆ విండోను తిరిగి తీసుకురావడానికి దాన్ని తెరువు ఎంచుకోండి, లేదా ఇక్కడే ఉంచు. సెట్టింగ్‌లు › సాధారణం › లేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి ప్రతి ప్యానెల్‌ను అది మొదట ప్రారంభమైన చోటుకు తిరిగి ఉంచుతుంది. ప్యానెళ్లను కొత్త విండోకు తరలించినప్పుడు ఏదీ తెరుచుకోకపోతే, బ్రౌజర్ పాప్-అప్‌ను అడ్డుకుంది. యాప్ అది చెబుతుంది మరియు ప్యానెళ్లను ఉన్న చోటే ఉంచుతుంది. getcadcraft.com కోసం పాప్-అప్‌లను అనుమతించండి, తర్వాత మళ్లీ ప్రయత్నించండి. ఈ లేఅవుట్ ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లో ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా, మీ ఇతర సెట్టింగ్‌లతో పాటు ఉంచబడుతుంది.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"రిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేఅవుట్‌లో పని చేయి","summary":"యాప్‌ను డ్రాయింగ్ పైన ట్యాబ్‌ల రిబ్బన్‌కు మార్చండి, ఎడమవైపు ప్రాపర్టీలు మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్‌తో, ప్రతి తెరిచిన వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్‌తో, మరియు మీకు ఇష్టమైనప్పుడు క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి మారండి.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["లేఅవుట్","వర్క్‌స్పేస్","రిబ్బన్","రిబ్బన్ ట్యాబ్‌లు","ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్","బ్రౌజర్","ప్యాలెట్‌లు","ప్రాపర్టీస్ ప్యాలెట్","వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు","స్టేటస్ బార్","క్విక్ యాక్సెస్","రిబ్బన్ మడత","రిబ్బన్ కనిష్టీకరించు","రిబ్బన్ విండో","క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్","లేఅవుట్ మార్చు","లేఅవుట్ మారు","డాక్ చేయబడింది"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"ఎంచుకోండి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"ఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"కీబోర్డ్ నుండి రిబ్బన్ ఉపయోగించు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"రిబ్బన్‌ను మడవు లేదా దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"మీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి","level":2},{"id":"section-6","text":"క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ యొక్క మూడు వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేఅవుట్‌లలో ఒకటి. పై భాగంలో ట్యాబ్‌ల రిబ్బన్ నడుస్తుంది, ప్రాపర్టీలు మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌ను పంచుకుంటాయి, తెరిచిన ప్రతి వ్యూ డ్రాయింగ్ పైన ఒక ట్యాబ్ పొందుతుంది, మరియు దిగువన స్టేటస్ బార్ ఉంటుంది. టూల్స్ యాప్ స్వంతం: క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో మీరు చేయగలిగినదంతా ఇక్కడ కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు మీ డ్రాయింగ్, మీ మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు అలాగే ఉంటాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eఎంచుకోండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E ట్యాబ్ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E కు వెళ్లండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eలేఅవుట్ వెంటనే మారుతుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం ఒకటే: ప్రతి యాప్ అప్పటినుంచి దానితోనే తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు పేజీ గీయడానికి ముందే అది ఉంటుంది, కాబట్టి క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్ మొదట కనిపించదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eటైటిల్ బార్:\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ పేరు, క్విక్ యాక్సెస్ బటన్లు (తెరువు, సేవ్, చర్య రద్దు, మళ్ళీ చేయి), తెరిచిన ఫైల్ పేరు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఇక్కడ ప్రతి సాధనం ఏమి చేస్తుంది\u003C/span\u003E (శోధన బాక్స్‌తో ప్రతి సాధనం జాబితా, ఏది ఇప్పుడు సిద్ధంగా ఉందో మరియు ఇతరవి ఎందుకు కావో చెబుతూ), ఫుల్ స్క్రీన్, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసహాయం\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E. ఈ లేఅవుట్‌లో టైటిల్ బార్ యాప్ బార్ స్థానాన్ని తీసుకుంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరిబ్బన్:\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ యొక్క టూల్స్ ట్యాబ్‌లలో, ప్రతి ట్యాబ్ గ్రూప్‌లలో. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఫైల్\u003C/span\u003E ఎడమవైపు తెరుస్తుంది, సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు మిగతావి. కొన్ని యాప్‌లు మీరు ఒక నిర్దిష్ట మోడ్ లేదా వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు తమ స్వంత ట్యాబ్‌ను జోడిస్తాయి; అది వర్తించేటప్పుడు అది వెలుగుతుంది, తర్వాత అది వెళ్లిపోతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఎంపికల బార్:\u003C/strong\u003E రిబ్బన్ కింద, నడుస్తున్న సాధనానికి మీ నుండి ఏమి కావాలో, లేదా ఏమి ఎంచుకోబడిందో.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఎడమ ప్యానెల్:\u003C/strong\u003E పైన \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రాపర్టీలు\u003C/span\u003E, దాని కింద ప్యాలెట్‌లు (ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ స్వంత కంటెంట్) ట్యాబ్‌లుగా. వాటి మధ్య గీతను లాగి స్థలాన్ని పంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు:\u003C/strong\u003E మీరు తెరిచిన ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ (షీట్, ప్లాన్, దృశ్యం…). దాని మూసివేత బటన్‌తో ఒకదాన్ని మూసివేయండి; ఫైల్ తెరిచే ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవ్యూ బార్ మరియు స్టేటస్ బార్:\u003C/strong\u003E డ్రాయింగ్ కింద, వ్యూ యొక్క స్వంత నియంత్రణలు (స్కేల్, జూమ్); దిగువన యాప్ సందేశాలు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనం బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది, దాని టూల్‌టిప్ ఎందుకో చెబుతుంది (ఉదాహరణకు &quot;ముందు ఫైల్ తెరువు&quot;, లేదా యాప్‌కు ఆ సాధనం ఇంకా లేదని). దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా దానిపై \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, కారణాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌లో చదవడానికి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eకీబోర్డ్ నుండి రిబ్బన్ ఉపయోగించు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eరిబ్బన్ ట్యాబ్‌పై ఫోకస్ ఉన్నప్పుడు, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ట్యాబ్‌ల మధ్య కదులుతాయి, మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మొదటి మరియు చివరి దానికి వెళ్తాయి. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మొదట నడుస్తున్న సాధనాన్ని ముగిస్తుంది, క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో లాగానే, మరియు ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్ మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు) ముందులాగే పనిచేస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను మడవు లేదా దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eరిబ్బన్ ట్యాబ్‌పై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే రిబ్బన్ దాని ట్యాబ్‌లకు మడవబడుతుంది; అప్పుడు ట్యాబ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేస్తే మీరు ముందుకు వెళ్లే వరకు దాని సాధనాలు చూపుతాయి. తెరిచి ఉంచడానికి మళ్లీ డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. రిబ్బన్ కుడి చివరలో ఉన్న \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E బటన్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను మడవండి\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను తెరిచి ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E కూడా ఉన్నాయి. ప్రతి యాప్ దానిని గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E రిబ్బన్‌ను దాని స్వంత విండోలో ఉంచుతుంది, ఉదాహరణకు రెండవ మానిటర్‌లో. అక్కడ నొక్కిన కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్‌ను తిరిగి ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E, లేదా ఆ విండోను మూసివేయడం, దానిని ఇంటికి తీసుకువస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eటూల్‌బార్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు రిబ్బన్‌కు వర్తిస్తాయి: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బార్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిహ్నాలు మరియు లేబుల్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిహ్నాలు మాత్రమే\u003C/span\u003E (అప్పుడు రిబ్బన్ ప్రతి గ్రూప్‌కు రెండు వరుసల చిన్న చిహ్నాలను చూపుతుంది) లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేబుల్‌లు మాత్రమే\u003C/span\u003E, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్ పేర్లు చూపు (File, Draw, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eమీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఎడమ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెళ్లు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు\u003C/a\u003E లో వివరించిన ప్రతిదాన్ని కలిగి ఉంటాయి: డాక్, తేలియాడు, పిన్, దాచు, ట్యాబ్‌లు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-6\"\u003Eక్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E తెరిచి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft క్లాసిక్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. ప్రతి టూల్‌బార్, ట్యాబ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ అవి ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి వెళ్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఫోన్‌లో, లేదా 760 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు లేదా అంతకంటే తక్కువ ఉన్న విండోలో, మీరు ఏమి ఎంచుకున్నా యాప్ తన ఫోన్ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. మరింత వెడల్పు ఉన్న విండో మీ లేఅవుట్‌ను తిరిగి తెస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమరో అమరికను ప్రయత్నించడానికి, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ లేఅవుట్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"రిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ యాప్ యొక్క మూడు వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేఅవుట్‌లలో ఒకటి. పై భాగంలో ట్యాబ్‌ల రిబ్బన్ నడుస్తుంది, ప్రాపర్టీలు మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ ఎడమ ప్యానెల్‌ను పంచుకుంటాయి, తెరిచిన ప్రతి వ్యూ డ్రాయింగ్ పైన ఒక ట్యాబ్ పొందుతుంది, మరియు దిగువన స్టేటస్ బార్ ఉంటుంది. టూల్స్ యాప్ స్వంతం: క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో మీరు చేయగలిగినదంతా ఇక్కడ కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు మీ డ్రాయింగ్, మీ మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు అలాగే ఉంటాయి. ఎంచుకోండి సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా Ctrl+,). గ్లోబల్ ట్యాబ్ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత లేఅవుట్ › వర్క్‌స్పేస్ కు వెళ్లండి. రిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ ఎంచుకోండి. లేఅవుట్ వెంటనే మారుతుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం ఒకటే: ప్రతి యాప్ అప్పటినుంచి దానితోనే తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు పేజీ గీయడానికి ముందే అది ఉంటుంది, కాబట్టి క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్ మొదట కనిపించదు. ఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది టైటిల్ బార్: యాప్ పేరు, క్విక్ యాక్సెస్ బటన్లు (తెరువు, సేవ్, చర్య రద్దు, మళ్ళీ చేయి), తెరిచిన ఫైల్ పేరు, ఇక్కడ ప్రతి సాధనం ఏమి చేస్తుంది (శోధన బాక్స్‌తో ప్రతి సాధనం జాబితా, ఏది ఇప్పుడు సిద్ధంగా ఉందో మరియు ఇతరవి ఎందుకు కావో చెబుతూ), ఫుల్ స్క్రీన్, సహాయం మరియు సెట్టింగ్‌లు. ఈ లేఅవుట్‌లో టైటిల్ బార్ యాప్ బార్ స్థానాన్ని తీసుకుంటుంది. రిబ్బన్: యాప్ యొక్క టూల్స్ ట్యాబ్‌లలో, ప్రతి ట్యాబ్ గ్రూప్‌లలో. ఫైల్ ఎడమవైపు తెరుస్తుంది, సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు మిగతావి. కొన్ని యాప్‌లు మీరు ఒక నిర్దిష్ట మోడ్ లేదా వర్క్‌స్పేస్‌లో పని చేస్తున్నప్పుడు తమ స్వంత ట్యాబ్‌ను జోడిస్తాయి; అది వర్తించేటప్పుడు అది వెలుగుతుంది, తర్వాత అది వెళ్లిపోతుంది. ఎంపికల బార్: రిబ్బన్ కింద, నడుస్తున్న సాధనానికి మీ నుండి ఏమి కావాలో, లేదా ఏమి ఎంచుకోబడిందో. ఎడమ ప్యానెల్: పైన ప్రాపర్టీలు, దాని కింద ప్యాలెట్‌లు (ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ స్వంత కంటెంట్) ట్యాబ్‌లుగా. వాటి మధ్య గీతను లాగి స్థలాన్ని పంచుకోండి. వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు: మీరు తెరిచిన ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ (షీట్, ప్లాన్, దృశ్యం…). దాని మూసివేత బటన్‌తో ఒకదాన్ని మూసివేయండి; ఫైల్ తెరిచే ఉంటుంది. వ్యూ బార్ మరియు స్టేటస్ బార్: డ్రాయింగ్ కింద, వ్యూ యొక్క స్వంత నియంత్రణలు (స్కేల్, జూమ్); దిగువన యాప్ సందేశాలు. ఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనం బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది, దాని టూల్‌టిప్ ఎందుకో చెబుతుంది (ఉదాహరణకు \"ముందు ఫైల్ తెరువు\", లేదా యాప్‌కు ఆ సాధనం ఇంకా లేదని). దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా దానిపై Enter నొక్కండి, కారణాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌లో చదవడానికి. కీబోర్డ్ నుండి రిబ్బన్ ఉపయోగించు రిబ్బన్ ట్యాబ్‌పై ఫోకస్ ఉన్నప్పుడు, ← మరియు → ట్యాబ్‌ల మధ్య కదులుతాయి, మరియు Home మరియు End మొదటి మరియు చివరి దానికి వెళ్తాయి. Esc మొదట నడుస్తున్న సాధనాన్ని ముగిస్తుంది, క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో లాగానే, మరియు ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్ మరియు Ctrl+Shift+F (స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు) ముందులాగే పనిచేస్తాయి. రిబ్బన్‌ను మడవు లేదా దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు రిబ్బన్ ట్యాబ్‌పై డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేస్తే రిబ్బన్ దాని ట్యాబ్‌లకు మడవబడుతుంది; అప్పుడు ట్యాబ్‌పై క్లిక్ చేస్తే మీరు ముందుకు వెళ్లే వరకు దాని సాధనాలు చూపుతాయి. తెరిచి ఉంచడానికి మళ్లీ డబుల్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. రిబ్బన్ కుడి చివరలో ఉన్న రిబ్బన్‌ను తరలించు బటన్‌లో రిబ్బన్‌ను మడవండి మరియు రిబ్బన్‌ను తెరిచి ఉంచు కూడా ఉన్నాయి. ప్రతి యాప్ దానిని గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది. రిబ్బన్‌ను తరలించు › కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు రిబ్బన్‌ను దాని స్వంత విండోలో ఉంచుతుంది, ఉదాహరణకు రెండవ మానిటర్‌లో. అక్కడ నొక్కిన కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి. రిబ్బన్‌ను తిరిగి ఉంచు, లేదా ఆ విండోను మూసివేయడం, దానిని ఇంటికి తీసుకువస్తుంది. టూల్‌బార్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు రిబ్బన్‌కు వర్తిస్తాయి: సెట్టింగ్‌లు › గ్లోబల్ › టూల్‌బార్ › చిహ్నాలు మరియు లేబుల్‌లు, చిహ్నాలు మాత్రమే (అప్పుడు రిబ్బన్ ప్రతి గ్రూప్‌కు రెండు వరుసల చిన్న చిహ్నాలను చూపుతుంది) లేదా లేబుల్‌లు మాత్రమే, మరియు గ్రూప్ పేర్లు చూపు (File, Draw, …). మీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి ఎడమ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెళ్లు ప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు లో వివరించిన ప్రతిదాన్ని కలిగి ఉంటాయి: డాక్, తేలియాడు, పిన్, దాచు, ట్యాబ్‌లు, కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు మరియు లేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి. క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు సెట్టింగ్‌లు › గ్లోబల్ › లేఅవుట్ › వర్క్‌స్పేస్ తెరిచి CadCraft క్లాసిక్ ఎంచుకోండి. ప్రతి టూల్‌బార్, ట్యాబ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ అవి ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి వెళ్తుంది. ఫోన్‌లో, లేదా 760 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు లేదా అంతకంటే తక్కువ ఉన్న విండోలో, మీరు ఏమి ఎంచుకున్నా యాప్ తన ఫోన్ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. మరింత వెడల్పు ఉన్న విండో మీ లేఅవుట్‌ను తిరిగి తెస్తుంది. మరో అమరికను ప్రయత్నించడానికి, టూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ లేఅవుట్ చూడండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"టూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ లేఅవుట్‌లో పని చేయి","summary":"యాప్‌ను ఎడమవైపు మీ స్వంత స్లాట్‌లతో సాధనాల కాలమ్‌కు మార్చండి, నడుస్తున్న సాధనం లేదా మీరు ఎంచుకున్నదాన్ని అనుసరించే ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ పైన, కుడివైపు నావిగేటర్, ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్, త్వరిత ఎంపికలు మరియు ఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్; మరియు మీకు ఇష్టమైనప్పుడు క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి మారండి.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["లేఅవుట్","వర్క్‌స్పేస్","టూల్‌బాక్స్","టూల్ కాలమ్","ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్","ఇన్ఫో బార్","నావిగేటర్","ప్రాజెక్ట్ మ్యాప్","క్విక్ బార్","త్వరిత ఎంపికలు","ఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్","నా టూల్స్","ఇష్టమైన టూల్స్","వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు","టూల్‌బాక్స్ విండో","క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్","లేఅవుట్ మార్చు","లేఅవుట్ మారు"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"ఎంచుకోండి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"ఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"టూల్‌బాక్స్‌కు దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"మీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి","level":2},{"id":"section-5","text":"క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ యొక్క మూడు వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేఅవుట్‌లలో ఒకటి. డ్రాయింగ్ టూల్స్ ఎడమవైపు ఒక కాలమ్‌లో నిలుస్తాయి, పైన ఉన్న ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ నడుస్తున్న సాధనానికి ఏమి కావాలో లేదా మీరు ఎంచుకున్నది చూపిస్తుంది, ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ ఉంటుంది, మరియు డ్రాయింగ్ కింద ఉన్న క్విక్ బార్ కొన్ని రోజువారీ ఎంపికలను కలిగి ఉంటుంది. టూల్స్ యాప్ స్వంతం: క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో మీరు చేయగలిగినదంతా ఇక్కడ కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు మీ డ్రాయింగ్, మీ మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు అలాగే ఉంటాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eఎంచుకోండి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E ట్యాబ్ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E కు వెళ్లండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eలేఅవుట్ వెంటనే మారుతుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం ఒకటే: ప్రతి యాప్ అప్పటినుంచి దానితోనే తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు పేజీ గీయడానికి ముందే అది ఉంటుంది, కాబట్టి క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్ మొదట కనిపించదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eటూల్‌బార్:\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ స్వంత టూల్‌బార్ దాని మెనూలతో డ్రాయింగ్ ప్రాంతం పైభాగంలోనే ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఇన్ఫో బాక్స్:\u003C/strong\u003E టూల్‌బార్ కింద. ఒక సాధనం నడుస్తున్నప్పుడు అది సాధనాన్ని మరియు దానికి ఏమి కావాలో చూపిస్తుంది. మీరు ఏదైనా ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు అది దాని ప్రధాన సెట్టింగ్‌లను చూపుతుంది (గోడ కోసం: దాని రకం, మందం, పొడవు, స్థాన రేఖ మరియు ఎత్తు), మరియు మీరు వాటిని అక్కడే మార్చవచ్చు: అక్కడ చేసిన మార్పు ప్రాపర్టీలలో చేసినట్లే, మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E దానిని వెనక్కి తీసుకుంటుంది. ఏదీ ఎంచుకోకపోతే &quot;ఏదీ ఎంచుకోలేదు&quot; అని చెబుతుంది. ప్రతి యాప్ ఇక్కడ తన స్వంత బాక్స్‌లను జోడిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు ప్రస్తుత లేయర్ లేదా లెవెల్, లేదా తెరిచిన డాక్యుమెంట్.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్:\u003C/strong\u003E ఎడమవైపు కాలమ్, యాప్ యొక్క టూల్స్ గ్రూప్‌లలో. గ్రూప్ పేరుపై క్లిక్ చేస్తే దాన్ని మడవండి లేదా మళ్లీ తెరవండి. నడుస్తున్న సాధనం వెలుగుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eనా టూల్స్:\u003C/strong\u003E టూల్‌బాక్స్ యొక్క మొదటి గ్రూప్, మీరు ఎక్కువగా ఉపయోగించే సాధనాల కోసం ఎనిమిది ఖాళీ స్లాట్లు. ఏదైనా సాధనం లేదా కమాండ్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి ఖాళీ స్లాట్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి; లేదా టూల్‌బాక్స్‌లోని ఒక సాధనంపై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేసి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనా టూల్స్‌కు జోడించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి; లేదా ఒక సాధనాన్ని ఒక స్లాట్‌పైకి లాగండి. స్లాట్‌ను మార్చడానికి లేదా ఖాళీ చేయడానికి దానిపై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. మీ స్లాట్‌లు ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లో ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eనావిగేటర్:\u003C/strong\u003E కుడి ప్యానెల్. యాప్ యొక్క ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ పైన ఉంటుంది (ప్లాన్‌లు, సెక్షన్‌లు, ఎలివేషన్‌లు, 3D వ్యూలు, షెడ్యూల్‌లు, షీట్‌లు, యాప్‌కు ఉన్నవి ఏవైనా): దానిపై క్లిక్ చేసి తెరవండి. దాని కింద, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రాపర్టీలు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు కుడి ప్యానెల్ యొక్క ఇతర విషయాలతో ఒక ట్యాబ్.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eవ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు:\u003C/strong\u003E తెరిచిన ఫైల్ యొక్క ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ (షీట్, ప్లాన్, దృశ్యం…), డ్రాయింగ్ పైన.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eక్విక్ బార్:\u003C/strong\u003E డ్రాయింగ్ కింద, యాప్ కలిగి ఉన్న వ్యూ సెట్టింగ్‌లు మరియు రోజువారీ ఎంపికలు, ఉదాహరణకు స్కేల్, వివరాల స్థాయి, వ్యూ పరిధి, ఫిట్, ప్లాన్, స్ప్లిట్ లేదా 3D, స్నాప్‌లు మరియు యూనిట్లు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్:\u003C/strong\u003E డ్రాయింగ్‌లో దేనిపైనైనా క్లిక్ చేస్తే, పాయింటర్ పక్కన ఒక చిన్న ప్యాలెట్ వస్తుంది, దాని కోసం యాప్ యొక్క ఎడిట్ కమాండ్‌లతో, ఉదాహరణకు తరలించు, కాపీ చేయి, తిప్పు, ప్రతిబింబం, నకలు లేదా తొలగించు, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eప్రాపర్టీలు\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, మౌస్ వీల్, మరో క్లిక్ లేదా దాని మూసివేత బటన్ దానిని దూరంగా ఉంచుతాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనం బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది, మరియు దాని టూల్‌టిప్ ఎందుకో చెబుతుంది. దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E నొక్కండి, కారణాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌లో చదవడానికి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E మొదట నడుస్తున్న సాధనాన్ని ముగిస్తుంది, క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో లాగానే, మరియు ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్ మరియు \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు) ముందులాగే పనిచేస్తాయి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్‌కు దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్ పైభాగంలో ఉన్న \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్‌ను తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E బటన్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E ఉంటుంది: టూల్‌బాక్స్ తన స్వంత ఇరుకైన విండోలో తెరుచుకుంటుంది, దానిని యాప్ పక్కన లేదా రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై ఉంచవచ్చు. అక్కడ నొక్కిన కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్‌ను తిరిగి ఉంచు\u003C/span\u003E, లేదా ఆ విండోను మూసివేయడం, దానిని ఇంటికి తీసుకువస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eటూల్‌బార్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు టూల్‌బాక్స్‌కు కూడా వర్తిస్తాయి: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eటూల్‌బార్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిహ్నాలు మరియు లేబుల్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eచిహ్నాలు మాత్రమే\u003C/span\u003E లేదా \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేబుల్‌లు మాత్రమే\u003C/span\u003E, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్రూప్ పేర్లు చూపు (File, Draw, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eమీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఎడమ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెళ్లు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు\u003C/a\u003E లో వివరించిన ప్రతిదాన్ని కలిగి ఉంటాయి: డాక్, తేలియాడు, పిన్, దాచు, ట్యాబ్‌లు, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eకొత్త విండోకు తరలించు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-5\"\u003Eక్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్‌స్పేస్\u003C/span\u003E తెరిచి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft క్లాసిక్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. ప్రతి టూల్‌బార్, ట్యాబ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ అవి ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి వెళ్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఫోన్‌లో, లేదా 760 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు లేదా అంతకంటే తక్కువ ఉన్న విండోలో, మీరు ఏమి ఎంచుకున్నా యాప్ తన ఫోన్ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. మరింత వెడల్పు ఉన్న విండో మీ లేఅవుట్‌ను తిరిగి తెస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమరో అమరికను ప్రయత్నించడానికి, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేఅవుట్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"టూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ యాప్ యొక్క మూడు వర్క్‌స్పేస్ లేఅవుట్‌లలో ఒకటి. డ్రాయింగ్ టూల్స్ ఎడమవైపు ఒక కాలమ్‌లో నిలుస్తాయి, పైన ఉన్న ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ నడుస్తున్న సాధనానికి ఏమి కావాలో లేదా మీరు ఎంచుకున్నది చూపిస్తుంది, ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ ఉంటుంది, మరియు డ్రాయింగ్ కింద ఉన్న క్విక్ బార్ కొన్ని రోజువారీ ఎంపికలను కలిగి ఉంటుంది. టూల్స్ యాప్ స్వంతం: క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో మీరు చేయగలిగినదంతా ఇక్కడ కూడా పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు మీ డ్రాయింగ్, మీ మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు అలాగే ఉంటాయి. ఎంచుకోండి సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా Ctrl+,). గ్లోబల్ ట్యాబ్ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత లేఅవుట్ › వర్క్‌స్పేస్ కు వెళ్లండి. టూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ ఎంచుకోండి. లేఅవుట్ వెంటనే మారుతుంది. ఈ ఎంపిక ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం ఒకటే: ప్రతి యాప్ అప్పటినుంచి దానితోనే తెరుచుకుంటుంది, మరియు పేజీ గీయడానికి ముందే అది ఉంటుంది, కాబట్టి క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్ మొదట కనిపించదు. ఏది ఎక్కడ ఉంది టూల్‌బార్: యాప్ స్వంత టూల్‌బార్ దాని మెనూలతో డ్రాయింగ్ ప్రాంతం పైభాగంలోనే ఉంటుంది. ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్: టూల్‌బార్ కింద. ఒక సాధనం నడుస్తున్నప్పుడు అది సాధనాన్ని మరియు దానికి ఏమి కావాలో చూపిస్తుంది. మీరు ఏదైనా ఎంచుకున్నప్పుడు అది దాని ప్రధాన సెట్టింగ్‌లను చూపుతుంది (గోడ కోసం: దాని రకం, మందం, పొడవు, స్థాన రేఖ మరియు ఎత్తు), మరియు మీరు వాటిని అక్కడే మార్చవచ్చు: అక్కడ చేసిన మార్పు ప్రాపర్టీలలో చేసినట్లే, మరియు Ctrl+Z దానిని వెనక్కి తీసుకుంటుంది. ఏదీ ఎంచుకోకపోతే \"ఏదీ ఎంచుకోలేదు\" అని చెబుతుంది. ప్రతి యాప్ ఇక్కడ తన స్వంత బాక్స్‌లను జోడిస్తుంది, ఉదాహరణకు ప్రస్తుత లేయర్ లేదా లెవెల్, లేదా తెరిచిన డాక్యుమెంట్. టూల్‌బాక్స్: ఎడమవైపు కాలమ్, యాప్ యొక్క టూల్స్ గ్రూప్‌లలో. గ్రూప్ పేరుపై క్లిక్ చేస్తే దాన్ని మడవండి లేదా మళ్లీ తెరవండి. నడుస్తున్న సాధనం వెలుగుతుంది. నా టూల్స్: టూల్‌బాక్స్ యొక్క మొదటి గ్రూప్, మీరు ఎక్కువగా ఉపయోగించే సాధనాల కోసం ఎనిమిది ఖాళీ స్లాట్లు. ఏదైనా సాధనం లేదా కమాండ్ ఎంచుకోవడానికి ఖాళీ స్లాట్‌పై క్లిక్ చేయండి; లేదా టూల్‌బాక్స్‌లోని ఒక సాధనంపై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేసి నా టూల్స్‌కు జోడించు ఎంచుకోండి; లేదా ఒక సాధనాన్ని ఒక స్లాట్‌పైకి లాగండి. స్లాట్‌ను మార్చడానికి లేదా ఖాళీ చేయడానికి దానిపై రైట్-క్లిక్ చేయండి. మీ స్లాట్‌లు ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లో ప్రతి యాప్‌కు విడిగా ఉంచబడతాయి. నావిగేటర్: కుడి ప్యానెల్. యాప్ యొక్క ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ పైన ఉంటుంది (ప్లాన్‌లు, సెక్షన్‌లు, ఎలివేషన్‌లు, 3D వ్యూలు, షెడ్యూల్‌లు, షీట్‌లు, యాప్‌కు ఉన్నవి ఏవైనా): దానిపై క్లిక్ చేసి తెరవండి. దాని కింద, ప్రాపర్టీలు మరియు కుడి ప్యానెల్ యొక్క ఇతర విషయాలతో ఒక ట్యాబ్. వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు: తెరిచిన ఫైల్ యొక్క ప్రతి వ్యూకు ఒక ట్యాబ్ (షీట్, ప్లాన్, దృశ్యం…), డ్రాయింగ్ పైన. క్విక్ బార్: డ్రాయింగ్ కింద, యాప్ కలిగి ఉన్న వ్యూ సెట్టింగ్‌లు మరియు రోజువారీ ఎంపికలు, ఉదాహరణకు స్కేల్, వివరాల స్థాయి, వ్యూ పరిధి, ఫిట్, ప్లాన్, స్ప్లిట్ లేదా 3D, స్నాప్‌లు మరియు యూనిట్లు. ఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్: డ్రాయింగ్‌లో దేనిపైనైనా క్లిక్ చేస్తే, పాయింటర్ పక్కన ఒక చిన్న ప్యాలెట్ వస్తుంది, దాని కోసం యాప్ యొక్క ఎడిట్ కమాండ్‌లతో, ఉదాహరణకు తరలించు, కాపీ చేయి, తిప్పు, ప్రతిబింబం, నకలు లేదా తొలగించు, మరియు ప్రాపర్టీలు. Esc, మౌస్ వీల్, మరో క్లిక్ లేదా దాని మూసివేత బటన్ దానిని దూరంగా ఉంచుతాయి. ఇప్పుడు నడపలేని సాధనం బూడిద రంగులో ఉంటుంది, మరియు దాని టూల్‌టిప్ ఎందుకో చెబుతుంది. దానిపై క్లిక్ చేయండి, లేదా Enter నొక్కండి, కారణాన్ని ఒక బాక్స్‌లో చదవడానికి. Esc మొదట నడుస్తున్న సాధనాన్ని ముగిస్తుంది, క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌లో లాగానే, మరియు ప్రతి షార్ట్‌కట్ మరియు Ctrl+Shift+F (స్క్రీన్ మోడ్‌లు) ముందులాగే పనిచేస్తాయి. టూల్‌బాక్స్‌కు దాని స్వంత విండో ఇవ్వు టూల్‌బాక్స్ పైభాగంలో ఉన్న టూల్‌బాక్స్‌ను తరలించు బటన్‌లో కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు ఉంటుంది: టూల్‌బాక్స్ తన స్వంత ఇరుకైన విండోలో తెరుచుకుంటుంది, దానిని యాప్ పక్కన లేదా రెండవ మానిటర్‌పై ఉంచవచ్చు. అక్కడ నొక్కిన కీలు యాప్‌కు చేరతాయి. టూల్‌బాక్స్‌ను తిరిగి ఉంచు, లేదా ఆ విండోను మూసివేయడం, దానిని ఇంటికి తీసుకువస్తుంది. టూల్‌బార్ సెట్టింగ్‌లు టూల్‌బాక్స్‌కు కూడా వర్తిస్తాయి: సెట్టింగ్‌లు › గ్లోబల్ › టూల్‌బార్ › చిహ్నాలు మరియు లేబుల్‌లు, చిహ్నాలు మాత్రమే లేదా లేబుల్‌లు మాత్రమే, మరియు గ్రూప్ పేర్లు చూపు (File, Draw, …). మీ ప్యానెళ్లు మీవే ఉంటాయి ఎడమ మరియు కుడి ప్యానెళ్లు ప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు లో వివరించిన ప్రతిదాన్ని కలిగి ఉంటాయి: డాక్, తేలియాడు, పిన్, దాచు, ట్యాబ్‌లు, కొత్త విండోకు తరలించు మరియు లేఅవుట్ రీసెట్ చేయి. క్లాసిక్ లేఅవుట్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లు సెట్టింగ్‌లు › గ్లోబల్ › లేఅవుట్ › వర్క్‌స్పేస్ తెరిచి CadCraft క్లాసిక్ ఎంచుకోండి. ప్రతి టూల్‌బార్, ట్యాబ్ మరియు ప్యానెల్ అవి ఉన్న చోటుకు తిరిగి వెళ్తుంది. ఫోన్‌లో, లేదా 760 పిక్సెల్స్ వెడల్పు లేదా అంతకంటే తక్కువ ఉన్న విండోలో, మీరు ఏమి ఎంచుకున్నా యాప్ తన ఫోన్ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. మరింత వెడల్పు ఉన్న విండో మీ లేఅవుట్‌ను తిరిగి తెస్తుంది. మరో అమరికను ప్రయత్నించడానికి, రిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేఅవుట్ చూడండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"మీ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు మరియు మీ సెటప్‌లను సేవ్ చేయి","summary":"ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేదా ప్రాపర్టీలు ఏ ప్యానెల్ చూపాలో ఎంచుకోండి, టూల్‌బాక్స్ ఎక్కడ ఉంటుందో మరియు బార్‌లలో ఏముంటుందో నిర్ణయించండి, దానిని మీ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌గా సేవ్ చేయండి, మరియు మొత్తం సెటప్‌లను పేరుతో ఉంచి వాటి మధ్య మారండి.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["లేఅవుట్","వర్క్‌స్పేస్","అమర్చు","అనుకూలీకరించు","నా లేఅవుట్","స్వంత లేఅవుట్","ప్యానెళ్లు","ఎడిటర్లు","ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్","ప్రాపర్టీలు","టూల్‌బాక్స్","క్విక్ బార్","ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్","వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్","సెటప్","ప్రొఫైల్","సెటప్ సేవ్","సెటప్ మార్చు","సెటప్ ఎగుమతి","సెటప్ దిగుమతి","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"మీ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"మీ సెటప్‌ను వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్‌గా సేవ్ చేయి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eమూడు అంతర్నిర్మిత లేఅవుట్‌లతో పాటు మీరు ఒక స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చవచ్చు, మరియు మీ మొత్తం సెటప్‌ను ఒక పేరుతో సేవ్ చేసి, ఒక్క అడుగుతో తిరిగి మారవచ్చు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eమీ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెట్టింగ్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eగ్లోబల్\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eలేఅవుట్\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eమీ సొంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు…\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. డైలాగ్ మీరు ఇప్పుడు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న లేఅవుట్ నుండి మొదలవుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eదానికి పేరు ఇవ్వండి, తర్వాత ఎంచుకోండి:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్ పైన:\u003C/strong\u003E యాప్ యొక్క టూల్‌బార్, రిబ్బన్ లేదా ఏదీ కాదు; ఒక టైటిల్ బార్; ఒక ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ మరియు అది కలిగి ఉండే నియంత్రణలు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్ పక్కన:\u003C/strong\u003E ఎడమవైపు, కుడివైపు లేదా అసలు లేకుండా టూల్‌బాక్స్, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనా టూల్స్\u003C/span\u003E తో లేదా లేకుండా; ప్యాలెట్‌లను ఏ ప్యానెల్ కలిగి ఉంటుంది, దాని పైన ఏది ఉంటుంది (ఉదాహరణకు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేదా ప్రాపర్టీలు), మరియు దాని కింద ఏవి ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ఉంటాయి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eడ్రాయింగ్ ప్రాంతం:\u003C/strong\u003E వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు, మీరు టిక్ చేసిన నియంత్రణలతో కూడిన క్విక్ బార్ మరియు వ్యూ బార్, ఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్, స్టేటస్ బార్.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eరూపం:\u003C/strong\u003E పరిమాణం, బటన్ అంచులు మరియు ట్యాబ్ శైలి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఈ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. యాప్ వెంటనే దానికి మారుతుంది, మరియు లేఅవుట్‌లు ఉన్న ప్రతి యాప్ కూడా దానిని ఉపయోగిస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఈ యాప్‌లో ఉన్నవి మాత్రమే అందించబడతాయి. మీ లేఅవుట్ \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eయాడ్-ఆన్‌లు మరియు ఫైల్‌ల నుండి\u003C/span\u003E కింద జాబితా అవుతుంది; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఈ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఎగుమతి చేయి\u003C/span\u003E దానిని మీరు సహోద్యోగికి ఇవ్వగల ఫైల్‌గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు తొలగించు బటన్ దానిని తీసివేస్తుంది. అది నింపే ప్యానెళ్లు ఇంకా డాక్ అవుతాయి, తేలియాడతాయి మరియు తమ స్వంత విండోలకు తరలుతాయి, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003Eప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు\u003C/a\u003E లో ఉన్నట్లే.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eమీ సెటప్‌ను వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్‌గా సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eవర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్ అనేది ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం మీ మొత్తం సెటప్: లేఅవుట్, మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు, టూల్‌బార్ శైలి, ప్యానెళ్లు మరియు వాటి పరిమాణాలు, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనా టూల్స్\u003C/span\u003E. థీమ్ మరియు భాష అందులో భాగం కాదు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eసేవ్:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్‌లు\u003C/span\u003E కింద ఒక పేరు టైప్ చేసి \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eనా సెటప్ సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. ఉన్న పేరుతో సేవ్ చేస్తే ఆ సెటప్ మారుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eమారు:\u003C/strong\u003E జాబితాలో ఒక సెటప్ ఎంచుకుని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eదానికి మారు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి. యాప్ ఆ సెటప్‌తో రీలోడ్ అవుతుంది; మీకు సేవ్ చేయని పని ఉంటే, యాప్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది, ఏ రీలోడ్‌లోనైనా అది అడిగినట్లే.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eపంచుకో:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఎగుమతి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకున్న సెటప్‌ను \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E ఫైల్‌గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eసెటప్ దిగుమతి చేయి…\u003C/span\u003E ఒక ఫైల్ నుండి ఒకదాన్ని జోడిస్తుంది. ఒక ఫైల్ ఈ ఎంపికలను మాత్రమే సెట్ చేస్తుంది, మరేదీ కాదు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eతొలగించు:\u003C/strong\u003E ఒక సెటప్ ఎంచుకుని \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eతొలగించు\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు పన్నెండు సెటప్‌ల వరకు ఉంచుకోవచ్చు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eఅంతర్నిర్మిత అమరికలను ముందుగా ప్రయత్నించడానికి, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eటూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ లేఅవుట్\u003C/a\u003E మరియు \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eరిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేఅవుట్\u003C/a\u003E చూడండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"మూడు అంతర్నిర్మిత లేఅవుట్‌లతో పాటు మీరు ఒక స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చవచ్చు, మరియు మీ మొత్తం సెటప్‌ను ఒక పేరుతో సేవ్ చేసి, ఒక్క అడుగుతో తిరిగి మారవచ్చు. మీ స్వంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు సెట్టింగ్‌లు తెరవండి (గేర్, లేదా Ctrl+,), గ్లోబల్ ఎంచుకోండి, తర్వాత లేఅవుట్. మీ సొంత లేఅవుట్‌ను అమర్చు… ఎంచుకోండి. డైలాగ్ మీరు ఇప్పుడు ఉపయోగిస్తున్న లేఅవుట్ నుండి మొదలవుతుంది. దానికి పేరు ఇవ్వండి, తర్వాత ఎంచుకోండి: డ్రాయింగ్ పైన: యాప్ యొక్క టూల్‌బార్, రిబ్బన్ లేదా ఏదీ కాదు; ఒక టైటిల్ బార్; ఒక ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ మరియు అది కలిగి ఉండే నియంత్రణలు. డ్రాయింగ్ పక్కన: ఎడమవైపు, కుడివైపు లేదా అసలు లేకుండా టూల్‌బాక్స్, నా టూల్స్ తో లేదా లేకుండా; ప్యాలెట్‌లను ఏ ప్యానెల్ కలిగి ఉంటుంది, దాని పైన ఏది ఉంటుంది (ఉదాహరణకు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేదా ప్రాపర్టీలు), మరియు దాని కింద ఏవి ట్యాబ్‌లుగా ఉంటాయి. డ్రాయింగ్ ప్రాంతం: వ్యూ ట్యాబ్‌లు, మీరు టిక్ చేసిన నియంత్రణలతో కూడిన క్విక్ బార్ మరియు వ్యూ బార్, ఎడిట్ ప్యాలెట్, స్టేటస్ బార్. రూపం: పరిమాణం, బటన్ అంచులు మరియు ట్యాబ్ శైలి. ఈ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఉపయోగించు ఎంచుకోండి. యాప్ వెంటనే దానికి మారుతుంది, మరియు లేఅవుట్‌లు ఉన్న ప్రతి యాప్ కూడా దానిని ఉపయోగిస్తుంది. ఈ యాప్‌లో ఉన్నవి మాత్రమే అందించబడతాయి. మీ లేఅవుట్ యాడ్-ఆన్‌లు మరియు ఫైల్‌ల నుండి కింద జాబితా అవుతుంది; ఈ లేఅవుట్‌ను ఎగుమతి చేయి దానిని మీరు సహోద్యోగికి ఇవ్వగల ఫైల్‌గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది, మరియు తొలగించు బటన్ దానిని తీసివేస్తుంది. అది నింపే ప్యానెళ్లు ఇంకా డాక్ అవుతాయి, తేలియాడతాయి మరియు తమ స్వంత విండోలకు తరలుతాయి, ప్యానెళ్లను అమర్చు లో ఉన్నట్లే. మీ సెటప్‌ను వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్‌గా సేవ్ చేయి వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్ అనేది ఈ బ్రౌజర్‌లోని ప్రతి యాప్ కోసం మీ మొత్తం సెటప్: లేఅవుట్, మెనూ శైలి మరియు మీ కీలు, టూల్‌బార్ శైలి, ప్యానెళ్లు మరియు వాటి పరిమాణాలు, మరియు నా టూల్స్. థీమ్ మరియు భాష అందులో భాగం కాదు. సేవ్: వర్క్ ఎన్విరాన్‌మెంట్‌లు కింద ఒక పేరు టైప్ చేసి నా సెటప్ సేవ్ చేయి ఎంచుకోండి. ఉన్న పేరుతో సేవ్ చేస్తే ఆ సెటప్ మారుతుంది. మారు: జాబితాలో ఒక సెటప్ ఎంచుకుని దానికి మారు ఎంచుకోండి. యాప్ ఆ సెటప్‌తో రీలోడ్ అవుతుంది; మీకు సేవ్ చేయని పని ఉంటే, యాప్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది, ఏ రీలోడ్‌లోనైనా అది అడిగినట్లే. పంచుకో: ఎగుమతి ఎంచుకున్న సెటప్‌ను .ccenv.json ఫైల్‌గా సేవ్ చేస్తుంది; సెటప్ దిగుమతి చేయి… ఒక ఫైల్ నుండి ఒకదాన్ని జోడిస్తుంది. ఒక ఫైల్ ఈ ఎంపికలను మాత్రమే సెట్ చేస్తుంది, మరేదీ కాదు. తొలగించు: ఒక సెటప్ ఎంచుకుని తొలగించు ఎంచుకోండి. మీరు పన్నెండు సెటప్‌ల వరకు ఉంచుకోవచ్చు. అంతర్నిర్మిత అమరికలను ముందుగా ప్రయత్నించడానికి, టూల్‌బాక్స్ మరియు ఇన్ఫో బాక్స్ లేఅవుట్ మరియు రిబ్బన్ మరియు ప్రాజెక్ట్ బ్రౌజర్ లేఅవుట్ చూడండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"addons-basics","scope":"basics","title":"Add-ons and your account list","summary":"How add-ons work in every app and edition. Add one once in the Library and it follows your account, loads the first time an app needs it, and updates inside its major version. Sign in on a computer, pin a version, or keep working offline.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["add-ons","library","add","remove","update","pin","lab channel","your add-ons","sign in","offline","this file uses","engine","needs","orphan","team add-ons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-add-on-is","text":"What an add-on is","level":2},{"id":"your-list","text":"Your list","level":2},{"id":"needs","text":"Needs","level":2},{"id":"files-that-use-add-ons","text":"Files that use add-ons","level":2},{"id":"offline","text":"Offline","level":2},{"id":"ipad-and-iphone","text":"iPad and iPhone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-add-on-is\"\u003EWhat an add-on is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-list\"\u003EYour list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESign in\u003C/strong\u003E connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/strong\u003E lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep a version\u003C/strong\u003E pins it. \u003Cstrong\u003EFollow newest\u003C/strong\u003E unpins it. \u003Cstrong\u003ETry lab versions\u003C/strong\u003E moves one add-on to the lab channel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoll back\u003C/strong\u003E returns to the version you had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-use-add-ons\"\u003EFiles that use add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says &quot;This file uses ...&quot; and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload everything for offline\u003C/span\u003E fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ipad-and-iphone\"\u003EiPad and iPhone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What an add-on is An add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the Library and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet. Your list Open Settings › Add-ons › Your add-ons. Sign in connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser. Remove takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which. Check for updates lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes. Keep a version pins it. Follow newest unpins it. Try lab versions moves one add-on to the lab channel. Roll back returns to the version you had. Needs An add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days. Files that use add-ons A drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says \"This file uses ...\" and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched. Offline Download everything for offline fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used. iPad and iPhone Add-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో పని చేయడం","summary":"యాప్‌ను ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయండి, కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా ఏమి పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు దేనికి కనెక్షన్ కావాలి.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["ఆఫ్‌లైన్","ఇంటర్నెట్ లేదు","ఎయిర్‌ప్లేన్","సైట్ సందర్శన","కాష్","service worker","ఇన్‌స్టాల్","డౌన్‌లోడ్","కనెక్షన్"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"యాప్‌ను ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఏమి పనిచేస్తుంది","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"కనెక్షన్ ఏమి కావాలి","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"తెలుసుకోవలసినవి","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003Eయాప్‌లు తమ పనిని మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే చేస్తాయి, కాబట్టి వాటిలో చాలా భాగం ఏ కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా పనిచేస్తుంది. బ్రౌజర్‌కు ముందుగా యాప్ ఫైళ్లు సేవ్ అయి ఉండాలి, అంతే.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eయాప్‌ను ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు యాప్‌ను తెరవండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eపైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E ఎంచుకోండి.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం&quot; కోసం వేచి ఉండండి. యాప్ ఫైళ్లు, దాని ఫైల్ రీడర్లు మరియు ఈ సహాయం ఇప్పుడు మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో సేవ్ అయ్యాయి. DWG లేదా PDF ఫైళ్లు చదివే యాప్‌లు తమ రీడర్లను కూడా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి, కాబట్టి మొదటిసారి ఇది ఒక నిమిషం పట్టవచ్చు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eప్రతి యాప్‌కు, మీరు ఉపయోగించే ప్రతి బ్రౌజర్‌లో, ఒకసారి ఇలా చేయండి. తర్వాత కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా యాప్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లడానికి, మీరు ఇంతకుముందు ఉపయోగించిన అదే చిరునామా లేదా దాని బుక్‌మార్క్ తెరవండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు ఇప్పుడు ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నారు. ఈ యాప్ ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయబడి ఉంటే, దిగువన ఉన్నవన్నీ పనిచేస్తాయి. అది సేవ్ కాకపోతే, ఈ ట్యాబ్‌లో ఇప్పటికే లోడ్ అయినది ఇంకా ఉపయోగించవచ్చు, కానీ మీరు తిరిగి ఆన్‌లైన్‌కు వచ్చే వరకు దానిని రీలోడ్ చేయవద్దు.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఏమి పనిచేస్తుంది\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eమీ కంప్యూటర్ నుండి ఫైళ్లు తెరవడం, డ్రాయింగ్, ఎడిట్ చేయడం, కొలవడం మరియు తనిఖీ చేయడం.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eసేవ్ చేయడం మరియు ఎగుమతి: ప్రతి సేవ్ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఒక డౌన్‌లోడ్.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఈ సహాయం, శోధనతో సహా.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eకనెక్షన్ ఏమి కావాలి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eయాప్‌ను మొదటిసారి తెరవడం, మరియు దానిని ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయడం.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eయాప్ యొక్క కొత్త వెర్షన్ పొందడం. మీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, యాప్ తదుపరిసారి తెరిచినప్పుడు తాజా వెర్షన్‌ను తీసుకుంటుంది, మరియు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి\u003C/span\u003E సేవ్ చేసిన కాపీని రిఫ్రెష్ చేస్తుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eవెబ్‌సైట్‌కు లింక్‌లు, ఉదాహరణకు \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eవెబ్‌సైట్‌లో తెరువు\u003C/span\u003E మరియు ఇతర పేజీలకు లింక్‌లు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eకంప్యూటర్ల మధ్య క్లౌడ్ సేవ్ మరియు సింక్, ఇవి ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేవు.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eతెలుసుకోవలసినవి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eసేవ్ చేసిన కాపీ ఒక కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఒక బ్రౌజర్‌కు చెందుతుంది. మీ బ్రౌజర్ సైట్ డేటాను క్లియర్ చేస్తే అది తొలగించబడుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eకొన్ని బ్రౌజర్‌లలోని ప్రైవేట్ విండోలు, మరియు కొన్ని లాక్ చేసిన వర్క్ కంప్యూటర్‌లు, ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సేవ్‌ను అనుమతించవు. సేవ్ చేయలేనప్పుడు బటన్ మీకు చెబుతుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari, మీరు కొన్ని వారాలుగా సందర్శించని సైట్‌ల సేవ్ చేసిన సైట్ డేటాను తొలగిస్తుంది. ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు ఎప్పుడో ఒకసారి యాప్‌ను తెరవండి, లేదా ప్రయాణానికి ముందు మళ్లీ సేవ్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, ఒకే ఇన్‌స్టాల్‌లో మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లు ఉన్న డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్, getcadcraft.com/download వద్ద ఉంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"యాప్‌లు తమ పనిని మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే చేస్తాయి, కాబట్టి వాటిలో చాలా భాగం ఏ కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా పనిచేస్తుంది. బ్రౌజర్‌కు ముందుగా యాప్ ఫైళ్లు సేవ్ అయి ఉండాలి, అంతే. యాప్‌ను ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయి మీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు యాప్‌ను తెరవండి. పైన ఉన్న బార్‌లో ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి ఎంచుకోండి. \"ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సిద్ధం\" కోసం వేచి ఉండండి. యాప్ ఫైళ్లు, దాని ఫైల్ రీడర్లు మరియు ఈ సహాయం ఇప్పుడు మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో సేవ్ అయ్యాయి. DWG లేదా PDF ఫైళ్లు చదివే యాప్‌లు తమ రీడర్లను కూడా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి, కాబట్టి మొదటిసారి ఇది ఒక నిమిషం పట్టవచ్చు. ప్రతి యాప్‌కు, మీరు ఉపయోగించే ప్రతి బ్రౌజర్‌లో, ఒకసారి ఇలా చేయండి. తర్వాత కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా యాప్‌కు తిరిగి వెళ్లడానికి, మీరు ఇంతకుముందు ఉపయోగించిన అదే చిరునామా లేదా దాని బుక్‌మార్క్ తెరవండి. మీరు ఇప్పుడు ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నారు. ఈ యాప్ ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయబడి ఉంటే, దిగువన ఉన్నవన్నీ పనిచేస్తాయి. అది సేవ్ కాకపోతే, ఈ ట్యాబ్‌లో ఇప్పటికే లోడ్ అయినది ఇంకా ఉపయోగించవచ్చు, కానీ మీరు తిరిగి ఆన్‌లైన్‌కు వచ్చే వరకు దానిని రీలోడ్ చేయవద్దు. ఆఫ్‌లైన్‌లో ఏమి పనిచేస్తుంది మీ కంప్యూటర్ నుండి ఫైళ్లు తెరవడం, డ్రాయింగ్, ఎడిట్ చేయడం, కొలవడం మరియు తనిఖీ చేయడం. సేవ్ చేయడం మరియు ఎగుమతి: ప్రతి సేవ్ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌కు ఒక డౌన్‌లోడ్. ఈ సహాయం, శోధనతో సహా. కనెక్షన్ ఏమి కావాలి యాప్‌ను మొదటిసారి తెరవడం, మరియు దానిని ఆఫ్‌లైన్ ఉపయోగం కోసం సేవ్ చేయడం. యాప్ యొక్క కొత్త వెర్షన్ పొందడం. మీరు ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు, యాప్ తదుపరిసారి తెరిచినప్పుడు తాజా వెర్షన్‌ను తీసుకుంటుంది, మరియు ఆఫ్‌లైన్ కోసం సేవ్ చేయి సేవ్ చేసిన కాపీని రిఫ్రెష్ చేస్తుంది. వెబ్‌సైట్‌కు లింక్‌లు, ఉదాహరణకు వెబ్‌సైట్‌లో తెరువు మరియు ఇతర పేజీలకు లింక్‌లు. కంప్యూటర్ల మధ్య క్లౌడ్ సేవ్ మరియు సింక్, ఇవి ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేవు. తెలుసుకోవలసినవి సేవ్ చేసిన కాపీ ఒక కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఒక బ్రౌజర్‌కు చెందుతుంది. మీ బ్రౌజర్ సైట్ డేటాను క్లియర్ చేస్తే అది తొలగించబడుతుంది. కొన్ని బ్రౌజర్‌లలోని ప్రైవేట్ విండోలు, మరియు కొన్ని లాక్ చేసిన వర్క్ కంప్యూటర్‌లు, ఆఫ్‌లైన్ సేవ్‌ను అనుమతించవు. సేవ్ చేయలేనప్పుడు బటన్ మీకు చెబుతుంది. Safari, మీరు కొన్ని వారాలుగా సందర్శించని సైట్‌ల సేవ్ చేసిన సైట్ డేటాను తొలగిస్తుంది. ఆన్‌లైన్‌లో ఉన్నప్పుడు ఎప్పుడో ఒకసారి యాప్‌ను తెరవండి, లేదా ప్రయాణానికి ముందు మళ్లీ సేవ్ చేయండి. CadCraft Studio, ఒకే ఇన్‌స్టాల్‌లో మొత్తం ఎనిమిది యాప్‌లు ఉన్న డెస్క్‌టాప్ యాప్, getcadcraft.com/download వద్ద ఉంది.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"మీ ఫైళ్లు ఎక్కడికి వెళ్తాయి","summary":"ఫైళ్లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి, సేవ్ ఎలా పనిచేస్తుంది, మరియు బ్రౌజర్ ఏమి గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["గోప్యత","అప్‌లోడ్","క్లౌడ్","సేవ్","డౌన్‌లోడ్","స్టోరేజ్","లోకల్","భద్రత","గోప్యమైనది"],"headings":[{"id":"section","text":"మీ ఫైళ్లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి","level":2},{"id":"section-2","text":"సేవ్ ఎలా పనిచేస్తుంది","level":2},{"id":"section-3","text":"బ్రౌజర్ ఏమి గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది","level":2},{"id":"section-4","text":"క్లౌడ్ సేవ్","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"section\"\u003Eమీ ఫైళ్లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eమీరు ఒక డ్రాయింగ్, మోడల్ లేదా PDF తెరిచినప్పుడు, యాప్ దానిని మీ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల చదువుతుంది. \u003Cstrong\u003Eఫైల్ అప్‌లోడ్ చేయబడదు.\u003C/strong\u003E అది మాకు ఎప్పుడూ కనిపించదు, మరియు కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా అదే విధంగా పనిచేస్తుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-2\"\u003Eసేవ్ ఎలా పనిచేస్తుంది\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ పేజీ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఫైళ్లను స్వయంగా ఓవర్‌రైట్ చేయలేదు, కాబట్టి యాప్‌లు ఒక కొత్త ఫైల్‌ను \u003Cstrong\u003Eడౌన్‌లోడ్\u003C/strong\u003E చేయడం ద్వారా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eడౌన్‌లోడ్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లను ఉంచే చోటికి వెళ్తుంది, సాధారణంగా మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్, మీరు బ్రౌజర్‌ను ప్రతిసారీ అడగమని సెట్ చేస్తే తప్ప (చూడండి \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు కంప్యూటర్‌లు\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eఫైల్ మార్చే యాప్‌లు &quot;(edited)&quot; లేదా &quot;(marked up)&quot; వంటి కొత్త పేరుతో కాపీని సేవ్ చేస్తాయి, తద్వారా మీ అసలు ఫైల్ మారకుండా ఉంటుంది.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eసేవ్ చేయని పనితో మీరు ట్యాబ్‌ను మూసివేస్తే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (&quot;సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-3\"\u003Eబ్రౌజర్ ఏమి గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకొన్ని యాప్‌లు మీ రచయిత పేరు లేదా టూల్ శైలులు వంటి చిన్న సెట్టింగ్‌లను మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో గుర్తుంచుకుంటాయి. అవి ఆ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఆ బ్రౌజర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి, మరియు సైట్ డేటాను క్లియర్ చేస్తే అవి తొలగించబడతాయి. ప్రతి యాప్ సహాయం అది ఏమి ఉంచుతుందో చెబుతుంది.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-4\"\u003Eక్లౌడ్ సేవ్\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Eకంప్యూటర్ల మధ్య క్లౌడ్ సేవ్ మరియు సింక్ వాణిజ్య లైసెన్స్‌లో భాగం. అవి ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేవు. అప్పటివరకు, మీ ఫైళ్లను మీ ఇతర ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫైళ్లు ఉంచే చోటే ఉంచండి, మరియు సాధారణ పద్ధతిలో బ్యాకప్ చేయండి.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"మీ ఫైళ్లు మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి మీరు ఒక డ్రాయింగ్, మోడల్ లేదా PDF తెరిచినప్పుడు, యాప్ దానిని మీ బ్రౌజర్ లోపల చదువుతుంది. ఫైల్ అప్‌లోడ్ చేయబడదు. అది మాకు ఎప్పుడూ కనిపించదు, మరియు కనెక్షన్ లేకుండా కూడా అదే విధంగా పనిచేస్తుంది. సేవ్ ఎలా పనిచేస్తుంది బ్రౌజర్ పేజీ మీ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఫైళ్లను స్వయంగా ఓవర్‌రైట్ చేయలేదు, కాబట్టి యాప్‌లు ఒక కొత్త ఫైల్‌ను డౌన్‌లోడ్ చేయడం ద్వారా సేవ్ చేస్తాయి: డౌన్‌లోడ్ మీ బ్రౌజర్ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌లను ఉంచే చోటికి వెళ్తుంది, సాధారణంగా మీ డౌన్‌లోడ్‌ల ఫోల్డర్, మీరు బ్రౌజర్‌ను ప్రతిసారీ అడగమని సెట్ చేస్తే తప్ప (చూడండి బ్రౌజర్‌లు మరియు కంప్యూటర్‌లు). ఫైల్ మార్చే యాప్‌లు \"(edited)\" లేదా \"(marked up)\" వంటి కొత్త పేరుతో కాపీని సేవ్ చేస్తాయి, తద్వారా మీ అసలు ఫైల్ మారకుండా ఉంటుంది. సేవ్ చేయని పనితో మీరు ట్యాబ్‌ను మూసివేస్తే, బ్రౌజర్ ముందుగా అడుగుతుంది (\"సైట్ నుండి వెళ్లాలా?\"). బ్రౌజర్ ఏమి గుర్తుంచుకుంటుంది కొన్ని యాప్‌లు మీ రచయిత పేరు లేదా టూల్ శైలులు వంటి చిన్న సెట్టింగ్‌లను మీ బ్రౌజర్‌లో గుర్తుంచుకుంటాయి. అవి ఆ కంప్యూటర్‌లోని ఆ బ్రౌజర్‌లోనే ఉంటాయి, మరియు సైట్ డేటాను క్లియర్ చేస్తే అవి తొలగించబడతాయి. ప్రతి యాప్ సహాయం అది ఏమి ఉంచుతుందో చెబుతుంది. క్లౌడ్ సేవ్ కంప్యూటర్ల మధ్య క్లౌడ్ సేవ్ మరియు సింక్ వాణిజ్య లైసెన్స్‌లో భాగం. అవి ఇంకా అందుబాటులో లేవు. అప్పటివరకు, మీ ఫైళ్లను మీ ఇతర ప్రాజెక్ట్ ఫైళ్లు ఉంచే చోటే ఉంచండి, మరియు సాధారణ పద్ధతిలో బ్యాకప్ చేయండి.","lang":"te"},{"slug":"connect-ai-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Windows","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":63,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","windows"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-macos","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a Mac","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":64,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","macos"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-linux","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Linux","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":65,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","linux"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-mobile","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a phone or tablet","summary":"How your own AI app reaches the CadCraft apps from a phone or tablet: through the hosted connector, with CadCraft Studio open on a computer. Includes the one-line test.","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","android","ipad","phone","tablet","hosted connector","test"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What it can and cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the computer, open \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your AI app, add a connector (a &quot;remote&quot; or &quot;custom&quot; connector) with that address and sign in when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn An AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a hosted connector instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer. How On the computer, open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI and choose Hosted connector. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in. In your AI app, add a connector (a \"remote\" or \"custom\" connector) with that address and sign in when asked. Keep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. What it can and cannot do The hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"local-models","scope":"basics","title":"Use an AI that runs on your own computer","summary":"Work with a local AI model and no network: through an AI app that can use tools, .","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","local","offline","ollama","lm studio","llama.cpp","jan","air-gapped","private","model","ask box"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp&#039;s server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (&quot;MCP&quot;). Add the single \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E entry shown in \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan use tools through MCP\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELM Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ellama.cpp (llama-server)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, start it with \u003Ccode\u003E--jinja\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, turn on tool calling for the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOllama\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis list is taken from each program&#039;s own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaise the model&#039;s context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmall models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else stays the same: the permission levels, \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggest\u003C/strong\u003E mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. How Use an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp's server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\"). Add the single cadcraft entry shown in Connect your AI, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app. Program Can use tools through MCP LM Studio Yes llama.cpp (llama-server) Yes, start it with --jinja Jan Yes, turn on tool calling for the model Ollama Not on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front This list is taken from each program's own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system. Good to know Raise the model's context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit. Small models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools. Everything else stays the same: the permission levels, Suggest mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"engines","scope":"basics","title":"Run an engine on a model","summary":"The Engines panel runs the shared calculation engines (schematics, pipe and power networks, motion, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems, PLC) on a model you paste.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["engines","schematic","network","power","load flow","robot","kinematics","queue","simulation","schedule","critical path","requirements","risk","fault tree","modbus","circuit","spice","board","pcb","modelica","plc"],"headings":[{"id":"run-one","text":"Run one","level":2},{"id":"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene","text":"Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open \u003Cstrong\u003EEngines\u003C/strong\u003E from the button in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and type &quot;engines&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-one\"\u003ERun one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come back as tables, with the engine&#039;s own problems above them and the limits of its method below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the example text back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene\"\u003EControls on a model, or on the 3D scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to a flow model\u003C/span\u003E is the queues engine&#039;s model. In ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to the 3D scene\u003C/span\u003E ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads \u003Ccode\u003EName.x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.z\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.rotZ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EName.hidden\u003C/code\u003E, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and \u003Ccode\u003Evalues\u003C/code\u003E says what off and on mean (\u003Ccode\u003E[0, 2]\u003C/code\u003E puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;keep&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProblems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Some calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open Engines from the button in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+K and type \"engines\". Run one Choose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls. Choose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text). Press Run. The results come back as tables, with the engine's own problems above them and the limits of its method below. Reset puts the example text back. Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene The controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. The program bound to a flow model is the queues engine's model. In ModelCraft, The program bound to the 3D scene ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads Name.x, Name.y, Name.z, Name.rotZ or Name.hidden, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a scale (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and values says what off and on mean ([0, 2] puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \"keep\": true to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name. Things to know Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file. Problems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source. The examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design. The results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"community-testing","scope":"basics","title":"Community testing","summary":"How to try a test on the Community testing page and send your result.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["community","testing","test","results","report","feedback","files","passed","failing"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-result","text":"Send a result","level":2},{"id":"sending-files","text":"Sending files","level":2},{"id":"your-privacy","text":"Your privacy","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work","text":"What counts as \"known not to work\"","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-result\"\u003ESend a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECommunity testing\u003C/strong\u003E page on the website and choose a test.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse the public build named in \u003Cstrong\u003EBefore you start\u003C/strong\u003E, never a lab build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollow the \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps\u003C/strong\u003E and compare with the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpected result\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in \u003Cstrong\u003ESend a result\u003C/strong\u003E: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-files\"\u003ESending files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-privacy\"\u003EYour privacy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour first name is shown next to a result only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow my first name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENotes are shown to others only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAnyone may read my notes\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work\"\u003EWhat counts as &quot;known not to work&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Some checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help. Send a result Open the Community testing page on the website and choose a test. Use the public build named in Before you start, never a lab build. Follow the Steps and compare with the Expected result. Fill in Send a result: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened. You do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files. Sending files When a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages. Your privacy A result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page. Your first name is shown next to a result only if you tick Show my first name. Notes are shown to others only if you tick Anyone may read my notes. Files are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first. What counts as \"known not to work\" Each test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"input-devices","scope":"basics","title":"Use a trackpad, 3D mouse or controller","summary":"Set what a trackpad, your mouse's side buttons, a pen, finger taps, a 3D mouse, a game controller or a MIDI controller does, for every app or for one app.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","two fingers","pinch","scroll","mouse","side button","back button","forward button","tilt wheel","pen","stylus","eraser","tap","3D mouse","space mouse","6 axis","game controller","gamepad","joystick","MIDI","knob","fader","pad","input device","customize","assign"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"every-app-or-only-this-app","text":"Every app, or only this app","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons","text":"Mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-finger-taps","text":"Pen and finger taps","level":2},{"id":"3d-mouse","text":"3D mouse","level":2},{"id":"game-controller","text":"Game controller","level":2},{"id":"midi-controller","text":"MIDI controller","level":2},{"id":"share-your-settings","text":"Share your settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInput devices\u003C/span\u003E, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-app-or-only-this-app\"\u003EEvery app, or only this app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThese settings are for\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly\u003C/span\u003E this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E again to drop this app&#039;s own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app&#039;s apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan the view\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit (3D views)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E: zoom, as the app does, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse the direction\u003C/span\u003E apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons\"\u003EMouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack side button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward side button\u003C/span\u003E: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit everything in view\u003C/span\u003E. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWheel tilted sideways\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel&#039;s zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app&#039;s own tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-finger-taps\"\u003EPen and finger taps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser end\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, or any command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThree-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPalm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-mouse\"\u003E3D mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect a 3D mouse\u003C/span\u003E and pick it in the browser&#039;s list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach movement (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELift and press\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwist\u003C/span\u003E) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the strongest movement at a time\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach button runs a command or fits the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"game-controller\"\u003EGame controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse up and down on the sticks\u003C/span\u003E are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"midi-controller\"\u003EMIDI controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect MIDI\u003C/span\u003E. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a control for\u003C/span\u003E (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn it\u003C/span\u003E, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-settings\"\u003EShare your settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the settings shown as a file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads one back (settings this version doesn&#039;t know are left out, and you&#039;re told how many). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the defaults\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change for the settings shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app&#039;s own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in Settings › Global › Input devices, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before. Every app, or only this app At the top, These settings are for: Every app (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own. Only this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps. Choose Every app again to drop this app's own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app's apps. Trackpad Two-finger scroll: What the app does, Pan the view, Zoom or Orbit (3D views). Pinch: zoom, as the app does, or Nothing. Speed and Reverse the direction apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit. The app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does. Mouse buttons Back side button and Forward side button: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or Fit everything in view. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page. Wheel tilted sideways: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing. The wheel's zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app's own tab, under Menus, keys and mouse. Pen and finger taps Side button and Eraser end: what the app does, or any command. Two-finger tap and Three-finger tap (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or Nothing. Palm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under Pen and touch. 3D mouse Choose Connect a 3D mouse and pick it in the browser's list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer: Each movement (Push left and right, Push forward and back, Lift and press, Tilt forward and back, Tilt left and right, Twist) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and Reverse turns it round. Speed, Dead zone (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and Only the strongest movement at a time. Each button runs a command or fits the view. A 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Game controller Plug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. Speed, Dead zone and Reverse up and down on the sticks are there too. MIDI controller Choose Connect MIDI. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under Add a control for (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose Learn it, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Share your settings Export saves the settings shown as a file; Import reads one back (settings this version doesn't know are left out, and you're told how many). Back to the defaults undoes every change for the settings shown. Not built yet A wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app's own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"full-screen-crosshair","scope":"basics","title":"Full-screen crosshair","summary":"Two thin lines through the pointer, edge to edge across the drawing or view, to check by eye that things line up. One switch turns it on in every app.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","crosshair","cursor","cursorsize","cursorsize 100","edge to edge","line up","alignment","align","guide lines","cross hairs"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), go to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECrosshair from edge to edge in every app\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EFull-screen crosshair\u003C/strong\u003E. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EToolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003ECursor\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,), go to General and tick Crosshair from edge to edge in every app under Full-screen crosshair. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open. What it does The lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running. Toolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them. With a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen. In CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: Settings › CadCraft › Cursor › Full screen, or type CURSORSIZE and 100.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"te","chain":["te","en"]};
